window.STORIES = [{"id": "oba-oranyan", "emoji": "👑", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#1a0a00,#4a2800)", "category": "itan-oba", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "9 min", "title": {"en": "Oranyan: The Founder Who Rode Out of Ife", "yo": "Ọ̀rànyàn"}, "desc": {"en": "The prince who rode out of Ife, founded Oyo where his expedition faltered, and began the line of the Alaafin.", "yo": "Ọmọ-ọba tí ó gẹṣin jáde láti Ifẹ̀, tí ó dá Ọ̀yọ́ sílẹ̀ níbi tí ìrìn-àjò rẹ̀ ti kùnà, tí ó sì bẹ̀rẹ̀ ìlà àwọn Aláàfin."}, "yo_full": true, "primary_category": "Itan Oba", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Alaafin, the first Alaafin of Oyo, founder of the dynasty)", "segments": [{"id": "oba-oranyan#s1", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Gather close, for tonight we begin the greatest chain of stories our people keep, the line of the Alaafin of Oyo, king after king after king, from the very first who mounted a horse and rode north to found a kingdom, down through the ages to the one who sits upon that same throne in our own day. It is a long road we will walk together, and we will walk it one king at a time, giving each his own night by the fire, for every one of them was a whole man with a whole life, and it would dishonor them to blur them into a list. And it begins, as so many Yoruba things begin, at Ile Ife, the navel of the world, and with a man named Oranyan.", "yo": "Ẹ parapọ̀ súnmọ́ tòsí, nítorí lálẹ́ yìí ni àwa ó bẹ̀rẹ̀ ẹ̀wọ̀n ìtàn tí ó tóbi jùlọ tí àwọn ènìyàn wa pa mọ́, ìlà àwọn Aláàfin Ọ̀yọ́, ọba lẹ́yìn ọba lẹ́yìn ọba, láti ọ̀dọ̀ ẹni àkọ́kọ́ tí ó gun ẹṣin tí ó sì gẹṣin lọ sí àríwá láti dá ìjọba sílẹ̀, títí dé àwọn ọjọ́ orí wa lónìí tí ẹni náà jókòó sórí ìtẹ́ kan náà. Ọ̀nà gígùn ni a ó jọ rìn, a ó sì rìn ín ní ọba kọ̀ọ̀kan, a ó fún ẹnìkọ̀ọ̀kan ní òru tirẹ̀ lẹ́gbẹ̀ẹ́ iná, nítorí ẹnìkọ̀ọ̀kan wọn jẹ́ ọkùnrin pípé pẹ̀lú ẹ̀mí pípé, yóò sì bu ẹ̀gàn fún wọn láti da wọ́n pọ̀ mọ́ àtòjọ lásán. Ó sì bẹ̀rẹ̀, gẹ́gẹ́ bí ọ̀pọ̀lọpọ̀ ohun Yorùbá ṣe ń bẹ̀rẹ̀, ní Ilé-Ifẹ̀, ìhín-ìhín ayé, pẹ̀lú ọkùnrin kan tí orúkọ rẹ̀ ń jẹ́ Ọ̀rànyàn."}, {"id": "oba-oranyan#s2", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Oranyan, whom some call Oranmiyan, was of the very blood of Oduduwa, the great ancestor from whom the crowned kings of the Yoruba descend. He was the youngest of the mighty sons and grandsons who scattered out from Ife to found kingdoms across the land, and the songs remember him as the most restless and the most warlike of them all, a man who could not sit still while there were lands unwon and wrongs unavenged. He was a horseman in an age when the horse was still a wonder, tall and fierce and proud, and where he rode, kingdoms were born behind him.", "yo": "Ọ̀rànyàn, tí àwọn kan ń pè ní Ọ̀rànmíyàn, wá láti inú ẹ̀jẹ̀ Odùduwà fúnra rẹ̀, baba ńlá tí àwọn ọba adé Yorùbá ti ṣẹ̀. Òun ni àbíkẹ́yìn nínú àwọn ọmọ àti ọmọ-ọmọ alágbára tí wọ́n tú káàkiri láti Ifẹ̀ lọ dá àwọn ìjọba sílẹ̀ jákèjádò ilẹ̀, orin sì rántí rẹ̀ gẹ́gẹ́ bí aláìnísinmi àti ológun jùlọ nínú gbogbo wọn, ọkùnrin tí kò lè jókòó jẹ́ẹ́ nígbà tí àwọn ilẹ̀ wà tí a kò tí ì ṣẹ́gun tàbí àwọn àìtọ́ tí a kò tí ì gbẹ̀san. Ẹlẹ́ṣin ni ní àkókò tí ẹṣin ṣì jẹ́ ìyanu, ó ga, ó le, ó sì gbéraga, àti ní ibi tí ó bá gẹṣin dé, àwọn ìjọba a bí sílẹ̀ lẹ́yìn rẹ̀."}, {"id": "oba-oranyan#s3", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "The elders tell that when Oduduwa grew old and the question came of how his lands should be divided among his heirs, there was a great sending out of princes to found towns of their own, and Oranyan, being restless, went farthest and fought hardest. But before Oyo, there was a long wandering. The most famous of the old accounts tells of a great expedition that Oranyan led to the far northeast, meaning to avenge or to reclaim, riding out with a host to settle old scores in the lands from which the ancestors were said to have come. It was a march of great ambition, and like many such marches it met the hard truth of distance and disunity. Quarrels rose among the peoples who marched with him. The expedition faltered. And Oranyan, unable to go forward and unwilling to crawl home defeated, turned aside to make something new.", "yo": "Àwọn àgbà sọ pé nígbà tí Odùduwà dàgbà tí ìbéèrè sì dé nípa bí a ó ṣe pín ilẹ̀ rẹ̀ láàrín àwọn àrólé rẹ̀, ìrànṣẹ́ ńlá kan wà tí a rán àwọn ọmọ-ọba jáde láti lọ dá àwọn ìlú tiwọn sílẹ̀, Ọ̀rànyàn, gẹ́gẹ́ bí aláìnísinmi, lọ jìnnà jùlọ ó sì jà le jùlọ. Ṣùgbọ́n ṣáájú Ọ̀yọ́, ìrìn-àjò gígùn kan wà. Èyí tí ó gbajúmọ̀ jùlọ nínú àwọn ìtàn àtijọ́ sọ nípa ìrìn-àjò ológun ńlá kan tí Ọ̀rànyàn darí lọ sí àríwá-ìlà-oòrùn jíjìn, pẹ̀lú ète láti gbẹ̀san tàbí láti tún gbà padà, tí ó gẹṣin jáde pẹ̀lú ọ̀wọ́ ọmọ-ogun láti yanjú àwọn ọ̀ràn àtijọ́ ní àwọn ilẹ̀ tí a sọ pé àwọn baba ńlá ti wá. Ìrìn-àjò ìfẹ́-ọkàn ńlá ni, àti bí ọ̀pọ̀ irú ìrìn bẹ́ẹ̀, ó pàdé òtítọ́ líle ti ìjìnnà àti àìníṣọ̀kan. Ìjà dìde láàrín àwọn ènìyàn tí wọ́n bá a rìn. Ìrìn-àjò náà kùnà. Ọ̀rànyàn, tí kò lè tẹ̀síwájú tí kò sì fẹ́ yọ́ padà sílé ní ìṣẹ́gun, yà sí ẹ̀gbẹ́ láti dá ohun tuntun kan."}, {"id": "oba-oranyan#s4", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Here the story gives us one of its loveliest images, and I will tell it to you as the elders tell it, whether it be plain fact or the poetry in which a people wraps its beginnings. They say that Oranyan, halted and uncertain where to plant his new kingdom, was given a sign. A great serpent, or in some tellings a sacred bird set upon the ground by the diviners, showed him the spot. Where the serpent coiled and stayed, or where the bird alighted and scratched the earth, there he was to build. And the serpent settled, and after some days it vanished into the ground, and on that blessed spot Oranyan founded the city that would become Oyo, the seat of a power that would one day stretch its hand across the whole of Yorubaland and beyond.", "yo": "Níhìn-ín ni ìtàn náà fún wa ní ọ̀kan nínú àwọn àwòrán rẹ̀ tí ó lẹ́wà jùlọ, èmi ó sì sọ ọ́ fún yín gẹ́gẹ́ bí àwọn àgbà ṣe ń sọ ọ́, yálà òtítọ́ gbangba ni tàbí ewì tí àwọn ènìyàn fi ń pa ìpilẹ̀ṣẹ̀ wọn mọ́. Wọ́n sọ pé Ọ̀rànyàn, tí ó dúró tí kò sì dá a lójú ibi tí yóò gbin ìjọba tuntun rẹ̀ sí, ni a fún ní àmì. Ejò ńlá kan, tàbí nínú àwọn ìtàn kan ẹyẹ mímọ́ kan tí àwọn babaláwo gbé lé ilẹ̀, fi ibi náà hàn án. Ibi tí ejò náà bá ká tí ó sì dúró, tàbí ibi tí ẹyẹ náà bá bà tí ó sì hó ilẹ̀, níbẹ̀ ni kí ó kọ́. Ejò náà sì dúró, lẹ́yìn ọjọ́ díẹ̀ ó sì parẹ́ sínú ilẹ̀, àti ní ibi ìbùkún náà ni Ọ̀rànyàn dá ìlú tí yóò di Ọ̀yọ́ sílẹ̀, ìjókòó agbára tí yóò nà ọwọ́ rẹ̀ ní ọjọ́ kan káàkiri gbogbo ilẹ̀ Yorùbá àti kọjá."}, {"id": "oba-oranyan#s5", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "So was Oyo born, not in ease but out of a broken expedition and a wandering prince’s refusal to fail quietly. And this is the first lesson the founder leaves us, before ever he is crowned in our telling: that a great thing is often built on the ground where a different great thing fell apart, and that the mark of a true leader is not that he never fails, but that when the road forward closes, he has the vision to build where he stands.", "yo": "Bẹ́ẹ̀ ni a bí Ọ̀yọ́, kì í ṣe nínú ìrọ̀rùn bíkòṣe láti inú ìrìn-àjò tí ó fọ́ àti kíkọ̀ ọmọ-ọba arìnrìn-àjò láti kùnà ní ìdákẹ́jẹ́ẹ́. Èyí sì ni ẹ̀kọ́ àkọ́kọ́ tí olùpilẹ̀ṣẹ̀ fi sílẹ̀ fún wa, kí a tó dé adé fún un nínú ìtàn wa: pé ohun ńlá ni a sábà ń kọ́ sórí ilẹ̀ tí ohun ńlá mìíràn ti wó lulẹ̀, àti pé àmì olórí tòótọ́ kì í ṣe pé kì í kùnà láé, bíkòṣe pé nígbà tí ọ̀nà iwájú bá sé, ó ní ìríran láti kọ́ ní ibi tí ó dúró sí."}, {"id": "oba-oranyan#s6", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "But Oyo was not the only city Oranyan’s hands raised, and this is a thing you must know, for it shows the reach of the man. Before or during his wanderings, the traditions tie him also to the founding of the royal line of Benin, that great kingdom to the east. The people of Benin, so the old accounts say, having need of a ruler, sent to Ife for a prince of Oduduwa’s blood, and Oranyan went to them, and though he did not stay, he left behind a son who became the founder of the dynasty of the Obas of Benin. So the same restless prince stands at the head of two of the mightiest crowns in all this region, Oyo in the Yoruba country and Benin among their neighbors, a bridge of blood between two great peoples.", "yo": "Ṣùgbọ́n Ọ̀yọ́ kì í ṣe ìlú kan ṣoṣo tí ọwọ́ Ọ̀rànyàn gbé dìde, èyí sì jẹ́ ohun tí ẹ gbọ́dọ̀ mọ̀, nítorí ó fi ìtóbi ọkùnrin náà hàn. Ṣáájú tàbí nígbà ìrìn-àjò rẹ̀, àwọn àṣà so ó pọ̀ pẹ̀lú ìpilẹ̀ṣẹ̀ ìlà ọba Bìní, ìjọba ńlá yẹn ní ìlà-oòrùn. Àwọn ará Bìní, gẹ́gẹ́ bí àwọn ìtàn àtijọ́ ṣe wí, tí wọ́n nílò olórí, rán sí Ifẹ̀ fún ọmọ-ọba nínú ẹ̀jẹ̀ Odùduwà, Ọ̀rànyàn sì tọ̀ wọ́n lọ, bí ó tilẹ̀ jẹ́ pé kò dúró, ó fi ọmọkùnrin kan sílẹ̀ tí ó di olùpilẹ̀ṣẹ̀ ìdílé àwọn Ọba Bìní. Bẹ́ẹ̀ ni ọmọ-ọba aláìnísinmi kan náà dúró ní orí adé méjì tí ó lágbára jùlọ ní gbogbo agbègbè yìí, Ọ̀yọ́ ní ilẹ̀ Yorùbá àti Bìní láàrín àwọn aládùúgbò wọn, afárá ẹ̀jẹ̀ láàrín àwọn ènìyàn ńlá méjì."}, {"id": "oba-oranyan#s7", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "And there is a saying that came from his time in Benin that the elders still repeat, for it captures the whole of Oranyan’s nature. When he judged the land not to his liking and resolved to leave, he is remembered to have said, in words that became his praise and his epitaph both, that the wise traveler knows when a place has given him what it will and when it is time to ride on. The name by which a certain quarter and a certain memory of him are kept in Benin echoes this parting. He was a man who founded and moved on, who planted crowns and did not cling to them, whose gift was beginning rather than sitting still.", "yo": "Òwe kan sì wà tí ó ti ìgbà rẹ̀ ní Bìní wá tí àwọn àgbà ṣì ń sọ, nítorí ó gba gbogbo ẹ̀dá Ọ̀rànyàn. Nígbà tí ó dá ilẹ̀ náà lẹ́jọ́ pé kò wù ú tí ó sì pinnu láti lọ, a rántí rẹ̀ pé ó sọ, nínú ọ̀rọ̀ tí ó di ìyìn àti àkọlé-ikú rẹ̀ méjèèjì, pé arìnrìn-àjò ọlọ́gbọ́n a mọ̀ nígbà tí ibì kan bá ti fún òun ní ohun tí yóò fún òun àti nígbà tí ó tó láti gẹṣin kúrò. Orúkọ tí a fi ń pa àdúgbò kan àti ìrántí kan nípa rẹ̀ mọ́ ní Bìní ṣe àfihàn ìpínyà yìí. Ọkùnrin ni tí ó dá sílẹ̀ tí ó sì ń lọ, tí ó gbin àwọn adé tí kò sì rọ̀ mọ́ wọn, tí ẹ̀bùn rẹ̀ jẹ́ ìbẹ̀rẹ̀ dípò jíjókòó jẹ́ẹ́."}, {"id": "oba-oranyan#s8", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "When at last his years of war and wandering were behind him, the tradition tells that Oranyan returned toward Ife, the city of his fathers, and there ended his days. At Ife to this day there stands a tall monument of stone, ancient and weathered, that the people call the Staff of Oranyan, Opa Oranyan, a great standing pillar taller than several men, studded with iron nails in a pattern the meaning of which the centuries have half worn away. Whether it truly marks his grave or stood for some other purpose in the deep past, the people have long held it as his memorial, the planted staff of the founder, pointing at the sky in the middle of the holy city, so that no one walking there could forget the man who rode out and made kingdoms.", "yo": "Nígbà tí àwọn ọdún ogun àti ìrìn-àjò rẹ̀ wà lẹ́yìn rẹ̀ níkẹyìn, àṣà sọ pé Ọ̀rànyàn padà sí Ifẹ̀, ìlú àwọn baba rẹ̀, níbẹ̀ ni ó sì parí ọjọ́ rẹ̀. Ní Ifẹ̀ di òní olónìí, ère òkúta gíga kan dúró, àtijọ́ tí ó sì ti gbó, tí àwọn ènìyàn ń pè ní Ọ̀pá Ọ̀rànyàn, òpó tó dúró tí ó ga ju ọkùnrin púpọ̀ lọ, tí a fi ìṣó irin kọ sí ní ìlà tí ìtumọ̀ rẹ̀ ti fẹ́rẹ̀ẹ́ parẹ́ ní ọ̀pọ̀ ọ̀rúndún. Yálà ó jẹ́ àmì sàréè rẹ̀ ní tòótọ́ tàbí ó dúró fún ìdí mìíràn ní ìgbà jíjìn, àwọn ènìyàn ti pẹ́ pa á mọ́ gẹ́gẹ́ bí ìrántí rẹ̀, ọ̀pá tí olùpilẹ̀ṣẹ̀ gbin, tí ó tọ́ka sí ọ̀run ní àárín ìlú mímọ́, kí ẹnikẹ́ni tí ó bá ń rìn níbẹ̀ má lè gbàgbé ọkùnrin tí ó gẹṣin jáde tí ó sì dá àwọn ìjọba."}, {"id": "oba-oranyan#s9", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "Now before he passed from the scene, Oranyan did the thing that makes him not merely a hero but the founder of a line, the thing without which there would be no chain of stories for us to tell. He left an heir, and he left a throne, and he set in motion the succession that would run unbroken, king after king, down the long centuries to our own time. The tradition remembers that he left behind a son who would take up the rule after him, and from that son the line descends. Whether the very next to reign was the gentle Ajaka or the thunderous one whose story we will tell after this, the elders debate, and we will honor that debate when we come to it. What matters tonight is that Oranyan did not only conquer. He established. He turned a war camp into a capital and a bloodline into a dynasty, and that is the harder and the greater work.", "yo": "Nísisìyí kí ó tó kúrò lójú ìran, Ọ̀rànyàn ṣe ohun tí ó mú kí ó má ṣe jẹ́ akọni lásán bíkòṣe olùpilẹ̀ṣẹ̀ ìlà kan, ohun tí láìsí rẹ̀ kò ní sí ẹ̀wọ̀n ìtàn fún wa láti sọ. Ó fi àrólé sílẹ̀, ó fi ìtẹ́ sílẹ̀, ó sì mú ìtẹ̀léra tí yóò sáré láìsẹ́, ọba lẹ́yìn ọba, dìde, títí dé àwọn ọ̀rúndún gígùn dé àkókò tiwa. Àṣà rántí pé ó fi ọmọkùnrin kan sílẹ̀ tí yóò gba ìjọba lẹ́yìn rẹ̀, àti láti ọ̀dọ̀ ọmọ náà ni ìlà náà ti sọ̀kalẹ̀. Yálà ẹni tí ó tẹ̀lé e lórí ìjọba ni Àjàká pẹ̀lẹ́ tàbí ẹni aárá tí a ó sọ ìtàn rẹ̀ lẹ́yìn èyí, àwọn àgbà ń jiyàn, a ó sì bọ̀wọ̀ fún ìjiyàn náà nígbà tí a bá dé ibẹ̀. Ohun tí ó ṣe pàtàkì lálẹ́ yìí ni pé Ọ̀rànyàn kò ṣẹ́gun nìkan. Ó fìdí rẹ̀ múlẹ̀. Ó sọ àgọ́ ogun di olú-ìlú àti ìlà-ẹ̀jẹ̀ di ìdílé-ọba, èyí sì ni iṣẹ́ tí ó le tí ó sì tóbi jù."}, {"id": "oba-oranyan#s10", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "For any strong man can win a battle. It takes a founder to build a thing that outlives him. Oranyan’s true monument is not the stone pillar at Ife, tall as it is. His true monument is every Alaafin who came after him, forty and more of them across the centuries, each one sitting on a throne that a restless prince set up when a failed expedition forced him to build where he stood. Every story we will tell in this long chain, every king wise or foolish, every golden age and every catastrophe, hangs from the peg that Oranyan drove into the earth. He is the root. All the rest is the tree.", "yo": "Nítorí ẹnikẹ́ni alágbára lè ṣẹ́gun ogun. Olùpilẹ̀ṣẹ̀ ni ó ń gbé ohun tí ó ju ara rẹ̀ lọ kalẹ̀. Ìrántí tòótọ́ Ọ̀rànyàn kì í ṣe òpó òkúta tí ó wà ní Ifẹ̀, bí ó ti ga tó. Ìrántí tòótọ́ rẹ̀ ni gbogbo Aláàfin tí ó dé lẹ́yìn rẹ̀, ogójì ó lé tí wọ́n jẹ ní àwọn ọ̀rúndún, ẹnìkọ̀ọ̀kan jókòó sórí ìtẹ́ tí ọmọ-ọba aláìnísinmi gbé kalẹ̀ nígbà tí ìrìn-àjò tí ó kùnà fi agbára mú un láti kọ́ ní ibi tí ó dúró. Gbogbo ìtàn tí a ó sọ nínú ẹ̀wọ̀n gígùn yìí, gbogbo ọba ọlọ́gbọ́n tàbí òmùgọ̀, gbogbo sànmánì wúrà àti gbogbo àjàkálẹ̀, so mọ́ èèkàn tí Ọ̀rànyàn lù sínú ilẹ̀. Òun ni gbòǹgbò. Igi ni ìyókù."}, {"id": "oba-oranyan#s11", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So remember Oranyan first of all, the youngest and the fiercest of the children of Oduduwa, the horseman who rode farther than his brothers, who founded Benin’s line and then founded Oyo’s, who knew when to build and when to move on, and whose staff still stands at Ife pointing at the heavens. When you hear the drums praise the Alaafin, when you hear that ancient title rolled out with all its weight, remember that it began with one man’s refusal to fail quietly in a far country. He built where he stood. And where he stood, an empire rose. Tomorrow night we will meet the one who came after him, and the great chain will have its second link.", "yo": "Nítorí náà ẹ rántí Ọ̀rànyàn ṣáájú gbogbo rẹ̀, àbíkẹ́yìn àti olóró jùlọ nínú àwọn ọmọ Odùduwà, ẹlẹ́ṣin tí ó gẹṣin jìnnà ju àwọn arákùnrin rẹ̀ lọ, tí ó dá ìlà Bìní sílẹ̀ tí ó sì tún dá ti Ọ̀yọ́ sílẹ̀, tí ó mọ nígbà tí yóò kọ́ àti nígbà tí yóò lọ, tí ọ̀pá rẹ̀ sì ṣì dúró ní Ifẹ̀ tí ó ń tọ́ka sí ọ̀run. Nígbà tí ẹ bá gbọ́ tí ìlù ń yin Aláàfin, nígbà tí ẹ bá gbọ́ tí a ń ta orúkọ oyè àtijọ́ yẹn jáde pẹ̀lú gbogbo ìwúwo rẹ̀, ẹ rántí pé ó bẹ̀rẹ̀ pẹ̀lú kíkọ̀ ọkùnrin kan láti kùnà ní ìdákẹ́jẹ́ẹ́ ní ilẹ̀ jíjìn. Ó kọ́ ní ibi tí ó dúró. Àti ní ibi tí ó dúró sí, ilẹ̀-ọba dìde. Lóru ọ̀la a ó pàdé ẹni tí ó dé lẹ́yìn rẹ̀, ẹ̀wọ̀n ńlá náà yóò sì ní ìjápọ̀ kejì rẹ̀."}]}, {"id": "oba-ajaka", "emoji": "👑", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#1a0a00,#4a2800)", "category": "itan-oba", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "9 min", "title": {"en": "Ajaka: The Gentle King in a Warrior Age", "yo": "Ajaka"}, "desc": {"en": "The gentle second Alaafin, set aside for his fiery brother Sango and then called back to reign a second time.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Itan Oba", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Alaafin, the second to reign at Oyo, who reigned twice)", "segments": [{"id": "oba-ajaka#s1", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Last night we told of Oranyan the founder, the horseman who built Oyo where his expedition failed. Tonight we take the second link of the chain, and it is a strange and instructive link, for it is a king who reigned, and was set aside, and reigned again, a gentle man in an age that had little patience for gentleness. His name was Ajaka, sometimes called Dada Ajaka, and his story is the eternal argument between the soft answer and the hard hand, an argument our people have never finished having, and neither has the world."}, {"id": "oba-ajaka#s2", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Ajaka came to the throne after Oranyan, of the founder’s own blood. And where his father had been all fire and horses and war, Ajaka was of a milder metal altogether. He was a peaceable man, a lover of quiet, more inclined to let a quarrel settle itself than to ride out and crush it. In the tellings he is remembered as gentle almost to a fault, a king who preferred the calm of his own courtyard to the clamor of the battlefield, who would rather keep the peace with a troublesome vassal than break him. And in the ordinary run of things, such a man might have made a fine and beloved king. But the times were not ordinary, and the young kingdom of Oyo was surrounded by rivals who measured a king by the sharpness of his sword."}, {"id": "oba-ajaka#s3", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Now Ajaka had a brother, and the brother was everything Ajaka was not. Where Ajaka was gentle, the brother was fierce. Where Ajaka soothed, the brother struck. This brother was a man of terrible energy and towering will, a warrior and a wielder of strange powers, and his name, which we will speak in full on the night that belongs to him, was Sango. For now know only that the two brothers were as different as the cool of evening and the crack of the storm, and that the difference between them would decide the early fate of the throne."}, {"id": "oba-ajaka#s4", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "For the chiefs and the war captains of Oyo grew restless under Ajaka’s gentle rule. They watched their king decline to punish a defiant tributary, watched him seek accommodation where they wished to see conquest, and they grumbled that Oyo was becoming a soft thing that its neighbors would soon devour. A kingdom in its first generations, they felt, could not afford a peacemaker on the throne. It needed a hammer. And so, in the way of such things, the great men of the realm moved against their own king, not to kill him, for he was of the sacred blood, but to set him aside, to persuade or compel him to step down, so that his fierce brother might take up the rule and give the kingdom the iron it craved."}, {"id": "oba-ajaka#s5", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "And here is the first remarkable turn in Ajaka’s story, the thing that tells you what kind of man he truly was. He went. Faced with the loss of his throne, this gentle king did not plunge the young kingdom into civil war to keep his seat. He did not summon what forces were loyal to him and drench Oyo in the blood of its own people for the sake of his crown. He stepped aside, and let his brother take the throne, and withdrew into the quiet he had always preferred. Some in that hard age surely called it weakness. But consider it again. It takes a kind of strength to hold power, yes. It takes another kind entirely, and perhaps a rarer one, to let it go rather than destroy your own house fighting for it."}, {"id": "oba-ajaka#s6", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "So Sango reigned, and his was a reign of thunder, and we will tell it fully in its place. But every storm blows itself out, and Sango’s reign ended, as his story will show, in fire and in a departure so strange the people made him a god. And when the thunder had passed and the throne of Oyo stood empty once more, the kingdom turned, and looked around, and found that the gentle brother it had set aside was still alive, still of the sacred blood, still there in his quiet. And so Ajaka was called back. The man who had been judged too soft for the throne was asked to sit upon it a second time, because the fierce alternative had burned too hot and consumed itself."}, {"id": "oba-ajaka#s7", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "And here the elders tell a thing that makes you smile and think at once. They say that when Ajaka returned to the throne the second time, he was no longer quite so gentle. The years, the loss, the watching of his brother’s hard reign and hard end, had taught him something. In his second reign, the accounts say, Ajaka ruled with more firmness than before, having learned that a king who will not sometimes be hard cannot protect the peace he loves. He had gone away a man who could not strike, and he came back a man who could strike when he must, but who still would rather not. That is not the same as becoming his brother. It is something better. It is a gentle man who has learned the uses of strength without falling in love with it."}, {"id": "oba-ajaka#s8", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Think on that, my child, for it is the whole wisdom of Ajaka’s double reign. The first time, he was gentleness without strength, and gentleness without strength could not hold the throne. His brother was strength without gentleness, and strength without gentleness burned itself to ash. And only in Ajaka’s second reign did the two finally meet in one man, the gentleness that was his nature joined at last to the firmness his nature had lacked. He had to lose everything and come back to become a complete king. Few of us learn that lesson even once. Ajaka’s life is a whole parable of it."}, {"id": "oba-ajaka#s9", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "We do not know as much of Ajaka’s deeds as we would wish, for these are the deep early days of the kingdom, and the record thins the further back we reach. Much of what the elders keep of him is this shape of his life, the gentle king set aside for a fierce brother and then called back, rather than a long list of wars and works. But that shape is itself a treasure, because it is true to something in every generation. There is always the argument between those who say a leader must above all be strong, and those who say a leader must above all be good, and Ajaka’s life whispers to both sides that they are each holding only half of the answer."}, {"id": "oba-ajaka#s10", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "And there is a dignity in the very fact that the kingdom called him back. It would have been easy to let the gentle brother fade from memory once the fierce one had worn the crown. But Oyo remembered him, and wanted him again, and that tells you that even in that hard warrior age, the people knew the worth of a peaceable king, knew that a hammer is not the only tool a kingdom needs, and that a man who will not spill his people’s blood to keep his own seat is a man worth calling home."}, {"id": "oba-ajaka#s11", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So remember Ajaka, the second to sit where Oranyan had sat, the gentle king in a warrior age. Remember that he was judged too soft and stepped aside without a war, and that this was not only weakness but also a kind of grace. Remember that he came back harder and wiser, gentleness and strength joined at last in one man. And remember, when you are told that only the fierce can rule, that Oyo tried the fierce and then sent for the gentle one a second time. The chain of the Alaafin does not run only through hammers. Its second link was a man who learned, the long and painful way, how to be both soft and strong. Tomorrow, the thunder. Tomorrow, Sango."}]}, {"id": "oba-sango-alaafin", "emoji": "👑", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#1a0a00,#4a2800)", "category": "itan-oba", "rating": 9.6, "duration": "13 min", "title": {"en": "Sango: The King Who Became the Thunder", "yo": "Sango"}, "desc": {"en": "Sango's reign as the fourth Alaafin of Oyo: the fierce king whose fall became the birth of the thunder orisa.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Itan Oba", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Alaafin, the thunder king of Oyo, deified as the orisa of lightning)", "segments": [{"id": "oba-sango-alaafin#s1", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Now we come to the link in the chain that is not iron but lightning, the king whose life burned so fiercely that when it ended the people could not believe he had merely died, and so they made him a god instead. Sango, third to reign at Oyo, brother of gentle Ajaka whom he displaced, is the only Alaafin who is worshipped to this day on three continents, whose name is called in Cuba and Brazil and Trinidad, whose color is red, whose day is thundered from the sky. Tonight I will tell you of the man before he became the god, for both are true, and the man is the harder and the more human tale."}, {"id": "oba-sango-alaafin#s2", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Let me say first a word about how to hear this story. When a king becomes a god, the memory of him grows charged, and the plain history and the sacred myth twine together until no one can fully pull them apart. Some elders will tell you Sango’s whole tale as the doings of a divinity who walked the earth. Others speak of him as a mortal king whose powers and whose ending were later robed in the language of worship. I will honor both, and where the record is only firelight and song rather than certain fact, I will tell you so. That is not to lessen him. The truest thing about Sango is not any single date. It is that he made an impression so deep on his people that four hundred years and an ocean could not wash it out."}, {"id": "oba-sango-alaafin#s3", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Sango was, as we heard, the fierce brother, and when the chiefs set gentle Ajaka aside, it was Sango they lifted onto the throne, because they wanted a king with iron in him and Sango had iron to spare. He was a big man, broad and powerful, with a voice like a drum and eyes that made brave men look at their feet. He wore his hair plaited like a woman’s in the old royal fashion, and he loved fine things, fine cloth and beads and the trappings of majesty, and he loved above all the display of power. From the very first, this was a king who filled a room, who could not enter quietly, whose presence was itself a kind of weather."}, {"id": "oba-sango-alaafin#s4", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "And there was the matter of his powers. The traditions are full of them. They say Sango could breathe fire, that flame came from his mouth when his anger rose. They say he had a charm, a medicine, that could call lightning down from the sky, and that he used it and marveled at it and feared it. Whether you take these as the literal gifts of a divinity or as the memory of a king who worked with fire and with the terrible new power of gunpowder and with the dread arts of the babalawo, the meaning is the same. Sango commanded forces that ordinary kings did not, and everyone around him knew it, and it made him magnificent and it made him dangerous, for a man who holds the lightning is never quite safe to be near, and is never quite safe himself."}, {"id": "oba-sango-alaafin#s5", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "His reign was one of expansion and of strength. Under Sango, Oyo pressed on its neighbors and grew. He was a warrior on the throne, and the war captains who had wanted a hammer got their hammer, and for a time the kingdom rang with his victories. He built the power of the crown, he overawed the vassals who had grown insolent under his gentle brother, and he made the name of the Alaafin a thing to be reckoned with in the land. Had his story been only this, he would be remembered as a strong early king and no more. But Sango’s fire, which built his greatness, was also the thing that consumed him, and here the tale turns toward its strange and famous end."}, {"id": "oba-sango-alaafin#s6", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "The most famous telling of Sango’s fall goes like this, and I give it to you as the elders give it. Among Sango’s mighty war chiefs were two men of great power, Timi and Gbonka, and they were rivals, and each was a terror on the field. Sango, it is said, grew uneasy at having two such fierce men so close, and thought to set them against each other so that one would remove the other and leave him only one overmighty subject to manage. So he sent them to fight, and the contest went strangely, and Gbonka in the end prevailed and returned, but returned resentful, humiliated by how the king had used him, and there was a terrible confrontation. In some tellings Gbonka defies Sango before the whole court, survives an attempt to burn him alive, and shames the king utterly, breaking his authority in front of his own people."}, {"id": "oba-sango-alaafin#s7", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "And here the fire that lived in Sango turned inward and became his ruin. In his rage and his testing of his own dread powers, the story tells, Sango called down lightning, and the lightning did not strike where he aimed it. It struck his own palace. It burned his own household. In the most sorrowful versions, the king who commanded the storm brought it down upon the roofs that sheltered his own wives and his own children, and the thing he was proudest of, his power over the sky, became the instrument of his deepest grief. The magnificent king had reached too far, gripped too much power, and the power had turned in his hand like a live coal and burned the one who held it."}, {"id": "oba-sango-alaafin#s8", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "Broken by what he had done, shamed by Gbonka, his authority in ruins and his household in ashes, Sango left. He walked out of Oyo, away from the throne, into the forest, alone. And what happened then is the heart of the mystery. The plain telling is the saddest one, that in his despair the great king took his own life, that he was found hanged from a tree in the wild, that the thunder king ended in the silence of the forest by his own hand. The people who loved him could not bear it. And so a different telling rose up to answer the grief, and it is the telling that made him a god."}, {"id": "oba-sango-alaafin#s9", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "For his devoted followers refused the shameful ending. Oba ko so, they cried, the king did not hang, and that phrase, Koso, became a holy word and a holy place. The king did not die like a broken man in the forest, they insisted. He did not hang. Rather, when his time on earth was finished, Sango ascended. He went up into the sky by a chain, or he sank into the earth at a place that became sacred, and he did not perish but was transformed, passing from the world of the living into the company of the orisa, taking his rightful place as a god, the god of thunder and lightning that in truth he had always been. His power over the storm was not a mere king’s charm after all. It was the sign that he was more than a king, that a divinity had walked among them wearing a crown, and had now gone home to the sky."}, {"id": "oba-sango-alaafin#s10", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "And to prove it, they said, look up. When the thunder rolls and the lightning strikes, that is Sango, still speaking, still present, hurling his thunderstones down from heaven. Where lightning strikes the earth, the priests of Sango would come and find the smooth stone axe-heads of the ancient forgotten peoples, and call them the thunderstones of Sango, the very bolts he had thrown. His worship spread through all Oyo and beyond, his priests dancing in red, carrying the double axe that is his sign, entering into trances in which the god himself was said to mount them and speak. The king who had ended in shame and fire was lifted by the love and the need of his people into the ranks of the eternal."}, {"id": "oba-sango-alaafin#s11", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "And then, my child, came the ocean. When the wars and the slave ships carried the people of Oyo across the water in their tens of thousands, they could not carry their farms or their thrones or their homes. But they carried Sango. In the holds of the ships and in the barracoons and on the far plantations, the enslaved kept calling his name, and Sango crossed the water with them and lived. In Cuba he is Chango, one of the mightiest of the orisa of the Lucumi. In Brazil he is Xango, thundering in the houses of Candomble. In Trinidad the Shango faith bears his very name. The king of a single African city became a god worshipped across a hemisphere, called upon by millions who never heard of Oyo, all because a man once burned so brightly that death could not convince his people he was gone."}, {"id": "oba-sango-alaafin#s12", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "But let us not lose the man in the god, for that would be the Afonja mistake, to flatten a whole life into a single bright symbol. Sango was not only lightning. He was a brother who displaced his brother, a king who built his kingdom’s strength, a ruler who grew jealous of his own mighty men and schemed to weaken them and set in motion the tragedy that undid him. He was proud, and his pride was both his engine and his poison. He loved power, and power that is loved too much always turns at last on the one who loves it. His is a cautionary tale as much as a glory, the story of a magnificent man who could not master the one enemy that masters most great men, which is himself."}, {"id": "oba-sango-alaafin#s13", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "And yet, and this is why we crown him even so, out of that flawed and fiery and finally broken life, his people made something that has outlasted empires. They took a king who ended in shame and refused to let shame be the last word. They said, our king did not die defeated, our king ascended, our king reigns forever in the storm. There is a lesson in that too, and it belongs to the people as much as to Sango. A people who love greatly can lift even a fallen hero out of his fall and set him among the stars. The making of the god Sango is not only a story about a king. It is a story about the love and the defiance of the ones who would not let him go."}, {"id": "oba-sango-alaafin#s14", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So remember Sango, third link of the great chain, the only Alaafin who is also a god. Remember the man, proud and mighty and undone by his own fire, so that you learn the danger of loving power too well. And remember the god, thundering still over Oyo and over Cuba and Brazil and everywhere our people were scattered, so that you learn how deep a mark one blazing life can leave. When the sky cracks and the rain comes down and the lightning walks the horizon, an old woman somewhere still lifts her head and says, softly, Kabiyesi, the king is passing. Oba ko so. The king did not hang. The king is thunder now. Tomorrow, the chain goes on."}]}, {"id": "oba-aganju-king", "emoji": "👑", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#1a0a00,#4a2800)", "category": "itan-oba", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "8 min", "title": {"en": "Aganju: The King of the Deep Earth", "yo": "Aganju"}, "desc": {"en": "The early Alaafin later remembered among the orisa of the wilderness and the deep earth.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Itan Oba", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Alaafin, an early Alaafin, later remembered among the orisa of the wilderness)", "segments": [{"id": "oba-aganju-king#s1", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Tonight we take the fourth link of the chain, and like the third it is a link that shines with a double light, for here again is a king whose memory grew so large that it passed beyond the throne into the company of the divinities. His name is Aganju, and where his kinsman Sango became the lord of the storm above, Aganju is remembered as the lord of the deep earth below, of the wilderness, of the molten fire at the heart of the ground, of the great uncrossable rivers and the trackless bush where no farm has ever been cut. He is one of the harder kings to see clearly, for the record of his reign is thin and the myth around his name is thick, and I will be honest with you about that as we go."}, {"id": "oba-aganju-king#s2", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "In the ordering that many of the elders keep, Aganju stands among the earliest to sit at Oyo, close to the founding, in the generations when the kingdom was still young and the line of Oranyan was still establishing what it would be. Because these are the deep early days, we have less of him as a man of council chambers and campaigns and more of him as a towering presence, a name spoken with awe. And that awe itself tells us something. Not every early king left a mark that outlived him. Most are only names on the list, remembered that they reigned and little more. Aganju is remembered as a force. Whatever he did in his years upon the throne, he did it largely enough that his people reached, as they had for Sango, past the ordinary words for a dead king and toward the language they used for the powers of nature itself."}, {"id": "oba-aganju-king#s3", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "The name Aganju itself carries the meaning of the wilderness, the open uncultivated country, the wild expanse that lies beyond the last farm and the edge of the town. To the Yoruba mind that was no empty phrase, for the wilderness was a place of both terror and power, the home of dangerous beasts and dangerous spirits, but also the source of medicine, of game, of the sacred things a hunter or a herbalist must go into the deep bush to find. A king remembered under such a name is a king associated with the mastery of that wild power, one who could go where ordinary men feared to go and command what ordinary men fled from. In the tellings, Aganju is a figure of enormous physical strength and of dominion over the untamed, a king who tamed the wild both in the land and, the poets suggest, in the hearts of a still half wild young kingdom."}, {"id": "oba-aganju-king#s4", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "The sacred traditions weave his name into the family of the orisa in ways the elders tell differently from town to town, and I will not pretend to a single settled version, for there is none. In some tellings Aganju is bound closely to Sango, named as father or forebear or kinsman in the divine genealogy, so that the lord of the earth and the lord of the storm stand together, the fire below and the fire above. In some, he is joined in the sacred stories to the great mother of waters, Yemoja, in the endless family drama of the gods that the priests preserve. What runs through all of them is that Aganju belongs to the deep and elemental things, to earth and fire and river, to the raw foundations of the world rather than to the gentle surface where crops grow and children play."}, {"id": "oba-aganju-king#s5", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "And there is a particular power the tradition gives him that I want you to hold, for it is beautiful. Aganju is called the one who carries others across, the ferryman of the great river, the strength that bears the traveler over the flood that would otherwise drown him. Where a river is too wide and too fierce to cross, there, the image says, is where Aganju’s power is needed, the mighty strength that can carry a soul from one bank to the other. It is an image of a king as the one who gets his people across the impossible places, who bears the weight when the way is deep and the current is strong. Whatever the man Aganju truly did in his reign, his people remembered him as that kind of strength, the strength that carries others over."}, {"id": "oba-aganju-king#s6", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Let me be plain with you now, as I promised. Of Aganju the reigning king, the man who sat on a particular stool in a particular year and made particular decisions, we know very little with certainty. The deep past keeps its secrets. What we have is the shape his memory took, the crown of myth his people set upon him. And a wise child learns to read even that, for the myth a people makes of a king is itself a kind of history. It tells you what they valued, what they feared, what they thought a great ruler was for. When the people of early Oyo remembered Aganju as the master of the wilderness and the strength that carries others across the flood, they were telling us what they hoped for in a king: dominion over the wild and dangerous forces of the world, and the strength to bear the people safely through the deep places."}, {"id": "oba-aganju-king#s7", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "And there is dignity in acknowledging the limits of our knowing. A lesser storyteller would invent battles and dates to fill the silence and make Aganju sound as documented as a king of last century. I will not do that to you, for a story built on invented certainty is a house built on sand, and one day it falls and takes the true things down with it. Better to say honestly, here the record thins, here we have myth and not chronicle, and to treasure the myth for what it is. Aganju is a reminder that the chain of the Alaafin reaches back into a time before writing, into the country of song and memory, and that even there, our ancestors kept faith with the names of their kings."}, {"id": "oba-aganju-king#s8", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So remember Aganju, fourth in our telling, the king of the deep earth and the wild bush and the strength that ferries the traveler across the impossible river. Remember that some kings are known to us in the full daylight of chronicle, and some come to us only in the firelight of myth, and that both are ours to keep. And when you stand one day at the edge of some great difficulty in your own life, some flood too wide to cross, remember the old image of the king whose strength carried others over, and know that our people have always believed that such strength exists, and have always named their kings for it. Tomorrow, the chain goes on, and the daylight of history begins, slowly, to return."}]}, {"id": "oba-kori", "emoji": "👑", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#1a0a00,#4a2800)", "category": "itan-oba", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "7 min", "title": {"en": "Kori: The Keeper of the Young Kingdom", "yo": "Kori"}, "desc": {"en": "The Alaafin who kept the young kingdom of Oyo steady in its founding generations.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Itan Oba", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Alaafin, an early Alaafin of the founding generations)", "segments": [{"id": "oba-kori#s1", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Tonight we take the fifth link of the chain, a king of the early generations named Kori, and with him I must teach you something about how to love a long history honestly, for Kori is one of the many kings of whom the record keeps more the fact of him than the color of him. He reigned. He held the throne of Oyo in the deep days when the kingdom was still young and still fragile, still learning whether it would survive at all. And that holding, that keeping, is itself a thing worth a night’s telling, even when the drums have not preserved for us a long roll of his particular deeds."}, {"id": "oba-kori#s2", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Let me explain why this matters, for it is easy to skip past a king like Kori and rush to the famous ones, the conquerors and the tragedies and the ones who became gods. But a kingdom is not built only by its blazing figures. It is built, even more, by the steady ones who keep the thing alive between the blazes, who hold the throne firm through the ordinary years, who pass the crown on intact to the next hand. Every dynasty that lasts a long time does so because for every Sango who burns across the sky there are several Koris who simply keep the fire banked and the house standing. Without them the line breaks, and there is no chain for us to tell. So tonight we honor the keepers, in the person of Kori."}, {"id": "oba-kori#s3", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Kori reigned in the era after the first great generations, when the raw shock of the founding had settled and the kingdom had to become something that could endure. Think of what that early time demanded. Oranyan had planted the throne. Sango had blazed and burned. Ajaka had taught the lesson of gentleness and strength. And now the young kingdom needed to become ordinary in the best sense, to develop the settled rhythms by which a state lives from year to year: the pattern of the seasons and the tribute, the ordering of the chiefs and the titles, the quiet daily work of justice and market and farm that is never sung in the heroic songs but without which no kingdom stands a single generation."}, {"id": "oba-kori#s4", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "The elders remember Kori as part of the sequence that carried the line forward toward its greatness, a king whose importance lies partly in what came through him and after him. For it was in the generations around Kori that the royal house continued to strike root, that the succession held, that the sons and grandsons who would carry Oyo toward its imperial age were born and raised. A king who keeps the line alive and hands it on stronger than he found it has done the fundamental work of kingship, even if no bard ever composed a great praise poem to a single dramatic victory of his. The greatness of Oyo that was to come rested on the fact that in these early reigns, king after king, the throne did not fall."}, {"id": "oba-kori#s5", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "And we should not imagine those keeping years were easy or empty. A young kingdom surrounded by rivals is never at peace for long. There were surely borders to defend, vassals to hold, quarrels among the great families to settle, seasons of famine and seasons of plenty, all the endless labor of holding a realm together. That Kori’s reign does not come down to us wrapped in dramatic disaster is, if you think rightly, a kind of praise. It often means a king did his work well enough that his years did not become a cautionary tale. The reigns we remember most vividly are frequently the ones where something went terribly wrong. A quiet reign is often a competent one."}, {"id": "oba-kori#s6", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "There is one more thing the tradition binds to Kori’s name, and it points forward into the greatness to come. For it is in this stretch of the line, around Kori and those near him, that the tellings begin to gather the threads that would produce the mighty kings of the middle era, the ones who would take a modest inland kingdom and make it into the terror and wonder of the whole region. The seed of empire was planted in these quiet keeping reigns. When later we tell of the great warrior kings and the golden age of Oyo, of the cavalry that thundered across the savannah and the tribute that flowed in from a hundred towns, remember that all of it grew from soil that kings like Kori tended when there was nothing yet to show for it but a throne that had not fallen and a line that had not broken."}, {"id": "oba-kori#s7", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "This is a hard truth for the young, who want every hero to blaze. But you will learn, as you grow, that most of the good that holds up the world is done quietly by people whose names are not sung, the ones who simply keep the thing going, who do their duty in the ordinary years, who hand on to their children something a little stronger than they received. Kori is the Alaafin of that truth. His night by the fire is shorter than Sango’s, not because he mattered less, but because he did the kind of work that does not make loud stories, the kind without which there would be no stories at all."}, {"id": "oba-kori#s8", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So remember Kori, fifth in our telling, keeper of the young kingdom in the fragile years. Remember that a chain is only as strong as its quietest links, and that the throne of Oyo reached its later glory because in the early days, king after king, steady hands held it firm. When you are tempted to measure a life only by its loud achievements, remember the keepers, and remember that to hold a good thing steady and pass it on unbroken is one of the great and unsung labors of the world. Tomorrow, the chain goes on, and soon the great kings of the golden age will begin to rise."}]}, {"id": "oba-oluaso", "emoji": "👑", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#1a0a00,#4a2800)", "category": "itan-oba", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "7 min", "title": {"en": "Oluaso: The King of the Long and Fruitful Reign", "yo": "Oluaso"}, "desc": {"en": "The early Alaafin remembered for a long, fruitful reign and a vast royal household.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Itan Oba", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Alaafin, an early Alaafin remembered for a long reign and a vast household)", "segments": [{"id": "oba-oluaso#s1", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Tonight we take the sixth link of the chain, a king named Oluaso, and his is a happier tale than many we will tell, for he is remembered as a king of long life and long reign, a father of a great multitude of children, a ruler under whom the kingdom rested and grew fat and multiplied. After the fire of Sango and the deep myth of Aganju and the quiet keeping of Kori, here at last is a king the tradition remembers as simply blessed, and it will do us good to sit with a season of blessing before the storms of the later reigns come upon us."}, {"id": "oba-oluaso#s2", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Oluaso reigned, the elders say, for many years, a long and stable reign, and in the old reckoning a long reign was itself accounted a great blessing and a sign of the favor of heaven upon a king. A short reign meant disorder, meant a king who fell to enemies or to intrigue or to the anger of the gods. But a king who sat upon the throne for many years, who grew old in his palace and saw his children’s children, such a king was held to have pleased the powers above and below, to have ruled in a way that the land and the ancestors and the orisa found good. Oluaso was such a king, and his long reign is his first and greatest praise."}, {"id": "oba-oluaso#s3", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "And then there are his children. The tradition remembers Oluaso as a king of an enormous household, a father of a great swarm of sons and daughters, and this too was reckoned among the highest of blessings in the old understanding. For a people who lived close to the hard edge of survival, where sickness took many children before they grew, a man rich in living children was a man the gods had smiled upon. And for a king it was more than personal joy. A king with many children had many hands to send out as governors, many daughters to bind other towns to the throne in marriage, many heirs to secure the line. Oluaso’s fruitfulness was a political strength as much as a private happiness, and the elders remember it as one of the marks of his blessed reign."}, {"id": "oba-oluaso#s4", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "But there is a shadow that falls across every king with a great many sons, and the wise storyteller names it even in a happy tale, for it will matter greatly in the reigns to come. Many sons means many possible heirs, and many possible heirs means the seed of many possible quarrels. A throne that can go to only one, in a house with dozens of princes, is a throne around which ambition gathers like flies at the market. In Oluaso’s own blessed lifetime this was strength. But the crowd of royal descendants that his fruitfulness produced would, in the generations after, become one of the tangled roots of the succession disputes that troubled Oyo. This is the way of things, my child. The very blessing of one generation can become the burden of the next. It is no fault in Oluaso. It is only the truth that nothing under heaven is a blessing pure and simple, without its own long shadow."}, {"id": "oba-oluaso#s5", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "Under Oluaso the kingdom is remembered as prosperous and at peace, growing in numbers and in settled strength. This was part of the long gathering of power that would eventually make Oyo great. Peace and plenty and a growing people are the soil in which empires are grown, though the growing is slow and undramatic. When you later hear of the mighty army of Oyo and the wide reach of its power, remember that armies are raised from populations, and that populations grow in the long peaceful reigns of blessed kings like Oluaso. The golden age did not spring from nothing. It grew, in part, from the fruitfulness of this quiet fortunate king."}, {"id": "oba-oluaso#s6", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "And yet Oluaso’s reign, blessed as it was, sits near the edge of a hard time, for it was in the generation after him that Oyo would meet its first great catastrophe, when a mighty neighbor to the north would fall upon the kingdom and drive its king and people into exile for many years. We will tell that bitter story in its place. But it is worth marking now that Oluaso’s happy reign was, in a way the people living it could not know, a last long summer before a winter. The fruitfulness he built, the people he multiplied, the strength he gathered, all of it would be tested almost to destruction in the reigns just ahead. That his blessings helped the kingdom survive that testing is the deepest, quietest part of his legacy."}, {"id": "oba-oluaso#s7", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "So when you enjoy a season of peace and plenty in your own life, remember Oluaso, and remember two things at once. Be grateful for the blessing, and use it well, for a fruitful peaceful time is a gift and a chance to grow strong. But do not imagine it will last forever, for no summer does, and the wise use their good years to prepare for the hard ones that always, in the turning of the world, eventually come. Oluaso’s people grew strong in his long summer, and that strength is what carried them through the winter that followed. Blessings are not only to be enjoyed. They are to be stored against the lean years."}, {"id": "oba-oluaso#s8", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So remember Oluaso, sixth in our telling, the king of the long life and the great household and the fruitful peace. Remember that a long reign was itself a crown of honor, that many children were counted a blessing from heaven, and that the quiet prosperity of his years was the soil from which Oyo’s later greatness grew. And remember the shadow too, that the blessing of many heirs would sow future quarrels, and that his summer stood on the edge of a coming winter. Enjoy your blessings, my child, and grow strong in them, and keep something back for the hard road ahead. Tomorrow, the chain goes on, and the winter begins to gather."}]}, {"id": "oba-onigbogi", "emoji": "👑", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#1a0a00,#4a2800)", "category": "itan-oba", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "7 min", "title": {"en": "Onigbogi: The King Who Lost the City", "yo": "Onigbogi"}, "desc": {"en": "The Alaafin under whom the Nupe drove Oyo out of its capital and into exile.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Itan Oba", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Alaafin, the Alaafin driven into exile by the Nupe)", "segments": [{"id": "oba-onigbogi#s1", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Tonight the tale turns dark, for we come to the winter I have been warning you of, and to the king upon whom it broke. His name was Onigbogi, and in his reign the unthinkable happened. The city of Oyo, the seat of Oranyan’s throne, the heart of the kingdom, fell to an enemy, and the Alaafin himself was driven out of his own capital into a long and bitter exile. It is a hard story, but you must hear it, for our people do not tell only of victories. We tell of the falls too, because it is in how a people rises after a fall that its true greatness is measured, and Oyo’s greatest greatness lay on the far side of this catastrophe."}, {"id": "oba-onigbogi#s2", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "The enemy came from the north, from across the great river, a people the Yoruba called the Tapa, whom the wider world calls the Nupe. They were a strong kingdom in their own right, mounted and warlike, and there had long been friction along the northern edge of Oyo’s world. In Onigbogi’s day that friction became fire. The Nupe fell upon Oyo in force, and the army of the kingdom, for all the strength it had gathered in the long fruitful reigns before, could not hold them. The tellings speak of defeat, of the enemy pressing in upon the capital itself, of a disaster so complete that the king could not remain in his own city and live."}, {"id": "oba-onigbogi#s3", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "The elders give a further reason for the disaster, and it is a reason woven of the sacred as much as the military, so I will tell it as they tell it. They say that a matter of religion and of insult lay at the root of Onigbogi’s fall. In some accounts, the worship of Ifa, the great oracle, the wisdom of Orunmila, had not yet been firmly established at Oyo, or had been slighted or driven out, and that the kingdom, lacking the guidance of the oracle, walked blind into its catastrophe. In the telling, Onigbogi is a king who in some way failed the sacred order, who did not keep faith with the powers that a king must keep faith with, and who therefore lost their protection when the Nupe came. Whether you read this as true cause or as the way a people explains a disaster to itself, the lesson the elders draw is the same: a king who loses the sacred foundation loses everything, for the throne stands on more than swords."}, {"id": "oba-onigbogi#s4", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "So Onigbogi fled. The Alaafin, the descendant of Oranyan, the holder of the most sacred throne among the Yoruba, went out from his fallen city a refugee, and with him went his household and his chiefs and such of his people as could follow, out into exile among neighboring peoples to the north and west. Imagine the shame and the grief of it. The city that Oranyan had founded upon the sign of the sacred serpent, the seat of a line of kings, was in the hands of the Tapa, and its king was a wanderer eating the bread of strangers. It was the lowest point the kingdom had known since its founding, a fall from which many a lesser people would never have risen."}, {"id": "oba-onigbogi#s5", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "And Onigbogi did not rise from it. This is the hardest part of his story, and I will not soften it. He went into exile, and in exile, so the tellings run, he remained, and it was not given to him to see the restoration. He was the king under whom the city was lost, and he carried that loss to the end of his days in a foreign place. The work of recovery, the long climb back, would fall to those who came after him. Some kings are remembered for the mountain they climbed. Onigbogi is remembered for the valley into which the kingdom fell in his time, and for the courage, if we may call it that, of surviving the fall at all, of keeping the sacred line alive in exile so that there would be someone left to begin the climb."}, {"id": "oba-onigbogi#s6", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Let me be fair to Onigbogi, for it is easy to make a villain of the king who was on the throne when the roof fell in, and that would be the Afonja mistake, to reduce a whole man to his worst hour. The truth is that great catastrophes are rarely the work of one man’s failure alone. The Nupe were strong, and the pressures on the northern frontier had been building for generations before Onigbogi ever wore the crown. He inherited a dangerous position and it broke in his hands, and another king might have broken under it too. History remembers him as the one who lost the city, but history is often unjust to the man who happens to be standing where the long fault line finally cracks. He was not the cause of every weakness that the Nupe exploited. He was the one left holding the crown when the account came due."}, {"id": "oba-onigbogi#s7", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "And there is something to honor even in a defeated king who does not simply vanish. Onigbogi kept the line alive. He did not let the sacred blood of Oranyan be extinguished with the fall of the city. In exile, humiliated, he preserved the one thing that made restoration possible: the continuation of the dynasty, the survival of the king and the heirs, the keeping of the idea of Oyo even when the place of Oyo was lost. A throne can survive the loss of its city if the line survives. Onigbogi’s grim, unglamorous achievement was to be the bridge of survival across the worst years, so that his successors would have something to restore."}, {"id": "oba-onigbogi#s8", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So remember Onigbogi, seventh in our telling, the king who lost the city. Remember him without cruelty, for the fall was larger than any one man, and remember that even in defeat he kept the sacred line alive so that others could one day climb back. When you face a season in which everything you have built seems to be falling, and you are the one left holding the ruin, remember that survival itself can be a victory, that to keep the seed alive through the winter is sometimes all that can be done, and that it is enough, if someone after you plants it again in the spring. Tomorrow, the exile continues, and slowly, the long climb back begins."}]}, {"id": "oba-ofinran", "emoji": "👑", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#1a0a00,#4a2800)", "category": "itan-oba", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "9 min", "title": {"en": "Ofinran: The King Who Was Born in Exile", "yo": "Ofinran"}, "desc": {"en": "The Alaafin born and crowned in the wandering years after Oyo Ile was lost.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Itan Oba", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Alaafin, the Alaafin who reigned in the years of wandering after the loss of Oyo Ile)", "segments": [{"id": "oba-ofinran#s1", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Come closer, children. Move nearer the fire, for the story I hold tonight is a story of homelessness, and homelessness is a thing best spoken of where there is warmth. Last night we sat with Onigbogi, the king who lost the city, the Alaafin who watched Oyo Ile fall to the Nupe horsemen and fled into the north with his people behind him like a river spilling out of its bed. We left him in the land of exile, an old man carrying a crown that no longer sat over a capital. Tonight we go to his son. Tonight we sit with Ofinran, and Ofinran is the king who inherited not a throne but a wound."}, {"id": "oba-ofinran#s2", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Understand what it means to be born into exile. There are children who are born into palaces, and they learn the weight of the crown slowly, as a soft thing they grow into. And there are children who are born on the road, in a borrowed town, under a borrowed roof, and these children learn the crown as a hunger, as a thing that was taken before they could hold it. Ofinran was such a child. He grew up hearing his father and the old chiefs speak of Oyo Ile the way a man speaks of a wife who has died, with a love that had nowhere to go. He grew up knowing that somewhere to the south there was a place that was his, and that strangers walked its streets, and that the drums that should have called his name were silent under the dust."}, {"id": "oba-ofinran#s3", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "We must be honest here, as we are always honest at this fire. The years of the exile are years that memory holds unevenly. This was long ago, in the deep of the sixteenth century by the reckoning of the strangers who later wrote such things down, and the griots who kept these matters kept them as living speech, not as ink. So when I tell you of Ofinran, I tell you what the tradition remembers, the shape of the thing, the truth of the arc, and where the small details grow thin I will not pour false water into an empty pot to make it seem full. That is not the way of the honest teller. What is remembered is this: that after Onigbogi, the line of Oranyan did not sit again at once in a great capital, but wandered, and settled in places that were not home, and that Ofinran was among the kings of this wandering."}, {"id": "oba-ofinran#s4", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "The Yoruba call this the season of Igboho and the towns of the north, though the full settling at Igboho belongs a little further down the chain than Ofinran himself. What matters for tonight is the spirit of the man who reigned while the dynasty still had no fixed ground beneath it. Imagine ruling a kingdom that exists more in memory than on any map. Imagine holding a court where the chiefs remember titles that belong to a palace nobody can enter. This was Ofinran’s inheritance, and he did not run from it."}, {"id": "oba-ofinran#s5", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "There is a thing the elders say about the exile kings, and I want you to hold it carefully. They say that the greatness of Oranyan’s line was not that it never fell, but that it refused to stay fallen. Any house can be proud when it stands on its own land. It is a rarer, harder pride that keeps a house standing when the land is gone, when all that remains is the name and the memory and the stubborn refusal to let the name die. Ofinran carried that stubbornness. He did not have armies to march back on Oyo Ile in his day, not yet, for the Nupe were strong and the dynasty was still gathering its breath. But he kept the court together. He kept the titles alive. He kept the sons and the chiefs and the drummers and the diviners bound to one another so that when the day of return finally came, generations later, there would still be a people to return, and not merely a scattering of families who had forgotten they were once a kingdom."}, {"id": "oba-ofinran#s6", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "That is a quiet kind of heroism, children, and it is the kind the world too often forgets. We praise the king who wins the battle. We forget the king who kept the people together in the long grey years between the losing and the winning, so that there would still be someone alive to fight the battle at all."}, {"id": "oba-ofinran#s7", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Let me tell you what the tradition holds of his temper and his troubles, for I will not flatten him into a mere placeholder between his father’s fall and his descendants’ return. That would be to commit the very sin we warned against when we spoke of Afonja, the sin of shrinking a whole man down to a single sentence. Ofinran was a man of the road, and the road makes men hard. He ruled a people who were tired, a people who had known defeat, and defeated people are difficult to lead. There are always among the exiled those who whisper that it is better to forget, better to melt into the towns that shelter you, better to marry into the strangers and let the old name go quietly into the ground. Ofinran had to fight that whisper. He had to hold up the memory of Oyo Ile against the easy comfort of forgetting, and that is a war fought not with spears but with the daily labor of a king who will not let his people sleep too soundly in a borrowed bed."}, {"id": "oba-ofinran#s8", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "The tradition remembers him as a king who kept moving, who sought better ground, who was not content to sit forever in the first town that took the dynasty in. Some of the wandering of these years was the wandering of a people still looking for a place strong enough to become a new base, a place from which the return might one day be launched. Ofinran was part of that search. He did not find the final ground himself. But he kept the people searching, and a people who are still searching are a people who have not yet given up."}, {"id": "oba-ofinran#s9", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "Now, there is a strand in the old telling that speaks of Ofinran’s death, and here the tradition takes on the color of the north where he wandered. Some say he died away from the homeland, in the country of the exile, far from the sacred groves of Oyo Ile where his fathers were laid. Think on what that means. A king of the line of Oranyan, the line that rode out of Ife itself, dying in a land that was not his, buried in soil that his ancestors never walked. There is no sharper picture of what the fall cost the dynasty than this, a king in exile laid to rest among strangers."}, {"id": "oba-ofinran#s10", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "And yet, even here, the story does not end in despair. For Ofinran left sons. He left a line. He handed the crown and the wound and the stubbornness forward, to Eguguoju and to those who came after, and each of them carried the memory of Oyo Ile a little further down the road toward the day of return. That is the thing about a dynasty that refuses to die. Each king may fall short of the great return. But each king who keeps the line alive brings the return one generation nearer. Ofinran did not walk back into Oyo Ile in triumph. But without Ofinran, and without the quiet keeping of these exile years, there would have been no one left to walk back at all."}, {"id": "oba-ofinran#s11", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So remember Ofinran, children. Remember the king who was born into loss and did not let the loss become surrender. Remember that there is a courage that wins battles and a courage that simply refuses to disappear, and that the second kind is often the harder and the less praised. When your own house is low, when what you have built has been taken and you are living, as it were, in a borrowed town, remember the exile king who kept the drummers drumming and the titles alive and the memory of home burning low but never out. The city will be won back. Not in his day. But the winning back begins with those who refuse to forget. Tomorrow, the wandering continues, and the line pushes on toward Igboho and toward the long, patient labor of return."}]}, {"id": "oba-eguguoju", "emoji": "👑", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#1a0a00,#4a2800)", "category": "itan-oba", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "9 min", "title": {"en": "Eguguoju: The King Who Gathered the Scattered", "yo": "Eguguoju"}, "desc": {"en": "The Alaafin of the exile years who gathered the scattered court and people.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Itan Oba", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Alaafin, an Alaafin of the exile years, keeper of the wandering court)", "segments": [{"id": "oba-eguguoju#s1", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Sit, children. The fire is good tonight, and the story is a good one, though it is a story with dust on its feet. We are still in the long season of homelessness, the years after Oyo Ile fell to the Nupe, the years when the crown of Oranyan traveled from town to town like a trader with no market of his own. Last night we sat with Ofinran, the king born in exile, the king who kept his people from forgetting who they were. Tonight we go one step further down the chain, to Eguguoju, and Eguguoju is remembered as one of the kings of the gathering, one of the hands that held the scattered pieces of the dynasty together in the middle of the wandering."}, {"id": "oba-eguguoju#s2", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Let me speak plainly first, as I always do, so that you know the ground you stand on. These are old, old matters. The kings of the exile lived long before the strangers came with their books and their careful dates, and what we hold of them we hold in the mouth, passed from teller to teller across many lifetimes. So the tradition remembers Eguguoju more as a name in the sacred line, a link in the great chain, than as a man whose every deed is written out. I will not stand here and invent for you a false catalog of his battles and his wives and his words, for that would be to lie at the fire, and a griot who lies at the fire poisons the well that all his listeners must drink from. What I can give you is true, and it is this: Eguguoju belongs to the generations who kept the line of Oranyan alive during the wandering, so that the return to greatness would one day be possible."}, {"id": "oba-eguguoju#s3", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Now, why do we honor a king whose deeds are held only in outline? I will tell you, and listen well, for this is the heart of tonight’s telling. We honor him because he stands in the place where the chain could have broken and did not. Picture a great chain of iron, each link a king, running from Oranyan who rode out of Ife all the way down to the Alaafin who sits today. If any single link in the exile years had broken, if any one of those wandering kings had let the court dissolve, had let the chiefs scatter to the four winds, had let the sons marry into the strangers and forget the sacred titles, then the whole chain from that point on would never have existed. There would be no imperial Oyo. No golden age. No empire stretching to the coast. The exile kings are the thin, unglamorous links that held under strain, and Eguguoju is one of them."}, {"id": "oba-eguguoju#s4", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "There is a lesson in this for every one of you, and I want you to carry it out of this circle when the fire dies down. Not every life is called to be the great climax of the story. Some lives are called to be the link that holds, the hand that keeps the thing from falling apart in the hard middle years, the quiet keeper who makes the later glory possible without ever seeing it. Eguguoju did not live to see Oyo restored to its full height. But the restoration stood on his shoulders, and on the shoulders of the kings who wandered with him. Do not despise the work of holding on. It is often the work that saves everything."}, {"id": "oba-eguguoju#s5", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "Let me paint you the world these kings moved through, so that you feel the weight of what they carried. The northern country where the dynasty wandered was not empty. It had its own peoples, its own towns, its own kings and its own gods. The exiled Oyo were guests, and guests are never wholly free. A guest must be careful. A guest must keep the goodwill of his hosts while never quite becoming his host, never quite dissolving into the household that shelters him. This is a knife’s edge to walk, and the exile kings walked it for generations. Eguguoju walked it. He had to keep his people distinct, keep them Oyo, keep the drums beating the old rhythms and the diviners casting in the old way, while living cheek by jowl with strangers who might, in one bad season, turn from hosts into enemies."}, {"id": "oba-eguguoju#s6", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "And there was always the memory of the Nupe to the matter. The Nupe horsemen who had broken Oyo Ile were not a phantom of the past. They were a living power, and the exiled dynasty had to reckon with that power, sometimes fleeing it, sometimes bargaining with it, sometimes learning from the very cavalry that had defeated them. For this is one of the great turns of the whole Oyo story, children, and I will only plant the seed of it tonight so that it grows in your minds. The horse that destroyed Oyo would one day become the horse that made Oyo an empire. The exiled dynasty, wandering in the northern country, learned the ways of cavalry, learned the grasslands where horses could be raised and ridden, and in the fullness of time turned the weapon of their enemy into the foundation of their glory. Eguguoju and the wandering kings lived in the years when that hard lesson was being learned, in the marrow, through defeat and exile and patient watching."}, {"id": "oba-eguguoju#s7", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "I will not pretend to you that these were happy years. Exile is not happy. There must have been quarrels among the chiefs, for tired and displaced people quarrel. There must have been seasons of hunger, for a people without their own farmland eats at the mercy of others. There must have been young men who slipped away and were lost to the line, and daughters given to strangers, and old customs bent by the pressure of living among other peoples. A king in such times is not a king of triumphs. He is a king of endurance, a king whose victories are measured in the simple fact that at the end of his reign the people were still a people and the crown still passed to a son of Oranyan. By that measure, Eguguoju was a victor."}, {"id": "oba-eguguoju#s8", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "And I want to say something about the dignity of endurance, because our age loves the loud and the sudden and forgets the slow and the steady. There is a picture the elders keep, of the exile king holding court under a tree in a borrowed town, the old titles being called out one by one, the Basorun’s place, the Agbaakin’s place, all the great offices named and honored even though the palace that gave them meaning lay in ruins far to the south. To an outsider it might have looked like a game of pretend, grown men honoring titles attached to a lost capital. But it was not pretend. It was the keeping of a constitution, the keeping of a shape, so that when the ground was finally found and the return finally made, the kingdom could be poured back into its proper vessel because the vessel had never been allowed to shatter. Eguguoju kept the vessel."}, {"id": "oba-eguguoju#s9", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So remember Eguguoju, children, the king who gathered the scattered and held them. Remember that there is a greatness that shines and a greatness that simply endures, and that without the enduring kind the shining kind would have nothing to stand on. Remember, when your own days are grey and the great victory feels far off, that the holding-on is itself a kind of winning, and that the ones who keep the chain from breaking in the hard middle years are as worthy of the fire’s honor as the ones who ride into the final triumph. The wandering is not over yet in our telling. But the ground is being sought, the horse is being learned, and the day of the great woman who reigned as king draws nearer. Tomorrow, we come to Orompoto."}]}, {"id": "oba-orompoto", "emoji": "👑", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#1a0a00,#4a2800)", "category": "itan-oba", "rating": 9.6, "duration": "10 min", "title": {"en": "Orompoto: The Woman Who Reigned as Alaafin", "yo": "Orompoto"}, "desc": {"en": "The woman who took the throne of Oyo, reigned as Alaafin, and led its warriors.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Itan Oba", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Alaafin, the woman who took the throne of Oyo and led its warriors as king)", "segments": [{"id": "oba-orompoto#s1", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Ah. Sit up now, children, all of you, for tonight the fire burns for a great one. Tonight the story is of Orompoto, and Orompoto is a name to be spoken with the back straight and the voice clear, for she was a woman who sat on the throne of Oyo and ruled it not as a queen beside a king, but as Alaafin, as sovereign, as the crowned head of the whole people. The girls among you, lean in. And the boys, lean in too, for a woman who could hold the empire of Oyo together in the years of its rebuilding is a person every child should carry in the heart."}, {"id": "oba-orompoto#s2", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Let me set the ground, for you must understand where we are in the great chain. We have walked through the exile, through Onigbogi who lost the city, through Ofinran born in wandering, through Eguguoju who held the scattered together. By the time we reach Orompoto, the dynasty had found firmer ground in the north, in and around the town the tradition remembers as Igboho, the great redoubt of the exile years. Oyo Ile itself still lay unrecovered, but the dynasty was no longer a mere scattering of refugees. It was gathering strength. It was learning the horse. It was becoming, once more, a power. And into this hard, hopeful season came a ruler that no one at first expected to reign at all, because that ruler was a woman."}, {"id": "oba-orompoto#s3", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Here is what the tradition holds, and I will hold it carefully, for a story this precious must not be dressed up in false finery, nor stripped of its rightful glory. When the male line faltered in the succession of those years, when there was a gap where a son should have stood, it was Orompoto who took the throne. Some tellings say she was daughter, some say sister, of the kings around her in the line, and the deep details of the household are held differently by different mouths. But on the great fact all the mouths agree, and it is this: Orompoto reigned. She was recognized as Alaafin. And she did not reign as a caretaker keeping the seat warm for a man. She reigned as a warrior-king who led Oyo through some of its most dangerous years and left it stronger than she found it."}, {"id": "oba-orompoto#s4", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Now, you may ask, how could a woman hold the throne of Oyo, in a world where the crown passed from father to son? I will tell you, and this is the first great teaching of tonight. The Yoruba have never been a people who could not imagine power in a woman’s hands. Think of the Iyalode, the great female chief who spoke for all the women of a town in its councils. Think of the market queens who commanded wealth that made kings borrow from them. Think of the priestesses of the orisa before whom even warriors bowed. Power in a woman’s hand was not unthinkable to our ancestors. It was uncommon on the Alaafin’s throne, yes, and that is exactly why Orompoto shines so bright, because she stepped into the very highest and most male of all the seats, and she filled it."}, {"id": "oba-orompoto#s5", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "And she filled it, children, in a time of war. This is what lifts Orompoto above being merely a curiosity, a woman who happened to sit on a throne. She was a war-leader. The Nupe, remember, were still a power to the north, the very people whose horsemen had broken Oyo Ile and sent the dynasty into its long exile. A woman reigning might have seemed, to Oyo’s enemies, like a moment of weakness, a moment to strike. And strike they did, or so the tradition remembers, testing the kingdom to see whether a woman on the throne meant that Oyo had grown soft. They learned. Oh, they learned. For Orompoto met them not with trembling but with cavalry."}, {"id": "oba-orompoto#s6", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "This is the great turning I planted like a seed in the earlier tellings, and now it flowers. The horse. Under the war-kings of the Igboho years, and Orompoto is remembered strongly among them, Oyo mastered the cavalry that would make it an empire. In the open grasslands of the north the exiled dynasty had learned to breed and ride and fight from horseback, turning the weapon of their conquerors into their own. And the tradition remembers Orompoto as a builder of Oyo’s mounted strength, a ruler who fielded horsemen and led them against the enemies of her people. Picture it. A woman crowned as Alaafin, at the head of a column of Oyo cavalry, the dust rising, the enemy who thought her weakness discovering their error in the worst of all ways. That is Orompoto."}, {"id": "oba-orompoto#s7", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "There is a tale the elders tell of Orompoto, and I give it to you as tradition gives it to me, as a thing that may be embroidered by love but that carries a true thread at its heart. It is said that in the thick of one great battle against the Nupe, when the fighting was fierce and the outcome hung in the balance, a horse was struck down beneath one of her chief warriors, or in some tellings beneath a figure close to the throne, and that in the confusion of that moment a strange and fearsome apparition rose to turn the tide, so that the enemy broke and fled. Some tellings wrap the moment in the marvelous, as our stories often do when they wish to mark a moment as sacred. Whether you take the marvel as it stands or as the way our people clothe a great victory in wonder, the thread beneath it is firm. Orompoto won her wars. The Nupe who tested her went home defeated. And Oyo, under a woman’s crown, was safer at the end of her reign than at the beginning."}, {"id": "oba-orompoto#s8", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "I will not flatten her, children, as we have sworn never to flatten the great. Do not carry away only the picture of the warrior. Orompoto was also a ruler of the ordinary daily kind, the kind who must settle disputes, and feed a court, and keep the chiefs from one another’s throats, and hold the sacred rites, and manage the slow rebuilding of a dynasty that still dreamed of returning to its lost capital. All of that she carried on top of the wars. A war-king who cannot also govern loses in peace what she won in battle. Orompoto governed. The line held under her. The strength grew under her. The road back toward Oyo Ile ran on beneath her feet."}, {"id": "oba-orompoto#s9", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "Let me pause and speak to you plainly, girls of this circle, and boys as well, for the lesson is for all. There will be people in your lives who look at you and decide, before you have spoken a word, that you cannot. Because you are a girl. Because you are young. Because you come from the wrong town or the poor side of it. Because the seat you wish to fill has only ever been filled by others who do not look like you. Remember Orompoto. She sat in a seat that had known only men, in an age when the crown was spoken of as a thing for sons, and she did not merely occupy it. She defended it with cavalry, she governed it with a firm hand, and she handed it on stronger than she received it. The world did not make a place for her. She made the place, and then she held it against all who came to take it."}, {"id": "oba-orompoto#s10", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "And here is the deeper thing. Orompoto is not remembered as an oddity or an accident that the tradition is faintly embarrassed by. She is remembered with pride, sung as one of the strong ones, counted among the war-kings who rebuilt Oyo’s might in the Igboho years. Her people did not merely tolerate a woman on the throne. In the end they honored her for it, because she earned the honor in the only currency that has ever truly counted, the currency of results. She kept the people safe. She kept the line alive. She pushed the great return nearer."}, {"id": "oba-orompoto#s11", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So remember Orompoto, children, the woman who reigned as Alaafin, the war-king in the years of rebuilding, the one who met those who mistook her for weakness and taught them the meaning of her strength. Remember that a throne belongs to the one who can carry it, and that the ancestors knew this even when later ages forgot it. Remember, when someone tells you that a seat is not for the likes of you, the dust rising behind Oyo’s cavalry and the woman crowned at its head. The exile is nearly ended now in our telling. The strength is gathered. The horse is mastered. Soon the dynasty will turn its face south again toward the lost city and the long dream of return. But tonight we honor the woman who made much of that strength possible. Tomorrow, the chain goes on to Ajiboyede and the hard, proud kings who carried the rebuilding forward."}]}, {"id": "oba-ajiboyede", "emoji": "👑", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#1a0a00,#4a2800)", "category": "itan-oba", "rating": 9.6, "duration": "10 min", "title": {"en": "Ajiboyede: The Hard King of the Rebuilding", "yo": "Ajiboyede"}, "desc": {"en": "The hard king of the Igboho years whose reign was marked by war, grief, and iron discipline.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Itan Oba", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Alaafin, the Alaafin of the Igboho years whose reign was marked by war, grief, and iron discipline)", "segments": [{"id": "oba-ajiboyede#s1", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Draw close, children, and settle yourselves, for tonight the fire lights a harder face than last night’s. Last night we sang of Orompoto, the woman crowned as Alaafin, the war-king whose cavalry taught the Nupe the price of mistaking her for weakness. Tonight we come to Ajiboyede, and Ajiboyede is not a king to be loved easily. He is a king to be reckoned with. He ruled in the Igboho years, in the season when Oyo had gathered strength in exile and had not yet returned to its lost capital, and he ruled with a hand of iron in a time that had need of iron. But iron, children, cuts the hand that holds it as well as the enemy it strikes, and Ajiboyede’s story is the story of a strength that saved the kingdom and wounded it at the same time."}, {"id": "oba-ajiboyede#s2", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Let me be honest about the ground, as I always am. Ajiboyede lived long ago, in the sixteenth century by the strangers’ reckoning, before books were kept in this land, and so what we hold of him we hold in the mouth of the tradition. But the tradition holds him vividly, more vividly than some, because he did things that were remembered, some for pride and some for sorrow. And that is the mark of a true figure of history, children. The forgotten kings slip through the net of memory. The ones we remember are the ones who made the water boil, for good or for ill, and Ajiboyede made the water boil."}, {"id": "oba-ajiboyede#s3", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "The tradition remembers Ajiboyede first as a warrior, and here he stands in the proud line of the Igboho war-kings who rebuilt Oyo’s might. The Nupe were still the great enemy to the north, and the wars with them did not end simply because Orompoto had won her battles. War is a field that must be plowed again and again. Ajiboyede took up that plowing. He led Oyo’s forces against the Nupe, and the tradition remembers him as a king who won, who pressed the enemy hard, who carried forward the recovery of Oyo’s honor after the humiliation of the exile. Under kings like him, the memory of the fall of Oyo Ile was slowly being answered, blow for blow, until the day would come when the dynasty could think of returning to the homeland not as beggars but as a power."}, {"id": "oba-ajiboyede#s4", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "But I must tell you of the sorrow at the heart of his war, for it is the thing the tradition remembers most sharply, and it is the thing that teaches the deepest lesson. In the course of his campaigns against the Nupe, it is said that Ajiboyede lost his own son. The prince, his heir, the child of his own body, fell in the fighting. Now think, children, on what that means for a king. He had led Oyo to victory, and the victory had cost him the one thing a father cannot replace. He had won the war and lost the future he had imagined for his own house. There is no triumph so complete that it cannot be hollowed out from within by such a grief."}, {"id": "oba-ajiboyede#s5", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "And here the tradition tells a thing that has echoed down all the generations, a thing that shows us both the greatness and the terror of the man. In his grief for his fallen son, Ajiboyede summoned the great festival, the gathering of all the chiefs and all the notables of the kingdom, the assembly where the whole realm came together. And in that assembly, wild with sorrow and with the fierce pride of a king who would not be pitied, he demanded that the chiefs mourn his son as deeply as he mourned, and he watched their faces to see who wept truly and who wept only for show. Those whose grief he judged false, those he suspected of secret gladness or mere pretense, the tradition says he dealt with harshly, so harshly that the day is remembered with a shudder."}, {"id": "oba-ajiboyede#s6", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "I will not dress this up as a clean and noble thing, for it was not. It was the act of a man whose power had no check on it in the moment of his deepest pain, and such power in such a moment is a terrible thing. The tradition does not hide it. And that, children, is one of the reasons I honor the old griots who kept these stories, because they did not sand the kings down into smooth idols. They kept the terror alongside the glory. They let Ajiboyede be what he was, a mighty war-king and a grieving father and a man whose grief became a danger to those around him. A people that only remembers the beautiful parts of its kings learns nothing. A people that remembers the whole man learns wisdom."}, {"id": "oba-ajiboyede#s7", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Let us draw the lesson gently but truly, for you are young and the lesson is old. Power without restraint is a fire with no hearth around it. Ajiboyede’s strength was real. His victories were real. His grief was real and deserving of compassion, for who among us would not be broken by the loss of a child? But when strength and grief came together in a man who answered to no one, the result was cruelty, and the cruelty stained a reign that might otherwise have shone clean. The greatness and the fault grew from the same root, which was the iron of the man. This is why the elders say that the hardest thing a strong person must learn is not how to strike but when to stay the hand."}, {"id": "oba-ajiboyede#s8", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "And yet, children, do not let me leave you thinking Ajiboyede was merely a tyrant, for that too would be a flattening, and we have sworn at this fire never to flatten a great figure into a single word. The same iron that made him fearsome in grief made him unbreakable in war. The same refusal to be pitied that turned cruel in the assembly kept Oyo from being pitied by its enemies. A softer king in those hard rebuilding years might have let the recovery slip. Ajiboyede did not let it slip. Whatever the cost to his soul and to those who feared him, the kingdom under his hand grew stronger, not weaker, and the road back toward Oyo Ile ran on beneath his feet as it had beneath Orompoto’s. He was a hard king for a hard age, and the age, in its cruelty, perhaps required exactly the hardness that was also his sin."}, {"id": "oba-ajiboyede#s9", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "There is one more thing the tradition sets down about Ajiboyede, and it points ahead to the great turning that is now almost upon us in our telling. It was in these late Igboho years, under kings like Ajiboyede and those close around him, that the dream of returning to Oyo Ile began to move from longing into plan. The strength had been gathered. The Nupe had been answered. The cavalry had been built. The question was no longer whether Oyo could survive, but whether it would dare to go home, to walk back south into the ruined homeland and raise the capital from its ashes. That dream did not come to its fullness in Ajiboyede’s own day. But the strength that made it possible was in no small part the strength of the hard war-kings, and he was among the hardest and the strongest of them."}, {"id": "oba-ajiboyede#s10", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "So when you weigh Ajiboyede, weigh him whole. Set the victories on one side and the cruelty of the mourning-assembly on the other. Set the grief of a father who lost his son beside the terror of a king who made others suffer for his loss. Do not resolve him too neatly, for real people do not resolve neatly, and Ajiboyede was very real. He is a warning and a wonder at once."}, {"id": "oba-ajiboyede#s11", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So remember Ajiboyede, children, the hard king of the rebuilding. Remember that strength and cruelty can grow from a single root, and that the measure of a person is not only what they can do but what they refrain from doing when nothing but their own conscience stands in the way. Remember the father broken by grief and the king undone by having no one to check his grief, and be gentler than he was when your own turn to hold power comes, as it will. But remember too that he did not let Oyo fall back into weakness, that he answered the old enemy blow for blow, and that the strength which finally carried the dynasty home was in part the strength of iron kings like him. The exile is nearly over now. The homeland is calling. Tomorrow we come to Abipa, the king who at last led Oyo back to Oyo Ile, and the long wandering finds its end."}]}, {"id": "oba-abipa", "emoji": "👑", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#1a0a00,#4a2800)", "category": "itan-oba", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "10 min", "title": {"en": "Abipa: The King Who Led Oyo Home", "yo": "Abipa"}, "desc": {"en": "The Alaafin who ended the long exile and led Oyo home to rebuild Oyo Ile.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Itan Oba", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Alaafin, the Alaafin who ended the long exile and rebuilt Oyo Ile in the homeland)", "segments": [{"id": "oba-abipa#s1", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Tonight, children, the fire burns for a homecoming, and there is no story sweeter to a people who have wandered than the story of the road that finally turns toward home. Gather close. We have walked a long way together through the exile. We stood with Onigbogi as the city fell. We wandered with Ofinran who was born on the road, with Eguguoju who held the scattered together, with Orompoto the woman war-king, with hard Ajiboyede whose iron rebuilt Oyo’s strength. Through all of it, one dream burned low but never died, the dream of returning to Oyo Ile, the ancient capital lost to the Nupe, the homeland that the dynasty had not seen in generations. Tonight that dream comes true. Tonight we sit with Abipa, and Abipa is the king who led Oyo home."}, {"id": "oba-abipa#s2", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Feel the weight of that, children. For lifetimes, the crown of Oranyan had sat in borrowed towns. Kings had been born, had reigned, had died and been buried in the northern country, never once setting foot in the sacred city of their fathers. Oyo Ile had become a place of memory, spoken of by grandfathers to grandchildren, a name heavy with longing, its groves overgrown, its palace fallen, its streets silent or walked by strangers. And now, at last, a king arose with the strength gathered by all who came before him, and he said the words the ancestors had waited generations to hear. We are going home."}, {"id": "oba-abipa#s3", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "But do not imagine, children, that a homecoming is an easy thing, that a people simply pick up their loads and stroll cheerfully back down the road. No. Going home was dangerous, and it was frightening, and not everyone wanted to go. This is the true heart of Abipa’s story, and it is a heart full of human truth. There were those in Oyo who had been born in the exile, who had grown up in the northern towns, who had made their farms there and buried their parents there, and to whom the return to Oyo Ile meant leaving the only home they had ever known to march into a ruined and dangerous frontier. Not everyone shares a king’s dream. Abipa had to lead a people, many of whom were afraid, back into a land that had once devoured them."}, {"id": "oba-abipa#s4", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "And there was fear in the very omens, or so the tradition remembers. When Abipa resolved to rebuild Oyo Ile and return, the diviners were consulted, as was always the way, and there were those who spread word that the signs were bad, that the sacred hill of Ajaka near the old city foretold disaster, that the return would end in ruin. Now here is where Abipa shows the cunning that the tradition loves in a good king, for a good king is not only brave, he is shrewd. It is said that Abipa suspected these dark omens were not the true voice of the gods at all, but the invention of chiefs who did not want to leave their comfortable northern lives, chiefs who were using fear and false divination to keep the dynasty from going home."}, {"id": "oba-abipa#s5", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "So Abipa did a clever thing, and the tradition tells it with relish. He sent trusted men secretly ahead to the old city, to the very hill from which the frightening voices of ill omen were said to come. And when the next dark prophecy was proclaimed, when the voice cried out from the hill that the return would bring doom, Abipa’s men were waiting, and they seized the ones who were making the false oracle, and the trick was exposed for all to see. The bad omens had not come from the gods at all. They had come from the mouths of men who feared the journey. And once the fraud was uncovered, the last excuse for staying in exile crumbled away. The road home lay open, and the fearful had no more false prophecies to hide behind."}, {"id": "oba-abipa#s6", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "I love this story, children, and I want you to love it too, but I want you to love it for the right reason. It is not merely a clever trick. It is a lesson about fear and the lies that fear tells. When people are afraid to do a hard and necessary thing, they will often dress up their fear as wisdom. They will say the omens are bad. They will say the time is not right. They will find a hundred solemn reasons why the difficult road should not be taken, and often they will half believe their own reasons. Abipa understood that some of the voices counseling against the homecoming were not the voice of true caution but the voice of comfort dressed up as prophecy. And he had the courage and the shrewdness to test the omens rather than simply obey them. Test the prophecies of the fearful, children. Fear is a great forger of oracles."}, {"id": "oba-abipa#s7", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "And so Oyo went home. Under Abipa, the dynasty marched south out of the exile and back to Oyo Ile, and there they raised the fallen city from its ruins. Picture that homecoming. Picture the old ones who had heard of Oyo Ile all their lives finally seeing it with their own eyes, weeping at the sight of the overgrown groves where their ancestors had walked. Picture the young ones, born in exile, setting foot for the first time in the homeland that had been only a story to them. Picture the sacred sites being cleared and reconsecrated, the palace being rebuilt, the drums that had been silent for generations sounding once more in the streets of the ancient capital. This is why Abipa is remembered with such honor. He did not merely win a battle. He restored a people to their place in the world."}, {"id": "oba-abipa#s8", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "And this restoration, children, is the foundation of everything that comes after in our great telling. Everything you will hear of the glory of Oyo, of the empire that stretched to the coast, of the golden age of the mighty Alaafin who made kings tremble from the forest to the sea, all of it stands on the ground that Abipa reclaimed. Without the homecoming there is no empire. The wandering dynasty, however strong, could never have become the great power of the Yoruba world while it sat in borrowed towns. It needed its own ground, its sacred capital, its ancient seat, and Abipa gave it back that ground. He is the hinge on which the whole story turns from exile to empire."}, {"id": "oba-abipa#s9", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "Let me not flatten him, though, for we never flatten the great. Abipa was not only the shrewd exposer of false omens and the leader of the march home. He was the careful rebuilder of a shattered city, which is slow, unglamorous labor. He had to resettle a people, apportion land, reestablish the shrines and the offices and the whole delicate machinery of a kingdom in a place that had lain in ruins. He had to make the frontier safe, for the Nupe and other powers had not vanished. He had to knit together those who had wanted to return and those who had not, so that the kingdom did not split along that painful seam. A lesser king might have led the people home and then watched the fragile new settlement fall apart. Abipa made it hold. He made the homecoming permanent."}, {"id": "oba-abipa#s10", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "And in doing so he set the stage for the imperial dawn. The Oyo that Abipa rebuilt would, under the kings who came after him, rise to a height its founders could scarcely have dreamed, its cavalry ranging across the grasslands, its authority reaching to distant peoples, its Alaafin becoming one of the mightiest sovereigns in all the land of the Yoruba and beyond. But every one of those later kings ruled from the city that Abipa reclaimed. Every triumph of the golden age was won from the seat that Abipa restored. He is the quiet giant on whose shoulders the loud giants stood."}, {"id": "oba-abipa#s11", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So remember Abipa, children, the king who led Oyo home. Remember that a homecoming can be as hard and as brave as any battle, and that it takes courage not only to fight but to leave a comfortable exile and reclaim a difficult inheritance. Remember the trick of the false omens, and learn from it to test the fears that dress themselves as wisdom. Remember that the great glories to come were built upon the patient, dangerous, unglamorous work of restoration, and honor the ones who do that work even when the songs remember only those who came after. The exile is ended now, children. The long wandering that began when Onigbogi fled the Nupe finds its rest at last in the rebuilt streets of Oyo Ile. From here, the story climbs toward empire. Tomorrow, we begin the golden age, and the Alaafin of Oyo become a power that shakes the whole of the Yoruba world."}]}, {"id": "oba-obalokun", "emoji": "👑", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#1a0a00,#4a2800)", "category": "itan-oba", "rating": 9.6, "duration": "9 min", "title": {"en": "Obalokun: The King Who Opened the Road to the Sea", "yo": "Obalokun"}, "desc": {"en": "The Alaafin of the early imperial age who grew rich on trade and reached toward the coast.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Itan Oba", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Alaafin, the Alaafin of the early imperial age, remembered for wealth, trade, and the reach toward the coast)", "segments": [{"id": "oba-obalokun#s1", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Sit, children, and let the fire warm you, for tonight we cross a great threshold in our telling. Behind us lies the long night of exile and the sweet dawn of the homecoming. We have seen Oyo Ile fall and lie in ruins, and we have seen Abipa lead the dynasty home to raise the sacred city from its ashes. Now the sun climbs higher. Now Oyo, restored to its ancient seat, begins to grow into the great power it was destined to become. And at the opening of this imperial dawn stands Obalokun, and Obalokun is remembered as a king of wealth, a king of trade, a king whose reach stretched from the grasslands of the interior all the way toward the far salt water of the sea."}, {"id": "oba-obalokun#s2", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Let me be honest with you first, as I always am at this fire, so that you know the weight of what I hand you. These early imperial kings lived in the seventeenth century by the strangers’ reckoning, and the tradition holds them with more detail than the shadowy figures of the exile, but still the deep particulars are held differently by different mouths. What is remembered strongly of Obalokun is the shape of his greatness, that he presided over a time of growing prosperity, that Oyo under him reached outward toward the coast and the trade that flowed there. I will give you that truth in full color, and where the small details grow uncertain I will not invent, for a griot who fills the gaps with lies dishonors both the ancestors and the children at his feet."}, {"id": "oba-obalokun#s3", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Now understand what it meant for Oyo to reach the sea, children, for this is the engine of the whole golden age. Oyo Ile sat in the interior, in the northern savanna where the horse could run. It was a land power, a cavalry power. But wealth and greatness in those days flowed also along the roads of trade, and the richest roads led to the coast, where in these years the ships of distant strangers had begun to gather, hungry for what the interior could sell and heavy with goods the interior desired. To reach that coast, to open and hold the long roads running south from the savanna down through the forest to the lagoons and the sea, was to tap a river of wealth. And the tradition remembers Obalokun as a king under whom Oyo’s reach toward that river grew strong."}, {"id": "oba-obalokun#s4", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "The elders say his very name speaks of the sea, and whether that is the root of it or a later honor laid upon him, it fits the man the tradition remembers, a king whose horizon was not bounded by the walls of the interior but stretched to the far water. Picture the caravans in his day, children. Picture the long lines of traders and porters moving south from Oyo Ile through the forest paths, carrying the goods of the savanna, and returning laden with the goods of the coast, with salt, with the strange and precious things the sea-ships brought. Picture the wealth of that trade flowing back to the Alaafin’s court, filling the treasury, buying horses, buying loyalty, buying the sinews of empire. That is the world Obalokun’s reach helped to build."}, {"id": "oba-obalokun#s5", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "I must speak plainly to you, children, about a hard shadow that lay across this trade, for I will not tell you a golden story with the darkness scrubbed out of it. We have sworn at this fire to tell the whole truth of the great, never the flattered half. The trade that flowed to the coast in these centuries was not only salt and cloth and beads. It was also, increasingly, the terrible trade in human beings, the selling of captives to the ships that carried them across the water, never to return. This is the great wound of that age, and Oyo, like the other powers of the region, was drawn into it. The wealth that made empires was in part a wealth soaked in the sorrow of the enslaved."}, {"id": "oba-obalokun#s6", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "I tell you this not to shame the ancestors, for the world they were born into was already tangled in this trade before any one of them drew breath, and the deepest guilt lies with the distant buyers who built the whole cruel machine and grew fat upon it. But I tell you because a people that remembers only the glory of its empire and forgets the cost paid by the captured and the sold is a people that has not yet grown wise. Obalokun’s road to the sea was a road of wealth, and it was also, in part, a road of chains. Both things are true. Hold them both. Do not let anyone give you a history so shining that it has no shadow, for such a history is a child’s tale, not the truth."}, {"id": "oba-obalokun#s7", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "And yet, children, within his own world and by the measures of his own time, Obalokun was a mighty and successful king, and I will not flatten him into either a mere merchant or a mere link in a chain of sorrow. He was a builder of Oyo’s strength. Under him the restored kingdom did not merely survive its homecoming, it prospered. A newly rebuilt capital is a fragile thing, and many a restored kingdom has withered in the generation after its return, unable to turn survival into strength. Obalokun turned survival into wealth, and wealth into power. He knit the trade roads to the court. He filled the treasury that later kings would spend on conquest. He is remembered as a king of plenty, and plenty, in a world of hunger and war, is no small achievement."}, {"id": "oba-obalokun#s8", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "There is a tradition, too, that speaks of the reach of his court toward distant powers, of embassies and contacts stretching far, even, some tellings hold, toward lands across the great desert or the great water. Here the record grows uncertain, and I will not build you a tower of specifics on soft ground. But the spirit of the thing is remembered clearly, and it is this. Obalokun was an outward-looking king. He did not sit hunched over the interior, guarding a small thing. He looked to the horizon, to the roads, to the wider world and the wealth and knowledge that flowed along it. A king who looks outward grows his kingdom. A king who looks only inward slowly shrinks it. Obalokun looked outward, and Oyo grew."}, {"id": "oba-obalokun#s9", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "Let me draw the lesson gently, for you are young and the world will one day ask you which kind of person you will be. There are those who guard what they have, hunched and fearful, letting the horizon shrink until their whole world is the little patch beneath their feet. And there are those who look outward, who build roads instead of walls, who reach toward the wider world and bring its wealth and its wisdom home. Obalokun was of the second kind, and under him a rebuilt city became the seed of an empire. But remember also the shadow on his road, and learn the harder lesson too, that wealth won at the cost of others’ suffering carries a debt that history eventually calls in. The greatest kingdoms of that age were built in part on the trade in human sorrow, and that debt fell due, in the fullness of time, in ways we will see before our telling of Oyo is done."}, {"id": "oba-obalokun#s10", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "So carry Obalokun whole. The king of wealth and the far horizon. The builder who turned homecoming into prosperity. And the ruler whose golden road ran, in part, through the shadow of the chains. All of it is true. All of it is Oyo."}, {"id": "oba-obalokun#s11", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So remember Obalokun, children, the king who opened the road to the sea. Remember that greatness often comes to those who look outward and build roads toward the wider world, and that a people who reach beyond their own walls grow, while those who only guard and hoard slowly shrink. But remember too the shadow on the golden road, the sorrow of the sold, and never let anyone tell you a history so bright that it casts no shadow at all. The empire is rising now, children. The treasury is filling. The roads run to the sea. Tomorrow, the war-kings take up what the trader-kings have built, and Oyo’s cavalry rides out to make an empire that shakes the whole of the Yoruba world."}]}, {"id": "oba-ajagbo", "emoji": "👑", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#1a0a00,#4a2800)", "category": "itan-oba", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "9 min", "title": {"en": "Ajagbo: The War-King of the Long Spear", "yo": "Ajagbo"}, "desc": {"en": "The warrior Alaafin of the long spear, remembered for conquest and a long reign.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Itan Oba", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Alaafin, the great warrior Alaafin of the imperial age, remembered for conquest and a long reign)", "segments": [{"id": "oba-ajagbo#s1", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Come close, children, and let no one drowse tonight, for tonight the fire burns for a warrior, and the drums beneath this story beat the rhythm of the war-camp. Last night we sat with Obalokun, the king of wealth and the far horizon, who filled the treasury and opened the roads to the sea. Tonight the war-kings take up what the trader built, for wealth without swords is a feast laid out for robbers, and Oyo did not mean to be robbed. Tonight we sit with Ajagbo, and Ajagbo is remembered as one of the mightiest warrior-kings in all the long line of the Alaafin, a king of conquest, a king of the long spear, a king whose reign the tradition holds as long and terrible and great."}, {"id": "oba-ajagbo#s2", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Let me set the ground honestly, for that is my duty. Ajagbo reigned in the imperial age, and the tradition remembers him with the special vividness it saves for the great warriors, for our people have always loved a war-king and kept his deeds bright in the mouth. Some tellings give him a reign of astonishing length, so long that it strains belief, and here I will say to you what an honest teller must say. When the tradition remembers a king as reigning for a great span of years, it is telling you something true even if the exact count is uncertain. It is telling you that this was a reign that filled the memory, a reign so full of deeds that it seemed to stretch across generations. Whether Ajagbo truly reigned for the vast span some tellings claim, or whether the length is the tradition’s way of honoring a reign packed with greatness, the truth beneath is the same. Ajagbo loomed enormous in the memory of his people."}, {"id": "oba-ajagbo#s3", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Why did he loom so large? Because he was a maker of war, and a winner of it. Under Ajagbo, the tradition remembers, Oyo’s armies marched far and wide, and the cavalry that had been forged in the exile now became the terror of the whole region. Remember the horse, children, that thread I have followed all through our telling. The horse that destroyed Oyo at the hands of the Nupe, the horse the exiled dynasty learned to breed and ride in the northern grasslands, the horse Orompoto led into battle. Under the imperial war-kings, and Ajagbo above them, that horse became the foundation of an empire. Oyo’s mounted armies could strike far and fast, ranging out from the savanna, and few forces in the region could stand against a disciplined charge of Oyo cavalry. Ajagbo wielded that weapon, and he wielded it to build an empire."}, {"id": "oba-ajagbo#s4", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "The tradition remembers him extending Oyo’s power in many directions, pressing its authority over neighboring peoples, making the name of the Alaafin a name that made distant rulers tremble. This is the season in which Oyo becomes not merely a kingdom but an empire, a power whose reach extends over other kingdoms and peoples who must send their tribute and bend to the will of the Alaafin. And the making of an empire is the work of war-kings, of men willing to spend the years of their reign in the saddle and the war-camp, and Ajagbo was such a man. He did not build his greatness sitting in the cool of the palace. He built it on the march, in the dust, at the head of his armies."}, {"id": "oba-ajagbo#s5", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "Now I must give you a picture the tradition keeps of Ajagbo’s methods, for it shows the mind of the man and the shape of Oyo’s war-machine. It is said that Ajagbo formalized the leadership of Oyo’s armies, that in his reign the great war-titles took on their weight, the commanders who would lead the imperial forces in the field. For an empire’s armies cannot be led by the Alaafin’s person alone, marching to every distant war. The Alaafin sits at the sacred center, in the capital, hedged about with taboo and ceremony, and the empire’s wars are fought by his generals in his name. The greatest of these war-commanders would in time bear the title we will honor deeply later in our telling, the Aare Ona Kakanfo, the generalissimo of Oyo, the field-marshal of the empire. The shaping of Oyo’s military command, the machinery by which a single king could project power across a vast region, took form in the reigns of war-kings like Ajagbo."}, {"id": "oba-ajagbo#s6", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Think on the greatness of that, children, and on its danger too, for I will not flatten Ajagbo into simple triumph. To build a war-machine mighty enough to make an empire is to create a force that can also, one day, turn upon its maker. The very generals who won the empire’s wars would grow powerful, and powerful generals are a danger to kings. The seeds of Oyo’s later troubles, the terrible quarrels between the Alaafin and his over-mighty war-chiefs that would one day help bring the empire down, were planted in the very soil of its military greatness. Ajagbo built the spear that made Oyo an empire. But a spear, once made, can be turned around. We will see, before our telling ends, how the war-machine of the golden age became, in lesser hands and harder times, the instrument of the empire’s ruin. That is not Ajagbo’s fault. But it is Ajagbo’s legacy, the double-edged gift of a mighty army."}, {"id": "oba-ajagbo#s7", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Let me not leave you with only the clash of arms, for a war-king is still a king, and a king rules more than his soldiers. Ajagbo’s long reign meant stability, and stability is a gift a people rarely appreciate until it is gone. A king who reigns long and reigns strong gives his people something precious, a lifetime without the chaos of disputed succession, without the weakness that enemies exploit, without the constant turning-over of the throne that keeps a kingdom off balance. Under a long strong reign, farms are planted and harvested in peace behind the shield of the frontier wars, markets grow, the arts and the crafts and the sacred rites flourish, children are born and raised to adulthood under one steady crown. Ajagbo gave Oyo that steadiness across a great span, and much of the wealth and confidence of the golden age grew in the long shadow of his reign."}, {"id": "oba-ajagbo#s8", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "And here is a thing worth holding, children. We remember the battles because battles are loud and easy to sing. But the deepest gift of a great reign is often the quiet thing, the peace behind the frontier, the ordinary life made possible by the king’s strength. The market woman who could trade without fear, the farmer who could harvest without raiders, the child who grew up knowing only one king and one order, all of these lived in the peace that Ajagbo’s spear purchased. The war at the edges bought the peace at the center. That is the hard arithmetic of empire, and Ajagbo was a master of it."}, {"id": "oba-ajagbo#s9", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So remember Ajagbo, children, the war-king of the long spear, one of the mightiest of all the Alaafin. Remember that empires are built by those willing to spend their years in the dust and danger of the frontier, and that the peace enjoyed at the center is often purchased by the wars fought at the edge. Remember too the double edge of the spear he forged, the war-machine that made Oyo great and that would one day, in weaker hands, help to break it, for every strength a people build carries within it the shape of a possible ruin, and wisdom lies in knowing this even in the hour of triumph. The empire stands tall now, children. Its cavalry rides far. Its name makes distant kings tremble. Tomorrow, the golden age goes on, through the proud and quarrelsome kings who ruled the empire at its height."}]}, {"id": "oba-odarawu", "emoji": "👑", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#1a0a00,#4a2800)", "category": "itan-oba", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "9 min", "title": {"en": "Odarawu: The King Whose Anger Ruled Him", "yo": "Odarawu"}, "desc": {"en": "An Alaafin of the imperial age whose fierce temper cut his reign short.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Itan Oba", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Alaafin, an Alaafin of the imperial age remembered for a fierce temper and a short reign)", "segments": [{"id": "oba-odarawu#s1", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Draw near, children. Tonight the fire lights a cautionary face, the face of a king who had power in his hands and could not master the one enemy no army can defeat, the enemy that lived inside his own chest. Last night we sang of Ajagbo, the mighty war-king of the long spear, who spent a long and glorious reign building the empire. Tonight we come to Odarawu, and Odarawu’s reign was neither long nor glorious, and the reason it was neither is a lesson every one of you must carry out of this circle and keep for the whole of your lives. Odarawu was ruled by his own anger, and a man ruled by his anger, even a king, is not truly a ruler at all. He is a servant of his worst self wearing a crown."}, {"id": "oba-odarawu#s2", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Let me set the ground honestly. Odarawu belongs to the imperial age, in the generations of the golden age of Oyo, and the tradition remembers him more for the manner of his reign than for a long catalog of deeds, because his reign was cut short. I will not invent for you a false record of great wars and conquests, for that is not what the tradition hands down. What it hands down is sharper and simpler and, in its way, more useful than a list of battles. It hands down the memory of a king whose temper was his ruin, and that memory has been kept alive precisely because it teaches something that every generation needs to learn again."}, {"id": "oba-odarawu#s3", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "The tradition remembers Odarawu as a man of fierce and hasty temper, a king quick to wrath and slow to reflection. Now hear me, children, for there is a subtle thing here. In a common man, a fierce temper is a fault that harms mostly himself and those close around him. But in a king, in a man whose word can move armies and end lives, a fierce and ungoverned temper is a public danger, a fault that can shake a whole kingdom. The higher a man climbs, the more his flaws cost, not only himself but everyone beneath him. A hot temper in a farmer spoils a household. A hot temper in an emperor can spoil a nation. This is why the ancestors watched their kings so carefully, and why they built so many restraints around the Alaafin, because they understood that great power in the hands of an ungoverned man is a great danger."}, {"id": "oba-odarawu#s4", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "The tale the tradition keeps of Odarawu turns on exactly this. It is said, in the tellings that come down to us, that in the heat of his anger the king ordered a campaign or an act of vengeance driven not by the cold calculation of statecraft but by the hot flush of personal fury, and that this rashness set in motion the events that brought his reign to its unhappy end. I will not pretend to hand you every particular with certainty, for the tellings vary and I will not forge specifics to make a smoother tale. But the shape is clear and it is remembered clearly. Odarawu let his anger make his decisions, and decisions made in anger are the ruin of kings."}, {"id": "oba-odarawu#s5", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "Now, children, let me set Odarawu against the great constitution of Oyo, for his story cannot be understood without it, and understanding it will teach you something about the wisdom of our ancestors that few peoples of the world ever matched. The Alaafin of Oyo was mighty, yes, the sovereign of an empire, but he was not a tyrant free to do whatever his temper demanded. The ancestors, in their deep wisdom, had built into the very constitution of Oyo a check upon the king. There was the great council of state, the Oyo Mesi, the seven principal chiefs led by the Basorun, and this council was not a mere gathering of flatterers. It had a terrible power. If an Alaafin ruled badly, if he became a tyrant, if his conduct brought ruin or dishonor upon the kingdom, the Oyo Mesi could, in the last resort, reject him. They would send him the message that the gods and the people had turned against him, and by the custom of the land, a rejected Alaafin was required to take his own life, so that a bad king could be removed without tearing the sacred fabric of the kingship apart."}, {"id": "oba-odarawu#s6", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Think on the wisdom of that, children, and think on its terror. Our ancestors had solved a problem that has broken many nations, the problem of what to do about a bad king. They did not simply pray that each king would be good. They built a machinery to remove the ones who were not, a check upon the throne itself. The Alaafin was mighty, but he ruled under a law greater than himself, and if he forgot that, the Oyo Mesi could remind him in the most final of ways. And a king ruled by his anger, a king like Odarawu, was exactly the kind of king who might collide with that terrible check. For a man who cannot govern his temper cannot long govern within the restraints that a wise constitution places upon him."}, {"id": "oba-odarawu#s7", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "I will not flatten Odarawu into a mere warning, for even he was a whole man and not a single lesson. A fierce temper often grows in soil that also holds courage, and passion, and a fierce love of honor. The very heat that made Odarawu dangerous may also have made him bold, quick, unwilling to bear insult to himself or his people. The fault and the strength, as so often, grew from one root. There is a kind of king who is too cold, too calculating, who will swallow any insult and bear any dishonor if it serves his advantage, and such a king has his own diseases. Odarawu was not that king. His fire was real, and in another man, better governed, that same fire might have made a great and passionate ruler. The tragedy is not that he had fire. The tragedy is that the fire had him."}, {"id": "oba-odarawu#s8", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "And that, children, is the heart of the whole matter, the thing I most want you to carry away. It is not wrong to feel anger. Anger is a fire the gods placed in every human breast, and rightly used it can warm a just cause and forge a righteous courage. The question is never whether you will feel anger. The question is whether you will rule your anger, or let your anger rule you. Odarawu let it rule him, and it burned down his reign. The mightiest king in the region, the sovereign of an empire, was brought low not by any foreign army but by the enemy inside his own chest, the ungoverned fire of his own temper."}, {"id": "oba-odarawu#s9", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So remember Odarawu, children, the king whose anger ruled him. Remember that the higher you climb the more your flaws will cost, not only yourself but all who depend upon you, and that a fault a common man may carry lightly becomes a public danger in one who holds power. Remember the great wisdom of our ancestors, who did not merely hope their kings would be good but built a check to remove the ones who were not. And remember, above all, the enemy inside the chest, the fire of anger, and learn young what Odarawu never learned, that the first and hardest kingdom any of us must rule is the kingdom of our own heart, and that whoever cannot govern that inner kingdom will never truly be fit to govern anything else. Tomorrow, the golden age continues, through the proud kings and the deepening quarrel between the Alaafin and the great chiefs who could make and unmake him."}]}, {"id": "oba-kanran", "emoji": "👑", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#1a0a00,#4a2800)", "category": "itan-oba", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "8 min", "title": {"en": "Kanran: The King and the Over-Mighty Chief", "yo": "Kanran"}, "desc": {"en": "An Alaafin whose reign deepened the struggle between the throne and the over-mighty chiefs.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Itan Oba", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Alaafin, an Alaafin of the imperial age, whose reign deepened the struggle between the throne and the great chiefs)", "segments": [{"id": "oba-kanran#s1", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Sit close, children, for tonight the fire lights not a battlefield against a foreign foe but a quieter and deadlier struggle, the struggle inside the very house of Oyo, between the Alaafin on his sacred throne and the great chiefs who stood around it. This is a struggle that ran, like an underground river, all through the golden age, and that would one day burst to the surface and help to drown the empire. Tonight we sit with Kanran, and Kanran’s reign the tradition remembers as one in which that old struggle deepened, the long tug of war between the king and the council that could make and unmake him."}, {"id": "oba-kanran#s2", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Let me be honest about the ground, as always. The imperial-age kings between the great war-lords and the famous reformers are held by the tradition in less detail than the giants on either side of them, and Kanran is among these. I will not invent a false chronicle of his wars and his words. What the tradition gives, and what the whole shape of Oyo’s history makes clear, is the deepening of the constitutional struggle in these generations, and it is that struggle, more than any single deed, that gives Kanran’s reign its meaning in the great telling. So tonight I will teach you the deep machinery of Oyo’s power, the balance and the rivalry at the empire’s heart, for without understanding it you can understand neither the empire’s greatness nor its fall."}, {"id": "oba-kanran#s3", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "You remember, children, what I told you of the Oyo Mesi, the great council of seven principal chiefs, led by the Basorun. And you remember the terrible check they held, that they could reject an Alaafin who ruled badly, and that a rejected Alaafin was required by custom to take his own life. Now, this was wise, for it guarded against tyranny. But wisdom, children, is a knife that can cut in two directions. For consider. If the chiefs could unmake a king, then the chiefs held a fearful power over the king, and power, once held, is rarely held gently. What began as a check against bad kings could become, in the wrong hands, a weapon of ambitious chiefs against good ones. The Basorun who could rightly remove a tyrant could also, if he were ambitious and unscrupulous, threaten to remove a king who merely stood in his way."}, {"id": "oba-kanran#s4", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "This is the deep tension of the Oyo constitution, and I want you to hold it carefully, for it is one of the great political lessons of all human history. Every check upon power is also a power in itself, and every power can be abused. The Oyo Mesi were meant to guard the kingdom against tyrannical kings. But over the generations, as the empire grew rich and the stakes grew high, the Oyo Mesi, and above all the Basorun, grew into a power that could rival and even overshadow the throne. The check upon the king became, at times, a chain upon the king. And the reigns of the middle golden age, the reigns of kings like Kanran, are the years in which this tension wound tighter and tighter, until it would one day snap."}, {"id": "oba-kanran#s5", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "Picture the court of Oyo Ile in these years, children, and feel the strain of it. At the sacred center sits the Alaafin, mighty in ceremony, hedged about with taboo, called the companion of the gods, the one whose face is like the sun that none may gaze upon directly. His power is immense. And yet, around him, stand the great chiefs, each with his own title, his own following, his own wealth and warriors and pride. The Basorun above all, the head of the Oyo Mesi, a man who leads certain of the greatest rites, who speaks with a voice the king cannot simply ignore. Between the Alaafin and the Basorun runs a current of rivalry as old as the constitution itself, for the constitution made them partners and rivals at once, each needing the other, each wary of the other, each watching for the moment when the other might grow too strong."}, {"id": "oba-kanran#s6", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "A wise Alaafin in these years had to be more than a warrior. He had to be a master of this dangerous inner game, balancing the chiefs against one another, rewarding loyalty, checking ambition, keeping any single chief from growing mighty enough to threaten the throne, all while ruling an empire and holding the frontiers. It was a game of the highest skill, played for the highest stakes, for the loser might lose not merely an argument but his very life. And the tradition remembers the imperial age as a time when this game grew ever more intense, when strong chiefs pressed against strong kings, and the balance that held the empire together grew ever more delicate. Kanran reigned within this tightening vise, and whether he was a master of the game or a victim of it, his reign belongs to the story of its deepening."}, {"id": "oba-kanran#s7", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Let me draw the lesson broadly, children, for it reaches far beyond Oyo. Every human community that has ever tried to hold power in check has faced the puzzle at the heart of Kanran’s reign. Give a king absolute power, and you risk tyranny. Give others the power to check the king, and you risk that those others will grow into tyrants themselves, or that king and checkers will tear each other and the whole community apart in their struggle. There is no perfect answer to this puzzle. The wisest peoples do not solve it once and for all but manage it, generation by generation, through the skill and restraint of those who hold the various powers. When that skill and restraint are present, the balance holds and the community flourishes. When they fail, when kings grow tyrannical or chiefs grow overweening or both at once, the balance breaks and the community suffers."}, {"id": "oba-kanran#s8", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "Oyo’s ancestors had built a balance of remarkable sophistication, a constitution that divided power in ways that many a greater and prouder nation never managed. That such a balance existed at all is a glory of our people, a proof of a political wisdom that the arrogant strangers who later came to these shores were too blind to see. But a balance is not a machine that runs itself. It requires wise hands on every side. And in the reigns of the middle golden age, the hands grew less wise, or the stakes grew too high, and the balance that had held the empire together began, slowly, to fail. Kanran’s reign is a chapter in that slow failing, and that is why we remember it, not for a single glorious deed, but as a link in the chain that runs from the empire’s greatness toward its fall."}, {"id": "oba-kanran#s9", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So remember Kanran, children, and remember above all the deep struggle his reign embodies, the tug of war between the throne and the great chiefs at the heart of Oyo. Remember that every check upon power is itself a power that can be abused, and that the hardest task of any community is not to grant power nor to check it but to keep both in a living balance that neither tyranny nor faction can overthrow. Honor the ancestors who built so wise a constitution, and learn from the generations who let it fray. The empire still stands tall in Kanran’s day, its cavalry still ranging far, its wealth still flowing. But the crack at its heart is widening. Tomorrow, the golden age goes on, and we shall see the empire climb to its very peak even as the old struggle winds tighter beneath its feet."}]}, {"id": "oba-jayin", "emoji": "👑", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#1a0a00,#4a2800)", "category": "itan-oba", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "9 min", "title": {"en": "Jayin: The King and the Grief Within the Palace", "yo": "Jayin"}, "desc": {"en": "An Alaafin remembered for the sorrow that struck within the royal house.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Itan Oba", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Alaafin, an Alaafin of the imperial age whose reign the tradition remembers for sorrow within the royal house)", "segments": [{"id": "oba-jayin#s1", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Come, children, and sit near, for tonight the fire lights not the frontier nor the council chamber but the inner rooms of the palace itself, where the private sorrows of kings are hidden from the crowd but felt all the more keenly for being hidden. Last night we sat with Kanran and the great struggle between the throne and the chiefs. Tonight we turn from the public struggle to a private grief, for we come to Jayin, and Jayin’s reign the tradition remembers for a sorrow that struck within the royal house itself, a reminder that even the mightiest king in the region, the sovereign of an empire whose name made distant rulers tremble, could not command peace and happiness within his own walls."}, {"id": "oba-jayin#s2", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Let me be honest, as I am always honest. Jayin belongs to the imperial age, and like several of the kings between the great war-lords and the famous reformers, he is held by the tradition more in outline than in a full chronicle of deeds. The tellings that come down carry the memory of trouble within the royal household in his time, sorrow touching the king’s own family and the question of succession. I will not forge for you a false catalog of specifics, for the tellings vary and I keep faith with the truth even when the truth is spare. But I will tell you what the shape of it teaches, for it teaches something the loud chronicles of war and conquest often forget, that a king is also a man, a father, a husband, and that the palace which houses an empire’s power also houses an ordinary human heart that can be broken."}, {"id": "oba-jayin#s3", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Think, children, on the strange loneliness of a great king. All the world sees his power. All the world sees the ceremony, the wealth, the armies, the tribute flowing in from distant lands. What the world does not see is the man inside the ceremony, the human being hedged about with so much taboo and so much awe that he can scarcely have an ordinary friend, an ordinary conversation, an ordinary moment of simple human warmth. The Alaafin of Oyo was so sacred that in the fullness of the tradition his very face was hidden, his eating unseen, his person wrapped in mystery. Such sacredness is a kind of glory. It is also a kind of prison. And within that prison, the ordinary sorrows of human life, the quarrels of a household, the griefs of fatherhood, the treacheries that can grow even among one’s own kin, pressed upon the king with a weight made heavier by his loneliness."}, {"id": "oba-jayin#s4", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "The royal household of a great king was not a simple family. It was a vast establishment, with many wives, many children, many servants and officials, and around the question of who would succeed to the throne there gathered ambition, rivalry, and intrigue. For the sons of an Alaafin, and those who attached themselves to this prince or that, the succession was the highest of all prizes, and where there is so high a prize, there is always the temptation to treachery. A king in such a household could not always trust even those of his own blood. The very children who should have been his comfort might become, through the poison of ambition, his rivals or his dangers. This is the shadowed world in which Jayin’s remembered sorrows unfolded, the world where the private and the political could not be untangled, where a father’s grief and a king’s succession crisis were often the same wound."}, {"id": "oba-jayin#s5", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "I will speak to the deep thing here, children, for it is the heart of tonight’s telling. We look at the powerful and we imagine they have escaped the ordinary pains of life. We imagine that wealth and power are a wall behind which a person is safe from grief. It is not so. It was never so. The powerful grieve as we grieve. They lose children as we lose children. They are betrayed by those they love as we are betrayed. And in some ways their pain is sharper, for they must often bear it alone, behind the mask of majesty, unable to weep openly as a common man may weep, forced to keep the face of power even while the heart breaks behind it. Jayin, whatever the exact shape of his sorrows, stands in our telling for this truth, that the crown does not shield the heart, and that the loneliest griefs are often those suffered in the highest places."}, {"id": "oba-jayin#s6", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "And there is a further teaching, harder still. The sorrows within the royal house were not merely private tragedies. They had consequences for the whole empire. A disputed succession, a treachery among princes, a king weakened by grief or by the loss of an heir, these were not just family matters, for the strength of the empire depended on the strength and stability of the throne. When the royal house was troubled, the whole kingdom trembled, for enemies watched for weakness, and ambitious chiefs, remember the Oyo Mesi and their fearful power, watched for the moment when a grieving or embattled king might be pushed aside. The private griefs of the palace and the public fate of the empire were bound together with a single cord. To wound the one was to endanger the other."}, {"id": "oba-jayin#s7", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Let me not flatten Jayin into a figure of mere sorrow, for even a king remembered for grief was more than his grief. To reign at all in the imperial age, to hold the sacred center of a mighty empire, to keep the frontiers and manage the chiefs and perform the endless rites on which the people believed the very fertility of the land depended, this was no small thing, and a king who did it even through personal sorrow showed a strength the loud war-chronicles do not always capture. There is a courage in simply continuing, in keeping the crown steady while the heart is breaking, in doing the daily work of a kingdom when one’s own house is in ruins. If the tradition remembers Jayin’s sorrows, it also remembers that he reigned, that the empire endured through his reign, that the sacred order was kept. That too is a kind of quiet heroism, the heroism of endurance under private pain."}, {"id": "oba-jayin#s8", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "So hold Jayin whole, children. The mighty king and the grieving man. The sovereign of an empire and the father who could not command peace within his own walls. The public power and the private wound. Do not imagine, when you look upon the powerful of any age, that their power has made them strangers to the pains you know. The tears of a king are as wet as the tears of a farmer. The loss of a child breaks an emperor’s heart as surely as a beggar’s. This is a hard truth, but it is also, in its way, a kind and joining truth, for it reminds us that beneath all the differences of rank and wealth, we are one kind, one people, sharing the same handful of great joys and great sorrows from the highest throne to the humblest mat."}, {"id": "oba-jayin#s9", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So remember Jayin, children, the mighty king who knew grief within his own house. Remember that the crown does not shield the heart, that the powerful grieve as we grieve, and that the loneliest sorrows are often those borne in the highest places behind the mask of majesty. Remember that in a kingdom the private and the public are bound with a single cord, and that the troubles of the royal house could shake the whole empire. And when you look upon those who hold power, do not envy them so simply, nor imagine their lives are free of the pains you know, for the tears of a king are as wet as your own. The empire still stands in Jayin’s day, but the strain within and the struggle at its heart wind ever tighter. Tomorrow, the golden age goes on toward its brightest peak and its longest shadows."}]}, {"id": "oba-ayibi", "emoji": "👑", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#1a0a00,#4a2800)", "category": "itan-oba", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "9 min", "title": {"en": "Ayibi: The King Who Inherited a Hard Season", "yo": "Ayibi"}, "desc": {"en": "An Alaafin who inherited and reigned through a hard season of strain within the kingdom.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Itan Oba", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Alaafin, an Alaafin of the imperial age who reigned through a time of strain within the kingdom)", "segments": [{"id": "oba-ayibi#s1", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Draw near, children, and let the fire steady you, for tonight the story is of a king who came to the throne in a difficult hour, and who must be honored not for easy triumphs but for holding a hard line in a hard season. Last night we sat with Jayin and the sorrows within the palace. Tonight we come to Ayibi, and Ayibi belongs to that stretch of the golden age when the empire, though still mighty and still feared, was carrying within it the strains that would one day undo it, the tightening struggle between the throne and the chiefs, the pressures of holding a vast dominion together, the endless labor of keeping a great thing great."}, {"id": "oba-ayibi#s2", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Let me speak the truth of the ground first. Ayibi is among those imperial-age kings whom the tradition holds in outline rather than in full chronicle. The great giants of the age, the mighty war-kings and the famous reformers, are remembered in rich detail, but between them reigned kings whose deeds the tradition kept more sparely. I will not dress Ayibi in borrowed glories nor invent for him a false record of conquests he may never have made. That is not the honest way. What I can give you truly is the shape of his season and the meaning of a reign spent holding a great and straining empire steady, and in that meaning there is wisdom worth more than a dozen invented battles."}, {"id": "oba-ayibi#s3", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "For here is a thing our songs too often forget, children. We love to sing of the kings who conquered, who added new lands, who made the empire larger and grander. We sing of the builders and the expanders. But an empire, once built, must also be held, and the holding is harder and less glorious than the building. A king who inherits a vast dominion inherits also a vast burden, a thousand frontiers to guard, a hundred subject peoples who must be kept loyal, a court full of ambitious chiefs who must be balanced, a treasury that must be filled and a sacred order that must be maintained. To keep all of this from flying apart, year after year, is a labor beyond the imagining of those who see only the crown and the ceremony. And it wins no songs, for holding steady makes no dramatic tale. It is only when the holding fails that anyone notices how hard it was."}, {"id": "oba-ayibi#s4", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Ayibi’s reign belongs to this unsung labor of holding. He did not, so far as the tradition remembers, ride out to add great new provinces to the empire. But the empire that had been built did not collapse in his day, and in a season of tightening strains, that is no small thing. Think of a man carrying a great load up a long hill. The one who first lifted the load is praised for his strength. But the one who carries it through the hardest, steepest stretch, who does not set it down, who does not let it roll back and crush those behind him, that one deserves honor too, though the songs may not remember his name as brightly. Ayibi carried the load through a steep stretch. He did not let it roll back. The empire passed through his hands still whole."}, {"id": "oba-ayibi#s5", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "Let me speak to you of the deeper strain that ran through these middle imperial reigns, for it is the key to understanding not only Ayibi but the whole long descent that would follow. Remember the Oyo Mesi, the council of chiefs, and the Basorun who led them, and the fearful power they held to reject a king. In the early golden age, strong war-kings had held that power in check by their own might and glory, for it is hard for chiefs to move against a king who has led them to victory and filled their hands with the spoils of conquest. But as the age wore on, and the reigns of the great conquerors passed, the balance began to shift. The chiefs, grown rich and confident on the wealth of empire, pressed harder against the throne. The Basorun’s power waxed. And a king in these later reigns had to be ever more careful, ever more skilled in the dangerous inner game, simply to keep his throne, let alone to rule with a free hand."}, {"id": "oba-ayibi#s6", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "This is why I say Ayibi inherited a hard season. It was not merely that the frontiers needed guarding, though they did. It was that the very heart of the empire, the balance between king and chiefs on which all else rested, was growing unstable. Each generation, the chiefs grew a little stronger, the throne a little more hemmed in. It was like a house whose foundation is slowly settling, so that year by year the doors fit a little worse and the walls strain a little more, though the house still stands and to a careless eye looks sound. Ayibi lived in such a house, an empire still grand to look upon but settling at its foundation, and his labor was to keep living in it, to keep it standing, to pass it on to the next king still whole. That he did so is his quiet achievement."}, {"id": "oba-ayibi#s7", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "And let me draw the lesson, children, for it is one you will need all your lives, in whatever you build or inherit. It is a fine thing to build. But most of life is not building, it is maintaining, holding, keeping steady the good things that others built or that we built in our stronger years. A marriage is built in a season of romance, but it is held through long years of ordinary faithfulness. A farm is cleared in a burst of labor, but it is kept through endless seasons of weeding and watching. A kingdom is conquered by war-kings, but it is held by the patient, unglamorous labor of a hundred ordinary days. The world praises the founders and the conquerors. But the world is actually held up, day by day, by the maintainers, the holders, the ones who keep the load from rolling back down the hill. Do not despise that labor when it falls to you. It is the labor that most of life is made of, and it is the labor on which everything else depends."}, {"id": "oba-ayibi#s8", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "I will not flatten Ayibi even into the noble maintainer, for he was a whole man whose full heart the tradition does not fully preserve, and I will not pretend to a knowledge I do not have. But this much the shape of history teaches, that he held a straining empire through a hard season and passed it on unbroken, and in an age when the cracks were widening, the kings who kept the whole thing from breaking in their own day did a service that the louder chronicles undervalue. Honor him for the load he carried, not for the songs he did not win."}, {"id": "oba-ayibi#s9", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So remember Ayibi, children, the king who inherited a hard season and held the line. Remember that building is glorious but holding is harder, and that most of the good in this world is not built in a burst of glory but maintained through long, patient, unsung labor. Remember the settling foundation of the empire, the slow strain between the throne and the chiefs, and the quiet worth of the king who kept the great house standing through his own day even as the cracks widened. And when the labor of holding steady some good thing falls to you, as it will, do not despise it, for it is the labor that most lives are made of and on which all founding finally depends. The empire endures, still mighty, still strained. Tomorrow, the golden age climbs at last toward its very peak, and we shall meet Ojigi, under whom Oyo’s power reaches its farthest and most terrible height."}]}, {"id": "oba-ojigi", "emoji": "👑", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#1a0a00,#4a2800)", "category": "itan-oba", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "9 min", "title": {"en": "Ojigi: The King at the Empire’s Farthest Height", "yo": "Ojigi"}, "desc": {"en": "The Alaafin under whom Oyo's power reached its widest and most terrible extent.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Itan Oba", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Alaafin, the great Alaafin under whom Oyo’s power reached its widest and most terrible extent)", "segments": [{"id": "oba-ojigi#s1", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Sit up now, all of you, and let the fire blaze high, for tonight we stand at the very summit of the mountain we have been climbing since the founding. Tonight the empire of Oyo reaches its farthest, its widest, its most terrible height, and the king who reigns at that summit is Ojigi. Remember the name, children. Ojigi. For under him, the cavalry of Oyo rode farther than it had ever ridden, the tribute flowed in from more distant lands than ever before, and the name of the Alaafin became a word of dread from the grasslands of the interior all the way to the shore of the great water and beyond. If you would know how high Oyo climbed, look to the reign of Ojigi, for he stood at the peak, and after the peak, as with all mountains, the path leads down."}, {"id": "oba-ojigi#s2", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Let me be honest, as ever, about how the tradition holds him. Ojigi is remembered strongly, for the great conquerors are always remembered strongly, and his reign is associated with the farthest reach of Oyo’s imperial power. The tradition ties to his age the campaigns that carried Oyo’s authority to its greatest extent, including the pressing of Oyo’s might toward the kingdom of Dahomey to the west, a campaign of the first importance in the history of the whole region. Some of the exact particulars of dates and sequences are held differently by different tellers and different records, and I will not forge a false precision. But the great fact stands firm in the memory of the people. Under Ojigi and the war-machine of his age, Oyo reached the summit of its power."}, {"id": "oba-ojigi#s3", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Let me tell you of the war with Dahomey, children, for it is one of the great deeds of the imperial age and it shows you the reach of Oyo’s power. To the west of the Yoruba country lay the kingdom of Dahomey, a rising and formidable power, a kingdom of fierce warriors and iron discipline. Dahomey had been expanding, swallowing its neighbors, pressing toward the coast and its rich trade. And Oyo, the great empire of the interior, would not suffer a rival power to rise unchecked upon its flank. So the cavalry of Oyo rode west, out of the savanna, against Dahomey."}, {"id": "oba-ojigi#s4", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "And here is a thing of great importance, children, a thing that shows the peculiar genius and the peculiar limit of Oyo’s power. Oyo’s strength was its cavalry, its horsemen, and the horse ruled the open grassland. But as one moved south and west toward the coast, the land changed. The open savanna gave way to forest, and in the deep forest and the wet coastal country, the horse could not thrive, for the tsetse fly and the damp and the close trees were death to horses. So Oyo’s cavalry was mighty in the grassland and weaker in the forest, and its empire took the shape of the land itself, spreading wide across the open country where horses could run, and reaching less securely into the forest zones. When Oyo struck at Dahomey, it struck with the cavalry advantage of the open country against a forest kingdom, and in the campaigns of Ojigi’s age, Oyo brought Dahomey low, made it a tributary, forced the proud western kingdom to bend to the Alaafin and to send its tribute year by year to Oyo Ile."}, {"id": "oba-ojigi#s5", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "Think on the greatness of that, children. From the sacred city of Oyo Ile in the interior, the will of the Alaafin now reached west to humble Dahomey, a mighty kingdom in its own right, and stretched across a vast dominion of subject peoples and tributary states. This was no longer a kingdom. This was an empire in the full and terrible sense, a power that made other kings into servants, that drew wealth and tribute from a huge sweep of the region, that could put fear into the heart of any ruler within the reach of its cavalry. The Alaafin of Oyo in Ojigi’s day was among the mightiest sovereigns in all of this part of the world, and the strangers from across the sea, the traders on the coast, learned to reckon with the great power of the interior whose horsemen could shake the whole trade of the region."}, {"id": "oba-ojigi#s6", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "But I will not give you only the glory, children, for we have sworn at this fire never to tell a shining tale with the shadow scrubbed away. The empire at its height was mighty, but its might rested in part upon the same dark trade I spoke of when we sat with Obalokun. The wars of empire produced captives, and captives flowed, too many of them, into the terrible commerce of the coast, sold to the ships that carried them across the water into a bondage from which they never returned. The greatness of Oyo and the sorrow of the enslaved were bound together in that age with a knot that history has never fully untied. To tell you of Ojigi’s conquests and hide this from you would be to lie by omission, and I will not do it. The empire was glorious. The empire was also, in part, a machine that fed the trade in human sorrow. Both are true. A wise child learns to hold both without letting either erase the other."}, {"id": "oba-ojigi#s7", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Now let me tell you the deep thing about a summit, children, for it is the truest lesson of Ojigi’s reign. When you stand at the very top of a mountain, every direction is downhill. This is not a curse laid upon Oyo alone. It is the law of all great things under the sun. Every empire that has ever risen has also fallen. Every power that has climbed to a summit has, sooner or later, begun the descent. And often, the very things that carried a power to its height become, at the height, the seeds of its fall. The mighty war-machine that built the empire became a war-machine of over-mighty generals who would one day defy the throne. The vast dominion that brought such wealth became a vast burden of frontiers too many to hold and subjects too resentful to keep. The proud independence of the great chiefs, useful in conquest, became at the summit a factionalism that would tear the center apart. At the very moment of Oyo’s greatest glory, under Ojigi, the seeds of its ruin were already planted deep, and growing."}, {"id": "oba-ojigi#s8", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "I do not tell you this to darken the triumph, children, but to make you wise. Do not imagine, when you or your people reach some summit, that the summit is a resting place where you may stay forever. The summit is the most dangerous place of all, for from the summit the only new direction is down, and the pride and ease of the height are exactly the things that begin the descent. The wise, at the summit, do not celebrate carelessly. They look to the paths downward and ask how the fall may be slowed, how the height may be held a little longer, how the seeds of ruin, always present at every height, may be checked before they grow. Oyo, at its summit under Ojigi, did not fully do this. It stood tall, and it was mighty, and it did not sufficiently tend to the cracks widening at its own foundation. And so the summit of Ojigi became, in the fullness of time, the beginning of the long descent."}, {"id": "oba-ojigi#s9", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So remember Ojigi, children, the king at the empire’s farthest height, under whom Oyo’s cavalry humbled Dahomey and the will of the Alaafin reached across a vast dominion. Remember the peculiar shape of Oyo’s power, mighty in the open grassland where the horse could run, and learn from it that every strength is bound to the ground it grows in and cannot reach everywhere alike. Remember the shadow on the glory, the trade in human sorrow that ran beneath the empire’s wealth, and never let anyone give you a history so bright it casts no shadow. And remember, above all, the law of the summit, that from the highest place every direction is down, and that the seeds of ruin are always planted at the very height of glory, so that the wise celebrate their summits with a watchful and humble heart. Oyo stands now at its peak. After the peak, the descent begins. Tomorrow, the golden age enters its late afternoon, and the long shadows begin to lengthen across the empire."}]}, {"id": "oba-gberu-amuniwaiye", "emoji": "👑", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#1a0a00,#4a2800)", "category": "itan-oba", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "8 min", "title": {"en": "Gberu and Amuniwaiye: The Kings of the Late Afternoon", "yo": "Gberu and Amuniwaiye: The Kings of the Late Afternoon"}, "desc": {"en": "Two Alaafin of the empire's long afternoon, as the struggle with the Basorun sharpened.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Itan Oba", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Alaafin, two Alaafin of the late golden age, whose reigns fell in the empire’s long afternoon as the old struggle sharpened)", "segments": [{"id": "oba-gberu-amuniwaiye#s1", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Come, children, and sit, for tonight the fire lights the late afternoon of the empire, the hours after the noon-high glory of Ojigi, when the sun still shone bright and the shadows had begun, quietly, to lengthen. Tonight I will hold two kings together in a single telling, Gberu and Amuniwaiye, for they belong to the same season and teach the same hard lesson, the lesson of the deepening struggle between the throne and the great chiefs that would, before long, break the empire apart. I hold them together not to slight them but because the tradition remembers them as men of the same troubled hour, and their two stories are truly one story, the story of the throne beginning to lose its grip on the mighty machine that the war-kings had built."}, {"id": "oba-gberu-amuniwaiye#s2", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Let me be honest about the ground, as I always am. Gberu and Amuniwaiye are held by the tradition in the shadow of a single towering figure who overshadows them both, and whom you must know to understand them. That figure is not a king at all. He is a chief. He is the Basorun Gaha, the most powerful and most terrible of all the Basoruns of Oyo, the over-mighty chief who would come to dominate the throne itself and make and unmake kings at his pleasure. The reigns of the late golden age cannot be told without him, for he is the storm that gathers over all of them. So tonight, as I tell you of Gberu and Amuniwaiye, I am really telling you of the rise of the shadow that would swallow the throne, the rise of Gaha."}, {"id": "oba-gberu-amuniwaiye#s3", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "You remember the Oyo Mesi, the council of chiefs, and the Basorun who led them, and the fearful power they held to reject an Alaafin. I told you, when we sat with Kanran, that this power was a wise check that could curdle into a weapon, that the guard against tyrannical kings could itself become a tyranny of chiefs. Now, in the late golden age, that curdling comes to its bitter fullness in the person of one man. Gaha became Basorun, and Gaha was no ordinary chief. He was cunning, ambitious, ruthless, and utterly determined to make the Basorun, not the Alaafin, the true master of Oyo. And through a run of reigns, he bent the throne to his will, so that kings sat at his sufferance and fell at his word."}, {"id": "oba-gberu-amuniwaiye#s4", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Into this gathering storm came Gberu, and Amuniwaiye after him. Picture what it was to be Alaafin in such an hour. You wear the sacred crown, you sit at the holy center, all the ancient majesty of Oranyan’s line rests upon your shoulders, and yet the true power in the land is slipping into the hands of a chief who does not wear the crown, who rules from behind the throne through fear and faction and the terrible check of the Oyo Mesi. To be king in such an hour was to hold a hollow majesty, to wear the ornaments of power while the substance drained away into the hands of another. This was the bitter cup that Gberu and Amuniwaiye drank, the cup of kingship in the age of the over-mighty Basorun."}, {"id": "oba-gberu-amuniwaiye#s5", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "The tradition remembers these reigns as troubled and, for the kings, dangerous. When a Basorun grows strong enough to threaten the throne, the life of an Alaafin becomes a perilous thing, for the very check that was meant to remove only tyrants can be turned against any king who resists the Basorun’s will. A king who tried to rule freely, who tried to break the grip of the over-mighty chief, risked being declared rejected, risked receiving the fatal message that custom made a sentence of death. And so these kings of the late afternoon lived in the shadow of that threat, some bending to the Basorun to survive, some resisting and paying the price. The details of each reign the tradition holds unevenly, and I will not forge false specifics. But the shape is terribly clear. The throne was losing its mastery, and the kings who sat upon it in these years were, more and more, the prisoners of the chief who should have been their servant."}, {"id": "oba-gberu-amuniwaiye#s6", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Let me draw the deep lesson, children, for it is one of the gravest in all our telling. A constitution, however wise, is only as good as the character of those who work it. The ancestors built a brilliant balance, a division of power meant to guard against tyranny from any single quarter. But no machinery, however clever, can save a people if the men who operate it lose their virtue. Gaha took the wise check of the Oyo Mesi and turned it into a tyranny worse than any single bad king could have managed, for it wore the mask of the constitution itself. The very institution meant to prevent tyranny became the instrument of it. And the kings of the late afternoon, Gberu and Amuniwaiye among them, were caught in the machinery, unable to break free."}, {"id": "oba-gberu-amuniwaiye#s7", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "I will not flatten these kings into mere victims, though victims in large part they were. To hold the sacred center at all in such an hour, to keep the rites, to keep the dignity of the crown even as its power was stolen away, was itself a labor of endurance. And the very fact that the line continued, that the crown passed on, that Oyo did not simply dissolve into the naked rule of Gaha, meant that something of the throne’s sacred authority survived even its deepest humiliation. The idea of the Alaafin, the sacred kingship of Oranyan’s line, proved stronger than any single Basorun’s ambition, and though Gaha bent the throne, he could not finally abolish it. That endurance, that stubborn survival of the sacred kingship through its darkest hour, is the quiet dignity of these overshadowed reigns."}, {"id": "oba-gberu-amuniwaiye#s8", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "And there is hope planted even here, in the darkest soil, for you must know where this story is going. The tyranny of Gaha, terrible as it was, would not last forever. A king was coming, a king of patience and cunning and iron will, who would one day break the power of the over-mighty Basorun and avenge the humiliated throne. His name we will speak with honor in a later telling. His name is Abiodun. But before Abiodun could rise, the night of Gaha had to be endured, and Gberu and Amuniwaiye are among the kings who endured the gathering of that night. They held the crown through the darkening. They kept the sacred line alive until the dawn could come."}, {"id": "oba-gberu-amuniwaiye#s9", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So remember Gberu and Amuniwaiye, children, the kings of the late afternoon, who wore the crown in the age when the throne was losing its grip to the over-mighty Basorun Gaha. Remember that no constitution, however wise, can save a people whose leaders lose their virtue, and that the very institutions built to guard against tyranny can, in wicked hands, become the instruments of it. Remember the hollow majesty of a king whose power has been stolen, and honor the stubborn dignity of those who kept the sacred crown alive through its darkest hour. The shadow of Gaha lies heavy now across the empire. But a dawn is coming. Tomorrow, we sit with Onisile, another king of this hard age, and draw nearer to the terrible climax of the struggle and the great king who would at last break the tyrant’s grip."}]}, {"id": "oba-onisile", "emoji": "👑", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#1a0a00,#4a2800)", "category": "itan-oba", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "9 min", "title": {"en": "Onisile: The King Who Defied the Tyrant", "yo": "Onisile"}, "desc": {"en": "The Alaafin who dared to resist the tyrant Basorun Gaha.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Itan Oba", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Alaafin, the Alaafin who dared to resist the over-mighty Basorun Gaha)", "segments": [{"id": "oba-onisile#s1", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Gather close, children, and let the fire burn bright, for tonight the story has courage in it, the hard, doomed, magnificent courage of a man who looked upon a tyrant and refused to bow, even knowing what it might cost him. Last night we spoke of the gathering shadow of the Basorun Gaha, the over-mighty chief who bent the throne of Oyo to his will and made kings tremble. Tonight we sit with a king who would not tremble, or who trembled and stood anyway, which is the truer and braver thing. Tonight we sit with Onisile, and Onisile is remembered as a king of pride and power who dared to defy the tyrant Gaha, and whose defiance the tradition keeps alive as a flame of dignity in a dark age."}, {"id": "oba-onisile#s2", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Let me be honest, as always, about how the tradition holds him. Onisile reigned in the terrible age of Gaha’s ascendancy, and he is remembered as a strong and proud king, a king who would not be a mere puppet, a king who pushed back against the Basorun’s stranglehold on the throne. The exact turns of his reign the tradition holds in the way it holds such things, vividly in spirit, unevenly in detail, and I will not forge false specifics to fill the gaps. But the heart of his memory is clear and it is stirring. In an age when kings had become the prisoners of an over-mighty chief, Onisile was a king who fought back, and though the age was against him, his defiance shines."}, {"id": "oba-onisile#s3", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Understand the courage of it, children, by understanding the danger. Gaha, the Basorun, held the terrible power of the Oyo Mesi, the power to declare a king rejected, and a rejected Alaafin, by the custom of the land, was required to take his own life. This was the sword that Gaha held over every king’s head. To defy Gaha was not merely to risk losing an argument or a struggle for influence. It was to risk receiving the fatal message, the calabash, the sign that the chiefs had turned against you and that custom now demanded your death. Most kings, faced with that sword, bent. They made their peace with the tyrant. They accepted the hollow majesty and let Gaha rule from behind the throne, because the alternative was death. And who can blame them? The love of life is planted deep in every human heart."}, {"id": "oba-onisile#s4", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "But Onisile, the tradition remembers, was not a bending kind of man. He was proud, he was strong, and he held a vision of the kingship as it was meant to be, the Alaafin as the true sovereign of Oyo, not the servant of an over-reaching chief. And so he pushed back. He asserted the dignity of the throne. He would not be Gaha’s creature. Picture the tension of that court, children, the king and the Basorun locked in a silent, deadly struggle, each measuring the other, each knowing that only one of them could finally hold the mastery of Oyo, and that the loser might well lose his life. Into that vise of fear, Onisile brought defiance. That is a rare and costly kind of courage, the courage to resist a power that holds your very life in its hand."}, {"id": "oba-onisile#s5", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "There is a tale the tradition keeps of Onisile’s power and his pride, a tale wrapped, as our great stories often are, in wonder. It is said that Onisile was a devotee of Sango, the thunder-orisa, the very king who became the god of lightning whom we honored in an earlier telling. And it is said that Onisile’s connection to that fierce power was so strong that the thunder itself answered to him, that lightning was at his command, that the storm was his ally. Now, whether you take this marvel as literal fact or as the way our people clothe a mighty and fearsome king in the garments of the sacred, the meaning beneath is clear. Onisile was a king of tremendous force of personality, a king whose power was felt as something almost more than human, a king who reached for the oldest and most terrible energies of the kingship to stand against the tyrant who would diminish it. He wrapped himself in the thunder of Sango and defied the shadow of Gaha."}, {"id": "oba-onisile#s6", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "And here, children, the tradition turns toward sorrow, for I will not give you a false triumph. The age of Gaha was a hard age, and the Basorun’s power was, for a season, greater than any single king’s. Onisile’s defiance, magnificent as it was, unfolded in a time when the tyrant’s grip had not yet been broken, and the tradition remembers his reign ending in the turbulence of that struggle. I will not forge the particulars of his end, for the tellings hold them variously, but the shape is that of a proud king who dared much in a dark hour, whose defiance did not, in his own day, overthrow the tyrant. That victory was reserved for another, for the patient and cunning Abiodun who would come after. Onisile fought the tyrant and did not live to see the tyrant fall. And yet his defiance was not wasted."}, {"id": "oba-onisile#s7", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "For hear the deep thing, children, the truest lesson of Onisile’s story. There is a courage that wins, and there is a courage that does not win but that matters anyway, that lights a fire others will carry to victory long after the one who kindled it is gone. Onisile did not break Gaha. But his defiance kept alive the idea that the throne could be defied for, the idea that the Alaafin was meant to be sovereign and not slave, the idea that the tyranny of Gaha was a wrong to be resisted and not simply a fact to be endured. When Abiodun finally rose to break the tyrant, he rose in a kingdom where the memory of proud kings like Onisile had kept the flame of the true kingship alive. The one who strikes the final blow always stands on the shoulders of those who struck the earlier blows that failed. Onisile is one of those shoulders."}, {"id": "oba-onisile#s8", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "Do not measure a life only by whether it won, children. Some of the noblest lives are the lives that did not win, that stood against a darkness too strong to overcome in their day, and that by their standing kept alive the hope and the honor that let others win later. The world remembers the final victor. But the final victor is made possible by a long line of the defeated brave, the ones who resisted when resistance seemed hopeless, who refused to bow when bowing would have been safe, who paid the price of defiance so that the idea of freedom would still be alive when the hour of victory finally came. Onisile stood in that noble line. He defied the tyrant and did not win, and his defiance mattered all the same."}, {"id": "oba-onisile#s9", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So remember Onisile, children, the king who defied the tyrant, who wrapped himself in the thunder of Sango and would not bow to the over-mighty Basorun Gaha. Remember that there is a courage that wins and a courage that does not win but lights a flame for others to carry, and that some of the noblest lives are those that stood against a darkness too strong for their own day. Remember that the final victor always stands on the shoulders of the defeated brave, and honor those who resist when resistance seems hopeless, for they keep alive the hope that makes later victory possible. The tyranny of Gaha still grips Oyo. But its days are numbered now, for the king who will break it is coming. Tomorrow, at last, we sit with the great Abiodun, the patient king who broke the tyrant and gave Oyo its last age of glory before the fall."}]}, {"id": "oba-abiodun", "emoji": "👑", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#1a0a00,#4a2800)", "category": "itan-oba", "rating": 9.6, "duration": "11 min", "title": {"en": "Abiodun: The Patient King Who Broke the Tyrant", "yo": "Abiodun"}, "desc": {"en": "The patient Alaafin who destroyed Basorun Gaha and gave Oyo its last age of glory.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Itan Oba", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Alaafin, the great Alaafin who destroyed the Basorun Gaha and gave Oyo its last age of glory)", "segments": [{"id": "oba-abiodun#s1", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Sit up now, every one of you, and let no one so much as blink, for tonight the fire burns for one of the greatest of all the Alaafin, a king whose story has patience in it, and cunning, and a long-suffering endurance that finally ripened into a terrible and righteous victory. Tonight we sit with Abiodun, and Abiodun is the king who broke the tyrant Gaha, who avenged the humiliated throne, who lifted Oyo out of the long night of the over-mighty Basorun and gave it one last bright age of glory before the darkness closed in. Remember this name above almost all the others, children. Abiodun. For his story is a lesson in the deepest kind of strength, the strength that knows how to wait."}, {"id": "oba-abiodun#s2", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Let me tell you the shape of the darkness he inherited, so that you feel the weight of what he overcame. You remember the Basorun Gaha, the terrible chief who had made himself the true master of Oyo, who ruled from behind the throne through the fearful power of the Oyo Mesi, who had bent king after king to his will and destroyed those who resisted. For many years, across the reigns of several Alaafin, Gaha’s shadow lay over the whole empire. He grew rich, he grew powerful, he placed his own kinsmen in offices across the land, he made himself feared as no chief had ever been feared. The kings came and went, some bending, some resisting and perishing, and through it all Gaha endured, the spider at the center of the web, the real power behind the sacred but hollow crown. This was the darkness. This was the tyrant. And into this darkness came Abiodun."}, {"id": "oba-abiodun#s3", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Now hear the wisdom of Abiodun, children, for it is not the wisdom the young usually admire, and yet it is the wisdom that wins. Abiodun did not, when he came to the throne, immediately hurl himself against Gaha in open defiance as proud Onisile had done. He had seen where open defiance led. He had seen brave kings break themselves against the tyrant’s power and perish. Abiodun was patient. He was cunning. He understood that Gaha, for all his power, could not be beaten by a frontal blow while his web was still whole, while his kinsmen still held the offices and his terror still gripped the land. So Abiodun waited. He bided his time. He wore, for a season, the mask of a king who had accepted the Basorun’s dominance, all the while watching, planning, gathering, waiting for the one moment when the tyrant might be struck down."}, {"id": "oba-abiodun#s4", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "This is a hard teaching, children, and I want you to sit with it, for the young heart rebels against it. Every fiber of a brave young spirit cries out to strike at injustice now, at once, openly, whatever the cost. And there is a nobility in that, the nobility of Onisile, and it is not to be despised. But there is a deeper wisdom that knows a tyrant cannot always be beaten by a brave blow struck too soon, that sometimes the righteous cause requires patience, requires the long game, requires the discipline to wait through the dark years until the moment is truly ripe. Abiodun had that discipline. He swallowed his pride and hid his purpose and waited, not out of cowardice but out of a strategist’s understanding that the tyrant must be struck once, and struck to kill, and that a blow struck too soon would only strengthen him."}, {"id": "oba-abiodun#s5", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "And when the moment came, children, Abiodun struck with the fury of all those patient years behind it. He had gathered his allies in secret. He had found the ones across the empire who hated Gaha and hungered to be free of him, and there were many, for a tyrant makes enemies with every act of cruelty. And when all was ready, Abiodun moved. The great reckoning came, and it was terrible. The forces loyal to the true kingship rose against Gaha and his kinsmen, and the web of the tyrant was torn apart in a single convulsion of long-pent fury. Gaha himself, the mighty Basorun who had made kings tremble for a generation, was brought low at last, seized, and put to a hard death, for the vengeance of all the years he had ruled through terror fell upon him at once. The tradition remembers the fall of Gaha as a great and violent purging, the breaking of a tyranny that had gripped Oyo for far too long."}, {"id": "oba-abiodun#s6", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "There is a saying that came out of those days, children, a saying the people made to remember the fall of the tyrant, that speaks of how Gaha had done evil for many years but that Abiodun’s day of reckoning repaid it all in a single accounting. I will not repeat the old words exactly, but the meaning is this, that a tyrant may prosper for a long time, may seem untouchable across many years, but that the day of reckoning, when it finally comes, settles the whole account at once. Gaha had ruled through terror for a generation. His fall came in a single terrible season, and it repaid the whole long debt of his cruelty. The patient king had waited, and the waiting had ripened into a victory as complete as it was long delayed."}, {"id": "oba-abiodun#s7", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "But I will not let you think, children, that Abiodun was only the destroyer of a tyrant, for that would be to flatten a great king into a single deed, and we have sworn never to do that. After the breaking of Gaha, Abiodun gave Oyo something it had not known in a long time, peace and prosperity under a throne that was once again truly sovereign. His reign is remembered as a last golden afternoon, a time of wealth, of flourishing trade, of a kingdom at rest after the long fever of the tyranny. Abiodun turned from war and faction toward commerce and order. He looked to the trade roads, to the wealth of the coast, to the prosperity of his people. Under him, the tradition remembers, Oyo enjoyed a bright and peaceful season, the calm after the storm, the last full flowering of the empire before the frost."}, {"id": "oba-abiodun#s8", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "And yet, children, here is the sorrow woven into the glory, the thing that makes Abiodun’s reign not only a triumph but a warning. In turning from war toward trade and peace, Abiodun let something essential grow weak. The military strength of Oyo, the mighty war-machine of cavalry that had built and held the empire, was not tended as it had been by the war-kings of old. The soldiers, the generals, the whole apparatus of imperial war, grew neglected in the long peace. And an empire that lets its strength decay in a season of peace is an empire storing up ruin for its children. The prosperity of Abiodun’s reign was real, but it was, in part, the prosperity of a man spending down the strength his fathers had built, enjoying the fruit without tending the tree. When Abiodun died, he left a kingdom rich but militarily softened, and into that softness the coming storms would pour. The last golden afternoon was also, though few could see it, the beginning of the end."}, {"id": "oba-abiodun#s9", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "Let me draw the twin lessons, children, for Abiodun teaches two great things and you must hold them both. The first is the wisdom of patience, the strength that knows how to wait, that does not hurl itself against a tyrant too soon but bides its time until the blow can be struck to kill. That wisdom broke Gaha and freed Oyo, and it is a wisdom the impatient young must learn, that some victories belong only to those who can endure the long dark before the dawn. But the second lesson is the shadow on the first. Even a great and wise king can plant the seeds of ruin in his very hour of triumph, by enjoying a peace so much that he lets the strength that peace depends upon decay. Abiodun broke the tyrant and won the peace, and in the peace he let the sword grow rusty, and the rusty sword would fail his successors in their hour of need. Greatness and the seeds of ruin, once again, growing from the same soil."}, {"id": "oba-abiodun#s10", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "So hold Abiodun whole, children, as the tradition holds him. The patient king who waited through the dark years. The righteous avenger who broke the tyrant Gaha and settled the long account of his cruelty. The bringer of a last golden afternoon of peace and wealth. And the king in whose bright peace the empire’s sword quietly rusted, so that his triumph and the coming fall were bound, as ever, with a single cord. He is one of the greatest of the Alaafin, and his reign is the last full glory of Oyo, and the twilight begins the moment his sun goes down."}, {"id": "oba-abiodun#s11", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So remember Abiodun, children, the patient king who broke the tyrant. Remember the strength that knows how to wait, that does not strike too soon but bides its time until the blow can be struck to kill, for some victories belong only to the patient. Remember that the day of reckoning, however long delayed, finally settles the whole account of a tyrant’s cruelty. But remember too the shadow on his glory, the sword left to rust in the long peace, and learn that even in our hours of triumph we must tend the strength that our peace depends upon, or we plant ruin for those who come after. Oyo’s last golden afternoon fades now, children. The sun of Abiodun sets. And into the twilight comes the king in whose reign the whole mighty empire will finally break. Tomorrow, we sit with Awole, and the fall of Oyo begins."}]}, {"id": "oba-awole", "emoji": "👑", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#1a0a00,#4a2800)", "category": "itan-oba", "rating": 9.6, "duration": "11 min", "title": {"en": "Awole: The King and the Curse at the Fall of Oyo", "yo": "Awole"}, "desc": {"en": "The ill-fated Alaafin in whose reign the empire broke, remembered for his dying curse.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Itan Oba", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Alaafin, the ill-fated Alaafin in whose reign the empire broke, remembered for the dying curse he laid upon the land)", "segments": [{"id": "oba-awole#s1", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Come close, children, closer than usual, and let the fire burn low and solemn tonight, for the story I must tell is a heavy one, the heaviest in all our long telling. Tonight the great empire of Oyo, which we have watched rise from a lonely rider out of Ife, which we have followed through exile and homecoming and golden conquest, tonight that empire breaks. And the king who sits upon the throne as it breaks, the king who becomes the very face of the fall, is Awole. His is a name spoken with sorrow, and with a shudder, for the tradition remembers not only his fall but the terrible curse he is said to have spoken as he died, a curse whose shadow the people believed fell across the whole land for generations. Listen well, for there is deep and painful wisdom here."}, {"id": "oba-awole#s2", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Let me set the ground honestly, as I always do. Awole reigned in the closing years of the eighteenth century, after the death of the great Abiodun, and the tradition remembers him as an unlucky and, in some tellings, an unwise king, in whose reign the long-gathering weaknesses of the empire finally broke into open ruin. Remember what I told you of Abiodun, that his golden peace had let the sword of Oyo grow rusty, that the military strength of the empire had decayed in the long ease. Awole inherited that rusty sword. He inherited an empire rich in appearance but hollowing at the core, its great chiefs over-mighty, its war-generals ambitious, its subject peoples restless, its cavalry decayed. He inherited, in truth, a catastrophe already in motion, and whatever his own faults, he was in large part a man crushed by a weight his predecessors had piled up."}, {"id": "oba-awole#s3", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Now let me tell you of the great and fatal quarrel at the heart of the fall, for you cannot understand Awole without it. Remember the war-machine of Oyo, and remember how I warned you, when we sat with Ajagbo, that the mighty generals who won the empire’s wars could one day turn upon the throne that commanded them. That day now came. The supreme war-commander of Oyo, the generalissimo, bore the title we will honor deeply in the telling still to come, the Aare Ona Kakanfo. And in Awole’s day, that office was held by a mighty and dangerous man, a warlord of the frontier town of Ilorin, a man named Afonja. And between Awole the king and Afonja the war-chief there grew a quarrel, a deadly quarrel of pride and power and mutual distrust, and that quarrel was one of the great hammer-blows that shattered the empire."}, {"id": "oba-awole#s4", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "I will not tell you the whole of Afonja’s story tonight, children, for Afonja deserves his own telling, and he will have it when we come to the chronicle of the Aare Ona Kakanfo, for he is one of the towering and tragic figures of that line. Tonight I tell you only his shadow as it falls across Awole, that the king and his over-mighty general came to hate and fear each other, that each sought the other’s ruin, and that their quarrel tore open the empire at the very moment it could least afford to bleed. When the king cannot trust his greatest general, and the general will not obey his king, the state is already dying, for an empire is held together by the bond between the throne and the sword, and when that bond breaks, all breaks. In Awole’s reign, that bond broke."}, {"id": "oba-awole#s5", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "The chiefs turned against Awole. The Oyo Mesi, remember their terrible power to reject a king, turned against him. Whether through his own faults, or the machinations of his enemies, or simply because a falling empire seeks someone to blame, Awole was rejected. The message came to him, the sign that the chiefs and, they claimed, the gods had turned against him, the demand by ancient custom that he take his own life so that the kingdom might be rid of a king judged unfit. This was the fate that had hung over so many Alaafin, the fatal check of the constitution, and now it fell upon Awole. He was ordered to die."}, {"id": "oba-awole#s6", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "And here, children, comes the terrible moment that the tradition has never forgotten, the moment that makes Awole’s name ring down the centuries. It is said that before he died, Awole rose and faced those who had condemned him, and he spoke a curse. He took an earthen dish, some tellings say, and dashed it to the ground so that it shattered into countless pieces, and he declared that as the dish was broken and could not be gathered, so should the land be broken, so should the people be scattered and enslaved and unable to come together again. He cursed the kingdom that had cast him out. He prophesied, or called down, a scattering, a breaking, a carrying-away of the people to distant lands. And then he died."}, {"id": "oba-awole#s7", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Now hear me carefully, children, for I must speak wisely about this curse, and you must listen with a wise heart. Did the words of a dying king truly break an empire and scatter a people? I will not tell you to believe in the magic of a curse as a simple thing. But I will tell you what the tradition understood, and it is deeper than magic. In the terrible generations that followed Awole’s death, everything the curse foretold came to pass. The empire did break. The people were scattered. And, most bitterly of all, vast numbers of the Yoruba were captured in the wars of the collapse and sold into slavery, carried across the sea to distant lands, to Brazil, to Cuba, to the islands, to the far Americas, torn from their homeland exactly as the shattered dish could never be made whole. The curse of Awole became, in the memory of the people, the name they gave to the whole catastrophe of the fall, the shattering and the scattering and the great carrying-away."}, {"id": "oba-awole#s8", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Do you see the deep thing, children? Whether or not a dying man’s words held any power in themselves, they gave the people a way to remember and to name a real and terrible history. The wars that followed Awole’s fall, the collapse of the empire, the flood of captives into the slave trade, the scattering of the Yoruba across the whole world, all of this really happened, and it was as bitter as any curse. And the people, looking back on that unspeakable sorrow, remembered it through the image of the broken dish and the dying king’s dreadful words. The curse of Awole is history wearing the mask of prophecy. It is the way a people gave a name and a shape to the greatest catastrophe of their past."}, {"id": "oba-awole#s9", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "Let me not flatten Awole even in his tragedy, for he was a whole man and not merely a curse. He was, in large part, a victim, a king handed a collapsing empire and blamed for a fall that generations of his predecessors had prepared. The rusty sword was not his doing. The over-mighty chiefs were not his creation. The ambitious generals, the restless provinces, the decayed cavalry, all of this he inherited. He may have made mistakes, and the tradition remembers him unkindly in places, but no single king, however wise, could easily have saved the empire from the weight of ruin that fell upon it in his day. There is a terrible unfairness in becoming the face of a fall you did not cause, and Awole carries that unfairness. When you judge him, remember the weight he was crushed beneath, and be slower to blame him than the chiefs who cast him out were."}, {"id": "oba-awole#s10", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "And carry away this hard wisdom, children, the deepest lesson of the fall. Great things do not usually collapse all at once, from a single blow, in a single reign. They rot slowly, from within, across many years, while everything still looks grand from the outside. The seeds of Oyo’s fall were planted at its very summit, in the pride of the golden age, in the over-mighty chiefs, in the ambitious generals, in the sword left to rust in Abiodun’s peace. By the time the collapse became visible, in Awole’s day, it was already too late, the rot had gone too deep. This is how empires die, children, and nations, and families, and any great thing built by human hands. Not usually in a sudden catastrophe, but in a long, hidden rotting that only shows itself at the end, when the beautiful shell finally cracks and everyone cries out in surprise at a ruin that was decades in the making."}, {"id": "oba-awole#s11", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So remember Awole, children, the ill-fated king at the fall of Oyo, and remember the shattered dish and the dying curse that became the name of a whole catastrophe. Remember that the curse foretold a real and bitter history, the breaking of the empire and the scattering of the people across the world in the terrible trade in human souls, and that a people sometimes remembers its deepest sorrows through such images of prophecy. Be slow to blame the one who becomes the face of a fall he did not cause, and remember that great things rot slowly from within long before they visibly break. The empire is broken now, children. The dish is shattered. The long night of the wars and the scattering begins. Tomorrow, we shall follow the fall into its bitter depths, and then, in time, watch a broken people begin, with unbreakable courage, to gather the pieces and build again."}]}, {"id": "oba-atiba", "emoji": "👑", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#1a0a00,#4a2800)", "category": "itan-oba", "rating": 9.6, "duration": "9 min", "title": {"en": "Atiba: The King Who Built a New Oyo from the Ruins", "yo": "Atiba"}, "desc": {"en": "The Alaafin who founded a new capital at present-day Oyo after the old empire fell.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Itan Oba", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Alaafin, the Alaafin who founded the new capital at present-day Oyo after the fall of the old empire)", "segments": [{"id": "oba-atiba#s1", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Sit close, children, and take heart, for though we have passed through the darkness of the fall, tonight the story turns, as our people always turn, toward building again. We have wept with Awole at the shattering of the old empire. We have seen the great Oyo of the interior broken, its capital doomed, its cavalry decayed, its people caught in the terrible wars and the scattering that Awole’s curse foretold. But a people who can build can also rebuild, and out of the ruins of the old empire there arose a king who gathered the survivors and raised a new Oyo from the ashes. His name is Atiba, and Atiba is the founder of the Oyo that stands to this day, the New Oyo, the capital that carries the ancient name into our own age."}, {"id": "oba-atiba#s2", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Let me set the ground truly. Atiba reigned in the middle of the nineteenth century, in the terrible aftermath of the empire’s collapse, when the old Oyo Ile in the northern savanna had become untenable, threatened and finally abandoned in the chaos of the wars, above all the wars with the forces spilling out of the north, the jihad and the rise of Ilorin and the pressure of the Fulani horsemen who now ranged where Oyo’s own cavalry had once ruled. The old capital, the sacred Oyo Ile that Abipa had rebuilt so long ago, was lost, this time for good. The heartland was overrun. And the remnant of the Oyo people moved south, into the safer forest country, seeking a place to begin again. It was there, at a town called Ago Oja, that Atiba raised the new capital and named it Oyo, so that the ancient kingdom, though its old body had died, might live on in a new one."}, {"id": "oba-atiba#s3", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Think on the greatness of that act, children, and on its poignancy. Atiba did not have the power to restore the old empire. Those days were gone, and no king could call them back. The vast dominion, the tribute of distant lands, the cavalry that had humbled Dahomey, all of that was finished. What Atiba could do, and what he did, was to save the thing itself, the kingship, the sacred crown of Oranyan’s line, the identity of the Oyo people, by giving it a new home. He carried the ancient name south and planted it in new soil. He built a new palace, established the new city, and gathered around him the scattered chiefs and titles and rites of the old order, so that the Alaafin might reign again, even if over a smaller and humbler kingdom than the mighty empire of old."}, {"id": "oba-atiba#s4", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "This is a profound kind of wisdom, children, the wisdom that knows what can be saved when everything cannot. There is a foolish pride that says, if I cannot have the whole of what I have lost, I will have nothing, that would rather let the ancient name die in the ruins of the old capital than see it live on in a smaller new one. Atiba did not have that foolish pride. He understood that it is better to save the seed than to die clutching the dead tree. He could not save the empire. He saved the kingship. He could not restore Oyo Ile. He founded a new Oyo. And because he did, the sacred line of Oranyan did not perish in the wars of the fall but lived on, and lives on still, into the age in which you yourselves are sitting by this very fire."}, {"id": "oba-atiba#s5", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "But I will not flatten Atiba into a simple savior, for his story carries a deep and fateful complexity, and we have sworn to tell the whole truth of the great. In founding the new Oyo, Atiba had to reckon with the new powers that the fall had raised up, the mighty war-chiefs who now truly held the strength of the Yoruba country. Chief among these was the great Ibadan, the warrior city that had risen in the chaos to become the real military power of the region, and the war-lord Kurunmi of Ijaye, another towering figure of the age whom we will honor in his own telling. Atiba’s new Oyo was, in truth, a kingdom that reigned more than it ruled, that held the sacred prestige of the ancient crown but depended on the real might of Ibadan and the other war-cities for its defense. The Alaafin was honored as the sacred sovereign, the heir of the ancient glory, but the swords that guarded the new kingdom were largely not his own."}, {"id": "oba-atiba#s6", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "And here, children, Atiba made a fateful arrangement, one that shows both his cunning and the tragic bind he was caught in. To manage the balance of these mighty war-powers, he is remembered as having distributed the great war-titles among them, seeking to bind Ibadan and Ijaye and the others to the throne by honoring their leaders with the ancient offices. He tried, in effect, to make the new realities serve the old crown. But such arrangements are fragile, for you cannot truly command men whose swords are stronger than your own, and the balance Atiba built among the war-cities would not hold after him. In the reign of his son, the rivalry between Ibadan and Ijaye would explode into a terrible war, and the fragile peace Atiba had woven would tear apart. He built what he could, but he built on the shifting ground of a shattered world, and no builder, however wise, can make a perfectly stable house on ground that will not stop moving."}, {"id": "oba-atiba#s7", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Let me draw the lesson, children, for it is one of the most hopeful and the most realistic in all our telling, and you must hold both halves of it. The hopeful half is this. No fall is ever the end for a people with the courage to rebuild. The old Oyo died, but Atiba built a new one, and the ancient name lives to this day. Whatever you lose, however complete the ruin seems, if you have the wisdom to save the seed and the courage to plant it in new soil, life can begin again. This is one of the deepest truths of our people, who have been scattered and broken more times than can be counted, and who have risen and rebuilt every single time. We are a people who know how to gather the pieces."}, {"id": "oba-atiba#s8", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "But hold the realistic half too, for it will keep you wise. The new thing is rarely the old thing restored. Atiba’s new Oyo was not the mighty empire reborn. It was something humbler, something that reigned in prestige while depending on others for real power, something built on shifting ground that would shift again. When you rebuild after a fall, do not deceive yourself that you are simply restoring what was lost. You are making something new, in a new world, with new powers to reckon with, and it will have its own strengths and its own fragilities. Atiba understood this. He did not try to pretend the empire back into existence. He built the real thing that could be built in the real world he lived in, and that clear-eyed realism, married to his courage, is why his new Oyo survived when a proud fantasy of restoration would have perished."}, {"id": "oba-atiba#s9", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So remember Atiba, children, the king who built a new Oyo from the ruins, who carried the ancient name south out of the lost heartland and planted it in new soil, so that the sacred line of Oranyan lives on to this day. Remember the wisdom that knows what can be saved when everything cannot, the wisdom that saves the seed rather than dying clutching the dead tree. Remember that no fall is the end for a people with the courage to rebuild, but remember too that the new thing is never simply the old thing restored, and that the wise rebuilder sees clearly the new world he must build in. The new Oyo stands now, humbler than the old but alive, the ancient crown reigning in a changed and dangerous age. Tomorrow, we follow the new kingdom into the great wars of the war-cities, and sit with Adelu, in whose reign brother turned against brother in the terrible strife of Ibadan and Ijaye."}]}, {"id": "oba-adelu", "emoji": "👑", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#1a0a00,#4a2800)", "category": "itan-oba", "rating": 9.6, "duration": "10 min", "title": {"en": "Adelu: The King in the Age of the War-Cities", "yo": "Adelu"}, "desc": {"en": "The Alaafin of New Oyo during the great wars between the Yoruba war-cities.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Itan Oba", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Alaafin, the Alaafin of New Oyo in whose reign the great wars between the Yoruba war-cities raged)", "segments": [{"id": "oba-adelu#s1", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Come, children, and sit, for tonight the fire lights an age of terrible strife, an age when the Yoruba country, already broken by the fall of the old empire, tore at itself in war after war, brother against brother, city against city. We have seen Atiba build the new Oyo from the ruins and try, with all his cunning, to bind the mighty war-cities to the ancient crown. Tonight we sit with his son and successor, Adelu, and in Adelu’s reign the fragile balance his father built came crashing down, and the greatest of the internal wars, the terrible war between Ibadan and Ijaye, set the whole country ablaze. This is a hard chapter, children, but an important one, for it is the age that shaped the Yoruba world that the strangers would soon come to rule, and it is the age that sent so many of our people, in chains, across the sea."}, {"id": "oba-adelu#s2", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Let me set the ground honestly. Adelu reigned in the middle of the nineteenth century, succeeding his father Atiba in the new capital. His very succession, the tradition remembers, marked a break with an ancient custom, for by the old law, when an Alaafin died, his eldest son, the Aremo who had shared in his rule, was expected to die with him, so that a new king could begin fresh without an over-powerful heir. But when Atiba died, this ancient and terrible custom was set aside, and Adelu, the Aremo, was allowed to live and to succeed his father upon the throne. Here you see, children, how even the deepest customs change under the pressure of hard times. The old law had made sense in the old world. In the broken new world, the Oyo could not afford to throw away a capable heir, and so the custom was let go, and Adelu lived and reigned."}, {"id": "oba-adelu#s3", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Now let me tell you of the great war that defines his age, the war between Ibadan and Ijaye, for it is one of the most important and most tragic events of the whole century. You remember that after the fall of the old empire, the real military power of the Yoruba country had passed to the great war-cities that rose in the chaos, above all to Ibadan, the mighty warrior republic, and to Ijaye, the stronghold of the great war-lord Kurunmi, whose own towering and tragic story we will tell when we come to the chronicle of the Aare Ona Kakanfo. Atiba had tried to balance these two giants, honoring both, binding both to the crown. But two such powers, so close, so proud, so mighty, could not long share the same country in peace. And in Adelu’s reign, the balance broke, and Ibadan and Ijaye went to war."}, {"id": "oba-adelu#s4", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "The Ijaye War was a catastrophe, children, a long and grinding and bloody struggle that drew in city after city across the Yoruba country. Kurunmi of Ijaye, the mighty generalissimo, made his stand against the rising power of Ibadan, and the war raged, and in the end Ijaye was destroyed, utterly, its great war-lord broken and dead, its people scattered. And the destruction did not stop there, for the Ijaye War flowed into the wider and even longer wars that followed, the great Ibadan wars, the sixteen-year war, a whole generation of conflict that kept the Yoruba country bleeding almost to the very end of the century. This was the world of Adelu’s reign, a world of near-constant war among the very peoples who should have been kin, a world in which the old unity that the empire had once provided was gone, and nothing had yet arisen to replace it but the endless rivalry of the war-cities."}, {"id": "oba-adelu#s5", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "And here, children, I must speak the bitterest truth of this age, and I will not soften it, for you are old enough now to hear it and wise enough to need it. These wars, these endless struggles among the Yoruba war-cities, produced captives. War always produces captives, and in that terrible century the captives of the Yoruba wars flowed, in their multitudes, into the machinery of the slave trade. Our own people, taken in our own wars, by our own kin and their allies, were sold to the traders of the coast and carried across the sea into bondage, to Brazil, to Cuba, to Sierra Leone as liberated captives, scattered across the whole Atlantic world. The scattering that Awole’s curse foretold reached its terrible fullness in these wars of the war-cities. This is the great wound of the age, that the Yoruba, in their disunity, fed their own children into the trade in human souls."}, {"id": "oba-adelu#s6", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "I do not tell you this to shame our ancestors beyond what is just, for the deepest guilt lies always with those who built and drove the vast machine of the trade, the distant buyers who grew rich on human misery and who armed and inflamed the wars of the coast to feed their hunger for captives. But I tell you because a people that will not look honestly at the hardest parts of its own past can never learn from them. The disunity of the Yoruba in this age was a catastrophe, and it cost us beyond all reckoning, in lives, in freedom, in the countless thousands scattered across the sea. And out of that very catastrophe, children, out of that scattering, came the great Yoruba diaspora, the millions across the Americas who carry our gods and our language and our blood to this day, a people torn from home who kept their soul alive in exile. The sorrow and the survival are bound together, as ever."}, {"id": "oba-adelu#s7", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Let me not flatten Adelu into a mere spectator of catastrophe, for he was a king who reigned through this storm and kept the sacred crown alive within it. It was not in his power to stop the wars of the war-cities, for the real military might was not his, but Ibadan’s and the others’. What he could do, and did, was to keep the ancient kingship going, to hold the prestige and the sacred authority of the Alaafin as a still point amid the chaos, so that even in the age of the war-lords there remained one throne that all the Yoruba, however divided, still recognized as the heir of the ancient glory. That continuity mattered. When, at the very end of the century, the wars finally exhausted themselves and the strangers came with their treaties and their power, it was the surviving prestige of the Alaafin’s crown, kept alive through kings like Adelu, that gave the Yoruba a center around which a sense of shared identity could still gather. He held the still point in the storm. That was his service."}, {"id": "oba-adelu#s8", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "And draw the hard lesson, children, the lesson of the war-cities. A people divided against itself is a people that feeds its own children to its enemies. The Yoruba in this age were mighty in war, each city a fortress of brave and skilled warriors, and all that might was turned inward, brother against brother, city against city, while the real profit of all that bloodshed flowed to the slavers of the coast and the powers beyond the sea. There is no enemy so dangerous to a people as its own disunity. When you are grown, children, and the quarrels of your own day tempt you to turn against your brothers, remember the Ijaye War, remember the sixteen-year war, remember the countless thousands sold across the sea because the Yoruba could not hold together. Disunity is the curse that opens the door to every other curse. Guard against it as you would guard against fire in the dry season."}, {"id": "oba-adelu#s9", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So remember Adelu, children, the king in the age of the war-cities, who reigned through the terrible wars of Ibadan and Ijaye and held the sacred crown as a still point in the storm. Remember the bitterest truth of that age, that a people divided against itself feeds its own children to its enemies, and that the disunity of the Yoruba in the century of wars scattered our people across the whole world in chains. But remember too that out of that scattering came the great diaspora that keeps our soul alive across the seas, for sorrow and survival are forever bound together in the story of our people. The wars grind on now, children, and the strangers gather at the edges of our world, watching, waiting. Tomorrow, we come to Adeyemi the First, and the age when the power of the distant empire finally closes its hand around the ancient kingdom of Oyo."}]}, {"id": "oba-adeyemi-i", "emoji": "👑", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#1a0a00,#4a2800)", "category": "itan-oba", "rating": 9.6, "duration": "9 min", "title": {"en": "Adeyemi the First: The King When the Strangers’ Power Closed in", "yo": "Adeyemi Alowolodu"}, "desc": {"en": "The Alaafin in whose reign British power reached the ancient kingdom.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Itan Oba", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Alaafin, Alaafin Adeyemi Alowolodu, in whose reign British power reached the ancient kingdom)", "segments": [{"id": "oba-adeyemi-i#s1", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Sit near, children, for tonight the fire lights the coming of a great and fateful change, the hour when the power of a distant empire, an empire from far across the sea, closed its hand around the ancient kingdom of Oyo and the whole of the Yoruba country. We have followed Oyo through its founding, its greatness, its fall, and its rebuilding in the new capital. We have seen the long century of the wars of the war-cities. Now, at the close of that century, comes a new kind of conqueror, not a rival Yoruba city, not a Nupe or Fulani horseman, but the British, the strangers from beyond the sea, whose power would soon rule not only Oyo but the whole vast land that they would come to call Nigeria. And the Alaafin who reigned as that power closed in was Adeyemi the First, called Alowolodu."}, {"id": "oba-adeyemi-i#s2", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Let me set the ground honestly. Adeyemi the First reigned in the closing decades of the nineteenth century, in the years when the long wars of the war-cities were grinding at last toward exhaustion, and when the British, already established on the coast at Lagos, were reaching their power ever deeper into the interior. This was the age of what the strangers called the scramble, when the powers of Europe carved up the whole of Africa among themselves, drawing lines on maps of lands they barely knew, and claiming dominion over ancient kingdoms and peoples without their consent. Oyo, and all the Yoruba country, fell within the sphere the British claimed. And in Adeyemi’s reign, the ancient independence of the Alaafin’s kingdom passed, through treaty and pressure and the shadow of superior force, under the overlordship of the British crown."}, {"id": "oba-adeyemi-i#s3", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Understand what this meant, children, for it is one of the great turning points in all our history. For all the long centuries we have followed, from Oranyan to this hour, Oyo had been its own master. Mighty or weak, rising or falling, at war or at peace, the kingdom had answered to no power but its own gods and its own crown. Even in the depths of the fall, even in the age of the war-cities, the Yoruba quarrels had been quarrels among the Yoruba themselves, decided by Yoruba hands. Now, for the first time, a foreign power from beyond the sea claimed the right to rule over the ancient kingdom, to set the bounds of its wars, to oversee its affairs, to make the Alaafin, however honored, a subject of a distant queen. The ancient sovereignty of Oranyan’s line, which had endured through every catastrophe for many centuries, now passed under a foreign overlordship. That is the great and bitter meaning of Adeyemi’s reign."}, {"id": "oba-adeyemi-i#s4", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Now, children, I must teach you to see this hour with clear and complex eyes, neither with a false comfort nor with a despair that forgets our people’s strength. The coming of British power was, in its root, a conquest, however much it was dressed in the language of treaties and protection and peace-bringing. The Yoruba did not freely choose to be ruled by a distant crown. The lines drawn on the strangers’ maps were drawn without our consent, joining peoples who had never been one nation and claiming dominion by the right of superior force. This is the truth of it, and no honest telling may hide it. The independence of the ancient kingdom was lost in these years, and its loss was a wound."}, {"id": "oba-adeyemi-i#s5", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "And yet, children, hear the fuller truth, for our people are never merely victims in any telling, and Adeyemi was not merely a king who surrendered. There is a wisdom in knowing when a thing cannot be resisted by force, and in choosing, instead of a hopeless war that would only bring destruction, to preserve what can be preserved through patience and negotiation. The British power that reached Oyo was backed by weapons and resources against which the exhausted war-cities of the Yoruba, after a century of tearing at one another, could not have prevailed in open war. A king who understood this, who chose to treat rather than to be crushed, who sought to keep the crown and the dignity and the people alive under the new overlordship rather than to lead them into a doomed and annihilating resistance, was not a coward but a realist, and his realism preserved things that a futile defiance would have destroyed."}, {"id": "oba-adeyemi-i#s6", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "For here is the remarkable thing, children, the thing that shows the deep resilience of our people. The British, in ruling the Yoruba country, chose in large part to rule through the existing kings and chiefs, through the ancient institutions, rather than sweeping them away. This was their method, which they called by various names, ruling through the traditional authorities. And so the ancient crown of Oyo, the sacred kingship of Oranyan’s line, did not perish under British rule. It endured. The Alaafin remained, honored, recognized, a figure of authority within the new order, even as the ultimate power passed to the distant crown. The strangers ruled, but they ruled in part through the ancient kingship, and so that kingship survived the conquest, as it had survived so many catastrophes before. Adeyemi, reigning as the power closed in, is part of how the sacred crown was carried, wounded but alive, across the great divide from the age of independence into the age of colonial rule."}, {"id": "oba-adeyemi-i#s7", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Let me not flatten Adeyemi into either a mere victim or a mere collaborator, for the truth is more human and more difficult than either. He was a king caught at a hinge of history, in an hour when the old world was ending and a new and foreign power was rising that no Yoruba king could have turned back. His task was not the glorious task of the conqueror but the harder, sadder, less celebrated task of the one who must guide his people through a defeat they cannot avoid, preserving what can be preserved, keeping the crown and the people alive, buying with patience and dignity the survival of the sacred line into a changed age. That is a thankless labor, and history does not sing of it as it sings of conquest. But it is a labor of real wisdom and real love for one’s people, and Adeyemi bore it in his hour."}, {"id": "oba-adeyemi-i#s8", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "And draw this lesson, children, for you will need it in your own lives, though the gods grant you never face it on so terrible a scale. There are hours when you cannot win, when the force against you is too great to be thrown back, and in such hours the deepest courage is not the courage to fight a hopeless fight and be destroyed, but the harder courage to endure, to preserve, to keep alive the seed of your people and your dignity through the long night of subjection, waiting for the day, however distant, when freedom might come again. The Yoruba did not stay under foreign rule forever. That night, too, would end, in the fullness of time. And the crown of Oyo, kept alive through the colonial age by kings who chose survival over annihilation, would still be standing when that night ended. There is a heroism in survival, children, that is quieter than the heroism of the sword, but no less real, and often harder."}, {"id": "oba-adeyemi-i#s9", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So remember Adeyemi the First, children, the king in whose reign the power of the distant empire closed its hand around the ancient kingdom of Oyo. Remember the great and bitter turning, when the sovereignty that had endured from Oranyan through every catastrophe passed at last under a foreign overlordship. But remember too the wisdom that knows when a thing cannot be resisted by force and chooses to preserve rather than to be crushed, and the quiet heroism of survival that kept the sacred crown alive across the divide into the colonial age. The strangers rule now, children, but the crown of Oranyan still stands, wounded but alive, and the long night of subjection carries within it, always, the seed of a freedom yet to come. Tomorrow, we follow the ancient kingship into the age of colonial rule, and the kings who kept it alive through the twentieth century."}]}, {"id": "oba-lawani-ladigbolu-i", "emoji": "👑", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#1a0a00,#4a2800)", "category": "itan-oba", "rating": 9.6, "duration": "9 min", "title": {"en": "Lawani and Ladigbolu the First: The Kings of the Early Colonial Age", "yo": "Lawani and Ladigbolu the First: The Kings of the Early Colonial Age"}, "desc": {"en": "Alaafin Lawani Agogoja and Siyanbola Ladigbolu I, who held the crown through early British rule.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Itan Oba", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Alaafin, Alaafin Lawani Agogoja and Alaafin Siyanbola Ladigbolu I, who held the ancient crown through the early decades of British rule)", "segments": [{"id": "oba-lawani-ladigbolu-i#s1", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Come, children, and sit, for tonight the fire lights the ancient crown reigning under a foreign sky, in the early decades of colonial rule, when the Alaafin of Oyo remained a great and honored figure even as the ultimate power lay with the distant British crown. I will hold two kings together tonight, Lawani Agogoja and, above all, the long-reigning Siyanbola Ladigbolu the First, for they belong to the same age and teach the same lesson, the lesson of how the sacred kingship of Oranyan’s line survived, and even in some ways flourished, through the early colonial century, keeping the dignity and the soul of the Yoruba alive under the rule of strangers."}, {"id": "oba-lawani-ladigbolu-i#s2", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Let me set the ground honestly. These kings reigned in the late nineteenth and the early to middle twentieth centuries, in the age after the British had established their overlordship, when Oyo was part of the colonial order the strangers built. The British ruled the Yoruba country in large part, as I told you, through the existing kings and chiefs, a method they leaned upon heavily. And so the Alaafin, though no longer an independent sovereign, remained a figure of enormous prestige, the sacred head of the Oyo people, honored by the colonial rulers as the paramount traditional authority of a great region, and revered by the people as the living heir of the ancient glory. Lawani held that crown for a time, and then, from the second decade of the twentieth century, came the great and long reign of Ladigbolu the First, which lasted for decades and became one of the defining reigns of the colonial age."}, {"id": "oba-lawani-ladigbolu-i#s3", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Let me tell you of Ladigbolu the First, children, for his was a reign that showed how the ancient kingship could carry real weight even under foreign rule. He reigned for a very long time, across the middle decades of the colonial era, and he became a towering figure, not only within Oyo but across the whole Yoruba country and beyond. He was recognized by the colonial authorities as a paramount ruler of the first rank, consulted, honored, given a place of dignity in the councils of the colonial order. And among his own people, he was revered as the sacred Alaafin, the keeper of the ancient rites, the living link to Oranyan and all the mighty kings we have spoken of across these many nights. He held the two worlds together in his own person, the ancient sacred kingship and the new colonial order, and he held them with a dignity that raised the honor of the crown."}, {"id": "oba-lawani-ladigbolu-i#s4", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Think on the strange and difficult art of that, children. To be a great king under the rule of foreigners is to walk a knife’s edge every day. Lean too far toward the colonial rulers, become merely their instrument, and you lose the love and trust of your own people, who will see you as a servant of the conqueror. Lean too far toward defiance, and you bring down the wrath of a power you cannot overcome, and lose the crown and perhaps the people’s protection along with it. The great colonial-era kings, and Ladigbolu the First above them, had to walk that edge, keeping the dignity and the sacred authority of the ancient crown, using their honored position to protect and to speak for their people within the colonial order, while never becoming either a mere puppet of the strangers or a doomed rebel against them. It was a labor of the subtlest political skill, and Ladigbolu performed it across a long reign with a mastery that made the crown of Oyo shine even in the shadow of foreign rule."}, {"id": "oba-lawani-ladigbolu-i#s5", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "And here is the deep thing, children, the thing I most want you to carry from tonight’s telling. Under colonial rule, the ancient kingship became something it had not quite been before, a vessel for the survival of the Yoruba soul itself. When a people loses its political independence, when foreigners rule its lands and command its affairs, the danger is not only political but spiritual. The danger is that the people will lose themselves, will forget who they are, will let the conqueror’s ways and the conqueror’s contempt hollow out their own identity until nothing is left but a people ashamed of their own past. Against that deepest danger, the ancient kingship stood as a mighty shield. So long as the Alaafin reigned, so long as the sacred rites were kept, so long as the drums sounded the praises of Oranyan’s line and the people gathered to honor the living heir of their ancient kings, the Yoruba could not forget who they were. The crown kept the memory alive. The crown kept the soul alive."}, {"id": "oba-lawani-ladigbolu-i#s6", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "This is why the survival of the kingship through the colonial age matters so much, children, and why we honor the kings who kept it alive. Ladigbolu the First and the colonial-era Alaafin were not the mighty conquerors of the golden age. They ruled no empire. Their cavalry did not shake distant kingdoms. But they did something in its way as important. They kept the ancient identity of a great people alive under the rule of strangers, so that when the day of freedom came again, as it would, the Yoruba would still know who they were, would still have their gods and their language and their history and their sacred crown, would still be a people and not merely a scattered remnant ashamed of a forgotten past. The kings of the colonial age were the guardians of the soul in the long night of subjection, and there is no higher service a king can render than that."}, {"id": "oba-lawani-ladigbolu-i#s7", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Let me not flatten these kings into mere symbols, for they were living men who ruled real people through real and difficult times. There were famines and epidemics to be faced, the great influenza that swept the world, the hardships of the colonial economy, the disruptions of the wider world’s wars that reached even into the Yoruba country. There were disputes to be settled, chiefs to be balanced, the endless daily labor of holding a great community together. And there was the constant delicate work of representing the people to the colonial power, pressing for their interests, softening the harshness of foreign rule where it could be softened, using the prestige of the ancient crown to shield the people where a lesser authority would have been simply swept aside. Ladigbolu the First bore all of this across his long reign, and bore it with a dignity that made him one of the revered figures of his age."}, {"id": "oba-lawani-ladigbolu-i#s8", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "And draw the lesson, children, for it reaches beyond kings and beyond the colonial age. When hard times come, when you cannot have the freedom or the power you long for, the deepest task is to keep your soul alive, to remember who you are, to guard the memory and the dignity and the identity that no conqueror can take from you unless you surrender it yourself. A people can lose its independence and win it back. But a people that loses its soul, that forgets itself, that comes to despise its own past, may never recover, for it has lost the very thing that freedom would restore. The colonial-era kings kept the Yoruba soul alive. Whatever your own long nights may be, remember their example, and guard your soul through the darkness, for the dawn cannot restore to you what you have thrown away yourself."}, {"id": "oba-lawani-ladigbolu-i#s9", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So remember Lawani and Ladigbolu the First, children, the kings who held the ancient crown through the early colonial age, and above all the long and dignified reign of Ladigbolu, who made the crown of Oyo shine even under foreign rule. Remember the knife-edge art of being a great king under the rule of strangers, neither puppet nor doomed rebel but the honored keeper of a people’s dignity. And remember, above all, that the deepest service of the kingship in the long night of subjection was to keep the Yoruba soul alive, so that a people who had lost their independence would not lose themselves, and would still know who they were when the dawn of freedom finally came. The colonial night wears on now, children, but the crown still shines, and the soul of the people is kept. Tomorrow, we follow the crown toward the middle of the twentieth century, and the kings who reigned as the long night of colonial rule began, at last, to give way to the dawn of freedom."}]}, {"id": "oba-adeyemi-ii-ladigbolu-ii", "emoji": "👑", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#1a0a00,#4a2800)", "category": "itan-oba", "rating": 9.6, "duration": "10 min", "title": {"en": "Adeniran Adeyemi II and Ladigbolu II: The Kings of the Dawn of Freedom", "yo": "Adeniran Adeyemi II and Ladigbolu II: The Kings of the Dawn of Freedom"}, "desc": {"en": "The two Alaafin who reigned as colonial rule gave way to independence.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Itan Oba", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Alaafin, Alaafin Adeniran Adeyemi II and Alaafin Gbadegesin Ladigbolu II, who reigned as colonial rule gave way to independence)", "segments": [{"id": "oba-adeyemi-ii-ladigbolu-ii#s1", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Come, children, and sit close, for tonight the fire lights the turning of the long night toward the dawn, the age when the rule of the strangers ended and the peoples of this land, the Yoruba among them, became free once more to govern themselves. We have followed the ancient crown of Oyo through the whole of the colonial century, kept alive and shining by the great kings who guarded the soul of the people through the long subjection. Tonight we come to the kings who reigned as that long night ended, Adeniran Adeyemi the Second and Gbadegesin Ladigbolu the Second, and in their age the flag of the strangers came down and the peoples of Nigeria, the Yoruba among them, took up the governing of their own affairs at last."}, {"id": "oba-adeyemi-ii-ladigbolu-ii#s2", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Let me set the ground honestly, and let me speak carefully here, for we are now in an age close enough to our own that the memory of it is still living, and where memory is living the honest teller must be especially careful to keep to what is true. These kings reigned in the middle decades of the twentieth century, in and around the great year of independence, when Nigeria became a nation governing itself, no longer ruled by the distant British crown. Adeniran Adeyemi the Second held the crown through part of this stirring age, and Ladigbolu the Second after him, and their reigns span the passage from the last years of colonial rule into the first years of freedom. The exact particulars of each reign are matters of recent record that the careful keepers of such things hold precisely, and I will keep to the shape and the meaning rather than pretend to a false detail."}, {"id": "oba-adeyemi-ii-ladigbolu-ii#s3", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Understand the greatness of the age these kings reigned in, children. For the first time since the power of the strangers had closed around the ancient kingdom in the days of Adeyemi the First, the Yoruba, and all the peoples of this land, were free again to govern themselves. The long night of subjection that had begun generations before was ending. The seed of freedom that the colonial-era kings had guarded through the darkness, keeping the soul of the people alive so that a free people would still know who they were, that seed now sprouted into the dawn. This was one of the great turnings of all our history, as great in its way as the fall of the old empire or the founding of the new Oyo, and these kings reigned as it came to pass."}, {"id": "oba-adeyemi-ii-ladigbolu-ii#s4", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "But hear the fuller truth, children, for freedom is never a simple gift, and the dawn brought its own hard questions. When the strangers withdrew, they left behind them a nation of their own making, a nation drawn on their maps, joining together many peoples who had never been one, the Yoruba and the Igbo and the Hausa and the Fulani and a hundred others, all now bound within a single state called Nigeria. Building one nation out of so many peoples, each with its own ancient identity and its own memory of greatness, was a labor of enormous difficulty, and it brought struggles and rivalries and, in time, terrible conflicts that lie beyond the edge of tonight’s telling. The ancient kingship of Oyo now had to find its place within this new nation, no longer an independent sovereignty, no longer merely a piece of a colonial order, but a revered traditional institution within a modern self-governing state. That was a new and untried role, and the kings of the dawn of freedom had to begin working out what the sacred crown of Oranyan should mean in the age of the independent nation."}, {"id": "oba-adeyemi-ii-ladigbolu-ii#s5", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "Let me speak to the deep thing, children, the meaning of the ancient kingship in the age of freedom. In the old days, the Alaafin had been a sovereign, the ruler of an empire, wielding real power over life and death and war. Under the colonial order, he had become an honored traditional authority through whom the strangers partly ruled. Now, in the age of the independent nation, the crown became something else again, a revered guardian of identity and culture and tradition, a moral and spiritual authority, a living link to the ancient past, standing above the rough struggles of party politics as a symbol of the unity and the dignity and the deep history of the Yoruba people. The power to command armies and levy tribute was gone forever, and would not return. But a different kind of authority remained, and in some ways grew, the authority of memory, of identity, of the sacred, of the deep roots that a fast-changing modern world makes people long for all the more."}, {"id": "oba-adeyemi-ii-ladigbolu-ii#s6", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "This is a profound transformation, children, and I want you to grasp it, for it is the shape of the kingship in your own age. The modern world, with its governments and its parties and its elections and its rapid change, might have seemed to have no place at all for an ancient sacred crown. What need has a modern nation of an Alaafin? And yet the crown did not wither. It endured, and in enduring it took on a new meaning, becoming the keeper of the things the modern world cannot supply, the deep identity, the ancient roots, the sacred continuity that binds a people across the centuries to their ancestors and their gods. In a world of ceaseless change, the ancient crown became a still point, a reminder to a modern people of who they have always been. The kings of the dawn of freedom, Adeniran Adeyemi the Second and Ladigbolu the Second, reigned as this new meaning was being born, guiding the sacred crown into its role in the age of the free nation."}, {"id": "oba-adeyemi-ii-ladigbolu-ii#s7", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Let me not flatten these kings into mere figures of a transition, for they were living men who bore real burdens in a turbulent and hopeful age. There were the excitements and the dangers of the coming of freedom, the fierce politics of the new nation, the rivalries that would in time bring great troubles to Nigeria, the constant need to guide the Oyo people through a time of dizzying change. A traditional king in the age of modern politics walks a hard road, for he must keep the dignity and the sacredness of an office that stands above the political fray, while the world around him is convulsed by political struggle, and every faction may wish to claim the crown’s prestige for its own side. To keep the ancient crown honored and above the fray in such an age takes wisdom and restraint of a high order. The kings of the dawn of freedom bore that burden as the new nation was born, and kept the crown of Oyo standing as a symbol of unity above the struggles of the age."}, {"id": "oba-adeyemi-ii-ladigbolu-ii#s8", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "And draw the lesson, children, for it is a hopeful one and you have earned it after so many nights of hard history. The long night of subjection did end. Freedom did come again. The seed that the colonial-era kings guarded through the darkness did sprout into the dawn. This is the great and hopeful truth of our whole telling, that no night lasts forever for a people who keep their soul alive, that subjection and sorrow, however long, are not the final word, that the dawn does come. But learn too that the dawn brings its own labors, that freedom is not the end of the story but the beginning of a new and difficult chapter, in which a free people must learn to govern themselves wisely, to build unity out of difference, to make of their hard-won freedom something worthy of all that was suffered to win it. That labor is not finished, children. It is the labor of your own age, and one day it will be the labor of your hands."}, {"id": "oba-adeyemi-ii-ladigbolu-ii#s9", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So remember Adeniran Adeyemi the Second and Ladigbolu the Second, children, the kings who reigned as the long night of colonial rule gave way to the dawn of freedom. Remember that no night lasts forever for a people who keep their soul alive, and that the seed of freedom guarded through the darkness did sprout at last into a new day. Remember the great transformation of the ancient crown, from sovereign to traditional authority to the revered guardian of a people’s identity in the age of the free nation, a still point in a world of ceaseless change. And remember that freedom is not the end of the story but the beginning of a new and difficult chapter, whose labors fall in time to each new generation, and one day to yours. The dawn has come now, children. The people are free. And the ancient crown of Oranyan still stands, transformed but unbroken, into the modern age. Tomorrow, we come to the great long reign of Lamidi Adeyemi the Third, who carried the crown of Oyo across the turn of a new century."}]}, {"id": "oba-lamidi-adeyemi-iii", "emoji": "👑", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#1a0a00,#4a2800)", "category": "itan-oba", "rating": 9.6, "duration": "10 min", "title": {"en": "Lamidi Adeyemi the Third: The King of the Long Modern Reign", "yo": "Lamidi Olayiwola Adeyemi III"}, "desc": {"en": "The Alaafin who reigned from 1970 and carried the ancient crown into the twenty-first century.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Itan Oba", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Alaafin, Alaafin Lamidi Olayiwola Adeyemi III, who reigned from 1970 and carried the ancient crown across the turn of the twenty-first century)", "segments": [{"id": "oba-lamidi-adeyemi-iii#s1", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Sit near, children, for tonight the fire lights a reign that many of your own parents and grandparents lived through, the long and mighty reign of Alaafin Lamidi Olayiwola Adeyemi the Third, who took the ancient crown of Oyo in the year 1970 and held it for half a century, carrying the sacred kingship of Oranyan’s line across the turn of a new millennium and into the modern world you yourselves are being born into. His was one of the longest and most consequential reigns of any Alaafin in the modern age, and because it reaches so near to our own day, I will tell it with special care, keeping close to what is truly known and remembered."}, {"id": "oba-lamidi-adeyemi-iii#s2", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Let me set the ground honestly. Lamidi Adeyemi the Third ascended the throne of Oyo in 1970 and reigned until his death, which came in his old age after a reign of about fifty years. He was, for the whole of that long span, one of the most prominent traditional rulers in all of Nigeria, a towering figure of the Yoruba world, revered as the paramount custodian of the ancient traditions of Oyo and honored across the nation and beyond. He reigned through a time of extraordinary change, across the decades that carried Nigeria from the years just after independence, through the age of military governments and the return of civilian rule, into the twenty-first century, a period in which the whole world was transformed by technology and change more rapid than any age before it. Through all of it, he held the ancient crown with a dignity and a presence that made him a living symbol of the deep Yoruba past in a fast-changing modern nation."}, {"id": "oba-lamidi-adeyemi-iii#s3", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Let me speak of what his long reign meant, children, for it is a lesson in the enduring power of an ancient institution in a modern age. When Lamidi Adeyemi took the throne in 1970, there might have been those who wondered what place a traditional king could have in the modern nation, with its elected governments and its written constitution and its rush toward the future. What need had a modern country of an Alaafin? And yet, across half a century, Lamidi Adeyemi the Third answered that question by the sheer weight and dignity of his reign. He showed that the ancient crown was not a relic to be set aside but a living authority of a different kind, a moral and cultural and spiritual authority, a guardian of identity and tradition, a voice that could speak for the deep continuity of a people across the churning changes of the modern world."}, {"id": "oba-lamidi-adeyemi-iii#s4", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "He became a champion and a custodian of Yoruba culture and tradition in an age when such things were under pressure from every side, from the rush of modern life, from foreign influences, from the forgetting that a fast-changing world encourages. He stood for the language, the history, the customs, the deep identity of the Yoruba, using the great prestige of the ancient crown to keep them honored and alive. In a world that too easily forgets, he was a keeper of memory. In a nation of many peoples struggling to hold together, he was a symbol of the dignity and the ancient greatness of one of its great peoples. And in the specific struggles of his own region and nation, he was a figure whose voice carried weight, consulted and honored, a moral presence above the rough struggles of party politics."}, {"id": "oba-lamidi-adeyemi-iii#s5", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "Now let me draw for you the deep meaning of so long a reign, children, for length of days is itself a kind of teaching. To reign for half a century is to become a thread of continuity across an age of change, a single steady presence while governments rise and fall, while the world transforms around you, while generations are born and grow and have children of their own, all under one crown. The child born at the start of such a reign is a grandparent by its end, and through the whole of that person’s life, the same Alaafin has sat upon the ancient throne, a living link to a past that would otherwise seem impossibly distant. This is a gift that only long reigns can give, the gift of continuity, of a steady point of reference across the decades, of a living connection between the generations. Lamidi Adeyemi the Third gave the Yoruba that gift across half a century, and it is no small thing."}, {"id": "oba-lamidi-adeyemi-iii#s6", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "And there is a deeper teaching still, children, in the endurance of the ancient crown across so modern an age. We might have thought that the old and the new could not coexist, that the ancient sacred kingship must wither in the light of the modern world, that a people rushing toward the future would have no room in their hearts for a crown that reaches back to Oranyan and beyond. But the long reign of Lamidi Adeyemi showed the opposite. It showed that the deeper a people rush into the ceaseless change of the modern world, the more they long for the ancient roots that anchor them, the more they treasure the sacred continuity that reminds them who they are. The ancient crown did not wither in the modern age. It became more precious, a still point that a dizzy and changing world needs all the more. This is a great truth, children, and you will feel it in your own lives, that the faster the world changes, the more the heart hungers for the deep and ancient things that do not change."}, {"id": "oba-lamidi-adeyemi-iii#s7", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Let me not flatten even so great a king into simple praise, for a reign of fifty years is a human reign, with its difficulties and its controversies as well as its glories, and an honest teller does not pretend otherwise. A long reign spans many struggles. There were the hard politics of the region and the nation, the rivalries among traditional rulers over precedence and honor, the tensions between the traditional institutions and the modern governments, the endless difficult questions of how an ancient crown should conduct itself in a modern democracy. No king reigns for half a century without facing hard choices and without drawing, at times, both praise and criticism, for that is the nature of any long tenure of high office. I will not pretend to weigh each of these matters, for they are recent and living and best judged by those with full knowledge of them. But I will say that across all of it, the crown of Oyo endured with its prestige not merely intact but enhanced, and that is the measure of a consequential reign."}, {"id": "oba-lamidi-adeyemi-iii#s8", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "And here I must speak to you with the honesty I have kept all through our telling. My own sure knowledge of these recent matters reaches only to a certain point in time, to the early part of the decade of the 2020s, and the world moves on beyond what I can surely know. Lamidi Adeyemi the Third reigned for about fifty years and died in his old age, full of years and honor, and a new Alaafin was in time installed to succeed him, of whom I will speak in our final telling of this great chronicle. Where the most recent particulars are concerned, I tell you honestly that you should seek the freshest knowledge from those who keep the current record, for a griot must never pretend to a certainty about the passing day that he does not truly possess. What I can tell you with certainty is the shape and the greatness of the long reign, and that shape is clear, a half-century of dignity that carried the ancient crown across the turn of a new age."}, {"id": "oba-lamidi-adeyemi-iii#s9", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So remember Lamidi Adeyemi the Third, children, the king of the long modern reign, who took the ancient crown of Oyo in 1970 and held it for half a century, a living thread of continuity across an age of ceaseless change. Remember that he showed the enduring power of an ancient institution in a modern world, becoming a revered custodian of Yoruba culture and identity, a still point in a churning age, a keeper of memory in a world that too easily forgets. Remember the gift that long reigns give, the gift of continuity across the generations. And remember the great truth his reign revealed, that the faster the world changes, the more the heart hungers for the deep and ancient things, and that the sacred crown of Oranyan, far from withering in the modern age, became more precious than ever. The long reign has ended now, children, in the fullness of years. And a new Alaafin has risen to carry the ancient crown onward into a future that is yours to shape. Tomorrow, in our final telling of this great line, we come to the present day, and the Alaafin who reigns now."}]}, {"id": "oba-owoade", "emoji": "👑", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#1a0a00,#4a2800)", "category": "itan-oba", "rating": 9.6, "duration": "10 min", "title": {"en": "The Alaafin of Our Own Day: The Crown Comes to Owoade", "yo": "Abimbola Owoade"}, "desc": {"en": "The present Alaafin, Oba Abimbola Owoade, newest link in the unbroken chain from Oranyan.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Itan Oba", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Alaafin, the present Alaafin of Oyo, Oba Abimbola Owoade, whose reign begins in our own time)", "segments": [{"id": "oba-owoade#s1", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Gather close, children, all of you, for tonight we come to the end of a very long journey, the journey of the Alaafin, from Oranyan who rode out of Ife in the mists of the beginning, all the way down the great chain of kings to the Alaafin who reigns in our own day. We have walked together through founding and exile, through homecoming and golden empire, through tyranny broken and empire fallen, through the new Oyo raised from the ruins, through the long colonial night and the dawn of freedom, through the half-century reign of Lamidi Adeyemi the Third. And now, at last, we arrive at the present, at the living crown, at the Alaafin who sits upon the ancient throne as you yourselves sit here by this fire. And that Alaafin, in our own time, is Oba Abimbola Owoade."}, {"id": "oba-owoade#s2", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Let me speak with the fullest honesty now, children, more carefully than at any other point in our long telling, for we have arrived at the very edge of the present, and about the present a truthful teller must be especially humble. After the long reign of Lamidi Adeyemi the Third came to its end, the great question arose that always arises when a mighty and long-reigning king passes, the question of who should next wear the ancient crown. The choosing of a new Alaafin is a solemn and intricate matter, governed by ancient custom, by the ruling houses of Oyo, by the kingmakers who hold that sacred trust, and in the modern age also touched by the workings of the state. And out of that solemn process, a new Alaafin was chosen and installed, Oba Abimbola Owoade, who took the throne in the year 2025, becoming the newest link in the great chain of Oranyan’s line."}, {"id": "oba-owoade#s3", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Now I must tell you plainly, children, as I promised I always would, where the limits of my sure knowledge lie. My reliable knowledge of these matters reaches to the early part of this decade, and the installation of the new Alaafin is a very recent thing, at the very frontier of what I can surely tell you. So of the deeds of his reign, of what he will do and become, of how his kingship will unfold, I cannot honestly speak, for it is happening now, in your own time, beyond the edge of what any teller who is not living through it day by day can truly know. For the freshest and fullest knowledge of the present Alaafin and his reign, you must turn to those who keep the current record, to the living news of your own day, and not to an old teller whose sure knowledge ends where the present begins. This is not a weakness in the telling. It is the honesty that a good griot owes you, never to pretend to a certainty about the passing day that he does not possess."}, {"id": "oba-owoade#s4", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "But there are things I can tell you truly even about the present, children, and they are the deepest things, the things that do not change with the passing news. I can tell you that the crown Oba Owoade now wears is the same sacred crown that Oranyan first bore, carried down through every king we have honored across these many nights, through Sango who became the thunder, through Orompoto the woman war-king, through Abipa who led the people home, through Ojigi at the empire’s height, through Abiodun who broke the tyrant, through Atiba who built the new Oyo from the ruins, through all the kings of the colonial night and the dawn of freedom, through the long reign of Lamidi Adeyemi. All of that history, all of that greatness and sorrow and endurance, all of it now rests upon the shoulders of the Alaafin of our own day. He is the living heir of the whole long chain we have followed. When he wears the crown, he wears the weight of all of it."}, {"id": "oba-owoade#s5", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "Think on the wonder of that, children. The line that began with a lonely rider out of Ife, in a time so distant that its exact beginning is lost in the mists of memory, that line has never broken. Empires have risen and fallen. The old capital was lost and a new one built. Foreign powers came and ruled and departed. The whole world was transformed, from the age of the horse and the spear to the age of machines that fly and voices that cross the earth in an instant. And through all of it, unbroken, the sacred crown of Oranyan has passed from king to king, down and down across the centuries, until it rests, in your own lifetime, upon the head of Oba Abimbola Owoade. There are few things in all the world so old and so unbroken as the line of the Alaafin. You are alive at one more link in a chain that stretches back beyond the memory of any living people. That is a marvel, children. Do not let its nearness make you forget its wonder."}, {"id": "oba-owoade#s6", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "And here is the deepest teaching of all, the teaching with which our whole long chronicle of the Alaafin comes to rest. The chain is not finished. The story is not over. The crown that rests now on the head of the present Alaafin will one day pass to another, and to another after that, on and on into a future that none of us can see. And you, children, sitting here by this fire, you are not merely the listeners to this story. You are its future. The Yoruba people whose kings we have honored across all these nights, that people goes on, and its going-on is now in your hands. The gods and the language and the customs and the deep identity that the ancient crown has guarded across all these centuries, these are now yours to keep and to carry forward, as every generation before you kept and carried them, so that the children who sit by this fire long after you are gone will still know who they are."}, {"id": "oba-owoade#s7", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Let me speak the final wisdom of the whole line, children, gathering up all the nights into one. We have seen that greatness and ruin often grow from the same root, so be humble in your strength. We have seen that the day of reckoning comes at last for every tyrant, so do not despair under injustice. We have seen that no fall is the end for a people with the courage to rebuild, so never surrender to defeat. We have seen that a people divided against itself feeds its own children to its enemies, so guard against disunity above all. We have seen that the deepest task in the long night of subjection is to keep the soul alive, so never let anyone make you forget or despise who you are. And we have seen, across the whole vast sweep of it, that a people who keep their identity, their memory, and their courage can endure any catastrophe and rise again. This is the testament of the Alaafin, written across the centuries in the lives of all the kings we have honored. Carry it with you."}, {"id": "oba-owoade#s8", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "And so we honor the present Alaafin, Oba Abimbola Owoade, not for deeds I cannot yet know, but as the living bearer of this whole great inheritance, the newest link in the unbroken chain, the keeper in our own day of the sacred crown of Oranyan. May his reign be long and wise. May the crown he carries pass one day, in the fullness of time, to worthy hands. And may the Yoruba people, whose kings we have followed from the first rider out of Ife to the Alaafin of this very hour, endure and flourish and remember, generation after generation, for as long as the drums still sound and children still gather by the fire to hear the old stories told."}, {"id": "oba-owoade#s9", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So remember, children, the Alaafin of our own day, Oba Abimbola Owoade, who took the ancient crown in the year 2025, the living heir of the whole long line we have honored across all these nights. Remember that his crown is the same crown Oranyan first bore, carried unbroken across the centuries to rest, in your own lifetime, upon his head. Seek the freshest knowledge of his reign from those who keep the present record, for it unfolds now, in your own time, beyond the edge of an old teller’s certain knowledge. And remember, above all, that the chain is not finished, that the story goes on, and that its future is now in your hands. You are the next link, children. Keep the crown of your identity unbroken, and carry it forward, as every generation before you has done, into the days that are yet to come. Here the great chronicle of the Alaafin comes to rest, from Oranyan to our own day, an unbroken line, a testament of endurance, a story without an ending, because its ending has not yet been lived."}]}, {"id": "oba-ewuare", "emoji": "👑", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#1a0a00,#4a2800)", "category": "itan-oba", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "9 min", "title": {"en": "Oba Ewuare and the Mirror of Benin-Yoruba Relations", "yo": "Ewuare"}, "desc": {"en": "Benin's great Oba Ewuare and the mirror his court held up to its Yoruba neighbours.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Itan Oba", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Oba, from “Oba Ewuare and the Mirror of Benin-Yoruba Relations”)", "segments": [{"id": "oba-ewuare#s1", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "There is a truth about the Yoruba that surprises people who imagine the old kingdoms as separate boxes on a map, each minding its own affairs. The truth is that the Yoruba world was never sealed off from its neighbors, and no relationship shows this more richly than the deep and ancient bond between the Yoruba and the kingdom of Benin, the great Edo empire that rose just to the east. To hold up the story of Benin is to hold up a mirror to the Yoruba themselves, because the two peoples share a founding legend, a line of kingship, and centuries of exchange in art, ideas, and blood. And no figure stands taller in that shared history than Oba Ewuare the Great, the transforming king of Benin, whose reign is a window into how these two great civilizations shaped and reflected one another."}, {"id": "oba-ewuare#s2", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Begin with the legend that ties them, because the Edo themselves tell it. The tradition of Benin holds that in the deep past the people of Benin, dissatisfied with their rulers, sent to Ile-Ife, the sacred cradle of the Yoruba, the place where the world was said to have been created and where Oduduwa reigned, and asked for a prince to come and rule over them. And from Ife came Oranmiyan, a son of the royal line, one of the great figures of Yoruba tradition, the same Oranmiyan connected with the founding of the Oyo dynasty. He came to Benin, and though he found the ruling of it difficult and eventually returned, he left behind a son born of a Benin woman, and that son, Eweka, became the first Oba of the new dynasty of Benin. So the kings of Benin traced their line, in this telling, back to Ife, back to the Yoruba source, and the two royal houses were understood as branches of one ancient tree."}, {"id": "oba-ewuare#s3", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Whatever the precise history behind the legend, and historians debate it, the meaning is unmistakable. The Yoruba and the Edo saw themselves as kin, their kingships as related, their sacred origins as shared. Ife remained, for both, a place of profound spiritual authority, the source. And across the centuries the two peoples traded, warred, intermarried, borrowed, and influenced one another so thoroughly that in the border regions between them the cultures blur and blend, towns speaking both tongues, keeping both sets of customs, belonging to both worlds at once. To understand the Yoruba, then, you cannot ignore Benin, and the reign of its greatest king shows why."}, {"id": "oba-ewuare#s4", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Oba Ewuare, who reigned in the fifteenth century, came to the throne the hard way, through struggle and exile and a bitter contest for power, and the traditions say that in the course of that struggle the old city was much damaged, even burned. But out of that difficult beginning came one of the great reigns of West African history. Ewuare rebuilt and remade Benin so thoroughly that he is remembered as its second founder, the king who turned a kingdom into an empire. He is credited with reorganizing the government, strengthening the power of the Oba against the fractious chiefs, and creating the elaborate court system, the palace societies and the ranks of title-holders, that would define Benin for centuries. He rebuilt the capital on a grand scale, and here we come to one of the wonders of the pre-colonial world."}, {"id": "oba-ewuare#s5", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "For the Benin that Ewuare built and his successors expanded became famous for its scale and order. The city was laid out with broad avenues, ringed and divided by an astonishing system of walls and moats, earthworks so vast that they are counted among the largest such constructions ever made by human beings anywhere, running for thousands of miles in total across the kingdom when all the linked walls of town and village are added together. When European visitors finally reached Benin centuries later, they wrote home in astonishment at the size and cleanliness and order of the city, at its great palace, its long straight streets, its disciplined life, comparing it favorably to the cities they knew in Europe. This was no small forest settlement. This was a metropolis, the capital of an empire, and Ewuare was the king who set it on that path."}, {"id": "oba-ewuare#s6", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "And then there is the art, which is where the mirror between Benin and the Yoruba shines brightest. Benin became one of the supreme centers of bronze and brass casting in the history of the world, its guild of casters producing the famous Benin bronzes, the plaques and heads and figures of breathtaking skill that recorded the kings and the court and the great deeds of the kingdom. The technique of casting metal in this refined way is deeply connected to Ife, to the Yoruba source, for Ife too produced metal and terracotta sculpture of astonishing naturalism and beauty, works that rank among the greatest art Africa has ever made. The tradition of the royal portrait head, the mastery of the lost-wax casting method, the placing of art at the very heart of kingship, these things flow between Ife and Benin, teacher and student, cousin and cousin, each reflecting and enriching the other across the generations."}, {"id": "oba-ewuare#s7", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Ewuare’s empire did not stop at building and art. Under him and the warrior kings who followed, Benin expanded outward, its armies pushing west into Yoruba country and east and south into the lands of many peoples, until at its height the kingdom of Benin held sway over a great swath of what is now southern Nigeria. Some Yoruba towns came under Benin’s influence or its outright rule, paying tribute, receiving Benin governors, absorbing Benin customs, and in the long give and take of empire, Benin took much from the Yoruba and gave much back. This is the untidy truth of neighboring powers. They are kin and they are rivals at the same time. They share a cradle and they fight over borders. They lend each other their gods and their art and their words, and they also raid each other’s towns and carry off each other’s people. The mirror shows both faces."}, {"id": "oba-ewuare#s8", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "The story of Benin, like so many African stories, has a dark and bitter later chapter that the Yoruba story also knows in its own form. Centuries after Ewuare, in the closing years of the nineteenth century, the British came, as they came everywhere, and Benin, proud and independent, resisted. In 1897 a British force marched on the city, and after a short and brutal campaign the ancient kingdom fell, the Oba was sent into exile, and the invaders looted the palace and the shrines of their treasures, carrying away the great bronzes and ivories by the thousands, scattering the artistic heritage of an empire into the museums and private collections of Europe, where a great deal of it remains to this day, the subject of a long and continuing struggle over whether stolen heritage should be returned. The fall of Benin echoes the fall of the Yoruba kingdoms to the same colonial power in the same era, kindred peoples meeting the same conquest."}, {"id": "oba-ewuare#s9", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So when a Yoruba person looks toward Benin, they see, truly, a mirror. They see a kingship that legend ties to their own sacred source at Ife. They see an art that grew from the same root as the incomparable sculpture of Ife. They see a great city and a great empire built by a transforming king, Ewuare, who stands in Benin’s memory much as the founding heroes stand in theirs. They see a neighbor who was kin and rival, partner and adversary, across a thousand years. And they see, in Benin’s conquest and looting, a version of their own encounter with the wider world’s violence. The lesson the elders draw from this shared history is a large one and a generous one. It is that no people stands alone, that the great civilizations of this region grew up together, borrowing and blending and enriching one another, and that the pride any Yoruba takes in Ife’s art or Oyo’s power belongs, rightly understood, to a wider brotherhood of peoples who built wonders side by side. To honor Benin, and its great king Ewuare, is in a real sense to honor the Yoruba story too, for the two were always reflections of each other."}]}, {"id": "oba-okunade-sijuade", "emoji": "👑", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#1a0a00,#4a2800)", "category": "itan-oba", "rating": 9.4, "duration": "4 min", "title": {"en": "Okunade Sijuade Olubuse II: The Ooni as Warlord-Diplomat", "yo": "Okunade Sijuade Olubuse II"}, "desc": {"en": "The Ooni of Ife who wielded prestige, presence, and diplomacy as his weapons.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Itan Oba", "secondary": [], "source_note": "", "segments": [{"id": "oba-okunade-sijuade#s1", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Narrator opens: Not all warlords carry weapons. Some of the most effective warriors in Yoruba history fought with words, with presence, with the strategic deployment of prestige. The story of leadership in Yorubaland is not only about men who rode horses and carried iron — it is also about those who understood that power has many faces. This context is important for understanding the broader tradition of Yoruba military leadership. The Aare Ona Kakanfo was the apex of"}, {"id": "oba-okunade-sijuade#s2", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "a pyramid of military titles that ran from the great commanders down through ranks of war chiefs, each town and kingdom having its own military hierarchy. The Balogun — literally the war commander — existed in virtually every major Yoruba city. Ibadan had its Balogun. Oyo had its Balogun. Ijebu had its Balogun. And each Balogun commanded his own forces, operated within his own protocol, and answered to the Oba and the Kakanfo in the broader military hierarchy."}, {"id": "oba-okunade-sijuade#s3", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "This elaborate system produced extraordinary warriors — men who spent their entire adult lives in military service, who knew the terrain of Yorubaland better than any map, who understood the psychology of enemy communities, who could read the signs of battle the way a farmer reads the sky for rain. The story of each Kakanfo, from Afonja the first to Gani Adams the fifteenth, is a story of this culture at its most concentrated — the full weight of Yoruba martial tradition embodied in one man, given one title, and sent to defend or extend the reach of the state."}]}, {"id": "oba-owo-kingdom", "emoji": "👑", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#1a0a00,#4a2800)", "category": "itan-oba", "rating": 9.4, "duration": "4 min", "title": {"en": "The Origin of the Owo Kingdom: Where Yoruba Met Benin", "yo": "Owo"}, "desc": {"en": "How the Owo kingdom arose where Yoruba and Benin worlds met.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Itan Oba", "secondary": [], "source_note": "", "segments": [{"id": "oba-owo-kingdom#s1", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Narrator opens: Owo is a city that sits at the crossroads. Geographically, it lies at the southeastern edge of Yorubaland, where the forest thickens and the road to Benin begins. Culturally, it is a place where Yoruba and Benin traditions have intertwined for centuries, producing a civilization that is unmistakably Yoruba in language and ancestry but deeply inflected by its Benin neighbors in art, ceremony, and palace protocol. The Olowo — the king of Owo — is one of the four most senior Yoruba obas, alongside the Ooni, the Alafin, and the Oba of Benin. This ranking reflects Owo's antiquity and its cultural significance."}, {"id": "oba-owo-kingdom#s2", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "The founding myth of Owo says that the original settlers came from Ife — descended, as all Yoruba kingdoms claim, from Oduduwa. The founding ancestor is Ojugbelu, who journeyed from Ile-Ife and established the first settlement that would become Owo. Over generations, as Owo grew and traded with Benin to the east, it absorbed Benin artistic traditions, particularly in bronze and ivory carving. The Owo bronzes discovered at a site called Igbo Laja in the late 20th century are among the most significant archaeological finds in Nigeria. They date to around the 15th century CE and show a style that is neither purely Ife nor purely Benin — it is Owo. It is a synthesis, a creative conversation between two great artistic traditions that produced something entirely new."}, {"id": "oba-owo-kingdom#s3", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "This is a lesson in what civilization actually is. It is not a pure thing that comes from one place. It is a living conversation between peoples, a trading of techniques and ideas and images across borders and generations. Owo shows us that the most interesting cultures are often the ones that sit at the crossroads — absorbing from multiple directions, filtering through their own genius, and producing something that belongs to everyone and to no one. The Olojo festival, the palace ceremonies, the masked traditions of Owo — all of them show this beautiful hybridity. The Yoruba songs of Owo have words that are pure Yoruba. The masquerade movements have gestures that come from Benin. The bronze objects in the palace look like they come from both places and neither place simultaneously. Owo. A city at the crossroads. A city that remembered where it came from and chose also to become where it arrived."}]}, {"id": "oba-ijesa-owa-obokun", "emoji": "👑", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#1a0a00,#4a2800)", "category": "itan-oba", "rating": 9.4, "duration": "4 min", "title": {"en": "The Ijesa Kingdom and the Owa Obokun: Kings of the Sacred Throne", "yo": "Owa Obokun"}, "desc": {"en": "The Ijesa kingdom and the Owa Obokun, kings of the sacred throne of Ilesa.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Itan Oba", "secondary": [], "source_note": "", "segments": [{"id": "oba-ijesa-owa-obokun#s1", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Narrator opens: Between the Ekiti hills and the Osun River, in a landscape of dense forest and clear streams, the Ijesa people built a kingdom that was at once fiercely independent and deeply connected to the Yoruba world. The Owa Obokun — the king of Ilesa, the Ijesa capital — holds one of the highest ranks among Yoruba kings, and the title itself is a compressed history: Owa (from Ile-Ife's royal vocabulary) and Obokun (a reference to the founding ancestor's connection to Oduduwa). The Ijesa are known for several things: their warrior tradition, their commercial enterprise, and a certain cultural stubbornness that has made them both celebrated and occasionally exasperating to their neighbors. They say: \"Ijesa lo p'olu\" — it is the Ijesa who killed the termite mound, meaning the Ijesa do what others will not."}, {"id": "oba-ijesa-owa-obokun#s2", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "The founding of the Ijesa Kingdom, like most Yoruba kingdom foundations, traces back to a prince from Ile-Ife. Obokun Adimula, the founding ancestor, was a child of Oduduwa who journeyed from Ife and established his throne at Ilesa. The specific geography of Ilesa — on the edges of the forest belt, close to the trade routes that connected the interior to the coast — made it a natural center of commerce. The Ijesa Kingdom's greatest challenge came during the 19th century, when the wars of the Oyo collapse period brought instability to the entire region. The Ijesa found themselves in an uncomfortable position — squeezed between Ibadan to the west and the Ekiti kingdoms to the north, with the Fulani pressure from Ilorin adding a third dimension. The Ijesa kings of this period — notably Owa Bepo and Owa Ajimoko — had to navigate extraordinary diplomatic complexity while maintaining their kingdom's integrity."}, {"id": "oba-ijesa-owa-obokun#s3", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "It was in this context that Ogedemgbe emerged — the Seriki of the Ijesa, the military genius who would lead the Ekiti Parapo against Ibadan. Ogedemgbe was not himself Owa Obokun. He was a war chief — a strategic commander whose brilliance lay in military organization, in intelligence gathering, in the kind of patient strategic thinking that wins long wars. His alliance with the Ekiti kingdoms in the Kiriji War was the Ijesa Kingdom's finest military moment. The Ijesa also produced some of the first Yoruba Christians and Christian intellectuals. The London Missionary Society established a presence in Ilesa in the 1860s, and within a generation, Ijesa students were traveling to Lagos and Freetown for education, returning with degrees and ideas that would reshape the community. The Ijesa Improvement Society — founded in the early 20th century — was one of the most active community development organizations in colonial Nigeria."}, {"id": "oba-ijesa-owa-obokun#s4", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "Today, the Owa Obokun's palace in Ilesa is a center of the city's cultural life. The Owa presides over festivals, settles disputes, and serves as the spiritual anchor of a community that has always known how to navigate between tradition and modernity. The Ijesa. Killers of termite mounds. Builders of kingdoms. Keepers of themselves."}]}, {"id": "oba-oba-of-benin-bronzes", "emoji": "👑", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#1a0a00,#4a2800)", "category": "itan-oba", "rating": 9.4, "duration": "4 min", "title": {"en": "The Oba of Benin and the Bronzes: Cultural Sovereignty in the 21st Century", "yo": "The Oba of Benin and the Bronzes: Cultural Sovereignty in the 21st Century"}, "desc": {"en": "The Oba of Benin and the fight to bring the looted bronzes home: royal sovereignty in the 21st century.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Itan Oba", "secondary": [], "source_note": "", "segments": [{"id": "oba-oba-of-benin-bronzes#s1", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Narrator opens: In February 1897, British soldiers marched into Benin City on what they called a \"Punitive Expedition\" — retribution for the ambush of a British trade delegation that the Benin Kingdom had attacked after repeated provocations. The soldiers burned the palace, looted the sacred objects of the royal court, and shipped approximately three thousand bronze plaques, ivory sculptures, and sacred objects to London. In three days, they removed what it had taken the Benin Kingdom six hundred years to create."}, {"id": "oba-oba-of-benin-bronzes#s2", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "The Benin Bronzes — as the world now calls them — are among the most technically and artistically extraordinary objects ever made by human hands. Cast using the lost-wax method perfected in Ile-Ife and elevated to supreme mastery in Benin, they were created over six centuries by court artists working in the service of the Oba to commemorate battles, ceremonies, Portuguese visitors, significant events, and the lineages of great leaders. They were historical records in bronze and ivory. They were sacred objects in a royal cult. They were the material memory of a civilization. They were taken. And they ended up in the British Museum, the Berlin Ethnological Museum, the Smithsonian, the Pitt Rivers Museum, and hundreds of other collections across Europe and North America."}, {"id": "oba-oba-of-benin-bronzes#s3", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "For more than a century, the Oba of Benin and the Nigerian government asked for them back. For more than a century, the answer was no — or a prolonged version of no, dressed in the language of \"guardianship,\" \"universal heritage,\" and the practical concern that if one collection was returned, all would follow. The conversation shifted dramatically in the 2010s and 2020s. A new generation of museum directors — many of them from backgrounds other than the colonial tradition that built the great European collections — began to acknowledge what their predecessors had refused to say: that the objects were taken by force, that the communities they were taken from had not consented, and that the philosophical arguments for indefinite retention were colonial arguments dressed in post-colonial language."}, {"id": "oba-oba-of-benin-bronzes#s4", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Germany moved first at significant scale. The German government committed to returning its Benin Bronze collection — hundreds of objects held in Berlin, Hamburg, Cologne, and other museums — to Nigeria. The Benin Dialogue Group, which brought together museum directors and Benin Kingdom representatives, developed protocols for return. In 2022, the first major returns began. The political complexity in Nigeria — over whether returned objects should go to the federal government, to the Edo State government, or directly to the Benin royal court — showed that even the justice of return could be complicated by the politics of the state receiving what is returned. But the principle had been conceded. The bronzes were coming home."}, {"id": "oba-oba-of-benin-bronzes#s5", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "This story is still unfolding. The British Museum's collection — more than 900 Benin objects, the largest single holding in the world — remains the most contested. The debate about what return means, who has custody, and how the returned objects will be preserved and displayed continues. What is not contested: these objects were made by Yoruba-Edo civilization. They belong in that civilization. Their return is not charity. It is justice."}]}, {"id": "ologun-kokoro-igangan", "emoji": "🗡️", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#2a0500,#6a1400)", "category": "aare", "rating": 9.6, "duration": "10 min", "title": {"en": "The First of the War-Chiefs: Kokoro Igangan and the Making of the Aare Ona Kakanfo", "yo": "Kokoro Igangan"}, "desc": {"en": "The first Aare Ona Kakanfo and the birth of the title that bound the empire's sword to its crown.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Aare Ona Kakanfo", "secondary": [], "source_note": "", "segments": [{"id": "ologun-kokoro-igangan#s1", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Come closer, my children, and let the fire warm your faces, for tonight we begin a new line of stories. We have walked the long road of the Alaafin, from Oranyan who came down on the iron chain to the crown that sits in Oyo in our own day. Now we turn to another line, a line not of kings but of the men who carried the sword before the kings. We speak now of the Aare Ona Kakanfo, the field marshal of the whole Yoruba nation, the man whose title alone could make grown warriors grow quiet.And where does a river of war begin? It begins with the first drop. It begins with a man whose name the old ones still whisper: Kokoro Igangan.· · ·Understand first what this title means, for if you do not understand the title, you cannot understand the men who carried it."}, {"id": "ologun-kokoro-igangan#s2", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "The Alaafin sat in his palace, sacred, half a god, too holy to lead armies into the field himself. A king who dies in battle is a wound the whole nation must carry. So the wisdom of the old Oyo made a second power, a power of the sword to stand beside the power of the throne. They created the office of the Aare Ona Kakanfo, the supreme war commander, the generalissimo of all the armies of Oyo.But listen, for here is the thing that made this office terrible and great at once. The Aare Ona Kakanfo could not live in the capital. He was forbidden to dwell in the same city as the Alaafin. He was given a town of his own, far from the palace, and there he ruled his warriors like a prince of blood."}, {"id": "ologun-kokoro-igangan#s3", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "It was said that no Aare Ona Kakanfo should ever bow to any man save the Alaafin himself, and even that bow was a stiff and proud thing. He was the storm the empire kept chained, and released only when it wished to break something.And there was a shadow woven into the office from its very first day. The Aare Ona Kakanfo was expected to win. To lose a war and return alive was a disgrace no oath could wash clean. Custom held that a defeated Aare had three choices, and all three were hard: to conquer, to die in the field, or to take his own life rather than carry defeat home. This was the weight placed upon the man who wore the title. Glory on one shoulder, and death standing quietly on the other.· · ·Now, who was the first to carry this weight?"}, {"id": "ologun-kokoro-igangan#s4", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "The old accounts give us the name Kokoro Igangan, a man of the town of Iwoye, remembered as the first Aare Ona Kakanfo of Oyo. I will be honest with you, as a good griot must be. The records of that first age are thin, and the mouths that could have told us every detail closed long ago. Some things we remember clearly, and some things we can only say were remembered a certain way. I will not dress up a guess in the robes of a fact. That is not the way of this fire.But this we hold firmly: the office began, and a first man carried it, and after him came a line that would run for centuries down to our own time. Kokoro Igangan stands at the head of that line the way Oranyan stands at the head of the kings."}, {"id": "ologun-kokoro-igangan#s5", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "He is the door through which all the others walked.· · ·Picture the Oyo of that early age. It was a young power still testing the reach of its arm. The cavalry that would one day make Oyo the terror of the grasslands was being built, horse by horse, rider by rider. The Yoruba had learned that on the open savanna to the north, a nation with horses could strike like lightning and be gone before the enemy could gather. And a cavalry needs a commander who thinks not of one village but of the whole board, the way a wise old man thinks many moves ahead in ayo, lifting the seeds and dropping them into the far houses.Kokoro Igangan was made that commander. When Oyo needed to punish a rebellious town, when a tribute went unpaid, when a neighbor grew too proud, it was the Aare and his war-chiefs who rode out."}, {"id": "ologun-kokoro-igangan#s6", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "The Alaafin gave the word; the Aare gave the death. That was the division of the world in old Oyo.· · ·Let me tell you what the office demanded of the body and the spirit of a man, for the first Aare set the mold that all the others were poured into.He had to be fearless, yes, but fearlessness alone is cheap. Any fool can be brave for a moment. The Aare had to be fearless across years, across long campaigns in the rain and the harmattan dust, across the slow grinding sieges where hunger kills more men than spears. He had to be a leader whom hard men would follow into fire, for warriors do not follow titles, they follow the man who has earned their trust with his own scars."}, {"id": "ologun-kokoro-igangan#s7", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "And he had to be loyal, though loyalty was the hardest test of all. Think of it. Here is a man given his own town, his own army, his own glory, forbidden to enter the capital, told he is second only to the king. Such a man carries a great temptation in his own strength. The whole later history of the Aare Ona Kakanfo, as you will hear in the nights to come, is in one sense the story of this single question: will the sword serve the throne, or will the sword one day decide it no longer needs the throne? At the beginning, in the days of Kokoro Igangan, the answer was loyalty. The storm stayed chained. But the chain was always being tested."}, {"id": "ologun-kokoro-igangan#s8", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "There is a lesson here, my children, that I want you to carry away tonight even if you forget the names. The old Yoruba did not fear power. They organized it. They knew that a nation needs a hand that heals and a hand that strikes, and that it is dangerous to put both hands on the same body. So they set the sacred king in one place and the war marshal in another, and they made each need the other. The Alaafin without the Aare had no arm to strike with. The Aare without the Alaafin had no throne to fight for, no legitimacy, no sacred word to make his violence into justice rather than mere banditry."}, {"id": "ologun-kokoro-igangan#s9", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "This is wisdom that older and larger empires than Oyo have failed to learn, and paid for the failure with their lives. To separate the crown from the sword, and yet to bind them, that is a subtle and a difficult thing. Kokoro Igangan stood at the birth of that arrangement. He was the first sword."}, {"id": "ologun-kokoro-igangan#s10", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "I will not pretend to give you a long list of his battles, for I would be inventing what I do not know, and I have told you that is not done at this fire. What I give you instead is the truer thing: his meaning. Kokoro Igangan is the first name in a roll of honor and terror that stretches down the centuries. After him would come Oyabi, who broke a great rebellion in the north. After him, in a darker age, would come Afonja, whose ambition helped bring the old empire crashing down. After him, Kurunmi of Ijaye, that lion of the last wars. After him, Latoosa of Ibadan, the last of the old Aare of the fighting age. And then, when the guns of the empire fell silent, the title would sleep, and wake again in a new age upon the shoulders of new men: a premier, a chief who became a martyr of democracy, and in our own day an activist who guards the customs of the land."}, {"id": "ologun-kokoro-igangan#s11", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "All of them, every one, are the sons of the office that Kokoro Igangan first carried. When you hear their stories in the nights to come, remember that they are all walking a road whose first stone this man laid. So we honor him tonight not for a hundred battles we cannot name, but for being the first. The first to be trusted with the whole nation’s sword. The first to carry the terrible custom that victory was the only honorable homecoming. The first to prove that a war-marshal could hold enormous power and still bow his proud head to the sacred throne of the Alaafin. The line of the Aare Ona Kakanfo begins with him. And a line, my children, is only as strong as the first knot that ties it. Kokoro Igangan tied that first knot, and it held."}, {"id": "ologun-kokoro-igangan#s12", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "Tomorrow, we sit with Oyabi, the Aare who rode north and broke the great rebellion, and who showed the empire what the office could truly do when a strong man filled it."}]}, {"id": "ologun-oyabi", "emoji": "🗡️", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#2a0500,#6a1400)", "category": "aare", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "9 min", "title": {"en": "The Hammer of the North: Oyabi and the Breaking of the Great Rebellion", "yo": "Oyabi"}, "desc": {"en": "The war-marshal who rode north, broke the great rebellion, and showed the empire the full terror of the office.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Aare Ona Kakanfo", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Aare Ona Kakanfo, the story of the war-marshal who rode to the far north and showed the empire the full terror of the office)", "segments": [{"id": "ologun-oyabi#s1", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Sit, my children, and warm your hands, for last night we spoke of Kokoro Igangan, the first man to carry the sword of the whole nation. Tonight we speak of one who filled that office so fully that his name became a byword for what an Aare Ona Kakanfo could do when a strong man wore the title. His name was Oyabi, and he was the hammer of the north."}, {"id": "ologun-oyabi#s2", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Before I tell you of his war, let me remind you of the shape of the world he lived in. Oyo in his day was near the height of its strength. The cavalry that Kokoro Igangan’s generation had begun to build was now a true and terrible thing, thousands of horsemen who could cross the grassland faster than news could travel. The Alaafin in his palace ruled tribute-towns that stretched far beyond the Yoruba heartland, north toward the Niger, west toward the land of the Aja and the Fon. An empire is a great tree, my children, and a great tree draws envy. There are always those who look up at it and think, if it fell, I might have its place in the sun."}, {"id": "ologun-oyabi#s3", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Now, to the far north of Oyo lay the country of the Bariba, which our people also called Borgu. The Borgu were proud horsemen themselves, hard men of a hard land, and they did not love the reach of Oyo’s arm. And in that same age there rose a great rebellion, a confederacy of towns and peoples who decided that the tribute-chain of Oyo had bound them long enough. They gathered. They armed. They sharpened their defiance into a spear pointed at the heart of the empire. When news of this reached the palace, the Alaafin did what the wisdom of Oyo had prepared him to do. He did not ride out himself, for the sacred person of the king was not to be risked on the open field. He summoned his storm. He sent for the Aare Ona Kakanfo, Oyabi."}, {"id": "ologun-oyabi#s4", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Imagine the gathering of that host. Picture the dust rising over the plain as the war-chiefs brought in their contingents, town by town, each under its own banner and its own drum. The Eso, the picked war-captains of Oyo, seventy of them by the old reckoning, men who had sworn never to turn their backs in battle, formed the iron core. Around them the cavalry, lances bright, horses stamping and blowing. And over them all, on the finest horse, in the robes and the plaited war-crest of his office, rode Oyabi."}, {"id": "ologun-oyabi#s5", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "A good general, my children, is not merely a brave man. Bravery he must have, but bravery is the cheapest thing in an army. What Oyabi had, and what made him remembered, was the harder gift: the gift of moving a great host across a great distance and arriving still strong, still fed, still ready to strike. To march an army north for many days and to hold it together, that is a labor that has broken commanders greater than many a famous king. Oyabi marched, and he marched far. The old accounts remember this campaign as one that carried the arms of Oyo deep into the northern country, into the very borderlands of Borgu, to break the rebellion at its root rather than merely to slap at its branches. This was the wisdom of the hammer. You do not chase a swarm of bees one by one. You find the hive, and you destroy it."}, {"id": "ologun-oyabi#s6", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "And here, let me be honest with you as I always am at this fire. The precise field on which the great battle was fought, the exact count of the fallen, the names of every chief who stood against him, these details the centuries have worn smooth, the way the river wears a stone. I will not invent them for you. But the shape of the thing is remembered clearly and firmly: Oyabi rode north, met the great rebellion, and broke it. The confederacy that had gathered to defy Oyo was shattered and scattered. The tribute-chain held. The tree stood."}, {"id": "ologun-oyabi#s7", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "Think of what that victory meant, not just in that season but for a generation after. When a rebellion rises and succeeds, every discontented town in the empire takes note, and soon the whole structure trembles. But when a rebellion rises and is crushed utterly by the war-marshal of the king, then every discontented town takes a different note. They remember. They say to their hot-blooded young men, do you recall what happened to those who rose in the north? Do you recall Oyabi? And the young men grow quiet, and the tribute is paid, and the peace of the empire, which is a peace held up by the memory of the sword, endures a while longer."}, {"id": "ologun-oyabi#s8", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "This is a hard truth about all empires, my children, and I will not hide it from you to make the story prettier. The peace of Oyo was a real peace, and under it markets flourished and roads were safe and the arts of the city grew rich. But it was a peace that rested upon fear as well as upon justice. The Aare Ona Kakanfo was the keeper of that fear. Oyabi kept it well."}, {"id": "ologun-oyabi#s9", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "There is a lesson in Oyabi for those who would lead anything, an army or a household or a town. It is this. The strong man’s true test is not the battle he fights but the campaign he sustains. Any man can burn hot for an hour. The one who is remembered is the one who can carry the weight day after day, over the long dusty road, when the food runs low and the men grumble and the enemy is still far off and the glory feels like a lie someone told you in your youth. Oyabi carried that weight all the way to the north and back, and he carried victory home. That is why his name outlived him."}, {"id": "ologun-oyabi#s10", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "And notice, my children, one more thing, for it matters greatly to the whole line of stories we are telling. Oyabi was enormously powerful. He commanded the entire military might of the empire. He had marched further and won bigger than most kings ever dream of. A lesser man, drunk on such success, might have turned that army around and pointed it at the palace itself. Who could have stopped him? But Oyabi did not. He brought the sword home and laid its glory at the feet of the Alaafin, and he returned to his own town, and the storm went back into its chains."}, {"id": "ologun-oyabi#s11", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "Remember this restraint. Hold it in your minds. Because the day is coming in our stories, generations down the line, when an Aare Ona Kakanfo will look at that same power in his own hands and make the opposite choice, and the whole empire will burn for it. His name is Afonja, and we will come to him soon enough. But tonight belongs to Oyabi, who had every reason to break faith and did not. So we honor him. The hammer of the north. The general who could move a nation’s army across a wilderness and arrive still strong. The war-marshal who broke the great rebellion and by breaking it bought the empire a longer life. And, not least, the loyal sword who proved once more, as Kokoro Igangan had proved before him, that a man could hold thunder in his hand and still bow his head to the sacred throne."}, {"id": "ologun-oyabi#s12", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "Tomorrow, we sit with a harder and a sadder story. We come to Afonja, the Aare Ona Kakanfo of Ilorin, the brilliant and bitter man whose quarrel with his king would tear the old empire apart and change the map of our whole world. Prepare your hearts, for it is a tale of how the greatest sword can become the deepest wound."}]}, {"id": "ologun-afonja", "emoji": "🗡️", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#2a0500,#6a1400)", "category": "aare", "rating": 9.8, "duration": "20 min", "title": {"en": "The Day Oyo Trembled: The Revolt of Afonja", "yo": "Afonja"}, "desc": {"en": "The Aare Ona Kakanfo of Ilorin whose revolt against his king, and fatal alliance with the Fulani, helped bring down the Oyo empire.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Aare Ona Kakanfo", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Oba, from “The Day Oyo Trembled: The Revolt of Afonja”)", "segments": [{"id": "ologun-afonja#s1", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Empires rarely fall to their enemies. More often they fall to themselves, undone from within by the ambition of their own great men, by quarrels at the top that crack the whole structure open and let the outside pour in. So it was with Oyo, the mighty empire that had ruled Yorubaland for centuries, whose Alaafin was one of the great sovereigns of West Africa, whose cavalry ranged far and wide and whose tribute flowed in from a hundred subject towns. Oyo did not fall because a stronger empire conquered it. Oyo fell because of a quarrel between a king and a general, because of the revolt of one ambitious and aggrieved commander named Afonja, whose rebellion cracked the empire at its foundation and let loose the forces that would tear it, and all of Yorubaland, apart. The day Oyo trembled was the day Afonja said no to his king, and the tremor never stopped until the whole edifice had come down."}, {"id": "ologun-afonja#s2", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Afonja was the Aare Ona Kakanfo, the supreme field commander of the empire, the generalissimo who led Oyo’s armies in war, and he held his power at the great frontier town of Ilorin, a strategic place on the northern edge of Yoruba country where the savannah met the forest, where the empire touched the wider world of the Muslim north. This was a position of enormous power, and Afonja was a man of enormous ambition, and the two together made him dangerous to any king. For the tension between the Alaafin, the sacred sovereign in his capital, and the Kakanfo, the mighty general in the field, was built into the very structure of the empire, a tension that wise kings and loyal generals managed but that a bad turn could inflame into open war. And in Afonja’s day the turn was very bad indeed."}, {"id": "ologun-afonja#s3", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "The details of the quarrel are tangled in the traditions, as such things always are, matters of insult and command and the old poisoned relationship between the throne and the Kakanfo. But the heart of it is simple enough. Afonja, powerful, ambitious, and aggrieved, came to believe that the Alaafin meant to destroy him, or he simply saw his chance and reached for it, and he made the fatal choice. He revolted. He, the supreme commander of the empire’s armies, turned against the empire itself, refused the authority of the Alaafin, and set himself up at Ilorin as an independent power. It was the gravest blow the empire could suffer, its own war-chief in open rebellion, and it announced to every ambitious man in every subject town that the center could be defied, that the great Oyo was not invincible after all."}, {"id": "ologun-afonja#s4", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "To understand how deep the quarrel ran, and why Afonja is remembered as a great fighter and not merely a rebel, you must go back to the moment that lit the fuse, and it was a moment built on an old and murderous custom of the Oyo court. For it was the dark habit of some Alaafins, when they wished to be rid of a Kakanfo who had grown too powerful or too troublesome, to send him on a campaign designed to kill him, an errand of death dressed as an honor. The unwritten rule was iron. A Kakanfo who was sent to take a town and failed to take it was required to die, to take his own life rather than return in defeat. So the order to attack an impregnable place was, in truth, a sentence of death, a way for a jealous king to destroy his general while keeping his own hands clean. And this was the trap the Alaafin set for Afonja. He was ordered to march against Iwere Ile."}, {"id": "ologun-afonja#s5", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Iwere Ile was no ordinary target. It was a town of great natural strength, set among hills, well defended, and, more to the point, it was a place bound up with the Oyo royal house itself, a town connected to the very mothers of the kings, so sacred and so protected that to attack it was close to sacrilege, and to take it was thought impossible. The order was a masterpiece of malice. If Afonja obeyed and failed, as he was surely meant to fail, he would be bound by the code to kill himself. If he obeyed and by some miracle succeeded, he would have committed an outrage against the sacred heart of the dynasty. Either way the Alaafin would be rid of him. Afonja saw the trap for exactly what it was, and he made the choice that changed history. He refused. He would not march to his own scripted death. He would not put his head into the noose the king had tied for him. The great Kakanfo said no to his sovereign, and in that refusal the long unraveling began."}, {"id": "ologun-afonja#s6", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "But Afonja was no mere mutineer sulking in his tent, and here the iron in him showed. Around the same arc of events stands the sacking of Apomu, and it reveals the fighter he was and the pride that drove him. Apomu was a great market town, a famous trading center, and there had been an outrage there, an insult or a violation touching the honor of the Oyo, in some tellings the seizing or selling of persons who should never have been touched, a breach of the sacred peace of the market and of the dignity of the empire. Afonja, as the empire’s sword, fell upon Apomu and destroyed it, sacking the town in punishment and in fury. It was a demonstration of his power and his willingness to use it, a warning to all who thought the Kakanfo could be trifled with. Afonja could break a great town when he chose. The man the king was trying to murder by proxy was one of the most formidable soldiers in the land, and he was done being anyone’s disposable instrument."}, {"id": "ologun-afonja#s7", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "From that refusal, matters could only move one way, toward open and total rupture. Afonja did not merely disobey a single order and then wait quietly for the king’s pardon. He crossed the line from disobedience into rebellion, and then from rebellion into something even greater, a declaration that he and Ilorin would no longer bow to Oyo at all. He threw off the authority of the Alaafin entirely, declaring Ilorin independent of the empire, refusing its commands, withholding its due, and standing as a sovereign power in his own right on the empire’s northern flank. This was not a sulk. This was secession, the deliberate tearing away of one of the most important towns and one of the most powerful offices from the body of the empire, and it struck at the very foundation of Oyo’s authority, because if the Kakanfo himself could renounce the Alaafin and make it stick, then the whole edifice of obedience on which the empire rested was exposed as hollow."}, {"id": "ologun-afonja#s8", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "And Afonja went further than declaring himself free. He turned the tables on the throne so completely that, in the memory of the tradition, it was the king’s authority that was expelled rather than his own. Where once the Alaafin’s word had reached into Ilorin and commanded it, now the Alaafin’s reach was thrown back, his officials and his influence driven out, his sovereignty over that whole region simply erased. The town that had been a loyal frontier fortress of the empire became instead a dagger pointed at its heart, an independent power openly defying the capital and drawing to itself all the discontented and the ambitious and the armed. For a proud old empire accustomed to the trembling obedience of a hundred subject towns, to have its own supreme general expel its authority from a great city and live to boast of it was a humiliation and a mortal danger at once, and every other vassal and chief across Yorubaland watched and drew the obvious lesson, that the day of unquestioned Oyo supremacy was ending."}, {"id": "ologun-afonja#s9", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "For a time, it must be said plainly, Afonja won. This is the part too often swallowed up by the story of his fall, but the fighter deserves his due. In the years of his defiance he was, by the measure of raw power, triumphant. He had refused the death the king designed for him and lived. He had punished Apomu and shown his strength. He had declared his independence and made it hold. He had gathered a formidable army and beaten back or overawed the forces sent against him, and he sat at Ilorin as a great lord in his own right, feared across the land, the man who had defied the mighty Alaafin and prospered. Had the story ended there, Afonja would be remembered simply as the bold general who broke free of a treacherous king and carved out his own dominion, a hero of self-liberation. It did not end there, and the reason it did not is the tragedy that the rest of the tale unfolds, the summoned fire that would consume the very hand that lit it."}, {"id": "ologun-afonja#s10", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "But Afonja needed strength to make his revolt stick, more strength than he could raise from Ilorin alone against the still-formidable power of Oyo, and here he made the decision that would destroy him and change the history of the whole region. He reached out for allies among the Muslim north, calling to his side the Fulani and the Hausa fighters and the Muslim scholars and the growing movement of Islamic reform and jihad that was sweeping across the savannah in that era. He welcomed a charismatic Fulani cleric to Ilorin, and he called to himself the jama, the Muslim fighters, many of them slaves and freedmen drawn from the Yoruba towns themselves, promising freedom and plunder, and they came, flocking to Ilorin, a growing army of committed and battle-hardened fighters bound together by faith and by the hope of a new order. With this force at his back, Afonja was for a time immensely powerful, and Oyo could not bring him down."}, {"id": "ologun-afonja#s11", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "The trouble with calling in a fire to fight your enemy is that fire does not know whose house to burn. The forces Afonja had gathered had their own leaders, their own faith, their own ambitions, and their own momentum, and they did not, in the end, exist to serve the personal quarrel of a Yoruba general. As the movement at Ilorin grew, power slipped from Afonja’s hands into the hands of the very allies he had summoned. The Fulani leadership and the Muslim fighters came to see that they did not need Afonja, that they could rule Ilorin themselves, that his usefulness was ending. And so, in one of the great turns of treachery in Yoruba history, the allies Afonja had called in turned upon him. He who had raised the fire was consumed by it. Afonja was killed by his own confederates, cut down, the traditions vividly say, in a hail of arrows so thick that his body was held upright by the shafts, or burned upon the pile of arrows that felled him, a horrifying end for the mighty Kakanfo who had thought to use others and found himself used and discarded."}, {"id": "ologun-afonja#s12", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "And with Afonja dead, Ilorin did not return to Oyo. It passed instead fully into the hands of his former allies, becoming a Fulani emirate, an outpost of the Sokoto Caliphate, a Muslim state carved out of the northern edge of Yorubaland, the one Yoruba-region city to fall permanently under the rule of the northern jihad. From this base at Ilorin, the forces that Afonja had unleashed pressed southward against the heartland of Oyo itself, and the empire, already cracked by the revolt, now faced an enemy on its very border that it had, through Afonja’s folly, helped to create. The cavalry came down from Ilorin, and Oyo, weakened and divided, could not hold. In time the great capital of Old Oyo itself, the ancient seat of the empire, was abandoned and destroyed, its people fleeing south, the mighty city left to the grass and the ruin, and the empire that had ruled for centuries simply ceased to exist as a power. The tremor that began with Afonja’s revolt had brought down the whole mountain."}, {"id": "ologun-afonja#s13", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "And yet the Yoruba did not simply accept the loss of Ilorin. This too belongs in the story, because the fall of the town to the north was not the end but the beginning of a long and bitter struggle, generations of war fought to push the invader back and to reclaim what Afonja’s folly had opened up. For once Ilorin had become a base of the northern jihad, its cavalry did not rest. It pressed south again and again, raiding deep into Yoruba country, and the Yoruba, though they had lost their old empire, gathered what strength remained and fought back. The greatest and most famous of these early efforts was the campaign remembered as the Eleduwe war, when a grand coalition of Yoruba forces, together with allies, marched north with the aim of crushing Ilorin once and for all and driving the horsemen out of the land. It was a mighty effort, carrying the hopes of a wounded people who wanted their frontier back and their honor restored."}, {"id": "ologun-afonja#s14", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "But the war went badly, and its failure sealed the northern loss for good. The great coalition was defeated before Ilorin, broken in a disaster that cost the Yoruba dearly and left Ilorin more firmly established than ever as a permanent emirate on their soil, a piece of the Sokoto Caliphate lodged in the north of Yorubaland where it remains to this day. The horsemen of Ilorin, riding the open country of the savannah, held an advantage that the Yoruba, whose strength lay in the forest, could not easily overcome on that ground, and campaign after campaign broke against the town without dislodging it. The dream of simply reconquering Ilorin and erasing the wound faded, and a hard new reality took its place, a standing frontier of conflict between the Muslim north and the Yoruba south that would shape the wars of the whole century."}, {"id": "ologun-afonja#s15", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "In time it fell instead to a different power to finally halt the southward pressure. It was the rising war-city of Ibadan, the great forest camp built by the refugees of the fallen empire, that took up the burden of shielding Yorubaland from the Ilorin cavalry, and at last, at the famous battle fought on the hills at Osogbo, the Ibadan forces met the horsemen of Ilorin on ground that favored the defenders and dealt them a decisive defeat, checking their advance and marking the limit of how far the north could push. Ilorin was never reclaimed for the Yoruba, and it never would be, but its expansion was finally stopped, and the line that Afonja’s revolt had torn open was, after long bloodshed, held at last. So the story of the reclaiming wars ends not in triumph but in a hard, costly stalemate, a permanent border drawn in blood, and it stands as the long and bitter aftermath of one man’s fateful refusal, the price paid over generations for the day the Kakanfo broke faith with his king and let the fire in."}, {"id": "ologun-afonja#s16", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "The consequences of Afonja’s revolt cannot be overstated, for it was not merely the fall of one empire but the opening of the whole tragic age that followed. With Oyo gone, there was no longer any central power to keep the peace across Yorubaland, and the country dissolved into a century of war, town against town, warlord against warlord, the age of Ijaye and Ibadan and the endless campaigns, the age when the salt roads ran with refugees and the slave ships were fed by Yoruba selling Yoruba. The refugees from the fallen north streamed south and founded the new cities in the forest. The whole map of the Yoruba world was redrawn in blood. And at the end of that long agony, exhausted and shattered, the Yoruba fell one region at a time under the colonial power that arrived to impose the peace they could no longer make for themselves. All of it, the whole cascade, traces back to the crack that Afonja opened when he rose against his king."}, {"id": "ologun-afonja#s17", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So the elders keep the story of Afonja, and they keep it as one of the sharpest warnings in all their history, a warning aimed straight at the ambitious and the aggrieved. Afonja had a real grievance, perhaps, and real power, and a real chance to make himself great. And he threw the whole world into ruin, and destroyed himself in the bargain, because his ambition was larger than his loyalty and his cunning was larger than his wisdom. The lesson the elders draw is double. First, that the man who puts his own ambition above the good of his people can bring down far more than he ever meant to, that the general who breaks the empire to spite his king may find he has broken the world his own children must live in. And second, the bitter lesson of the summoned fire, that to call in a greater power to win your private quarrel is to invite your own destruction, for the ally who is strong enough to save you is strong enough to devour you, and will."}, {"id": "ologun-afonja#s18", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "Afonja is remembered, then, not as a hero and not quite as a mere villain, but as a tragic and terrible figure, a mighty man whose choices doomed him and his nation together. There is even a kind of grim pity in the telling, for he was betrayed in the end by the very people he had trusted, and died a horrible death, and got for all his ambition not a throne but a grave and an eternal reputation as the man who broke Oyo. The Yoruba do not forget him, and they do not forgive the folly, and they tell his story to every generation that produces its own ambitious men, its own aggrieved and powerful figures tempted to put self above nation and to call in dangerous friends to win their fights. Remember Afonja, the elders say. Remember the day Oyo trembled, and why. Remember the arrows that held him upright in death. And before you break something great to serve yourself, be very sure you can live in the ruin you will make."}]}, {"id": "ologun-kurunmi", "emoji": "🗡️", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#2a0500,#6a1400)", "category": "aare", "rating": 9.6, "duration": "10 min", "title": {"en": "Kurunmi of Ijaye: The Lion Who Would Not Bend", "yo": "Kurunmi"}, "desc": {"en": "The Aare Ona Kakanfo who built Ijaye and fought a doomed war against Ibadan for a law he would not betray.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Aare Ona Kakanfo", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Aare Ona Kakanfo, the story of the last great war-marshal of the old kind, who fought a doomed and heroic war for a law he would not betray)", "segments": [{"id": "ologun-kurunmi#s1", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Gather close, my children, for tonight we sit with a lion. His name was Kurunmi, the Aare Ona Kakanfo of Ijaye, and of all the war-marshals whose stories we tell, none was more purely a man of the old iron than he. He rose in the broken age after Afonja, when the great empire of Oyo lay shattered and the Yoruba country had become a field of contending war-cities. And he died fighting for a principle so old and so pure that the new world had no more room for it. Kurunmi is the story of a man who would rather break than bend."}, {"id": "ologun-kurunmi#s2", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "First, my children, understand the world he was born into, for it was not the world of Oyabi. The old Oyo had fallen. You remember from the stories of the kings how the Alaafin Atiba gathered the scattered remnant of the royal house and built a New Oyo further south, and how a new order of great war-cities arose from the wreckage: Ibadan, that camp of warriors that grew into a mighty power, and Ijaye, and Abeokuta, and the rest. This was an age of soldiers. The old sacred balance was gone. Power now belonged to whoever could raise and hold an army, and the whole country was a chessboard of ambitious captains."}, {"id": "ologun-kurunmi#s3", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Into this age rose Kurunmi, and he made Ijaye great. He was a general of the front rank, a commander of enormous ability, and under his hand Ijaye became one of the most powerful of the war-cities, its army feared across the land. When the Alaafin Atiba rebuilt the offices of the old empire in his New Oyo, he gave to Kurunmi the greatest of the military titles: he made him the Aare Ona Kakanfo, the supreme war-marshal, the heir of Kokoro Igangan and Oyabi and, yes, of Afonja too. And Kurunmi took that title not as an empty honor but as a sacred trust. Here is the key to the whole man, my children, so mark it well. Kurunmi believed in the law. He believed in the old constitution of Oyo the way a priest believes in his god. And that belief, that stubborn, magnificent, unbending belief, would lead him straight to his doom."}, {"id": "ologun-kurunmi#s4", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Now I must tell you of the quarrel that made his war, and it is a quarrel about a law of succession. Bear with me, for it matters. By the ancient custom of Oyo, when an Alaafin died, his eldest son, the Aremo, who had shared in his father’s power during his life, was expected to die with him. The son did not inherit the throne. This was a hard law, a terrible law even, but it was an old law, and its purpose was wise: it prevented an over-mighty crown prince from simply seizing the throne, and it kept the choosing of the new king in the hands of the kingmakers rather than making it a thing of mere blood inheritance. The Aremo went to join his father in death, and the kingmakers chose a new Alaafin from among the eligible princes."}, {"id": "ologun-kurunmi#s5", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "When the Alaafin Atiba came to the end of his life, he did not want this law to fall upon his own beloved son, Adelu. So Atiba worked to change the custom, to have it agreed that his son Adelu should live and inherit the throne rather than die. The men of Ibadan, the rising power, supported this change, for it suited them. And so when Atiba died, Adelu did not die with him. Adelu became the Alaafin."}, {"id": "ologun-kurunmi#s6", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "But Kurunmi would not have it. Kurunmi, the Aare Ona Kakanfo, the guardian of the old law, declared that this was a violation of the sacred constitution of Oyo. The Aremo must die; the son must not inherit; the law was the law and no king’s love could unmake it. Kurunmi refused to recognize Adelu as the legitimate Alaafin. And when Ijaye refused to submit, the quarrel that had begun over a point of ancient law became a war, the great and terrible Ijaye War, with Ibadan marching against Kurunmi’s Ijaye."}, {"id": "ologun-kurunmi#s7", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "Now here, my children, is where you must hold two truths at once, as I have always taught you. Was Kurunmi right? In the narrow letter of the old law, perhaps he was. The custom he defended was real and ancient. But the world had moved. The old sacred order that gave that law its meaning had already died with the old Oyo. Kurunmi was defending the roof of a house whose foundations were already dust. He was, in a sense, gloriously and tragically out of his time, a man of pure old iron in an age of shifting bronze."}, {"id": "ologun-kurunmi#s8", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "And yet, is there not something magnificent in a man who will fight and die rather than see a law he holds sacred be bent for the convenience of the powerful? In an age when everyone else was cutting deals and shifting alliances for advantage, Kurunmi stood like a rock and said: no. This is the law. I will not bend it, though it cost me everything. There is a terrible grandeur in that, my children, even when the cause is doomed. The Ijaye War was long and bitter, and it drew in the great powers of the whole country. Ibadan besieged Ijaye. Kurunmi, that master soldier, defended his city with all his old skill, and for a long time he held. Abeokuta and others were drawn into the widening struggle. The war ground on, and it consumed men and towns and seasons, one of the great conflicts of that whole age of war-cities."}, {"id": "ologun-kurunmi#s9", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "But slowly, inexorably, the tide turned against Ijaye. And now the story darkens toward its end, and I must tell you of the grief that broke the lion’s heart before the spear ever reached him. Kurunmi had sons, brave sons, war-captains like their father. And in the fighting of that long war, they fell. The accounts our fathers passed down tell us that Kurunmi lost his sons in battle, several of them, cut down in the defense of the cause their father would not abandon. Imagine it, my children. Imagine the old lion, master of a hundred battles, receiving the news of son after son fallen for his stubborn principle. Imagine the weight of that. The law he defended was costing him not only his city but his own blood, his own children, the future of his own house."}, {"id": "ologun-kurunmi#s10", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "And the accounts tell us that this grief, piled upon the failing fortunes of the war, broke him at last. The proud old Aare Ona Kakanfo, who had never bent to any man, was bent at last by sorrow. It is said that Kurunmi died in the course of that war, broken by grief and by the ruin of his cause, and that his beloved Ijaye fell soon after and was destroyed and scattered, its people fleeing as refugees to Abeokuta and elsewhere. So passed the great war-city, and so passed its lion. Now let us weigh him, for that is why we tell the tale. What shall we say of Kurunmi?"}, {"id": "ologun-kurunmi#s11", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "We shall not call him a fool, though his cause was doomed. We shall call him faithful, faithful to a law and an order he loved, faithful past the point of wisdom, faithful unto death. He is the last great Aare Ona Kakanfo of the old kind, the last to fight a war in the old spirit, for a sacred principle rather than for mere advantage. After him the world would change utterly, for the guns of a new empire, the British, were already gathering on the horizon, and soon no war-marshal, however brave, could stand against them."}, {"id": "ologun-kurunmi#s12", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "Kurunmi’s tragedy is the tragedy of the faithful man in a faithless age. He would not bend, and so he broke. But there are those, my children, and I am one of them, who would rather be remembered as the rock that broke than as the reed that bent to every wind. When you must choose what to be faithful to, choose wisely, for faith to a dying law will cost you as dearly as faith to a living one. But when you have chosen, and when the cause is just, then be like Kurunmi: stand, and do not bend, and let them remember that you did not bend."}, {"id": "ologun-kurunmi#s13", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "And let me add one honest word, as I always do at this fire. The wars of Kurunmi’s age, the Ijaye War and all the long wars of the war-cities, were terrible for our people. They filled the roads with refugees and the markets of the coast with captives, for this was still the age of the slave-trade, and the endless warring of Yoruba against Yoruba fed those ships with our own children. We do not tell these stories to glorify war. We tell them to remember, honestly, both the grandeur and the grief, both the lion’s courage and the ruin his age poured upon the land. Kurunmi was magnificent, and his age was a catastrophe, and both of those things are true."}, {"id": "ologun-kurunmi#s14", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "Tomorrow, we sit with Latoosa of Ibadan, the last of the great fighting Aare of the old sort, in whose lifetime the guns of the white men would close their grip upon the whole Yoruba country, and the long age of the war-marshals would come to its end."}]}, {"id": "ologun-oyatope", "emoji": "🗡️", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#2a0500,#6a1400)", "category": "aare", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "7 min", "title": {"en": "Oyatope of Iwoye: The Second Sword, Keeper of the Young Office", "yo": "Oyatope"}, "desc": {"en": "The second generalissimo of Oyo, who steadied the young office.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Aare Ona Kakanfo", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Aare Ona Kakanfo, the story of the second generalissimo of Oyo, who followed Kokoro Igangan and steadied the new office in its early days)", "segments": [{"id": "ologun-oyatope#s1", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Come and sit, my children, for a good storyteller does not leap over the small steps to reach the loud ones. Last we spoke of Kokoro Igangan of Iwoye, the very first Aare Ona Kakanfo, the man who first carried the sword of the whole nation. But a single man does not make a tradition. A tradition is made when a second man takes up what the first laid down and carries it on, and then a third, and a fourth, until the thing becomes not one man’s greatness but the settled custom of a people. Tonight we honor that second man. His name was Oyatope, and like the first, he came from the town of Iwoye."}, {"id": "ologun-oyatope#s2", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Now I must be honest with you at once, as I always am at this fire, for honesty is the marrow of a true telling. The records of Oyatope are thin. He lived in the early age of the office, when the great chroniclers who would one day write down our history had not yet set their pens to the deeds of the war-marshals. His battles are not remembered to us name by name. His victories have been worn smooth by the long river of time, the way the current polishes a stone until you can no longer see the marks the chisel first made."}, {"id": "ologun-oyatope#s3", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "I will not do what a lazy or a boastful storyteller does, which is to invent grand deeds and hang them on a name to make the tale swell. That is a kind of lie, and lies dishonor the ancestors far more than honest silence ever could. What I can give you of Oyatope is the truth of his place, and that truth is worth more than a hundred invented battles. Here is his place. Oyatope was the second Aare Ona Kakanfo. He came from Iwoye, the same town that gave the office its first holder, and this itself tells us something. It tells us that in those early days the office had roots, that certain towns bred the kind of hard, capable war-captains from whom a generalissimo could be drawn. Iwoye was such a town, a nursery of soldiers, and Oyatope was its second gift to the empire’s high command."}, {"id": "ologun-oyatope#s4", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Consider, my children, what it means to be the second of anything great, for there is a hidden difficulty in it that the first man never faces. The first man is remembered simply for being first. His name shines because it stands at the head of the line. But the second man labors in a harder light. If he does well, people say, ah, he only continued what the first began. If he does poorly, people say, see, the first was the only good one, the office was a fluke. The second man must prove that the thing is not a single accident but a real and repeatable strength. He must show that the office of the Aare Ona Kakanfo can pass from hand to hand and lose none of its power in the passing."}, {"id": "ologun-oyatope#s5", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "This, I believe, is the true work Oyatope did, whatever the particular battles were. He proved that the office could survive its founder. He took the sword that Kokoro Igangan had first carried, and he carried it in his turn, and he handed it on, and by doing so he turned a single man’s appointment into an enduring institution. The kingmakers and the Alaafin looked at Oyatope and saw that the office worked a second time, and so they trusted it a third time, and a fourth, and the line grew strong."}, {"id": "ologun-oyatope#s6", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Let me draw the lesson out for you, for it is a lesson for every family and every craft and every people, not only for empires. The founder gets the songs. But it is the faithful successor, the second and the third and the tenth, the ones whose names may even be half forgotten, who turn a bright beginning into a lasting inheritance. A single great harvest does not make a farm. It is the steady planting, season after season, by hands whose names the market never learns, that feeds the generations."}, {"id": "ologun-oyatope#s7", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "So when I tell you that Oyatope’s deeds are dim to us, do not hear that as a small thing. Hear it rightly. He belonged to the quiet, load-bearing generation, the ones who hold up the roof so that later men can dance beneath it. The empire of Oyo would one day reach a height under war-marshals like Oyabi, whose story we have told, where the arm of Oyo struck as far as the northern country. But that height rested upon foundations laid by men like Oyatope, who kept the office alive and honorable in the years when it was still young and might easily have withered."}, {"id": "ologun-oyatope#s8", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "And there is one more thing I will say of him, drawn not from a list of battles but from the very nature of the office he held. To be Aare Ona Kakanfo in that age was to live under the terrible custom I told you of before: that the war-marshal must conquer, or die in the field, or take his own life rather than carry defeat home. Oyatope lived under that shadow every day of his tenure. Every campaign was a wager of his own life against the honor of his office. That he held the title, and passed it on, tells us he did not fail in the way that custom would not forgive. He met the demand of the office. He kept its honor whole."}, {"id": "ologun-oyatope#s9", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So we honor Oyatope of Iwoye tonight, not with invented glories, but with the true and sufficient honor of the faithful second. He steadied the young office. He proved the line could hold. He carried the nation’s sword through his own age without disgrace and handed it on unbroken. In the long roll of the Aare Ona Kakanfo, from the first to the one who holds the title in our own day, Oyatope is the second knot in the rope, and a rope, my children, is only as trustworthy as its second knot is after its first. He held. That is enough. That is, in truth, a great deal. Tomorrow, we travel to the town of Jabata, which gave the empire two of its war-marshals, and we sit first with Adeta, another of the early generalissimos who kept the sword of Oyo bright in the generations before the empire reached its noon."}]}, {"id": "ologun-adeta", "emoji": "🗡️", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#2a0500,#6a1400)", "category": "aare", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "7 min", "title": {"en": "Adeta of Jabata: The War-Marshal from the Town of Soldiers", "yo": "Adeta"}, "desc": {"en": "The early generalissimo from the soldier-town of Jabata.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Aare Ona Kakanfo", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Aare Ona Kakanfo, the story of the early generalissimo of Jabata who served the empire in the age of its climbing strength)", "segments": [{"id": "ologun-adeta#s1", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Draw near, my children, for tonight our road brings us to the town of Jabata, a place that would give the empire not one but two of its supreme war-marshals. That alone tells you what kind of town it was. As Iwoye had bred Kokoro Igangan and Oyatope, so Jabata bred hard men who could lead the armies of a nation. Tonight we sit with the first of Jabata’s two gifts to the office, a war-marshal remembered by the name Adeta."}, {"id": "ologun-adeta#s2", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "And again I begin, as an honest griot must, by telling you the shape of what we know and what we do not. Adeta belongs to the earlier and middle age of the Aare Ona Kakanfo, before the great chroniclers had begun to set down the doings of the war-marshals in careful order. His particular campaigns, the names of the towns he humbled, the count of his victories, these the centuries have not preserved for us in full. I could stand here and invent a mighty war for him, complete with the number of the slain and the name of the defeated chief, and you might not know the difference. But I would know, and the ancestors would know, and so I will not. A name honestly kept is worth more than a legend falsely built."}, {"id": "ologun-adeta#s3", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "What I can give you, and give you truly, is his place in the great line and the meaning of the office he bore. Adeta was an Aare Ona Kakanfo of Oyo, a man of Jabata, and he carried the sword of the whole nation in his generation. To understand him, you must understand what that burden was, for every man who held it, remembered or forgotten, carried the same terrible weight."}, {"id": "ologun-adeta#s4", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Remember the custom, my children, for it never changed. The Aare Ona Kakanfo was forbidden to dwell in the same city as the Alaafin. He ruled his own town like a prince. He commanded the whole military might of the empire, the dreaded cavalry, the sworn war-captains, the levies of a hundred towns. And over him hung always the hardest law of any office in the land: he must win. Victory was the only honorable homecoming. To be defeated and yet return alive was a disgrace that custom would not wash clean. So the Aare must conquer, or fall in the field, or die by his own hand rather than carry shame to his people. That Adeta held the office, and is remembered as one who held it, means he lived beneath that law and did not fall under its judgment. He met the demand. He kept the honor of Jabata and of the sword whole in his time."}, {"id": "ologun-adeta#s5", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "Let me tell you of the age he served in, for a man is shaped by his age as a blade is shaped by the fire and the hammer. Adeta’s Oyo was a climbing power, building the cavalry arm that would one day make it the terror of the grasslands. The wealth of the empire flowed from tribute and from the trade routes that ran north to the desert lands and south toward the coast. And a climbing empire is an empire always at war, for tribute must be enforced, and rebellious towns must be brought to heel, and ambitious neighbors must be reminded of the strength of Oyo’s arm."}, {"id": "ologun-adeta#s6", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "In such an age the Aare Ona Kakanfo was rarely idle. When the Alaafin in his sacred palace decreed that a lesson must be taught, it was the war-marshal who rode out to teach it. Adeta was one of the hands by which the empire made its will felt in the world. The kings gave the word; men like Adeta turned the word into iron. Now I want to draw a lesson from Adeta’s very obscurity, for there is wisdom in it. Our people remember his name and his town and his office, but not his battles. And yet without the men like him, the famous ones we do remember, the Oyabis and the Latoosas, would have had no tradition to inherit, no office grown strong and respected by long use. The bright names of history stand upon the shoulders of a crowd of half-remembered men who did their duty in their day and passed the trust along."}, {"id": "ologun-adeta#s7", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "This is true in every life, my children. For every name that the market shouts, there are a hundred faithful hands whose work made that name possible, the grandmother who held the family together, the elder who kept the peace between quarreling houses, the farmer who fed the town through the lean year. History is loud about a few and silent about the many, but it is the many who carry the world. Adeta is one of that great company. Honor him as such."}, {"id": "ologun-adeta#s8", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "And notice, too, what Jabata’s giving of two war-marshals tells us about the old order. The office of Aare Ona Kakanfo was not the property of one town or one family. It moved. It was given by the Alaafin and the kingmakers to the man judged fittest to carry it, and it could come to Iwoye twice, or to Jabata twice, or to Ogbomoso, or to Ibadan, wherever a great soldier arose worthy of the trust. This was a wise thing. It kept the office from becoming the hereditary tool of a single ambitious house. The sword served the nation, not a dynasty. Adeta held it in his turn and returned it to the nation when his turn was done."}, {"id": "ologun-adeta#s9", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So we honor Adeta of Jabata tonight with the honor that is true and sufficient: the honor of the faithful war-marshal of the middle age. He came from a town of soldiers. He carried the nation’s sword under the terrible law of victory-or-death, and he did not fall under its judgment. He kept the tradition alive and handed it on unbroken. His battles are dim to us, but his fidelity is not. He was one of the strong knots in the long rope that runs from the first Aare to the one who holds the title in our own day. Tomorrow, we remain in Jabata a little longer, for that town of soldiers gave the empire a second war-marshal, and we sit with Oku, the other son of Jabata to carry the sword of Oyo."}]}, {"id": "ologun-oku", "emoji": "🗡️", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#2a0500,#6a1400)", "category": "aare", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "8 min", "title": {"en": "Oku of Jabata: The Second Son of the Soldier-Town", "yo": "Oku"}, "desc": {"en": "The second Aare Ona Kakanfo from Jabata, in the empire's warring years.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Aare Ona Kakanfo", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Aare Ona Kakanfo, the story of the second generalissimo from Jabata, who carried the nation’s sword in the empire’s warring years)", "segments": [{"id": "ologun-oku#s1", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Sit again, my children, for we have not yet left the town of Jabata. Last night we honored Adeta, the first of Jabata’s war-marshals. Tonight we honor the second, a man remembered by the name Oku. When a single town gives an empire two supreme commanders, it is no accident of chance. It is the fruit of a certain spirit that lives in a place, a hardness, a discipline, a way of raising boys into men who do not flinch. Jabata had that spirit, and Oku was its second gift to the high command of Oyo."}, {"id": "ologun-oku#s2", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "And once more, my children, I open with honesty, for I will not trade the truth for a prettier tale. Oku belongs to the earlier stretch of the long line, before the careful chroniclers came, and the particular record of his wars has been worn thin by time. I cannot recite for you the roll of towns he conquered or the names of the chiefs who bowed to him, because those things were carried in the memories of men who are long silent, and much of what they knew went with them into the ground. What survives is his name, his town, and his office, and from these three true things a faithful storyteller can still draw the marrow of a man."}, {"id": "ologun-oku#s3", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "So let us draw it. Oku was Aare Ona Kakanfo of Oyo, of the town of Jabata, in the age when the empire was a great and warring power. Think about what it meant that the office came a second time to his town. It meant that when the Alaafin and the kingmakers looked across the whole broad country of the Yoruba for a man fit to carry the nation’s sword, their eyes fell once more upon Jabata. That is a testament not only to Oku but to the school of war that his town had become. He had grown up, we may be sure, among men who remembered Adeta, among warriors who knew the weight of the great office and the discipline it demanded. He was formed by that inheritance and then judged worthy to carry it himself."}, {"id": "ologun-oku#s4", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Let me speak now of what the office asked of any man who bore it, Oku no less than the rest, for this is the truest way to honor a war-marshal whose battles are dim. The Aare Ona Kakanfo commanded the entire military strength of the empire. When he took the field, he took it not as a village champion but as the sword-arm of a nation that stretched across the grasslands. He led the cavalry that was Oyo’s terror and pride, thousands of horsemen who could cross the savanna faster than warning could travel. He led the Eso, the sworn war-captains who had vowed never to turn their backs to an enemy. And he carried, every single day, the crushing law of his office: conquer, or die in the field, or take your own life rather than bring defeat home."}, {"id": "ologun-oku#s5", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "Do you understand what it is to live under such a law, my children? Every campaign was a wager of his own life, not merely against the enemy’s spear, but against the shame that custom would not forgive. A lesser office allows a man to fail and try again. The office of Aare Ona Kakanfo allowed no such mercy. That Oku held it, and is counted among the honored line rather than among the fallen and disgraced, tells us plainly: he did not fail in the way his people could not forgive. He met the demand. He kept the honor of Jabata and of the sword."}, {"id": "ologun-oku#s6", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Now here is a lesson I want you to carry from Oku and his town, and it is a lesson about the making of excellence. Jabata gave two war-marshals not because it was lucky but because it built the kind of men from whom war-marshals are made. Excellence is not a single lightning-flash. It is a tradition, cultivated, passed down, a fire kept burning from one generation to the next. When the young men of Jabata trained, they trained knowing that their town had produced an Aare Ona Kakanfo, that the highest office in the land was not a distant impossibility but a thing a son of Jabata had actually held. That knowledge lifts a people. It tells the young: this height is reachable, for one of our own has reached it."}, {"id": "ologun-oku#s7", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "So it is in every family and every town, my children. When one of your own rises, they do not only win glory for themselves. They widen the sky for everyone who comes after. They turn an impossible thing into a possible one merely by having done it. Adeta made Oku possible. And Oku, by succeeding in his turn, confirmed for all who came after that Jabata was a town where greatness could grow. That is a gift a person gives not only to their own name but to their whole people."}, {"id": "ologun-oku#s8", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "And let me place him honestly in the long unfolding, for that is the griot’s duty. Oku served in the age before the great fall, before Afonja’s ruinous revolt, before the empire cracked and the war-cities rose from the wreckage. His was the age of the empire’s strength, when the office he held was the terror of Oyo’s enemies rather than, as it would later become in Afonja’s hands, a danger to the throne itself. Oku belonged to the loyal, load-bearing generations who kept the sword faithful to the crown. Remember that, for it makes the later tragedy of Afonja sharper by contrast. There was nothing inevitable about a war-marshal turning against his king. For generations, men like Oku held enormous power and never dreamed of such a betrayal. The fall, when it came, was a fall from a real height of loyalty, not from a low place."}, {"id": "ologun-oku#s9", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So we honor Oku of Jabata, the second son of the soldier-town to carry the nation’s sword. His battles are dim, but his fidelity is bright. He proved that his town’s first war-marshal was no accident. He carried the terrible office under the law of victory-or-death and kept its honor whole. And he handed the sword on, unbroken, to the men who came after. In the long rope of the Aare Ona Kakanfo, Oku is another strong knot, quiet but firm, holding the line together through the warring years. Tomorrow, we leave the early age behind and move toward the storm. We will sit with the war-marshals of the age of the great fall and the war-cities, men like Toyeje of Ogbomoso, who held the sword in the terrible years when the old empire was breaking apart and the whole Yoruba world was sliding into the long night of war."}]}, {"id": "ologun-toyeje", "emoji": "🗡️", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#2a0500,#6a1400)", "category": "aare", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "8 min", "title": {"en": "Toyeje of Ogbomoso: The War-Marshal in the Hour of the Fall", "yo": "Toyeje"}, "desc": {"en": "The Ogbomoso generalissimo who held the middle as the old empire broke apart.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Aare Ona Kakanfo", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Aare Ona Kakanfo, the story of the generalissimo of Ogbomoso who carried the nation’s sword as the old empire was breaking apart)", "segments": [{"id": "ologun-toyeje#s1", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Come close, my children, and let the fire steady you, for tonight we sit with a man who held the great office in the worst of all times to hold it. His name was Toyeje, and his town was Ogbomoso, that famous town on the road to the north. He was Aare Ona Kakanfo in the very hour of the empire’s breaking, when Afonja’s revolt had cracked the old order and the whole Yoruba world was sliding toward the long night of war. To be a war-marshal in the age of victory, as Oyabi was, is one thing. To be a war-marshal in the age of collapse, when the ground itself is giving way beneath your feet, is a harder and a lonelier fate. That was Toyeje’s portion."}, {"id": "ologun-toyeje#s2", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Let me set the scene, for you cannot understand the man without the storm he stood in. You remember the story of Afonja, the Aare Ona Kakanfo of Ilorin who turned against his Alaafin and invited the northern power into the land. You remember how his revolt helped bring the tottering empire to its knees, and how the old center of Oyo could no longer hold. Well, in the churning of those years, as the great empire fractured, the office of Aare Ona Kakanfo itself became a contested and a wandering thing. And it came, in that troubled age, to Toyeje of Ogbomoso."}, {"id": "ologun-toyeje#s3", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Now here I must be careful and honest, for the age of the fall is a tangled one, and the accounts of it do not always agree. What our traditions hold is that Toyeje was a leading war-chief of the crumbling empire, that he held the high title of Aare Ona Kakanfo, and that he was among those who tried to rally the scattered strength of the Yoruba against the rising tide of Ilorin and the chaos of the collapse. He was a bridge-figure, my children, a man who stood between the old world that was dying and the new world of the war-cities that was struggling to be born."}, {"id": "ologun-toyeje#s4", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Think of what that meant in the doing. When an empire is strong, the war-marshal has a clear master, the Alaafin, and a clear enemy, whoever the king points him toward. But when an empire is falling, everything blurs. Who is the rightful king when the palace itself is shaken? Who is the enemy when yesterday’s ally is today’s rival and old friends carve the corpse of the empire among themselves? The war-marshal of a falling empire must fight not only with his spear but with his judgment, choosing again and again whom to trust, which cause to serve, which of many crumbling authorities still deserves the loyalty of his sword."}, {"id": "ologun-toyeje#s5", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "Toyeje lived in that fog. He led armies in the early wars that followed the collapse, the confused and bitter struggles as the old provinces fought over the inheritance of fallen Oyo. He tried, as the traditions remember, to hold something of the old order together, to keep the sword of the nation meaning something even as the nation itself came apart in his hands."}, {"id": "ologun-toyeje#s6", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "And I will tell you honestly, my children, that this was in large part a losing struggle, for no single man could have held back that flood. The forces tearing the empire apart were vaster than any war-marshal. The slave-trade had poisoned the whole land, making it profitable for Yoruba to war upon Yoruba and sell the captives to the ships at the coast. The guns that came up from the coast in exchange for those captives had changed the very nature of war. The northern power at Ilorin, born of Afonja’s fatal invitation, pressed steadily southward. Against all of this, Toyeje raised his sword. He did not stop the flood. No one could have. But he stood in it, and that standing has its own honor."}, {"id": "ologun-toyeje#s7", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Here is the lesson I want you to take from Toyeje, and it is a hard and grown-up lesson, not a child’s tale of easy triumph. There is a kind of greatness that shows itself in victory, and we have praised it in Oyabi. But there is another kind of greatness, quieter and sadder, that shows itself in loyalty to a losing cause, in the refusal to abandon your post merely because the walls are falling. Toyeje was called to be the war-marshal of a dying empire. He could have thrown down the title, seized what he could for himself, and become merely another warlord grabbing at the pieces. Many in that age did exactly that. Instead he tried, however imperfectly, to be what his office demanded, the sword of the whole nation, even when the nation was flying apart."}, {"id": "ologun-toyeje#s8", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "Not every worthy struggle is a winning one, my children. Sometimes the most honorable thing a person can do is to stand faithfully at a post that is doomed, to hold the line not because the line will hold but because holding the line is right. The soldier who guards the gate of a city that will fall anyway, the nurse who tends the sick in the season of plague, the elder who keeps trying to make peace in a quarrel that will not be quieted, these are the Toyejes of ordinary life. They will not always be remembered as victors. But they should be remembered as faithful."}, {"id": "ologun-toyeje#s9", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "And let me be honest, too, about the darkness of his age, for I will not paint the past in false gold. The wars that Toyeje fought in were terrible for our people. They filled the roads with refugees and the coast with captives. Towns that had stood for centuries were burned and their people scattered or sold. This was the beginning of the long agony that the Alaafin Awole had cursed the land to suffer when his kingdom was betrayed. Toyeje was a man doing his best inside a catastrophe, and we honor his effort without pretending the catastrophe was anything but what it was. That is the honest way to remember a fallen age: with sorrow for the suffering and respect for the few who tried to hold things together within it."}, {"id": "ologun-toyeje#s10", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So we honor Toyeje of Ogbomoso, the war-marshal of the breaking, the man who carried the nation’s sword in the hour when there was hardly a nation left to carry it for. His was not the clean glory of the age of strength. His was the harder, dimmer honor of faithfulness in the time of ruin. He stood at a doomed post and did not flee it. In the long line of the Aare Ona Kakanfo, he is the knot that held, barely, at the very place where the rope was fraying most, and by holding there he kept the line unbroken so that it could reach the days of Kurunmi and Latoosa and, in the end, our own time. Tomorrow, we sit with Edun of Gbagura, another of the war-chiefs who bore the great title in that same troubled age of the fall and the wars, as the map of the whole Yoruba country was being redrawn in fire."}]}, {"id": "ologun-edun", "emoji": "🗡️", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#2a0500,#6a1400)", "category": "aare", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "8 min", "title": {"en": "Edun of Gbagura: The War-Chief of the Egba Frontier", "yo": "Edun"}, "desc": {"en": "The Gbagura war-chief who bore the great title on the Egba frontier.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Aare Ona Kakanfo", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Aare Ona Kakanfo, the story of the generalissimo of Gbagura who bore the great title amid the wars of the scattering)", "segments": [{"id": "ologun-edun#s1", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Gather in, my children, for our journey through the line of war-marshals brings us tonight to Gbagura, one of the great sections of the Egba country, and to a war-chief remembered by the name Edun. He carried the high title of Aare Ona Kakanfo in the same troubled age as Toyeje, the age of the fall and the wars, when the old empire had shattered and the Yoruba peoples were being scattered and reforged in fire."}, {"id": "ologun-edun#s2", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "As always, I begin with the truth about what is remembered and what is not, for that honesty is the foundation of every tale I tell you. Edun of Gbagura belongs to that tangled and violent age when the office of Aare Ona Kakanfo was itself caught up in the general breaking of the old order. The full and orderly record of his deeds has not come down to us as cleanly as we might wish. But his name is held in the line, his town is remembered, and the office he bore is certain. From these true things, and from a faithful understanding of his age, we can honor him rightly."}, {"id": "ologun-edun#s3", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Let me tell you of Gbagura and the Egba, for a war-marshal is rooted in his people, and Edun’s people were passing through one of the great trials of our whole history in his day. The Egba were a cluster of many towns in the forest country to the south. In the age of Oyo’s strength, they had lived under the empire’s wide shadow. But as the empire fell and the wars spread, the Egba towns were battered by the same storms that were tearing the whole land apart. Out of that suffering would come one of the great acts of our people’s endurance: the gathering of the scattered Egba towns into a single new refuge, the founding of Abeokuta, the city under the great rock, where the broken pieces of the Egba nation came together to survive. Edun of Gbagura belonged to that world of the Egba, in the very generation of their trial and their gathering."}, {"id": "ologun-edun#s4", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Now think, my children, about what it meant to be a war-chief among a people who were fighting not for empire, not for tribute, not for glory, but simply for survival. The great war-marshals of the old strong Oyo, the Oyabis, fought to extend the reach of a mighty power. But the war-chiefs of the age of scattering fought a different and more desperate fight. They fought to keep their people alive. They fought to hold a refuge against the raiders. They fought so that there would still be an Egba people, a Yoruba people, when the long night finally lifted. That is a harder and a holier kind of soldiering, for the stakes are not glory but existence itself."}, {"id": "ologun-edun#s5", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Edun bore the title of Aare Ona Kakanfo in that age of desperate fighting. Whatever the particular battles that history has not preserved for us, this we can say with confidence: he was a war-leader of his people in the time of their greatest danger, honored with the highest military title the Yoruba world knew."}, {"id": "ologun-edun#s6", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "And let me draw out something important here about the office itself, for Edun’s story reveals it. In the age of the strong empire, there was one Aare Ona Kakanfo, clearly appointed by the one Alaafin, commanding the one army of the one empire. But as the empire shattered into contending powers, the great title became a thing that different centers of Yoruba power might claim or bestow, each rallying its own strength under its own war-marshal. This is why, in this tangled age, we find war-chiefs of different regions, an Ogbomoso man, a Gbagura man, each remembered as bearing the great title. The office had not one clean channel anymore but several, because the nation itself had broken into several streams."}, {"id": "ologun-edun#s7", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "There is a truth in that worth pausing on, my children. When the center holds, honor flows from one source and everyone knows their place. When the center breaks, honor and authority become contested, and many men may lay claim to the same high title, each with some justice. This is not necessarily corruption. It is what happens when a great unity comes apart and its pieces each try to carry on the tradition in their own corner. Edun of Gbagura carried the tradition in the Egba corner, in the age when there was no longer one clear center to carry it for the whole."}, {"id": "ologun-edun#s8", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Here is the lesson I would have you keep from Edun. When the great house falls, it is the ones who gather the survivors and defend the refuge who save a people’s future. Not the conquerors. The gatherers. The defenders of the last strong place. The Egba survived their age of scattering because men were willing to fight for the mere continuance of their people, and because they had the wisdom to come together at Abeokuta rather than be picked off one broken town at a time. Edun belonged to that generation of defenders and gatherers. His fighting was not for an empire’s pride but for a people’s life."}, {"id": "ologun-edun#s9", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "And this, my children, is worth remembering in your own lives, though I pray you never face so hard an age. There are seasons of building, when the task is to raise something grand. And there are seasons of preserving, when the task is merely to keep what matters alive through a bad time, so that it can flower again when the season turns. Do not despise the work of preserving. Sometimes to simply keep a family, a faith, a people, or a craft alive through a dark season is the greatest work a generation can do. Edun’s generation did that work for the Egba, and because they did, there is an Egba nation to this day."}, {"id": "ologun-edun#s10", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "And, as always, let me be honest about the shadow over the whole age. These were the years when the slave-trade and the endless wars fed each other in a terrible circle, when Yoruba was sold by Yoruba to the ships of the coast, when the roads were full of the captured and the fleeing. Edun fought inside that darkness, as all the war-chiefs of his age did. We honor his defense of his people without pretending the age was anything but a tragedy. That is the only honest way to remember it."}, {"id": "ologun-edun#s11", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So we honor Edun of Gbagura, war-marshal of the Egba in the age of the scattering, one of the defenders who fought so that a people might survive to see a better day. His battles are dim, but the cause he served, the endurance of his people, is written plainly in the fact that his people endured. In the long line of the Aare Ona Kakanfo, he is one of the knots tied in the broken years, holding a strand of the tradition together in the Egba country when the great rope of the empire had come apart. Tomorrow, we sit with Amepo of Abemo, another war-marshal of that same age of fire, before we come at last to the towering figures of the war-cities, to Kurunmi whom you have already met, and to the great Ojo Aburumaku and Latoosa who follow."}]}, {"id": "ologun-amepo", "emoji": "🗡️", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#2a0500,#6a1400)", "category": "aare", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "8 min", "title": {"en": "Amepo of Abemo: The War-Marshal and the Town That Fell", "yo": "Amepo"}, "desc": {"en": "The Abemo war-marshal of an age when whole towns were swallowed by war.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Aare Ona Kakanfo", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Aare Ona Kakanfo, the story of the generalissimo of Abemo, who bore the great title in the age when whole towns were swallowed by the wars)", "segments": [{"id": "ologun-amepo#s1", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Sit close tonight, my children, and let me tell you of Amepo of Abemo, a war-marshal whose very town-name carries a shadow in it. For Abemo was one of the towns that rose to importance in the churning age of the war-cities, and it was also one of the towns that the wars devoured. Amepo held the great office of Aare Ona Kakanfo in that fierce and unforgiving age, and his story, like his town’s, is bound up with both the height and the ruin that the wars could bring."}, {"id": "ologun-amepo#s2", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "As always, let me be honest with you first, for the honesty is the whole worth of the telling. The age of the war-cities was an age of many rising and falling powers, and the record of it is tangled, with towns swelling to greatness in one decade and being wiped from the map in the next. Amepo of Abemo belongs to this age. The full chronicle of his particular wars has not survived clear and whole. But his name is held among the war-marshals, his town of Abemo is remembered, and the age he lived in is one we understand well in its terrible outlines. From these things we can tell his story truly, without inventing false particulars."}, {"id": "ologun-amepo#s3", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Let me tell you what kind of age it was, for Amepo was made and unmade by it. After the fall of the old empire, as you have heard, the Yoruba country became a chessboard of contending war-cities. Ibadan rose, a mighty camp of warriors. Ijaye rose under Kurunmi. Abeokuta gathered the Egba. And many smaller towns, Abemo among them, rose too, each trying to hold its own place, to protect its people, to survive amid the endless raiding and warring. Some of these towns flourished. Others were besieged, taken, burned, their people slain or scattered or sold to the coast. This was the pitiless arithmetic of that age: to be strong was to survive, and to be weak, even for a season, was often to be erased."}, {"id": "ologun-amepo#s4", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Into this age Amepo rose to bear the great title of Aare Ona Kakanfo. Consider what courage and ability that required. To lead in such a time was to gamble your life and your people’s lives on every campaign, in a world where the losing side did not merely retreat but was often destroyed utterly. There were no small defeats in that age. A war-marshal who failed did not simply lose a battle; he might lose his whole town, its walls thrown down, its people vanished. Amepo carried that weight. He led the fighting men of his people through the fire of those years."}, {"id": "ologun-amepo#s5", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "And here, my children, is the hard heart of Amepo’s story, and I will not soften it, for the softening would be a lie. His town of Abemo was among those that the wars overtook. In the grinding conflicts of that age, Abemo was besieged and fell. This was the fate of many a town in those years, and it teaches us the cruelest lesson of the age of the war-cities: that no amount of courage in a leader could always save a people, because the forces of destruction were so vast and so many. A war-marshal might be brave, might be skilled, might do everything right, and still see his town swallowed, because the storm was larger than any one man’s strength."}, {"id": "ologun-amepo#s6", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "Do not turn away from this hard truth, my children, for there is deep wisdom in facing it. We like our stories to end in triumph. We like to believe that if a leader is good enough and brave enough, his people will be saved. But the honest history of our own ancestors teaches us that this is not always so. Sometimes the good and the brave are overwhelmed. Sometimes the town falls despite the best of men defending it. To pretend otherwise is to lie to ourselves and to dishonor those who fought and lost through no fault of their own courage."}, {"id": "ologun-amepo#s7", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "What, then, do we owe to a war-marshal like Amepo, whose town fell? We owe him this: the recognition that he stood, that he fought, that he bore the great office in an age that broke stronger things than any man. We do not measure him only by the outcome, for the outcome was decided by forces beyond his single arm. We measure him by his fidelity to his charge. He was called to defend his people. He tried. The age was against him. That is a tragedy, not a disgrace."}, {"id": "ologun-amepo#s8", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "And let me draw the larger lesson, the one I want you to carry from Amepo’s fallen town into your own lives. The world is not always fair. Effort does not always earn its just reward. There are storms so large that no wisdom and no courage can fully withstand them, and sometimes the finest people are caught in them and lose what they most wished to protect. When you see such a thing, do not conclude that the person was weak or foolish. Ask first how great the storm was. Often you will find that they were strong, and the storm was simply greater. And resolve, too, to build in the good seasons the kind of strength and unity that gives a people the best chance against the storms that always, sooner or later, come."}, {"id": "ologun-amepo#s9", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "The towns that survived the age of the war-cities, the Ibadans and the Abeokutas, survived not only through the courage of their war-chiefs but through size, through unity, through the gathering of many into one strong place. The lonely town, however brave its Aare, was terribly vulnerable. There is a lesson in that for every generation: in a dangerous age, unity is not a luxury but the very price of survival. Abemo’s fall, and Amepo’s grief, are a warning written in fire across our history."}, {"id": "ologun-amepo#s10", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "And, once more, let me name the shadow honestly. The wars that swallowed Abemo were fed by the slave-trade, that poison that made the destruction of a neighbor profitable, that turned the captured people of a fallen town into merchandise for the ships. We do not romanticize this age. We remember it as the long agony it was, the very suffering that the curse of Awole had foretold. Amepo was a man of courage caught in a catastrophe, and we honor his courage while mourning his age."}, {"id": "ologun-amepo#s11", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "So we honor Amepo of Abemo, the war-marshal whose town the wars devoured. He bore the great title in the pitiless age of the war-cities. He fought for his people against a storm too large to master. His town fell, but not for want of a defender, and his name endures in the line of the Aare Ona Kakanfo as a reminder that courage and tragedy often walk the same road. He is one of the knots tied in the hardest part of the rope, where many strands were breaking, and that his name survived the breaking of his town is itself a kind of victory over the darkness of his age."}, {"id": "ologun-amepo#s12", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "Tomorrow, my children, we come to the giants. We return to Kurunmi of Ijaye, whose full tale you have already heard, the lion who would not bend, and we go on to Ojo Aburumaku of Ogbomoso and then to the mighty Latoosa of Ibadan, in whose lifetime the guns of the white men would close their grip upon the whole land and bring the long age of the war-marshals to its end."}]}, {"id": "ologun-maye", "emoji": "🗡️", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#2a0500,#6a1400)", "category": "aare", "rating": 9.3, "duration": "4 min", "title": {"en": "Maye: The Third Kakanfo and the Lesson of Restraint", "yo": "Maye"}, "desc": {"en": "Maye of Ikoyi, the Kakanfo remembered for what he chose not to do.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Aare Ona Kakanfo", "secondary": [], "source_note": "", "segments": [{"id": "ologun-maye#s1", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Narrator opens: Between the explosive drama of Afonja and the tragic defiance of Kurunmi, there was Maye of Ikoyi — the third holder of the title of Aare Ona Kakanfo, a man whose story is told less loudly than those of his predecessor and successor but whose significance lies precisely in what he chose not to do. Maye served as Kakanfo during one of the most tumultuous periods in Oyo's decline — the years when the empire was hemorrhaging territory in the north, when the Ilorin wars were consuming resources, when the internal politics of the palace were in constant turbulence. In this environment, a Kakanfo with ambitious could have done what Afonja had done — turned military power into political power, declared independence, carved out a domain for himself. Maye did not do this."}, {"id": "ologun-maye#s2", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "He served. He fought. He maintained the discipline of the office even when the office itself was under pressure. His loyalty to the Alaafin was not naive — he was a sophisticated political actor who understood exactly how precarious the Oyo system had become. But he chose institutional loyalty over personal ambition, and that choice is itself a kind of heroism. The lesson of Maye is not as dramatic as the lesson of"}, {"id": "ologun-maye#s3", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "Afonja or Kurunmi. But it is perhaps more important: systems of governance survive not only because of the people who resist their collapse from outside, but because of those who maintain them from within. Every institution that has lasted has had its Mayes — the ones who showed up, did the work, honored the title, and went home without betraying anything. The third Kakanfo. The one who held the line without breaking it."}]}, {"id": "ologun-ojo-aburumaku", "emoji": "🗡️", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#2a0500,#6a1400)", "category": "aare", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "8 min", "title": {"en": "Ojo Aburumaku of Ogbomoso: The War-Marshal of the Long Wars", "yo": "Ojo Aburumaku"}, "desc": {"en": "The Ogbomoso war-marshal, the general who slept standing, through the great wars of the nineteenth century.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Aare Ona Kakanfo", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Aare Ona Kakanfo, the story of the generalissimo of Ogbomoso who carried the nation’s sword through the great wars of the nineteenth century)", "segments": [{"id": "ologun-ojo-aburumaku#s1", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Come and sit, my children, for tonight we take up the sword again, and this time we place it in the hand of a man of Ogbomoso, that great town on the northern road that has given our line more than one war-marshal. His name was Ojo, and our people gave him a second name that tells you at once what manner of man he was: Aburumaku. He was Aare Ona Kakanfo in the thick of the long wars of the nineteenth century, when the Yoruba country had become a land of contending armies and the sword never rested for long in its sheath."}, {"id": "ologun-ojo-aburumaku#s2", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "As always, my children, I begin with the honest truth of what is remembered. Ojo Aburumaku belongs to the age of the great war-cities, the age of Ibadan and Ijaye and Abeokuta, the age of Kurunmi and, soon, of Latoosa. The record of that age is fuller than the dim early days, but it is also tangled, for it was an age of many campaigns and many shifting alliances. What our traditions hold firmly is that Ojo Aburumaku of Ogbomoso bore the high title of Aare Ona Kakanfo, that he was a war-leader of real weight in the conflicts of his time, and that his name stands in the honored line between the earlier holders and the mighty Latoosa who would bring the fighting age to its close. From these true things, and from a faithful understanding of his violent age, we tell his story."}, {"id": "ologun-ojo-aburumaku#s3", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Let me remind you of the shape of his world, for it was a hard one. The old empire of Oyo was gone, shattered generations before by Afonja’s revolt and the northern power at Ilorin. In its place stood the war-cities, each a power unto itself, each with its armies and its ambitions. Ibadan had grown into a mighty war-camp, the strongest of them all, pushing its power in every direction. Ilorin pressed down from the north. Dahomey struck from the west. And the towns of the Yoruba fought one another endlessly, town against town, alliance against alliance, in wars that ran on for years and swallowed a whole generation of young men."}, {"id": "ologun-ojo-aburumaku#s4", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Ogbomoso, Ojo Aburumaku’s town, stood in a place of great danger and great importance in this age, on the northern frontier where the Yoruba country met the power of Ilorin. A war-marshal of Ogbomoso was a man on the front line of the whole nation’s defense against the pressure from the north. This was no quiet posting. It was the edge of the blade."}, {"id": "ologun-ojo-aburumaku#s5", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Think, my children, of what it meant to bear the great title of Aare Ona Kakanfo in such an age. In the old empire, the war-marshal had ridden out on grand campaigns to enforce the will of a mighty Alaafin. But in the age of Ojo Aburumaku, the war-marshal was a war-leader among war-leaders, fighting the long, grinding, merciless wars of the war-cities. These were not the swift cavalry raids of old Oyo across the open grassland. These were long sieges, drawn-out campaigns, wars that dragged across seasons and years, wars in which the guns brought up from the coast made the killing more terrible than it had ever been."}, {"id": "ologun-ojo-aburumaku#s6", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "Ojo Aburumaku fought in these long wars. He carried the great title through years of conflict, leading the fighting men of his people in the endless struggles of the age. That he held the honored title of Aare Ona Kakanfo tells us he was reckoned among the foremost soldiers of a soldiering age, an age that produced hard men because it demanded them."}, {"id": "ologun-ojo-aburumaku#s7", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Now let me draw a lesson from Ojo Aburumaku’s long wars, for there is one that matters greatly. We tend to imagine that war is a matter of a single glorious battle, a clash, a victory, and then peace. But the wars of Ojo Aburumaku’s age were not like that. They were long. They ground on and on. And this teaches us something about the nature of the hardest struggles: that the deepest test is not the flash of courage in a single battle but the endurance to keep going through years of difficulty, when the war has become not an adventure but a weary and bloody way of life."}, {"id": "ologun-ojo-aburumaku#s8", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "There is a courage of the moment, the courage that carries a warrior forward in the heat of a single charge. And there is a courage of endurance, the far rarer courage that carries a leader through year after year of hard struggle without breaking, without giving up, without losing heart. The war-marshals of the long wars needed the second kind of courage above all. Ojo Aburumaku, carrying the great title through the grinding conflicts of his age, was a man of that enduring kind. Remember, my children, that in your own long struggles, whatever they may be, it is often the endurance and not the brilliance that finally tells."}, {"id": "ologun-ojo-aburumaku#s9", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "And let me place him honestly beside the other giants of his age, for the griot must be fair. This was the age of Kurunmi of Ijaye, whose full and tragic tale you have already heard, the lion who would not bend and who fell in the great Ijaye War. It was the age when Ibadan was rising to dominate the whole Yoruba country. Ojo Aburumaku of Ogbomoso was one of the war-marshals of this crowded and violent generation, holding the front against Ilorin in the north while the war-cities contended among themselves. He was part of the great web of warriors whose collective struggles filled the nineteenth century with both terrible suffering and remarkable feats of arms."}, {"id": "ologun-ojo-aburumaku#s10", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "And as always, my children, I will not hide the shadow. The long wars of this age were a catastrophe for our people even as they produced great soldiers. They filled the land with captives, and the captives filled the ships at the coast, for the slave-trade still cast its poison over everything, making the endless warring profitable and so helping to keep it endless. A whole century of Yoruba life was consumed in these wars. We honor the courage and endurance of the war-marshals like Ojo Aburumaku while grieving honestly for the age that demanded such courage, an age of our people’s own long agony."}, {"id": "ologun-ojo-aburumaku#s11", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So we honor Ojo Aburumaku of Ogbomoso, the war-marshal of the long wars, the man of enduring courage who held the northern front and carried the nation’s sword through the grinding conflicts of the nineteenth century. His was the harder, longer kind of soldiering, the endurance that outlasts the glory. In the line of the Aare Ona Kakanfo, he stands among the giants of the fighting age, one of the strong knots in the rope in the years just before the whole age of the war-marshals reached its thunderous close. Tomorrow, my children, we come at last to the mightiest and the last of the great fighting Aare, to Latoosa of Ibadan, in whose lifetime the guns of the white men would close their grip upon the whole Yoruba country, and the long age of the war-marshals that began with Kokoro Igangan would finally end."}]}, {"id": "ologun-latoosa", "emoji": "🗡️", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#2a0500,#6a1400)", "category": "aare", "rating": 9.6, "duration": "10 min", "title": {"en": "Latoosa of Ibadan: The Last of the Great War-Marshals", "yo": "Latoosa"}, "desc": {"en": "The mighty Ibadan generalissimo in whose lifetime the British guns closed on Yorubaland.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Aare Ona Kakanfo", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Aare Ona Kakanfo, the story of the mighty generalissimo of Ibadan in whose lifetime the guns of the white men closed upon the whole Yoruba country)", "segments": [{"id": "ologun-latoosa#s1", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Come close, my children, closer than usual, for tonight we sit with the last of a breed. His name was Latoosa, sometimes called Obadoke Latoosa, and he was the Aare Ona Kakanfo of Ibadan, the mightiest war-city the Yoruba country had ever raised from the ruins of old Oyo. He was the last of the great fighting war-marshals of the old kind, and in his lifetime an age that had begun long ages before, in the days of Kokoro Igangan, came to its thunderous and final close. After Latoosa, the world of the war-marshals was gone, swept away by a power that no cavalry, however brave, could withstand: the power of the British Empire and its guns."}, {"id": "ologun-latoosa#s2", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Let me set him in his place, for Latoosa was a giant, and giants must be measured against the mountains they stood among. Ibadan in his day was the greatest power in the Yoruba country. It had risen from a war-camp into a mighty city-state, an empire in its own right, whose armies had pushed the frontier of Yoruba power in every direction and had become the shield of the whole land against the pressure of Ilorin and the north. Ibadan was ruled not by a sacred king in the old Oyo style but by a council of war-chiefs, a meritocracy of soldiers, where a man rose by his prowess in war. And at the height of this warrior-state, holding the greatest of its military titles, stood Latoosa, the Aare Ona Kakanfo."}, {"id": "ologun-latoosa#s3", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Remember the lesson I have taught you again and again, my children: never flatten a great man into a single word. Latoosa was a mighty warrior and a proud one, a man of enormous ambition and force. Some remember him as overbearing, a man whose pride and whose hunger for supremacy helped draw Ibadan into a war that would exhaust it. Others remember him as a great patriot who fought to hold Ibadan’s power together against a ring of enemies. Both of these are true. He was proud, and he was mighty, and he was, in the end, tragic, for his greatest war became the war that broke the back of the old fighting age. Hold all of this together, as a grown person must."}, {"id": "ologun-latoosa#s4", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Now I must tell you of the great war of his life, the war that our people remember simply as the Kiriji War, or the Ekitiparapo War, one of the longest and most terrible conflicts of our whole history. It came about like this. Ibadan, under Latoosa and the war-chiefs, had grown so mighty that it ruled over many other Yoruba peoples, and its rule had grown heavy. The Ekiti and the Ijesha and others, chafing under Ibadan’s overlordship, formed a great confederacy, the Ekitiparapo, to throw off that rule. And so the mightiest war-city found itself facing a grand alliance of the peoples it had dominated."}, {"id": "ologun-latoosa#s5", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Latoosa led Ibadan into this war. And what a war it was, my children. It was fought on a great scale, army against army, and it dragged on not for a season but for years upon years, sixteen years by some reckonings, the two great hosts encamped against each other along a range of hills, so long that the war-camps became like towns, with markets and families and children born in the shadow of the battle-lines."}, {"id": "ologun-latoosa#s6", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "And here, my children, is where the age itself turned. For in this great war a new thing appeared that changed the very nature of Yoruba warfare forever. The Ekitiparapo confederacy, through their connections to the coast and to their kinsmen who had returned from across the seas, obtained modern rifles, breech-loading guns far superior to the old flintlocks of Ibadan. For the first time, our people heard the roar of rapid, long-range gunfire on a Yoruba battlefield. The very name Kiriji, some say, echoes the sound those great guns made. The old ways of war, the brave massed charge, the cavalry, the valor of the sworn war-captain, all of these met the new firepower and could not simply overcome it. The war ground to a bloody, exhausted deadlock, neither side able to break the other, both bleeding year after year."}, {"id": "ologun-latoosa#s7", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "And now, watch closely, for this is the moment the whole age ends. As the great Yoruba powers exhausted themselves against one another in this endless war, a new master was quietly closing its hand around the whole land. The British, established on the coast at Lagos, watched the Yoruba destroy themselves in the interior, and they moved. It was the British who at last intervened to end the Kiriji War, sending their agents to broker the peace that the exhausted combatants could no longer make themselves. The guns fell silent not because a Yoruba war-marshal had triumphed, but because a foreign empire had decided the war should end, and had the power to make its decision felt."}, {"id": "ologun-latoosa#s8", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Do you see the terrible turning, my children? Latoosa was the mightiest war-marshal of the mightiest war-city, and yet his greatest war ended not in his victory but in his exhaustion, and the peace was dictated not by any Yoruba power but by the British. The age of the war-marshals, the age in which the Aare Ona Kakanfo had been the supreme arbiter of force in the Yoruba world, ended in that moment. A greater power had come, and before it the old office, however glorious, was now merely a relic of a vanishing world. Latoosa himself did not long survive the ruin of his great war. The accounts tell us that he died worn down by the long struggle and its bitter, inconclusive end, his Ibadan exhausted, the old supremacy broken, the British shadow now lying across the whole land. So passed the last of the great fighting Aare Ona Kakanfo. With him passed the age itself."}, {"id": "ologun-latoosa#s9", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Now let us weigh him, for that is the griot’s duty. Latoosa was a mighty man, perhaps the mightiest soldier of his generation. His pride and ambition helped bring on the war that exhausted Ibadan, and in that sense his story carries the old warning against a leader whose hunger for supremacy outruns his wisdom. But he was also a titan who fought to the very end of the old world, who commanded the greatest army the Yoruba had, and who had the tragic distinction of standing at the exact hinge of history, where the old age of Yoruba self-rule by the sword gave way to the age of colonial conquest."}, {"id": "ologun-latoosa#s10", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "The lesson of Latoosa is the lesson of an age that could not see the true danger. While the great Yoruba powers spent their strength fighting one another, the real threat, the power that would swallow them all, was gathering on the coast. They were so absorbed in their old rivalries that they did not unite against the new peril until it was too late, and the peril simply waited for them to exhaust themselves and then took the whole land. There is a warning here for every people and every age, my children: beware of spending all your strength on quarrels with your brothers while a greater danger watches and waits. Disunity in the face of a rising external power has been the ruin of many a people, and it was, in the end, the ruin of the old Yoruba independence."}, {"id": "ologun-latoosa#s11", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "And let me name the last shadow honestly. With the end of the Kiriji War and the coming of the British came the close of the long agony of the wars and the slave-trade, but it came at the price of our people’s independence. The Yoruba country passed under colonial rule. The old kingdoms and war-cities became subjects of an empire ruled from across the sea. This was a complicated ending, bringing both an end to the terrible internal wars and the loss of self-rule. We remember it honestly, as we remember all things at this fire, without pretending it was simply good or simply bad."}, {"id": "ologun-latoosa#s12", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So we honor Latoosa of Ibadan, the last of the great fighting war-marshals, the titan who stood at the hinge of the ages. With him, the long line of the Aare Ona Kakanfo as a living war-command came to its end. The office would not die, my children, for you shall hear how it was raised again in a new age upon new shoulders. But never again would an Aare Ona Kakanfo lead the massed armies of the Yoruba into the field as Kokoro Igangan and Oyabi and Kurunmi and Latoosa had done. That age was over. Latoosa closed the door on it, and the sound of its closing was the roar of the great guns at Kiriji."}, {"id": "ologun-latoosa#s13", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "Tomorrow, my children, we leap across the years, across the whole age of colonial rule and into the time of a new Nigeria, to find the ancient title reborn upon the shoulders of a modern man: Chief Samuel Ladoke Akintola of Ogbomoso, premier and politician, in whose story the old sword becomes a new and different kind of power."}]}, {"id": "ologun-aare-ona-kakanfo-title", "emoji": "🗡️", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#2a0500,#6a1400)", "category": "aare", "rating": 9.4, "duration": "5 min", "title": {"en": "The Aare Ona Kakanfo", "yo": "The Aare Ona Kakanfo"}, "desc": {"en": "The supreme war title of the Yoruba: an honour no one could refuse and few survived holding, and the roll of its holders.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Aare Ona Kakanfo", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Ologun / Kakanfo, from “The Aare Ago-Kakanfo”)", "segments": [{"id": "ologun-aare-ona-kakanfo-title#s1", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "There was a title in the old Yoruba world that a man could not want and stay alive, and could not refuse and keep his honor, and could not hold and die easy in his bed. It was the title of Aare Ona Kakanfo, the supreme war commander, the generalissimo of the whole army, the sword-arm of the empire. To be named Kakanfo was the highest honor a fighting man could receive. It was also, very often, a sentence, and the men who understood the title best were the ones least eager to have it."}, {"id": "ologun-aare-ona-kakanfo-title#s2", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "The Kakanfo was chosen from among the greatest warriors of the age, a man proven in a hundred fights, and he was given command of all the forces of the empire in the field. But the honor came wrapped in iron rules, and the rules were terrible. The Kakanfo was not permitted to live in the same city as the Alaafin, the king, for a war commander of such power beside the throne was a danger no king could sleep beside. So he was posted to a frontier town, kept at the edge of the empire, close to the enemy and far from the comfort of the court. And there was a deeper rule still, the rule that gave the title its dread. A Kakanfo who was sent to war and did not win was expected not to come home. Victory or death. There was no third road, no honorable retreat, no living with a defeat. He conquered, or he fell on the field, or he took his own life rather than return beaten."}, {"id": "ologun-aare-ona-kakanfo-title#s3", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "Think of what that rule made of a man. When the Kakanfo marched out at the head of the army, he marched knowing there was no way back except through victory. Behind him lay only disgrace and the cord or the poison cup. Ahead lay the enemy. This did something to the way such a man fought, and to the way his soldiers followed him. An ordinary commander might weigh a hard battle and think, if it goes badly we can withdraw and try again. The Kakanfo could not think that thought. For him a battle was total, because his life was staked on it as surely as the lives of the men he sent forward. There is a kind of courage that comes from having other choices and choosing bravery anyway, and there is a colder, stranger kind that comes from having no choice at all, and the Kakanfo lived in the second kind."}, {"id": "ologun-aare-ona-kakanfo-title#s4", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "It made them magnificent and it made them dangerous. A man who fights with his own life already forfeit is capable of victories that a cautious man could never reach, because he will attempt things a cautious man would call madness, and sometimes madness wins. But the same iron that made him formidable in war made him a hard neighbor in peace, and the history of the title is thick with Kakanfo who quarreled with their kings, who grew too powerful to control, who turned the army that was meant to defend the empire into a tool of their own ambition. The most famous of them all, Afonja of Ilorin, was a Kakanfo, and his revolt against the Alaafin helped bring the whole old order crashing down. The title that was meant to be the empire’s strongest shield became, in the wrong hands, the blade that cut its own throat."}, {"id": "ologun-aare-ona-kakanfo-title#s5", "scene": "Aàrẹ Ọ̀nà Kakanfò", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So the Aare Ona Kakanfo stands in the memory of the Yoruba as one of those figures in which greatness and doom are braided together and cannot be pulled apart. To honor a man with the title was to load him with glory and with a death sentence in the same breath, to make him the mightiest servant of the state and to place him where he could most easily become its master. The elders tell of the Kakanfo not only to remember great warriors, but to hold up a hard truth about power, that the same qualities which make a man able to save his people can, if the leash slips, make him able to destroy them, and that a wise nation must think as carefully about how it binds its strongest men as about how it arms them."}]}, {"id": "ologun-eso-of-oyo", "emoji": "⚔️", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#2a0a00,#5a1800)", "category": "ologun", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "8 min", "title": {"en": "The Eso of Oyo: The Seventy Warriors of the Kingdom", "yo": "Eso"}, "desc": {"en": "The seventy elite warriors of the Oyo kingdom and their code.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Ologun / Warlords", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Ologun / Kakanfo, from “The Eso of Oyo: Seventy Warriors of the Kingdom”)", "segments": [{"id": "ologun-eso-of-oyo#s1", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Every great empire that has ever ridden to power has had, at the sharp point of its spear, a brotherhood of chosen warriors, an order of the very best and the very bravest, set apart from the common soldiers and bound by a code that made them more than men. Rome had its legions, and other lands their own famed companies, and the empire of Oyo, at the height of its glory, had the Eso. They were the seventy war captains of the kingdom, the elite of the elite, the mounted champions upon whose courage the whole terror of Oyo rested, and to be counted among them was the highest honor a fighting man of that world could dream of, and also, very often, a sentence of a hard and early death. This is the story of that brotherhood, of the code they lived and died by, and of what it meant to be one of the seventy in the days when Oyo made the whole country tremble."}, {"id": "ologun-eso-of-oyo#s2", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "The Eso were created to serve the war and the crown, and they were chosen for one thing above all others, valor that had been proven in the field. A man did not inherit a place among the Eso, and he could not buy it. He won it, with his own hands, in war, by deeds so brave that they could not be ignored, and his fellow warriors and the great war chiefs marked him and raised him up. Each of the seventy held a war title, and behind each of them stood a following of fighting men, so that the Eso were at once individual champions and the commanders of the kingdom’s strength. They were horsemen, for Oyo was a cavalry power, and to picture them you must see the tall northern war horses, the riders in their war dress with their charms and their weapons, the whole terrible splendor of the mounted host of Oyo with the seventy champions at its head. When the Eso rode out, kingdoms fell."}, {"id": "ologun-eso-of-oyo#s3", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "But it was the code that made the Eso truly fearsome, and it is the code that the elders dwell upon, because it reveals a whole philosophy of what a warrior was meant to be. An Eso did not flee. This was the iron heart of it. An Eso might win or an Eso might die, but an Eso did not run, did not surrender, did not come home beaten with his life saved and his honor spent. The disgrace of defeat was, for these men, worse than death itself, and the code held that a captain who lost his battle should not survive it. Some tellings say that an Eso who was defeated was expected to die rather than return, that to come back alive from a lost fight was a shame no true member of the seventy could carry. So these were men who rode into every battle with no road of retreat behind them, for whom the only honorable outcomes were victory or a warrior’s death, and you may imagine what such men were like to face across a field. An enemy could beat them only by killing them, for they would not break, and an army of soldiers who will not break, led by champions who would rather die than flee, is very nearly unstoppable."}, {"id": "ologun-eso-of-oyo#s4", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Think of what that code demanded, and what it created. A young warrior, raised into the Eso by his own courage, given a great title and a following and a place among the seventy champions of the mightiest empire in the land, knew from the day of his elevation that he had also, in a sense, agreed to die. Every campaign might be his last, and the day he was defeated would be the day he was expected to end. This bred a kind of man the ordinary world does not produce, one who had already made his peace with death and therefore feared nothing that lesser men feared, who fought with the terrible freedom of one who has nothing left to lose because he has already surrendered the thing others cling to hardest, the simple wish to survive. The Eso were the sharp edge of Oyo precisely because they had given up, in advance, the softness that dulls other men’s blades. And the empire fed on their courage. Every tribute Oyo collected, every kingdom it humbled, every road it held, was bought in part with the lives of the seventy who would not run."}, {"id": "ologun-eso-of-oyo#s5", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "And when Oyo fell, the Eso fell with it, and there is a bitter poetry in how it happened. The order that had been built on the horse died when the horse was lost, for once Ilorin cut the northern road and the great war animals could no longer be replaced, the mounted champions of Oyo lost the very thing that had made them the terror of the land. And in the last convulsions of the empire, in the wars and betrayals that tore Oyo apart, the seventy who would not flee were used up, killed in hopeless battles, scattered when the towns that raised them burned. The code that forbade retreat, so magnificent in the days of victory, became a kind of doom in the days of defeat, for men who would not run could only die, and die they did, the flower of Oyo’s warriors falling in the ruin of the empire they had served. When the old order finally collapsed, the Eso as an institution collapsed with it, and the age of the seventy champions passed away."}, {"id": "ologun-eso-of-oyo#s6", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "Yet the memory of them did not pass, and the elders keep it deliberately, because the Eso stand for something the Yoruba never wished to forget, an idea of honor pushed to its very limit, of courage so complete that it swallowed even the fear of death. When the elders speak of a truly brave man, a man who will not be moved, who would rather fall than flee, who holds his ground when all reason says run, they reach for the Eso, and they say that such a one has the heart of the old seventy. There is warning in the memory too, for the same code that made them unbreakable also made them expendable, and a wise listener notices that the empire spent these magnificent men freely and then died anyway, taking them down with it. But mostly there is awe, clean and simple awe, at the idea of the seventy champions of Oyo riding out in their splendor with no thought of return, carrying an empire on the strength of their refusal to run. So when you hear a Yoruba praise a person as being brave like the Eso, know that you are hearing an echo of the seventy, the chosen warriors of a vanished empire, whose courage outlived their horses, their titles, their kingdom, and even the memory of the particular battles they died in, and lives on still as the very measure of what it means to be unafraid."}]}, {"id": "ologun-ogundele", "emoji": "⚔️", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#2a0a00,#5a1800)", "category": "ologun", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "7 min", "title": {"en": "Ogundele the Blacksmith-Warrior: The Man Who Forged His Own War", "yo": "Ogundele"}, "desc": {"en": "The blacksmith-warrior who forged the weapons of his own war.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Ologun / Warlords", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Ologun / Kakanfo, from “Ogundele the Blacksmith-Warrior”)", "segments": [{"id": "ologun-ogundele#s1", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "In the old Yoruba world there was a deep and sacred link between the forge and the battlefield, between the man who made the iron and the man who carried it into war, and both of them belonged to the same great orisa, Ogun, the god of iron, of blacksmiths, of hunters, and of warriors alike. It was fitting, then, that one of the most remarkable war leaders the stories remember was not a prince or a born chief but a blacksmith, a maker of blades who became a wielder of them, a man named, aptly, Ogundele, which carries the name of the iron god himself. His is the story of a craftsman who understood war from the inside out, who knew the weapons of his age not as a customer but as their creator, and who turned that knowledge into a kind of generalship no ordinary warrior could match."}, {"id": "ologun-ogundele#s2", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "To understand why a blacksmith could rise to lead men in war, you must understand what the blacksmith was in that world. He was no mere laborer. He was a master of the most powerful and mysterious craft the society knew, the taking of dull rock from the earth and the transforming of it, through fire and muscle and secret skill, into the shining edge that could clear a forest, kill an enemy, or win a kingdom. The blacksmith made the hoes that fed the people and the blades that defended them. He was a figure of awe, half craftsman and half priest, for the forge was sacred to Ogun and the smith stood closest to the god’s fierce power. And in an age of endless war, the man who made the weapons held a special kind of authority, for every warrior who ever raised a blade had, in a sense, been armed by him, and he understood better than any general the true reach and limit of the tools that decided battles."}, {"id": "ologun-ogundele#s3", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Ogundele did not stay at his forge. In the fury of the war years, when every town needed every strong and skillful man it could raise, the blacksmith took up the very blades he had made and went to war, and he brought to the battlefield a mind trained by the discipline of the forge. A smith works with patience and precision, understanding stress and strain, knowing exactly how much a blade can bear before it shatters, learning through a thousand hammer blows where strength lies and where weakness hides. Ogundele fought the way he forged. He was not merely brave, though he was that. He was exact. He understood the weapons on both sides of a battle as no ordinary warrior could, knew which enemy blades would hold and which would fail, knew how to keep his own men’s weapons sound through a long campaign when others found their edges chipped and their guns fouled and useless. An army is only as good as its iron, and Ogundele’s men always had good iron, kept sharp and sound by a leader who had made such things with his own hands since boyhood."}, {"id": "ologun-ogundele#s4", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "And there was more to it than the mending of blades, for the blacksmith’s knowledge shaped the way he made war. He thought about weapons the way a general should think about the whole of his force, always asking what a tool could truly do and what it could not, never fooled by the mere appearance of strength. When new weapons came into the country, the trade guns and later the better rifles, it was men like Ogundele, the smiths who could take a strange gun apart and understand it, who grasped fastest what the new iron meant, how it changed the reach of a battle, what tactics it demanded and what old ways it made suicidal. In an age when war itself was being transformed by new weapons, the warrior who understood iron from the inside had an advantage that pure courage could never supply, and Ogundele used it. He is remembered as a war leader who won not by throwing away his men’s lives in brave frontal rushes, but by the smith’s cool calculation, by knowing his tools and his enemy’s tools and choosing the fight where his iron would tell."}, {"id": "ologun-ogundele#s5", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "There is a spiritual dimension to Ogundele’s story that the elders never leave out, for the blacksmith-warrior lived under the sign of Ogun, and Ogun is a god of terrible double meaning. Ogun clears the forest so the farm can be planted, and Ogun’s blade opens the throat in war. He is the maker of every good and useful iron thing, the hoe and the knife and the axe, and he is also the spirit of the killing edge, of blood and battle and the sword. To carry Ogun’s power is to hold creation and destruction in the same hand, and the blacksmith-warrior embodied this as no one else could, a man whose hands made the tools of life at the forge and dealt the strokes of death on the field, both in the service of the same fierce god. The people saw in Ogundele the living presence of Ogun, and they both revered and feared him for it, for a man so close to that power was blessed and dangerous at once, as the god himself is."}, {"id": "ologun-ogundele#s6", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So the elders tell Ogundele to teach a truth that runs deeper than war, that true mastery of anything comes from making it yourself, from knowing a thing in your hands and not only in your head. The warriors who merely used their weapons were at the mercy of those who understood them, and Ogundele understood them because he had made them. Know your tools as their maker knows them, the elders say, and you will never be a slave to them, and never be deceived by them, and never be caught helpless when they fail. It is a lesson for the farmer and the trader and the craftsman as much as for the soldier. But there is honor too, plain honor, in the memory of the man from the forge who took up his own blades and led men to war and won by wisdom rather than waste, and who showed that the humble craft of the smith, rightly understood, could raise a working man to stand among the great captains of his age. Ogundele forged his own war, in every sense, and the elders keep his name so that no child ever again believes that greatness belongs only to those who are born to it, and not also to those who make it, spark by spark and blow by blow, with their own two hands."}]}, {"id": "ologun-kuku-interpreter", "emoji": "⚔️", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#2a0a00,#5a1800)", "category": "ologun", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "6 min", "title": {"en": "Kuku: The War Interpreter", "yo": "Kuku"}, "desc": {"en": "The war interpreter whose tongue, carrying words between enemies and Europeans, shaped wars as much as any musket.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Ologun / Warlords", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Ologun / Kakanfo, from “Kuku: The War Interpreter”)", "segments": [{"id": "ologun-kuku-interpreter#s1", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Not every man who shapes a war carries a weapon, and the story of Kuku the war interpreter is the story of a different kind of power in the age of the warlords, the power of the tongue. In a country of many towns and many tempers, where the wars were fought not only with muskets and machetes but with alliances and negotiations, with envoys and treaties and the delicate handling of proud and dangerous men, the interpreter, the man who could carry words truly and shrewdly between enemies and allies, held a place of quiet and enormous importance. Kuku was such a man, and his story reminds us that in war it is often the one who speaks, and not the one who strikes, who decides how many will live and how many will die."}, {"id": "ologun-kuku-interpreter#s2", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "By Kuku’s day the Yoruba wars had drawn in a new and powerful party, the Europeans on the coast, the British at Lagos with their trade and their guns and their growing hunger for the interior, and the missionaries who moved between the warring towns carrying messages along with their preaching. Suddenly the old wars were not only Yoruba against Yoruba but Yoruba dealing with a strange people who spoke a strange tongue, who wrote their agreements on paper, whose intentions were hard to read and whose power was hard to measure. In such a world the man who could stand between the two peoples, who understood both the Yoruba way of speaking, with all its proverbs and indirection and layered courtesy, and the blunt written speech of the Europeans, became a figure of great consequence. A single interpreter, carrying words between a Yoruba war chief and a British officer, could make peace or make war by the way he chose to render a phrase, could soothe an insult or sharpen one, could open a road to agreement or slam it shut, and the men on both sides, unable to check him, were in his hands."}, {"id": "ologun-kuku-interpreter#s3", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "Kuku made himself indispensable in exactly this space between the worlds. He learned the strangers’ tongue and their ways, and he kept his mastery of his own people’s deep and subtle speech, and he became the bridge across which words and meanings and intentions had to pass. When a war chief needed to treat with the British, when a peace was to be negotiated or a threat delivered or an alliance sounded out, it was Kuku who carried the words, and carried also, whether he wished it or not, an enormous discretion, for translation is never a simple thing. A Yoruba elder’s speech is thick with proverb and allusion, a thing of hints and honor and careful indirection, and to render it into the flat directness the Europeans expected required not just knowledge but judgment, a constant choosing of what to sharpen and what to soften, what to explain and what to let pass. In every such choice lay the power to shape what each side believed the other had said, and therefore the power to shape what they would do."}, {"id": "ologun-kuku-interpreter#s4", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "And here the elders find the deep matter of the story, for such power is a terrible temptation and a terrible trust. An interpreter can serve his people faithfully, straining to carry every meaning true, using his position to prevent the misunderstandings that start wars and to build the trust that ends them. Or he can serve himself, telling each side what he wishes them to hear, playing the two against each other for his own gain, growing rich and powerful on the ignorance of the men who depend on him and cannot check him. The war interpreter walked this knife’s edge every day, and his own soul was as much at stake as any battle. The Yoruba, who understood these things well, both valued and watched such men closely, for they knew that the tongue that could save a thousand lives could also, with a single crooked rendering, send them to their deaths, and that the man in the middle, trusted by both and belonging fully to neither, held a power more dangerous in some ways than the warlord’s, because it worked in the dark, inside the meaning of words, where no one could see it working."}, {"id": "ologun-kuku-interpreter#s5", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So the elders tell of Kuku the interpreter to honor a kind of service the war stories too often forget, and to teach the young a lasting truth about the power of language. The one who controls the meaning of words controls more than the one who controls the blade, they say, for the blade can only kill what is in front of it, but the crooked word can turn friends into enemies a hundred miles apart who never even met. Guard the truth of your speech, they say, especially when you stand between two who cannot understand each other, for in that place you are not a mere carrier of words but a maker of peace or war, and the whole weight of what follows rests on whether you choose to be honest. Kuku is remembered because he stood in that dangerous middle place in a dangerous age, and because his story holds, better than any tale of battle, the truth that in the affairs of peoples the tongue is a weapon greater than the sword, and the honest interpreter a warrior greater than he knows."}]}, {"id": "ologun-battle-of-osogbo", "emoji": "⚔️", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#2a0a00,#5a1800)", "category": "ologun", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "9 min", "title": {"en": "The Battle of Osogbo: The Turning Point", "yo": "Osogbo"}, "desc": {"en": "The battle where Ibadan stopped the Ilorin cavalry and turned back the northern tide.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Ologun / Warlords", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Ologun / Kakanfo, from “The Battle of Osogbo: The Turning Point”)", "segments": [{"id": "ologun-battle-of-osogbo#s1", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "There are battles that decide who wins a war, and there are battles that decide who a people will be for the next hundred years. The Battle of Osogbo was the second kind. If you had stood on the walls of that town in the dry season of the 1830s and watched what came out of the north, you would have understood at once that this was not another quarrel between towns over a market or a boundary. This was the edge of a flood that had already drowned kingdoms, and Osogbo was the last high ground before the water reached the sea."}, {"id": "ologun-battle-of-osogbo#s2", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "To understand why the men who fought there fought the way they did, you have to understand what they had already lost. Oyo, the great empire of the savanna, the mother of Yoruba kings, had fallen. Its capital stood empty, its cavalry scattered, its Alaafin a shadow of what the title had once meant. The horsemen of Ilorin, riding under the banner of the jihad that had swept down from the far north, had taken the open grassland and turned south, and everywhere they rode the story was the same. Towns that resisted were burned. Fields were emptied. Long lines of the captured were driven north with their wrists bound, never to be seen again. The Yoruba had a word for what was happening, and the word was not war. It was closer to the end of the world."}, {"id": "ologun-battle-of-osogbo#s3", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Into this terror came the men of Ibadan. Ibadan was new then, a rough war-camp of a town swollen with refugees and soldiers from a dozen broken places, a settlement with no old royal line to speak of and no patience for ceremony, but full of something the older cities had lost. It was full of fighting men who had nowhere left to run and had decided, at last, to stop running. When the call came that Osogbo was besieged and that the Ilorin host meant to take it and open the road to the whole southern forest, Ibadan did not send excuses. Ibadan sent its army."}, {"id": "ologun-battle-of-osogbo#s4", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "The march to Osogbo is remembered as a thing of grim silence. These were not fresh soldiers looking for glory. Many of them had already lost their home towns to the same enemy they were now going to meet. They knew exactly what would happen if they failed, because they had already watched it happen elsewhere, and that knowledge did something to an army that no drum or war-song could do. It made them still. It made them certain. A man who is afraid of dying can be broken. A man who has already lost everything and has come to take one thing back is a different kind of danger altogether."}, {"id": "ologun-battle-of-osogbo#s5", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "The Ilorin strength was the horse. On the open savanna the cavalry had been unstoppable, a wall of galloping animals and flashing steel that could shatter any line of foot soldiers and run down anyone who broke and ran. The whole terror of the northern advance was built on those horses. But the men of Ibadan had learned something in their losing years, something bought with blood, and it was this. The horse rules the grassland. The horse does not rule the forest."}, {"id": "ologun-battle-of-osogbo#s6", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "So the Ibadan commanders did not offer battle in the open where the cavalry wanted it. They chose their ground with cold care, near the water, near the trees, on land where a galloping horse would find roots and undergrowth and soft earth instead of a clean field to run on. And then they waited, hidden in the bush and along the treeline, muskets loaded, and let the great host come to them. It is one thing to know a trap in your head. It is another to lie flat in the grass and listen to the ground begin to shake, and to hold your fire, and to keep holding it, while the thunder grows."}, {"id": "ologun-battle-of-osogbo#s7", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "When the Ilorin cavalry came, they came expecting the same easy slaughter they had known for years. They saw a lesser force and they charged, and for a few heartbeats it must have looked to them like every other victory. Then the ground betrayed the horses. Hooves that had flown across open savanna now fought roots and mud and the tangle of the forest edge. The great charge lost its speed, and a cavalry charge that loses its speed is no longer a charge. It is a crowd of large frightened animals standing in a killing field."}, {"id": "ologun-battle-of-osogbo#s8", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "And then Ibadan fired. The volleys came out of the treeline where no one had been visible a moment before, and horses screamed and went down, and the riders behind piled into the riders in front, and the beautiful terrible order of the cavalry dissolved into chaos. This was the moment the men of Ibadan had crossed all that country to reach. They came out of the bush with sword and spear into the wreck of the charge, and they did not fight like men hoping to survive. They fought like men collecting a debt. Every burned town, every stolen child, every humiliation of the losing years was in their arms that day."}, {"id": "ologun-battle-of-osogbo#s9", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "The rout was total. The host that had ridden south to swallow the forest was broken and scattered and driven back the way it had come, and many who had come to take captives were themselves taken. The Yoruba, who for a generation had been the hunted, stood at the end of that day on a field of their own victory, breathing hard, hardly able to believe what their own hands had done. The flood had reached its high ground, and the high ground had held."}, {"id": "ologun-battle-of-osogbo#s10", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "What made Osogbo the turning point was not only the killing. It was the meaning. For years the Ilorin advance had carried an aura of the inevitable, as though the fall of the whole Yoruba country were only a matter of time and the sensible thing was to submit. Osogbo shattered that spell. It proved that the cavalry could be beaten, that the forest could be defended, that the southward flood could be stopped by men who chose their ground and held their nerve. The line of the Fulani advance was fixed at last, and it never crossed into the heart of the southern forest. Yorubaland kept its Yoruba south. Everything below Osogbo, the cities of the forest, the coast, the future, remained the Yoruba’s own."}, {"id": "ologun-battle-of-osogbo#s11", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "The victory made Ibadan. A town that had been a crude camp of refugees became, almost overnight, the great military power of the Yoruba world, the shield of the south, the city whose war chiefs would dominate the region for the rest of the century. And it gave the Yoruba something they had been starving for through all the years of loss, which was a story of themselves as victors and not only victims. Long after the muskets fell silent, mothers told their children about the day the horses of the north were broken at the edge of the forest, and the children stood a little straighter for the telling."}, {"id": "ologun-battle-of-osogbo#s12", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So the elders remember Osogbo as the hinge on which a whole people turned. Before it, the Yoruba south was a thing being lost. After it, the Yoruba south was a thing being held. The men who lay in the grass that day and let the thunder come to them, and then rose and broke it, did not know they were deciding the shape of a nation. They only knew they had run far enough. But that is often how the great turnings of history are made, not by men who set out to change the world, but by ordinary desperate people who simply decide, on one particular field, on one particular day, that here and no further is where they will make their stand."}]}, {"id": "ologun-abiri-scout", "emoji": "⚔️", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#2a0a00,#5a1800)", "category": "ologun", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "6 min", "title": {"en": "Abiri the Scout", "yo": "Abiri"}, "desc": {"en": "Abiri the scout, the eyes that went before the army.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Ologun / Warlords", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Ologun / Kakanfo, from “Abiri the Scout”)", "segments": [{"id": "ologun-abiri-scout#s1", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Every army remembers its generals. The drums beat their names, the praise-singers polish their deeds, the children learn to say them like a lesson. But the men who win wars are often not the men whose names survive, and Abiri the Scout is remembered precisely because he was one of the forgotten kind, the man who went out alone into the dark before the battle so that the battle could be won at all. In the war-craft of the old Yoruba, no soldier was more valued and none was more quietly heroic than the scout, and Abiri was the scout the other scouts spoke of in low voices."}, {"id": "ologun-abiri-scout#s2", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "He was not a big man, and that was part of it. The great warriors of the age were built like the walls of a compound, wide across the shoulders, arms like the roots of an iroko tree, men whose whole presence on a field was a kind of announcement. Abiri was the opposite of an announcement. He was small and lean and quiet, the sort of man you would not notice in a crowded market and would forget the moment he passed, and in the work he had chosen, being forgettable was worth more than being strong. A scout who is noticed is a dead scout. A scout who is remembered by the enemy has already failed."}, {"id": "ologun-abiri-scout#s3", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "What Abiri could do, no drum could celebrate, because it happened where no one could see. He could leave a war-camp at dusk and be gone all night in country crawling with enemies, and come back before the cooking fires were lit with a picture of the whole enemy position held whole in his head. How many they were. Where the horses were kept and how many. Which side of the camp was watched and which was lazy. Where the water was, and the food, and the powder. Whether the men were fresh or worn, fed or hungry, confident or afraid, because a scout who was truly good could read the mood of an army from the way its sentries stood. He moved through the bush the way water moves through sand, leaving nothing behind, and he had trained his body to a stillness that was almost unnatural. It was said he could lie within a spear’s length of an enemy sentry through a whole watch of the night, breathing so slow and quiet that the man never knew a Yoruba soldier had been close enough to touch him."}, {"id": "ologun-abiri-scout#s4", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "The generals loved him and feared for him in equal measure, because a general with a good scout fights with his eyes open while his enemy fights blind, and that difference decided more battles than courage ever did. Before Abiri went out, the commanders knew only rumor and boast. After he came back, they knew the truth, and knowing the truth they could choose their ground, set their ambush, strike the weak side and avoid the strong, or, wisest of all, decline a battle that could not be won and live to fight a better one. More than once Abiri’s night walk saved an army not by winning a fight but by warning it away from a fight it did not know was a trap. The victories he made possible were counted to other men. The disasters he prevented were never counted at all, because a disaster that does not happen leaves no mark in memory."}, {"id": "ologun-abiri-scout#s5", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "There is a story told of a night when Abiri went out to count an enemy that had camped in the forest, meaning to fall on a Yoruba town at first light. He found them, counted them, read the whole shape of the coming attack, and on his way back through the dark he came upon a thing that tested him. A boy from his own side, a young untrained soldier who had wandered too far and lost his way, was lying hurt in the bush, close enough to the enemy camp that a single cry would have brought the whole host down on them both. The easy thing, the scout’s cold arithmetic, said leave him. One boy against a whole town warned in time. But Abiri did not leave him. He carried the boy the whole way back through enemy country in the dark, silent as ever, and delivered his count to the generals in time for the ambush that broke the attack, and set the boy down among his own people, and said nothing about it. The town was saved. The boy lived to be an old man. And the old man told the story, because Abiri never would have."}, {"id": "ologun-abiri-scout#s6", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So the elders keep Abiri’s name for a reason that goes deeper than war. His story is a lesson about the worth of the work that is never seen, the courage that wins no praise, the service that leaves no monument. Not every hero stands in the front of the battle where the singers can find him. Some do their bravest work alone in the dark, so that others can stand in the light and be called brave. The generals had the glory. Abiri had the truth, and the towns that slept safe because of him, and that, the elders say, is glory enough for a wise man, even if it is a glory that fits in the palm of one hand and is never spoken aloud."}]}, {"id": "ologun-ibikunle", "emoji": "⚔️", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#2a0a00,#5a1800)", "category": "ologun", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "7 min", "title": {"en": "Balogun Ibikunle: The Gentle Giant of Ibadan", "yo": "Ibikunle"}, "desc": {"en": "The great Balogun of Ibadan: mighty, generous, the temperate giant who built the city's walls and broke Egba resistance.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Ologun / Warlords", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Jagunjagun, the story of Ibikunle, the great Balogun of Ibadan, the mighty and generous head of the war-chiefs, the temperate giant beside the fierce Ogunmola)", "segments": [{"id": "ologun-ibikunle#s1", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Children, I promised you the rival and partner of Ogunmola, and here he stands, and he is one of the few great war-lords of whom the tellings speak with real warmth. For among the hard men of old Ibadan, men whose greatness was so often braided with cruelty, Ibikunle stands out as something rarer, a giant who was also, by the measure of his brutal age, a man of some restraint and generosity. His name was Ibikunle, and he was the Balogun, the head of the war-chiefs of Ibadan."}, {"id": "ologun-ibikunle#s2", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "He was a big man, children, big in body and big in presence, one of those men whose sheer size and calm seem to fill a room and quiet the shouting in it. He rose, like every Ibadan chief, through war, proving himself on campaign after campaign until he reached the highest military title in the city, the Balogun, the commander of the whole host. And in that seat he became, for a season, the first man of Ibadan."}, {"id": "ologun-ibikunle#s3", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "But it is not his rank that the tales dwell on. It is his temper, in the old sense of that word, the temperature of his soul. Where so many war-lords were quick to kill, quick to seize, quick to crush, Ibikunle is remembered as slower, steadier, more inclined to mercy where mercy was possible, more generous with the men who followed him and even, at times, with the enemies he defeated. In a world of fire, he was something closer to deep water."}, {"id": "ologun-ibikunle#s4", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "This is why the drummers loved him, children, and why his name comes down to us wrapped in affection rather than only in dread. A generous war-lord was a treasure beyond price to the ordinary fighting man. Under a greedy chief, the common soldier bled and starved while the lord grew fat on the plunder. Under Ibikunle, it is said, the spoils were shared with a fairer hand, the followers were cared for, and a man could serve with something like loyalty and even love, not merely out of fear. And so warriors flocked to him, for men will die more readily for a lord they love than for one they only fear."}, {"id": "ologun-ibikunle#s5", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "And here is the great balance of old Ibadan that I want you to see, children, through the pairing of these two men. Ibadan’s strength in its golden age rested on two giants standing together, Ibikunle the Balogun and Ogunmola the Otun, and they were opposites. Ibikunle was the elder statesman, the big calm giant, generous and somewhat cautious, respected by all. Ogunmola was the hungry blade, fierce, ruthless, endlessly ambitious. They were rivals, yes, and the rivalry was real and sometimes bitter. But between them they gave Ibadan both things a great power needs, the caution that keeps a state from destroying itself and the ferocity that lets it destroy its enemies."}, {"id": "ologun-ibikunle#s6", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "While Ibikunle lived and led, he was the check on Ogunmola’s fire. He was the steadying hand, the senior man whose weight and wisdom kept the more reckless energies of the city in some kind of balance. It is often said that as long as Ibikunle stood at the head, Ibadan’s wars were fought with a little more measure, a little more restraint, than they were after he was gone. He led Ibadan through some of the hardest of the mid-century wars, the long and grinding struggle against Ijaye and the coalition of enemies that rose against Ibadan’s growing power. These were enormous conflicts, children, wars that drew in every major Yoruba power and dragged on for years, sieges and battles that emptied whole districts. Through much of this, Ibikunle was the commander, bearing the crushing weight of leading a great army in a great and terrible war."}, {"id": "ologun-ibikunle#s7", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "And it was in the midst of these mighty struggles that his end came. He did not, by the tellings, fall gloriously in a single heroic charge. Like many great men, he wore down, sickened, and died in the long labour of the war he was fighting, leaving the field and the leadership to pass into other, harder hands, chiefly those of his rival Ogunmola."}, {"id": "ologun-ibikunle#s8", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Now the Afonja lesson, children, for even the gentle giant must be weighed honestly and not turned into a saint. Ibikunle was still a war-lord of Ibadan, and Ibadan’s whole enterprise in that age was one of conquest and dominance. However temperate he was compared to his peers, he still led armies that broke towns, still took captives, still fed, however reluctantly, the same machine of war and slavery that scarred the whole land. His gentleness was real, but it was the relative gentleness of a leading soldier in a savage time, not the gentleness of a man of peace. We must not, in our love for the kinder figure, forget that he too was a maker of war."}, {"id": "ologun-ibikunle#s9", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "Hold the two palms. On one side, a commander of conquering armies in a predatory age. On the other, a giant remembered, almost uniquely among his peers, for generosity, for restraint, for being loved and not merely feared, a man who proved that even in the hardest of trades, a person could choose to be a little more humane than the world required him to be."}, {"id": "ologun-ibikunle#s10", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "And that, children, is the lesson I press into your hands from Ibikunle. It is a quiet one, but it may be the most useful of all the war-tales. It is this. The circumstances of a person’s life may force them into a hard trade, a cruel age, a bloody work. Ibikunle did not choose to be born into a century of endless war. But within the hard place where life set him, he still found room to choose. He chose generosity over greed where he could. He chose the fairer share, the steadier hand, the little mercy. He could not escape his violent age, but he refused to let it make him as cruel as it made so many others."}, {"id": "ologun-ibikunle#s11", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "You too, children, will find yourselves in hard places you did not choose. Remember the gentle giant. Even when you cannot choose your circumstances, you can almost always choose, in some small and stubborn way, to be more humane than your circumstances demand. That choice is never taken from you. Ibikunle made it, in the middle of a war, at the head of an army, and it is why, of all the fierce lords of Ibadan, his is one of the names we speak with love. Tomorrow I will carry you out of Ibadan at last, to the other war-lands of the Yoruba, and I will begin with the mighty founder-warrior of Ogbomoso, the Soun Ogunlola, whose spear opened a whole city."}]}, {"id": "ologun-ogedengbe", "emoji": "⚔️", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#2a0a00,#5a1800)", "category": "ologun", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "8 min", "title": {"en": "Ogedengbe: The Lion of Ijesaland", "yo": "Ogedengbe Agbogungboro"}, "desc": {"en": "The war-lord of Ilesa and generalissimo of the Ekitiparapo who held the line against Ibadan.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Ologun / Warlords", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Jagunjagun, the story of Ogedengbe Agbogungboro, the great generalissimo of the Ekitiparapo, the war-lord of Ilesa who held the line against Ibadan in the last great war)", "segments": [{"id": "ologun-ogedengbe#s1", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Children, we go now to the eastern hills, to Ijesaland and the Ekiti country, the land of forested ridges and proud towns, and to the greatest war-lord those hills ever produced. For a long age Ibadan had been the terror of all Yorubaland, its armies ranging out to conquer and to raid, and the eastern peoples, the Ijesa and the Ekiti, had suffered grievously under Ibadan’s heavy hand. And then, in the last great war of the century, these eastern peoples rose up together, and they found a leader mighty enough to make even Ibadan tremble. His name was Ogedengbe, and they called him Agbogungboro, and he was the lion of Ijesaland."}, {"id": "ologun-ogedengbe#s2", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "He was a son of Ilesa, the great town of the Ijesa people, born in the earlier part of the last century into the hard world of the wars. And like so many of the war-lords, his own life carried the wound of the age, for the tellings say that he himself knew captivity in his time, was taken and held as so many were in those slaving wars, before he rose. If that is so, and the accounts suggest it, then it deepens the meaning of everything he later did. A man who had himself felt the chains would fight all the harder against the power that forged them. He rose as a warrior of tremendous personal prowess, a man of the battlefield, huge in courage and in the loyalty he inspired. He gathered followers, he won renown, and he became the foremost war-captain of the Ijesa."}, {"id": "ologun-ogedengbe#s3", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Now hear the great war, children, for it is one of the most important in all our history, the war that finally broke the long dominance of Ibadan. The eastern peoples, the Ijesa and the Ekiti and their allies, weary beyond endurance of Ibadan’s raids and its arrogance, formed a great alliance. They called it the Ekitiparapo, which means, more or less, the Ekiti banded together, the confederation of the eastern towns standing as one at last. And to lead the armies of this alliance, they chose Ogedengbe. He became the generalissimo, the supreme field commander of the Ekitiparapo, the sword of the whole east."}, {"id": "ologun-ogedengbe#s4", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "The war that followed is remembered as the Kiriji war, and the name itself is a marvel, children. For it is said that Kiriji is the sound the great guns made, the boom and the roar of the cannon and the new rifles echoing off the hills, kiriji, kiriji, so that the people named the whole long war after the very sound of its thunder. This was a new kind of war, children. For alongside the old spears and the swords and the bows, both sides now had firearms in great number, and the eastern alliance in particular managed to bring in improved guns from the coast, so that they could at last meet Ibadan’s might with fire of their own."}, {"id": "ologun-ogedengbe#s5", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "And on the high ground of the Ekiti hills, at Kiriji, at Oke-Imesi, the two great hosts dug in and faced each other, and there they stayed, locked in a titanic struggle, for years. Years, children. This was not a war of a single dramatic battle. It was a long, grinding, entrenched war, two mighty armies facing each other across the ridges, season after season, neither able to break the other. Ogedengbe held the eastern line, and Ibadan, for all its famous might, could not push him off the hills."}, {"id": "ologun-ogedengbe#s6", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "That, children, is the towering achievement of Ogedengbe. For generations Ibadan had been all but invincible, the hammer that broke every anvil. And here, in the eastern hills, Ogedengbe and his Ekitiparapo stopped the hammer. They did not merely resist. They held, year upon year, and in holding they broke the spell of Ibadan’s invincibility forever. The lion of Ijesaland proved that Ibadan could be faced and matched, and that the proud eastern towns would not be slaves to Ibadan’s ambition. For every Ijesa and Ekiti man and woman who had suffered under Ibadan’s raids, Ogedengbe was vindication and pride made flesh."}, {"id": "ologun-ogedengbe#s7", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "The long war ended not in a clean victory for either side but in exhaustion and, at last, in the intervention of the British from the coast, who by now were pressing their power inland and who brokered a peace between the weary combatants, freezing the armies where they stood and beginning the process that would soon bring all Yorubaland under colonial rule. So the age of the great wars closed, and Ogedengbe, the mighty generalissimo, lived on into the beginning of the new colonial era, a giant of the old world watching the new one arrive."}, {"id": "ologun-ogedengbe#s8", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Now the Afonja lesson, children, honestly. Ogedengbe was a war-lord of his age, and that age was cruel on every side. The Ekitiparapo were defending their freedom against Ibadan, yes, and that is a noble cause, and it is right that the east remembers him as a hero and a liberator. But war is war, and the eastern armies too took captives, too dealt in the harsh coin of the times. And we should note, too, that the peace which ended the long stalemate came at the price of the colonizer’s growing grip on the whole land, a bitter irony, that the exhaustion of the Yoruba fighting one another opened the door for a foreign power to master them all. No war-lord of that age, however heroic in his own cause, is free of the deep tragedy that the Yoruba, one people, spent that whole century tearing at one another until a stranger walked in and took the house."}, {"id": "ologun-ogedengbe#s9", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "Hold the two palms. On one side, the tragedy of a great man’s genius spent in the long civil war that weakened the whole nation before the colonizer. On the other, a true hero of the eastern peoples, a man who may himself have worn the chains of the slaving wars and who rose to break the tyranny of the mightiest army in the land, who held the line for the freedom of his people through years of thunder, and who stands forever as the proof that Ijesaland and the Ekiti bowed to no one."}, {"id": "ologun-ogedengbe#s10", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "So remember Ogedengbe Agbogungboro, children, the lion of Ijesaland. Remember the man who stopped the unstoppable, who held the eastern hills against Ibadan through the long years of Kiriji, whose name is still a source of fierce pride to every son and daughter of the east. Remember that no power, however mighty, however long it has ruled by fear, is truly invincible, and that a people banded together in a just cause, with a great leader at their head, can face down the hammer that all others fled. And remember the sorrow underneath it too, the sorrow of a people so busy fighting one another that they did not see the stranger at the gate. That, children, is the deepest lesson of the whole war-age, and I will speak of it again before these tales are done."}, {"id": "ologun-ogedengbe#s11", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "Tomorrow I will bring you the fierce war-chief of Oke-Imesi, the brave Fabunmi who lit the spark of the eastern rising, and then the older and more legendary war-figures whose names, like Onikoyi and the fighting lords of the deep past, come to us wrapped in the mist of the very old times."}]}, {"id": "ologun-warlords-code", "emoji": "⚔️", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#2a0a00,#5a1800)", "category": "ologun", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "6 min", "title": {"en": "The Warlord’s Code: Rules of Yoruba Warfare", "yo": "The Warlord’s Code: Rules of Yoruba Warfare"}, "desc": {"en": "The unwritten rules of Yoruba warfare: what a warrior could and could not do.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Ologun / Warlords", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Ologun / Kakanfo, from “The Warlord’s Code: Rules of Yoruba Warfare”)", "segments": [{"id": "ologun-warlords-code#s1", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "There is an idea, common among people who have never studied it, that war among the old Yoruba was simply chaos, that armies met and hacked at each other until one side ran, and that the only rule was to be stronger and crueler than the other man. Anyone who believes that has misunderstood the whole thing. Yoruba warfare was a craft, and like every craft it had its laws, its customs, its unwritten code that every serious war chief carried in his bones. The code was not written on paper. It was written in the training of a lifetime, passed from old warriors to young ones, and a commander who broke it did not merely lose honor. Very often he lost the war, and sometimes his head."}, {"id": "ologun-warlords-code#s2", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "The first law was the law of the ground. A Yoruba general did not simply march at the enemy. He read the land the way a hunter reads the forest, because the land, more than numbers, decided battles. The great lesson of the age, bought with the blood of a fallen empire, was that terrain could beat cavalry, that a smaller force in the right ground was stronger than a larger force in the wrong ground. So the war chief studied the forest and the river and the hill before he studied the enemy, and the best of them could look at a stretch of country and see, laid out before them like a board, exactly where a battle there would be won or lost."}, {"id": "ologun-warlords-code#s3", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "The second law was the law of the eyes. No general worth the name moved without scouts, without spies in the enemy town, without the long patient work of knowing before you struck. To march into a battle blind was not brave, it was a crime against your own men, and a commander who lost soldiers because he had not bothered to learn what waited for them was despised even if he happened to win. Knowledge came first and iron came second. The war chiefs said that the battle was mostly over before the first shot, decided in the quiet days beforehand by the side that had learned the most and given away the least. A great warrior who could not keep his own plans secret was only half a warrior."}, {"id": "ologun-warlords-code#s4", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "The third law was the law of the powder and the season. Wars were fought in the dry months, when the roads were hard and the streams were low and an army could move and eat, and they paused in the rains, when the forest turned to mud and swallowed men and horses alike. Powder and shot were precious and hard to come by, and a wise chief hoarded them like a miser and spent them like a surgeon, in tight controlled volleys at the moment they would do the most, never in wild wasteful firing that emptied a man’s pouch and left him with a club in his hands when the real crisis came."}, {"id": "ologun-warlords-code#s5", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "But the code was not only about winning. It had another face, and this is the part that people who imagine only chaos always miss. There were customs of restraint, of mercy, of the limits a decent war chief would not cross. There were rules about the treatment of the captured, about the sparing of certain people, about the sanctity of a messenger under a green branch who came to talk, about the way a beaten town might submit and be spared the worst rather than annihilated. A war fought entirely without limit is a war that never ends, because it leaves the beaten side with nothing but hatred and no reason ever to stop fighting, and the wisest of the war chiefs understood this in their bones. They fought hard, but they left doors open, because they knew that the enemy of today might have to become the neighbor of tomorrow, and a wise man does not burn every bridge he may one day need to cross."}, {"id": "ologun-warlords-code#s6", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "There was also a code of the warrior’s own conduct, a matter of bearing and honor. A war chief was expected to lead from the front, to eat what his men ate, to face what he asked them to face. A commander who hung back safe while he spent other men’s lives earned a contempt that no victory could wash away. Courage was not enough by itself, for the age was full of brave fools who got their men killed, but a leader without courage had nothing, because men will not follow into fire a man who will not walk into it beside them. The greatest of the war chiefs, the ones whose names outlived them, were almost always men who carried their share of the danger and more, and their soldiers loved them for it and fought like lions under them."}, {"id": "ologun-warlords-code#s7", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So the elders tell of the warlord’s code to correct a lie and to teach a truth. The lie is that war among their ancestors was mere savagery. The truth is that it was a demanding discipline with its own deep intelligence, a craft that rewarded knowledge over rage, patience over haste, restraint over cruelty, and honor over mere strength. And in that old code, the storytellers say, there is wisdom that reaches far beyond the battlefield, for the same laws that made a great war chief, know the ground, keep your eyes open, spend your strength wisely, leave a door open for peace, and never ask of others what you will not do yourself, are the same laws that make a great leader of any kind, in any age, in war or in peace."}]}, {"id": "ologun-balogun-ibu", "emoji": "⚔️", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#2a0a00,#5a1800)", "category": "ologun", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "5 min", "title": {"en": "Balogun Ibu: The Man They Called the Mountain", "yo": "Ibu"}, "desc": {"en": "Balogun Ibu, the war-chief they called the Mountain.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Ologun / Warlords", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Ologun / Kakanfo, from “Balogun Ibu: The Man They Called the Mountain”)", "segments": [{"id": "ologun-balogun-ibu#s1", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Some men earn a name in war that says everything about them in a single word, and the war chief the people called Ibu, the Mountain, was one of these. They did not call him the Mountain because he was tall, though he was. They called him the Mountain because of what he did on a battlefield, which was to plant himself in one place and become a thing that could not be moved. Armies broke against him the way water breaks against rock. Charges that had scattered other men simply washed over him and drained away, and when the noise died down he was still standing exactly where he had been, and that, more than any charge of his own, was how he won."}, {"id": "ologun-balogun-ibu#s2", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "In an age that loved the dashing warrior, the man of the sudden brilliant stroke, Balogun Ibu was something different and rarer. He was the master of the defense, the anvil rather than the hammer, and the older war chiefs knew that battles are won as often by the man who cannot be broken as by the man who breaks others. Any hot-blooded young captain could lead a charge. It took a particular and unglamorous kind of greatness to hold a line hour after hour under everything the enemy could throw, to keep frightened men standing shoulder to shoulder when every instinct screamed at them to run, and to still be there, unmoved, when the enemy at last ran out of fury. That was Ibu’s gift, and in the long grinding wars of his time it was worth more than a dozen brilliant charges."}, {"id": "ologun-balogun-ibu#s3", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "There is a battle remembered in the old telling where Ibu earned his name for all time. A great enemy host had come down meaning to smash through and take the town behind, and the whole weight of their attack fell on the section of the line that Ibu held. Wave after wave came against him through a long and terrible day. His men fell around him, the powder ran low, the sun climbed and burned and began to sink, and still the enemy came, certain that one more push must surely break this stubborn knot of defenders. And still Ibu held. He walked his line under fire, calm as a man walking through his own compound, steadying this one, pulling that one back into place, and where he stood his men would not run, because a man does not run while the mountain beside him stands. By the time the enemy’s fury was finally spent, their best had been thrown against Ibu’s line all day and had gained not one step of ground, and it was they, not he, who broke at last and streamed away in the dusk."}, {"id": "ologun-balogun-ibu#s4", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "The victory belonged to the whole army, but everyone knew whose day it truly was. Other chiefs led the counterstroke that turned the broken enemy into a rout, and they took their share of the praise. But it was Ibu’s immovable defense that had made the counterstroke possible, for you cannot chase a fleeing enemy until you have first refused to be the one who flees. He understood a truth that impatient men never learn, that in war, as in much of life, the side that endures one moment longer than the other is the side that wins, and that there is a fierce and quiet heroism in simply refusing to fall. He asked for no songs about himself, and when the praise-singers came he sent them to sing about the young men who had charged, saying that holding still was no great feat and any post could do it. It was not true, and everyone knew it was not true, and his modesty only made the soldiers love him more."}, {"id": "ologun-balogun-ibu#s5", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So the elders tell of Balogun Ibu, the Mountain, to honor a kind of strength the world is always too quick to overlook. We remember the men who charge and conquer, the bold ones, the ones who make the ground shake. We forget the men who simply would not move, and yet without them the bold ones would have had nowhere to charge from and nothing to come home to. Ibu’s story teaches that endurance is its own form of courage, that to stand firm is sometimes braver than to rush forward, and that in every fight and every trial there is a place for the one who plants his feet and says, quietly, that here he will remain, and whatever comes against him, he will not be moved. The world needs its hammers. But it is saved, again and again, by its anvils."}]}, {"id": "ologun-night-raid", "emoji": "⚔️", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#2a0a00,#5a1800)", "category": "ologun", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "6 min", "title": {"en": "The Night Raid: Tactics of the Yoruba Forest War", "yo": "The Night Raid: Tactics of the Yoruba Forest War"}, "desc": {"en": "The tactics of the Yoruba forest war and the terror of the night raid.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Ologun / Warlords", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Ologun / Kakanfo, from “The Night Raid: Tactics of the Yoruba Forest War”)", "segments": [{"id": "ologun-night-raid#s1", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "The great pitched battles are what the songs remember, the two armies facing each other in the open, the volleys and the charges, the day that decides a war. But anyone who thinks that is how the forest wars of the Yoruba were mostly fought has never lain awake in a bush camp in the small hours listening for the sound that every soldier feared more than the roar of any daylight battle. That sound was the soft one. The snapped twig that should not have snapped. The bird that called at the wrong hour. The silence where the night insects had suddenly stopped. Because in the forest war, the deadliest thing was not the army you could see coming across a field. It was the raid that came out of the dark when you thought you were safe."}, {"id": "ologun-night-raid#s2", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "The night raid was a craft of its own, and the war chiefs who mastered it could do more damage with a handful of chosen men than a whole host could do in open battle. The forest itself was the weapon. Under its canopy a small band that knew the paths could move unseen right up to the edge of a sleeping enemy camp, and in a few violent minutes could set the powder store ablaze, stampede the animals, cut down the sentries, fire the granaries, seize a captive of value, and be gone back into the trees before the camp had even understood what was happening. A raid like that did not need to kill many men to win. It needed only to destroy the thing the enemy could not fight without, and to leave behind it a terror that no daylight courage could quite cure."}, {"id": "ologun-night-raid#s3", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "The men chosen for such work were a breed apart. Not the biggest or the loudest, but the quietest, the most patient, the ones who could move without sound and wait without moving and keep their nerve in the dark where every shadow is an enemy. They trained to know the forest the way a fish knows water, to travel by the feel of the ground under bare feet, to read the paths by starlight, to tell the enemy’s sentries from his sleepers by the small sounds a camp makes at night. They carried little, for anything that clinked or caught was a betrayal, and they blackened themselves with ash and mud so that no gleam of skin would give them away. Above all they mastered the hardest discipline of the raider, which was patience. A raid might mean lying flat and still at the very edge of the enemy for hours, close enough to hear the sentries talk, waiting for the one moment when the watch grew tired and careless, and only then, when the whole camp had sunk into the deep sleep of the middle of the night, uncoiling all at once into sudden violence."}, {"id": "ologun-night-raid#s4", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "The purpose of the raid was rarely simple slaughter. It was to break the enemy’s mind. An army in the field could bear the losses of open battle, for those were expected, understood, part of the bargain of war. What it could not bear for long was the slow poison of never feeling safe. When a camp had been raided once in the dark, every night after became a torment. Men would not sleep. Sentries fired at shadows and wasted powder on the wind. The smallest sound sent whole companies scrambling for their weapons at nothing. And an army that cannot sleep is an army that is losing the war whether or not it ever loses a battle, because exhausted, frightened, jumping men make bad soldiers by day. The master raiders knew this. They understood that they were not merely burning powder stores. They were stealing the enemy’s rest, and a man robbed of his rest is halfway to being robbed of his courage."}, {"id": "ologun-night-raid#s5", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "But there was another side to the craft, for the forest cut both ways, and every chief who could raid also had to defend against raids. This bred a whole art of the camp, the choosing of ground that could not easily be crept upon, the clearing of the near bush so that no man could approach unseen, the placing of sentries in rings and the changing of them often, the use of dogs and geese whose ears were sharper than any man’s, the false fires lit away from where the men truly slept. The best commanders slept lightly and never twice in a way the enemy could predict, and they trained their men that the danger was greatest exactly when everything felt most quiet. In the forest war, comfort was the enemy’s ally, and the chief who let his camp grow lazy in a peaceful stretch was the chief who woke one night to fire and screaming."}, {"id": "ologun-night-raid#s6", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So the elders tell of the night raid to teach that war is fought as much in the mind as on the field, and that the greatest damage is often done not by the largest force but by the cleverest one. The raiders remind us that patience is a weapon, that knowledge of the ground is worth more than numbers, and that the fight is often won by whoever can strike where the other feels safe. And the defenders remind us of the harder, humbler lesson, that the price of safety is watchfulness that never sleeps, and that the moment we feel most secure is exactly the moment we are in the most danger, for it is then, and not before, that the quiet ones come out of the dark."}]}, {"id": "ologun-fall-of-modakeke", "emoji": "⚔️", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#2a0a00,#5a1800)", "category": "ologun", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "6 min", "title": {"en": "The Fall of Modakeke: When Brothers Made War", "yo": "Modakeke"}, "desc": {"en": "When brothers made war: the long, bitter conflict between Ife and Modakeke.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Ologun / Warlords", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Ologun / Kakanfo, from “The Fall of Modakeke: When Brothers Made War”)", "segments": [{"id": "ologun-fall-of-modakeke#s1", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Of all the wars in the long turbulent history of the Yoruba, none is sadder than the one fought between Ife and Modakeke, because it was a war between people who should have been one people, a quarrel between neighbors so close that their compounds all but touched, and it went on, off and on, for the better part of a hundred years. There is a special bitterness to a war like that. When you fight a distant enemy, you can hate him cleanly and go home when it is over. But when you fight the town across the stream, the town your grandmother came from, the town whose market you shopped in as a child, the hatred has nowhere to go when the fighting stops, and it festers, and it waits, and it breaks out again in the next generation, and the next."}, {"id": "ologun-fall-of-modakeke#s2", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "The roots of it lay in the great scattering of the nineteenth century, when the fall of Oyo sent waves of refugees flooding south into the forest. Many of these displaced Oyo people were settled near Ile-Ife, the ancient holy city, the cradle of the whole Yoruba world, and in time they built up their own large and thriving town beside it, and called it Modakeke. For a while host and guest lived side by side. But land is land, and pride is pride, and a guest who grows strong and numerous and prosperous begins to look, to the anxious eyes of the host, less like a guest and more like a rival planted in his own front yard. Grievances gathered on both sides like clouds before a storm, over land, over tribute, over old slights remembered and new ones invented, until at last the storm broke."}, {"id": "ologun-fall-of-modakeke#s3", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "What followed was not one war but a long chain of them, flaring up, dying down, flaring again across generation after generation. The two towns burned each other’s farms and raided each other’s roads and fought pitched battles almost within sight of the sacred groves of Ife itself. Modakeke, for all that it was the younger town, proved a fierce and formidable fighter, and there were long stretches when it held the upper hand and pressed Ife hard. Ife, the mother-city of all the Yoruba, the place from which the whole race believed itself to have sprung, found itself locked in a death-grip with a town of its own making, a town of its own kinsmen, and neither could quite destroy the other, and neither would let the quarrel rest. Alliances shifted. The great powers of the day, Ibadan and the others, meddled and took sides for their own reasons, and the fire that should have been smothered in its first season was fanned instead for decades."}, {"id": "ologun-fall-of-modakeke#s4", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "The cruelty of it was the cruelty peculiar to civil wars everywhere. These were not strangers meeting on a distant field. They spoke the same language, worshipped the same orisa, traced their descent to the same ancient source. Families were split. A man might have kin on the other side of the line, might find himself fighting cousins, might burn a compound where his own mother had been born. The wounds such a war leaves do not heal the way the wounds of an ordinary war heal, because there is no clean line between us and them, only a line drawn through the middle of what was once a single people, and every act of the war tears at something that both sides, in their hearts, know should never have been torn. That is why the Ife-Modakeke quarrel is remembered with such sorrow and not with the pride that other wars are given. There were no true victors in it, only a long shared wound."}, {"id": "ologun-fall-of-modakeke#s5", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "The end, when it finally came, came less through victory than through exhaustion and the slow intervention of a wider peace, as the colonial order settled over the land and imposed a stillness that the two towns themselves had never managed to reach. Modakeke suffered greatly in the last convulsions of the long quarrel, its people scattered and its town broken more than once, only to gather and rebuild, for a people are hard to erase when their roots are in the ground and their dead are in the soil. In time the guns fell silent for good, and Ife and Modakeke were left to be what they had always truly been beneath the hatred, which was neighbors, kinsmen, two branches grown from one old root, condemned by history to share a stream, a border, and a sorrow."}, {"id": "ologun-fall-of-modakeke#s6", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So the elders tell of the fall of Modakeke and the long war with Ife not to celebrate any warrior, but to hand down a warning that reaches into every family, every town, every people. The most dangerous quarrels are not the ones with strangers. They are the ones with our own, the ones where pride and grievance turn brother against brother and neighbor against neighbor, for those are the quarrels that do not end when the fighting stops, but sink into the ground and wait. Guard against them, the storytellers say. Swallow the small insult before it grows into the great one. Remember the shared root before you draw the line. Because a house divided against itself burns hottest of all, and the fire, once lit between kinsmen, can smoulder for a hundred years."}]}, {"id": "ologun-osi-ologun", "emoji": "⚔️", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#2a0a00,#5a1800)", "category": "ologun", "rating": 9.4, "duration": "5 min", "title": {"en": "Osi Ologun: The Left-Hand General", "yo": "Osi Ologun"}, "desc": {"en": "The left-hand general and the role of the second-in-command.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Ologun / Warlords", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Ologun / Kakanfo, from “Osi Ologun: The Left-Hand General”)", "segments": [{"id": "ologun-osi-ologun#s1", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Among the Yoruba, the right hand is the hand of honor. You greet with it, you eat with it, you give and receive with it, and to offer the left hand is very nearly an insult. So when the war chiefs of the old towns arranged their commanders, the place of highest honor, the right wing of the army, went to the senior general, and the left wing went to another, and the title of that second man carried the word for left, Osi. To be the Osi was to be, by the very name, the second man, the left hand, the one who was not first. And this is the story of a general who wore that name and turned it, by sheer brilliance, into something greater than the title of first was ever meant to be."}, {"id": "ologun-osi-ologun#s2", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "He had been passed over for the first place, whether by the accident of birth order, or the politics of the chiefs, or simply because another man had come up before him, the tellers do not all agree. What they agree on is that he had every reason for bitterness and showed none. A lesser man in the second seat spends his strength resenting the first, undermining him, hoping quietly for him to fail so that the seat might come open. The Osi did the opposite. He decided that if he could not be the first in rank, he would be the first in skill, and he poured into the mastery of his craft all the energy that a bitter man would have wasted on envy. He studied the wars of the past, he trained his wing until it was the sharpest instrument in the whole army, and he made himself so good at the work of the left hand that commanders began to whisper that the left wing under the Osi was worth more than the right."}, {"id": "ologun-osi-ologun#s3", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "The left wing, in the arrangement of a Yoruba battle, was often where the hardest and most delicate work fell, the flanking movement, the sudden turn, the stroke that came from the side while the enemy’s eyes were fixed on the center. It was work that demanded timing and daring and a cool head, for a flank attack launched too early or too late was worse than useless, and the general who led it had to read the whole shifting shape of a battle and know the one exact moment to strike. This was the Osi’s genius. Again and again, in battle after battle, it was his wing, coming in hard from the unexpected side at the perfect instant, that turned a struggle into a rout. The right wing held the enemy by the nose. The left hand, the Osi, delivered the blow that broke him."}, {"id": "ologun-osi-ologun#s4", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "What made him truly great, though, was not only his skill but his spirit. He never let the second seat sour him, and he never let his growing fame tempt him to grab for the first. When the senior general commanded, the Osi obeyed, and obeyed loyally, and gave his whole art to making the other man’s plan succeed. He understood something that eats away most ambitious men before they ever learn it, that to be second and excellent is worth infinitely more than to be first and mediocre, and that the man who does his own work superbly, whatever his rank, is in the only way that finally matters the first among them all. His loyalty made him trusted, his skill made him feared, and between the two he became, without ever holding the senior title, the most valued warrior in the army. The right hand had the honor. The left hand won the battles."}, {"id": "ologun-osi-ologun#s5", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So the elders tell of the Osi Ologun, the left-hand general, to teach one of the hardest and most freeing lessons a person can learn. You will not always be given the first place. Life, and other people, and plain luck will often seat you second, or third, or nowhere near the front at all. And in that seat you have a choice. You can spend your days poisoned by envy of the one ahead of you, and be small, or you can pour yourself into your own work until you are so good at it that rank stops mattering, and be great. The left hand is called the lesser hand. But the Osi proved that it is not the honor of the seat that makes the man, it is the excellence he brings to it, and that a man who masters the work he is given, however humble its name, can become the finest of them all."}]}, {"id": "ologun-dane-gun", "emoji": "⚔️", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#2a0a00,#5a1800)", "category": "ologun", "rating": 9.4, "duration": "5 min", "title": {"en": "The Dane Gun: When New Weapons Changed Old Wars", "yo": "The Dane Gun: When New Weapons Changed Old Wars"}, "desc": {"en": "How the Dane gun and new firearms changed the old ways of Yoruba war.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Ologun / Warlords", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Ologun / Kakanfo, from “The Dane Gun: When New Weapons Changed Old Wars”)", "segments": [{"id": "ologun-dane-gun#s1", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "There are moments in the life of a people when a single new thing arrives from outside and quietly rewrites everything, and for the Yoruba war-world of the nineteenth century, one of those things was the gun. The Europeans on the coast called it by many names, but in the towns of the interior it came to be known simply as the Dane gun, a long-barrelled flintlock musket traded up from the coast in enormous numbers, and its coming changed the shape of war more deeply than any single general ever did. For the old world of the Yoruba warrior had been a world of the bow, the spear, the sword, and above all the horse, and the gun came into that world like a stone dropped into still water, and the ripples reached every corner of the land."}, {"id": "ologun-dane-gun#s2", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Consider first what the gun did to the horse. The great terror of the northern cavalry had rested on the charge, the wall of galloping animals that no line of men on foot could stand against with spear and sword alone. But a massed volley of musket fire could break a charge before it struck home, could bring down horses in tangled heaps and turn the beautiful order of the cavalry into carnage. The gun, in the hands of disciplined foot soldiers in the right ground, was the answer to the horse, and once the forest towns had guns in quantity, the age of the unstoppable cavalry was over. Power shifted from the open savanna, where the horse was king, to the forest, where the gunman lay hidden until the moment to fire, and the whole center of gravity of the Yoruba world shifted south with it, into the trees, where Ibadan and the forest towns rose to greatness."}, {"id": "ologun-dane-gun#s3", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "But the gun was a hard master as well as a mighty servant. The Dane gun was a crude thing, slow to load, fouled easily, misfired in the damp, and was wildly inaccurate beyond close range. It did not make war cleaner or quicker. If anything it made it longer and more grinding, for now a battle was a matter of two sides blazing away at each other from behind cover, neither able to close and finish it, and wars that might once have been decided in a sharp afternoon now dragged into sieges that lasted years. The endless crackle of musketry gave the great eastern war its very name, Kiriji, the word the ear made of the ceaseless guns. The gun did not end the age of long wars. It helped create it."}, {"id": "ologun-dane-gun#s4", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "And there was a deeper change still, one that reached beyond the battlefield into the very soul of the wars. The guns and the powder came from the coast, and the coast wanted something in return, and the thing it wanted, more than anything, was people. Captives. Slaves for the ships. So the towns that wanted guns had to make war to take captives to trade for the guns to make more war to take more captives, and the whole terrible wheel turned faster and faster, a wheel greased with human beings. The gun did not only change how the Yoruba fought. It changed why they fought, binding their wars to the hunger of the slave ships in a way that poisoned everything it touched. The weapon that a town needed to defend itself was bought with the freedom of its neighbors’ children, and no one could step off the wheel without leaving himself defenseless before those who stayed on it."}, {"id": "ologun-dane-gun#s5", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So the elders tell of the Dane gun to teach a truth about the power that comes from outside, the mighty new thing that seems at first a pure gift. The gun made weak towns strong and broke the tyranny of the horse, and in that it was a liberation. But it also lengthened the wars, deepened the slaughter, and chained the whole land to the cruel commerce of the coast, and in that it was a curse. It was both at once, as such things almost always are. The storytellers do not say the gun was good or evil. They say it was powerful, and that power from a stranger’s hand always comes with a hook in it, and that the wise are those who ask, before they reach for the new weapon that will make them strong, what exactly they will have to become, and to whom they will have to sell their souls, in order to keep it."}]}, {"id": "ologun-ajele", "emoji": "⚔️", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#2a0a00,#5a1800)", "category": "ologun", "rating": 9.4, "duration": "5 min", "title": {"en": "Ajele: The Men the Towns Hated", "yo": "Ajele"}, "desc": {"en": "The Ajele: the resident agents of the war-cities whom the subject towns hated.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Ologun / Warlords", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Ologun / Kakanfo, from “Ajele: The Men the Towns Hated”)", "segments": [{"id": "ologun-ajele#s1", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "When a Yoruba town conquered another town in the wars of the nineteenth century, it did not always burn it or carry off its people. Often it did something that was, in the long run, more galling to the conquered than any single sack. It left the town standing, let its people keep their houses and their farms and their own chiefs, and then it planted in their midst a single man to represent the conqueror’s power and to make sure the tribute flowed and the orders were obeyed. That man was the ajele, the resident agent of the overlord town, and of all the figures thrown up by the wars, none was more universally, more bitterly hated than he."}, {"id": "ologun-ajele#s2", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "The ajele was not a soldier, though soldiers stood behind him. He was something subtler and, to the people who had to live under him, something worse. He was the daily face of their subjection. Where a battle was a single terrible event that ended, the ajele was a permanent humiliation that never ended, a stranger living among them who answered not to them but to a distant master, who took a cut of their harvest and their trade, who sat in judgment over disputes that were none of his business, who could summon a man and shame him, or fine him, or worse, and against whom there was no appeal except to the very power he served. He was the tax collector, the spy, the enforcer, and the constant living reminder that the town was no longer its own, all rolled into one resented person."}, {"id": "ologun-ajele#s3", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "The office bred its own kind of man, and rarely a good one. Some ajele were merely stern servants doing an unpleasant job with a measure of fairness, but the position tempted even decent men and ruined weak ones, because it handed a stranger great power over people who had no way to hold him to account. Far from his own town, answerable only to a master who cared mostly that the tribute arrived, an ajele could squeeze and bully and enrich himself, and many did. They demanded more than their due and pocketed the difference. They took the prettiest goods from the market without paying. They leaned on the local chiefs and made them dance. And the people watched, and swallowed their rage, and remembered, because a people can be conquered and still keep a long and patient memory of every insult, storing them up the way a dry season stores the fuel for a fire."}, {"id": "ologun-ajele#s4", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "That stored rage was the ajele’s true legacy, and in the end it was the undoing of the whole system that created him. When the eastern towns finally rose against Ibadan in the great Kiriji war, the fury that drove them was not only about tribute or land in the abstract. It was personal, and it had a face, and the face was the ajele’s. The first thing many rising towns did was to seize or kill the resident agents who had lorded over them for so long, and the settlements that ended the wars almost always included, as a first and non-negotiable demand, the withdrawal of the ajele forever. The men the towns had hated became the symbol of everything the towns would no longer endure, and their removal was, more than any treaty clause, the thing that told a freed people they were truly free again."}, {"id": "ologun-ajele#s5", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So the elders tell of the ajele not to honor him, for there is little to honor, but to teach a lesson about the nature of power and the wisdom of the ruler. A conqueror can win a battle with an army, but he cannot hold a people with an army forever, and the ajele was the tool of those who tried. The lesson the storytellers draw is that domination which humiliates plants the seed of its own destruction, that a power resting on daily resentment is a power counting down to a reckoning, and that the wise overlord, if he must rule others at all, rules with a light enough hand that the ruled do not spend every day dreaming of his ruin. Ibadan was mighty, mightier than any single one of the towns it held. But it forgot that a hundred small daily cruelties, gathered up in a hundred patient memories, can in the end outweigh the greatest army, and that the men its towns hated most were, in the end, the men who cost it the most."}]}, {"id": "ologun-ode-remo", "emoji": "⚔️", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#2a0a00,#5a1800)", "category": "ologun", "rating": 9.4, "duration": "5 min", "title": {"en": "Ode Remo: The Town That Chose Negotiation", "yo": "Ode Remo"}, "desc": {"en": "The town that chose negotiation over war.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Ologun / Warlords", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Ologun / Kakanfo, from “Ode Remo: The Town That Chose Negotiation”)", "segments": [{"id": "ologun-ode-remo#s1", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "In an age of warriors, when the drums beat for war and a town’s honor was measured by the number of its enemies and the boldness of its Balogun, there is a particular kind of courage that gets no songs, and it is the courage to say no to the fighting. This is the story of Ode Remo, a town in the Remo country, and of a choice it made that the hot-blooded men of the day sneered at and the wise men of later generations came to understand. When the great powers came pressing, and the easy, honorable, expected thing was to take up arms and be crushed gloriously, Ode Remo chose instead the harder and less glamorous road of negotiation, and by choosing it, saved itself."}, {"id": "ologun-ode-remo#s2", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "The Remo towns sat in a dangerous place, caught between the great forces of the age, the might of Ibadan pressing from the interior, the Egba and the Ijebu with their own ambitions and their control of the vital roads to the coast, the trade in guns and salt and slaves flowing through their country like a river everyone wanted to dam for himself. A small town in such a position had, on the face of it, only two choices, and both were bad. It could resist the strong and be destroyed, or it could submit meekly and be swallowed. Ode Remo, guided by chiefs who valued the survival of their people above the applause of the war-drums, found a narrow third road between the two, and walked it with a skill that was its own kind of heroism."}, {"id": "ologun-ode-remo#s3", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "They negotiated. Where a prouder town would have shut its gates and dared the enemy to come, Ode Remo sent envoys. It played the great powers against one another, leaning toward this one when that one grew too strong, offering its friendship and its position on the roads as things of value to be bargained with rather than simply seized. It made itself more useful alive and independent than it would have been conquered and resentful, and it swallowed a great deal of pride in the doing, accepting slights and demands that set the young warriors’ teeth on edge, because the chiefs understood a thing the young men did not, that a town that is still standing can recover its pride tomorrow, but a town that has been burned to prove its pride today has no tomorrow at all."}, {"id": "ologun-ode-remo#s4", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "It was not weakness, though the warriors called it that. It took a cool head and a strong nerve to sit across from a mightier power and bargain, to know exactly how far you could bend before you broke, to give ground on the small things so as to hold the great one, which was the town’s very existence. The diplomat who kept Ode Remo alive risked more in some ways than the Balogun who led a charge, for the warrior needed only courage, but the negotiator needed courage and patience and cunning and the strength to endure being called a coward by his own people while he quietly saved their lives. And when the wars at last burned out, and the proud towns that had chosen glorious resistance lay in ashes with their people scattered or enslaved, Ode Remo was still there, still whole, its people alive, its market open, its children playing in streets that had never been put to the torch."}, {"id": "ologun-ode-remo#s5", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So the elders tell of Ode Remo to balance all the tales of warriors with one tale of a different kind of wisdom. Not every problem is solved by the sword, and not every refusal to fight is cowardice. There is a time to stand and die, and the songs are right to honor it, but there is also a time to bend, to bargain, to swallow pride and save the people, and that choice, though it wins no praise-songs, is sometimes the braver and the wiser one. The warrior who dies gloriously is remembered for a season. The town that survives is remembered by its living grandchildren, generation after generation, who owe their very breath to the unfashionable courage of the men who chose to talk when everyone else was shouting for war."}]}, {"id": "ologun-ogundipe", "emoji": "⚔️", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#2a0a00,#5a1800)", "category": "ologun", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "5 min", "title": {"en": "Ogundipe: The Egba General Who Held the Line", "yo": "Ogundipe"}, "desc": {"en": "The Egba general who held the line for Abeokuta.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Ologun / Warlords", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Ologun / Kakanfo, from “Ogundipe: The Egba General Who Held the Line”)", "segments": [{"id": "ologun-ogundipe#s1", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Abeokuta, the city under the rock, was born out of catastrophe. When the wars of the early nineteenth century shattered the old Egba towns and sent their people fleeing, a scattered and broken folk gathered at last beneath the great grey shelter of Olumo Rock and built there a new city out of refugees, a city whose very name, Abeokuta, means the underneath of the rock. It had no ancient royal pedigree, only the desperate unity of survivors, and from its earliest days it had to fight simply to go on existing, hemmed in by Dahomey to the west and Ibadan to the north, coveted by every power for its position on the roads to the coast. Into the leadership of that embattled city, in its hour of greatest danger, rose the general the Egba remember as the man who held the line, Ogundipe."}, {"id": "ologun-ogundipe#s2", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "The greatest test came from the west, from the kingdom of Dahomey, whose kings had made war into a machine and whose armies included the famous companies of women warriors whose ferocity was spoken of with dread across the whole region. Dahomey had sworn to break Abeokuta, to erase the upstart city of refugees, and more than once its host came down against the walls under the rock. These were not skirmishes. They were assaults meant to end a people, and the survival of the Egba nation hung on whether the city could hold. It was in the crucible of these Dahomey wars that Ogundipe made his name, organizing the defense of the walls, positioning the guns, steadying the fighters, and turning a city of frightened refugees into a fortress that the most feared army in the west could not crack."}, {"id": "ologun-ogundipe#s3", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "Ogundipe understood that Abeokuta’s strength was not in matching the great powers charge for charge in the open, for the city could not win that kind of war. Its strength was in the rock, in the walls, in the fierce unity of a people who knew that behind them was not another town to retreat to but the sea, the end, extinction. He built the defense around that truth. He made every attacker pay a price at the walls that no glory could justify, so that even when Abeokuta could not defeat an enemy in the field, it could exhaust him, bleed him, make the taking of the city cost more than the city was worth. Time and again the great hosts came, and time and again they broke against the rock and the walls and the stubborn defense Ogundipe had built, and drew off, and Abeokuta remained."}, {"id": "ologun-ogundipe#s4", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "But Ogundipe was more than a defender of walls. He was a statesman in a warrior’s harness, and he saw what many of the pure fighting men of his age did not, that the world was changing, that new powers were rising on the coast, that the missionaries and the traders and the distant empire of the British were forces that could not simply be fought with muskets and had to be dealt with by cunning as much as by courage. He steered Abeokuta through that treacherous new water, using the outsiders where they were useful, resisting them where they overreached, always with the single fixed purpose of keeping the Egba nation independent and alive. He held not only the physical line at the walls but the harder political line, the line of Abeokuta’s freedom, in an age when freedom was slipping from the grasp of towns all around."}, {"id": "ologun-ogundipe#s5", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So the elders tell of Ogundipe to honor the guardian, the general whose greatness lay not in conquest but in preservation. He conquered no empire and left no ruins with his name upon them. What he left was greater and quieter, a people who survived, a city that stood when it might so easily have fallen, a nation that kept its name and its face through an age that swallowed so many others. His story teaches that there is a heroism in simply holding on, in being the wall behind which a frightened people can go on living, and that the man who saves what already exists deserves a place beside the man who wins new glory, for it is one thing to build a house, and quite another, in a burning street, to be the one who will not let it burn."}]}, {"id": "ologun-fall-of-owu", "emoji": "⚔️", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#2a0a00,#5a1800)", "category": "ologun", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "9 min", "title": {"en": "The Fall of Owu: The Fire That Swallowed a City", "yo": "Owu"}, "desc": {"en": "The Owu war: the fire that swallowed a city and opened the age of Yoruba wars.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Ologun / Warlords", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Oba, from “The Fall of Owu: Fire That Swallowed a City”)", "segments": [{"id": "ologun-fall-of-owu#s1", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "There are cities that die slowly, fading over generations until one day a traveler passes the place where they stood and sees only bush and cannot believe anyone ever lived there. And there are cities that die all at once, in fire and screaming, in a single terrible season, so completely that their fall becomes a warning told for two hundred years afterward. Owu was the second kind. It was one of the great walled cities of the Yoruba, old and proud and strong, and then in the space of a long siege it was broken and burned and scattered to the winds, and its people became wanderers, and its name became a byword for total destruction. When the old people wanted to speak of a ruin beyond all mending, they said it was like Owu. This is the story of how a city that seemed eternal came to end."}, {"id": "ologun-fall-of-owu#s2", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "To understand the fall you have to understand the height from which it fell. Owu was no small town. It was a kingdom in its own right, one of the earliest and most powerful of the Yoruba city-states, said to have been founded by a grandson of Oduduwa himself, which placed it among the most senior and most respected of all the towns. Its ruler carried a great title. Its walls were high and thick, ringing the city in the old way, with deep ditches before them, so that an enemy who came against Owu came against stone and earth and a determined people, and for a very long time no enemy could break through. Owu grew rich on trade and on farming, its markets famous, its warriors feared, its arrogance, some would later say, growing with its strength."}, {"id": "ologun-fall-of-owu#s3", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "For it must be admitted that Owu had made enemies, and pride was part of the reason. A city that is strong for long enough begins to believe it cannot fall, and begins to treat its neighbors as lesser, and Owu, secure behind its walls, had quarreled with the towns around it and interfered in their markets and their disputes in ways that were not forgiven. The immediate spark, as the histories tell it, had to do with the great market and with captives and with the tangled politics of a Yorubaland already beginning to come apart as the old empire of Oyo weakened in the north. But sparks only matter when there is dry grass all around, and around Owu the grass was very dry. Its neighbors had been waiting, and gathering their grievances, and their armies."}, {"id": "ologun-fall-of-owu#s4", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "The alliance that came against Owu was a coalition, and that was the thing that doomed it, because Owu could beat any one of its neighbors but not all of them together. The forces of Ife and Ijebu came, and with them a new and frightening element that was reshaping every war in Yorubaland at that time, the soldiers from the collapsing north, the displaced Oyo warriors and others, hardened men with guns and cavalry experience, drifting south as their own world fell apart, selling their skill in war to whoever would feed them. These were the Ijebu with their firearms bought from the coast, and the roaming warbands, and the gathered anger of every town Owu had ever slighted. They surrounded the city and they settled in for a siege, and the siege of Owu became one of the longest and most terrible in Yoruba memory, lasting, the traditions say, for years."}, {"id": "ologun-fall-of-owu#s5", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "A siege is a slow horror. The walls of Owu did what walls do, and held, season after season. The attackers could not break in, and the defenders could not drive them off, and so the two sides sat locked together while time did the killing that weapons could not. Inside the walls the farms could not be worked, because the enemy held the land outside, and so the food began to run down. First the stores, then the livestock, then whatever could be found, and then hunger, real hunger, the kind that hollows the strong and takes the children and the old first. The old accounts speak of famine within Owu, of people reduced to eating things no one should eat, of the slow breaking of a proud people not by the sword but by the empty belly. And still the walls held, and still the enemy waited, because they knew what hunger does, and they had time."}, {"id": "ologun-fall-of-owu#s6", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "It was hunger, in the end, and not the breach of the walls, that decided it, though a breach came too. A city can only starve so long. The defenders, weakened, fewer, desperate, could no longer man the whole circuit of the walls, and the day came, as it always comes in these stories, when the attackers found the place that was not held, or forced the gate, or came over in the dawn when the exhausted watchmen slept. And once they were inside, the end was swift and merciless. Owu was not merely defeated. It was destroyed, in the way that the wars of that broken age had begun to destroy, with a totality meant to erase. The city was put to the fire. Those who could flee, fled. Those who could not were killed or taken, and the taking, in that era, meant the barracoons and the ships and the long crossing, for this was the time when Yoruba war fed the Atlantic trade, and the fall of a city meant thousands of its people carried away in chains."}, {"id": "ologun-fall-of-owu#s7", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "What happened to the people of Owu is, in a way, the most important part of the story, because a city is not only its walls, and Owu did not entirely die. Its survivors scattered across Yorubaland, refugees carrying nothing but their lives and their memory of who they were. Many of them, together with refugees from other fallen towns, drifted to a war camp that was growing in the forest to the south, a place that would take in the broken and the displaced from all over the collapsing country and forge them into something new. That camp became Ibadan, the great city of warriors that would dominate the next century of Yoruba history. Other Owu people founded a new Owu near Abeokuta, keeping the name and the identity alive in a new place, as scattered peoples do, so that even now there are those who proudly say they are of Owu, children of the fallen city, its memory refusing to die with its walls."}, {"id": "ologun-fall-of-owu#s8", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "And this is why the fall of Owu mattered so far beyond Owu itself. It was one of the first great falls in the long chain of wars that tore Yorubaland apart in the nineteenth century, the wars that emptied whole regions, that filled the slave ships, that ended only when exhaustion and the colonial power finally imposed a peace. Owu was the warning that came too early to be heeded. It showed what the new warfare could do, the coalitions, the guns, the sieges, the total destruction, and it showed that no city, however old, however proud, however strong its walls, was safe in that new and merciless age. The towns that watched Owu fall did not learn the lesson in time to save themselves, and one after another many of them would follow it into ruin over the terrible decades that came after."}, {"id": "ologun-fall-of-owu#s9", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So the elders kept the story, and they told it not as mere history but as a lesson pressed into the memory of the people. They told it as a warning against the pride that isolates a city from its neighbors, so that when the enemy comes there is no friend to stand beside you. They told it as a warning against the wars among brothers that fed strangers and filled the ships, Yoruba selling Yoruba, town destroying town, while the real profit sailed away across the sea. And they told it, simply, as a reminder that nothing built by human hands is beyond falling, that the strongest walls are only as strong as the unity and the wisdom of the people behind them. Owu was great, and Owu is gone, and its fire still lights the way for anyone willing to look back and learn."}]}, {"id": "ologun-ekitiparapo-war", "emoji": "⚔️", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#2a0a00,#5a1800)", "category": "ologun", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "8 min", "title": {"en": "The Ekitiparapo War: The Alliance That Would Not Kneel", "yo": "The Ekitiparapo War: The Alliance That Would Not Kneel"}, "desc": {"en": "The eastern alliance that would not kneel to Ibadan: the Ekitiparapo and the Kiriji war.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Ologun / Warlords", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Oba, from “The Ekitiparapo War”)", "segments": [{"id": "ologun-ekitiparapo-war#s1", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "To understand this story you must first understand a bitterness, and the bitterness is this. When Old Oyo fell and the empire of the horsemen broke, the country did not simply grow quiet. Into the empty place where the Alaafin’s power had been rushed a new power, and that power was Ibadan. Ibadan was a war camp that had become a city, a place with no ancient crown and no old dynasty, ruled not by a king born to a throne but by war chiefs who rose by the spear, and Ibadan was hungry. It fought the Ilorin horse and checked them, which was a great service to the whole country, but having grown strong on war it did not know how to stop, and it turned its armies east and south, and it began to eat its neighbors. It placed its war chiefs, its ajele, over the towns it dominated, and these ajele sat in the eastern towns like tax collectors and overlords, and they were proud and grasping, and the people they ruled grew to hate them with a deep and patient hatred."}, {"id": "ologun-ekitiparapo-war#s2", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "The eastern country is the country of the Ekiti and the Ijesa, a land of many hills and many small kingdoms, proud old towns each with its own oba and its own history, and these were not people accustomed to bowing to a war camp full of upstarts. For years they endured the ajele of Ibadan, the insults, the seizures, the sense of being ruled by men they considered their inferiors in everything but violence. And the genius of what came next, the thing that makes this story matter, is that these many small and famously quarrelsome kingdoms, who had spent centuries feuding among themselves, did something they had never managed to do before. They united. They set aside their old rivalries, Ekiti and Ijesa together, town after town, and they formed one alliance against the common oppressor, and they called it the Ekitiparapo, which means the Ekiti bound together, the Ekiti made one. That word itself is the heart of the story, for it names the miracle, that the divided at last became one.· · ·"}, {"id": "ologun-ekitiparapo-war#s3", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "The alliance needed a general, and it found one, and his name rings down the years, Ogedengbe of Ilesa, the great war captain of the Ijesa, a man whose very name became a byword for stubborn valor. He took command of the Ekitiparapo forces, and under him the eastern towns fielded an army that Ibadan, for all its ferocity, could not simply brush aside. The two hosts met on a long front in the hills, at a place the war itself is named for, Kiriji, a name said to imitate the sound of the great guns, and there they locked together in one of the longest wars the country ever knew. It was not a war of a single battle and a quick decision. It was a war of years, of two armies dug into their positions across a valley, of trenches and stockades and long dry-season campaigns followed by wet-season pauses, of a whole generation of young men growing old in the war camps. This was the Kiriji War, and men would speak for the rest of their lives of having been there."}, {"id": "ologun-ekitiparapo-war#s4", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Now here is the iconic turn, the thing that made this war different from all the wars that came before it, and it is a matter of guns. The old wars had been fought with the flintlock, the slow and clumsy trade musket, and with sword and spear and the cavalry of the north. But the Ekitiparapo had friends. Ijesa and Ekiti men who had gone down to Lagos and to the coast, who had prospered there as traders and had not forgotten their homeland, formed a committee and did a remarkable thing. They bought modern rifles, the breech-loading Snider rifles that could fire far faster and farther and truer than anything on the field, and they smuggled these weapons up the secret paths to the war front. When the Ekitiparapo lines suddenly bristled with rifles that outranged everything Ibadan carried, the balance of the whole war shifted. Ibadan could no longer simply overwhelm the east with numbers and fury. The upstart war camp had met, at last, an enemy it could not eat, armed with the future.· · ·"}, {"id": "ologun-ekitiparapo-war#s5", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "And so the war ground on toward stalemate, which was itself a kind of victory for the east, for the whole point had been that Ibadan could not conquer them, and now, plainly, Ibadan could not. But a long stalemate is its own disaster. The farms went untended, the trade roads closed, the young men died or grew old in the trenches, and the whole country between the coast and the interior was strangled because the war sat astride the roads. It was into this exhaustion that a new power inserted itself, the British at Lagos, who wanted the roads open for their own trade and who saw in the endless war both a nuisance and an opportunity. They sent messengers to the war fronts, and in the end they brokered a peace. In the year 1886 the great treaty was made, the armies at Kiriji were ordered to break camp and go home, and the long war ended not in the triumph of one side but in the arrival of a third. Ogedengbe’s men went back to their hills. The Ibadan chiefs withdrew. And the British, having ended the war, had quietly made themselves the arbiters of the whole country, which is a lesson the elders never fail to point out, that when two brothers fight until both are spent, it is very often a stranger who walks off with the house."}, {"id": "ologun-ekitiparapo-war#s6", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "But do not let the ending swallow the meaning, for the meaning of the Ekitiparapo is not defeat. Its meaning is the alliance itself, the miracle of it. The Ekiti and the Ijesa had been raided and ruled and held in contempt, and they refused it. They did what the proud and divided so rarely manage, they made themselves one, they found a great general, they reached across the whole country to their sons on the coast and armed themselves with the newest weapons in the world, and they fought the strongest military power of the age to a standstill in the hills. They were never conquered by Ibadan. They ended the war unbroken. And the very name they gave themselves, Ekitiparapo, the Ekiti bound together, lives on today as the proud name of a whole people’s solidarity, the memory of the season when the many quarreling towns of the eastern hills stood shoulder to shoulder and would not kneel. That is why the elders tell it, so that no one forgets what a divided people can do on the day they finally decide to be one."}]}, {"id": "ologun-alaafins-horses", "emoji": "⚔️", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#2a0a00,#5a1800)", "category": "ologun", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "6 min", "title": {"en": "The Alaafin’s Horses: Why the Empire Rode and Why It Fell", "yo": "The Alaafin’s Horses: Why the Empire Rode and Why It Fell"}, "desc": {"en": "Why Oyo's empire rode on the northern war-horse, and why dependence on that horse helped it fall.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Ologun / Warlords", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Oba, from “The Alaafin’s Horses”)", "segments": [{"id": "ologun-alaafins-horses#s1", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "If you want to understand how Old Oyo became the greatest of all the Yoruba powers, and how it rose to rule an empire that stretched far beyond the Yoruba country itself, you must understand the horse. Not the little bush ponies of the forest, but the tall war horses of the north, the cavalry mounts that could carry an armored man at a gallop and turn a battle in an afternoon. The whole story of Oyo’s greatness, and much of the story of its fall, is written in the hooves of those animals, and the elders who tell it know that a kingdom’s strength and a kingdom’s weakness are very often the same thing seen from two sides."}, {"id": "ologun-alaafins-horses#s2", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Oyo sat in the north of the Yoruba country, up where the thick forest gives way to open grassland, and this was the making of it. For horses do not thrive in the deep forest. The damp and the biting flies of the closed woodland kill them, and among the close trees a mounted man has no room to charge. But in the open savannah of the north, the horse is king. And Oyo, sitting on that grassland and trading with the powers further north who bred the great horses, was able to do what no forest kingdom could. It built cavalry. It bought the tall horses from the north, season after season, herd after herd, and it raised a class of mounted warriors, and with that cavalry it did what cavalry has always done to foot soldiers who have no answer to it. It swept the field."}, {"id": "ologun-alaafins-horses#s3", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Out of this rose the glory of the empire. Oyo’s cavalry made it the terror and the master of the whole region. It fielded an elite of mounted warriors, and the empire grew until it reached far to the west, until the kingdom of Dahomey itself, a formidable power in its own right, was made to pay tribute to the Alaafin year after year, until the trade roads to the coast lay under Oyo’s hand and the wealth of the great slaving ports flowed up into the Alaafin’s treasury. Ambassadors came to Oyo. Lesser kings sent their sons to the Alaafin’s court. The horse had built all of this, for it was the cavalry that beat down resistance and held the roads open and made the tribute flow, and every year the northern horse fairs saw Oyo’s buyers come with their silver, and every year the herds came south to keep the empire’s power fed. The Alaafin’s horses were not merely animals. They were the empire itself, moving on four legs."}, {"id": "ologun-alaafins-horses#s4", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "But here is the deep truth the elders draw out, that the very thing which made Oyo great also chained it, and the chain was this. Oyo could not breed its own war horses. The forest belt to its south, the tsetse fly, the damp, all of it killed the northern horse, so that a horse brought south lived only a few seasons and had to be replaced. This meant that Oyo’s whole strength depended on a road, the road north to the horse country, and on the goodwill or at least the trade of the northern powers who bred the animals. Let that road be cut, let the northern trade turn hostile, and Oyo’s cavalry would wither within a few years, no matter how brave its warriors or how proud its court. The empire that rode to greatness on the horse could be brought down by anyone who could keep it from the horse. And in the fullness of time, that is very nearly what happened."}, {"id": "ologun-alaafins-horses#s5", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "For when the great troubles came, the revolt of Afonja and the rise of Muslim Ilorin, the road north was exactly what was lost. Ilorin sat astride the very country between Oyo and the horse fairs, and once Ilorin turned hostile and the northern trade fell into unfriendly hands, the Alaafin’s herds could not be replaced. Oyo’s cavalry, the terror of the region for two centuries, could not be rebuilt once the animals began to die and no new ones came. The empire that had ridden to greatness found itself, within a generation, an empire without horses, and an Oyo without cavalry was like a bird without wings, still proud, still remembering its glory, but unable to rise. The tribute stopped. Dahomey threw off the yoke. The subject towns rebelled. And the horsemen of Ilorin, who now had the horses Oyo could no longer get, rode down and finished what the loss of the road had begun."}, {"id": "ologun-alaafins-horses#s6", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So the elders, when they show a child a horse, sometimes tell this whole long story in a few words, that Oyo lived by the horse and died by the horse, that the same animal which carried it up to empire could not save it when the road to the north was closed. There is a lesson in it deeper than history, and the elders make sure it is not missed. A power that rests its whole strength on a single thing it cannot make for itself, that must always buy from someone else, is only as strong as its supplier’s goodwill, and the day that goodwill turns, all the past glory in the world will not put a live horse under a warrior. Build what you depend on, the elders say, or one day you will watch your greatness die for want of a thing you never learned to make. That is the tale of the Alaafin’s horses, a tale of splendor and of warning, told wherever the Yoruba remember how the greatest of their empires rose, and how it fell."}]}, {"id": "ologun-oluyole", "emoji": "⚔️", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#2a0a00,#5a1800)", "category": "ologun", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "9 min", "title": {"en": "Basorun Oluyole: The Iron Lord of Early Ibadan", "yo": "Oluyole"}, "desc": {"en": "Grandson of an Alaafin and first great lord of the war-camp that became Ibadan.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Ologun / Warlords", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Jagunjagun, the story of Oluyole, grandson of an Alaafin, first great lord of the war-camp that became Ibadan, the iron ruler who made a city out of soldiers)", "segments": [{"id": "ologun-oluyole#s1", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Children, come and sit, for tonight I open a new chapter of our war-tales. You have heard the roll of the Aare Ona Kakanfo, the supreme field marshals of the whole Yoruba nation, from Kokoro Gangan to Gani Adams. But a nation is not defended by its generalissimos alone. Beneath and beside them stood a great host of other war-lords, men who were not the Aare but who were mighty in their own lands, whose names still shake the air when the drummers call them. Tonight, and in the nights to follow, I will give these other giants their due. And I begin with the man who, more than any other single person, made Ibadan into Ibadan. His name was Oluyole. They called him the Basorun."}, {"id": "ologun-oluyole#s2", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "To understand Oluyole, children, you must first understand the broken world he was born into. The old empire of Oyo, the empire the Alaafins had ruled for centuries, was falling. Afonja’s revolt at Ilorin had cracked it open, the Fulani horsemen had poured into the breach, the capital itself was abandoned, and the Yoruba people were scattered south like seeds thrown from a torn sack. Whole towns emptied. Refugees streamed through the forests looking for anywhere safe to lay their heads. Out of this chaos and this terror, new towns were born, war-camps really, gatherings of fighting men and the frightened people who sheltered behind them. The greatest of these was Ibadan. And into the leadership of that raw, dangerous, growing war-camp stepped Oluyole."}, {"id": "ologun-oluyole#s3", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "He was not a man of low birth, children. Far from it. His blood was of the highest. His mother, it is said, was a daughter of an Alaafin of Oyo, so that royal blood ran in his veins, the blood of the very kings whose empire was now crumbling. His father was a chief of standing. So Oluyole was born into the old order, into privilege and expectation, and he watched that old order collapse around him in his own lifetime. That is a thing that either breaks a man or forges him into iron. It forged Oluyole into iron."}, {"id": "ologun-oluyole#s4", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "He rose in the new Ibadan through the only ladder that mattered in a war-camp, the ladder of war itself. He was a commander, a gatherer of fighting men, a man who could lead a host into the forest and bring back captives and plunder and glory. And he was clever in the councils as well as brave in the field, which is a rarer and more dangerous combination. He climbed until he held the title of Basorun."}, {"id": "ologun-oluyole#s5", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Now hear the weight of that title, children. In the old Oyo, the Basorun was the head of the Oyo Mesi, the council of noble kingmakers, the second most powerful man in the whole empire after the Alaafin himself, and sometimes, in truth, more powerful than the Alaafin. For Oluyole to bear that title in Ibadan was for him to claim the mantle of the old greatness in the new town. He was, in all but name, the ruler of Ibadan, its first true strongman, the head of its war and its government both."}, {"id": "ologun-oluyole#s6", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "Under his hand, Ibadan grew from a war-camp into a power. He drew fighting men to himself by the thousand, for in those days a warrior went where the war was good and the loot was plentiful, and under Oluyole both were plentiful. He sent his armies out across the land, expanding Ibadan’s reach, breaking rivals, bringing tribute and captives home. He built a compound so vast it was a town within the town. He surrounded himself with a household of warriors, wives, slaves, and hangers-on beyond counting. He made Ibadan feared."}, {"id": "ologun-oluyole#s7", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "And in making it feared, he made it safe, which is the hard paradox of those broken years. In a time when a weak town was simply swallowed, its men killed and its women and children sold across the water, only strength could protect a people. Oluyole’s iron fist was terrible, but behind that fist a great many ordinary people found the first safety they had known since the empire fell. They ate, they traded, they raised their children, because Oluyole’s spears stood between them and the horror in the forest."}, {"id": "ologun-oluyole#s8", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "But now, children, the Afonja lesson, and with Oluyole it must be told plainly, for he was a hard man and the tales do not soften him. Oluyole was a tyrant. There is no gentler word. His power was near absolute in Ibadan, and he used it with a cruelty that frightened even other warriors, men not easily frightened. The stories that come down to us are grim. It is said he could order a man killed on a whim, for a small offence or for no offence at all, to make a point or simply because he could. It is said he was greedy, seizing the wealth and the wives of men he destroyed. It is said the people both revered and dreaded him, and that his death, when it came, was met in some quarters with a secret relief."}, {"id": "ologun-oluyole#s9", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "I do not tell you these things to blacken his name, children. I tell you them because a griot who paints only the glory is a liar, and because Oluyole’s cruelty is itself a lesson. He shows us the terrible bargain of those years. The very hardness that let a man hold a war-camp together and shield thousands from slavery was the same hardness that made him a domestic tyrant. The strength that saved the people was inseparable from the strength that oppressed them. Hold both palms, children. Do not pretend he was a gentle guardian. Do not pretend, either, that a gentle man could have held Ibadan together in that storm."}, {"id": "ologun-oluyole#s10", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "There is a saying that lived after him. Something like, Oluyole does not do things by halves, whether in kindness or in terror. When he loved a man he raised him high. When he turned against a man, that man was finished. He was a giant of appetites, of temper, of ambition, all outsized, all dangerous."}, {"id": "ologun-oluyole#s11", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "He died in the middle of the century, and Ibadan did not fall apart when he went, which is the truest measure of what he built. He had made something that outlasted him. After him came other great Ibadan war-lords, Ibikunle, Ogunmola, Latoosa, a whole line of fighting chiefs, and I will tell you of some of them in the nights to come. But they all built on the foundation Oluyole laid. He was the first stone. He took a frightened huddle of refugees and warriors and he hammered it, by sheer force of will and terror and genius, into the mightiest military power in all of Yorubaland, a power that would dominate the region for the rest of the century and shape the whole later history of our people."}, {"id": "ologun-oluyole#s12", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So remember Oluyole, children, and remember him whole. Remember the grandson of an Alaafin who watched the old world die and refused to die with it. Remember the iron lord who made a city out of soldiers. And remember, too, the tyrant whose cruelty was the shadow cast by his own terrible strength. He was not a good man as we would measure goodness at this peaceful fire. But he was the right man, perhaps the only man, for a wrong and bloody age. And because he was hard when hardness was survival, thousands of our ancestors lived who would otherwise have died or been sold, and Ibadan rose to become the great city it remains to this day. Tomorrow I will bring you the war-lord who came after him, a man of the battlefield more than the council chamber, one of the greatest pure soldiers Ibadan ever produced, whose name was Ogunmola."}]}, {"id": "ologun-ogunmola", "emoji": "⚔️", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#2a0a00,#5a1800)", "category": "ologun", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "8 min", "title": {"en": "Ogunmola: The Slave Who Became a War-King", "yo": "Ogunmola"}, "desc": {"en": "The Basorun of Ibadan who rose from bondage to command armies.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Ologun / Warlords", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Jagunjagun, the story of Ogunmola, the Basorun of Ibadan, the pure soldier who rose from bondage to command armies, terror of the battlefield and rival of Ibikunle)", "segments": [{"id": "ologun-ogunmola#s1", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Children, last night I told you of Oluyole, the iron lord who founded the greatness of Ibadan. Tonight I bring you one of the mightiest of the war-lords who came after him, a man whose life is one of the most astonishing climbs in all our history. For this man did not begin as the grandson of an Alaafin, with royal blood and high expectation. This man, it is said, began his life at the very bottom, in bondage, a servant, a nobody. And by the sword alone he climbed until he was one of the two most powerful men in the greatest military city of the Yoruba. His name was Ogunmola."}, {"id": "ologun-ogunmola#s2", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Hear the shape of his beginning, children, for it tells you everything about the world of old Ibadan. In most Yoruba towns, in most of the world in those days, a man born low stayed low and died low. Birth was destiny. But Ibadan was a strange and terrible and wonderful exception. Ibadan was a republic of war. It had no king by birthright in the ordinary sense, no sacred dynasty that ruled by blood alone. It was ruled by its war-chiefs, and a man became a war-chief not by his father’s name but by what he could do on the field of battle. Ibadan asked a man one question only. Can you fight? Can you lead? Can you win? And so a place like Ibadan could take a man who began in servitude and, if he had the fire in him, raise him to command armies. Ogunmola was such a man."}, {"id": "ologun-ogunmola#s3", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "He rose, as all Ibadan men rose, through war. Campaign after campaign, he proved himself, gathering followers, gathering captives, gathering the reputation that in Ibadan was the only true currency of power. Men flocked to a successful war-lord, because to follow a winner was to eat well and grow rich on plunder, while to follow a loser was to starve or die. Ogunmola won, and so men came to him, and his power swelled. He was, the tales agree, a pure soldier, a man of the battlefield above all. Where Oluyole had been as much a schemer of the council chamber as a general, Ogunmola’s genius was war itself. He understood the movement of hosts, the timing of the charge, the holding of a line, the breaking of an enemy’s will. He was cunning, patient, and utterly ruthless when the moment came to strike."}, {"id": "ologun-ogunmola#s4", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "And here, children, is the great drama of his life, the rivalry that the drummers still recall. In the Ibadan of his prime, two giants stood at the top, and they were rivals. One was Balogun Ibikunle, the head of the war-chiefs, a big, powerful, and by the tellings a somewhat more temperate and generous man. The other was Ogunmola, the Otun or the second, hungrier, sharper, more ferociously ambitious. For years these two great war-lords led Ibadan together and against each other both, an uneasy partnership of titans, each watching the other, each commanding huge personal armies within the one city."}, {"id": "ologun-ogunmola#s5", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "It is one of the marks of Ibadan’s strange strength that it could contain two such giants at once and still not tear itself fully apart. They quarreled, they competed, they sometimes nearly came to blows, and yet when Ibadan marched to war against outside enemies, they marched together, and few could stand against the combined might of Ibikunle and Ogunmola. When Ibikunle passed, Ogunmola rose to the very summit. He took the title of Basorun, the same great title Oluyole had borne, and he became the supreme lord of Ibadan. The servant, the man who had begun with nothing, now ruled the mightiest army in Yorubaland. Think on that, children. Think what fire must have burned in a man to carry him that far from that beginning. Think what it says about a city that it would let him."}, {"id": "ologun-ogunmola#s6", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "Under Ogunmola, Ibadan’s armies ranged far, fighting the long, grinding wars of that century, the wars against Ijaye, against the Egba, against the Ijesa and Ekiti, the endless struggle for dominance over the fractured Yoruba country. He led these campaigns with the hard skill of a lifetime’s soldiering."}, {"id": "ologun-ogunmola#s7", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Now the Afonja lesson, children, for Ogunmola too casts a long shadow. He was ruthless, as a man does not climb from bondage to a war-throne by being tender. The wars he fought and led were wars of conquest, and conquest in those days meant burned towns, and captives, and many of those captives were sold, fed into the same terrible traffic across the water that swallowed so many of our people. The Ibadan war-machine that these men built was magnificent and it was also a devourer, and honesty requires that we say so. The glory of Ibadan was watered with the tears of the towns it broke. Ogunmola was a maker of that glory and a maker of those tears both."}, {"id": "ologun-ogunmola#s8", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "Hold the two palms, children. On one side, a war-lord whose campaigns brought suffering to many. On the other, a man of the very lowest beginning who rose by sheer courage and genius to the height of his world, and who helped make Ibadan the shield that, for all its predatory hunger, kept the heart of Yorubaland from being wholly overrun by outside powers in a desperate age. He died as Basorun, at the top, which for a man of his origins is a kind of miracle. And the lesson he leaves is one of the proudest and most double-edged in all our history."}, {"id": "ologun-ogunmola#s9", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "The proud side is this. In old Ibadan, a man was measured by his deeds and not his birth. No child, however low his cradle, was told that greatness was closed to him forever. The path was open to any who had the courage and the skill to walk it. That is a noble thing, children, rarer in the old world than you might think, and Ibadan should be honoured for it. Ogunmola is the living proof of it, the slave who became a war-king. The double edge is this. The ladder he climbed was a ladder of war, and every rung was someone else’s suffering. His greatness and his cruelty were, once again, the same thing seen from two sides."}, {"id": "ologun-ogunmola#s10", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So remember Ogunmola, children. When someone tells you that where you started must decide where you finish, remember the servant who ended his days as the lord of the greatest army in the land. And when someone paints war as pure glory, remember too that the war-king’s crown was heavy with the grief of the conquered. Both are true. Hold them both. Tomorrow I will tell you of his great rival and partner, the big and mighty Balogun Ibikunle, and then of the war-lords of the other Yoruba lands, the ones who rose in Ogbomoso and Ilesa and Ijaye and beyond, so that no corner of our warring nation goes unremembered."}]}, {"id": "ologun-soun-ogunlola", "emoji": "⚔️", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#2a0a00,#5a1800)", "category": "ologun", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "7 min", "title": {"en": "Soun Ogunlola: The Hunter Whose Spear Founded Ogbomoso", "yo": "Ogunlola"}, "desc": {"en": "The hunter-warrior and first Soun whose courage founded Ogbomoso.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Ologun / Warlords", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Jagunjagun, the story of Ogunlola, the mighty hunter-warrior, first Soun of Ogbomoso, the man whose courage against a dreaded enemy opened a whole city)", "segments": [{"id": "ologun-soun-ogunlola#s1", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Children, we leave Ibadan now, and we travel north and west, to the town of Ogbomoso, and to the story of its founding, which is a war-story of the oldest and grandest kind, the kind where one brave man with a spear stands between his people and a terror, and by his courage a city is born. His name was Ogunlola, and he became the first Soun of Ogbomoso, the founder-king of that great town, and his beginning was that of a hunter."}, {"id": "ologun-soun-ogunlola#s2", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Understand, children, that in the old days the hunter was a special and honoured kind of man, close cousin to the warrior. The hunter went alone or in small bands deep into the forest, where the ordinary farmer dared not go, into the domain of the wild beasts and the spirits and the bandits. To be a great hunter, a man had to be brave beyond the common measure, skilled with weapons, patient, and armed as well with the deep knowledge of medicines and charms that the hunters guarded, the herbs and the incantations that gave a man strength, that stanched his blood, that turned an enemy’s blade. The hunters were the frontiersmen of our world, and from their ranks came many of our greatest warriors. Ogunlola was of this breed."}, {"id": "ologun-soun-ogunlola#s3", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Now the tale, as it comes down to us. In those unsettled days, the region where Ogbomoso would rise was troubled, as all the land was troubled, by war and by raiders. And the tellings speak of a particular terror, a dreaded enemy, a fearsome marauder or war-leader whose name struck fear into the whole district, a man who raided and killed and could not be stopped, before whom the people trembled. Some of the old accounts give this dreaded figure the name of a fierce raider from the troubles of that time, a scourge upon the land. The people were in despair, for none could stand against this terror. And it was Ogunlola, the hunter, who did what no one else dared. He went out against the dreaded enemy, and in a feat of enormous courage, he killed him. He brought down the terror that had made a whole region tremble."}, {"id": "ologun-soun-ogunlola#s4", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "Hear what this meant, children. To kill the unkillable terror was not merely a personal glory. It was salvation for a whole people. Where before there had been only fear and flight, now there was a champion, a man proven mighty enough to protect. And people do a natural and ancient thing when they find such a champion. They gather to him. They settle where he settles. They shelter behind his strength. And so, around the courage of Ogunlola the hunter, people gathered, and a settlement grew, and that settlement became the town of Ogbomoso. He did not merely win a battle. By his single act of enormous bravery he created the safety in which a whole city could be born. He became its founder and its first ruler, taking the title of Soun, which the rulers of Ogbomoso bear to this day."}, {"id": "ologun-soun-ogunlola#s5", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "And here is a thing of great beauty and importance, children, that lifts Ogbomoso’s story a little out of the darkness of the war-age. Because Ogbomoso was founded on the strength of a mighty protector, it became, in the terrible times that followed, a refuge. When the wars of the falling empire scattered our people, when towns were burned and populations fled, Ogbomoso, strong and well-placed, took in the refugees. It swelled with the frightened and the homeless who sought its shelter. The town that a hunter’s courage had founded became a harbour in the storm for thousands who had nowhere else to run. So Ogunlola’s spear did not only end one terror. It planted a place of safety that would go on saving lives long after he was gone, through the whole century of war. That is the finest kind of legacy a war-lord can leave, not merely enemies slain, but a lasting shelter built."}, {"id": "ologun-soun-ogunlola#s6", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Now, children, let me be honest with you in the way a griot must, about the nature of this tale. The story of Ogunlola comes to us from the deep well of tradition, from the founding-legends that every great town keeps and treasures and, over the generations, polishes. The precise details, the exact name of the terror he slew, the exact manner of the deed, these are wrapped in the mist that always gathers around a founding hero. Different tellings differ. I will not stand at this fire and pretend to you that I know the founding of Ogbomoso with the same certainty that I know the deeds of the war-lords of the last century, for I do not, and no honest teller does. What I give you is the tradition, the treasured story the people of Ogbomoso keep of their own beginning, and I give it to you with the respect such traditions deserve, neither swallowing every detail as literal fact nor scorning the deep truth that lives inside it."}, {"id": "ologun-soun-ogunlola#s7", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "And the deep truth inside it is this, and it is not in doubt. Ogbomoso was founded on the courage of a hunter-warrior. Its very existence is a monument to one man’s willingness to face a terror that everyone else fled. That much the whole history confirms, whatever the mist may hide of the details. So remember the Soun Ogunlola, children, the hunter whose spear founded a city. Remember that the deepest kind of courage is not the courage that conquers for glory or for plunder, but the courage that stands between the helpless and the thing they fear. Ogunlola did not go out to build an empire or to grow rich. He went out because a terror was devouring his people and someone had to face it, and he was the one brave enough to be that someone."}, {"id": "ologun-soun-ogunlola#s8", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "Every great and safe place, children, every city where children can sleep without fear, was founded, somewhere back in the mist, on an act of courage like his. When you walk the streets of a peaceful town, remember that somewhere at its root stands a hunter with a spear who faced the terror so that others could one day forget that the terror ever existed. That forgetting is his gift. That safety is his monument. Tomorrow I will tell you of the war-lords of the eastern Yoruba hills, of the mighty Ogedengbe of Ilesa and the fierce defenders of the Ekiti country, who in the last great war held the line against the armies of Ibadan itself."}]}, {"id": "ologun-fabunmi", "emoji": "⚔️", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#2a0a00,#5a1800)", "category": "ologun", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "5 min", "title": {"en": "Fabunmi of Oke-Imesi: The Spark That Lit the Eastern Fire", "yo": "Fabunmi"}, "desc": {"en": "The Oke-Imesi war-chief whose defiance lit the Ekitiparapo rising.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Ologun / Warlords", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Jagunjagun, the story of Fabunmi, the fierce war-chief of Oke-Imesi, whose defiance helped ignite the Ekitiparapo rising against Ibadan)", "segments": [{"id": "ologun-fabunmi#s1", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Children, a great fire must be lit by a first spark, and the great eastern war I told you of last night, the long war of Kiriji where Ogedengbe held the hills, had its spark too. And one of the men remembered as striking that first defiant flame was a war-chief of the Ekiti town of Oke-Imesi, a fierce and unyielding man named Fabunmi."}, {"id": "ologun-fabunmi#s2", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "To understand Fabunmi, children, you must remember the weight the eastern towns carried in those days. The Ekiti and the Ijesa had long groaned under the dominance of Ibadan. Ibadan stationed its officers, its ajele, in the subject towns, agents who sat in the conquered communities to collect tribute and enforce Ibadan’s will, and by the tellings these agents were often arrogant and rapacious, squeezing the people, insulting the local chiefs, taking what they wished. It was the daily humiliation of a proud people held down by a stronger one. And humiliation, children, when it is heaped high enough upon a proud people, does not make them weak. It makes them dangerous."}, {"id": "ologun-fabunmi#s3", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Fabunmi was a chief of Oke-Imesi, a man of war, and a man who had had enough. When the great moment of rising came, when the eastern towns began to throw off the yoke, Fabunmi is remembered as one of the first and fiercest to strike, to turn on the hated agents of Ibadan and to raise the banner of revolt in his own country. His defiance at Oke-Imesi was one of the sparks that set the whole east ablaze, that turned scattered resentment into the organized war of the Ekitiparapo. And it was fitting, children, that the great war then settled around his own country, for the long entrenched struggle of Kiriji was fought in that very region, at Oke-Imesi, on the ground Fabunmi knew and defended. He fought through the long war as one of the war-chiefs of the eastern alliance, under the supreme command of Ogedengbe, a fierce captain in the host that held the hills."}, {"id": "ologun-fabunmi#s4", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Now, children, honesty at the fire, as always. Fabunmi is a figure of the war, and the records of a war-chief who was not the supreme commander are thinner than those of the giants above him. Different tellings give his deeds different weights, and I will not inflate him beyond what the traditions carry. What is remembered, and what matters, is his role as a kindler of the rising, a man of fierce local defiance whose refusal to bear the yoke any longer helped turn the whole east toward war. He stands for something larger than the exact tally of his battles. He stands for the moment when the humiliated finally say, no more."}, {"id": "ologun-fabunmi#s5", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "And like all the war-chiefs of that age, on every side, his hands were the hands of a man of war, with all the hardness that meant. The rising he helped kindle was just, born of real oppression, but it was still war, with all of war’s cruelty. Hold the two palms, as always. The lesson of Fabunmi, children, is the lesson of the spark. Great events, the risings of whole peoples, the breaking of long tyrannies, do not begin all at once and everywhere. They begin somewhere, with someone, with a first defiant act by a person who has decided they will bear it no longer, whatever the cost. The one who strikes first takes the greatest risk, for the fire has not yet caught, and they may be crushed alone before the others find their courage. Fabunmi struck, and the fire caught, and a whole people rose behind him."}, {"id": "ologun-fabunmi#s6", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "Remember, children, that you may one day be the spark. You may be the one in the room who first says the thing that everyone feels but no one has dared to voice, the one who first refuses the injustice that everyone has silently endured. It is a frightening place to stand, at the very front, before the fire has caught. But every liberation in the history of the world began with someone standing exactly there. Fabunmi of Oke-Imesi stood there, and the eastern hills caught fire behind him. Tomorrow I will take you far back, into the deep and misty past, to the ancient and legendary war-lords whose names, Onikoyi the mighty and the fierce lords of old Oyo’s provinces, come down to us less as documented history and more as the thunder of legend, and I will tell you honestly what we know and what we only sing."}]}, {"id": "ologun-onikoyi-legends", "emoji": "⚔️", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#2a0a00,#5a1800)", "category": "ologun", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "8 min", "title": {"en": "The Onikoyi and the Lords of Legend", "yo": "Onikoyi"}, "desc": {"en": "The Onikoyi war-lineage of old Oyo and the half-legendary war-figures of the deep past.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Ologun / Warlords", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Jagunjagun, consolidates the memory of the Onikoyi war-lineage of old Oyo and the half-legendary war-figures of the deep past, told with an honest hand about what is history and what is song)", "segments": [{"id": "ologun-onikoyi-legends#s1", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Children, gather very close, for tonight I tell you a different kind of war-tale, and I must be more honest with you than usual about the ground we walk on. Until now I have given you war-lords of the last century and the one before, men whose deeds are written down, whose battles have dates, whose lives we can trace with some confidence. But tonight I take you further back, into the deep past of the old Oyo empire, and into the half-lit country where history fades into legend. Here the names are mighty but the facts grow thin, and a griot who pretends otherwise is deceiving you. So let me tell you what is remembered, and let me tell you honestly which parts are firm ground and which are mist."}, {"id": "ologun-onikoyi-legends#s2", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Begin with the Onikoyi. This is one of the great and ancient names of the old Oyo empire, children. The Onikoyi was the ruler of the town of Ikoyi, and more than that, the Onikoyi held one of the highest and most honoured of all the war-titles of old Oyo. In the days of the empire’s glory, the Onikoyi was reckoned among the greatest of the provincial war-lords, a commander of enormous prestige, sometimes spoken of as the head or the foremost of the empire’s war-chiefs, the mighty right arm of Oyo’s military power."}, {"id": "ologun-onikoyi-legends#s3", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "The town of Ikoyi was famed as a town of warriors, a place that bred fighting men, and its ruler the Onikoyi embodied that martial fame. When old Oyo marched to war in its centuries of greatness, the Onikoyi and the men of Ikoyi were among the fiercest of its host. So great was the reputation of Ikoyi’s warriors that their name echoes still. The title carried such weight that it stood near the very summit of the empire’s war-nobility."}, {"id": "ologun-onikoyi-legends#s4", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "But here, children, is where I must be honest. The Onikoyi is not one single man. It is a title, a line, a succession of rulers stretching back through the centuries, and the deeds of the mighty Onikoyi of legend are gathered from across many generations and many holders of the office, blended in the memory of the people into one towering image of martial glory. When the drummers praise the Onikoyi, they praise not one hero with a birth-date and a death-date, but a whole ancient lineage of war, an idea of Ikoyi’s greatness as much as any single life. That is not a flaw in the story. It is simply the nature of the deep past, where individual men blur into the great names of their houses."}, {"id": "ologun-onikoyi-legends#s5", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "And there are other names, children, that come to us from that misty country, names of war-figures and champions of the old time that live now more in tradition, in the praise-poetry, in the oriki that families still chant, than in any chronicle. There are fierce lords remembered in the deep genealogies of the old provinces, champions whose single names ring with the memory of some great feat of arms, some defense, some conquest, now half-forgotten in its details. Some you may hear named in the oriki of old families, in the proud chanting of a lineage’s warrior-ancestors, names spoken with reverence though the precise histories behind them have thinned almost to nothing with the passing of the generations."}, {"id": "ologun-onikoyi-legends#s6", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "I will not stand at this fire, children, and invent for you deeds and dates for figures whose true histories the centuries have worn away. That would be a betrayal of the very thing a griot exists to protect, which is the truth of the people’s memory. What I will tell you is this. Behind each of these half-legendary names stood, once, a real man or woman of courage, a champion of some town or some house, whose deeds were great enough in their day that their name survived when a thousand others were forgotten. The survival of the name is itself the monument. That a family still chants an ancestor’s warrior-name three hundred years on tells you that once, long ago, that ancestor did something that mattered enough to be worth remembering forever."}, {"id": "ologun-onikoyi-legends#s7", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Now hear the deep lesson of the misty country, children, for it is important. There are two ways to fail the ancestors of the deep past. The first is to forget them entirely, to let their names fall silent, which is the death after death, the true and final ending. The second, and it is a subtler failure, is to lie about them, to dress the thin bones of legend in the false flesh of invented detail, to make up battles and speeches and dates to fill the silence. Both are betrayals. The first erases the ancestors. The second replaces them with a fiction and calls it them."}, {"id": "ologun-onikoyi-legends#s8", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "The honest path, the griot’s path, is the narrow one between. We keep the names alive. We chant the oriki, we honour the Onikoyi and the old champions, we hold their memory in reverence. And we are truthful about the mist. We say plainly, this much we know, and this much we only sing, and behind the song stood a real greatness whose details are lost. That honesty is not a weakness in the telling. It is the deepest respect. It says to the ancestors, we will not forget you, and we will not lie about you either. We will hold your names in truth."}, {"id": "ologun-onikoyi-legends#s9", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "So remember the Onikoyi, children, and the mighty war-tradition of Ikoyi, one of the greatest war-names of the old empire. And remember, too, all the half-legendary champions whose names survive in the oriki of the old families, the warriors of the deep past whose deeds have thinned to a proud echo. Honour them not with invented tales but with the truth, which is that they were real, they were mighty in their day, and their names have outlived three hundred years and more of forgetting, which is itself the greatest victory a mortal can win over death."}, {"id": "ologun-onikoyi-legends#s10", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "And when you hear an old person chant the warrior-names in the oriki of your own family, children, listen with reverence, even where the stories behind the names have faded. For each of those names is an ancestor who once stood in the storm of the world and did something great enough that you, their child, still speak their name today. That unbroken chain of remembered names, stretching back into the mist, is the truest treasure any people possesses. Guard it. Chant it. Pass it on."}, {"id": "ologun-onikoyi-legends#s11", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "And so, children, we close the long roll of the war-lords, the Aare Ona Kakanfo who led the whole nation, and now these others, the founders and the defenders and the fierce provincial lords, Oluyole and Ogunmola and Ibikunle of Ibadan, Ogunlola of Ogbomoso, Ogedengbe of Ijesaland, Fabunmi of the Ekiti, the ancient Onikoyi of old Oyo, and all the champions of the misty past. A warring people, yes, too often turned against one another. But a brave people, an unconquerable people, who bred giants in every corner of their land, and whose names, held in truth at fires like this one, will never wholly die."}]}, {"id": "ologun-lisabi", "emoji": "⚔️", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#2a0a00,#5a1800)", "category": "ologun", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "8 min", "title": {"en": "Lisabi: The Farmer Who Freed the Egba", "yo": "Lisabi Agbongbo-Akala"}, "desc": {"en": "The farmer-champion whose cunning and courage freed the Egba from Oyo.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Ologun / Warlords", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Jagunjagun, the story of Lisabi Agbongbo-Akala, the hero of the Egba, the farmer-champion whose cunning and courage broke the yoke of old Oyo and founded a people’s freedom)", "segments": [{"id": "ologun-lisabi#s1", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Children, tonight I bring you a liberator, and one of the best-loved heroes in all of Yorubaland, the champion of the Egba people, a man whose name is still a rallying cry, still shouted at festivals, still carried with fierce pride by every Egba son and daughter. His name was Lisabi, and unlike so many of our war-lords, he did not begin as a prince or a noble or even a professional soldier. He began as a farmer. And that is the first beautiful thing about him."}, {"id": "ologun-lisabi#s2", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Here is the world Lisabi was born into, children. The Egba people, who lived in a cluster of towns in the forest country, were in those days subjects of the great empire of Oyo. And Oyo’s rule over them had become a heavy and hated thing. Oyo sent its officers, its representatives, to sit in the Egba towns and collect tribute and enforce the empire’s will. And by the tellings, these officers had grown cruel and rapacious and arrogant, oppressing the Egba, seizing their goods, humiliating them, treating a proud people as little better than a herd to be milked. The Egba groaned under this yoke, but Oyo was mighty, the mightiest power in the land, and to resist seemed impossible. A single Egba town could no more defy the empire than a single goat could defy a leopard."}, {"id": "ologun-lisabi#s3", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Into this bondage came Lisabi. He was a farmer of the Egba, a man of the ordinary people, but a man of extraordinary gifts, brave, cunning, and above all able to see what others could not, which was that the Egba’s weakness was not their lack of strength but their lack of unity. Scattered, quarreling, each town alone, they were helpless. United, they might be a force even Oyo would have to reckon with."}, {"id": "ologun-lisabi#s4", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "So Lisabi did a patient and clever thing. He worked to unite his people. The tellings speak of how he built up a society, a brotherhood, using the guise of a traditional mutual-aid group, the kind where men band together to help one another with the hard labour of the farms, clearing land and harvesting in turns. Under this ordinary, unsuspicious cover, a society of working farmers, Lisabi quietly gathered and organized the young men of the Egba, building bonds of loyalty and cooperation across the towns, forging, in secret, the unity that had never existed before. He turned a farmers’ work-club into the seed of an army and a nation."}, {"id": "ologun-lisabi#s5", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "And then, children, came the day of the rising. When the organization was ready, when the moment was right, Lisabi struck. In a coordinated blow, the Egba rose against their Oyo overlords and killed the oppressive officers, the hated representatives who had squeezed them for so long. It was a bold and bloody stroke, a whole people throwing off the yoke in a single coordinated act of defiance that Lisabi’s patient organizing had made possible."}, {"id": "ologun-lisabi#s6", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "And then they had to make it hold. For Oyo, enraged at the loss of its subjects and its officers, sent its army against the rebellious Egba to crush them and restore the yoke. And here the farmer-champion showed he was a war-leader too. Lisabi led the Egba in the desperate fighting to defend their new freedom, and by the tellings, through courage and cunning and the unity he had forged, the Egba withstood the assault. They kept their freedom. The yoke of Oyo was broken and did not return."}, {"id": "ologun-lisabi#s7", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "That, children, is the towering achievement of Lisabi. He took a scattered, subjugated, hopeless people, and by patient organizing and one bold stroke and a desperate defense, he made them free. He is, to the Egba, what a great national liberator is to any people, the father of their freedom, the man who broke their chains. Every Egba child learns his name. There is a festival in his honour still, Lisabi Day, when the Egba remember the farmer who freed them. He is woven into the very identity of the Egba people, their proudest possession."}, {"id": "ologun-lisabi#s8", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Now, children, the honest hand, for even a beloved liberator lives in the mist of the old times. Lisabi belongs to the deeper past, and his story, like all such stories, has been polished by generations of loving retelling. The exact details of his organizing, the precise course of the rising and the battles, the manner of his own end, these come to us in different versions, and some of the tale has surely grown grander in the telling, as the tales of national heroes always do. Some accounts say his end was mysterious, that he vanished or fell in circumstances the tellings dispute. I give you the heart of the tradition, the treasured story the Egba keep of their own liberation, and I am honest that the fine details lie partly in the mist."}, {"id": "ologun-lisabi#s9", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "But the heart of it is firm and not in doubt, children. The Egba were subject to Oyo. The Egba threw off that yoke and became free. And the memory of the whole people, held for generations, names Lisabi as the hero who made it so. That much is history, and it is enough."}, {"id": "ologun-lisabi#s10", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "And the Afonja lesson here, children, is a gentler one than usual, for Lisabi is one of our cleaner heroes, a liberator rather than a conqueror. But even here, hold the two palms honestly. The rising was bloody, as risings are, and the killing of the Oyo officers was a hard and violent deed, however justified by the oppression that provoked it. Freedom, even the most righteous freedom, is so often bought with blood, and we should not pretend otherwise even when we celebrate. But weigh it, and the scale comes down clearly. This was a subjugated people winning their liberty from a real oppressor. That is a cause we can honour with a whole heart."}, {"id": "ologun-lisabi#s11", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "So here is the lesson I press into your hands from Lisabi, children, and it is one of the most useful of all. The great secret he understood was unity. Alone, each Egba town was helpless. The empire could crush any one of them like a man crushing a single stick. But Lisabi knew the old truth that a bundle of sticks bound tightly together cannot be broken, though each stick alone snaps easily. His genius was not mainly in fighting. It was in binding the scattered sticks into one unbreakable bundle. That is how the weak overcome the strong, children, not by each becoming a giant, but by the many binding themselves into one."}, {"id": "ologun-lisabi#s12", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "And remember, too, that the liberator of a whole people began as a farmer, a man of the ordinary working people, with dirt under his nails. You need not be born a prince to free a nation. You need only see clearly what must be done, and have the patience to organize and the courage to strike. Remember Lisabi Agbongbo-Akala, the farmer who bound the sticks and broke the yoke, whenever anyone tells you that ordinary people are powerless against the mighty. Tomorrow I will carry you down to the lagoon, to the island city of Lagos, and to its fierce and tangled war-history, to the exiled king Kosoko and the mighty war-chief Oshodi Tapa, whose struggles were caught up in the coming of the white man’s power to our shores."}]}, {"id": "ologun-kosoko-oshodi-tapa", "emoji": "⚔️", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#2a0a00,#5a1800)", "category": "ologun", "rating": 9.6, "duration": "9 min", "title": {"en": "Kosoko and Oshodi Tapa: The Exiled King and His Faithful War-Lord", "yo": "Kosoko and Oshodi Tapa: The Exiled King and His Faithful War-Lord"}, "desc": {"en": "Kosoko, fierce prince-king of Lagos, and his loyal war-lord Oshodi Tapa, swallowed by British power.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Ologun / Warlords", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Jagunjagun, the story of Kosoko, the fierce prince-king of Lagos, and Oshodi Tapa, his mighty and loyal war-chief, whose struggles were swallowed by the coming of the white man’s power)", "segments": [{"id": "ologun-kosoko-oshodi-tapa#s1", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Children, tonight we go down to the water, to the island city of Lagos on its lagoon, Eko as our people call it, and to one of the most dramatic and consequential war-struggles in all our history, for it is bound up with the very moment the white man’s power took hold on our shores. And it is a story of two men, a king and his war-lord, bound together in loyalty through triumph and exile and loss. Their names are Kosoko and Oshodi Tapa."}, {"id": "ologun-kosoko-oshodi-tapa#s2", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Understand Lagos first, children. It was a kingdom of the water, ruled by an Oba, grown rich and powerful as a great port, a place where the trade of the interior met the ships of the sea. And in those days, terribly, much of that trade was the trade in human beings, for Lagos had become one of the great markets through which captives were funneled to the slave-ships. This is a hard truth and I will not hide it. The wealth and the wars of Lagos in that age were soaked in that traffic."}, {"id": "ologun-kosoko-oshodi-tapa#s3", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Into the royal house of Lagos was born Kosoko, a prince, and by the tellings a proud, fierce, capable, and popular one. But the royal house was torn by bitter rivalries over the throne, the kind of deadly family struggles that so often poisoned our kingdoms. Kosoko had powerful enemies within his own family, and through the twists of these struggles he was driven out, forced into exile, a prince robbed of his rightful place. But Kosoko was not a man to accept exile quietly. He gathered followers, he bided his time, and at last he returned in force and seized the throne of Lagos, driving out the reigning Oba, his rival. Kosoko became the Oba of Lagos, the king of the water, by the strength of his own arm."}, {"id": "ologun-kosoko-oshodi-tapa#s4", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "And at his right hand through all of this stood the man whose loyalty is the heart of this tale, Oshodi Tapa. Now hear the marvel of Oshodi’s origin, children, for it is another of those astonishing Yoruba stories of a man rising from the very bottom. Oshodi Tapa, as his very name suggests, was of Tapa origin, that is, of Nupe stock from the north, and he had come into Lagos, by the tellings, as a slave, a captive. A slave. And yet by his ability, his courage, and his fierce loyalty, this man rose to become the greatest war-chief and the closest, most trusted counsellor of the king himself. The captive became the kingmaker’s right hand, the mightiest war-lord of Lagos. Once again our history shows us that ability and loyalty could lift a man from the lowest place to the very heights."}, {"id": "ologun-kosoko-oshodi-tapa#s5", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Oshodi Tapa served Kosoko with a devotion that never wavered, through all that was to come, and that unbreakable loyalty is the reason his name is remembered with such honour to this day. Now, children, comes the great turning, the thing that makes this story matter to the whole later history of our people. In these very years, the British had turned against the slave trade and were using their warships to suppress it along the coast. And they cast their eyes on Lagos, that great slaving port. They wanted it stopped, and, if we are honest, they also wanted the influence and the control that intervening would bring them."}, {"id": "ologun-kosoko-oshodi-tapa#s6", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "Kosoko’s rival, the Oba he had driven out, saw his chance. He made himself agreeable to the British, promising to end the slave trade if they would restore him to the throne. And so the British, with their overwhelming firepower, their warships and their guns, intervened directly in the affairs of Lagos. They attacked."}, {"id": "ologun-kosoko-oshodi-tapa#s7", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "And here, children, was a war unlike any our war-lords had fought before. Kosoko and Oshodi Tapa were brave, and they were skilled, and they had the loyalty of many of the people of Lagos. But they were facing the bombardment of modern British warships, cannon that could level a town from the water, a technology of destruction against which the old courage and the old weapons could do little. The British shelled Lagos. The fighting was fierce and bloody, and Oshodi Tapa led the desperate defense with all his famous courage. But in the end, against that overwhelming force, Kosoko and Oshodi could not hold the island. They were driven out. Kosoko, the fierce king, was forced once more into exile, and Oshodi Tapa went with him, faithful still."}, {"id": "ologun-kosoko-oshodi-tapa#s8", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "The British installed their preferred Oba, and from that foothold, that intervention in a Lagos succession war, grew the whole edifice of British colonial power in our land. Lagos became a British possession, then a colony, and from Lagos the foreign power reached out, over the following decades, to swallow all of Yorubaland and beyond. The taking of Lagos was the doorway through which colonial rule walked into our whole world."}, {"id": "ologun-kosoko-oshodi-tapa#s9", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Kosoko lived on in his exile, a king without a throne, and Oshodi Tapa remained with him. And here the story turns to its quieter, sadder, wiser end. In time, after years of exile, terms were made. Kosoko was eventually allowed to return to Lagos, no longer as king, for that door was closed forever, but as a great chief, to live out his days in the city he had once ruled. And Oshodi Tapa too returned and was settled, a mighty war-lord in a Lagos that was now, irreversibly, under the white man’s flag. The very district of Oshodi in Lagos, which many of you will know, carries his name to this day, a monument in the living map of the city to the faithful war-chief who rose from slavery to greatness."}, {"id": "ologun-kosoko-oshodi-tapa#s10", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "Now the Afonja lesson, children, and this pair carries a heavy one. Let us be honest and hold every palm. Kosoko was a fierce and capable king, and there is genuine tragedy and even a kind of grandeur in his long struggle, driven from his throne, fighting the overwhelming power of the foreigner, exiled and returning. And Oshodi Tapa’s loyalty and his rise from bondage are genuinely magnificent. But we cannot forget what the war was fundamentally about. Kosoko’s Lagos was a great slaving port, and his resistance to the British was, in significant part, resistance to the ending of that terrible trade. This is the deep and painful complexity of this story, children. The brave defenders of Lagos against foreign invasion were also, in the same breath, defenders of a commerce in human misery. And the foreign invader who overthrew them, whatever his own greed for power, did in fact bring that particular horror to an end."}, {"id": "ologun-kosoko-oshodi-tapa#s11", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "There is no clean side to stand on here. Hold all the palms at once. The courage was real. The loyalty was real. The tragedy of foreign conquest was real. And the ugliness of what was partly being defended was also real. History, children, is very rarely a simple tale of the good and the wicked. More often it is a tangle of brave people caught in terrible systems, and the honest teller must show you the whole tangle. So remember Kosoko and Oshodi Tapa, children, and remember them whole. Remember the fierce exiled king and his faithful war-lord who rose from slavery. Remember their courage against the warships. Remember the loyalty that bound them through triumph and ruin, a loyalty so complete that the war-chief followed his fallen king into exile and back. And remember, too, the hard truths, the slaving port they defended, the doorway their defeat opened for the colonizer."}, {"id": "ologun-kosoko-oshodi-tapa#s12", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "And remember above all the great turning that their story marks. With the fall of Kosoko’s Lagos, the old world of our people, the world of the Alaafins and the Aare and the war-lords, the world of towns rising and falling by the spear, began its final passing. The white man’s flag was planted on the island of the water, and from it a new and foreign power would spread over all our land. The age of the war-lords was ending. A new and harder age was beginning. And the war-chief’s name, Oshodi, lives on in the streets of the great city, spoken every day by people who have forgotten the faithful giant it honours. Tomorrow I will bring you a war-lord of the Ijebu country, and then I will begin to draw the whole long roll of our warriors toward its close."}]}, {"id": "ologun-balogun-kuku", "emoji": "⚔️", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#2a0a00,#5a1800)", "category": "ologun", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "9 min", "title": {"en": "Balogun Kuku: The War-Lord and Merchant Prince of Ijebu", "yo": "Kuku"}, "desc": {"en": "The Balogun and Seriki of Ijebu: war-chief, wealthy trader, and lord of Ijebu's last independent age.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Ologun / Warlords", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Jagunjagun, the story of Kuku, the great Balogun and Seriki of the Ijebu, war-chief, wealthy trader, and lavish lord of the last age of Ijebu independence)", "segments": [{"id": "ologun-balogun-kuku#s1", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Children, for the last of the war-lords I bring you tonight, we go to the Ijebu country, that proud and independent land between the interior and the sea, whose people were famed as shrewd and formidable traders, the great middlemen who for generations controlled the roads down which the goods of the interior flowed to the coast and the goods of the sea flowed up to the interior. And I bring you a man who embodied the Ijebu genius, for he was both a mighty war-lord and a fabulously wealthy merchant. His name was Kuku, and he was the Balogun and Seriki of the Ijebu, a war-chief of the last age of Ijebu greatness."}, {"id": "ologun-balogun-kuku#s2", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Understand the Ijebu, children. Where Ibadan’s genius was pure war, and Egba’s was the founding of a refuge, the Ijebu genius was trade. The Ijebu guarded jealously their position as the gatekeepers of commerce, controlling the routes between the interior and the lagoon-ports, growing rich as the toll-takers and middlemen of a vast trade. They kept their land closed and independent, deeply suspicious of outsiders, especially of the encroaching Europeans, whom they wisely distrusted, keeping the white traders and missionaries at arm’s length while other lands let them in. And in this world of war and wealth combined, Kuku rose to be a giant. He was a war-chief, holding high military titles, a leader of Ijebu’s fighting men in the wars of the age. But he was also, and perhaps above all, a merchant of enormous success, a man who grew staggeringly rich in the trade that was the lifeblood of his people."}, {"id": "ologun-balogun-kuku#s3", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "The tellings dwell on his wealth and his splendour, children, for he was a lord who lived large. His household was vast, his followers many, his compound a place of grandeur. He was the very picture of the successful Ijebu big-man, the war-lord and merchant prince combined, whose power rested on both the spear and the purse. In an age when many war-lords lived by plunder alone, Kuku’s power had a deep root in commerce, in the patient accumulation of trading wealth, which is a more lasting foundation than loot. He was, by the accounts, a proud and imposing figure, a man of great personal force, generous to his followers in the lavish way of a great Yoruba lord, surrounding himself with the display and the retinue befitting his enormous standing."}, {"id": "ologun-balogun-kuku#s4", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "And Kuku’s life sat right at the hinge of history, children, at the very end of old Ijebu independence. For all their shrewdness in keeping the Europeans out, the Ijebu could not hold the door forever. The British power, spreading from Lagos, wanted the Ijebu roads open, wanted the trade under their control, wanted the proud independent Ijebu brought to heel. And near the end of the century, the British made war on Ijebu, and with their modern weapons they broke the Ijebu resistance and brought that proud land, too, under the colonial flag. The gatekeepers’ gate was finally forced. Kuku belonged to that last generation of fully independent Ijebu greatness, the world that the British conquest brought to an end. He was among the great figures of Ijebu in the age just before and around that fall, a lord of the old order in its final flowering."}, {"id": "ologun-balogun-kuku#s5", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "Now, children, the honest hand, for Kuku’s record, while real, is more the record of a great regional big-man than of a nation-shaping conqueror, and I will not inflate him beyond his true measure. He was a giant of Ijebu, mighty in war and wealth, but his stage was chiefly his own land, and the details of his campaigns and his dealings come to us less fully than those of the great Ibadan lords. What he represents, and represents magnificently, is a particular kind of Yoruba greatness that the war-tales too often overlook, the greatness of the war-lord who was also a master of wealth, who understood that power rests not on the spear alone but on the control of trade and the patient building of riches."}, {"id": "ologun-balogun-kuku#s6", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "And the Afonja lesson, children. Kuku’s wealth, like all the wealth of that age, was entangled in the trade of the times, which in that region and era was still shadowed by the commerce in human beings even as it turned increasingly to palm-oil and ordinary goods. The Ijebu middlemen grew rich as gatekeepers, and gatekeeping takes its toll from all who must pass, so that their prosperity was built in part on the tolls squeezed from the trade of others, including, in the darker years, the trade in captives. Hold the two palms. On one side, a lord whose splendour rested on the hard tolls of a trade with its own cruelties. On the other, a genuine giant of his people, a man who embodied the shrewd commercial genius that kept Ijebu proud and independent longer than most, and who stood among the last great lords of that freedom before the foreign flag came down."}, {"id": "ologun-balogun-kuku#s7", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Here is the lesson I draw from Kuku, children, and it is a shrewd and practical one, very much in the Ijebu spirit. Power that rests on the spear alone is brittle, for the spear can be broken by a sharper spear, or by a warship’s cannon. But power that rests also on wealth, on trade, on the patient control of the things people need, has a deeper root. The Ijebu understood this. They knew that the merchant who controls the road is, in his own way, as mighty as the warrior who controls the field, and often outlasts him. Kuku was both, and that is why he was so great in his day."}, {"id": "ologun-balogun-kuku#s8", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "And there is a second lesson, a warning this time. The Ijebu were shrewd, and they were right to distrust the Europeans and to try to keep them out. Their instinct was sound. And yet, in the end, even their shrewdness could not save them, because they faced a power whose weapons were simply of another age. Sometimes, children, wisdom and caution are not enough against overwhelming force, and a proud people, having done everything right, are broken anyway by a stronger power. That is a bitter truth, but a griot must tell the bitter truths along with the sweet. The fall of independent Ijebu, like the fall of Kosoko’s Lagos and the long exhaustion of Kiriji, was part of the great closing of our old world."}, {"id": "ologun-balogun-kuku#s9", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "So remember Balogun Kuku, children, the war-lord and merchant prince of Ijebu, the man of the spear and the purse both, lord of the last age of Ijebu freedom. And with his name I bring this long telling of the war-lords toward its rest. We have walked, my children, through a whole world of warriors. The supreme Aare Ona Kakanfo who led the nation. The iron lords of Ibadan, Oluyole and Ogunmola and the gentle giant Ibikunle. The hunter Ogunlola who founded Ogbomoso. The lion Ogedengbe who held the eastern hills, and Fabunmi who lit their fire. The ancient Onikoyi and the champions of legend. The unbending Kurunmi whose pride destroyed his city. The farmer Lisabi who freed the Egba. The exiled king Kosoko and his faithful Oshodi Tapa. And now Kuku of Ijebu. A hundred kinds of greatness, and a hundred kinds of shadow, all of it ours, all of it true."}, {"id": "ologun-balogun-kuku#s10", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "Remember them all, children, and remember them whole, the glory and the shadow together, for that is the only honest way to remember, and the only way that lets the dead teach the living. Guard their names. Chant them at your own fires. And when the last of us who tell these tales has gone cold, may you take up the telling, so that as long as there are Yoruba children to gather in the evening, the giants of our people will never wholly die."}]}, {"id": "ologun-nupe-wars", "emoji": "⚔️", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#2a0a00,#5a1800)", "category": "ologun", "rating": 9.4, "duration": "4 min", "title": {"en": "The Nupe Wars: When Oyo Fell to the North", "yo": "The Nupe Wars: When Oyo Fell to the North"}, "desc": {"en": "How pressure from the Nupe and the north helped break the Oyo empire and send its people south.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Ologun / Warlords", "secondary": [], "source_note": "", "segments": [{"id": "ologun-nupe-wars#s1", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Narrator opens: Empires do not collapse the way buildings do — all at once, in a single dramatic instant. They fall in pieces, over years, each piece taking with it a structure that had held others up. The fall of the Oyo Empire was exactly this kind of disintegration — slow at first, then catastrophic. The Nupe people to the north of Oyo were long-standing neighbors. Trade moved between them. Alliances shifted. There were marriages and wars and periods of peace. But the Nupe were also being transformed by the forces of the Fulani Jihad — the great Islamic reform movement that swept across the West African savanna in the early 19th century and overturned the political order of Hausaland."}, {"id": "ologun-nupe-wars#s2", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "When the Fulani jihadists under Usman dan Fodio conquered Nupe, they created a new emirate — the Nupe Emirate, centered at Bida — that became a base for raiding and expansion southward, into Yorubaland. The Nupe cavalry — armed, disciplined, motivated by both religion and material gain — began raiding into northern Yoruba territories. Oyo, already weakened by internal conflicts, could not mount an adequate defense. Towns along the northern frontier fell one by one. The town of Ilorin — which had been a military settlement of the old Oyo Empire — was itself captured by the Fulani after the treachery of Afonja. Ilorin became an emirate and a base for further incursions southward."}, {"id": "ologun-nupe-wars#s3", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "The Nupe raids, combined with the Ilorin wars, drove waves of Yoruba refugees southward. The roads were crowded with displaced families, livestock, household goods. Villages that had stood for generations were abandoned. The forest belt that had always been the southern boundary of Yoruba settlement was penetrated by population movements unprecedented in scale. This great disruption — which Yoruba historians call the period of the Yoruba wars — lasted from roughly 1817 to 1893 and reshaped the entire human geography of southwestern Nigeria. It created Ibadan. It created Abeokuta. It recreated Oyo in the south. It drove the Ijesa and Ekiti southward. It caused the expansion of the Atlantic slave trade — because captives from the Yoruba wars fed the slave ships anchored off Lagos and Badagry."}, {"id": "ologun-nupe-wars#s4", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "The Nupe invasions were not the only cause of Oyo's fall. But they were a significant force — a pressure from the north that, combined with the internal fractures, the Dahomean wars in the west, and the slave trade in the south, created the perfect storm that ended the great empire. The suffering of that period was real. Whole communities erased. Sacred places abandoned. Oral histories interrupted. Things that should have been passed down — lost. But the Yoruba rebuilt. They always do. From Ibadan's ridge to Abeokuta's rock, they built again. And from the ashes of Oyo's grandeur, they carried the memory of what had been — so that it would not be entirely lost."}]}, {"id": "ologun-ibadan-warlords", "emoji": "⚔️", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#2a0a00,#5a1800)", "category": "ologun", "rating": 9.4, "duration": "4 min", "title": {"en": "The Ibadan Warlords: How a City of Refugees Became an Empire", "yo": "The Ibadan Warlords: How a City of Refugees Became an Empire"}, "desc": {"en": "How a hilltop camp of refugees and soldiers became Ibadan, a city where talent and war could raise any man.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Ologun / Warlords", "secondary": [], "source_note": "", "segments": [{"id": "ologun-ibadan-warlords#s1", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Narrator opens: Ibadan was not planned. It was not founded by a divine ancestor descending from heaven or a royal lineage asserting primacy. Ibadan was built by people who had nowhere else to go — refugees, soldiers, warlords, camp followers, escaped slaves, and ambitious men from a dozen different Yoruba sub-groups who arrived at a hilltop location in the 1820s and began building. The Oyo Empire had collapsed. The Fulani jihad from the north had swept through Ilorin and was pushing south. The old centers of Yoruba power — Old Oyo, Owu, Ede — were burning or had burned. Hundreds of thousands of people were displaced, moving south and southwest in search of safety. And on the Oke Ibadan hill, among the sheltering trees, a community began to coalesce."}, {"id": "ologun-ibadan-warlords#s2", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "What distinguished Ibadan from the other refugee settlements was the character of its leadership. Ibadan was controlled by its military — specifically by a rotating group of warlords whose authority derived entirely from battlefield achievement and the loyalty of the men they commanded. There was no Oba in the traditional sense. There was no royal family claiming divine origin. There was the Are-Ona-Kakanfo, and below him the Bashorun, the Otun, the Balogun — titles that in other cities were administrative but in Ibadan were military ranks, earned through war."}, {"id": "ologun-ibadan-warlords#s3", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "This meritocracy of violence created a peculiar energy. Ibadan attracted talent precisely because talent could rise. A man with no lineage, no royal connection, no inherited wealth, could arrive in Ibadan and — if he could fight, if he could lead men, if he could organize a war camp and bring back plunder — rise to the highest levels of power. This was not possible in Ile-Ife or in the old Oyo court or in most established Yoruba cities. It was possible in Ibadan. The result was explosive military growth. By 1840, Ibadan had become the largest city in West Africa. By 1850, it was the dominant military power in the Yoruba-speaking world, conducting campaigns in every direction. By 1880, it was extracting tribute from dozens of subordinate states and functioning as a regional empire — without an emperor, without a royal dynasty, without any of the traditional structures of Yoruba kingship."}, {"id": "ologun-ibadan-warlords#s4", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "The Ibadan model — military meritocracy, non-royal governance, rapid expansion fueled by warfare and trade — was genuinely revolutionary in the Yoruba context. It was also ultimately unsustainable: without the constitutional checks of the Oyo Mesi system, without a legitimate succession mechanism, the military leadership was constantly internally contested. Ibadan fought external wars and internal succession crises simultaneously. But for sixty years, it was the most powerful city in Yorubaland. Not because it was the most legitimate. Because it was the most willing."}]}, {"id": "ologun-balogun-system", "emoji": "⚔️", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#2a0a00,#5a1800)", "category": "ologun", "rating": 9.4, "duration": "4 min", "title": {"en": "The Balogun System: How Ibadan Organized Its Wars", "yo": "The Balogun System: How Ibadan Organized Its Wars"}, "desc": {"en": "How Ibadan organized its wars through the Balogun system of ranked war-chiefs.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Ologun / Warlords", "secondary": [], "source_note": "", "segments": [{"id": "ologun-balogun-system#s1", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Narrator opens: The Ibadan military system was a marvel of improvised organization — built not from a theoretical framework of military governance but from the practical demands of a city of warlords who had to coordinate their forces in the field while managing their competition for power at home. At the top of the system was the Olubadan (later the Are-Ona-Kakanfo when that title was associated with Ibadan) — the supreme civil and military head of the city. Below him was the Balogun — the war chief, the supreme field commander. Below the Balogun was a hierarchy of junior war chiefs: the Otun Balogun (right hand), the Osi Balogun (left hand), the Ekerin Balogun (fourth war chief), and others, each commanding their own units and their own war camps."}, {"id": "ologun-balogun-system#s2", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "The system was meritocratic in a specific sense: promotion happened not by appointment from above but by movement through the ranks as senior officers died or were deposed. When the Balogun died, the Otun Balogun moved up. When the Otun died, the Ekerin moved up. This created a predictable succession that prevented some (not all) of the violent succession crises that plagued other Yoruba political systems. But the system also created a specific military culture. Every senior officer in Ibadan knew that his advancement depended on the death of his superior. This created a complex dynamic: officers were expected to fight together and to rely on each other in the field, while knowing that each other's mortality was the condition of their own advancement. The most sophisticated commanders managed this by making themselves personally indispensable to those above and below them in the hierarchy, ensuring that their death would genuinely harm the system rather than simply open a vacancy."}, {"id": "ologun-balogun-system#s3", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "The Balogun of Ibadan was, at the height of the empire, one of the most powerful military figures in West Africa. He commanded tens of thousands of warriors, controlled trade routes, received tribute from subordinate towns, and made the military decisions that shaped the entire region. The Balogun title passed through some of the most consequential figures of the nineteenth century — Ibikunle, Ajobo, Latoosa — each of whom shaped the empire's trajectory in their era. When Ibadan's power declined in the late nineteenth century and the British colonial administration took over military governance, the Balogun system survived as a civil title. It is still conferred today — the Balogun of Ibadan is a senior traditional chief whose appointment by the Olubadan is one of the most significant events in the city's traditional governance. The military content is gone. The memory of what the title once meant remains."}]}, {"id": "ologun-egba-wars", "emoji": "⚔️", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#2a0a00,#5a1800)", "category": "ologun", "rating": 9.4, "duration": "4 min", "title": {"en": "The Egba Wars of Resistance: How Abeokuta Fought for Survival", "yo": "The Egba Wars of Resistance: How Abeokuta Fought for Survival"}, "desc": {"en": "How Abeokuta fought for survival against Dahomey, Ibadan, Ijebu, and British pressure.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Ologun / Warlords", "secondary": [], "source_note": "", "segments": [{"id": "ologun-egba-wars#s1", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Narrator opens: The Egba people of Abeokuta fought for their survival with a consistency and an organization that their more celebrated neighbors have not always received the credit for providing. Surrounded by more powerful military forces — Dahomey to the west, Ibadan to the northeast, Ijebu to the east — and subjected to British pressure from Lagos to the south, the Egba maintained their independence through a combination of military capability, organizational sophistication, diplomatic agility, and the specific advantage of the Olumo Rock."}, {"id": "ologun-egba-wars#s2", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "The Rock — Olumo — is not merely a landscape feature in Abeokuta's story. It is the city's original fortress, the natural defensive position that the Egba refugees who settled Abeokuta in the 1830s immediately recognized as an asset of incalculable military value. The Rock rises from the town's center, commanding lines of sight in multiple directions, offering shelter to civilians during attack and elevated firing positions to defenders. The city was, in military terms, built around the Rock."}, {"id": "ologun-egba-wars#s3", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "The Dahomey attacks — 1851 and 1864 — were the most dramatic tests of this defensive system. Dahomey's army, which had devastated other Yoruba towns with the same strategy, found at Abeokuta something it could not simply overwhelm: a population organized, motivated, and positioned to use their terrain as a weapon. The repulsion of the 1851 attack — which astonished the British missionaries and observers who witnessed it — established Abeokuta's military reputation and demonstrated that even the most powerful raiding force in the region could be stopped by a community prepared to fight for its home."}, {"id": "ologun-egba-wars#s4", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "But the military history of Abeokuta is not only about defensive success. The Egba conducted offensive campaigns as well — against Ibadan's encroachments, against the Ijebu middlemen who controlled the trade routes, against the various threats from neighboring powers. The Egba military leadership — the Bashorun of the Egba and the war chiefs of the various Egba sub-groups — managed complex coalition warfare across decades of conflict. The British annexation of Lagos in 1861 changed the strategic environment dramatically. Suddenly the Egba had a colonial power on their southern border, with interests that conflicted with Egba commercial autonomy and that had the military capacity to enforce those interests. The management of the British relationship — simultaneously trading with Lagos, resisting British pressure for unlimited access, maintaining the independence of Abeokuta's governance — occupied the Egba leadership for the next forty years."}, {"id": "ologun-egba-wars#s5", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "The Egba Provisional Government, formed in the late nineteenth century, was one of the most sophisticated forms of political organization produced by any Yoruba community in the colonial era — a hybrid institution that combined traditional Yoruba governance with elements of the new political forms that the Saro community and the British colonial system had introduced. It was an attempt to navigate modernity without abandoning identity. It succeeded for a time, then fell to the British in 1914 when the protectorate system eliminated Egba independence as part of the amalgamation of Nigeria. The Egba fought. They adapted. They survived longer in independence than almost any other Yoruba community in the south. That is a military and political achievement that deserves full recognition."}]}, {"id": "eru-barracoons-of-badagry", "emoji": "⛓️", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#1a1308,#3a2a10)", "category": "itan-eru", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "6 min", "title": {"en": "The Barracoons of Badagry", "yo": "Badagry"}, "desc": {"en": "The holding pens of Badagry, the well of forgetting, and the walk to the surf that made the town's name one of the saddest words on the coast.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Itan Eru", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Eru / Slave Trade, consolidates “Barracoons of Badagry” and “Barracoon at Badagry”)", "segments": [{"id": "eru-barracoons-of-badagry#s1", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "On the low sandy coast where the lagoon meets the sea, there stood a town whose name became, for a time, one of the saddest words in the whole language of the Yoruba and their neighbors. Badagry. It was a place of trade, as many coastal towns were, but the trade that made it notorious was a trade in people, and the buildings that gave it its dark fame were the barracoons, the long low sheds and stockaded pens where captured human beings were held, sometimes for weeks or months, waiting for the ships that would carry them across the water and out of the world forever. To walk the ground of Badagry, the elders say, is to walk on one of the great wounds of the earth."}, {"id": "eru-barracoons-of-badagry#s2", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "The people who ended up in those pens came from every direction and every walk of life, and this is the part that the tellers insist we understand, because it is easy to imagine the captured as some faceless mass and miss the truth of them. They were farmers taken in a raid on their village at dawn. They were traders seized on the road. They were soldiers captured in the endless wars of the interior, and children snatched while they played, and men sold by their own rulers to pay a debt or remove a rival, and women taken from markets they had walked to a hundred times in safety. Each one had a name, a mother, a town, a whole life behind them that ended the moment the rope went round their wrists. Among them, in those very pens, were people whose descendants would one day shape the world, but in the barracoon they were only frightened human beings in the dark, listening to the sea."}, {"id": "eru-barracoons-of-badagry#s3", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "The conditions were a horror that the storytellers approach carefully, not to wallow in the suffering but to refuse to let it be forgotten. Human beings were packed into spaces too small for them, chained, underfed, sick, grieving, stripped of everything that had made them who they were, and left to wait. Some died in the pens before they ever saw a ship. Some, driven past the edge of endurance, tried to take their own lives rather than face what they knew was coming. And over it all lay the terrible ordinariness of it, the fact that this was business, that ledgers were kept and prices haggled and profits counted, that the buyers and sellers went home at night to their own children while the children of others wept in the sheds behind them."}, {"id": "eru-barracoons-of-badagry#s4", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "There was a ritual on that coast that the storytellers speak of in hushed voices, a thing said to have been done at a certain spot before the captives were rowed out to the waiting ships. They were made to walk around a particular object, or to drink from a particular well, in a ceremony the traders claimed would make them forget, forget their homes, forget their names, forget the land behind them so that they would not pine and die on the crossing or dream of return. Whether it worked, whether anything could truly erase a person’s home from their heart, no one can say. But the very existence of such a ritual tells you everything about the trade, that it understood it was not merely moving bodies but trying to murder memory, to sever human beings so completely from who they had been that they could be remade into property. And this, the elders say, was the deepest cruelty of all, deeper even than the chains, the attempt to steal not just a person’s freedom but their very self."}, {"id": "eru-barracoons-of-badagry#s5", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "Yet memory, it turned out, was harder to kill than the traders knew. Across the water, in the strange lands where the ships unloaded their human cargo, the stolen people did not entirely forget. They remembered the orisa and whispered their names in new tongues. They remembered the rhythms and beat them out on new drums. They remembered the foods and the words and the ways, and passed fragments of them to their children, and their children to theirs, until on the far side of the ocean whole new peoples grew who carried, without always knowing its source, the memory that Badagry had tried to erase. The well of forgetting failed. The home the traders tried to wash from a million hearts survived, buried but alive, and one day the descendants of the forgotten would come back across the water to Badagry looking for exactly the thing that had been taken, and would find that it had been waiting for them all along."}, {"id": "eru-barracoons-of-badagry#s6", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So the elders tell of the barracoons of Badagry not to reopen a wound for its own sake, but because some things must be remembered precisely so that they can never happen again. They tell it to honor the nameless ones who passed through those pens and vanished across the water, to give them back at least the dignity of being remembered as people and not as cargo. And they tell it to mark the strange, hard-won grace at the heart of the whole terrible story, that a trade built on the murder of memory ended by scattering Yoruba memory across the whole earth, so that today the orisa are worshipped and the drums are beaten and the old names are spoken in lands the captives were carried to in chains. Badagry meant to be the end of the story for the people who passed through it. Instead, against all the intentions of the men who ran it, it became the beginning of a story that spread across the whole world, and that is still being told."}]}, {"id": "eru-wars-and-supply-of-captives", "emoji": "⛓️", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#1a1308,#3a2a10)", "category": "itan-eru", "rating": 9.7, "duration": "15 min", "title": {"en": "The Yoruba Wars and the Supply of Captives", "yo": "The Yoruba Wars and the Supply of Captives"}, "desc": {"en": "How the nineteenth-century Yoruba wars fed captives to the Atlantic trade, and how the trade made the wars harder to end.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Itan Eru", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Oko-Eru, consolidates “The Yoruba Wars and the Supply of Captives” and “The Ijesha and the Internal Slave Markets”)", "segments": [{"id": "eru-wars-and-supply-of-captives#s1", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Come closer, and let the fire settle its light on our faces, because tonight I will tell you a hard thing gently, the way an elder holds a wound so that it can be cleaned. There is a story that Yorubaland does not like to tell about itself, and yet the ancestors ask that it be told, because a people who cannot look at their own scar cannot teach their children how the scar was earned. This is the story of how war became a market, and how the market ate its own children, and how, even in the belly of that darkness, our people remained people, with names, with courage, with the stubborn dignity that no chain could rust."}, {"id": "eru-wars-and-supply-of-captives#s2", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "To understand it, you must first understand that Yorubaland was not always at war. There was a season, long and golden, when Oyo sat like a lion at the top of the savannah, and its Alaafin sent his horsemen north to the edge of the desert and south to the lagoon, and the roads were safe enough that a woman could carry indigo cloth from Ilorin to Apomu and sleep under the stars without fear. Trade moved like blood through a healthy body. Kola went one way, salt the other, brass and beads and iron and dried fish, and the towns grew fat and proud and full of festivals. In those days a captive was a thing of the household, a debtor working off what he owed, a prisoner who might be ransomed, a stranger who might in time marry into the family that held him. Bondage existed, yes, for no old kingdom on this earth was innocent of it, but it was small, and slow, and bound by custom that gave even the lowest person a road back toward freedom."}, {"id": "eru-wars-and-supply-of-captives#s3", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Then the lion’s heart failed. When Afonja broke with the Alaafin at the turn of the nineteenth century, and the Fulani jihad came down out of the north to settle in Ilorin, the great house of Oyo cracked from its roof to its foundation. And when a great house falls, it does not fall on empty ground. It falls on the towns beneath it. The collapse of Oyo unleashed a rolling thunder of wars that lasted the better part of a century, war after war after war, Owu against the allied armies, Ibadan rising from the ashes of the broken north, Ijaye under Kurunmi, the long grinding Ekitiparapo struggle in the east, the Egba fortifying at Abeokuta, and a thousand smaller raids that history did not bother to name. This was the season the elders called the time when the roads drank blood."}, {"id": "eru-wars-and-supply-of-captives#s4", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Now understand the terrible machinery of it, my children, because this is where the story turns from sorrow into something you must never forget. War makes captives. It always has. When Ibadan’s army broke an Ekiti town, the warriors did not kill everyone, for a dead person is worth nothing, but a living person can be sold. The young man of nineteen with strong shoulders, the woman skilled at dyeing cloth, the boy quick enough to learn a trade, these were gathered like harvest and driven along the forest paths in coffles, their necks in forked branches, their wrists bound, walking toward the coast where the ships waited with their bellies open."}, {"id": "eru-wars-and-supply-of-captives#s5", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "And here is the poison that made the wound so slow to heal. The wars did not feed the trade by accident alone. The trade came, in time, to feed the wars. A war chief needed guns, and guns came from the coast, and the coast wanted only one payment above all others, human beings. So a Balogun would make war partly to win, and partly to take captives, and he would march those captives to Lagos or Badagry or Porto Novo, and there he would exchange them for kegs of powder and long Dane guns and flints and bars of iron, and with those weapons he would make more war, and take more captives, and buy more guns. It was a wheel, and the wheel was greased with our own blood. The Ijesa knew it. The Egba knew it. The Ibadan war-boys knew it. Every town along the frontier knew it, and yet no town could stop turning the wheel alone, for the town that laid down its guns first would simply become the next harvest."}, {"id": "eru-wars-and-supply-of-captives#s6", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Let me take you now to Ijesa country, to the town of Ilesa and the markets around it, because the Ijesa story shows you the machinery from the inside, and it is a story that refuses to let anyone off easily. The Ijesa sat in the eastern hills, proud and old, a people who traced their kings back to Ile-Ife itself and who bowed their heads to no Oyo overlord willingly. But their hills sat squarely on the road along which captives moved between the interior and the lagoon ports, and geography is a kind of destiny. Ilesa became a place where captives changed hands, where a person taken in a northern war might be sold to an Ijesa dealer, held for a season, and sold onward toward the coast. There were market days when the ordinary business of yams and cloth and pepper went on in one part of the square, and in another part, under the same sun, human beings stood waiting to be priced."}, {"id": "eru-wars-and-supply-of-captives#s7", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "I will not pretend to you that this was done by strangers. It was done by our own. Some Ijesa grew rich on it, built compounds with many wives and many horses, poured libations to their ancestors with palm wine bought by the sale of other people’s children. And this is the hardest lesson of the fire, the one I would spare you if I could but must not. Evil in this world rarely arrives wearing a stranger’s face. More often it wears the face of a respected man, a titled man, a man who loved his own children fiercely and could not see, or would not see, that the child he sold was as beloved somewhere as his own. The trade did not need monsters. It needed only ordinary people who agreed not to look too closely at the source of their prosperity."}, {"id": "eru-wars-and-supply-of-captives#s8", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "But do not let me leave you with the lie that our people were only the sellers, or only the sold, for the truth is braided from both, and there were those who fought the wheel with everything they had. Some towns refused to trade in people even when it cost them. Some families hid captives and quietly adopted them rather than sell them onward, folding a stranger’s child into their own lineage so completely that within a generation no one remembered the difference. Some chiefs used their portion of captives not to sell but to found new farm settlements, and those settlements grew into towns whose descendants walk free today and do not know how close their grandmothers came to the sea. And when the missionaries and the freed men began to return in the middle of the century, carrying letters and Bibles and a new fierce argument that no man should own another, there were Yoruba voices, our own voices, that took up that argument and would not put it down."}, {"id": "eru-wars-and-supply-of-captives#s9", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Let me give you one face, so that the wars do not remain a fog of names. Think of a young Ekiti woman, and let us call her Aduke, for the true names of most of the taken were never written and we owe them at least an invented dignity. She was the daughter of a hunter, skilled at weaving palm mats, betrothed to a drummer in her own town. One dry season the war-boys of a stronger town came at dawn, when the cocks had just begun, and by the time the sun stood overhead her town was smoke and her betrothed was dead in the yam furrows and she was walking, roped at the neck, in a line of forty others, toward a place she had never seen and whose name filled her with dread. She passed through three markets. At the first, an old woman pressed a ball of eko into her hand and wept for her without knowing her, because grief needs no introduction. At the second, a dealer priced her by pressing her arms and looking at her teeth as though she were a goat. At the third, near the lagoon, she saw the water for the first time, wide and grey and endless, and she understood that beyond it lay a country from which the taken did not return, and something in her went very quiet and very hard."}, {"id": "eru-wars-and-supply-of-captives#s10", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "I tell you Aduke’s story not because I know it happened to a woman of that exact name, but because it happened to tens of thousands of women, and men, and children whose names the fire has forgotten. The griot’s duty is to give the forgotten back their weight, to say with the mouth what the ledgers of the traders never bothered to record, that each of them had a mother’s song, a favourite food, a way of laughing, a name that meant something in a language that crossed the ocean and survived."}, {"id": "eru-wars-and-supply-of-captives#s11", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "Now let me show you the wider map, because the Yoruba wars did not burn alone. To the west stood Dahomey, a kingdom built like a spear, whose kings raided the Yoruba borderlands year after year and marched their captives down to Ouidah on the coast. To the north the disruption of the jihad sent its own currents of displaced people southward. And along the whole lagoon, from Lagos to Badagry to Porto Novo to Ouidah, the European ships waited, Portuguese and Brazilian and Spanish and, until their own laws turned against it, British and French, their captains indifferent to which town had defeated which, caring only that the coffles kept arriving. The demand of distant plantations in Brazil and Cuba and the islands reached its long invisible hand all the way into the Ekiti hills and the Ijesa markets and set town against town without ever showing its face. That is the thing you must understand about this evil. It was global. A sugar planter in Bahia who never saw Africa, who could not have found Ilesa on a map, was nonetheless tied by an unbroken chain of greed to the war-boy who burned Aduke’s town. The trade made the whole Atlantic into one dark room, and we were only one wall of it."}, {"id": "eru-wars-and-supply-of-captives#s12", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "And then, slowly, the tide began to turn, and this too is part of the story, for a people must know not only how the darkness came but how it broke. In 1807 the British outlawed the trade for their own ships, and by the 1820s their navy patrolled the coast, stopping slavers and freeing their captives at Freetown in Sierra Leone. Many of those freed were Yoruba, taken in the very wars I have described, and in Freetown they found one another, gathered into congregations, learned to read, and began, astonishingly, to sail home. They came back to Abeokuta and Lagos and Ibadan as the Saro, freed people who had seen the ocean’s far side and returned to argue, in their own language, that the wheel must stop. Among them was a boy taken from Osogun, sold, freed, and educated, who would become Samuel Ajayi Crowther, the first African bishop of the Anglican church, who would put the Yoruba language into written letters and translate the Bible into the tongue of his mother. The trade that tried to erase him instead sharpened him into one of the instruments of its own undoing. That is the strange arithmetic of history. The same current that carried our people away carried some of them back transformed, and they became the sword that cut the rope."}, {"id": "eru-wars-and-supply-of-captives#s13", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So what do we carry away from this fire tonight, my children? Not despair, for despair is a luxury the ancestors did not have and did not leave to us. We carry three things. We carry honesty, the courage to say that the trade was fed by our own hands as well as by foreign ships, because a people who blame only outsiders will never guard against the seller within. We carry sorrow, real and unhurried, for the millions of names the ledgers stole, the Adukes and the drummers and the mat-weavers whose only monument is our willingness to remember them. And we carry pride, stubborn and clear-eyed, in the truth that the trade did not win. It did not erase the language, it did not erase the orisa, it did not erase the drum, and it did not erase the people. Across the ocean our grandmothers’ grandchildren still call on Sango and Yemoja by their right names. The wheel turned for a hundred years and then it broke, and we are still here, telling the story, which is the one thing the traders never managed to buy."}, {"id": "eru-wars-and-supply-of-captives#s14", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "Sleep now, and dream not of chains but of the returning ships, the ones that came back full of freed people who had learned to read and could not be sold again. That is where this story points. Not down into the dark, but back up toward the light."}]}, {"id": "eru-osifekunde-of-ijebu", "emoji": "⛓️", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#1a1308,#3a2a10)", "category": "itan-eru", "rating": 9.6, "duration": "10 min", "title": {"en": "Osifekunde of Ijebu: Testimony Across the Water", "yo": "Osifekunde"}, "desc": {"en": "A boy stolen from Ijebu who, decades later and an ocean away, gave scholars one of the earliest detailed accounts of his homeland.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Itan Eru", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Eniyan, consolidates “Osifekunde of Ijebu: Testimony Across the Water”)", "segments": [{"id": "eru-osifekunde-of-ijebu#s1", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Gather in, my children, and let me tell you of a man who was stolen from Ijebu as a boy and who, forty years later and a whole ocean away, sat in the great libraries of Paris and gave to the scholars of France the first careful map of his own country from the inside. His name was Osifekunde, and his life is proof of a thing the traders never understood, that you may steal a man’s body and carry it across the water, but you cannot so easily steal the country he carries in his head. He kept Ijebu alive inside himself for four decades among strangers, and when at last a scholar thought to ask him what he remembered, out it came, whole and shining, the rivers and the markets and the titles and the trade routes, as though he had walked out of Ijebu that very morning."}, {"id": "eru-osifekunde-of-ijebu#s2", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Let me begin where his life began, in Ijebu-Ode, the proud old town of the Ijebu, a people known across Yorubaland as shrewd traders who guarded their roads and their secrets with equal care. Osifekunde was born there near the end of the eighteenth century, into a family that knew the water, for the Ijebu were canoe people as much as farm people, and their wealth flowed along the lagoons and creeks that lace that whole country like veins. As a boy he learned the names of the waterways and the goods that moved along them, cloth and ivory and salt and beads and, yes, captives, for the Ijebu were middlemen in the coastal trade and the boy grew up watching the traffic of the world pass through his people’s hands. He learned to paddle, to bargain, to read the moods of the tide. He was being shaped into a trader, a man of the creeks, and had the world left him alone he would have lived and died an Ijebu merchant of no particular fame, remembered only by his own descendants."}, {"id": "eru-osifekunde-of-ijebu#s3", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "But the world did not leave him alone. When he was perhaps nineteen or twenty, Osifekunde was travelling the waters on the business of his family, as he had done a hundred times, when his canoe was set upon and he was seized. In the tangle of the coastal trade a free Ijebu trader was, in the wrong stretch of water on the wrong day, simply another body that could be sold, and the same lagoons that had carried his people’s wealth now carried him away as cargo. He was taken to the coast, passed into the hands of the European ships, and loaded aboard a vessel bound for Brazil. Picture the boy who had known every creek of his homeland now shut in the dark below a ship’s deck, the water beneath him no longer the familiar lagoon of Ijebu but the vast and terrible Atlantic, carrying him toward a country whose very name he did not yet know."}, {"id": "eru-osifekunde-of-ijebu#s4", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "He survived the crossing, which by itself makes him one of the fortunate, if that cruel word can be used at all, for many did not. He was landed in Brazil and sold, and there he lived and laboured for many years, long enough to learn Portuguese, long enough to move through the world of the Brazilian ports, long enough that the boy from the Ijebu creeks became a grown man weathered by a life he had never chosen. And yet, through all of it, he did not let go of Ijebu. He remembered. He held the names and the routes and the customs the way a man holds a coal wrapped in leaves through a long journey, keeping it alive against the day he might need its fire again."}, {"id": "eru-osifekunde-of-ijebu#s5", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "By some turning of fortune, Osifekunde eventually came to France, to Paris itself, in the years around 1830. And there, in that city of scholars and salons, a remarkable thing happened. A French geographer named Marie Armand Pascal d’Avezac, a man hungry to understand the interior of West Africa, which to European mapmakers was still a great blank space marked mostly with guesses, found in this African man a living library. Osifekunde could describe Ijebu from the inside. Not the coast as glimpsed from a slaver’s deck, but the true country, its towns and its government, its trade and its neighbours, its beliefs and its language."}, {"id": "eru-osifekunde-of-ijebu#s6", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "And so began one of the strangest and most precious collaborations in the whole history of the trade. The geographer asked, and the Ijebu man answered, and out of Osifekunde’s memory came a portrait of a Yoruba country that no European had ever set eyes upon. He described the town of Ijebu-Ode and its ruler, the Awujale. He described the system of titles and the councils that governed the town. He described the lagoons and the canoe trade, the goods that moved and the peoples who traded them, the Ijebu, the Egba, the peoples of Benin to the east, the coastal ports to the south. He described the religion of his people, the orisa, the divination, the festivals. He even gave d’Avezac a vocabulary of the Ijebu dialect of Yoruba, words set down on paper by a European’s hand but drawn wholly from an African’s memory. When at last the geographer published this account in 1845, folded into a larger French work on the peoples of Africa, it became one of the earliest detailed descriptions of any Yoruba society ever committed to print, and every word of its worth came from a man who had been sold as cargo."}, {"id": "eru-osifekunde-of-ijebu#s7", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Now think carefully on the meaning of this, my children, for there are riches in it. First, consider the memory itself. Forty years, an ocean, two other languages layered over his own, a life of bondage and displacement that would have scattered a weaker mind, and yet Osifekunde held Ijebu intact. When we say that the trade could not erase a people, this is what we mean, not a slogan but a specific man who carried an entire country in his head across four decades and set it down whole. The plantations of Brazil could command his labour but they could not enter the room where he kept his home."}, {"id": "eru-osifekunde-of-ijebu#s8", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Second, consider the dignity of the exchange. In the libraries of Paris the roles quietly reversed. There the European was the ignorant one, the blank map, the man who did not know, and the African was the teacher, the source, the keeper of knowledge that Europe wanted and did not have. There is a justice in that image that no court ever delivered, the stolen man become the authority, his despised knowledge become the treasure. The Ijebu boy priced by the pound in a Brazilian market was, at the end, the master in that Paris room, and the geographer merely his scribe."}, {"id": "eru-osifekunde-of-ijebu#s9", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "Third, and this is the part I want you to carry longest, consider what Osifekunde chose to do with his one chance to be heard. He might have spoken only of his suffering, and no one would have blamed him. Instead he chose to give a full account of a whole civilisation, its government and its trade and its faith and its tongue, insisting through every detail that his people were not a blank space or a savage rumour but a society as complex and ordered as any in Europe. He used his voice, when at last he had one, not merely to accuse but to testify, to place on the permanent record the truth that Ijebu was a real place full of real institutions and real intelligence. In doing so he answered, across the centuries, the whole vile logic of the trade, which depended on pretending that the people it consumed were less than people. Here, the logic said, is a savage. And Osifekunde replied, quietly, in fluent detail, here is my country, here are its laws, here are the names of its rivers, now tell me again what a savage is."}, {"id": "eru-osifekunde-of-ijebu#s10", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "We do not know for certain how his life ended. Some accounts say he was given the chance to remain free in France, others that he chose in the end to return toward Brazil where he had built what fragments of a life bondage had allowed him. The records go quiet, as they so often do around our people, and the last chapter is lost to us. But it does not matter as much as you might think. What matters is what he left behind, a testimony that outlived him, a country preserved in a stolen man’s memory and set down where it could never again be denied."}, {"id": "eru-osifekunde-of-ijebu#s11", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So when someone tells you that the enslaved were only victims, that their story is only one of loss, remember the man from the Ijebu creeks who sat in Paris and taught France what Yorubaland was. Remember that he kept his home alive inside himself for forty years, and that when the moment came, he opened that room and let the whole world look in. That is not the story of a broken man. That is the story of a keeper of his people’s flame, who carried it across the water and would not let it go out."}]}, {"id": "eru-adunola-refused-to-board", "emoji": "⛓️", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#1a1308,#3a2a10)", "category": "itan-eru", "rating": 9.6, "duration": "10 min", "title": {"en": "Adunola: The Woman Who Refused to Board", "yo": "Adunola"}, "desc": {"en": "A captive woman who sat down on the sand at the water's edge, turned toward home, and would not walk to the ship.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Itan Eru", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Akikanju Obinrin, consolidates “Adunola: The Woman Who Refused to Board”)", "segments": [{"id": "eru-adunola-refused-to-board#s1", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Come and sit, my children, and let me tell you tonight of a woman whose name means crown-brings-honour, Adunola, and of the morning she stood on the sand at the water’s edge and would not walk up the plank onto the ship. This is not a story of a great queen or a famous warrior whose deeds are carved into the drums of history. It is the story of one woman, one of the countless taken, and yet I tell it to you above many others, because in her single act of refusal there lives the whole unconquered spirit of a people. When you feel that you are small, that you are one against many, that resistance is pointless because the powerful will do as they wish, I want you to remember Adunola, who was indeed one against many, who indeed could not win, and who refused anyway, and in refusing became larger than everyone who commanded her."}, {"id": "eru-adunola-refused-to-board#s2", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Let me first give her a life, because the traders wanted her to be only a body and a price, and it is our task at the fire to give her back her fullness. Adunola was a woman of the interior, born in a farming town in the years when the wars burned hottest. She was not a child when she was taken. She was a grown woman, a wife, a mother, a dyer of cloth whose indigo was praised at the market, a woman whose hands knew work and whose voice knew song. She had buried her own mother and raised her own daughters. She had standing in her compound and a seat among the women. She was, in short, a full person with a full life, the kind of life the trade pretended did not exist so that it could turn her into cargo without troubling its conscience."}, {"id": "eru-adunola-refused-to-board#s3", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "The war came to her town as it came to so many, at the grey hour before dawn, with fire and the screaming of horses. When it was over, Adunola was among the roped line of survivors driven south toward the coast. I will not dwell long on that walk, for you have heard its like before, the forked branches at the neck, the sores, the hunger, the ones who fell and did not rise. But I will tell you what the others on that march remembered about her, because it matters. They remembered that she did not weep the loud weeping of the broken. She wept, yes, for she was human and had lost everything, but hers was a quiet, hard grief, and along the road she used what little strength she had to steady others. She sang low songs of her town under her breath, songs the others knew, and where a younger captive faltered she pressed her shoulder against theirs and carried them a little way with her voice. She was becoming, even in the coffle, what she had always been, a keeper of others."}, {"id": "eru-adunola-refused-to-board#s4", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "At the coast she was held in the barracoon, that terrible holding pen where the taken waited for the ships, and there she waited through days that blurred into one another, the smell of the sea and the fear thick in the air. And then came the morning the story remembers, the morning a ship stood at anchor and the captives were driven down to the water to be loaded into the canoes that would carry them out to the vessel. This was the last threshold. On this side of it lay Africa, the soil, the ancestors, the possibility, however faint, of escape or ransom or return. On the far side of it lay the ship, the crossing, the plantation, the country from which no one came back. Every person in that line knew, in their bones, that to walk up the plank was to walk out of the world they knew forever."}, {"id": "eru-adunola-refused-to-board#s5", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "Most walked. What else could they do? The guns were there, the whips were there, the chains were there. To refuse was not to win freedom, it was only to be beaten, perhaps to be killed. Refusal changed nothing, the men with the guns had already decided, the ledger was already written. And so, one after another, the taken walked toward the canoes, because the arithmetic of survival said walk. Adunola stopped."}, {"id": "eru-adunola-refused-to-board#s6", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "She reached the wet sand where the land gives itself to the sea, and she would not go forward. She sat down. She turned her face back toward the land, toward the north, toward the direction of the town that was ash and the daughters she would never see again, and she would not move. The guards shouted. She did not move. They struck her. She did not move. They dragged her toward the canoe, and she went limp as water, and when they set her down she turned again to face the land. She had made herself into a stone that faced home."}, {"id": "eru-adunola-refused-to-board#s7", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Understand what she was doing, my children, for it was not madness and it was not simple despair. Adunola had weighed the same arithmetic every other captive weighed, and she had reached a different answer. She could not stop the ship. She could not free herself. She knew this. But there was one thing in all the world that still belonged to her, and it was the direction she chose to face, and the word her body spoke. And she chose to make her whole self, in that moment, into a single word, and the word was no. Not the no of a person who thinks she can win, but the deeper no of a person declaring, before the ancestors and the sea and her captors and her God, that this was wrong, that she was a human being and not a keg of powder, and that even if they could carry her body across the water they would carry it as a protest and never as a thing that had agreed."}, {"id": "eru-adunola-refused-to-board#s8", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "What became of her? The stories tell it more than one way, as stories of the taken always do, because the ledgers did not record the fates of women who would not board. Some say she was thrown into a canoe and carried out to the ship and died on the crossing, still facing back the way she had come, and that other captives from that ship carried her memory across the water and told it in the plantations of the far side, so that her refusal became a seed of defiance planted in a distant land. Some say the effort to force her broke into a struggle that spread down the line, that her stillness lit a fire, that others found their own no in the sound of hers, and that in the confusion a few slipped their bonds and vanished into the coastal creeks to freedom. Some say she never boarded at all, that she was left on the sand, spent and beaten, and that a fishing people of the coast took her in, and that she lived out her years by the water, an old woman who would sit at evening facing the interior and singing the low songs of a town that no longer stood."}, {"id": "eru-adunola-refused-to-board#s9", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "We do not know which telling is true. Perhaps all of them are, in the way that the stories of a people are true, carrying not the small truth of one fact but the large truth of one spirit. What we know for certain is this. Somewhere on that coast, in those terrible years, women like Adunola refused. They sat down on the sand. They faced home. They made their bodies into the word no. And whether they died on the ships or lived by the water or were dragged aboard still fighting, they proved a thing that the whole vast machinery of the trade, with all its guns and ledgers and chains, could never disprove, that a human spirit is not a thing that can be bought, only a thing that can be broken, and that some spirits will not even break."}, {"id": "eru-adunola-refused-to-board#s10", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So carry Adunola with you, my children. When you are one against many, when the powerful have already written the ledger and told you that your refusal changes nothing, remember the woman on the sand who could not win and refused anyway. Remember that dignity is not the same as victory, and that there is a kind of no that is larger than any yes the powerful can command. They took her body, perhaps. They never took her direction. To the last, she faced home. That is the story of Adunola. Sleep now, and if ever the world tries to make you agree to your own chains, remember to sit down on the sand and face the way of your fathers."}]}, {"id": "eru-ile-iwofa", "emoji": "⛓️", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#1a1308,#3a2a10)", "category": "itan-eru", "rating": 9.6, "duration": "10 min", "title": {"en": "The Ile Iwofa: Domestic Pawnship and Its Limits", "yo": "Iwofa"}, "desc": {"en": "The Yoruba system of pledging labour against debt, how it differed from slavery, and how the trade corrupted it.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Itan Eru", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Iwofa, consolidates “The Ile Iwofa: Domestic Pawnship and Its Limits”)", "segments": [{"id": "eru-ile-iwofa#s1", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Come and sit close, my children, for tonight I will teach you to tell two things apart that careless people often confuse, and the confusion of them has done great harm. There is bondage, and there is bondage, and they are not the same, and a wise person must learn to see the difference the way a farmer learns to tell the good yam from the one that will poison the pot. Tonight I speak of the iwofa, the pawn, an old institution of our own people, and I speak of it honestly, both its mercy and its danger, because to understand how the Atlantic trade twisted our world you must first understand what our world was before the ships came, and how a custom that once had limits was pushed by greed toward having none."}, {"id": "eru-ile-iwofa#s2", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "In the old Yoruba world, before and beneath the great Atlantic trade, there was a way that a person in need of money could raise it. There were no banks, no lenders’ offices, no papers of credit as the modern world knows them. So when a man needed a loan, to pay a bride price, to bury a father with honour, to survive a bad harvest, to settle a debt or a fine, he would pledge a person as security for the loan. Often he pledged himself. Often he pledged a young relative, a son, a daughter, a nephew. That pledged person went to live in the household of the lender and worked for them, and the labour of the pawn stood in the place of interest on the loan. The person was the iwofa, the pawn, and the arrangement was called the pawning, and it was woven deep into the ordinary life of our towns."}, {"id": "eru-ile-iwofa#s3", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Now hear me carefully, because here is the heart of the matter and the reason I insist you learn the difference. The iwofa was not a slave. Hold that truth firmly. The pawn kept his name and his lineage and his freedom of person. He was not sold. He could not be sold, for he did not belong to the lender, he was only security for a debt. When the loan was repaid, and it could be repaid by the pawn himself or by any of his kin, the iwofa simply went home, and the matter was closed as if it had never been. The pawn’s family knew exactly where he was and could visit him. He worshipped his own orisa. He married, and his children were free. He remained a member of his own compound, only lodged elsewhere for a season. Custom hedged the whole arrangement with rules. The lender who mistreated a pawn, who overworked or abused or dishonoured the young person in his keeping, brought shame on his own head and answered to the pawn’s family and to the elders of the town. A pawned girl was not to be taken advantage of, and a man who did so owed compensation and married her properly or faced disgrace. There were limits, my children. That is the whole point. There were limits, and the limits had teeth."}, {"id": "eru-ile-iwofa#s4", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "So the iwofa system, at its best, was a form of mutual help braided into a society that had no other way to lend. It let a poor family raise money in a crisis without losing anyone forever. It bound households together in webs of obligation. It was not gentle, for to labour in another’s house for a debt not of your making is a hard thing, and to be the child pledged away is a wound that a soft heart feels across all these years. I will not paint it as sweet. But it was bounded, reversible, and answerable to custom, and that made it a different creature altogether from what came across the water."}, {"id": "eru-ile-iwofa#s5", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "Because then the ships came, and the great Atlantic trade reached its long arm into the interior, and the price of a human being on the coast rose and rose until it distorted everything it touched, the way a strong smell spoils a whole pot. And here is the tragedy I must tell you plainly. When human beings became worth so much on the coast, the old bounded custom of pawnship came under a terrible pressure to break its own limits. A dishonest lender, or a lender himself squeezed by debt, might look at the iwofa in his household, the pledged girl or boy who by all custom was to be returned when the debt was paid, and see instead a fortune waiting to be seized. And so it happened, in the worst days, that pawns were seized and sold, that the promise of return was broken, that a person pledged for a temporary debt was spirited to the coast and put on a ship, crossing forever the very line that made pawnship different from slavery. The custom that had teeth found that in the chaos of the wars and the greed of the trade, its teeth could be knocked out. A family that pledged a child in good faith, expecting to redeem him, might come with the money in hand and find that the child was gone, sold, beyond all recovery, swallowed by the ocean."}, {"id": "eru-ile-iwofa#s6", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "This is the lesson, my children, and I want you to hold it in both hands. An institution is only as good as the limits that bind it, and limits hold only as long as a society has the will and the power to enforce them. The iwofa system was not slavery, and we do it no honour and history no service to pretend our ancestors were monsters who trafficked their own. But neither was it innocent, for it created a category of pledged persons who, when law broke down and greed rose up, could be pushed across the line into the very abyss the custom had been built to avoid. The trade did not invent the iwofa. What the trade did was worse in its way. It took a bounded institution of mutual help and dangled before it a temptation so enormous that the bounds began to give way."}, {"id": "eru-ile-iwofa#s7", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Let me give you one small scene to carry, so the teaching lives in a picture and not only in words. Imagine a father, a decent man fallen on a hard year, who pledges his young daughter as iwofa to a lender in a neighbouring town so that he can pay the fine that will keep his family’s name clean. He does this in sorrow but in trust, for this is how it has always been done, and he works through the season to gather the money to bring her home. He knows the household she is in. He has eaten with the lender. All is according to custom. And then the war comes, or the lender’s own creditors close in, or simple greed wins its argument in a weak heart, and when the father arrives at last with his coins wrapped carefully in cloth, ready to redeem his child, he is met at the compound gate with lowered eyes and the words that end his world. She is gone. Sold south. There is nothing to be done. The coins in the cloth are suddenly worth nothing at all, and the father walks home along the same road he had walked in hope, an old man now though he was not old that morning, carrying money that can no longer buy the only thing he came for."}, {"id": "eru-ile-iwofa#s8", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "That is what the trade did to the iwofa. It turned a custom of trust into a doorway through which the beloved could vanish."}, {"id": "eru-ile-iwofa#s9", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "So when you hear people speak carelessly of slavery in old Africa, as though everything was one undifferentiated darkness, I want you to be able to answer them with the truth, which is more painful and more precise than the careless version. Our people had bondage, yes, as all old peoples did, and some of it was the bounded, redeemable, custom-hedged pawnship of the iwofa, which is not the same as the chattel horror of the plantation, where a person and all their descendants became a thing to be owned and sold forever. Learn the difference, and teach it. But learn also the harder half of the truth, that the enormous greed of the Atlantic trade pressed upon our own gentler institutions until some of them cracked, and through those cracks our own people were sometimes lost. Both halves are true. A grown person can hold both without flinching."}, {"id": "eru-ile-iwofa#s10", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "Guard your institutions, my children. Guard their limits most of all. For a limit is a fragile and precious thing, and there will always be a price large enough to tempt someone to break it, and the only defence is a people determined that some lines will not be crossed no matter what is offered. That is the story of the iwofa. Sleep now, and dream of a custom kept honest, and a child redeemed, and a father who walks home with his daughter’s hand in his."}]}, {"id": "eru-popo-sailors", "emoji": "⛓️", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#1a1308,#3a2a10)", "category": "itan-eru", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "8 min", "title": {"en": "The Popo: Yoruba Sailors and the Atlantic Corridors", "yo": "The Popo: Yoruba Sailors and the Atlantic Corridors"}, "desc": {"en": "The surf-canoe men of the Bight of Benin who were the only passage between ship and shore: Africans as actors on the sea, not only its victims.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Itan Eru", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Atukowo, consolidates “The Popo: Yoruba Sailors and the Atlantic Corridors”)", "segments": [{"id": "eru-popo-sailors#s1", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Sit close, my children, and tonight let the fire smell of salt, for I take you to the water’s edge, to the lagoon towns and the surf and the long grey corridors of the Atlantic, and I tell you of the coast people the old records called the Popo, and of the Yoruba sailors and boatmen and canoe-men who lived where the land meets the sea and who knew the water the way a hunter knows the forest. This is a story that corrects a false picture, for when people imagine our ancestors and the ocean, they imagine only captives in the dark hold of a ship, helpless cargo carried where they did not wish to go. And that was true, and terrible, and I have told it. But it was not the whole truth. For there were also Yoruba and their coastal neighbours who were masters of the water, who worked it and lived by it and moved along it with skill, and their story restores something the trade tried to take, the image of the African as an actor upon the sea and not only its victim."}, {"id": "eru-popo-sailors#s2", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "First, who were the Popo? The name is an old one that the Europeans used along the Bight of Benin, and it came to be attached to the coastal peoples between the Yoruba country and the kingdom of Dahomey, the folk of Grand Popo and Little Popo and the lagoon settlements that laced that whole shoreline. They were a mingling people, Yoruba and Aja and Ewe and Gun, living in the borderland where our nation shades into our neighbours, and among them and around them the Yoruba presence was strong, in blood, in language, in the orisa they carried to the water. What bound the coast people together was not one pure lineage but a shared mastery of a difficult and dangerous element, the sea and the lagoons behind it."}, {"id": "eru-popo-sailors#s3", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "And do not imagine that mastery lightly, my children, for the coast of the Bight of Benin is one of the hardest shores in all the world to work. There are no natural harbours along much of it, only a long straight beach where the surf comes in heavy and treacherous, great rollers that can swallow a boat and its crew in a heartbeat. Behind the beach lie the lagoons, a vast network of sheltered waterways running for hundreds of miles parallel to the sea, connected here and there to the ocean by shifting, dangerous mouths. To live and trade on this coast, a people had to master two waters at once, the calm inner highway of the lagoon and the killing surf of the open sea."}, {"id": "eru-popo-sailors#s4", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "And so the coast people became some of the finest boatmen the Atlantic world had ever seen, and this is no small boast, my children, it is sober history. The great canoes of the Bight, hewn from single vast trees, crewed by teams of paddlers who knew the surf as intimately as their own heartbeats, were the only reliable way to cross the killing line where the rollers broke. When a European ship anchored off that harbourless coast, it could not simply pull up to a dock, for there was no dock. Everything and everyone that went between the ship and the shore, every barrel of goods, every keg of powder, every trader, and yes, in the dark years, every captive, had to be carried through the surf in the canoes of the coast men. The whole commerce of that shore, for good and for terrible ill, ran through their hands and their paddles. The Europeans depended on them utterly, and knew it, and paid for it, and the surf-canoe men of the Bight were famous from Liverpool to Bahia as the indispensable masters of a passage no outsider could safely make."}, {"id": "eru-popo-sailors#s5", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "Think on what that meant. On that coast, the African was not the helpless one. The proud European captain in his great ship, master of the ocean’s width, became a helpless passenger the moment he needed to reach the land, dependent entirely on the skill of a Popo canoe crew to carry him alive through the rollers.  The coast man read the sea like a sacred text, knew the seventh wave and the lull behind it, knew when to hold and when to drive the canoe forward on the shoulder of a roller, knew the sea’s moods as a priest knows his orisa. And he carried his gods with him onto the water. Olokun, lord of the deep sea, and Yemoja of the waters, were honoured on that coast with offerings before a dangerous crossing, for no man drove a canoe into that surf without first making his peace with the powers of the deep."}, {"id": "eru-popo-sailors#s6", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Now I must tell you the hard braid in this story, for it would be a false tale that showed you only the glory. The very skill that made the coast men magnificent also made them essential to the trade, and some of them grew rich carrying its terrible cargo through the surf. The canoe that ferried a free trader out to a ship also, on another day, ferried a captive out to that same ship, and the coast man’s paddle served the darkness as faithfully as it served honest commerce. I will not hide this from you. The mastery of the water was a great and genuine glory, and it was also, in those years, bound into the machinery of the trade, and both things are true at once. The coast people were not innocent bystanders any more than the Ijesa dealers were. They were actors, skilled and proud and sometimes complicit, full human beings caught in a system larger than any of them."}, {"id": "eru-popo-sailors#s7", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "But hold onto the part the trade wanted you to forget. Long before the ships came, and long after they were gone, the coast people worked that water for their own life and their own trade. They fished it, they carried salt and cloth and kola along the lagoon highway, they moved between the towns of a whole shoreline, they built a civilisation amphibious and skilled and utterly their own. The lagoon was their road, the canoe their horse, the surf their frontier. When the trade ended and the slavers’ ships stopped coming, the coast men were still there, still paddling, still reading the seventh wave, still carrying the ordinary goods of ordinary life through the rollers, because the sea was their country before the trade and remained their country after it."}, {"id": "eru-popo-sailors#s8", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So when you picture our ancestors and the ocean, do not picture only the hold of the slave ship, my children. Picture also the surf-canoe cresting a grey roller at dawn, the paddlers singing in time, the steersman reading the water like scripture, Olokun honoured with an offering in the bow. Picture the African as master of a passage no European could make alone. The trade tried to write our people into the story of the sea as cargo only. But the coast people wrote themselves into it as sailors, and that truth rode the waves long before the first slaver dropped anchor, and it rides them still. That is the story of the Popo and the coast men. Sleep now, and if you dream of the sea, dream of the canoe cresting the wave, and the free hands on the paddles, and the song that timed the crossing."}]}, {"id": "eru-memory-keepers", "emoji": "⛓️", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#1a1308,#3a2a10)", "category": "itan-eru", "rating": 9.6, "duration": "9 min", "title": {"en": "The Memory Keepers: Oral History and the Trade", "yo": "The Memory Keepers: Oral History and the Trade"}, "desc": {"en": "How raids targeted the keepers of oral memory, and why memory proved harder to kill than the slavers understood.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Itan Eru", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Arokin, consolidates “The Memory Keepers: Oral History and the Trade”)", "segments": [{"id": "eru-memory-keepers#s1", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Come very close tonight, my children, closer than usual, for this story is about the fire itself, and about the voice that speaks across it, and about you who listen. This is the story of the memory keepers, the arokin and the babalawo and the drummers and the grandmothers, the men and women whose whole sacred task was to remember, and to carry what they remembered forward, so that a people who could not always write could nonetheless never truly forget. And it is the story of how, when the trade came and tried to erase our people, these keepers of memory became a kind of army, an army with no weapon but the trained and faithful mouth, and how that army won a victory the slavers never imagined possible, the survival of the truth itself."}, {"id": "eru-memory-keepers#s2", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "First you must understand what the arokin were, for they were no ordinary tellers of tales. In the court of the Alaafin of Oyo, and in the courts of the great kings, there were official rememberers, the arokin, whose duty passed from father to son, whose whole life was the memorising of the royal genealogies, the deeds of the kings, the praise-poems called oriki, the histories of the wars and the migrations and the founding of the towns. They were living libraries, and the discipline of their art was ferocious. A boy trained as arokin might spend years learning to recite without a single error the line of kings stretching back through centuries, each with his oriki, his praises, his memorable deeds. The word was sacred and the accuracy was sacred, for to mangle the genealogy of kings was to insult the ancestors themselves. These were not idle entertainers. They were the memory of the nation, walking on two legs."}, {"id": "eru-memory-keepers#s3", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "And they did not work alone. Beside them stood the babalawo, the fathers of secrets, the priests of Ifa, who carried in their trained minds the vast corpus of the odu, the two hundred and fifty-six chapters of the Ifa oracle, each with its many verses, its stories, its proverbs, its accumulated wisdom of centuries. To become a babalawo was to submit to a discipline of memory as demanding as any university on earth, years upon years of learning the verses by heart, for Ifa was never written, it lived only in the trained mouths of its priests. And beside the babalawo stood the drummers, for the talking drum of our people is not mere rhythm, it is speech, it speaks the tonal language of Yoruba, it recites praises and proverbs and names, and a master drummer carried in his hands a library of the spoken word. And beside all of these, humblest and mightiest of all, stood the grandmothers at the evening fire, telling the folktales, the itan, the histories of the family, teaching the children who they were. Between them, these keepers held the entire memory of a civilisation without a single page of writing, and held it so well that it survived across centuries."}, {"id": "eru-memory-keepers#s4", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "Now here is where the trade enters, and here is the terror of it, and here is the triumph. The Atlantic trade was not only a theft of bodies. It was an attempted theft of memory. When a person was taken and sold across the water, the slavers tried to strip away everything that made them who they were, their name, their language, their history, their gods, their belonging. The plantation was a machine for producing forgetting, for turning a person with ancestors and a lineage and a place into a nameless unit of labour with no past. And the trade also tore at memory here at home, for when a town was destroyed and its people scattered, its arokin might be killed or sold, its babalawo lost, its drummers silenced, and with them could vanish the accumulated memory of generations, gone in the smoke of a single dawn raid."}, {"id": "eru-memory-keepers#s5", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "And yet, my children, hear the triumph, for it is greater than the terror. The memory keepers were harder to kill than the slavers understood. Memory, once it lives in trained minds and is passed from mouth to mouth, is a stubborn and slippery thing. You may burn a library of books, but you cannot burn a library that lives inside a hundred people and is spoken every evening. When one arokin fell, another had already learned the genealogies. When one babalawo was lost, the odu lived on in a dozen others. When a town was scattered, its people carried its itan in their heads to wherever they were driven, and told them by new fires in new places. And across the water, in the very belly of the plantation, the captives kept telling. They kept the folktales of Ijapa the tortoise. They kept the praises of the orisa. They kept, in Cuba and Brazil and beyond, the sacred verses in a Yoruba that would not die. The plantation tried to produce forgetting, and the memory keepers, without ink, without paper, without any weapon but the disciplined and faithful mouth, produced remembering instead, and won."}, {"id": "eru-memory-keepers#s6", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Think of what this means for how we know the trade at all. Much of what the world records about those years comes from ledgers and letters written by the traders themselves, the buyers and sellers, who saw our people as cargo and wrote of them as numbers. But there is another record, older and truer in its way, carried not in ink but in voice. It is the record the memory keepers kept, the oriki that still names a lineage’s origin, the itan that still tells how a family came to its town, the song a Cuban priestess still sings in words from Osogbo, the proverb a grandmother still speaks that came down unbroken from before the ships. When we want to know not the price of our ancestors but the truth of them, not what they cost but who they were, it is to the memory keepers we must turn, for they alone recorded our people as people."}, {"id": "eru-memory-keepers#s7", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "And now, my children, I must tell you the last and most important thing, and it is about you. The chain of memory does not end with the old arokin in the vanished court of Oyo. It runs through every fire like this one, through every grandmother’s tale, through every elder who takes the time to tell a child who they are. It runs, tonight, through me, and it is about to run through you. For a memory keeper is not only a person with a special title. A memory keeper is anyone who receives the story faithfully and passes it on. When you carry these tales forward, when you tell your own children of Adunola who would not board, of Osifekunde who taught France what Yorubaland was, of the Lucumi who kept the gods alive, of the coast men who mastered the surf, then you become what the arokin were, a keeper of the memory of your people, a link in the chain the trade could not break."}, {"id": "eru-memory-keepers#s8", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "That is why I tell you these stories, and that is why you must listen well, and that is why, one day, you must tell them yourself. The slavers are long dead. Their ledgers are dust. But the memory keepers won, and their victory lives as long as the stories are told, which means it lives as long as you choose to keep telling them. Sleep now, keeper of the flame. Tomorrow, or in twenty years, or when you hold your own child by your own fire, you will remember that you were told these things, and you will pass them on, and in that moment the arokin of old Oyo will speak again through your mouth, and the memory will not die."}]}, {"id": "eru-night-of-the-chains", "emoji": "⛓️", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#1a1308,#3a2a10)", "category": "itan-eru", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "8 min", "title": {"en": "The Night of the Chains: A Crossing Remembered", "yo": "The Night of the Chains: A Crossing Remembered"}, "desc": {"en": "The Middle Passage: the hold, the despair, the ones who chose the water as the road home, and the ones who rose against the ship.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Itan Eru", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Oru, consolidates “The Night of the Chains” and “What the Hold Remembered”)", "segments": [{"id": "eru-night-of-the-chains#s1", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Tonight the fire burns low, and I will tell you the hardest story, the one my own grandmother told me only once, and only because she said a people who will not look at their deepest wound will never let it heal. It is the story of the crossing itself, the middle passage, the weeks in the belly of the ship. I will not dress it in beauty, for there was none. But I will tell it as a story of people, not of cargo, because that is the one dignity we can still give them, to remember that each was a person with a name."}, {"id": "eru-night-of-the-chains#s2", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Begin on the shore, at the barracoons, those pens of stakes and mud where the captured were held until a ship came. A young man, let us call him Ayinde, for he surely had a name like that, waited there through the rainy weeks, chained at the ankle to strangers, watching the grey water, not yet understanding that the water was the road they meant to take him on. He had been a farmer. He had a wife in a town three days inland who did not know if he was alive. He had prayed to Ogun and to his own ori, his inner head, his destiny, and no answer had come that he could hear."}, {"id": "eru-night-of-the-chains#s3", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "When the ship came, they were rowed out to it in canoes through the surf, and Ayinde saw the great wooden thing rising from the water with its tall masts and its white men, and some of the captives fought even then, throwing themselves into the sea rather than be taken aboard, choosing the water and the sharks over the unknown. Those who did not fight, or could not, were passed up the side and down into the hold, and there Ayinde met the darkness that would be his world for many weeks."}, {"id": "eru-night-of-the-chains#s4", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "I will tell you only what you must know to understand, and no more. They were laid down in rows in a space too low to sit up in. They were chained wrist to wrist and ankle to ankle to the person beside them, so that no one could move without moving another. The air was thick and foul. In the heat, sickness spread. The sea threw the ship and threw the bodies against the timbers. And through all of it, in that groaning darkness, human beings did what human beings do. They wept. They prayed. And, my child, they sang."}, {"id": "eru-night-of-the-chains#s5", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "That is the part my grandmother wanted me to carry to you. Even there, they sang. In the hold of the ship the captives began to sing the songs of home, in Yoruba and in the other tongues of the coast, call and answer moving down the rows, one voice lifting a line and many voices catching it. They sang to keep from drowning in despair. They sang so that the person chained beside them, a stranger of another town, would know they were not alone. They sang the names of the orisa into the darkness. And the sailors above, hearing that sound rise from below the deck, sometimes shivered and did not know why."}, {"id": "eru-night-of-the-chains#s6", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "There were those who resolved not to arrive at all. Some refused to eat, turning their faces from the food, choosing to go home to the ancestors rather than to a life of bondage on the far shore, and the crew forced iron tools between their teeth to make them swallow, so determined were they to deliver living cargo. There was among many of the captives a belief that gave them a strange courage: the belief that in death the spirit would fly back across the water, back to the land of the fathers, and be free. So the water that carried them away was also, in their faith, the road home. Some chose that road. Who am I, who has never worn a chain, to say they chose wrongly."}, {"id": "eru-night-of-the-chains#s7", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "And there were those who chose to fight. On many a ship the captives rose, having loosened a chain or seized a moment when the hatch was open, and fell upon the crew with whatever they had, their bare hands and their fury. Most such risings failed and were punished terribly. A few succeeded, and the ship was turned back toward the coast, and some souls walked free again onto African sand who had thought never to see it. The slavers wrote of these risings in their logs as accidents and losses. We should remember them as what they were, the refusal of the human spirit to accept that it had become property, spoken in the only language left, the language of the fist."}, {"id": "eru-night-of-the-chains#s8", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "But most did not die and did not rise. Most simply endured, day after day in the dark, holding on, and this too is a kind of heroism, perhaps the hardest kind, the heroism of the ones who kept breathing when breathing was agony, who lived so that some thread of their people might reach the far shore and take root and one day, generations later, remember. Every Yoruba face in Cuba, in Brazil, in Trinidad, in all the Americas, is descended from someone who endured the hold and lived. They are the proof that the ones in the dark did not endure in vain."}, {"id": "eru-night-of-the-chains#s9", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "When at last the ship reached the far shore, Ayinde was brought up into a light so fierce after the weeks of darkness that it blinded him, and he was washed and oiled to look healthy, and led to a place where strangers looked into his mouth and felt his limbs and named a price. He did not know the words they spoke. He did not know the name of the land. He knew only that he was alive, that the sea had not taken him, and that whatever waited was to be borne, because to bear it was the last freedom left to him, the freedom to remain, inside, a man."}, {"id": "eru-night-of-the-chains#s10", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "And he did remain a man. On the far shore he learned new words but kept the old prayers. He married, perhaps, and told his children in whispers the names of the orisa and the name of the town three days inland where a wife had once waited. He planted a seed of memory. And that seed grew, through generations of bondage, until it flowered at last in free people who danced for Sango and sang in the old tongue and named their children in the old way, and who did not fully know, but felt, that they were the harvest of a man who had once sung in the dark hold of a ship and refused to let go of who he was."}, {"id": "eru-night-of-the-chains#s11", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So that is the night of the chains, and I have told it to you plainly. Do not carry away from it only the horror, though you must never forget the horror. Carry away also the singing. Remember that in the worst place human beings have ever made, our people sang. Remember that they believed the water was also a road home. Remember that they endured so that you could sit here, free, by this fire, and hear their story. You are their answered prayer. Live in a way that would make the ones in the hold glad they held on."}]}, {"id": "eru-ouidah-route", "emoji": "⛓️", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#1a1308,#3a2a10)", "category": "itan-eru", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "7 min", "title": {"en": "The Ouidah Route: The Road to the Water", "yo": "Ouidah"}, "desc": {"en": "The short, terrible road to the sea at Ouidah, past the tree of forgetting to the gate of no return.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Itan Eru", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Ona, consolidates “The Ouidah Route” and “The Gate of No Return”)", "segments": [{"id": "eru-ouidah-route#s1", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "There was a road, my child, that ran down to the sea, and it was the most terrible road in the world, not because of its length, for it was short, but because of what walked down it and did not walk back. It ended at a town on the coast that the traders called Whydah and the people called Ouidah, west of Yorubaland in the country of the Fon, and through its gate passed hundreds of thousands of souls, our people among them, out of Africa and into the ships and gone. Tonight I will walk that road with you in the telling, so that you understand how the trade actually reached the water, mile by mile."}, {"id": "eru-ouidah-route#s2", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Ouidah was not always a gate of sorrow. It began as an ordinary coastal town, a place of fishermen and salt and small trade, where a lagoon ran behind a strip of beach and the surf pounded on a shore with no true harbor, so that ships had to stand off in the open water and everything, goods and people, had to be carried out through the breakers in canoes. It had its own gods, its own market, its own quiet life. But it sat where the great currents of the trade converged, and that location, which might have made it merely prosperous, made it instead one of the busiest doors of the entire traffic in human beings."}, {"id": "eru-ouidah-route#s3", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "When the kingdom of Dahomey rose to power inland, its kings looked south to Ouidah and seized it, and they made it the mouth through which their kingdom breathed out captives and breathed in guns and cowrie shells and cloth and rum. Dahomey became a state organized to a frightening degree around war and capture. Each dry season its armies marched out, and among them marched the regiments of women soldiers, and they fell upon towns to the north and east and brought back long files of the captured, roped neck to neck, to be sold at the coast. Some of those captives were Yoruba, taken in the endless border wars, marched west into a strange country and down the terrible road to Ouidah."}, {"id": "eru-ouidah-route#s4", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Walk that last road with me. From the town of Ouidah to the beach was a distance a person could cover in a couple of hours, but it was measured not in time but in loss. The captives were led out of the barracoons where they had been held, and they walked the sandy track toward the sound of the surf. Along that road, the traders had built stations of a kind of anti pilgrimage. At one place, they say, the captives were made to circle a certain tree many times, a tree the slavers called the tree of forgetting, in a dark ritual meant to make them forget who they were, their names, their homes, their spirits, so that they would not rise up and would not, in death, find their way back to trouble the living. Think of the cruelty of that idea, to try to steal not only the body but the memory, to try to erase the very self."}, {"id": "eru-ouidah-route#s5", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "But here is what I want you to hold onto. It did not work. Whatever they made the captives do at that tree, the memory did not die. The proof is in every Yoruba drum that still sounds in Haiti and Cuba and Brazil, in every orisa still worshipped across the water, in the fact that you, tonight, still know the names they tried to make our ancestors forget. They circled the tree of forgetting, and they remembered anyway. There is no ritual strong enough to erase a people who refuse to be erased."}, {"id": "eru-ouidah-route#s6", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Farther down the road stood another marker, and this one the slavers called the gate of no return, near the edge of the sand where the ships waited beyond the surf. It was the last place on the soil of Africa that the captives stood before they were loaded into the canoes and taken through the breakers to the ships. Imagine standing there. Behind you, the whole continent, your language, your dead, your gods, everyone who ever knew your face. Before you, the pounding surf, the open water, the tall ships, and beyond them nothing you could name. The gate of no return. They passed through it and the land closed behind them like water closing over a stone."}, {"id": "eru-ouidah-route#s7", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "And yet, my child, even that name has been answered. In our own age, the descendants of the ones who passed through that gate have come back. They have crossed the water in the other direction, from Brazil and Cuba and the islands and America, and walked back up that road from the beach toward the town, retracing in reverse the steps of their stolen ancestors, standing at the gate of no return and passing through it the wrong way on purpose, back onto African soil, to say we returned, you did not erase us, the gate had a far side after all. The road that was built to carry our people out has become a road our people walk back in, carrying flowers and tears and drums. That is a victory the slavers never imagined and could not have prevented."}, {"id": "eru-ouidah-route#s8", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "Ouidah today keeps these memories. The road is marked. The gate stands. People come from across the world to walk it and to weep and to remember. What was meant to be a place of pure forgetting has become one of the great places of remembering in all the earth, a shrine to the millions who passed that way, our people among them, and did not come back, and yet, through their children, came back after all."}, {"id": "eru-ouidah-route#s9", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So remember the road to the water, and remember its two directions. One way it carried our people into the dark, past the tree of forgetting and through the gate of no return, and that is a wound we must never pretend away. But the other way, in our own time, it carries our people home, and that is a healing the slavers could never have foreseen. A road has two ends, my child. They thought they had built a road that ran only outward. They were wrong. Nothing that belongs to a living people is ever truly one way. The ones who walked out have walked back in, in the bodies of their grandchildren, and the gate of no return has learned, at last, to open both ways."}]}, {"id": "eru-sold-by-a-king", "emoji": "⛓️", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#1a1308,#3a2a10)", "category": "itan-eru", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "9 min", "title": {"en": "Sold by a King: The Complicity Within", "yo": "Sold by a King: The Complicity Within"}, "desc": {"en": "The harder half of the story: the Yoruba rulers and merchants who sold captives, told without excuse or false equivalence.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Itan Eru", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Otito, consolidates “Sold by a King: The Complicity of Yoruba Chiefs”)", "segments": [{"id": "eru-sold-by-a-king#s1", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Tonight I must tell you a harder truth than any I have told before, and I ask you to sit still and hear it to the end, because a people who will only tell the flattering half of their own story are children, and you are old enough now for the whole of it. So far I have told you of our people as the prey, the stolen, the ones carried off. That is true, and it is most of the truth. But it is not all of it. For the ugly fact, the fact that shames us and that we must nonetheless face, is that many of the captives who went down to the ships were sold there by other Africans, and some of them by Yoruba hands, by chiefs and kings and merchants of our own."}, {"id": "eru-sold-by-a-king#s2", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Do not turn your face away. A people that can only speak of what was done to them, and never of what some among them did, has not yet grown up. The Europeans built the ships and the demand, and without that demand none of it would have happened, and the greatest weight of the guilt is theirs. But the Europeans, in most places and most years, did not march inland and catch the captives themselves. They waited on the coast, in their forts and their ships, and they bought. And the ones who sold, who caught, who marched the captives down to the water, were very often Africans, and among them, in the wars of our own land, were Yoruba."}, {"id": "eru-sold-by-a-king#s3", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "But now, having told you the hard fact plainly, I must do the harder work, which is to tell it truly, for a truth told without understanding is only another kind of lie. So let me explain how this came to be, not to excuse it, for some of it cannot be excused, but so that you understand it as it really was, and do not carry away either a false innocence or a false, flat shame."}, {"id": "eru-sold-by-a-king#s4", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "First, know that in the old world of our ancestors, before ever a European ship came, there already existed forms of bondage, as there did in nearly every society on the earth in those centuries. A person captured in war might be held and made to labor. A person could fall into pawnship for a debt. But this old bondage, the iwofa and the war captive, was a different thing from the plantation slavery across the water. The bondsman kept his humanity, could marry, could sometimes rise, could be absorbed into the family or the town over the generations. It was not the bottomless, hereditary, race based horror that the Atlantic trade became. When the European demand arrived, it took these older, more limited kinds of bondage and it inflamed them, it distorted them, it poured guns and rum and endless money into them until it had built something far more terrible than anything that came before."}, {"id": "eru-sold-by-a-king#s5", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "And that is the heart of it. The European demand was like a fire dropped into a dry season. The wars that our warlords fought, that you have heard of in the great tellings of Afonja and the fall of Oyo and the rise of Ibadan, those wars had their own African causes, ambition and vengeance and the collapse of the old empire. But once the ships were waiting on the coast paying good guns and goods for every captive, war itself changed its nature. A chief who defeated a rival now had a new and terrible option. Instead of absorbing the captured or ransoming them, he could march them to the coast and sell them for muskets, and with those muskets make more war, and take more captives, and buy more muskets. The trade made a wheel of fire, and once it was turning, a chief who refused to feed it might be crushed by a rival who did not refuse. It became, for many, a matter of sell or be sold."}, {"id": "eru-sold-by-a-king#s6", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "And yet, my child, do not let me talk you into thinking it was all mere helplessness, all wheel of fire with no one to blame. That would be its own lie. There were kings and merchants who grew rich on this trade and loved their wealth more than their conscience. There were those who raided not from any pressure of war but from simple greed, who sold the weak and the debtor and the stranger, who looked into the faces of people who could have been their neighbors and saw only cowrie shells. There were rulers who could have turned from it and did not. The pressure of the trade was real, but so was choice, and some chose badly, and we do not honor the truth by pretending otherwise. To sell a human being is a wrong, and a wrong does not stop being a wrong because the times made it easy."}, {"id": "eru-sold-by-a-king#s7", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "But here, too, is a part of the truth you must not miss, for it is the part that gives back hope. There were also those who refused. There were towns that would not trade in people. There were rulers who forbade the sale of their own subjects, who fought the raiders, who sheltered the fleeing. Remember Abeokuta, whose whole existence was a refusal, a gathering of the hunted behind a rock to defy the slavers. Remember that when the freed men came home from across the water, some of the loudest voices against the trade, some of the fiercest workers to end it, were Yoruba, were African, were the very people the trade had wounded. The conscience that condemned the trade was not only a European conscience. It burned in African hearts too, in the hearts of the very people best placed to know its evil."}, {"id": "eru-sold-by-a-king#s8", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "So how are we to hold all of this, my child, without breaking? I will tell you how the elders hold it. We hold it the way a family holds the memory of a quarrel in which some of our own behaved shamefully. We do not deny it, for denial is the coward’s road. We do not drown in it either, for the wrong of some of our ancestors does not make slaves of our spirit forever. We name it. We mourn it. We learn from it. And we let it make us wiser and gentler and more watchful, so that never again will our own people mistake gold for a good reason to sell a human soul."}, {"id": "eru-sold-by-a-king#s9", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "And we remember the true shape of the guilt, which is shared and unequal. The Europeans who built the demand and the ships and the plantations, who made the whole vast machine and grew rich for centuries upon it, carry the greatest weight. The African sellers who fed the machine carry a real weight too, neither to be erased nor to be made equal to the other. And crushed between them, the millions of the sold, our own blood, who carried no guilt at all, only the wound. When we tell this story, it is above all to them that we owe the truth, for they were betrayed twice, once by the foreign buyer and once, too often, by a hand that spoke their own language."}, {"id": "eru-sold-by-a-king#s10", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "That is why I have told you the hard thing tonight. Not to shame you, for you did not do it, and not to shame the ancestors past all measure, for the times were a furnace few of us can imagine. I have told you so that you will carry the whole truth and not a comfortable half of it. A people strong enough to say we were wronged, and also some among us did wrong, is a people that has grown up, that can be trusted with its own history, that will not repeat it. Carry both halves, my child. Carry the wound and carry the reckoning. That is what it means to be an honest child of an honest people."}]}, {"id": "eru-william-craft", "emoji": "⛓️", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#1a1308,#3a2a10)", "category": "itan-eru", "rating": 9.6, "duration": "8 min", "title": {"en": "William Craft and the Yoruba Thread: An Escape in Disguise", "yo": "William Craft and the Yoruba Thread: An Escape in Disguise"}, "desc": {"en": "William and Ellen Craft's daring 1848 escape from Georgia bondage, traced back along a Yoruba thread.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Itan Eru", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Igbala, consolidates “William Craft and the Yoruba Connection”)", "segments": [{"id": "eru-william-craft#s1", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Now I will tell you a story that begins in chains in a far country and ends in freedom won by wit, and though it happened long after the ships stopped coming and on soil far from any Yoruba town, it is our story too, because the man in it carried our blood in his veins and our stubbornness in his heart. His name was William Craft, and with his wife Ellen he pulled off one of the boldest escapes from bondage that the world has ever heard told."}, {"id": "eru-william-craft#s2", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Understand where they were. This was in the American state of Georgia, in the years before the great war that would end slavery there. By then the trade across the ocean was outlawed, and the people held in bondage were mostly born into it on that soil, the children and grandchildren of the stolen. But the blood remembered. In the veins of many an enslaved family in the Americas ran the inheritance of Yorubaland, of Kongo, of Igbo country, of the Akan, mixed and scattered but not erased. And in the bearing of certain families there survived, people said, the particular pride of particular nations, the Yoruba refusal to be counted as nothing, the memory that their ancestors had been free people in cities with kings."}, {"id": "eru-william-craft#s3", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "William and Ellen Craft were husband and wife, held by different masters, living under the daily fear that any day one of them might be sold away and the marriage broken forever, as so many marriages in bondage were broken. Ellen was so light of skin, being the daughter of her own enslaver, that she could be mistaken by strangers for a white woman. William was a skilled carpenter, quick and observant, a man who watched the world and understood how it worked."}, {"id": "eru-william-craft#s4", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "And out of Ellen’s appearance and William’s cunning grew a plan so audacious it takes the breath away. They would not run by night through the swamps as most did, hunted by dogs. They would run in broad daylight, in plain sight, at the front of the train, and no one would stop them, because Ellen would travel as a young white gentleman, an invalid planter going north for medical treatment, and William would travel as that gentleman’s devoted enslaved manservant."}, {"id": "eru-william-craft#s5", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Consider the courage of it. Ellen cut her hair. She dressed in a fine coat and trousers. Because she could not write, and might be asked to sign hotel registers, they put her right arm in a sling, so that she could beg off with an injured hand. Because her face was smooth and young, they wrapped it partly in a poultice as though she suffered from toothache, hiding her features. She wore green spectacles over her eyes. And so disguised, a woman playing a man, a Black woman playing a white master, she walked out of bondage with her own husband walking behind her as her servant, the two of them acting a play in which a single wrong word meant capture, whipping, and worse."}, {"id": "eru-william-craft#s6", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "For four days they traveled north, by train and by steamer, through the very heart of the slaveholding country, sitting among planters and traders and officers who would have seized them in an instant had they known. Ellen, the pretended gentleman, had to converse with strangers, accept their courtesies, keep her voice and her manner exactly right, all while terrified. Once a man who knew Ellen’s true master sat down directly beside her, and she stared out the window and feigned deafness, her heart surely pounding like a war drum, until he gave up trying to talk to her. William, playing the servant, had to stand aside, hold his tongue, watch his wife carry the whole weight of the deception, and be ready to think fast if she faltered."}, {"id": "eru-william-craft#s7", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "At one stop an official grew suspicious and refused to let them board the boat, demanding proof that William truly belonged to the sickly young gentleman. It could have ended there. But another traveler vouched for the invalid planter, and they were waved through, freedom hanging by a thread of luck and nerve. And on Christmas Day, after four days of playing for their lives, William and Ellen Craft stepped off in the free city of Philadelphia. They had walked out of slavery through the front door, in costume, and no one had stopped them."}, {"id": "eru-william-craft#s8", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Think of what that took. Not the strength of arms, though they had that, but the strength of mind, the discipline to hold a terrifying performance together for four unbroken days, the refusal to accept that the papers of ownership had any right to decide what they were. That is a kind of courage the slavers never understood, because it did not look like rebellion. It looked like a quiet young gentleman and his quiet servant. It was rebellion all the same, the deepest kind, the kind that says I will use your own blindness as my road out."}, {"id": "eru-william-craft#s9", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "But freedom in Philadelphia was not the end, and this is where the story reaches back toward home. When a harsh new law was passed that allowed slave catchers to hunt escaped people even in the free northern states and drag them back south, the Crafts were no longer safe on American soil. Slave hunters came north with warrants for their capture. So William and Ellen fled again, this time across the ocean to England, where no man could own another, and there they lived free, raised their children free, and William wrote down their story so that the world would know how they did it."}, {"id": "eru-william-craft#s10", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "And in England, William Craft became more than a man who had escaped. He became a voice. He spoke before crowds who had never seen the face of one who had freed himself. He testified to the reality of the trade and the bondage. And in time his eyes turned back across the water toward Africa, toward the very continent from which his people had been carried, and William Craft actually traveled to the kingdom of Dahomey, into the lands that bordered Yorubaland, moving among the peoples of the West African coast, connecting in the flesh with the homeland that slavery had stolen from his ancestors."}, {"id": "eru-william-craft#s11", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "That is the Yoruba thread in this story, and it is a true one. Here was a man descended from the peoples of that coast, carried in blood down through American bondage, who freed himself by his wits, and who then closed the circle by returning across the ocean toward the land his forebears had been taken from. The trade had tried to make his line into property, a thing bought and sold with no country and no past. And his whole life was a refusal of that lie. He proved he had a mind no chain could hold, and then he went looking for the home that the ledgers had tried to erase."}, {"id": "eru-william-craft#s12", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So when your own road seems walled on every side, remember the young gentleman with the bandaged face and the arm in a sling, staring out of a train window, deaf to the danger sitting beside her, riding straight through the country of her captors and out the far side into freedom. Remember that the mind is a door the slaver never learned to lock. And remember that the blood of our people ran in that carriage, patient and proud and unbroken, all the way to England and all the way back toward the shores of home. They could carry our bodies across the water. They could not carry away what we are."}]}, {"id": "eru-thomas-peters", "emoji": "⛓️", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#1a1308,#3a2a10)", "category": "itan-eru", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "8 min", "title": {"en": "Thomas Peters: From Bondage to a Black Promised Land", "yo": "Thomas Peters: From Bondage to a Black Promised Land"}, "desc": {"en": "A man stolen from this coast who fought and petitioned his way from American bondage through Nova Scotia to the founding of Freetown.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Itan Eru", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Arinako, consolidates “Thomas Peters and the Yoruba Thread in Nova Scotia”)", "segments": [{"id": "eru-thomas-peters#s1", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Let me tell you of a man who was stolen from this coast as a young man, carried across the whole world in chains, and who then spent the rest of his long life walking, fighting, and sailing his way toward one thing: a piece of ground where a Black man could stand free and call it his own. His name that we know was Thomas Peters, though that was not the name his mother gave him, and his people were of this region, the Yoruba country and its neighbors, from which the ships took him. His life is a map of the whole diaspora drawn by a single pair of feet."}, {"id": "eru-thomas-peters#s2", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "He was born free in Africa, around the middle of the seventeenth hundreds, in the lands near the coast where the trade reached inland. As a young man he was seized, and he went through everything you now know, the barracoon, the road to the water, the hold of the ship, the crossing. He was sold in the American colonies, in the country that would become the United States, and there he labored in bondage, a man who remembered being free, which is a particular kind of torment, for he knew exactly what had been taken."}, {"id": "eru-thomas-peters#s3", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "He did not accept it. He ran, and was recaptured, and was punished, and ran again. This was a man the chains could not settle. And then came the great war in the American colonies, the war of the revolution, when the colonists rose against the king of England. The British, needing soldiers and wishing to weaken the rebels, made a promise: any enslaved man who left a rebel master and came to fight for the crown would be given his freedom. Thomas Peters heard that promise, and he took it. He walked away from bondage and into the ranks of the Black soldiers who fought under the British flag, the men they called the Black Pioneers, and he rose among them to lead."}, {"id": "eru-thomas-peters#s4", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "He fought. He bled. He earned his freedom with a musket in his hand. And when the war ended and the British lost, the crown had thousands of freed Black men and women on its hands, people who had been promised liberty and could not be left behind to be dragged back into slavery by the victorious rebels. So they were loaded once more onto ships, these formerly enslaved people, and carried north to the cold coast of Nova Scotia, in what is now Canada, to begin new free lives on land they had been promised."}, {"id": "eru-thomas-peters#s5", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "But the promise, my child, was only half kept, as promises to Black people so often were in that age. They reached Nova Scotia free, yes, but the good land they had been promised was slow to come or never came. They were given the worst plots or none at all. They labored in poverty and cold while white settlers prospered. Thomas Peters, now an old soldier, looked at this half freedom, this liberty without land, and he did what he had done all his life. He refused to accept it."}, {"id": "eru-thomas-peters#s6", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "So this man, who had been stolen from Africa, sold in America, freed by war, and cheated in Canada, did something extraordinary. He got himself onto a ship and crossed the ocean yet again, this time east, all the way to London, to stand before the powerful and demand that the promise be kept. Think of the nerve of it. A man born in an African village, once a piece of property with a price on his head, walked into the capital of the empire and spoke for thousands of his people, insisting they be given the land and dignity they had been pledged. He carried their petition. He would not be turned away."}, {"id": "eru-thomas-peters#s7", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "And out of his demand, and the work of others, came a plan that closed the great circle of his life. There was a project to carry free Black people from Nova Scotia back across the ocean to a new colony being founded on the coast of West Africa, at a place called Freetown, in Sierra Leone, a settlement meant to be a home for the freed. Thomas Peters helped lead that migration. He sailed with them. And so this man who had been carried out of Africa in chains as a young captive sailed back to Africa a free man and a leader, landing on the same continent from which he had been stolen decades before, to help build a town whose very name was Freedom."}, {"id": "eru-thomas-peters#s8", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "He did not live long after reaching Sierra Leone. The years and the journeys had spent him, and there were bitter quarrels in the young colony, for the authorities there did not love a Black man who insisted on leading. But he died on African soil, free, having come home the long way around, all the way around the whole Atlantic world, and having spent his life forcing the powerful to keep the word they had given his people."}, {"id": "eru-thomas-peters#s9", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "Now trace with your finger the road that one life traveled. Africa to America in the hold of a slave ship. America to Canada as a freed soldier of the crown. Canada to England as a petitioner for his people. England and Canada back to Africa as a founder of Freetown. He crossed the great ocean four times, in chains the first time and free every time after, and each crossing he made not for himself alone but dragging the promise of freedom along behind him, refusing to let it be forgotten. There are generals with statues who did less with their lives than this once enslaved man did with his."}, {"id": "eru-thomas-peters#s10", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "And here is the Yoruba thread in it, and why we claim him at our own fire. He was a son of this coast, taken from these lands. Everything the trade did to break a person, it did to him, and he answered every stroke of it with the same word, no. No, I will not stay a slave. No, I will not accept half a freedom. No, you will not forget your promise to my people. And in the end the coast that had lost him got him back, free and unbowed, and the town he helped to build, Freetown, would become the very place to which thousands of other freed Yoruba would return, the Saro, whose story is bound up with his. He helped build the door through which our stolen people came home."}, {"id": "eru-thomas-peters#s11", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So when someone tells you that a person can be broken, that circumstances are too strong, that a man born with nothing and robbed of even that must simply accept his lot, tell them about Thomas Peters. Tell them about the man who was carried out of Africa in a slave ship and sailed back a founder of a free city, who crossed the ocean four times and bent kings and governors to keep their word. He had no wealth and no letters and no army of his own. He had only a refusal that would not die. And with it he walked the whole world around and came home free."}]}, {"id": "eru-samuel-ajayi-crowther", "emoji": "⛓️", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#1a1308,#3a2a10)", "category": "itan-eru", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "8 min", "title": {"en": "The Man Who Gave His People Their Own Words: Samuel Ajayi Crowther and the First Yoruba Writing", "yo": "Samuel Ajayi Crowther"}, "desc": {"en": "The boy seized at Osogun and chained in a slave ship who was freed at sea, became the first African Anglican bishop, and gave the Yoruba their written language.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Itan Eru", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Oba, consolidates “The First Yoruba Newspaper” and “Bishop Crowther”)", "segments": [{"id": "eru-samuel-ajayi-crowther#s1", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "There is a particular kind of greatness that does not conquer a single town or win a single battle, and yet changes a whole people forever, because it gives them a thing they can never afterward be made to give up. Samuel Ajayi Crowther gave the Yoruba their own written language, their own Bible in their mother tongue, and a foundation upon which their newspapers, their books, and their whole literary life would one day be built. And the most astonishing part of the story is where he began, for the man who did all this began his life as a boy sold into slavery, chained in the hold of a ship, and everything he became he became after that, which is why his story is told not only as history but as one of the great deliverances, a life that ran from the slave market to the printed word."}, {"id": "eru-samuel-ajayi-crowther#s2", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "He was born Ajayi, a Yoruba boy of the Oyo country, in the terrible years when the empire was falling and the slave raiders rode where they pleased. When he was still a child, perhaps around thirteen, his town was attacked, and he was seized and sold, passed from hand to hand among the slavers, and finally packed aboard a Portuguese slave ship bound across the sea, one small terrified boy among the human cargo. But his ship was intercepted. A British naval squadron, part of the patrol that hunted the slave ships after Britain had turned against the trade, captured the vessel and freed its captives, and the boy Ajayi, along with the others, was landed at Freetown in Sierra Leone, the colony founded as a home for rescued and liberated Africans. He had lost his home, his family, his whole world. He had also, though he could not have known it, been set upon the strangest and most extraordinary road any Yoruba boy of his age would ever walk."}, {"id": "eru-samuel-ajayi-crowther#s3", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "In Freetown the boy was taken in by the missionaries, and he showed at once a hunger and a gift for learning that marked him out. He was baptized, and took the name Samuel Crowther after a clergyman of the missionary society, and he learned to read and write with such speed and brilliance that his teachers were astonished. He was among the first students of the institution that would become Fourah Bay College, he mastered English and then Latin and Greek, and he was eventually ordained a clergyman, the first of his people to rise so high in the church. But it is what he did with all this learning that makes him immortal, for Crowther did not simply become an educated man in a foreign tongue and leave his mother language behind. He turned the whole force of his mind back toward Yoruba, the language of the home he had been stolen from, and he set out to do something that had never been done. He would capture the Yoruba language in writing."}, {"id": "eru-samuel-ajayi-crowther#s4", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "This was a work of genius, and it is easy to forget how hard it was. Yoruba is a tonal language, where the same syllable means entirely different things depending on the pitch at which it is spoken, and no European alphabet had any way to show this. Crowther, working over years, listening with the ear of a native speaker and analyzing with the training of a scholar, worked out how to write his own language down, devising the system of marks and spellings that could carry the tones and the sounds of Yoruba onto the page. He produced a Yoruba grammar and a vocabulary, the first real study of the language ever made, and in doing so he did nothing less than give a spoken tongue a written body. Every Yoruba word that has ever been printed, every Yoruba newspaper, every Yoruba schoolbook and novel and letter, rests on the foundation that this one formerly enslaved boy laid down when he taught his language how to be written."}, {"id": "eru-samuel-ajayi-crowther#s5", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "With the language now written, Crowther turned to the work that would occupy much of his life and touch his people most deeply, the translation of the Bible into Yoruba. This too was an enormous labor, for to render the scriptures into Yoruba he had to find or forge Yoruba words for a thousand ideas and images, and he had to do it in a way that sang in the ear of an ordinary Yoruba listener and not merely in the eye of a scholar. He worked at it for decades, and the Yoruba Bible that came from his hand and the hands of those who worked with him became one of the most important books ever made in the language, not only a sacred text for the Christians among his people but a monument of the written tongue itself, a great reservoir of Yoruba prose that shaped how the language would be written ever after. To hold the scriptures in your own mother tongue, in the very words your mother spoke to you, was a gift whose weight is hard to measure, and it was the stolen boy from the Oyo country who gave it."}, {"id": "eru-samuel-ajayi-crowther#s6", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "His rise did not stop there. In recognition of his extraordinary gifts and his labors, Samuel Ajayi Crowther was consecrated a bishop, the first African bishop of the Anglican church, a height no man of his people had ever reached, and he was set over the mission on the great river Niger, carrying the faith and the written word up into the interior. For years he led that mission with wisdom and patience, an African bishop directing an African church, a living answer to everyone who claimed that Africans could not lead themselves. And yet the end of his life carried a bitterness that the elders do not hide, for as the century wore on and a harder, more racial spirit took hold among the younger European missionaries, they turned against the old bishop. They undermined him, accused his mission of failings, pushed him aside, and made it clear that they did not believe an African should hold such authority after all. The great man was humiliated in his last years by lesser men who could not have done a fraction of what he had done, and after his death they replaced him with a European, as if to erase the very possibility he had proved."}, {"id": "eru-samuel-ajayi-crowther#s7", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "But they could not erase it, and that is the triumph the elders come to at the last. The small-minded men who pulled down the old bishop are forgotten, their names dust, while Samuel Ajayi Crowther grows only greater with the years. For everything they took from him at the end, they could not take back the language he had written, the Bible he had translated, the proof he had given that a boy snatched from a slave ship could become one of the most learned and accomplished men of his age. Every Yoruba child who reads and writes the mother tongue today is his heir. Every Yoruba newspaper that was ever printed grew from the written language he built. He was stolen as property and he died a bishop and a scholar, and between those two points he gave his whole people a possession that no slaver and no colonizer and no small jealous man could ever take from them again, their own words, written down, forever. That is why the elders tell his story, so that no child of the language ever forgets the man who first taught it to live upon the page."}]}, {"id": "eru-male-revolt-1835", "emoji": "⛓️", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#1a1308,#3a2a10)", "category": "itan-eru", "rating": 9.4, "duration": "4 min", "title": {"en": "The Malê Revolt of 1835: When Yoruba Muslims Rose in Bahia", "yo": "Imale"}, "desc": {"en": "The planned 1835 uprising of enslaved, largely Yoruba Muslims in Salvador, Bahia.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Itan Eru", "secondary": [], "source_note": "", "segments": [{"id": "eru-male-revolt-1835#s1", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Narrator opens: On January 24, 1835, in the city of Salvador, Bahia, Brazil — something happened that the slave-owning class had always feared and never fully believed would come. The enslaved rose. Not in disorganized panic. Not in desperate, leaderless flight. They rose with planning, with leadership, with a written strategy, with religious conviction, and with a network of communication that had been built patiently over years while their captors assumed they were broken."}, {"id": "eru-male-revolt-1835#s2", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "The Male Revolt — named from the Yoruba word \"Imale,\" meaning Muslim — was organized and led primarily by Yoruba Muslims, many of them from the Oyo Empire's heartland. They had been enslaved during the brutal Yoruba civil wars of the early nineteenth century — wars that had fed the Atlantic slave trade with hundreds of thousands of captives. They arrived in Bahia not as a random collection of individuals but as members of a shared civilization, bearers of literacy (in Arabic), adherents of a faith that gave them solidarity and organizational structure, and people who had not forgotten who they were."}, {"id": "eru-male-revolt-1835#s3", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "The planning was meticulous. The revolt was timed for the final night of Ramadan — a night when the Muslim community would be awake for prayer, when their movements would not seem unusual, and when their collective spiritual state was one of heightened commitment. The leaders had written instructions on paper — a remarkable act in a context where enslaved people were systematically denied literacy. The papers, discovered when the revolt was betrayed before it could fully unfold, showed coordinated attacks on key points in Salvador, plans to seize the arsenal, signals for rising simultaneously across the city."}, {"id": "eru-male-revolt-1835#s4", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "It did not succeed in its full ambitions. The revolt was betrayed hours before the planned time, forcing the leaders to launch prematurely. Fighting broke out in the streets of Salvador. Seventy enslaved Yoruba Muslims confronted the armed forces of the Brazilian state and fought them in the streets for hours before being overwhelmed. Hundreds were killed in the fighting and the reprisals that followed. The survivors were flogged, imprisoned, and in some cases deported back to Africa — to a West Africa they had left in chains and would return to, now free, but carrying the wounds of a war on two continents."}, {"id": "eru-male-revolt-1835#s5", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "The Male Revolt failed to overthrow slavery in Bahia. But it permanently altered the psychology of the slave-owning class. The comfort of believing the enslaved were compliant was destroyed. Security costs increased. The political pressure for abolition, which came to Brazil only in 1888 — later than anywhere else in the Americas — found its argument sharpened by the knowledge that the enslaved were neither willing nor permanent. Among those who survived the revolt and were deported were the ancestors of the aguda community — the Yoruba-Brazilians who returned to West Africa and built the distinctive Brazilian-influenced architecture still visible in Lagos, Badagry, and Cotonou today. They came back changed, carrying Brazil in their architecture and their surnames, but still Yoruba in their bones."}, {"id": "eru-male-revolt-1835#s6", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "The Male Revolt of 1835 is one of the most significant events in the history of the African diaspora. It was planned in Yoruba. It was organized by men who remembered Oyo. It was fought by people who refused to forget what they were. And it reverberates — in the streets of Salvador, in the histories of the aguda families, in the fact that Yoruba culture survived the Atlantic crossing not just as a memory but as a living force capable of organized resistance."}]}, {"id": "eru-clotilda", "emoji": "⛓️", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#1a1308,#3a2a10)", "category": "itan-eru", "rating": 9.4, "duration": "4 min", "title": {"en": "The Last Slave Ship: Yoruba Captives and the Clotilda", "yo": "The Last Slave Ship: Yoruba Captives and the Clotilda"}, "desc": {"en": "The illegal 1860 voyage of the Clotilda, the last slave ship to the United States, and the captives who survived it.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Itan Eru", "secondary": [], "source_note": "", "segments": [{"id": "eru-clotilda#s1", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Narrator opens: In 1860, the Atlantic slave trade had been officially illegal for half a century. The British Navy's West Africa Squadron patrolled the coast. The United States had banned the importation of enslaved people since 1808. The era of the great slave ships was supposed to be over. And yet — in 1860, a man named Timothy Meaher made a bet. He wagered that he could smuggle a cargo of enslaved Africans into the United States undetected. He commissioned a ship. He sailed it to the coast of Dahomey. And he brought back 110 people, mostly Yoruba, taken in the wars that were then devastating Yorubaland."}, {"id": "eru-clotilda#s2", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "The ship was the Clotilda. It was the last known slave ship to bring enslaved Africans to the United States. After delivering its human cargo to Mobile Bay, Alabama, Meaher had the ship sunk and burned in an attempt to destroy evidence of his crime. The 110 Africans were distributed among plantations. The Civil War came five years later. They were emancipated. And then they did something extraordinary. They stayed together. They pooled whatever resources emancipation had given them — which was very little. They bought land north of Mobile, Alabama. They built a community. They called it Africatown."}, {"id": "eru-clotilda#s3", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "The founders of Africatown — among them the man who became their leader, Cudjo Lewis, born Oluale Kossola in Yorubaland — built a community specifically organized to maintain their African identity in America. They had their own church, their own cemetery, their own community governance. They maintained their Yoruba language among themselves. They told their children and their grandchildren where they came from."}, {"id": "eru-clotilda#s4", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Cudjo Lewis — Oluale Kossola — lived until 1935. He was the last known survivor of the Clotilda's cargo, and he was interviewed by the anthropologist Zora Neale Hurston, whose account of his life — \"Barracoon,\" written in 1931 but not published until 2018 — is one of the most important documents in the history of American slavery. Hurston sat with an old man who remembered being a young man in Yorubaland, who remembered the raid that took him, the ship that carried him, the seventy-five years in America that followed. He remembered Yoruba. He remembered his family's names. He had not forgotten where he came from."}, {"id": "eru-clotilda#s5", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "Africatown still exists in Mobile, Alabama, though diminished by industrial development that surrounded and compromised the community over the twentieth century. In 2019, researchers located the wreck of the Clotilda in Mobile Bay — confirming, with physical evidence, what the oral tradition had always said. The ship that brought the last enslaved Africans to America was found. The story was confirmed. Kossola is buried in Africatown. His grave is a pilgrimage site for descendants of the Clotilda survivors, for researchers, for people who understand that this quiet community in Alabama — built by Yoruba people who arrived in chains in 1860 and refused to forget who they were — is one of the most significant places in the African American story."}]}, {"id": "eru-trade-routes-mapo-hall", "emoji": "⛓️", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#1a1308,#3a2a10)", "category": "itan-eru", "rating": 9.4, "duration": "4 min", "title": {"en": "The Mapo Hall and the Trade Routes That Fed the Ships", "yo": "The Mapo Hall and the Trade Routes That Fed the Ships"}, "desc": {"en": "Following the trade backward from the coast to the interior markets, with Ibadan's Mapo Hill as vantage point.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Itan Eru", "secondary": [], "source_note": "", "segments": [{"id": "eru-trade-routes-mapo-hall#s1", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Narrator opens: The great slave trade was not simply a coastal affair. It was a system — a network of routes and relationships that extended deep into the West African interior, pulling people from their communities and funneling them toward the sea. To understand the slave trade in Yorubaland, you must follow the routes backward from the coast — backward through the mangrove swamps, through the forests, up into the savanna, to the markets where human beings were sold. The Mapo Hall in Ibadan sits atop one of the city's central hills. It was built in the colonial period and served as the administrative court of the colonial city. But the hill it sits on — Mapo Hill — was already a significant location in the pre-colonial era. It was one of the elevated points from which Ibadan's military and commercial activities were overseen."}, {"id": "eru-trade-routes-mapo-hall#s2", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "And among those activities, in the dark decades of the early-to-mid 19th century, was the slave trade. Ibadan was a city that grew partly on the proceeds of the Yoruba wars — and the Yoruba wars produced captives, and captives were traded. From Ibadan, trade routes ran southward to the lagoon coast. Middlemen — Ijebu traders, Awori merchants, and others who controlled the forest and lagoon routes — moved goods and people between the interior and the coast. The Ijebu were famously effective middlemen in this trade. Their control of the forest routes between Ibadan and the coast gave them leverage over what moved in both directions — European goods moving north, and slaves moving south. This middleman position was enormously profitable, and it was the wealth accumulated through this trade that funded the fortifications of Ijebu-Ode and the military capacity that resisted the British for so long."}, {"id": "eru-trade-routes-mapo-hall#s3", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "This economic narrative of the slave trade within Yorubaland is not widely understood outside of specialist circles. The common image of the trade focuses on the coast, on the ships, on the Middle Passage. But the trade had a vast interior infrastructure — markets, holding facilities, transportation routes, financing mechanisms — all of it organized by Yoruba traders and chiefs who found the trade compatible with their commercial and political interests. This is a painful truth, but it is a true truth. Honest memory requires it. The captives who moved through these trade routes were not exclusively strangers — prisoners taken in wars against distant peoples. They included Yoruba people enslaved by other Yoruba, sometimes by their own relatives, sometimes by their own community leaders. The categories of \"us\" and \"them\" that the slave trade exploited were more fluid and more brutal than simple ethnic narratives allow."}, {"id": "eru-trade-routes-mapo-hall#s4", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "The abolition of the Atlantic slave trade by Britain in 1807 did not immediately end the trade in West Africa. The demand from Brazil and Cuba — which did not abolish their slave trades until 1851 and 1867 respectively — kept the market alive. Lagos continued to export enslaved people illegally even after British naval patrols made the trade increasingly risky and expensive. When the British finally suppressed the Lagos slave trade in the 1850s and colonized Lagos in 1861, they justified these actions partly on humanitarian grounds — ending the slave trade. This justification was not entirely cynical. British abolitionists genuinely believed they were doing good. But the humanitarian arguments also provided useful cover for the commercial and strategic motivations behind British expansion in West Africa."}, {"id": "eru-trade-routes-mapo-hall#s5", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "The legacy of the trade routes is still visible in West African geography — in the placement of certain towns, in the old market sites, in the paths that are now paved roads. The routes that carried captives toward the coast became, in the colonial period, the routes along which palm oil and cocoa moved outward. The infrastructure of exploitation had a second life. Remember the routes. Remember who walked them. Remember that it was not only chains that bound people — economic systems bind as effectively as iron."}]}, {"id": "eru-epe-lagoon-route", "emoji": "⛓️", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#1a1308,#3a2a10)", "category": "itan-eru", "rating": 9.4, "duration": "4 min", "title": {"en": "Epe and the Lagoon Slave Route", "yo": "Epe"}, "desc": {"en": "The sheltered lagoon highway that let the trade run hidden from British naval patrols.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Itan Eru", "secondary": [], "source_note": "", "segments": [{"id": "eru-epe-lagoon-route#s1", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Narrator opens: The Atlantic Ocean was not the only water that carried enslaved people. In Yorubaland, the lagoon system that runs parallel to the coast — a network of interconnected bodies of water stretching from what is now the Republic of Benin to the Niger Delta — was the internal highway of the slave trade. Epe. A town on the eastern end of the Lagos Lagoon. Today a quiet fishing and agricultural community, historically connected to the Ijebu kingdom, associated with the Muslim community that was exiled from Lagos in the 19th century. But in the era of the slave trade, Epe was a node in the lagoon route — a point through which trade flowed, including the trade in human beings."}, {"id": "eru-epe-lagoon-route#s2", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "The lagoon route worked like this: captives from the interior were marched to the lagoon edge. At the lagoon, they were loaded onto large canoes — the same canoes used for general trade. These canoes moved along the lagoon, hidden from the open sea, sheltered from the British naval patrols that increasingly monitored the Atlantic coastline. The captives were moved from one holding point to another along the lagoon until they reached the coastal points — Badagry, Lagos, Epe, Porto-Novo — where the ocean-going slave ships were waiting. The lagoon route allowed the slave trade to continue long after the British Navy began intercepting ships on the open ocean. As long as enslaved people could be hidden in the mangroves and lagoons, they could be loaded onto ships at night, quickly, before the naval patrols could intercept. The trade adapted to the surveillance by going underground — or rather, underwater."}, {"id": "eru-epe-lagoon-route#s3", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "The towns along the lagoon route each have their own relationship to this history. Some were primarily holding points. Some were commercial centers where captives were sold and resold. Some were communities that resisted the trade, hiding refugees or assisting those who tried to escape. The complexity of this geography — the marshes, the islands, the connecting waterways — made the lagoon route extremely difficult to police and nearly impossible to close. Even as the Lagos slave trade was formally suppressed after 1851, lagoon routes to the east continued to function. The last recorded slave ship to load from the Lagos area left in the late 1860s. By then, the trade had contracted enormously under British naval pressure and the abolition of slavery in the Americas. But it had not entirely stopped."}, {"id": "eru-epe-lagoon-route#s4", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "The lagoon today is a highway of commerce, recreation, and fishing. Its waters are the same waters. The mangroves are the same mangroves. The canoes that move through them today are descendants of the canoes that moved through them then. History lives in geography. The water remembers what the land prefers to forget."}]}, {"id": "eru-abolitionist-missionaries", "emoji": "⛓️", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#1a1308,#3a2a10)", "category": "itan-eru", "rating": 9.4, "duration": "4 min", "title": {"en": "The Abolitionist Missionaries in Yorubaland", "yo": "The Abolitionist Missionaries in Yorubaland"}, "desc": {"en": "The missionaries who came both to end the trade and to open the way for empire.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Itan Eru", "secondary": [], "source_note": "", "segments": [{"id": "eru-abolitionist-missionaries#s1", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Narrator opens: The missionaries came to save souls. They also — and this is the part that is often forgotten — came to end the slave trade. In the mid-19th century, the two missions were inseparable in the minds of the evangelical Christians who were the primary architects of British West African missionary activity. The Church Missionary Society (CMS) established its first permanent station in Yorubaland at Badagry in 1845, followed by Abeokuta in 1846. The missionaries who came — Henry Townsend, Charles Gollmer, Thomas King — were men of genuine conviction. They believed that Christianity, literacy, and \"legitimate commerce\" (trade in agricultural products rather than people) were the path to the transformation of West Africa. They were also, in ways they did not always see clearly, agents of a colonial project that would eventually dispossess the very people they claimed to serve."}, {"id": "eru-abolitionist-missionaries#s2", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "This complexity is at the heart of the missionary story in Yorubaland. The same Townsend who campaigned vigorously against the slave trade was also deeply paternalistic toward the Yoruba Christians he worked with, reluctant to see them as full equals in the church. The same mission organizations that translated the Bible into Yoruba also provided intelligence to colonial officials that facilitated the British annexation of Lagos and the pacification of the Yoruba kingdoms. And yet: the mission schools educated Samuel Ajayi Crowther. The mission presses printed the first Yoruba newspapers. The mission hospitals treated the sick before any government health services existed. The mission records — the letters, the reports, the journals — are among the most detailed historical documents of 19th-century Yorubaland. Without them, much of what we know about this period would be lost."}, {"id": "eru-abolitionist-missionaries#s3", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "The missionaries opposed the slave trade consistently and sometimes at personal risk. When Lagos chiefs tried to continue the trade under the noses of the British authorities, it was often missionary reports that exposed them. When captives were held in Abeokuta against their will, it was missionary intervention — combined with the political organizing of the Saro community — that sometimes secured their release. This is the complexity of the missionary legacy: real good and real harm, often by the same actors, often simultaneously. To dismiss the missionaries as simply colonial agents is to ignore the genuine opposition to the slave trade and the genuine educational work. To celebrate them uncritically is to ignore the paternalism, the cultural destruction, and the colonial intelligence they provided. The missionaries were not saviors. They were people — flawed, sometimes courageous, sometimes condescending, often brilliant, always certain of their own rightness. In that, they were human."}, {"id": "eru-abolitionist-missionaries#s4", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "What they left behind — the schools, the linguistic work, the records — is genuinely valuable. What they also left behind — the cultural dislocation, the religious hierarchies that sometimes displaced traditional governance — is genuinely costly. Honor the good. Acknowledge the harm. That is all we can do with complex history."}]}, {"id": "diaspora-saro", "emoji": "🌍", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#001520,#003550)", "category": "diaspora", "rating": 9.7, "duration": "13 min", "title": {"en": "The Saro: The Children Who Came Home", "yo": "Saro"}, "desc": {"en": "The liberated Yoruba who returned from Sierra Leone to Lagos and Abeokuta and rebuilt what slavery had scattered.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Diaspora", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Ilu Okeere, the full story of the Saro, the liberated Yoruba who returned from Sierra Leone to Lagos and Abeokuta, and rebuilt what slavery had scattered)", "segments": [{"id": "diaspora-saro#s1", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Tonight, my child, I must tell you a story that begins in sorrow and ends, against all the odds, in a kind of homecoming, for it is the story of the ones who were stolen and then, by a strange turning of history, came back. We have spoken of the orisa and the ancient kings and the folktales of the fire. But now we turn to a more recent and a harder chapter, to the great wound of the slave trade, and to a people who carried that wound and yet, marvelously, found their way home again. Tonight I tell you of the Saro, the children who came home."}, {"id": "diaspora-saro#s2", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "You know already, for I have not hidden it from you in the tales of Afonja and the fall of Oyo, that there came a terrible time when our own land was torn by war, kingdom against kingdom, and that in those wars countless of our people were captured and sold, carried down to the coast in chains and loaded onto the ships of the strangers and taken across the great water, never, it seemed, to return. This was the slave trade, my child, the deepest sorrow of our history, and I will speak of it more fully another night. But tonight I tell you of a thread of hope that ran, unlooked for, out of that darkness."}, {"id": "diaspora-saro#s3", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "For there came a time when the trade in human beings was at last declared unlawful by some of the powers of the world, and ships of the British navy began to patrol the great water, stopping the slave ships, and freeing the captives they found chained in the holds. And these freed captives, these thousands of rescued souls taken off the slave ships, had to be put somewhere, and many of them were landed at a place on the coast of West Africa that came to be called Freetown, in the country of Sierra Leone, a town made for the freed, a settlement of liberated Africans from a hundred different peoples, all thrown together, all torn from their homes, all beginning again from nothing on a strange shore."}, {"id": "diaspora-saro#s4", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "And a great many of these liberated Africans, my child, a very great many, were Yoruba. Our own people, captured in the wars at home, sold, shipped, and then intercepted and freed and landed at Freetown, far from home but alive, and free. They found themselves in this new town among people of many tongues, and they had to build new lives. And build they did. They took up trades, they learned to read and write, many of them embraced the new religion of the missionaries and were baptized, they became teachers and clerks and traders and craftsmen and clergymen. They prospered, some of them, remarkably. And their children grew up in Freetown, educated, Christian, literate, skilled in the ways of the new world that had been forced upon their parents."}, {"id": "diaspora-saro#s5", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "But here is the marvel, my child, the thing that makes this a homecoming and not only an exile. These liberated Yoruba in Sierra Leone never forgot where they came from. They remembered home. They remembered Yorubaland, the towns they or their parents had been stolen from, the language, the people. And when they learned, in time, that the wars at home were easing, that the coast was opening to trade, that it might be possible to go back, a great longing rose in them, a longing to return to the land of their fathers, to go home."}, {"id": "diaspora-saro#s6", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "And so they began to return. From the eighteen thirties and forties onward, my child, these liberated Yoruba and their children began to sail back from Sierra Leone to the Yoruba coast, to Lagos, and up to Abeokuta, and to other towns, coming home to the very land their people had been torn from a generation before. And because they came from Sierra Leone, the people at home called them the Saro, a name made from Sierra Leone in the Yoruba mouth, and the Saro they have been called ever since. The children who were stolen, or the children of the stolen, coming home again, no longer as slaves, but as free men and women, educated, skilled, and full of purpose."}, {"id": "diaspora-saro#s7", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "Now think, my child, what these returnees carried home with them, for it is the heart of the story. They carried, first, the ache and the joy of homecoming itself, the reunion, in some cases, with relatives who had thought them lost forever, weeping in the streets of Abeokuta as families torn apart by slavery found each other again after a generation. Imagine that reunion, my child, a mother who had mourned a child sold into slavery long ago, meeting that child again, grown, returned, free. Such things happened among the Saro, and there is no measuring the joy of them."}, {"id": "diaspora-saro#s8", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "But they carried more. They carried the new learning, the literacy, the education, the crafts and trades and skills they had gained in exile. And they carried, many of them, the new religion, Christianity, and with it the printing press, the school, the church. And they carried a fierce energy, a determination to rebuild, to lift up the land of their fathers, to bring to it the new knowledge they had gained. The Saro became, my child, a great engine of change and building in the Yorubaland of the eighteen hundreds. They founded schools. They founded churches. They started newspapers, the first newspapers our land had known. They became merchants and traders, linking the Yoruba coast to the wider world of commerce. They became clergymen and teachers and clerks and lawyers and doctors. They became, many of them, leaders, shapers of the new world that was coming."}, {"id": "diaspora-saro#s9", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "And I must tell you of one Saro above all, my child, for he is the greatest of them, and his story is the whole story of the Saro in a single life. There was a boy, captured as a child in the wars at home, around the age of twelve or thirteen, sold, put aboard a slave ship. And that very ship was intercepted by the British navy and the boy was freed and landed at Freetown. His name in the new world became Samuel Ajayi Crowther. He was educated in Sierra Leone, he showed a brilliant mind, he embraced learning and the new faith, and he rose, and rose, and rose. He became a scholar of languages. He did the immense work of reducing the Yoruba language to writing, of translating the Bible into Yoruba, of creating a Yoruba dictionary and grammar, giving his own people, for the first time, their language in written form, an achievement of staggering importance, my child, for a language that can be written can be preserved and taught and spread as never before."}, {"id": "diaspora-saro#s10", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "And this same Ajayi Crowther, this boy who had been a slave on an intercepted ship, rose to become a bishop, the first African bishop of his church, a man of learning and dignity and immense accomplishment, honoured across the world. From a child in chains to a bishop and a scholar who gave his people their written language. That, my child, is the story of the Saro in a single astonishing life: the deepest sorrow of capture and slavery, turned, by the strange mercies of history and by the man’s own genius and will, into a homecoming and a gift to his people that endures to this day. When you read anything written in Yoruba, my child, you owe a debt to Ajayi Crowther, the freed slave who came home and gave his people their written word."}, {"id": "diaspora-saro#s11", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Now I will not pretend to you, my child, for I never do, that the story of the Saro was simple or without its shadows. The returnees came home changed by their exile, carrying the new religion and the new ways, and this sometimes set them apart from, and sometimes above, those who had never left, and there were tensions, misunderstandings, a sense among some at home that the Saro had become half-strangers, foreign in their own land, bringing the ways of the very people who had enslaved them. And the education and connections of the Saro tied them, in complicated ways, to the growing power of the British on the coast, so that they stood sometimes between two worlds, belonging fully to neither, the returned children who were home and yet not entirely home. These are real complications, my child, and an honest telling holds them. Homecoming after so great a rupture is never simple, and the Saro carried the strangeness of their exile even in their return."}, {"id": "diaspora-saro#s12", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "But hold to the marvel at the center, my child, for it outshines the shadows. A people were torn from their land and scattered across the water in the greatest sorrow of their history. And out of that scattering, by an unlooked-for turning, a portion of them came home, free, educated, purposeful, and they poured themselves into the rebuilding and the lifting up of the land of their fathers. The wound of slavery did not have the final word. The scattered children found their way home, and made of their very exile a gift to bring back to their people. That, my child, is a triumph of the human spirit and of a people’s unbreakable bond to their homeland, and it is a light shining out of the darkest chapter of our story."}, {"id": "diaspora-saro#s13", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "There is a deep teaching in the Saro, my child, and I want you to carry it. It is this: that even the greatest sorrow, even the deepest rupture, even a scattering that seems final and total, need not be the end of a people or a person. The bond to home can survive an ocean and a generation of exile. The gifts gained even in suffering can be carried back and made to serve. And the children who were stolen can come home, and in coming home can rebuild what was broken, and give back more than was taken. The Saro are the proof that a people cannot be finally destroyed so long as they remember who they are and long to return to what they came from. They are the children who came home, and their homecoming is one of the proudest and most hopeful chapters in all our long story."}, {"id": "diaspora-saro#s14", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So when you hear the great Saro family names, my child, the names that still stand for learning and building and leadership in our land, remember what they mean: they are the names of the children who were stolen and came home, who turned exile into homecoming and sorrow into gift, who rebuilt with their own educated hands the land their people had been torn from. Honour them. And remember, in your own life, whatever exile or rupture or scattering you may ever face, the lesson of the Saro: that home remembers you, that the bond cannot be finally broken, and that even the children of the deepest sorrow can come home, and build, and give back, and turn the darkest chapter into a light for those who come after."}, {"id": "diaspora-saro#s15", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "Tomorrow, if the ancestors are willing, I will tell you of the ones who did not come home, of the far greater number carried across the great water who never returned, and of the astonishing thing they did with their exile, of how, in Brazil and Cuba and Trinidad and Haiti, in chains and under the whip, they kept the orisa alive in secret and built new worlds of faith across the sea. But tonight belongs to the children who came home, to the Saro, and we will sleep in the joy of a homecoming that slavery could not prevent."}]}, {"id": "diaspora-brazilian-returnees", "emoji": "🌍", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#001520,#003550)", "category": "diaspora", "rating": 9.6, "duration": "10 min", "title": {"en": "The Brazilian Returnees: A Diaspora Comes Home", "yo": "Aguda"}, "desc": {"en": "The Aguda: freed Yoruba who came home from Brazil and became master builders of the coast.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Diaspora", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Aguda, consolidates “The Brazilian Returnees: A Diaspora Comes Home” and “Children of the Crossing: Yoruba Voices in Brazil”)", "segments": [{"id": "diaspora-brazilian-returnees#s1", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Draw close, my children, for tonight I tell you a story that runs the wrong way down the river of sorrow. You have heard how our people were taken across the water, and you have wept for them, as is right. But not every current runs in one direction. There is a chapter of our history in which the ocean, having swallowed so many, gave some of them back, and the ones it gave back walked up out of the surf at Lagos and Badagry and Porto Novo wearing strange clothes and speaking a strange tongue, and yet carrying in their chests the unmistakable heart of Yorubaland. We called them the Aguda, the Brazilians, and their story is one of the most astonishing our people have ever lived, for it is the story of a homecoming that no one planned and no one expected, a diaspora that turned its face back toward the rising sun and came home."}, {"id": "diaspora-brazilian-returnees#s2", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "To understand the Aguda you must first understand Bahia, that great Brazilian province of sugar and gold and suffering, where so many Yoruba captives were landed that the streets of Salvador came to echo with the names of our orisa. In Bahia the Yoruba were called Nago, and there were so many of them, arriving in such numbers through the first half of the nineteenth century, that they did not dissolve into the crowd the way a few scattered strangers would. They found one another. They kept their language on their tongues and their gods in their hearts, and in the slave quarters and the back streets and the markets of Salvador they rebuilt, brick by secret brick, a Yoruba world in miniature. They kept the worship of Sango and Yemoja and Osun and Esu, hidden behind the faces of Catholic saints when they had to be hidden, sung openly when they dared. They kept the drums. They kept the food. They kept, above all, the memory of a place across the water called home."}, {"id": "diaspora-brazilian-returnees#s3", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "And they did not keep it quietly. The Nago of Bahia were known throughout Brazil as the most rebellious of the enslaved, the hardest to break, the readiest to rise. Again and again through those decades they revolted, and the greatest of these risings came in 1835, the revolt the Brazilians remember as the Male uprising, when enslaved and freed Africans, many of them Yoruba, many of them Muslims who had learned their letters in Arabic before ever they saw a ship, rose in the streets of Salvador in a bid for freedom. The rising was crushed, as such risings so often were, and the reprisal was terrible. But it frightened the Brazilian authorities to the marrow, for they saw now that these Africans would never be content in chains, and among the punishments they devised was banishment. Many Africans, freed ones especially, were pressured, deported, or otherwise pushed to leave Brazil and sail back across the Atlantic toward the coast they had come from. And so the very defiance that made the Yoruba dangerous in Brazil became one of the doors through which they returned home."}, {"id": "diaspora-brazilian-returnees#s4", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "But do not think it was only banishment that brought them back, for that would rob them of their agency, and these were people of fierce will. Many came home because they chose to. Through the middle decades of the century, as some among them earned or bought their freedom, as they saved coin by coin from labour and trade, they made a decision that must have taken a courage we can barely imagine. They booked passage, sometimes chartering whole ships together, and they sailed back toward a homeland many of them had left as children and some had never seen at all, having been born in Brazil to Yoruba parents. Imagine it. Free at last, in a land where they had built lives, and yet choosing to cross that same dreaded ocean again, in the other direction, drawn by a pull stronger than comfort, the pull of the soil their mothers had sung about."}, {"id": "diaspora-brazilian-returnees#s5", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "They landed along the Bight of Benin, at Lagos and Badagry, at Porto Novo and Ouidah and Agoue, in the years from roughly the 1830s onward, and they became a people apart, neither wholly of the old Yorubaland they returned to nor any longer of the Brazil they had left. They built their own quarters in the coastal towns, the Brazilian quarters, and they brought with them the skills they had been forced to learn in bondage, and here is one of the deep ironies of their story. The trades that Brazil had beaten into them, masonry and carpentry and tailoring and baking and fine building in stone, they now turned to their own advantage as free people. The Aguda became the master builders of the coast. Walk through old Lagos, through the quarters of Porto Novo and Ouidah, and the beautiful storeyed houses with their carved balconies and their Brazilian shutters and their ornamented facades are the work of returnee hands. They built churches and mansions and whole streetscapes in a style the coast had never seen, a style that married Bahia to the Bight, and much of it still stands today, a diaspora written in mortar and stone."}, {"id": "diaspora-brazilian-returnees#s6", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "And they brought their names, and their food, and their faith, and their festivals. The Aguda kept their Catholicism, and to this day their descendants hold the Burial of the Christ and the bright masquerade they call the bumba, and the Brazilian carnival rhythms that still sound in Lagos and Porto Novo at certain seasons carry, if you listen, the far echo of Salvador. They kept surnames that ring strange and beautiful in Yoruba mouths to this day, da Silva, da Costa, Fernandez, Marinho, Pereira, Domingo, names that a Yoruba child today may carry with perfect pride, never thinking that each one is a scar and a survival at once, a mark of what was taken and a banner of what came home."}, {"id": "diaspora-brazilian-returnees#s7", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Let me give you a face for this, so it does not float away as a crowd. Think of a returnee we might call Manoel in Bahia and Ojo at home, for many of them carried two names and lived between two selves. Born in Salvador to a mother taken from Ketu, he grew up hearing Yoruba sung over the cooking fire and Portuguese barked in the street, worshipping Sango on Friday night and the saints on Sunday morning, a boy stitched together from two worlds. He learned masonry as a young man, laying stone under a hard sun for another man’s profit. When freedom came, and the chance to sail, he stood on the Salvador docks with his tools in a sack and his mother’s stories in his heart, and he chose the ship going east. He landed at Lagos a stranger in his own ancestral land, mocked at first for his accent, his clothes, his half-forgotten Yoruba. And then, stone by stone, he built. He raised houses that made the town stop and stare. He married, he prospered, he became a somebody, and his children grew up fully Yoruba again, the circle closing after three generations and an ocean. That was the Aguda life, and thousands lived some version of it."}, {"id": "diaspora-brazilian-returnees#s8", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "Now hear the meaning, my children, for this story carries a teaching that I want you to hold all your lives. The Aguda are the living proof that the trade did not have the last word. It took people out, and some of those very people, or their children, walked back in, richer in skill, unbroken in spirit, and they helped build the coast we know.  They are proof that a people can be scattered to the far side of the world and still find the road home, that memory can survive three generations of bondage and still point true north, that even from the depths of the sugar plantations there was a way back to the compound of the fathers. When you meet a Lagosian named da Silva or Pereira, you are not meeting a stranger with a borrowed name. You are meeting the descendant of survivors who crossed the ocean twice, once in chains and once in freedom, and who chose, when they had the choice, to come home."}, {"id": "diaspora-brazilian-returnees#s9", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So remember the Aguda when the story of the trade threatens to drown you in grief. Remember that the ocean gives as well as takes. Remember the ships that came east, full of free people with tools in their hands and Sango in their hearts, who stepped ashore and built beauty out of what suffering had taught them. The trade meant to erase us. Instead, it sent us out and brought some of us back stronger, and the houses those returnees built still stand along the coast today, catching the morning light, saying without words to everyone who passes, we were taken, and we returned, and we are still here. That is the story of the homecoming. Sleep now, and when you dream of ships, dream of the ones sailing east."}]}, {"id": "diaspora-lucumi-cuba", "emoji": "🌍", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#001520,#003550)", "category": "diaspora", "rating": 9.6, "duration": "9 min", "title": {"en": "Oduduwa’s Children in Cuba: Lucumi and Its Roots", "yo": "Lucumi"}, "desc": {"en": "How the children of Oduduwa rebuilt Yoruba religion in Cuba as Lucumi, one of its most complete survivals anywhere.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Diaspora", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Lucumi, consolidates “Oduduwa’s Children in Cuba: Lucumi and Its Roots” and “The Cuban Connection: Lucumi and the Yoruba Link”)", "segments": [{"id": "diaspora-lucumi-cuba#s1", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Draw close, my children, and let me carry you across the great water to an island where, against every expectation, the children of Oduduwa built a new Ile-Ife in the shadow of the sugar cane. The island is Cuba, and there our people were called Lucumi, and their story is one of the greatest wonders in the whole long tale of our diaspora, for on that island the orisa did not merely survive, they flourished, they were fed, they were housed in the hearts and homes of the enslaved and their descendants, and today, more than a century and a half after the last slave ship, there are men and women in Havana and Matanzas who speak the names of Sango and Yemoja and Obatala and Osun with a devotion as fierce and pure as any priest in Osogbo. How did this happen? How did the gods cross the ocean and not drown? Sit, and I will tell you."}, {"id": "diaspora-lucumi-cuba#s2", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "First, the name, for names carry history. On the island they called our people Lucumi, and the old ones say the word came from a Yoruba greeting, oluku mi, meaning my friend, which our captured people used among themselves so often that the Spanish took it for the name of the whole nation. Think of that, my children. The very name by which they were known in bondage was a word of friendship, a hand extended between strangers who recognised in each other the same lost home. In a place designed to make them nameless, they named themselves after friendship. That is where the Lucumi story begins, in the stubborn insistence of the taken that they were still a people, still kin, still oluku, friends to one another across the horror."}, {"id": "diaspora-lucumi-cuba#s3", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Now, the sugar. You must understand why so many Yoruba came to Cuba, and it is a bitter understanding. In the nineteenth century, just as the wars in our homeland were flooding the coast with captives, the island of Cuba was becoming the greatest sugar factory the world had ever seen. Its plantations devoured human beings the way a fire devours wood, and its planters bought captives by the tens of thousands, and a great many of those captives were Yoruba, taken in the very wars I have told you of, marched to Lagos and Ouidah, and shipped across to feed the Cuban cane. The tragedy that emptied our towns filled the island’s plantations. But because the Yoruba came in such numbers and so late, right up until the trade’s final years, they arrived while their traditions were still fresh and whole, and there were enough of them to find one another and rebuild."}, {"id": "diaspora-lucumi-cuba#s4", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "And here is where the wonder truly begins, in the cabildos. The Spanish, being Catholic, permitted the enslaved and free Africans to form religious brotherhoods, societies organised by nation, and these were called cabildos. The Spanish meant them as instruments of control and conversion, a way to teach the Africans to be good Catholics. But the Africans, being cleverer than their masters, turned the cabildo into a fortress for the very thing it was meant to destroy. Behind the permitted Catholic face of the brotherhood, in the drumming and the feast days and the gatherings the cabildo allowed, the Lucumi kept the orisa alive. When the calendar said it was the feast of Saint Barbara, the Lucumi feasted Sango, the lord of thunder, whose red and white the saint conveniently wore. When it was the feast of Our Lady of Regla, they honoured Yemoja, mother of waters. Behind Our Lady of Charity stood Osun of the sweet river. Behind Saint Lazarus stood Babalu-Aye. The saints became masks, and behind every mask an orisa smiled. This is the faith the world now calls Santeria, or more rightly Regla de Ocha, the rule of the orisa, and it is Yoruba religion carried whole across the ocean and hidden in plain sight until it was safe to show its face."}, {"id": "diaspora-lucumi-cuba#s5", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "Do not imagine this was a watering-down, a faint memory, a few surviving scraps. The Lucumi tradition of Cuba is one of the most complete and vigorous survivals of Yoruba religion anywhere on earth, our homeland included. The Cuban practitioners kept the divination, the Ifa of the babalawo, with its odu and its wisdom. They kept the initiation of priests, the making of the omo orisa, the child of the god. They kept the drums, the sacred bata drums whose rhythms call each orisa by name. They kept the songs, sung in a Yoruba so old and so lovingly preserved that Cuban singers today chant verses in our tongue that even we have half forgotten. They kept the foods, the offerings, the colours, the dances, the whole living body of the worship. When scholars from Nigeria have visited Cuba, they have wept to hear the old prayers, because the Lucumi kept safe across the water things that time and change had worn away at home."}, {"id": "diaspora-lucumi-cuba#s6", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Think on the meaning of that, my children, for it turns the whole story of loss on its head. We think of the diaspora as the place where things were taken and scattered and lost. And in Cuba we find, instead, a great storehouse, a place where our own heritage was guarded so faithfully by the enslaved and their children that it came back to us enriched. The gods that the plantation was meant to strip away became, on that island, the very thing that held a broken people together, gave them dignity, gave them a secret kingdom that no master could enter. In the cane fields, worked half to death, a Lucumi man knew a thing his owner could never know or take, that he was a child of Sango, that thunder was his father, that he carried within him a lineage older and grander than the whole Spanish empire. That secret knowledge was a freedom the whip could not reach."}, {"id": "diaspora-lucumi-cuba#s7", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Let me give you one image to seal the tale. In the city of Matanzas, which the drummers call the cradle of the sacred bata, there came a night, generations into slavery, when a young Cuban-born woman, whose grandmother had crossed on a ship from the Bight of Benin, was initiated as a priestess of Osun. She had never seen Africa. She spoke Spanish in the street. And yet through three days of ancient ritual, guided by elders who had guarded every word, she was crowned with the mysteries of the river goddess, dressed in yellow and gold, seated on the throne of the newly made, receiving the ashe, the sacred force, that had flowed unbroken from Osogbo across the ocean and down through the hidden years to reach her in that room. When she rose, she was Iyawo, a bride of the orisa, a full daughter of Osun, and every gesture of her crowning was Yoruba to its root. Her grandmother had been sold as a thing. Her grandmother’s granddaughter was crowned as a priestess. That is the arc the Lucumi drew across the darkness."}, {"id": "diaspora-lucumi-cuba#s8", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "So when the story of the trade weighs on you, remember Cuba, and remember the Lucumi, the friends, oluku, who would not let go of the gods. Remember that in the very belly of the plantation, our people built a secret Ile-Ife and kept its lamps burning for two hundred years. Remember that the orisa crossed the ocean and did not drown, that they hid behind the saints and waited, and that today they are worshipped openly on that island by millions who carry Yoruba prayers in their mouths. The trade tried to turn our people into cargo and our gods into ghosts. Instead, our people became keepers of a sacred flame, and our gods found a second home across the sea, and from that home they now return to teach us what we had half forgotten."}, {"id": "diaspora-lucumi-cuba#s9", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "That is the story of Oduduwa’s children in Cuba. Sleep now, and know that wherever the ocean carried our people, the orisa went with them, and would not be left behind."}]}, {"id": "diaspora-linguistics-of-loss", "emoji": "🌍", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#001520,#003550)", "category": "diaspora", "rating": 9.6, "duration": "9 min", "title": {"en": "The Linguistics of Loss: How a Language Crossed the Water", "yo": "The Linguistics of Loss: How a Language Crossed the Water"}, "desc": {"en": "The Yoruba words, names, and songs that survived the crossing in Brazil, Cuba, and the Caribbean.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Diaspora", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Ede, consolidates “The Linguistics of Loss” and “Words That Survived the Crossing”)", "segments": [{"id": "diaspora-linguistics-of-loss#s1", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Sit, and I will tell you a story about words. Not about kings or wars this time, though kings and wars are in it, but about the small unbreakable things that a person carries when everything else has been stripped away. A captive on the coast could be robbed of his name, his cloth, his beads, his freedom, his country, and his gods. But there was one thing the slaver could not reach into his throat and take, and that was his mother tongue. This is the story of how the Yoruba language crossed the ocean in the mouths of the stolen, and what happened to it on the far shore."}, {"id": "diaspora-linguistics-of-loss#s2", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Think of what a language is. It is not only a way to ask for water or to count goats. It is a whole world folded into sound. In the Yoruba tongue there are words for things the slavers had no words for, three tones that turn one shape of letters into three different meanings, proverbs that carry the wisdom of a thousand years in a single breath, the names of orisa that are also prayers, the drum patterns that speak the language so that a talking drum can say a man’s praise name across a valley. When a Yoruba person was carried onto a ship, all of that went with him, coiled inside him, waiting."}, {"id": "diaspora-linguistics-of-loss#s3", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "But the slave ships were built, cruelly and deliberately, to break the tongue as well as the body. The captains had learned that a shipload of people who all spoke one language could plan together, could rise up together, could throw the crew into the sea. So the traders mixed their captives on purpose. A Yoruba man would be chained beside a man from far up a different river whose speech he could not follow, beside a woman from the forests whose words were strange to both. In the darkness of the hold, surrounded by his own kind of humanity and yet unable to speak with most of them, the captive learned the first cruelty of the crossing: that language, which had bound his village together, could be turned into a wall between the suffering."}, {"id": "diaspora-linguistics-of-loss#s4", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "And on the far shore the breaking continued. A newly landed African was often forbidden to speak his own tongue. Owners feared any speech they could not understand. Children born in bondage were raised to the language of the master, and the mother tongue, if it survived at all, survived in whispers, in lullabies, in the words an old woman muttered over a cooking pot, in the secret names of gods."}, {"id": "diaspora-linguistics-of-loss#s5", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "You would think, then, that the language must simply have died on the far shore, drowned in the ocean of the master’s speech. And in many places it did fade. But here is the wonder, and it is a true wonder. In certain places the Yoruba tongue did not die. It went underground and it lived. It survived not as a whole living language of daily speech, but as something perhaps even more remarkable, as sacred language, as the frozen and faithful words of prayer and song and ritual, carried down the generations by people who in time no longer spoke Yoruba to buy bread but still spoke it, exactly, to call the orisa."}, {"id": "diaspora-linguistics-of-loss#s6", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "Go to Cuba, and you will hear it. In the ceremonies of the tradition they call Lucumi, priests and priestesses who grew up speaking Spanish sing in a language their grandmothers called Lucumi and that a Yoruba grandmother would recognize, imperfectly but unmistakably, as her own. The words for the orisa are there. Sango, Yemoja, Osun, Obatala, Esu, Ogun, the whole family of the gods answers to names that a person in Oyo would know. The praise songs, the oriki, ripple with phrases that survived three centuries of exile with astonishing faithfulness, worn smooth in places, half understood in others, but alive."}, {"id": "diaspora-linguistics-of-loss#s7", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "Go to Brazil, and you will hear it again in the houses of Candomble, where the sacred tongue is called Nago, another old name for the Yoruba, and where the songs and the divination verses preserve words and even whole proverbs from across the water. Go to Trinidad, to Haiti, and you will find fragments, glowing coals of Yoruba speech buried in the ash of the crossing, kept alive because they were holy, because you do not change the words a god has always answered to."}, {"id": "diaspora-linguistics-of-loss#s8", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Think of what that means. A daily language can drift and soften and mix with its neighbors. But a sacred language, spoken to the gods, is guarded. The priests correct the young. The elders insist on the old pronunciation. To sing the song wrong is to insult the orisa. And so, by an irony that should make you catch your breath, the very seriousness of Yoruba religion became the vessel that carried Yoruba words unbroken across an ocean and three hundred years, when everything else, the freedom and the names and the homeland, had been torn away."}, {"id": "diaspora-linguistics-of-loss#s9", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "There is one more turn to this story, and it happened not across the water but back at home, and it too grew out of the trade. When the freed captives gathered in Sierra Leone, they came from many towns and spoke many dialects of the same tongue, the speech of Oyo differing from the speech of Egba, differing from Ijebu, differing from Ekiti, all recognizably one language but each proud of its own way. In Freetown, crowded together, they needed to understand one another, and out of that need something new began to form, a common way of speaking that drew from all the dialects."}, {"id": "diaspora-linguistics-of-loss#s10", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "And among those freed people was that boy I told you of, Samuel Ajayi Crowther, who having learned to read set himself a task no one had done before. He set out to write the Yoruba language down. To choose letters for its sounds, to mark its tones, to fix its spelling, to translate books into it, to make a dictionary of it. A language that had lived for uncounted centuries only in the mouth now, in the hands of a man who had himself been sold as a slave, took on a second life on the page. The very disaster that scattered the Yoruba and gathered their refugees in one colony created the conditions in which their language was first written, standardized, and preserved in a form that would outlast every slaver’s ledger."}, {"id": "diaspora-linguistics-of-loss#s11", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "So the language lost much. Do not let me pretend otherwise. Countless children of the diaspora grew up unable to speak a word of their ancestral tongue, and that is a real grief, a robbery that no printing press repairs. But the language also refused, in the deepest way, to die. It hid in the mouths of priests. It rode home in the memories of the freed. It climbed onto the page in the hand of a former captive. Words, my child, are harder to kill than kings."}, {"id": "diaspora-linguistics-of-loss#s12", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So remember this when you speak. Every time you greet an elder in the proper way, every time you say the name of an orisa, every time you let a proverb fall into your speech the way your grandmother did, you are holding one end of a thread that runs unbroken back through the centuries, across the water, into the holds of the ships, back to the towns that fell, back to the first mouths that shaped these sounds. The slavers took almost everything. They did not take the words. And as long as the words are spoken, the people who first spoke them are not entirely gone. That is why you must speak your language, and teach it, and be proud of it. It is the one inheritance the sea gave back."}]}, {"id": "diaspora-baptism-and-resistance", "emoji": "🌍", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#001520,#003550)", "category": "diaspora", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "8 min", "title": {"en": "Baptism and Resistance: When the Old Gods Wore New Names", "yo": "Baptism and Resistance: When the Old Gods Wore New Names"}, "desc": {"en": "How the enslaved kept the orisa alive by teaching them to answer to the names of Catholic saints.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Diaspora", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Igbagbo, consolidates “Baptism and Resistance” and “The Saints Who Were Orisa”)", "segments": [{"id": "diaspora-baptism-and-resistance#s1", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Sit, and let me tell you how our people kept their gods alive by teaching them to answer to new names, and how the faith that the slavers meant as a chain became, in the hands of the enslaved, a key. This is a story about cleverness of the deepest kind, the cleverness of the spirit that will not be told what to worship, and it happened wherever our people were carried, but nowhere more beautifully than across the water in the lands of the Catholic masters."}, {"id": "diaspora-baptism-and-resistance#s2", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Understand the situation the captives faced on the far shore. The masters did not only own their bodies. They wished to own their souls as well, to make them forget the orisa and worship the god of the church, partly out of true belief and partly because a people who share your religion are easier to rule. In many places it was forbidden to worship the old gods. The drums were banned, or watched. To be caught calling on Sango or Yemoja could bring the whip. The whole weight of the master’s world pressed down and said, your gods are dead, worship ours."}, {"id": "diaspora-baptism-and-resistance#s3", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Now a lesser people might have simply obeyed, or simply refused and been broken. But our people did something far cleverer. They looked at the religion of the masters, this Catholic faith with its one high God and its crowd of saints, each saint a holy figure with his own colors, his own day, his own powers, his own pictures on the church walls. And the Yoruba, who knew the orisa each with their own colors and days and powers, looked at these saints and saw, hidden in plain sight, a place to keep their own gods safe."}, {"id": "diaspora-baptism-and-resistance#s4", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "So they made a bargain in the secret of their hearts. When the master saw them kneeling before the image of Saint Barbara, with her red cloak and her sword and her association with lightning and storm, the master saw a good Christian slave worshipping a Christian saint. But the worshipper saw Sango, lord of thunder and lightning, whose color is red, whose power is the storm. When they knelt before Our Lady in her blue robe by the water, the master saw the mother of the Christian god. The worshipper saw Yemoja, mother of waters, whose color is blue, whose home is the sea. Saint Lazarus with his sores became Babaluaye, lord of sickness and healing. The warrior saints became Ogun. Behind every permitted saint stood a forbidden orisa, and the master, looking on, saw only his own religion and was content."}, {"id": "diaspora-baptism-and-resistance#s5", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "Think of the genius of that, and the patience. It was not a betrayal of the old gods. It was their smuggling, their hiding, their preservation through a time when to worship them openly was death. The orisa put on the masks of the saints and walked freely through the master’s own church, and the master held the door open for them and never knew. This is how the religion the slavers meant to erase our faith with became instead the very costume in which our faith survived."}, {"id": "diaspora-baptism-and-resistance#s6", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "Out of this hiding grew whole new living faiths, and they are among the great treasures of our people scattered across the world. In Cuba they call it Santeria, the way of the saints, or by its truer name Lucumi, and its priests still sing to the orisa in words carried from Yorubaland. In Brazil it is Candomble, where the orisa are called by the old names and dance in the bodies of their devotees at ceremonies that would be familiar to any elder in Oyo. In Haiti the traditions mingled with others into Vodou, where among the spirits you will find figures whose roots run back to our gods. In Trinidad, in Puerto Rico, wherever our people were carried, the orisa found a way to live on, wearing whatever mask the times required."}, {"id": "diaspora-baptism-and-resistance#s7", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "And here is a thing to marvel at. These faiths did not merely survive. They grew strong, they grew beautiful, they grew large. Today there are millions of people across the Americas, of every color, who worship the orisa of the Yoruba, who know the names of Sango and Osun and Obatala, who drum and dance and divine in ways born on this very soil. A religion that the slavers tried to stamp out in the holds of their ships is now practiced by more people, in more countries, than ever knew it in the old days. The attempt to kill our gods only scattered their seeds across a hemisphere, and everywhere the seeds fell, they grew."}, {"id": "diaspora-baptism-and-resistance#s8", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "Now I will not tell you it was simple, or that nothing was lost. Much was lost. Many of our people did become Christian in full heart and forgot the orisa entirely, and that too is part of the story and not for me to judge. Faith is a road each soul walks in its own way. But the point I want you to carry is this: that even the soul, even the gods, even the innermost room of a person that the master most wished to conquer, our people found a way to keep free. They bowed their heads to the saints and prayed to the orisa. They were told what to worship and they quietly worshipped what they chose. There is no deeper freedom than that."}, {"id": "diaspora-baptism-and-resistance#s9", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "And do not think this was only a matter of hiding. It was also resistance, real and dangerous. Where our people gathered to drum for the orisa, even under the mask of a saint’s feast day, they gathered as a community, they remembered they were a people, they passed courage from hand to hand. Many a revolt on many an island was planned in the shelter of a religious gathering, blessed by a priest of the old gods, carried out by people who believed the orisa were with them. The faith that hid in the saints also armed the hand that rose against the master. Sango is a god of thunder, after all, and thunder does not only hide. Sometimes it strikes."}, {"id": "diaspora-baptism-and-resistance#s10", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So when you see, in some far country, a person of any color kneeling before a shrine of the orisa, drumming the old rhythms, calling the old names, know what you are seeing. You are seeing a victory three hundred years in the making. You are seeing gods who were sentenced to death by the whole power of empires, and who survived by wearing the empire’s own saints as a disguise, and who walked out the far side of slavery still alive, still answering, still dancing. Our gods went into the darkness with our people. And like our people, they came out the other side."}]}, {"id": "diaspora-children-of-two-worlds", "emoji": "🌍", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#001520,#003550)", "category": "diaspora", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "6 min", "title": {"en": "Children of Two Worlds: The Ones Born Between", "yo": "Children of Two Worlds: The Ones Born Between"}, "desc": {"en": "The children born between worlds, on the coast, in the plantations, and among the returnees, who became bridges.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Diaspora", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Omo Aye Meji, consolidates “Children of Two Worlds”)", "segments": [{"id": "diaspora-children-of-two-worlds#s1", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Come, and I will tell you of the children who were born between two worlds, belonging fully to neither, and how some of them turned that hard birthright into a bridge that carried our people across a terrible age. It is a gentler story than some I have told, but do not mistake gentle for small, for the ones born between often carried burdens the pure of any world never had to carry."}, {"id": "diaspora-children-of-two-worlds#s2", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "In the long years of the trade, wherever the coast met the ships, wherever African and European lived close, children were born who had a foot in each world. On the coast of Yorubaland and its neighbors there grew up families of mixed blood, the children of European traders and African women, some born of true partnership and some of far darker things. These children often spoke both tongues from the cradle. They knew the orisa from their mother’s people and the church from their father’s. They understood the market of the Africans and the accounts of the Europeans. And the world did not know quite what to do with them."}, {"id": "diaspora-children-of-two-worlds#s3", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "And there were other children of two worlds, born not on the coast but across the water. In the Americas, in the great houses and the fields, children were born of the union of enslaver and enslaved, most often born of the master’s power over a woman who could not refuse. These children carried the blood of both their parents and the status of only one, for by the cruel law of those lands the child of an enslaved mother was enslaved, no matter whose blood ran in its veins. So a man might own his own children, might see his own face in a child he treated as property, and this was one of the deepest rots at the heart of the whole system, that it turned fathers into masters and children into merchandise."}, {"id": "diaspora-children-of-two-worlds#s4", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "These children of the great house often lived a life of terrible in betweenness. Lighter of skin than the field hands, they were sometimes given easier work, in the house rather than the fields, and sometimes taught to read, and this set them apart from those they lived among. Yet they were still enslaved, still property, still subject to sale, and the white family whose blood they shared would rarely acknowledge them. They belonged fully nowhere. You have already heard of one such, Ellen Craft, so light she could pass for white, who used that very in betweenness, that painful accident of her birth, as the disguise that carried her and her husband out of bondage. Her story shows how the burden of the between could, in a bold heart, be turned into a weapon."}, {"id": "diaspora-children-of-two-worlds#s5", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "And across the water in the lands of the Spanish and the Portuguese, whole new peoples grew up of mixed African and European and sometimes native blood, and out of that mixing came new cultures, new musics, new ways of cooking and speaking and worshipping, in which the Yoruba strand was woven together with many others into something that had never existed in the world before. The child of two worlds, denied a clean place in either, became the maker of a third."}, {"id": "diaspora-children-of-two-worlds#s6", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "And think of who else was a child of two worlds, in a different way. The freed captives who came home from Sierra Leone and Brazil, the Saro and the Aguda, were children of two worlds too, though they had been born in one. They had left as Yoruba and returned carrying English or Portuguese, letters and new trades and new faiths, and when they came back to Abeokuta and Lagos they belonged to the homeland and yet stood a little apart from it, marked by all they had seen across the water. Samuel Ajayi Crowther, who could preach in English and translate the Bible into Yoruba and speak with a bishop in London and an elder in his home town alike, was a child of two worlds who used his double belonging to build bridges no purely one worlded person could have built."}, {"id": "diaspora-children-of-two-worlds#s7", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "That is the gift hidden inside the hard birthright. The one who belongs fully to a single world sees only that world. But the one born between, who has had to learn two tongues, two faiths, two ways of seeing, can stand in the gap and carry meaning across it. Every translator, every peacemaker, every person who has explained one people to another, has been in some sense a child of two worlds. In an age when the peoples of the earth were being thrown together by force and by trade, often with terrible violence, it was often these in between ones who found the words to make sense of the collision, who kept the human thread running through the chaos."}, {"id": "diaspora-children-of-two-worlds#s8", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So if you are ever made to feel that you belong nowhere fully, that you are too much of one thing to be purely another, too mixed, too between, remember the children of two worlds who came before you. Remember that the coast where our people met the wider world for good and for ill was a place of terrible pain, but also the birthplace of translators and bridge builders and whole new peoples. Remember Ellen Craft, who made her in betweenness into the very road to freedom. Remember Crowther, who turned his double belonging into a book that gave a whole nation its written tongue. The world may not know where to put the one born between. But it is very often the one born between who shows the world the way across."}]}, {"id": "diaspora-orisa-crossed-the-water", "emoji": "🌍", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#001520,#003550)", "category": "diaspora", "rating": 9.7, "duration": "13 min", "title": {"en": "The Orisa Crossed the Water: How the Gods Survived the Middle Passage", "yo": "The Orisa Crossed the Water: How the Gods Survived the Middle Passage"}, "desc": {"en": "How the orisa were carried in chains to Brazil, Cuba, Haiti, and Trinidad and survived the Middle Passage.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Diaspora", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Ilu Okeere, consolidates the tellings of the Yoruba diaspora across the Atlantic, the story of how the orisa were carried in chains to Brazil, Cuba, Trinidad, and Haiti, and lived on in secret to become the faith of millions)", "segments": [{"id": "diaspora-orisa-crossed-the-water#s1", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Last night, my child, I told you of the Saro, the children who came home. Tonight I must tell you of the far greater number who did not come home, the countless ones carried across the great water who never returned, and of the astonishing, the almost unbelievable thing they did in their exile. For this is a story that should fill you with wonder and with a fierce pride, my child: it is the story of how a people in chains, stripped of everything, forbidden their language and their names and their gods, nonetheless carried the orisa across the ocean hidden in their hearts, and kept them alive in secret through generations of slavery, so that today, across the whole of the Americas, millions upon millions of people worship the very orisa I have been telling you of these many nights. The gods crossed the water, my child. Slavery could not kill them."}, {"id": "diaspora-orisa-crossed-the-water#s2", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "You must understand first the enormity of what was done. Over the long centuries of the slave trade, my child, vast numbers of our people, and of many other African peoples, were captured and carried across the Atlantic in the ships, packed into the holds in conditions of such horror that I will not describe them to you fully tonight, chained in the dark, many dying on the terrible voyage that is called the Middle Passage, the crossing of the great water into slavery. And those who survived that crossing were sold, in Brazil, in Cuba, in the islands of the Caribbean, in Trinidad and Haiti and beyond, into a life of bondage and forced labour, and everything was taken from them: their freedom, their families, their homes, their names, and, so their masters intended, their gods and their whole way of understanding the world."}, {"id": "diaspora-orisa-crossed-the-water#s3", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "For the masters and the authorities did not permit the enslaved Africans to worship the orisa. They were forced to take the religion of their masters, forced into the churches of the Catholic faith in the lands ruled by the Portuguese and the Spanish, baptized with new names, forbidden their drums in many places, forbidden their gatherings, punished, sometimes terribly, for keeping the old ways. Everything was arranged to erase the orisa from the memory of the stolen people and to replace them with the religion of those who held them in chains."}, {"id": "diaspora-orisa-crossed-the-water#s4", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "But here, my child, is where the wonder begins, and where you must sit up and marvel at the genius and the courage and the unbreakable spirit of your people. The enslaved Yoruba did not give up their gods. They could not worship them openly, so they hid them. And the way they hid them is one of the most brilliant acts of spiritual survival in all the history of humankind. They looked at the Catholic saints their masters worshipped, the many saints with their many pictures and statues and feast days, and they saw a way. They matched each orisa to a saint. Behind the image of a Catholic saint, they hid the face of an orisa. When they knelt before the statue of a saint, praying aloud in the words their masters permitted, they were in truth worshipping the orisa concealed behind that saint’s face, and their masters, seeing only devotion to the saints, were deceived, and let them be."}, {"id": "diaspora-orisa-crossed-the-water#s5", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Think of the brilliance of it, my child. Sango, the thunder king, the lord of fire and lightning and justice, was hidden behind a saint associated with such things. Oshun, the sweet orisa of the river and of love and gold, was hidden behind an image of a lady associated with rivers and with copper and gold. Yemoja, the great mother of the waters, was hidden behind an image of a lady of the sea. Obatala, the cool white one, the shaper of heads, was hidden behind a white-robed figure. Esu, the keeper of the crossroads, was matched to his own figure. Behind every saint the masters permitted, an orisa lived on in secret, and the worship never died. The enslaved bowed to the saints with their mouths and worshipped the orisa with their hearts, and in this way, my child, in this cunning and courageous and faithful deception, the gods of your people survived the very slavery that was meant to destroy them."}, {"id": "diaspora-orisa-crossed-the-water#s6", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "And the faiths that grew from this survival, my child, are living and mighty to this day, worshipped by millions. In Cuba it is called Santeria, or Lucumi, the way of the saints, though the saints, remember, are the orisa in disguise, and the very word Lucumi comes from a Yoruba greeting, oluku mi, my friend. In Brazil it is called Candomble, where the orisa, called the orixas, are worshipped in great and beautiful ceremonies, and the Yoruba language itself, called Nago there, is preserved in the sacred songs, sung by people who may no longer speak it in daily life but who keep the holy words of the orisa alive across the centuries and the ocean. In Trinidad there is the worship they call Shango, after the thunder king himself, so central is he there that the whole faith bears his name. In Haiti, woven into the faith called Vodou, the spirits called the lwa carry much of the old African religion, with strong Yoruba threads among them. Across the whole of the Americas, my child, wherever the stolen people were carried, the orisa took root and lived and grew."}, {"id": "diaspora-orisa-crossed-the-water#s7", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "Let me make you feel the fullness of this marvel. It means, my child, that when our people were carried across the water in the deepest sorrow, stripped of everything, they carried with them the one thing that could not be taken by any chain or any whip or any master: the memory of their gods, and the will to keep them. And that memory and that will proved stronger than all the power arrayed against them. Today, in Havana and in Bahia and in Port of Spain and in cities across the Americas and now across the whole world, there are millions of people, of every colour, who worship Sango and Oshun and Yemoja and Obatala and Esu and Ogun, who drum for them and dance for them and are mounted by them in possession, who keep the festivals and speak the sacred Yoruba words, who trace their spiritual lineage back across the ocean to Yorubaland, to Ife, to the source. The orisa did not merely survive slavery, my child. They conquered it. They outlived the whole cruel system meant to destroy them, and they spread, and they flourished, and they became a world religion, carried first in the hearts of people in chains."}, {"id": "diaspora-orisa-crossed-the-water#s8", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Is there a prouder thing in all our long story, my child? A people so stripped of power that they owned not even their own bodies, and yet so unbreakable in spirit that they smuggled their entire religion across the ocean hidden behind the very saints of their oppressors, and kept it alive in secret for three hundred years, and passed it down through generations who had never seen Africa, and built of it a mighty living faith worshipped by millions today. That is the triumph of the human spirit over the very worst that other humans could do to it. That is the unbreakable thread that ties a devotee dancing for Sango in Bahia today back across three centuries and four thousand miles of ocean to the thunder king of old Oyo. The chain of slavery could not cut the thread of the orisa. It stretched across the whole Atlantic, and it held."}, {"id": "diaspora-orisa-crossed-the-water#s9", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "And now, my child, in our own age, that thread runs both ways, and this is the most beautiful turning of all. For just as the Saro came home to Yorubaland in the body, so now the orisa are coming home in the spirit, as the descendants of the stolen people, free now, seek out their roots, cross the water back to Yorubaland, come to Ife the source, come to Osogbo for the festival of Oshun, seeking the origin of the faith their ancestors kept alive in chains. The children of the diaspora are coming home, my child, not as slaves and not even as returnees to stay, but as pilgrims, seeking the source, reconnecting the far-scattered branches to the ancient root. And the worship flows back and forth across the ocean now, Yorubaland to the Americas and the Americas to Yorubaland, one people, one faith, divided by an ocean and a terrible history but bound by the orisa who crossed the water and never died."}, {"id": "diaspora-orisa-crossed-the-water#s10", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "I will be honest with you, my child, as always, about the sorrow that underlies all this wonder. The survival of the orisa across the water was a triumph, but it was a triumph wrung from an atrocity, and we must never let the marvel of the survival make us forget the horror of the crossing that made it necessary. Millions suffered and died. Families were destroyed forever. A wound was opened in our people and in all the African peoples that has never fully healed and perhaps never will. The triumph of the orisa’s survival does not cancel the crime of the slavery that they survived, and I will speak to you of that crime more fully and more directly another night, for it deserves its own telling and its own mourning. Tonight I tell you of the survival and the wonder, but I do not let you forget the sorrow beneath it. Both are true. The deepest triumph of the human spirit and the deepest crime against it are woven together in this single story, and a wise heart must hold them both."}, {"id": "diaspora-orisa-crossed-the-water#s11", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "But let the wonder be the note we end on tonight, my child, for it is a note of unbreakable hope. Remember, whenever you feel powerless, whenever you feel that some force is too great to resist, that everything you value might be stripped away, remember the enslaved Yoruba who owned nothing, not even themselves, and who yet kept their gods alive behind the faces of their oppressors’ saints, and passed them down through three centuries of bondage, and gave them to the world. There is a power in the human spirit, my child, in memory and faith and stubborn love for what is one’s own, that no chain can bind and no master can break and no ocean can drown. The orisa crossed the water in the hearts of people in chains, and they live today in the millions. Nothing that a people truly refuses to forget can ever be finally taken from them. That is the teaching of the diaspora, and it is one of the mightiest teachings in all our store."}, {"id": "diaspora-orisa-crossed-the-water#s12", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "Tomorrow, if the ancestors are willing, we will speak of the sorrow itself more directly, of the great wound, of the slave trade and how it came upon us and what it did, for you are old enough now to hear it fully and to mourn it rightly. But tonight belongs to the orisa who crossed the water, to the gods that slavery could not kill, and we will sleep in wonder at the unbreakable spirit of your people, whose gods live today on every shore the stolen children were carried to."}]}, {"id": "diaspora-name-that-survived", "emoji": "🌍", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#001520,#003550)", "category": "diaspora", "rating": 9.6, "duration": "12 min", "title": {"en": "The Name That Survived: Yoruba Identity Across the World", "yo": "The Name That Survived: Yoruba Identity Across the World"}, "desc": {"en": "How Yoruba names and identity endured from the plantations to the cities of Britain and America.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Diaspora", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Ilu Okeere, the story of how Yoruba names, blood, and spirit endured across the diaspora, from the plantations to the great cities of Britain and America, and how the scattered children are finding their way back)", "segments": [{"id": "diaspora-name-that-survived#s1", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Tonight, my child, before I turn to the great sorrow itself, I want to tell you one more story of the scattered children, a gentler one, a story of how a people endures not only in its gods but in its very names, its blood, its ways, its unquenchable sense of who it is, even when everything has been done to make it forget. For I have told you of the Saro who came home and of the orisa who crossed the water. Tonight I tell you of the long, patient survival of Yoruba identity itself, scattered across the whole world, and of how, in our own age, that scattered identity is gathering itself back together."}, {"id": "diaspora-name-that-survived#s2", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Begin with the names, my child, for names are powerful things among us, as you know. A Yoruba name is not a mere label. It carries meaning, it carries prayer, it carries the circumstances of a birth and the hopes of a family, it declares something true about the one who bears it. And when our people were carried into slavery, one of the first things taken from them was their names. They were given the names of their masters, or new names chosen for them, in the languages of Portugal and Spain and England, and their own true names, with all their meaning and their prayer and their memory, were meant to be erased forever."}, {"id": "diaspora-name-that-survived#s3", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "And yet, my child, the names did not entirely die. In some places, threads of the old naming survived, hidden, whispered, kept in secret within families, passed down as a precious and dangerous inheritance. And in our own time, as the descendants of the stolen people have sought their roots, many have reclaimed Yoruba names, taken them up again with pride, given them to their children, so that in cities far across the world you will now find people of the diaspora bearing proud Yoruba names once more, names reclaimed across centuries and oceans, a people renaming itself back into its own identity. To take back a name that slavery tried to erase, my child, is a small and mighty act of defiance and of homecoming, and it is happening now, all over the world."}, {"id": "diaspora-name-that-survived#s4", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "And the blood endured, my child, and the spirit. For you cannot erase a people simply by taking their names and forbidding their gods. Something deeper survives, in the ways of cooking, in the rhythms of music, in the manner of dance, in the values passed from mother to child, in a way of carrying oneself, in a thousand small inheritances that pass down through the generations beneath the notice of any master. Across the Americas, the culture the stolen Africans carried and preserved and blended with what they found became the root of whole new cultures, and the Yoruba contribution to that root was immense, in the music, in the faith, in the food, in the very rhythms that would one day give the world so much of its music, my child, for the drum of the orisa echoes, in the end, in a great deal of the music the whole world now dances to."}, {"id": "diaspora-name-that-survived#s5", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Now let me tell you of the great cities, my child, for the scattering did not end with the plantations. In our own age and the age just before it, our people, the Yoruba, and their diaspora cousins, have travelled and settled across the whole world, to the great cities of Britain, to London and Manchester and beyond, to the cities of America, to every corner of the earth, some as students who stayed, some as workers, some as professionals, some as the descendants of the enslaved seeking new lives, so that today there is hardly a great city anywhere in the world without its community of Yoruba and of the wider diaspora, keeping the language, cooking the food, honouring the traditions, gathering for the naming ceremonies and the weddings and the festivals, raising children who are of two worlds at once."}, {"id": "diaspora-name-that-survived#s6", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "And these communities, my child, carry the identity forward in the new lands. In a city like London or Manchester or New York, far from Yorubaland, you will find the language still spoken in homes, the drums still sounding at celebrations, the proper naming ceremonies still performed for new babies on the eighth day, the elders still honoured, the food still cooked, the traditions of respect and of family still taught to children who are growing up as citizens of distant lands. The identity travels. It adapts, it blends, it changes, as all living things must, but it does not die. A Yoruba child born in Manchester today, my child, may grow up knowing the praise-names of their lineage, eating the food of their grandmothers, dancing to the rhythms of the orisa, bearing a proud Yoruba name, fully a child of the new land and fully a child of the old, carrying the ancient identity forward into a future our ancestors could never have imagined."}, {"id": "diaspora-name-that-survived#s7", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "And here is the beautiful thing that is happening in our own age, my child, the great gathering-back. For centuries the scattering ran only one way, outward, the children carried or driven away from the source, to the plantations, to the far cities, ever further from Yorubaland. But now the current has turned, and the scattered children are seeking their way back, not always in the body, but in the heart, in knowledge, in reconnection. The descendants of the enslaved are tracing their lineages, discovering through the new sciences of ancestry that their roots run back to Yorubaland, coming as pilgrims to Ife and Osogbo, learning the language, taking the names, being initiated into the worship of the orisa at the very source. The diaspora of the great cities keeps its ties to home, sends its children back to know their grandparents, builds bridges across the oceans. The scattered branches, my child, are reaching back toward the root, and the root is reaching out toward the branches, and a people torn apart across the whole world is, slowly, in our own time, knitting itself back together into one great family conscious of its unity across every ocean and every border."}, {"id": "diaspora-name-that-survived#s8", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Think of what that means, my child. A people was scattered as violently and as widely as any people in all of history, torn across oceans, stripped of names and gods and memory, dispersed to every corner of the earth. And yet here, in our own age, that same people is finding one another again, reclaiming the names, reconnecting the lineages, gathering back toward the source, discovering across all the centuries and all the oceans that they are one, children of Oduduwa, children of Ife, children of the orisa, wherever in the world they were carried or driven or born. The scattering has not had the final word, my child. The gathering-back is the final word, and it is happening now, and you are living in the age of it."}, {"id": "diaspora-name-that-survived#s9", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "There is a teaching in this, my child, and it is one of the deepest in all our store. It is that identity, true identity, a people’s real knowledge of who they are and where they come from, is one of the most durable things in all the world, more durable than empires, more durable than slavery, more durable than oceans and centuries and every force arrayed to destroy it. You may take a people’s freedom, their names, their gods, their homeland, everything, and still, if even a thread of the memory survives, if even a whisper of who they are is passed down, then one day, perhaps centuries later, that identity can gather itself back together and stand again. Nothing that a people truly refuses to forget can be finally destroyed. We have seen it in the orisa who crossed the water, we have seen it in the Saro who came home, and we see it now in the great gathering-back of the scattered children across the whole world."}, {"id": "diaspora-name-that-survived#s10", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "So carry your identity with pride and with care, my child, for it is a precious and a durable inheritance, bought at a terrible price by ancestors who refused to let it die even in chains. Know your names and what they mean. Know your lineage and its praise-songs. Know the orisa and the tales and the ways. Speak the language, keep the customs, honour the elders, cook the food, dance the dances. For in keeping these things you are not merely being sentimental about the past, my child; you are holding a thread that has survived slavery and scattering and centuries and oceans, and that binds you to a great family spread across the whole earth, and that no force in all the world can break so long as you refuse to let it go. That is the inheritance of the diaspora, and it is yours, wherever in the world you may ever find yourself."}, {"id": "diaspora-name-that-survived#s11", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "We have walked now, my child, through the whole story of the scattering and the survival, of the Saro who came home in the body, of the orisa who crossed the water in the spirit, and of the identity that endured in names and blood and culture across the whole world and is now gathering itself back together. It is a story of the deepest sorrow, yes, but told from the side of the triumph, the astonishing, unbreakable survival of a people who could not be erased. And that is how I wanted you to meet the diaspora first, my child, from the side of its triumph, so that your heart would be strong."}, {"id": "diaspora-name-that-survived#s12", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "But I have promised you, and I will keep the promise tomorrow, to turn at last and look directly at the sorrow itself, at the great wound, at the slave trade and how it came upon us and what it did, without the comfort of the triumph, so that you may mourn it rightly, for a wound that is not mourned is never truly healed. That will be a hard telling, my child, the hardest I will give you, and you are ready for it now, having first seen how the scattered children survived and endured and are coming home. But tonight belongs to the enduring identity, to the reclaimed names, to the great gathering-back, and we will sleep in the strength of knowing that we are one great family across the whole world, and that nothing could ever finally tear us apart."}]}, {"id": "diaspora-candomble-brazil", "emoji": "🌍", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#001520,#003550)", "category": "diaspora", "rating": 9.4, "duration": "5 min", "title": {"en": "Candomble: How Yoruba Became Brazil", "yo": "Nago"}, "desc": {"en": "Candomble and the Nago of Bahia: how Yoruba religion and culture became part of Brazil.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Diaspora", "secondary": [], "source_note": "", "segments": [{"id": "diaspora-candomble-brazil#s1", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Narrator opens: In a city called Salvador, on the northeastern coast of Brazil, there is a neighborhood called Pelourinho — the Pillory, named for the public whipping post that once stood at its center. Its colonial architecture is painted in blue and yellow and white and red. On certain nights, drums fill the streets. The rhythms are not Brazilian. They are Yoruba. The songs are in a language that is not Portuguese. It is Yoruba — or rather, a sacred variant of Yoruba that has been preserved in Brazil for three hundred years, used exclusively in the sacred houses of the Candomble."}, {"id": "diaspora-candomble-brazil#s2", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Candomble is one of the most living, most vibrant, most culturally rich religious traditions in the Americas. It emerged from the West African spiritual traditions — primarily Yoruba, but also including Fon/Ewe and Central African elements — that enslaved people preserved and transformed in Brazil. It is practiced by millions of Brazilians, in the northeast and in cities across the country. It has deeply influenced Brazilian culture, art, music, literature, and popular consciousness. The story of how Candomble came to be is the story of resistance. In Brazil, enslaved Africans were required to convert to Catholicism. They were baptized, given Christian names, required to attend Mass. The practice of African religions was forbidden. Enslaved people who were caught practicing African spiritual traditions were punished — sometimes brutally."}, {"id": "diaspora-candomble-brazil#s3", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "And yet they practiced. They found ways to maintain their religious life under the cover of Catholic practice. The Orisas were paired with Catholic saints — Sango became Saint Barbara (both associated with thunder and fire), Obatala became Our Lady of the Immaculate Conception (both associated with purity and white), Ogun became Saint George (both associated with iron and battle). On the outside, the enslaved appeared to be honoring the Catholic saints. On the inside, they were honoring the Orisas. This syncretism — the creative blending of two religious traditions — is sometimes misunderstood as simply disguise or deception. But it became something more: a genuinely synthetic religious tradition with its own theology, its own ritual sophistication, its own artistic forms. Candomble is not \"African religion pretending to be Catholicism.\" It is something that emerged from the encounter between African and European and indigenous American elements in a specific historical context — and it is extraordinary."}, {"id": "diaspora-candomble-brazil#s4", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "The centerpiece of Candomble practice is the terreiro — the sacred house where ceremonies take place. The terreiro is a community — a family, really, organized around the mae de santo (mother of saints) or pai de santo (father of saints), the initiated priest or priestess who serves as the spiritual head of the community. The ceremonies involve elaborate ritual preparations, sacred songs in Yoruba and in derived sacred languages, the preparation of ritual foods for the Orisas, and most dramatically, the possession of initiates by the Orisas themselves."}, {"id": "diaspora-candomble-brazil#s5", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "Possession — the state in which an Orisa takes over a human body, rides it, uses it to communicate with the community — is at the heart of Candomble practice. When someone is \"mounted\" by Sango, they dress in Sango's colors, move with Sango's energy, speak in ways associated with Sango's personality. The community receives the Orisa through the person — can ask questions, can receive guidance, can experience the presence of the divine in embodied form. The Yoruba-origin Candomble tradition is called the Ketu Nation — because many of the enslaved Yoruba who came to Bahia came from the kingdom of Ketu (in what is now the Republic of Benin), which was heavily raided by Dahomey in the late 18th and early 19th centuries. The sacred houses of the Ketu Nation preserve Yoruba liturgical language, Yoruba sacred songs, Yoruba Orisa names and personalities, with greater fidelity than perhaps any other element of the diaspora tradition."}, {"id": "diaspora-candomble-brazil#s6", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "The three most famous Candomble houses in Brazil — the Casa Branca, the Gantois, and the Ile Ase Opo Afonja — are living institutions that have been in continuous practice for over 150 years. They are recognized by UNESCO. They have been visited by ethnographers, artists, politicians, and spiritual seekers from around the world. They are, quite simply, among the most important institutions for the preservation of African cultural heritage anywhere on earth. Candomble. The Yoruba Orisas in Brazil. Three hundred years of survival. Of drums. Of white cloth and sacred foods and the ancient songs that crossed the ocean and put down roots in new soil. The roots are deep. The tree is alive."}]}, {"id": "diaspora-trinidad-and-haiti", "emoji": "🌍", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#001520,#003550)", "category": "diaspora", "rating": 9.4, "duration": "4 min", "title": {"en": "Yoruba in Trinidad and Haiti: The Shango and Vodou Connections", "yo": "Yoruba in Trinidad and Haiti: The Shango and Vodou Connections"}, "desc": {"en": "The Shango faith of Trinidad and the Yoruba threads in Haitian Vodou.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Diaspora", "secondary": [], "source_note": "", "segments": [{"id": "diaspora-trinidad-and-haiti#s1", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Narrator opens: The Yoruba diaspora did not flow only to Brazil and Cuba. It spread across the Caribbean — to Trinidad, to Jamaica, to Haiti, to Barbados, to Louisiana. Each destination produced its own synthesis of African and local traditions, its own distinctive spiritual and cultural forms. And in each of these destinations, the Yoruba presence is visible to those who know how to look. In Trinidad, the Yoruba-derived tradition is called Shango — after Sango, the thunder Orisa, who gave his name to the entire tradition in this island. Trinidadian Shango (also called the Orisha religion or Spiritual Baptist Orisha) is a living tradition that has been practiced continuously since the enslaved Yoruba arrived in Trinidad in the 18th and 19th centuries. The Orisas are honored with their Yoruba names — Ogun, Sango, Osun, Yemoja, Esu — in ceremonies that include drumming, possession, and the preparation of ritual foods."}, {"id": "diaspora-trinidad-and-haiti#s2", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Trinidadian Shango has also absorbed elements from Hinduism — reflecting the significant Indian indentured laborer community that came to Trinidad after emancipation — and from Catholicism, creating a uniquely Trinidadian spiritual synthesis that shows how religious traditions adapt to their environments without losing their core structures. In Haiti, the story is more complex. Haitian Vodou — the most widely known and most widely misrepresented of the African diaspora religious traditions — draws primarily from the Fon and Ewe traditions of Dahomey (modern Benin) rather than from Yoruba. The lwa (spirits) of Vodou include figures like Papa Legba (the crossroads keeper, equivalent to Esu), Ogou (equivalent to Ogun), and Baron Samedi (the lord of the dead, with some similarities to the Egungun tradition). These are Fon-Ewe in their primary lineage but show significant Yoruba influence in their formation."}, {"id": "diaspora-trinidad-and-haiti#s3", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "The distinction matters because the West African diaspora to the Caribbean was not a monolithic \"African\" experience — it was a collection of specific communities from specific places, each bringing specific traditions. The Yoruba influence in Haiti is real but secondary to the Fon-Ewe influence. The Yoruba influence in Trinidad, in Cuba, in Brazil is primary. Understanding these distinctions helps us honor the specific cultural contributions of each West African people. What unites all these diaspora traditions — Candomble, Lukumi, Shango, Vodou — is the underlying African understanding of the divine. In each of these traditions, the gods are not distant, transcendent beings accessed only through prescribed ritual in specialized buildings. They are present. They can be invited. They can possess. They respond to music and food and dance and the quality of human attention. The boundary between the human and the divine is permeable, and the practice of religion is the cultivation of that permeability."}, {"id": "diaspora-trinidad-and-haiti#s4", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "This understanding of the divine — relational, immediate, embodied — is the great contribution of the African diaspora spiritual traditions to the world. It offers an alternative to both the austere transcendence of much Western religious practice and the arid materialism of much secular modernity. The Orisas crossed the ocean. They are still here. They are still answering."}]}, {"id": "diaspora-yoruba-in-the-us", "emoji": "🌍", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#001520,#003550)", "category": "diaspora", "rating": 9.4, "duration": "4 min", "title": {"en": "The Yoruba in the United States: The Return of the Tradition", "yo": "The Yoruba in the United States: The Return of the Tradition"}, "desc": {"en": "The return of Yoruba religious tradition among African Americans in the United States.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Diaspora", "secondary": [], "source_note": "", "segments": [{"id": "diaspora-yoruba-in-the-us#s1", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Narrator opens: For most of the twentieth century, the Yoruba spiritual tradition in the United States existed primarily within Cuban American communities — transmitted from Cuba to New York, Miami, and other diaspora hubs as Santeria/Lucumi practitioners migrated from the island and brought their Orisa with them. African Americans who encountered the tradition in these settings sometimes recognized something in it — a resonance with an Africa they had been severed from — but the tradition was mediated through Spanish, through Cuban cultural forms, through the specific hybrid that Cuba had created over four hundred years."}, {"id": "diaspora-yoruba-in-the-us#s2", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Beginning in the 1970s and accelerating through the 1980s and 1990s, something new happened: African Americans began to seek the tradition directly. They began traveling to Nigeria, to Brazil, to Cuba — seeking initiation from Yoruba-tradition priests, seeking the Ifa corpus in its most undiluted available form, seeking a spiritual connection to Africa that was not filtered through the European colonialism that had defined most of what Black America knew about the continent."}, {"id": "diaspora-yoruba-in-the-us#s3", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "The result was the growth of an African American Orisa and Ifa community that is now genuinely significant — with thousands of practitioners, with a growing number of Babalawo and Iyanifa (female Ifa priests, a role that is itself contested and expanding), with terreiros and ile (Orisa houses) in cities across the United States, with academic scholars studying the tradition from the inside, and with a political identity that links Yoruba spiritual practice to Black consciousness, to reparations conversations, to the question of what healing looks like for a community that carries four hundred years of cultural rupture."}, {"id": "diaspora-yoruba-in-the-us#s4", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Chief Fama (Iyanifa Luisah Teish), Oba Oseijeman Adefunmi (who founded Oyotunji Village in South Carolina — the first Yoruba traditional town in the United States), Baba Medahochi, and others built the bridges that made this possible. Oyotunji Village, founded in 1970 in the South Carolina Low Country, is a remarkable assertion: that a Yoruba-tradition community could exist in America, on American soil, practicing traditional Yoruba governance and religion, in the descendants of the people who were brought from that tradition in chains. The Yoruba in America are not a remnant. They are a return. They are people who, four centuries after the forced severance, found their way back to the names and the rhythms and the Orisa that were their inheritance. The path was long and costly and indirect. But the tradition was still there, preserved in Cuba and Brazil and Trinidad and Benin City and Osogbo, waiting for those who would come to claim it."}, {"id": "diaspora-yoruba-in-the-us#s5", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "The drums are playing in Brooklyn. The Ifa is being cast in Atlanta. The Osun festival is being celebrated in New York. The mother of waters is being called by name on the banks of the Harlem River. The return is not complete. It may never be complete. But it has begun. And that is enough."}]}, {"id": "diaspora-pierre-verger", "emoji": "🌍", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#001520,#003550)", "category": "diaspora", "rating": 9.4, "duration": "4 min", "title": {"en": "Pierre Verger: The French Photographer Who Became Babalawo", "yo": "Fatumbi"}, "desc": {"en": "Pierre Verger, the French photographer who became a babalawo and bridged Bahia and Yorubaland.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Diaspora", "secondary": [], "source_note": "", "segments": [{"id": "diaspora-pierre-verger#s1", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Narrator opens: Sometimes the most important bridge between two worlds is built by someone who belongs, by birth, to neither. Pierre Fatumbi Verger was born in Paris in 1902, the son of a French bourgeois family, trained as a photographer, and spent the first decades of his career taking pictures of people in exotic locations for magazines that paid him to make the world look interesting to European readers. He was nobody's idea of the person who would become a Babalawo — an initiated priest of the Ifa oracle — and one of the most important documenters of Yoruba-Brazilian cultural connections in the twentieth century."}, {"id": "diaspora-pierre-verger#s2", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "He arrived in Bahia in 1946, intending to pass through. He stayed for the rest of his life. He met the Candomble community. He met Mae Menininha of Gantois. He met the Orisa. And something happened to him that he spent the next fifty years trying to describe in photographs, in books, in the language of scholarship, and ultimately in the language of initiation itself. He began photographing the Candomble terreiros with a respectfulness and an intimacy that was unlike anything that had been done before. Previous photographs of African Brazilian religious practice were either hostile — documenting something to be suppressed — or voyeuristic — making the sacred into the exotic for European consumption. Verger's photographs were different. They were made from inside the community, by someone the community trusted, and they showed the ceremonies as what they were: acts of devotion, of community, of serious spiritual engagement."}, {"id": "diaspora-pierre-verger#s3", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "He then traveled to West Africa — to Dahomey, to Nigeria, to Yorubaland — to trace the connections between the Candomble Orisa and their West African counterparts. He walked from terreiro to terreiro in Bahia with photographs of Yoruba shrines and showed them to the priests and priestesses: do you recognize this? They did. The songs matched. The ritual objects matched. The Odu of the Ifa matched. He was documenting, in photographs and field notes, the extraordinary precision with which the Yoruba-Brazilian tradition had maintained its African sources across three centuries of the Middle Passage and its aftermath."}, {"id": "diaspora-pierre-verger#s4", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "His book \"Flux et Reflux\" — published in 1968, later translated as \"Trade Winds\" — documented the movement of people and culture between West Africa and Brazil across four centuries. It was a scholarly landmark: meticulous, beautifully illustrated with his own photographs, and making the case that the cultural connection between Brazil and West Africa was not sentimental heritage but documented historical reality. He was initiated as a Babalawo in 1953. He took the name Fatumbi — \"honored by Ifa.\" He was not playing at being Yoruba. He had been accepted, through the rigorous process of initiation, as a practitioner. This was controversial in some quarters — a white Frenchman claiming a Yoruba spiritual identity — but the community that initiated him made that decision, and Verger honored the commitment for the rest of his life."}, {"id": "diaspora-pierre-verger#s5", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "He died in Salvador in 1996, ninety-three years old. His photographic archive — over 70,000 photographs of African and African diaspora life — is held at the Pierre Verger Foundation in Salvador and constitutes one of the most important visual records of Black Atlantic culture in existence. He came to pass through. He stayed to belong. The road was long and the belonging was genuine. Some bridges are built by unexpected people."}]}, {"id": "diaspora-oyotunji-village", "emoji": "🌍", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#001520,#003550)", "category": "diaspora", "rating": 9.4, "duration": "4 min", "title": {"en": "Oyotunji Village: A Yoruba Kingdom in South Carolina", "yo": "Oyotunji"}, "desc": {"en": "Oyotunji, a Yoruba kingdom built in South Carolina.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Diaspora", "secondary": [], "source_note": "", "segments": [{"id": "diaspora-oyotunji-village#s1", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Narrator opens: In the summer of 1970, a group of African Americans drove to the South Carolina Low Country — to the swampy, Spanish-moss-draped landscape near the town of Sheldon — and began building a Yoruba kingdom. Not a heritage museum. Not a cultural center. A kingdom — with a traditional ruler, traditional governance structures, Yoruba language instruction, Orisa worship, and the explicit intention of recreating Yoruba civilization in America. Oyotunji African Village. Named for Oyo-tunji — \"Oyo rises again.\""}, {"id": "diaspora-oyotunji-village#s2", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "The founder was Oba Oseijeman Adefunmi I, born Walter Eugene King in Detroit in 1928. King had encountered the Santeria/Lucumi tradition in New York in the 1950s and was initiated into the tradition in Cuba — becoming one of the first African Americans to be initiated as a Yoruba-tradition priest. But he was dissatisfied with the Cuban form. The syncretism with Catholicism felt to him like another layer of imposed religion. He wanted the tradition without the Catholic overlay — Yoruba, direct, undiluted. He traveled to Nigeria. He learned. He connected with Ife and with Yoruba traditional authorities. He returned to America and in 1970 founded Oyotunji Village with the intention of creating a space where the Yoruba tradition could be practiced in its most direct form available to diaspora Africans."}, {"id": "diaspora-oyotunji-village#s3", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "The village grew. At its peak, it had a permanent population of several hundred people, living in compounds organized around traditional Yoruba lines, with an Oba (Adefunmi himself) as ruler, a council of chiefs, initiation ceremonies, Ifa consultation, festivals for the Orisa, instruction in Yoruba language and history. Children were born there and given Yoruba names and raised in the tradition. Adults came from across the United States to be initiated, to consult the oracle, to learn."}, {"id": "diaspora-oyotunji-village#s4", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Oyotunji represents the logical extreme of diaspora reclamation — the assertion that it is possible to rebuild, in America, a form of the civilization that was taken from enslaved Africans. The project has its contradictions: a man born Walter King, who grew up in Detroit's twentieth century, reimagining himself as an Oba in a reconstructed Yoruba kingdom in the American South. But the contradictions are part of the story's honesty. What does reclamation look like when the original was destroyed four hundred years ago? It looks like this — imperfect, determined, sometimes chaotic, always serious. Adefunmi died in 2005. His son Adejuyigbe Adefunmi II now rules the village. It continues. Smaller than at its peak, but still functioning, still initiating, still flying the green and black flag of Oyo."}, {"id": "diaspora-oyotunji-village#s5", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "In the same landscape where enslaved Africans worked rice fields for other people's profit, a Yoruba Oba sits on a throne. The Orisa are being called by name in the Low Country of South Carolina. The drums that were silenced in the holds of slave ships are being played again, by the descendants of those who carried them in their memories across the ocean. Oyo rises again. Even in South Carolina. Especially in South Carolina."}]}, {"id": "diaspora-american-music", "emoji": "🌍", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#001520,#003550)", "category": "diaspora", "rating": 9.4, "duration": "4 min", "title": {"en": "Yoruba Influence on American Music: From Blues to Hip Hop", "yo": "Yoruba Influence on American Music: From Blues to Hip Hop"}, "desc": {"en": "Yoruba rhythm in American music, from the blues to hip hop.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Diaspora", "secondary": [], "source_note": "", "segments": [{"id": "diaspora-american-music#s1", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Narrator opens: When Robert Johnson stood at the crossroads in Mississippi in the 1930s and made his legendary bargain with the figure who appeared there — the figure who taught him guitar in exchange for something he could not fully name — he was, in a specific theological sense, meeting Esu. The crossroads being and the blues musician. The trickster and the artist. The West African deity of communication and the African American music form built on the communication of pain and transcendence. The connection is not metaphorical. It runs through history."}, {"id": "diaspora-american-music#s2", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "The African Americans who created the blues were the descendants of enslaved people, many of them from West African origins that included the Yoruba world and its neighbors. The specific musical elements that characterize the blues — the call-and-response structure, the use of the voice as an instrument of emotional communication that cannot be fully notated, the relationship between rhythm and spiritual state, the concept of the \"blue note\" that falls between the formal notes of the European scale — these elements have traceable African origins in the musical traditions that survived the Middle Passage."}, {"id": "diaspora-american-music#s3", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "The Yoruba talking drum — the dundun, the bata — is built on the principle that the drum speaks: it uses tonal patterns to communicate words in the Yoruba language, functioning as a speech surrogate. When enslaved Africans in America were forbidden from communicating with drums (as they were in many colonial contexts, because the authorities correctly understood that the drums could coordinate rebellion), the communication moved into the voice and the body — into the blues moan, into the shout, into the field holler. The talking drum became the singing body. From the blues came jazz, from jazz came rhythm and blues, from R&B came rock and roll, from rock and soul came funk, from funk came hip hop. This is the genealogy of American popular music — the musical thread that runs from African shores through the Middle Passage and through four centuries of African American creativity into the dominant popular music forms of the contemporary world."}, {"id": "diaspora-american-music#s4", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "The Yoruba contribution to this genealogy is not direct quotation. You cannot point to a specific Yoruba song that became a specific blues song. The connection is structural — in the use of call and response, in the relationship between music and spiritual practice, in the concept of the musician as a griot-figure who carries the community's memory and speaks its truth, in the understanding that music is not entertainment separated from life but a mode of engaging with life's deepest realities. Every time a hip-hop artist starts their verse over a sample and their voice becomes the primary instrument of meaning — when the words and the rhythm together communicate more than either could alone — they are working in a tradition that reaches back, through many transformations, to the Yoruba griot who sat before the community and drummed and sang the history of the people."}, {"id": "diaspora-american-music#s5", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "The tradition has traveled. It has transformed beyond recognition. But the DNA is there, in the music, in the rhythm, in the relationship between the artist and the community that hears itself in what the artist makes."}]}, {"id": "diaspora-samba-remembered-sango", "emoji": "🌍", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#001520,#003550)", "category": "diaspora", "rating": 9.4, "duration": "4 min", "title": {"en": "The Samba That Remembered Sango", "yo": "The Samba That Remembered Sango"}, "desc": {"en": "How Sango's rhythms live on inside Brazilian samba.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Diaspora", "secondary": [], "source_note": "", "segments": [{"id": "diaspora-samba-remembered-sango#s1", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "If you know what to listen for, you can hear Sango in certain samba rhythms. Not in the melody — the melody has been Brazilian for a long time — but in the rhythm underneath, in the particular pattern of the drum that arrived on the ships and never left. It was beaten in the senzalas, the slave quarters of the sugar plantations. It was beaten at the candomblle ceremonies that the enslaved Yoruba and their descendants held in secret and then in the open. It was beaten at carnival, where it found a new audience and a new form while keeping its oldest heartbeat."}, {"id": "diaspora-samba-remembered-sango#s2", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "Music is the thing that crosses borders most completely. You can take a person's land, their name, their language, their freedom. You cannot take their capacity for rhythm. The body remembers what the mind has been made to forget. The drum calls and the body answers. This is not mysticism. This is neuroscience. This is also mysticism. The descendants of"}, {"id": "diaspora-samba-remembered-sango#s3", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "Yoruba people in Brazil, many of whom do not know the specific name of their ancestral homeland, feel the connection in the music before they can articulate it. They learn the articulation later, in Candomble ceremonies and community gatherings and the growing archive of research that traces the threads back. But the music was always there, already connected, already pulling."}]}, {"id": "diaspora-harlem", "emoji": "🌍", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#001520,#003550)", "category": "diaspora", "rating": 9.3, "duration": "4 min", "title": {"en": "Harlem and the Yoruba Connection", "yo": "Harlem and the Yoruba Connection"}, "desc": {"en": "Harlem and the Yoruba connection in Black American culture.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Diaspora", "secondary": [], "source_note": "", "segments": [{"id": "diaspora-harlem#s1", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "In 1959, a man named Walter King walked into a Candomble ceremony in Cuba and felt he had come home. He was African American, born in Detroit, a theatre artist with no direct knowledge of Yoruba culture. But something in the ceremony — the structure of it, the orisa, the particular way the sacred communicated through the body — reached past everything he thought he knew about"}, {"id": "diaspora-harlem#s2", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "himself and grabbed something older. He returned to the United States and founded the Yoruba Temple in Harlem. He took the name Oba Osijeman Adefunmi. He began to teach orisa worship not as a curiosity or an academic exercise but as a living religion for African Americans who were, in the midst of the civil rights movement, looking for a spiritual home that was entirely their own."}, {"id": "diaspora-harlem#s3", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "The movement he helped start grew into Oyotunji Village in South Carolina — a Yoruba-American village that still exists today. It is imperfect, as all human projects are. It is also remarkable: a community that reached back across the Atlantic, found its roots, and decided to live by them."}]}, {"id": "diaspora-faith-in-london", "emoji": "🌍", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#001520,#003550)", "category": "diaspora", "rating": 9.4, "duration": "4 min", "title": {"en": "Olódùmarè Knows My Name: Yoruba Faith in London", "yo": "Olódùmarè Knows My Name: Yoruba Faith in London"}, "desc": {"en": "Yoruba faith practised in London.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Diaspora", "secondary": [], "source_note": "", "segments": [{"id": "diaspora-faith-in-london#s1", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "In a flat in Peckham, South London, there is a small shrine in the corner of a bedroom. The woman who maintains it was born in Lagos, came to London at twenty-two for a degree in accounting, stayed for thirty years. She goes to church on Sunday mornings. She tends her shrine on Monday evenings. These two practices do not conflict for her, even if they would for others."}, {"id": "diaspora-faith-in-london#s2", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "The God she worships in church and the orisa she honours at home are not, in her understanding, in competition. They are different aspects of the same enormous mystery. This is the reality of Yoruba religious life in the diaspora: syncretic, adaptive, deeply personal, refusing to be sorted into categories that outsiders find more comfortable. Sociologists call it syncretism. The practitioners usually call it: just how we do it."}, {"id": "diaspora-faith-in-london#s3", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "Her shrine holds Oshun's offerings — honey, oranges, a small mirror, a brass figure her mother brought from Osogbo. Once a year she makes the journey back to Nigeria for the Osun-Osogbo festival. Once a year, Peckham and Osogbo are the same city in her heart, and the shrine in the bedroom corner pulses with something even she cannot fully name."}]}, {"id": "diaspora-brooklyn-ceremony", "emoji": "🌍", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#001520,#003550)", "category": "diaspora", "rating": 9.4, "duration": "4 min", "title": {"en": "An Orisa Ceremony in Brooklyn", "yo": "An Orisa Ceremony in Brooklyn"}, "desc": {"en": "An orisa ceremony in Brooklyn, New York.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Diaspora", "secondary": [], "source_note": "", "segments": [{"id": "diaspora-brooklyn-ceremony#s1", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "The apartment on Flatbush Avenue does not look like a sacred space from the outside. There is a buzzer system and a narrow staircase and the smell of someone cooking on the third floor. But inside the apartment at the top, the walls are hung with purple and white cloth, and the altar in the living room holds offerings that were placed there at dawn by hands that"}, {"id": "diaspora-brooklyn-ceremony#s2", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "have been doing this since childhood. Fourteen people arrive over the course of an hour. They come from Nigeria, Trinidad, Cuba, Brazil, Puerto Rico, and three different boroughs of New York City. Their Spanish and Portuguese and Yoruba and English move in and out of each other with the easy fluency of people who have never found that language boundaries and spiritual boundaries are in the same place."}, {"id": "diaspora-brooklyn-ceremony#s3", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "The ceremony begins. The drumming starts. Within twenty minutes, what was an apartment in Brooklyn has become something else — a place where a very old conversation between human beings and the sacred is being continued, as it has been continued, in one form or another, since before there was a name for it. The city outside is enormous and indifferent. Inside, something small and precise and necessary is happening."}]}, {"id": "diaspora-london-language-schools", "emoji": "🌍", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#001520,#003550)", "category": "diaspora", "rating": 9.4, "duration": "4 min", "title": {"en": "The Yoruba Language Schools of London", "yo": "The Yoruba Language Schools of London"}, "desc": {"en": "The Saturday schools keeping Yoruba alive for London-born children.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Diaspora", "secondary": [], "source_note": "", "segments": [{"id": "diaspora-london-language-schools#s1", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "On Saturday mornings in Tottenham and Woolwich and Lewisham, Yoruba children sit in community halls and church basements and learn the language their parents speak at home but that the rest of their world — school, television, the bus — has no space for. The teachers are volunteers, mostly mothers, sometimes fathers, occasionally grandparents who have retired to London from Nigeria and find that teaching is"}, {"id": "diaspora-london-language-schools#s2", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "still the best use of a Saturday morning. The children learn to read and write, but they also learn the things that language carries in its structure: the respect embedded in how you address elders, the specific warmth of certain greetings that have no English equivalent, the way humour works in Yoruba which is slightly different from how it works in English and requires a different timing."}, {"id": "diaspora-london-language-schools#s3", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "Their parents bring them to these classes because they understand something that is hard to explain to people who have never lost a language: once it is gone, you cannot get it back through nostalgia. You can only get it back through Saturday mornings. Through boring repetition. Through children who would rather be playing video games but sit and conjugate verbs anyway because someone who loves them has decided this matters."}]}, {"id": "diaspora-returning", "emoji": "🌍", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#001520,#003550)", "category": "diaspora", "rating": 9.4, "duration": "4 min", "title": {"en": "Returning: A Yoruba-American Goes Back", "yo": "Returning: A Yoruba-American Goes Back"}, "desc": {"en": "What it means for a Yoruba-American to go back to Yorubaland.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Diaspora", "secondary": [], "source_note": "", "segments": [{"id": "diaspora-returning#s1", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Adaeze Johnson — her mother was Yoruba, her father African-American from Baltimore — had lived in the United States for the entirety of her thirty-eight years. She had a hyphenated American identity that was already complicated enough without adding \"Yoruba\" to it. Growing up in Silver Spring, Maryland, with a name that teachers mispronounced and a mother who made moin-moin on weekends and spoke Yoruba on the phone to relatives in Abeokuta, she had learned to carry multiple things at once. The trip to Nigeria — her first — came in her late thirties. It came because her mother was aging and wanted to go back one more time, and wanted a companion, and Adaeze had run out of reasons to say not yet."}, {"id": "diaspora-returning#s2", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "Lagos was not what she had expected. She had absorbed, from American media and general anxiety, an image of Africa that had very little to do with the reality of a sprawling, chaotic, energetic megalopolis of twenty million people where everyone seemed to have somewhere urgent to be. The traffic was extraordinary. The food was extraordinary. The noise was extraordinary. And in the middle of it all, she had the persistent, disorienting sensation of recognition — not of specific places or people, but of something more atmospheric. The way families operated, the warmth of the hospitality, the particular combination of formality and informality in how people addressed each other. It felt, strangely, like something she had always known from somewhere she had never been."}, {"id": "diaspora-returning#s3", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "In Abeokuta, at her mother's family compound, she met relatives who greeted her with the ease of long familiarity — aunties who had heard about her for decades but never seen her, cousins who were immediately, unselfconsciously affectionate. An old woman, her mother's aunt, held Adaeze's face in both hands and looked at her for a long time and said something in Yoruba that her mother translated: \"She has the family forehead. They always come back.\" Adaeze cried in the car on the way back to Lagos. Not from sadness — she was done trying to explain those tears. She knew what they meant now. They meant something was whole that had been broken. Temporarily, incompletely, but whole enough."}]}, {"id": "diaspora-second-generation", "emoji": "🌍", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#001520,#003550)", "category": "diaspora", "rating": 9.3, "duration": "4 min", "title": {"en": "The Second Generation: Born Between", "yo": "The Second Generation: Born Between"}, "desc": {"en": "The second generation, born between Nigeria and the countries their parents moved to.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Diaspora", "secondary": [], "source_note": "", "segments": [{"id": "diaspora-second-generation#s1", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "If you were born in London to Yoruba parents, you have grown up between two stories about who you are. In one story you are British — you have the passport, you went to the schools, you know which football team is which and how to navigate the underground and what weather feels like when it is genuinely committed to being bad. In the other story you are Yoruba — you know the greetings, you know how to prostrate, you know which foods are made for which occasions, you know that certain things are not discussed in certain ways."}, {"id": "diaspora-second-generation#s2", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "Most second-generation Yoruba Britons do not experience this as a problem to be solved. They experience it as a particular kind of richness — a wider range of reference, a greater flexibility, a capacity to read rooms that people who have only one culture struggle to develop. You know how to code-switch. You know that identity is not fixed. You know that you can be"}, {"id": "diaspora-second-generation#s3", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "several things at once without being dishonest about any of them. This is the diaspora's quiet gift: it has produced people who are genuinely comfortable with complexity. In a world that desperately wants simple categories, these are the people who know that life doesn't actually sort that way, and who are building the bridges the rest of the world is still arguing about whether to build."}]}, {"id": "diaspora-lagos-boy-in-london", "emoji": "🌍", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#001520,#003550)", "category": "diaspora", "rating": 9.4, "duration": "4 min", "title": {"en": "The Lagos Boy in London: Navigating Two Cities at Once", "yo": "The Lagos Boy in London: Navigating Two Cities at Once"}, "desc": {"en": "A Lagos boy in London navigating two cities at once.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Diaspora", "secondary": [], "source_note": "", "segments": [{"id": "diaspora-lagos-boy-in-london#s1", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "He arrived in London at seventeen with two suitcases, an acceptance letter from a university in Birmingham, and a confident manner that he’d borrowed from his father and was slowly beginning to understand he would need to return, because it didn’t fit quite the same way on him as it had on the man who grew up speaking Yoruba in Mushin."}, {"id": "diaspora-lagos-boy-in-london#s2", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "The cold was the first thing. Lagos cold is a mild inconvenience. London cold in January is a different kind of thing entirely — not dangerous exactly, but persistent, and at first he found himself arguing with it internally as if it had made a choice to be unpleasant. The cold did not argue back. This was, in retrospect, an early lesson."}, {"id": "diaspora-lagos-boy-in-london#s3", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "What he carried that was genuinely his own: the ability to read a room quickly, something his mother had taught him by example in markets and at ceremonies and in the compound on Sunday afternoons when every relative had an opinion. The ability to be present in multiple conversations simultaneously. The capacity to wait without appearing to wait. These things served him in London as they had served him in Lagos. They were the portable parts of his formation, the things that crossed well."}]}, {"id": "diaspora-omo-wobe-generation", "emoji": "🌍", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#001520,#003550)", "category": "diaspora", "rating": 9.4, "duration": "4 min", "title": {"en": "Lagos to London: The Omo Wobe Generation", "yo": "Lagos to London: The Omo Wobe Generation"}, "desc": {"en": "The Omo Wobe generation moving between Lagos and London.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Diaspora", "secondary": [], "source_note": "", "segments": [{"id": "diaspora-omo-wobe-generation#s1", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "There is a generation of Yoruba-British young people who grew up in London — in Peckham, in Lewisham, in Tottenham, in Woolwich — who did not experience their culture as something preserved behind glass in a heritage museum. They experienced it as something alive, contested, creative, and cool. They were the children and grandchildren of the Yoruba migration wave of the 1980s and 1990s, and they grew up fluent in two registers: the English of their schools and streets, and the Yoruba-inflected Pidgin and home language of their parents' houses. They were, in many ways, the omo wobe — the children of the house — translated to an entirely different house. And they did what young people always do: they made it their own."}, {"id": "diaspora-omo-wobe-generation#s2", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "Afrobeats — the genre that conquered global music charts in the 2010s and 2020s — drew heavily from this generation. Artists who grew up between Lagos and London, between Yoruba and English, found that the combination was not a dilution but an amplification. The Yoruba tonal patterns that make the language almost musical already sat naturally on top of trap hi-hats and Afropop bass lines. The aso-oke and beaded jewellery appeared in music videos watched by hundreds of millions worldwide. Food was another front. A generation of young Yoruba cooks in London began doing something their parents had not done: they opened restaurants that didn't apologise. Not \"Nigerian food available here\" tucked in a corner of a West African grocery, but proper restaurants with good lighting and natural wine lists and egusi soup on the menu alongside a thoughtful paragraph explaining what egusi is and why you should eat it."}, {"id": "diaspora-omo-wobe-generation#s3", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "The culture did not disappear in London. It adapted, as it always has. The omo wobe generation took the thread their grandmothers had carried across water and wove it into something that their great-grandmothers would not have recognised but might, looking at it carefully, have loved."}]}, {"id": "diaspora-egbe-age-grades", "emoji": "🌍", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#001520,#003550)", "category": "diaspora", "rating": 9.4, "duration": "4 min", "title": {"en": "The Egbe: Yoruba Age-Grade Societies in the Diaspora", "yo": "Egbe"}, "desc": {"en": "Yoruba age-grade societies rebuilt in the diaspora.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Diaspora", "secondary": [], "source_note": "", "segments": [{"id": "diaspora-egbe-age-grades#s1", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "In traditional Yoruba communities, the egbe — the age-grade society, the peer group that moves through life’s milestones together — was one of the primary structures through which young people learned what it meant to belong to a community. The egbe had obligations: to help each other at ceremonies, to contribute to each other’s crisis moments, to celebrate and to mourn together. It was social"}, {"id": "diaspora-egbe-age-grades#s2", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "insurance before insurance existed. In the diaspora, the egbe has mutated but survived. Yoruba community associations in London, Houston, Toronto, and Sydney serve many of the same functions: mutual aid, cultural celebration, collective memory, and the particular comfort of being surrounded by people who know the same words to the same songs and who do not require you to explain who you are from the beginning."}, {"id": "diaspora-egbe-age-grades#s3", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "The challenge for diaspora egbe is membership renewal — how to bring in the second generation, who have grown up with one foot in Yoruba culture and one foot outside it, and who are making their own decisions about which affiliations are worth their limited time. The associations that succeed are the ones that offer something real: genuine community, genuine help in difficult moments, genuine joy in the celebrations. The ones that are merely obligatory don’t survive the second generation."}]}, {"id": "diaspora-beyonce-yoruba-thread", "emoji": "🌍", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#001520,#003550)", "category": "diaspora", "rating": 9.4, "duration": "4 min", "title": {"en": "Beyoncé and the Yoruba Thread: When Popular Culture Looks Back", "yo": "Beyoncé and the Yoruba Thread: When Popular Culture Looks Back"}, "desc": {"en": "When popular culture looks back: Yoruba imagery in Beyoncé's work.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Diaspora", "secondary": [], "source_note": "", "segments": [{"id": "diaspora-beyonce-yoruba-thread#s1", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "When Beyoncé released Lemonade in 2016, scholars of Yoruba culture immediately recognised the orisa. Oshun’s yellow dress, her river imagery, her association with feminine power and emotional truth. The film’s visual language drew explicitly on the Afro-diasporic traditions that trace back to Yorubaland, and the drawing was deliberate — a pop culture artist using her platform to say: this is where we come from, this"}, {"id": "diaspora-beyonce-yoruba-thread#s2", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "is what we carry, this is worth the reference. The conversation this sparked — about cultural appropriation, about diaspora reclamation, about who owns a spiritual tradition when it has been scattered across continents — was complicated and ongoing and largely healthy. It brought the orisa to the attention of millions of people who had never heard of them. Some of those people went looking for more."}, {"id": "diaspora-beyonce-yoruba-thread#s3", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "This is one of the ways living cultures sustain themselves in the modern world: through unexpected channels, through the platforms of people who were themselves formed by the tradition even if they didn’t always have the words for it. Oshun didn’t need Beyoncé’s endorsement. But she’s not ungrateful for the reach."}]}, {"id": "diaspora-port-harcourt-to-port-of-spain", "emoji": "🌍", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#001520,#003550)", "category": "diaspora", "rating": 9.4, "duration": "4 min", "title": {"en": "Port Harcourt to Port-of-Spain: The Long Connection", "yo": "Port Harcourt to Port-of-Spain: The Long Connection"}, "desc": {"en": "The long connection between Nigeria and Trinidad.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Diaspora", "secondary": [], "source_note": "", "segments": [{"id": "diaspora-port-harcourt-to-port-of-spain#s1", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "The connection between Yorubaland and Trinidad is two hundred years old and still being discovered. The enslaved Yoruba who arrived in Trinidad in the late eighteenth and early nineteenth centuries brought their orisa, their music, their food practices, and their particular understanding of what community meant. What they built there — the Shango Baptist tradition, the Yoruba-derived ceremonies that still happen in homes and temples across Trinidad —"}, {"id": "diaspora-port-harcourt-to-port-of-spain#s2", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "has survived colonialism, independence, urbanisation, and the global culture industry. In Port-of-Spain today, there are communities who know more about certain Yoruba ritual practices than many communities in Yorubaland itself, because the diaspora preserved things that the home community, disrupted by its own history of war and colonialism, let slip. The knowledge went across the ocean and was maintained there, while the source community was distracted by survival."}, {"id": "diaspora-port-harcourt-to-port-of-spain#s3", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "This is one of the paradoxes of diaspora: sometimes the branch keeps the seed that the root forgot. The Nigerian Yoruba who travel to Trinidad to learn ceremony are experiencing something strange and wonderful — going abroad to come home to something they didn’t know they’d lost."}]}, {"id": "diaspora-deji-in-minnesota", "emoji": "🌍", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#001520,#003550)", "category": "diaspora", "rating": 9.3, "duration": "4 min", "title": {"en": "Deji in Minnesota: A Yoruba Farmer in the Midwest", "yo": "Deji in Minnesota: A Yoruba Farmer in the Midwest"}, "desc": {"en": "A Yoruba farmer making a life in the American Midwest.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Diaspora", "secondary": [], "source_note": "", "segments": [{"id": "diaspora-deji-in-minnesota#s1", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "He had not planned to become a farmer. He had come to Minnesota for a graduate degree in agricultural science — planning to take the knowledge back to Osun State and improve his family’s land. But a professor offered him a research position, and the research position became a job, and the job became a life, and fifteen years later he was tending twelve acres outside of Rochester with his wife and their three children who called Minnesota home and considered Nigeria a very interesting place their father went to in December."}, {"id": "diaspora-deji-in-minnesota#s2", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "He grows Nigerian yams. This took years of experimentation — the Minnesota winter is not inclined to cooperate with tropical tubers — but he found varieties that could be coaxed into productivity in a greenhouse extended season, and he now supplies Yoruba restaurants in Minneapolis and Chicago and a small but loyal community of customers who drive considerable distances for yam that tastes like yam, not like the pale substitute"}, {"id": "diaspora-deji-in-minnesota#s3", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "that most American stores sell under that name. His children speak some Yoruba. Not as much as he would like. But they know how to prostrate before elders, they know the greetings, they know that the yam on the table has a story behind it that starts in Osun State and runs through a Minnesota greenhouse and is, in some way he cannot fully articulate, the same story the whole time."}]}, {"id": "diaspora-yoruba-food-diaspora", "emoji": "🌍", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#001520,#003550)", "category": "diaspora", "rating": 9.4, "duration": "4 min", "title": {"en": "Yoruba Food in the Diaspora: What Travels and What Stays", "yo": "Yoruba Food in the Diaspora: What Travels and What Stays"}, "desc": {"en": "Which Yoruba foods travel abroad and which stay home.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Diaspora", "secondary": [], "source_note": "", "segments": [{"id": "diaspora-yoruba-food-diaspora#s1", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Egusi soup travels. Jollof rice travels brilliantly, and then immediately gets into an argument with Ghanaian jollof rice about which is better (the Yoruba position is clear and correct and not worth elaborating here). Puff-puff travels. Akara travels. Suya travels, though it is different outside of the specific charcoal fire and the specific pepper blend and the specific time of evening when it is eaten in Ibadan. What does not travel:"}, {"id": "diaspora-yoruba-food-diaspora#s2", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "the specific smell of a Yoruba compound kitchen in the early morning. The sound of mortar and pestle before the radio is turned on. The particular quality of agbo — the herbal preparation your grandmother made for ailments she did not name because naming them would give them power — sitting in a bottle on the kitchen shelf. These are experiences, not recipes. They travel only in memory, and memory changes them."}, {"id": "diaspora-yoruba-food-diaspora#s3", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "Diaspora Yoruba cooks do not give up. They experiment with local ingredients. They source Nigerian spices by mail. They form WhatsApp groups where recipes are debated with the intensity usually reserved for politics. They feed their children the things their mothers fed them, because this is the edible version of saying: you come from somewhere, and that somewhere is delicious."}]}, {"id": "diaspora-manchester-community", "emoji": "🌍", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#001520,#003550)", "category": "diaspora", "rating": 9.4, "duration": "4 min", "title": {"en": "The Manchester Yoruba Community: Forty Years of Showing Up", "yo": "The Manchester Yoruba Community: Forty Years of Showing Up"}, "desc": {"en": "Forty years of the Yoruba community in Manchester.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Diaspora", "secondary": [], "source_note": "", "segments": [{"id": "diaspora-manchester-community#s1", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "The Yoruba community in Manchester, England, has been gathering for forty years. The gatherings started small — a few families who had come for work or study in the 1980s, meeting in someone’s sitting room because the Yoruba proverb says you do not eat alone if there are others nearby who are hungry. The community now has a formal association, a hall that it hires for large events,"}, {"id": "diaspora-manchester-community#s2", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "a welfare fund for members who fall on hard times, and an annual cultural day that has become one of the bigger Yoruba celebrations in the north of England. The older members remember the early days with a combination of fondness and mild horror — the cold, the culture shock, the difficulty of finding yam in the 1980s before globalisation made the African food shop a viable business model."}, {"id": "diaspora-manchester-community#s3", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "Their children grew up knowing the community as a fact of life — as natural and as assumed as school. Some of those children are now adults who bring their own children. The community is, quietly and without drama, in its second generation. This is the hardest transition any diaspora community makes. The Manchester Yoruba are making it. They show up every time."}]}, {"id": "orisa-obatala", "emoji": "⚡", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#150020,#350050)", "category": "orisa", "rating": 9.6, "duration": "11 min", "title": {"en": "Obatala: The Shaper of Heads", "yo": "Obatala"}, "desc": {"en": "The great white orisa, sculptor of humankind, whose single failing taught the world compassion.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Orisa", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Orisa, the story of the great white orisa, sculptor of humankind, keeper of purity, and the one whose single failing taught the world compassion)", "segments": [{"id": "orisa-obatala#s1", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Now we begin the greatest of all the tellings, my child, for we leave the small fires of the folktales and come to the great fire of the orisa, the shining ones, the powers that stood at the beginning of things. And it is right and proper that we begin with Obatala, for Obatala was there before almost anything, and the very shape of your own head, the seat of your destiny, came from his hands. Some call him Orisa-nla, the great orisa. Some call him the king of the white cloth. But by whatever name, he is the one to whom we owe our very forming, and so to him we owe the first tale."}, {"id": "orisa-obatala#s2", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "In the beginning, so the old ones say, there was only the sky above, where Olodumare the Almighty reigned with the orisa, and below there was only water, a wide and endless marsh, with no land at all, no place to set a foot, no field to plant, no town to build. And Olodumare looked down at all that water and decided that there should be solid ground, and creatures to walk upon it, and he chose Obatala for the work, for Obatala was the most trusted, the purest, the most careful of all the orisa."}, {"id": "orisa-obatala#s3", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Olodumare gave Obatala the materials of creation: a snail’s shell filled with loose earth, a white hen, a black cat for company, and a palm nut. And he told Obatala to descend from the sky on a long golden chain and to make the world. So Obatala set out, and this is the first thing you must know about him, that he was given the highest work of all, the making of the earth itself, because he was trusted above every other."}, {"id": "orisa-obatala#s4", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "But on the way down, the story tells us, Obatala grew thirsty, and he passed the palm trees, and he tapped them for wine, and he drank. And the palm wine, which is sweet and treacherous, went to his head, and he grew drowsy and careless. Hold onto this, my child, for it is the seed of the whole tale: even the greatest and purest of the orisa had a weakness, and his weakness was palm wine, and from that single weakness would come both a great sorrow and, in the end, a great mercy."}, {"id": "orisa-obatala#s5", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Now, in some tellings, because Obatala fell into a drunken sleep, it was his younger brother Oduduwa who took the shell of earth and descended and poured it upon the waters, and scattered it, and the white hen scratched and spread the earth until dry land appeared at a place called Ile-Ife, the source of the spreading, the navel of the Yoruba world. And this, my child, is why there is to this day a great matter between the people of Obatala and the people of Oduduwa over who truly made the land, and I will not settle it here, for it has not been settled in a thousand years and will not be settled by an old voice at a small fire. Let us only say that the land was made, one way or another, and that Obatala’s thirst had cost him something."}, {"id": "orisa-obatala#s6", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "But the making of the land was only half of Obatala’s work. The other half, and the one that no one disputes was his and his alone, was the shaping of human beings. For once there was dry land, Olodumare gave Obatala a second and even greater task: to mould the bodies of humankind out of clay, so that Olodumare himself might breathe life into them."}, {"id": "orisa-obatala#s7", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "And here Obatala did his true and holy work. He took the clay of the new earth, and with his own hands he shaped it into the forms of men and women, moulding the head with special care, for the head, the ori, is the seat of a person’s destiny and character, the most sacred part, the crown of the whole being. Day after day Obatala worked at his moulding table, shaping head after head, body after body, and each one he made with love and precision, and then Olodumare breathed the breath of life into them, and they lived, and walked, and the world filled with people, and every one of them, every single one, my child, including you, and me, and everyone you will ever meet, was shaped by the hands of Obatala. That is why we are called, in the old way, the moulded ones. We are his handiwork."}, {"id": "orisa-obatala#s8", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "But now comes the sorrow, and from the sorrow comes the deepest teaching of Obatala, so listen with your whole heart. There came a day when Obatala, at his moulding, once again drank palm wine. And once again it went to his head, and his careful hands grew unsteady, and on that day the beings he shaped came out not as the others had. Some he moulded with crooked limbs. Some with sightless eyes. Some with backs that would not straighten, or with forms the world would later call broken. He did not mean to. His heart had not changed; only his hands had faltered, dulled by the wine. And when Olodumare breathed life into these too, they lived, as fully alive and fully ensouled as any other, but different in their bodies from what Obatala had intended."}, {"id": "orisa-obatala#s9", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "When Obatala grew sober and saw what his unsteady hands had made, he was struck through with grief. He wept, for he loved every being he had shaped, and he had, without meaning to, given some of them a harder road to walk. And in that grief he made a vow, and the vow is the very heart of who Obatala is, so mark it well. He swore he would never again touch palm wine, not one drop, forever, and to this day his priests and his devotees drink no palm wine in his honour, and wear white, the white of purity and of the deep cleanness of a heart that has known its own failing and turned from it. That is the first part of the vow."}, {"id": "orisa-obatala#s10", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "But the second part is greater still. Obatala swore that from that day forward, all those whose bodies were shaped differently, the ones the world would call disabled, the blind, the lame, the ones born unlike the rest, would be his own special children. His sacred ones. Placed under his particular protection and care. He named them eni orisa, the people of the orisa, and he declared that to mock them, to scorn them, to harm them, was to mock and scorn and harm Obatala himself, the great orisa, the shaper of all heads."}, {"id": "orisa-obatala#s11", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Do you see, my child, what our ancestors did with this tale? Do you see the deep wisdom hidden in it? They took the hardest thing, the fact that some are born into harder bodies, and they did not explain it away and they did not pretend it was a punishment, for it was no one’s punishment. Instead they wove it into the very foundation of the world, and they declared that those born different are not cursed but chosen, not lesser but sacred, the special beloved children of the greatest orisa, under his direct and jealous protection. In a world that so often has been cruel to those it calls broken, our old ones built a story that made cruelty toward them a blasphemy against a god. That, my child, is what a great people do with a great sorrow. They turn it into a shield for the vulnerable."}, {"id": "orisa-obatala#s12", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "And so Obatala became known not only as the shaper of heads but as the patron of the pure of heart, the calm, the patient, the wise, and above all the protector of the weak and the different. He is cool where Sango is hot, patient where Ogun is fierce, white where the world is stained. When two people quarrel, it is Obatala’s coolness we invoke to settle them. When a mind is troubled, it is Obatala’s calm we pray for. He is the orisa of the clear head and the clean heart and the open hand held out to the one the world would push aside."}, {"id": "orisa-obatala#s13", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "There is a great deal more that could be told of Obatala, for the tales of the orisa are as many as the leaves of the forest. It is said that once he was imprisoned unjustly in a far kingdom, and that while he was wrongly held, that whole land suffered barrenness and drought, the rains refusing to fall and the women bearing no children, until at last the king discovered that he had been holding the pure one unjustly, and released him with great honour and apology, and the rains returned and the land bore again. And from that, my child, comes another teaching of Obatala: that where the innocent and the pure are unjustly held down, the whole land suffers, and cannot be well again until justice is done. Which is as true of kingdoms today as it was in the time of the orisa, and you may look about the world and see it for yourself."}, {"id": "orisa-obatala#s14", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So when you see someone wearing pure white cloth in devotion, or when you pass a person whose body walks a harder road than yours, remember Obatala. Remember that we are all the work of his hands, that the head upon your shoulders was moulded by him, and that he asks of us only what he learned the hard way himself: to keep a clear head, to guard against the sweet things that dull our care, to be gentle above all with those the world calls broken, and to know that purity is not the pride of one who has never failed, but the humility of one who has failed, and wept, and vowed to do better."}, {"id": "orisa-obatala#s15", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "That is Obatala, the great orisa, the shaper of heads, the father in white. Tomorrow, if the ancestors permit, I will tell you of a very different power, of iron and of fire, of the road and of the sword, of the orisa Ogun, who clears the path that all the others walk upon. But tonight belongs to the cool white one, and we will let his calm carry us into sleep."}]}, {"id": "orisa-ogun", "emoji": "⚡", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#150020,#350050)", "category": "orisa", "rating": 9.6, "duration": "12 min", "title": {"en": "Ogun: The Lord of Iron and the Opener of Roads", "yo": "Ogun"}, "desc": {"en": "The fierce orisa of iron and war who cleared the first road into the world.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Orisa", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Orisa, the story of the fierce orisa of iron, war, and the forge, who cleared the first path into the world and whose oath men still swear upon)", "segments": [{"id": "orisa-ogun#s1", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Come close to the fire, but not too close tonight, my child, for tonight we speak of the orisa of fire and iron, and it is well to keep a little distance from a power that hot. Yesterday I told you of Obatala, the cool white one, the shaper of heads. Tonight I tell you of his opposite and his brother, of Ogun, the lord of iron, the god of the forge and the sword and the road, the fierce one who goes first so that all the others may follow. For understand this from the very start: without Ogun, none of the other orisa could have come down into the world at all. He is the one who cleared the way."}, {"id": "orisa-ogun#s2", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "They say that in the far beginning, when the orisa first wished to come down from the sky to the newly made earth, they found the way blocked. Between the sky and the world lay a vast and tangled primordial bush, a wilderness so thick with thorn and vine and iron-hard wood that no blade of the other orisa could cut it. One by one the great ones tried. They took up tools of soft brass, of bronze, of gold and silver, the beautiful metals, and one by one their beautiful tools bent and broke and shattered against that terrible bush, and the way remained closed, and the orisa stood stranded, unable to reach the world that was waiting for them."}, {"id": "orisa-ogun#s3", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Then Ogun stepped forward. And Ogun alone possessed the secret of iron. Where the others had brought soft and shining things, Ogun brought the grey, unlovely, unbreakable metal, and he had forged from it a great cutlass, and with that iron blade he went first into the wilderness, and he cut. He cut and he cut, hewing a road through the impossible bush, sweating and bleeding and never stopping, until at last he had carved a clear path from the sky all the way down to the earth. And down that path, the path that Ogun made, all the other orisa walked in safety into the world."}, {"id": "orisa-ogun#s4", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Hold onto this image, my child, for it is the truest picture of Ogun there is: the one who goes first, into the hardest place, with the plainest tool, and by his own sweat and blood opens a road for everyone who comes after. That is why we call him the opener of roads. Every path you have ever walked, every road cut through the forest, every doorway cut through a wall, every way opened where there was no way, is Ogun’s work and Ogun’s gift."}, {"id": "orisa-ogun#s5", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "And because it was Ogun who cleared the way and did the hardest labour, the other orisa, in gratitude, offered to make him their king, to set the crown upon his head and let him rule over them all. But here is a strange and deep thing about Ogun, and it shows you that he is not simply a god of brute force. He refused. At first he refused the crown, for he was a worker, a maker, a doer, not one who wished to sit above others and be served. He preferred his forge to a throne. Only after much pleading did he finally accept a kingship, and even then he was never truly at ease as a ruler, for his heart was always in the work of his hands, in the ringing of the hammer on the hot iron, in the making of useful things."}, {"id": "orisa-ogun#s6", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "For that is Ogun’s domain, my child, and it is far wider than war, though people who do not know him think only of his fierceness. Ogun is the patron of every worker in iron and metal: the blacksmith at the forge, the hunter with his iron-tipped spear, the farmer with his hoe and cutlass, the warrior with his sword, and in our own day, the mechanic, the engineer, the driver of every vehicle, for the car and the lorry and the machine are all children of iron, and all under Ogun. When a driver ties a red cloth to his lorry, or pours a libation before a long journey, it is Ogun he honours, Ogun he asks to keep the iron faithful and the road open and safe. Ogun is civilization itself, my child, for it was iron that let us clear the forest, till the field, build the town, and defend it. Every tool that made us who we are came from his forge."}, {"id": "orisa-ogun#s7", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "But I told you to sit back from the fire, and now I will tell you why, for Ogun has a terrible side, and a good telling does not hide it. Ogun is the orisa of war, and war is his in all its faces, the just and the dreadful alike. When his blood is roused, Ogun is a fury that spares nothing, and there is a famous and terrible tale that our people tell to teach the danger of his rage, and I will tell it to you honestly, for it is part of him."}, {"id": "orisa-ogun#s8", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "It is said that once Ogun went to war on behalf of a certain town, and won a great victory for them, and returned exhausted and thirsty beyond bearing. And when he came into the town, worn from battle, he called for palm wine to slake his terrible thirst. But it happened, in some tellings, that the people were observing a sacred silence, a period in which no one might speak, and so when Ogun called for drink, no one answered him, not because they would not, but because they could not, being bound by the silence. And Ogun, not understanding, thirst-maddened and battle-frenzied, took their silence for insult and contempt, and his rage overcame him, and in his fury he drew his iron sword and turned it upon the very people he had just saved, and did not stop until the frenzy passed."}, {"id": "orisa-ogun#s9", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "And when at last Ogun’s rage cleared and he saw what he had done, saw that he had slaughtered his own people, the ones he had fought to protect, and all over a silence that was no insult at all but only a sacred custom he had failed to understand, Ogun was overcome with a grief and a shame so vast that he could not bear to remain among people. He is said to have driven his great sword point-down into the earth, and to have descended into the ground after it, vanishing from the world of men, going into the earth and into the iron forever, so heavy was his sorrow at his own unchecked wrath."}, {"id": "orisa-ogun#s10", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Do you feel the weight of that, my child? Here is the fiercest of the orisa, the mightiest warrior, the one who feared nothing, brought lower than any coward by the one enemy he could not conquer: his own rage. And so Ogun teaches, by his own catastrophe, the most necessary lesson for any strong person. Strength without self-command is not a blessing but a loaded weapon pointed at the ones you love. The stronger you are, the more terrible your anger, the more you must learn to master it, for Ogun could cut a road through a wilderness no other orisa could touch, and yet he could not, in the crucial moment, master his own thirst-maddened heart, and it cost him everything."}, {"id": "orisa-ogun#s11", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "This is why the oath of Ogun is the most binding oath a Yoruba can swear. To this very day, in the courts and among the people, when a man must swear that he speaks the truth, he swears upon iron, upon a piece of Ogun’s own metal, and he kisses it, or touches it to his tongue. For everyone knows that Ogun does not forgive a false oath sworn upon his iron, and that his justice, like his sword, is swift and without mercy. The iron that built the world will turn against the one who lies upon it. There is no more solemn promise a person can make."}, {"id": "orisa-ogun#s12", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "And yet, and this is important, Ogun is not evil, and we do not fear him as we would fear a wicked thing. We honour him. We love him, even, in the way you love a fierce grandfather whose temper you respect and whose strength has kept the family safe. For without his fierceness, who would have cut the road? Who would defend the town when enemies come? Who would do the hard, hot, dangerous work that soft hands and beautiful metals cannot do? Ogun does the work no one else will do, and takes on the danger no one else will face, and that is a kind of terrible love. The hunter prays to him before entering the forest. The soldier prays to him before battle. And they are right to, for Ogun protects his own with the same ferocity that makes him dreadful to his foes."}, {"id": "orisa-ogun#s13", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "His festivals blaze with his nature. Dogs are offered to him, for the dog is his creature, the hunter’s companion. Palm fronds and red and the smell of iron and roasted things fill his shrines. His devotees dance with a fierce and jerking energy, and some, in the height of it, handle fire and iron and are not burned, for the orisa is upon them. It is not a gentle worship. It is the worship of a gentle people for the fierce thing in themselves that they know they need and know they must keep chained."}, {"id": "orisa-ogun#s14", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So when next you cross a bridge, or ride in any vehicle, or hold any tool of iron, or walk any road that someone once had to cut through the wild, remember Ogun. Remember that someone always has to go first, into the hardest place, and clear the way with sweat and blood so that the rest may follow in safety. Honour that one. And remember too his sorrow, and let it teach you: that the measure of a strong person is not how fiercely they can strike, but how well they can hold their striking hand, even when they are thirsty, even when they feel wronged, even when the frenzy rises. For the road Ogun could not cut was the road through his own anger, and it is the hardest road of all."}, {"id": "orisa-ogun#s15", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "Tomorrow, if the ancestors are willing, we will cool ourselves again, and I will take you to the sweet waters, to the river, to gold and honey and laughter and love, and tell you of Oshun, the most beautiful of the orisa, whose kindness once saved the whole world when the fierce ones had failed. But tonight the forge glows red, and we will let Ogun’s fire bank down slowly into sleep."}]}, {"id": "orisa-oshun", "emoji": "⚡", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#150020,#350050)", "category": "orisa", "rating": 9.6, "duration": "11 min", "title": {"en": "Oshun: The Sweet Water That Saved the World", "yo": "Osun"}, "desc": {"en": "The orisa of the sweet river, love, and fertility, whose gentleness healed a world the fierce ones had broken.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Orisa", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Orisa, the story of the beautiful orisa of the river, of love, wealth, and fertility, whose gentleness once healed a world the fierce ones had broken)", "segments": [{"id": "orisa-oshun#s1", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Ah, tonight, my child, the fire is warm and the night is kind, and it is right, for tonight we speak of the sweetest of all the orisa, of Oshun, the lady of the river, the golden one, the orisa of love and beauty and cool clean water and abundance and laughter, and of the deep, deep power that lives inside gentleness, which the loud and the fierce so often mistake for weakness, to their sorrow. For I will tell you at the very start the great secret of Oshun: she is soft, yes, soft as river water, but water, my child, is the softest thing there is, and given time it carves the hardest stone into whatever shape it pleases. Never mistake her sweetness for weakness. It was her sweetness that saved the world when all the mighty ones had failed."}, {"id": "orisa-oshun#s2", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Oshun is the orisa of the river that bears her name, the Osun river that still flows through the sacred grove at Osogbo, where her worship has never ceased in all the long centuries, where the trees are hung with her offerings and the water is holy and the great festival draws her children home from across the whole earth every year. She is the youngest of the great orisa, in some tellings, the small one, the least imposing, and this too you must remember, for the tale that follows turns entirely upon the world’s mistake of thinking that the smallest and the sweetest could not possibly be the most important. Here is that tale, and it is perhaps the most important of all the tales of the orisa, so open your ears wide."}, {"id": "orisa-oshun#s3", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "In the beginning, when Olodumare sent the orisa down to establish the world and set it in order, he sent them as a company, and they were mostly the great male orisa, the powerful ones, the fierce and mighty makers and warriors. And among them came Oshun, the one woman, the sweet river, the small golden one. And the male orisa, in their pride, did not think much of her. They were about the great business of creating and arranging the world, of building and warring and commanding, the loud important work, and when they gathered to do it, they did not include Oshun. They pushed her aside. They dismissed her. What could the little river woman contribute to the mighty labour of ordering creation? Let her tend to her sweet water and her beauty and leave the real work to the strong. So the male orisa set about their tasks. And nothing worked."}, {"id": "orisa-oshun#s4", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Everything they attempted failed. They tried to make the world fruitful, and it stayed barren. They tried to make the rains fall, and the sky stayed shut. They tried to make the crops grow and the women bear children and the very earth bring forth life, and none of it, none of it, would come. The world remained empty and dry and dead, no matter how hard the mighty ones labored, no matter how they strained their great strength against the problem. They were baffled. They were the most powerful beings in all creation, and they could not make a single thing live. In their confusion they went back up to Olodumare, the Almighty, and they complained. Nothing works, they said. We have tried everything. We have used all our power. The world will not come to life. What is wrong?"}, {"id": "orisa-oshun#s5", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "And Olodumare, who sees all and knows all, asked them a single question that shamed them to their roots. He asked: When you set out to do all this work, did you include Oshun? The mighty orisa fell silent. No, they admitted. They had not. They had pushed her aside as unimportant, the small one, the sweet one, the mere river woman."}, {"id": "orisa-oshun#s6", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Then Olodumare told them the truth that they, in all their pride and power, had never understood. He told them that Oshun held a power that none of them possessed, and without it nothing they did could ever succeed. For Oshun was the very sweetness of life itself. She was fertility. She was the fresh water without which nothing green can grow and no child can be conceived and no life of any kind can begin. She was the power that makes things live. All their strength could build and cut and command, but only Oshun could make anything be born. Without her, Olodumare told them, they could accomplish nothing at all, for you cannot force life into the world by strength. Life comes only through sweetness, through water, through love, through the gentle power they had scorned. Go back, Olodumare said, and humble yourselves, and beg Oshun to join you, and honour her, and then, and only then, will the world come to life."}, {"id": "orisa-oshun#s7", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "So the mighty orisa, humbled, went back down to the earth, and they came to Oshun, the one they had dismissed, and they asked her forgiveness, and they begged her to join them, and to bring her sweet waters to the barren world. And here you see the greatness of Oshun’s character, my child, for she had every right to be bitter, every right to refuse them, to let the proud ones stew in the failure their pride had earned. But she did not. She was gentle. She forgave them. And she brought her fresh, sweet river water, and she poured it over the barren earth, and where her water flowed, life sprang up. The crops grew. The rains fell. The women conceived and bore children. The whole dead world burst into green and living abundance, all of it, everything that lives, all of it flowing from the sweetness of the orisa the mighty had scorned."}, {"id": "orisa-oshun#s8", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "That, my child, is how Oshun saved the world. Not with a sword, like Ogun. Not with thunder, like Sango. Not with fierce power at all. She saved it with sweetness, with water, with the gentle life-giving force that the strong had been too proud to value. And this is why, to this day, no important undertaking among her people is complete without honoring the feminine, without honoring Oshun, without remembering that all the strength in the world cannot make a single thing live, and that the softest power is often the most essential of all."}, {"id": "orisa-oshun#s9", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "So now you know why Oshun is beloved above almost all the orisa, why her festival at Osogbo is one of the greatest gatherings of our people anywhere on earth. She is the orisa of love, and lovers pray to her. She is the orisa of fertility, and women who long for children go down to her river and bathe in her sacred water and pray for a child, and it is said she is generous with this gift above all others, for she knows the ache of being wanted for what one can bring forth, and she is tender toward it. She is the orisa of wealth and of gold, and she loves brass and honey and sweet things and yellow and gold cloth and mirrors and fans and all things beautiful, for beauty is holy to her, and she wears it not from vanity but as a celebration of the sweetness of life she herself pours into the world."}, {"id": "orisa-oshun#s10", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "She loves honey most of all, but there is a small tale even about that, which shows her wisdom. It is said that one must always taste the honey oneself before offering it to Oshun, and this comes from a time when she was once offered honey that had been tampered with, and so now she requires that the giver taste it first, to prove it is pure and sweet and untainted. And from this we learn: do not offer to the sweet and the loving anything you have not first made sure is good yourself. Sweetness deserves sweetness. Do not bring a false gift to a true heart."}, {"id": "orisa-oshun#s11", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "But do not think, because she is gentle, that Oshun cannot be terrible when she must be. Remember, she is water. When the sweet river is wronged, when her children are threatened, she can rise in flood and sweep away everything in her path. She can withhold her waters and let the barrenness return. A people who scorn her, who dishonour the feminine, who value only the loud strength and despise the gentle life-giving power, will find, as the mighty orisa found at the beginning, that all their strength avails them nothing, and that the world grows barren in their hands. Her anger is slow to rouse, for she is patient as water is patient. But it is not to be provoked, for water that is patient is also water that carves stone."}, {"id": "orisa-oshun#s12", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So this is Oshun, my child, the sweetest and, in her way, among the mightiest of all the orisa. Let her teach you the lesson the proud gods had to learn the hard way. Never scorn the gentle. Never mistake softness for weakness or sweetness for uselessness. The one you push aside as unimportant may hold the single power without which all your labor is dead. Honour the life-givers. Honour the feminine. Honour the sweet waters. For all the iron of Ogun and all the fire of Sango cannot make one seed sprout, and it is the quiet river woman, forgiven and generous, who makes the whole world live."}, {"id": "orisa-oshun#s13", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "Tomorrow, if the ancestors permit, I will take you from the sweet river to the wide and salt-blue sea, and tell you of the great mother of waters, of Yemoja, the ocean herself, mother of many of the orisa, whose depths hold every secret and whose tides rock the whole world like a child. But tonight belongs to the golden one, to Oshun, and we will let her sweet waters carry us gently down into sleep."}]}, {"id": "orisa-yemoja", "emoji": "⚡", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#150020,#350050)", "category": "orisa", "rating": 9.6, "duration": "10 min", "title": {"en": "Yemoja: The Mother Whose Children Are the Fish", "yo": "Yemoja"}, "desc": {"en": "The great mother of waters, of ocean and river, whose children are the fish.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Orisa", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Orisa, consolidates the tellings of Yemoja across the collection, the story of the great mother of waters, of the ocean and the river, mother of the orisa, whose love is as deep and vast as the sea itself)", "segments": [{"id": "orisa-yemoja#s1", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Come, my child, and I will tell you of the mother of us all, or nearly all, for tonight we speak of Yemoja, whose name, when you unfold it in the old tongue, means the mother whose children are the fish, Yeye omo eja, and once you have heard that name spoken slowly you will understand almost everything about her, for a mother whose children are as countless as the fish of the sea is a mother whose love has no bottom and no shore. Last night I told you of Oshun and her sweet river. Tonight we go to the greater water into which all rivers finally pour, to the wide and salt-blue sea, to the mother, to Yemoja."}, {"id": "orisa-yemoja#s2", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Now I must tell you at the start that Yemoja is known in two ways among our people and among those who carried her across the great water, and both are true, for the orisa are larger than any single telling. In her old homeland she is the mother of the river, particularly the great Ogun river, the mighty fresh waters. But when our people were taken across the ocean in the time of sorrow, which I will speak of another night, they carried Yemoja with them in their hearts, and on the far shore, looking out at the endless sea that separated them forever from home, they knew her as the mother of the ocean itself. And so today Yemoja is honoured as the great mother of all waters, river and sea alike, and I will tell you of her as the ocean mother, for that is how her greatness is most fully seen."}, {"id": "orisa-yemoja#s3", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Yemoja is a mother above all things. That is her first nature and her last. She is the mother of many of the orisa themselves, for in many tellings the great orisa were born from her, poured out of her waters as all life first came from the sea. She is the mother of Sango the thunder king, in some accounts, and of Ogun, and of Oshun the river, and of many others, so that when the orisa quarrel, as the mighty do, it is often Yemoja who is the mother watching over all of them, grieving over their quarrels as any mother grieves to see her children fight, loving each of them even when they will not love one another."}, {"id": "orisa-yemoja#s4", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "And because she is the mother of the orisa, she is the mother of all who honour the orisa, which is to say she is your mother too, my child, and mine. When you have no one, you have Yemoja. When you are far from home, as our people were far from home across the sea, you have Yemoja, for the ocean touches every shore, and the mother who is the ocean can reach you wherever water can reach, which is everywhere. Motherless children are her special care. The lost, the orphaned, the ones who cry in the night with no one to hear, these are Yemoja’s own, and she gathers them to her vast and salt-blue breast."}, {"id": "orisa-yemoja#s5", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "There is a tale that shows the depth of her love and also her sorrow, for even the greatest mother has her griefs. It is told that Yemoja was married, and that in some tellings her marriage brought her great pain, that her husband wronged her, or that she was pursued and pressed and would not be mastered. And in one of the oldest and most striking of the tales, it is said that when Yemoja was fleeing, or when she was overwhelmed by grief, her waters broke forth from her, and from her flowing waters were born the rivers and the streams and many of the orisa, so that her very sorrow became the source of the world’s waters and the birth of its gods. Do you see the pattern, my child, the pattern we have seen already in Obatala’s failing and Ogun’s rage and Oshun’s scorning? Again and again our old ones took the deepest pains and made from them the sources of life. Yemoja’s grief became the rivers. From the mother’s sorrow flowed the waters that give the whole world drink."}, {"id": "orisa-yemoja#s6", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "And so Yemoja is the orisa to whom women turn in all the great passages of a woman’s life. Women who wish to conceive pray to her, for she is the mother of all. Women in childbirth call upon her, for the breaking of the birth-waters is her own mystery, the same waters that gave birth to the orisa. Mothers pray to her for their children’s safety, and it is said that a child under Yemoja’s protection is watched by the whole vast attention of the sea. She is the protector of the family, the guardian of the home, the deep and patient love that holds a household together through every storm."}, {"id": "orisa-yemoja#s7", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "But make no mistake, my child, for I have taught you already that the water orisa are gentle until they are not, and Yemoja is the greatest water of all. Think of the sea. Think of how it can lie calm and blue and rocking, gentle as a cradle, a mother humming her child to sleep. And think of how that same sea can rise in a storm and swallow ships whole, drag down whole towns, throw waves as high as the tallest trees, and care nothing for all the works and pride of men. That is Yemoja too. Her love is infinite, but her power is the power of the entire ocean, and the ocean is not to be trifled with. A mother’s love is the gentlest thing in the world and the most terrible, for there is nothing, nothing, a true mother will not do to protect her children, and Yemoja’s children are beyond counting."}, {"id": "orisa-yemoja#s8", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "When Yemoja is angered, when her children are threatened, when the balance she guards is broken, she rises, and her rising is the storm-sea, and it cannot be resisted, only endured and waited out. And yet even her fury is a mother’s fury, never wanton, never cruel for cruelty’s sake, always in defense of something loved. When the storm passes, the sea is calm again, and forgiving, and the mother gathers her children back to her as though the storm had never been. For that is the nature of the deepest love: it can rage like a hurricane and then, the anger spent in defense of its own, return instantly to tenderness."}, {"id": "orisa-yemoja#s9", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Her worship is beautiful, my child, and I wish you could see it. Yemoja loves the colours of the sea and the moon: deep blue and clear crystal and pure white, and silver, and pearl. Her devotees dress in blue and white and adorn themselves with beads of crystal and blue glass, and her offerings are placed at the water’s edge, and set upon little boats and pushed out onto the waves, and carried by the tide out to the mother. On her great festival days, especially where our people were carried across the sea, hundreds of thousands go down to the shore dressed in white and blue, and they send flowers and gifts and prayers out upon the water, and the whole beach becomes a single great act of love toward the mother, and it is said that if the sea accepts your offering and does not throw it back, the mother has heard your prayer and taken it into her deep heart."}, {"id": "orisa-yemoja#s10", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "She is dignified, is Yemoja, queenly, a great and stately mother, not girlish like her daughter Oshun the sweet river, but full and deep and grave and endlessly kind, the way the oldest and best of mothers are, the ones who have seen everything, forgiven everything, and love you still, and more, for all of it."}, {"id": "orisa-yemoja#s11", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "So when you stand someday at the edge of the great water, my child, and feel the wind off the waves and the salt on your lips, and watch the sea stretch out farther than your eyes can follow, remember that you are looking at the mother. Remember Yemoja, whose children are the fish, whose love is as deep as the sea is deep and as wide as the sea is wide, who is mother to the orisa and mother to the motherless and mother, yes, to you. Remember that no matter how far you travel, no matter how lost you become, no matter if oceans separate you from everything you have ever known, as they separated our people long ago, the mother is there, for the water touches every shore, and she has never once, in all the ages of the world, forgotten a single one of her countless children."}, {"id": "orisa-yemoja#s12", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "Tomorrow, if the ancestors are willing, we will leave the deep waters and speak of a very different and much stranger power, of the trickster, the messenger, the one who stands at the crossroads and opens the way between all worlds, the orisa Esu, whom the foolish fear and the wise respect, and whom no one, no one, may ignore. But tonight belongs to the mother, and we will let the great sea rock us, as she has rocked her children since the beginning of the world, gently down into sleep."}]}, {"id": "orisa-esu", "emoji": "⚡", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#150020,#350050)", "category": "orisa", "rating": 9.6, "duration": "12 min", "title": {"en": "Esu: The Keeper of the Crossroads", "yo": "Esu"}, "desc": {"en": "The divine messenger and trickster who guards the crossroads and carries sacrifice to heaven.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Orisa", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Orisa, consolidates the tellings of Esu across the collection, the story of the divine messenger and trickster who guards the crossroads, carries the sacrifices, and teaches that no one may be ignored)", "segments": [{"id": "orisa-esu#s1", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Sit, my child, and mind your manners tonight, for we speak of Esu, and Esu is listening, as Esu is always listening, at the crossroads, at the doorway, at the mouth of every road and the edge of every market. And the first thing I will teach you, before any tale, is the first rule of Esu: you do not ignore him. You may honour whom you please among the orisa, you may love this one and fear that one, but Esu you must always acknowledge, always greet, always give his portion first, for he is the one who carries messages between heaven and earth, between the orisa and humankind, and to slight him is to cut the very line by which all your prayers must travel. So we begin, always, with a word of respect for Esu."}, {"id": "orisa-esu#s2", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Now I must tell you a hard thing, for there has been a great confusion about Esu, a confusion brought by strangers who did not understand him. When the missionaries came, seeking a word in our tongue for their idea of the great evil one, the devil, they seized upon the name of Esu, and they translated their devil as Esu, and so a whole error was born that has troubled us ever since. But hear me clearly, my child, and unlearn that error: Esu is not the devil. Esu is not evil. There is no orisa of pure evil among us, for our world is not built that way. Esu is a trickster, yes, mischievous, yes, dangerous if disrespected, absolutely. But he is also the most faithful servant of Olodumare, the divine messenger, the enforcer of the sacred law, without whom the whole spiritual order could not function. To call him the devil is like calling the messenger who carries the king’s law a criminal. It is exactly backward."}, {"id": "orisa-esu#s3", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Let me tell you what Esu truly is. Esu stands at the crossroads. Think of what a crossroads is, my child: it is the place where roads meet and part, where you must choose, where every direction is possible and every choice has its price. That is Esu’s domain, the point of decision, of change, of possibility, the threshold between one thing and another. He is the orisa of the in-between, of the doorway, of the market where all things are exchanged, of the moment of choosing. And because all of life is a series of crossroads and choices, Esu has a hand in everything. Nothing happens without passing through his roads."}, {"id": "orisa-esu#s4", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "And Esu is the messenger, the one who carries. When you make an offering to any orisa, when you pour a libation, when you send a prayer up to Olodumare the Almighty, it is Esu who carries it. He is the road on which your sacrifice travels to reach the one it is meant for. And in return, it is Esu who carries the blessings and the answers back down to you. He is the two-way road between the human and the divine. This is why he must always be honoured first, before any other orisa, and given the first portion of any offering, for if the carrier is not paid, the message is not carried. Ignore Esu, and your prayers, however sincere, simply do not arrive. This is not cruelty on his part. It is the plain mechanics of the spirit world. You do not send a letter and refuse to pay the one who walks it to its destination."}, {"id": "orisa-esu#s5", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Now I will tell you the most famous tale of Esu, and it is a tale that will make you laugh and then make you think, which is exactly what Esu himself does to people, and it teaches the deepest lesson about why he exists at all. There were once two friends, the closest of friends, who had been inseparable since childhood, who farmed side by side on adjoining fields, and who swore that nothing in all the world could ever come between them. They were proud of their friendship, and they boasted of it to everyone, and, this is the important part, in all their boasting they never once thought to honour Esu, never gave him his portion, never acknowledged the keeper of the roads. They thought their friendship needed no one, least of all a trickster orisa. They ignored him."}, {"id": "orisa-esu#s6", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Esu heard them. Esu always hears. And Esu decided to teach them, not out of malice, but because a lesson was owed. One day, as the two friends were working in their fields on opposite sides of the boundary path, Esu put on a cap and walked down the path between them. And this cap was Esu’s own particular kind of joke: it was red on one side and white on the other, or in some tellings black on one side and red on the other, coloured differently on each side so that each man saw only the colour facing him."}, {"id": "orisa-esu#s7", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "After Esu had passed, one friend called across to the other, remarking on the man who had gone by wearing a fine red cap. And the other friend laughed and corrected him: the cap was not red, it was white, had he gone blind? No, said the first, it was red, I saw it plainly with my own eyes. White, insisted the second, and now growing annoyed, for he too had seen it with his own eyes. And each was certain. And each had, in fact, seen truly, for each had seen the true colour of the side that faced him. But neither could imagine that the other had seen something equally true and equally real, and so each concluded that the other was either a fool or a liar."}, {"id": "orisa-esu#s8", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "And so, my child, over a cap, over the colour of a cap, these two friends who had sworn nothing could divide them fell to quarreling, and the quarrel grew hot, and hot words became hotter, and their lifelong friendship, the friendship they had so proudly boasted needed no one’s blessing, shattered to pieces there on the boundary path, each man calling the other a liar, and both of them absolutely, honestly certain that they alone had seen the truth. That is Esu. Do you see what he did? He did not make them lie. He did not make them evil. He simply revealed something that was already true and already dangerous in them: their pride, their certainty, their inability to imagine that another person, seeing from another side, might see a different truth that was just as real as their own. The crack was already in their friendship. Esu only wore the cap that showed it."}, {"id": "orisa-esu#s9", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Now here is the wisdom, and it is layered, as all of Esu’s lessons are layered. The first lesson is the simple one about the two friends: that you must never be so certain of your own view that you cannot imagine another standing on the other side of the road seeing a different colour, equally real. Half the quarrels of the world, my child, half the wars, half the broken friendships and divided families, are two people looking at Esu’s cap from opposite sides, each seeing truly, each unable to grant that the other also sees truly. Wisdom is knowing that your view is a view, from where you happen to stand, and that the man across the path is not a liar just because his side is a different colour."}, {"id": "orisa-esu#s10", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "But there is a second, deeper lesson, and it is the one the two friends should have learned before the cap ever appeared: they were struck precisely because they had ignored Esu, because they were so proud they thought they needed to honour no one, to acknowledge no power beyond themselves and their own certainty. And this is the great teaching of Esu about human life. You cannot go through this world ignoring the forces of chance, of change, of the crossroads, of the other person’s truth. The ones who think themselves so strong, so secure, so self-sufficient that they need acknowledge nothing outside themselves, those are exactly the ones Esu comes for, cap in hand. Humility before the mystery of the roads, before the possibility that you do not see everything, that is what saves you. Pride that acknowledges nothing is what Esu delights to overturn."}, {"id": "orisa-esu#s11", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "So Esu is the guardian of a great truth, my child, and now you can see why he is no devil but a necessary and even a holy power. He is the one who keeps the world honest by keeping it uncertain. He is the check on all pride, all rigid certainty, all the smug confidence of those who think they have it all arranged. He introduces the unexpected, the reversal, the twist in the road, and though we curse him in the moment, it is often his twists that save us from our own arrogance and teach us what we would never have learned in a straight and easy path. A world without Esu would be a world of frozen certainties, of pride never humbled, of choices never truly free, and such a world would be dead."}, {"id": "orisa-esu#s12", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "He is honoured at the crossroads, and at the doorway, and his image, often a figure with a long curved projection from the head, sometimes carrying a club or a calabash, stands at the entrance to the compound and at the gate of the market, watching the coming and the going. His colours are red and black, the colours of heat and of the threshold. His offerings are placed where the roads meet. And always, always, in any gathering of the orisa, in any offering, in any prayer, his name is called first, for he is the beginning of the road, and without the road, nothing and no one arrives anywhere."}, {"id": "orisa-esu#s13", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So learn the manners of Esu, my child, for they are the manners of a wise life. Acknowledge the crossroads. Honour the messenger. Never be so proud that you imagine you owe nothing to any power outside yourself. And when you are certain, dead certain, that you have seen the truth and the other person is simply a fool or a liar, stop, and remember the cap that was red on one side and white on the other, and ask yourself, before you throw away a friendship or start a war, what colour the cap might be from where the other one is standing. That single question, that one humble pause at the crossroads, is Esu’s greatest gift, if only we are wise enough to receive it instead of cursing him for offering it."}, {"id": "orisa-esu#s14", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "Tomorrow, if the ancestors permit, we will speak of fire from the sky, of the thunder and the lightning, of a king who became a god, of the mighty and terrible Sango, whose double axe still splits the heavens when he is angry. But tonight, greet Esu on your way to your mat, give him his respect, and he will let you pass the crossroads of sleep in peace."}]}, {"id": "orisa-sango", "emoji": "⚡", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#150020,#350050)", "category": "orisa", "rating": 9.6, "duration": "12 min", "title": {"en": "Sango: The King Who Became the Thunder", "yo": "Sango"}, "desc": {"en": "The fourth Alaafin of Oyo, lord of thunder and lightning, who was once a man and became a god.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Orisa", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Orisa, consolidates ‘Sango’ and ‘Sango’s Crown’, the story of the fourth Alaafin of Oyo, lord of thunder and lightning, who was once a man and became a god, and whose double axe still splits the sky)", "segments": [{"id": "orisa-sango#s1", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Do you hear that, my child, far off, that low rolling growl behind the clouds? Sit closer. That is Sango walking. That is the one we speak of tonight, the mightiest and most feared and most magnificent of the orisa who was also, and this is the wonder of him, once a living man, a king, an Alaafin of Oyo, who ruled and erred and fell and rose again into the sky to become the thunder itself. For Sango is the great proof of a thing our people believe deeply: that the line between man and god is not fixed, that a human being of sufficient greatness and sufficient terror can pass through death and become a power, and reign forever in the heavens. Sango did this. Sango is our king who became the thunder."}, {"id": "orisa-sango#s2", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Now the historians and the priests tell it a little differently, and I will honour both, for both are true in their way. As history, Sango was the fourth Alaafin of the great empire of Oyo, in the deep past, a king renowned for his tremendous power, his fierce temper, and his mastery of a terrible and mysterious force. As sacred story, he is the orisa of thunder and lightning, of fire, of justice and vengeance, of royal power, of virility and dance and drumming and everything hot and magnificent and dangerous. Hold both of these in your mind, the king and the god, for the whole meaning of Sango is in the passage from the one to the other."}, {"id": "orisa-sango#s3", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Sango was a mighty king, but a hard one. He was a warrior of tremendous accomplishment, who expanded the power of Oyo and made its enemies tremble. And he possessed, the tales say, a fearsome gift: he could call down fire from his own mouth, he could summon lightning, he had command over the terrible power of the storm. He wore the double-headed axe, the oshe, the sign of the thunder-stone that falls from the sky when lightning strikes, and even today when lightning shatters a tree or a house, the people find in the earth beneath it a smooth stone, a thunder-axe, and they know that Sango has struck there, and these stones are sacred to him and kept in his shrines."}, {"id": "orisa-sango#s4", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "But a king with such power, and such a temper, is a dangerous thing, my child, dangerous most of all to himself, and here the tale turns toward its sorrow, as the tales of the greatest orisa so often do. For I have taught you already, with Obatala’s wine and Ogun’s rage, that our old ones did not make their gods flawless. They made them great and flawed together, so that the flaw and the greatness might both teach us something."}, {"id": "orisa-sango#s5", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Sango had powerful chiefs beneath him, and two of the mightiest were his own war-generals, and they grew so powerful and so proud that Sango came to fear them as rivals to his throne. And so, in some tellings, the cunning king set them against each other, encouraging them to fight, hoping they would destroy one another and leave his throne secure. They fought. One was defeated and the other victorious, but the victorious general returned so powerful, and so full of his victory, that he was now a greater threat than before, and he pressed Sango hard, and demanded, and overreached, until the two of them, king and over-mighty general, came to a final breaking."}, {"id": "orisa-sango#s6", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "And here it is told that Sango, in his fury and his fear, unleashed his terrible power, called down the lightning, and it is said that in that unleashing something went wrong, that the fire he summoned turned back upon his own household, that lightning struck his own palace and killed some of his own family, his own wives and children, by his own summoned storm. Do you feel the horror of it, my child? The same pattern once more: the mighty power, uncontrolled, turning against the very ones it was meant to protect. Sango’s fire, like Ogun’s sword, like Obatala’s hands, betrayed him in the moment of his passion, and the destruction he called down fell upon his own head."}, {"id": "orisa-sango#s7", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "Overcome with grief and shame at what his own uncontrolled power had done, and facing rebellion and the loss of his authority, Sango left. He walked out of Oyo, away from the throne, into the forest, alone. And here the tellings divide, and I will give you the one that matters most. It is said, my child, that in his despair Sango took his own life, that he hanged himself from a tree in the forest, in the depth of his shame and sorrow. And his enemies rejoiced, and they mocked him, and they cried through the land, Oba so, the king has hanged, the king is dead, saying it to shame his memory and to shame his followers. Oba so, oba so, they jeered."}, {"id": "orisa-sango#s8", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "But now, my child, comes the turn that made Sango a god, and it is one of the most powerful moments in all our sacred story, so listen with your whole heart. Sango’s faithful followers, who loved him still, who knew his greatness even alongside his terrible flaws, would not accept the shame his enemies heaped upon him. And when they went to the tree, some tellings say, they found no body there at all. The king was gone, vanished, taken up. And they understood, or they declared, that Sango had not simply died in shame like an ordinary broken man. He had ascended. He had passed from the tree into the sky, from man into god, from the fourth Alaafin of Oyo into the eternal orisa of the thunder. Oba ko so, they cried now, correcting the mockery of his enemies with a mightier truth. Oba ko so. The king did not hang. The king did not die. The king is not dead at all. And to this day, my child, Oba Koso is a praise-name of Sango and a holy cry, and it is the name of the very place, and it means, forever, the king did not hang, the king lives, the king became a god."}, {"id": "orisa-sango#s9", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "And then, they say, Sango proved it. For after his ascension, the enemies who had mocked him and driven him out found that a terrible new power was loose in the heavens. Lightning began to strike them. Storms fell upon the houses of those who had betrayed him. Fire from the sky sought out his enemies and burned them, and thunder rolled over the land in the voice of the king who had become a god and would not be mocked. Sango, from the sky, took his vengeance and demonstrated his divinity in fire and thunder, until even those who had jeered fell down and worshipped him and begged his forgiveness and built him shrines and became his priests. The king who had died in shame reigned now in the heavens, mightier than he had ever been on his earthly throne."}, {"id": "orisa-sango#s10", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "So now you understand, my child, why Sango is honoured as he is, and why his worship is the most dramatic and fiery of all. He is the orisa of thunder and lightning, and when the storm rolls and the sky splits with white fire, that is Sango, riding, striking, present and alive. He is the orisa of divine justice, and this is important: for all his temper, Sango became in death the great punisher of liars, thieves, and wrongdoers. His lightning seeks out the guilty. When a man is accused of theft or falsehood, he may be made to swear by Sango, and it is believed that Sango’s fire will strike the liar. He is the terror of the wicked and the defender of the wronged, hurling his thunder-stones at injustice from his throne in the clouds."}, {"id": "orisa-sango#s11", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "And he is the orisa of kingship itself, of royal authority, of the tremendous and dangerous power of the crown, for he was a king, and he knows both its glory and its peril. The Alaafins of Oyo who came after him traced their power to him. He is the patron of the mighty, of the powerful, of those who wield great force, and his lesson to all of them is written in his own life: that great power without self-mastery destroys not only others but the wielder himself, and yet that even from the depths of a shameful fall, one can rise, transformed, into something greater than before."}, {"id": "orisa-sango#s12", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "His worship blazes. His colours are red and white, fire and lightning. His devotees dance a fierce and virile dance, and in the height of their possession they are said to eat fire, to carry live coals in their bare hands and on their heads and in their mouths and take no harm, for the orisa of fire is upon them and fire will not burn its own. His drummers play the bata drums, the drums of thunder, in rhythms that call him down. His priests carry the oshe, the double axe, and wear their hair in the style of a woman in his honour, for reasons the tales tell in their own time. His festivals are loud and hot and magnificent, full of the beating of drums and the flashing of red cloth and the eating of fire, a fitting worship for the king who became the storm."}, {"id": "orisa-sango#s13", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So the next time the thunder rolls, my child, and the lightning walks across the sky, do not merely be afraid, though a little fear is only proper. Remember Sango. Remember the king who was so mighty and so flawed, whose own fire once turned against his own house, who fell into such shame that he left his throne and, in the deepest telling, ended his own life beneath a forest tree. And remember that his followers would not let that be the end of him, that they cried oba ko so, the king did not hang, the king lives, and that by their faith and his own terrible power he rose from the shame of the tree into the glory of the heavens, and reigns there still. There is a hope in that for every fallen and flawed and mighty soul, my child. Even the king who destroyed his own house by his own uncontrolled fire was not, in the end, beyond becoming a god. The tree was not the end. The lightning was the beginning."}, {"id": "orisa-sango#s14", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "Tomorrow, if the ancestors are willing, we will leave the fire of the sky and go to the quiet wisdom of the palm nuts, to the orisa of destiny and divination, to Orunmila, the one who was present when Olodumare fixed each of our fates, and who alone can read what is written on your ori. But tonight belongs to the thunder. Oba ko so. The king lives. Sleep, and let the storm pass over you as a friend."}]}, {"id": "orisa-sango-seven-thunderstones", "emoji": "⚡", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#150020,#350050)", "category": "orisa", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "10 min", "title": {"en": "Sango and the Seven Thunderstones", "yo": "Sango"}, "desc": {"en": "The legend of Sango's seven thunderstones.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Orisa", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Oba, from “Sango and the Seven Thunderstones”)", "segments": [{"id": "orisa-sango-seven-thunderstones#s1", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Some men are remembered as kings. A very few are remembered as gods. Sango is the only figure in Yoruba history who is remembered as both, a real Alaafin who sat on the throne of Oyo and ruled and fell, and then, in the strange alchemy of memory and worship, became one of the most powerful and most feared of all the orisa, the lord of thunder and lightning, whose name still shakes the air when the storm cracks over the land. To tell the story of Sango and the seven thunderstones is to tell the story of how a man becomes a god, and of the terrible power that both makes and destroys the ones who wield it. It is a story of fire, in every sense, and like all stories of fire it warns even as it dazzles."}, {"id": "orisa-sango-seven-thunderstones#s2", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Sango was, first, a man and a king. He was the brother of the gentle Ajaka, and where Ajaka was soft, Sango was hard, a warrior of enormous force and presence, exactly the kind of king the war chiefs of Oyo had wanted when they set the gentle brother aside. He took the throne and he ruled with a strength that no one could question. He expanded the reach of Oyo, made its enemies fear it, and filled the kingdom with the sense of a mighty hand upon the tiller. He was charismatic and powerful and proud, and around him even in life the stories began to gather, tales of his temper like a storm, of a voice like thunder, of a presence that seemed to carry something more than ordinary human power. And here the traditions add the detail that would define him forever. Sango, they say, had power over fire and lightning. He could breathe fire. Lightning answered to him."}, {"id": "orisa-sango-seven-thunderstones#s3", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Now a king who can call down lightning is a magnificent thing and a dangerous thing, and the danger is not only to his enemies. For power like that, uncontrolled, does not distinguish between foe and friend, between the thing you meant to strike and the thing you love. And Sango’s power, the stories agree, was not fully under his control. It was bound up with his temper, and his temper was a true storm, sudden and violent and beyond reason. This is the heart of the tragedy that the tale of the thunderstones circles around, the terrible truth that the very force that made Sango great was the force that would destroy him and everyone near him, because he could summon it but he could not always command it."}, {"id": "orisa-sango-seven-thunderstones#s4", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "The tellings differ, as living traditions always do, but the shape of the disaster is remembered across them all. In one of the most famous versions, Sango, wishing to test or display his power over lightning, or provoked into a rage, called down the storm, and the lightning came, but it did not strike only where he aimed it. It struck his own palace. And in the fire that followed, his own household was destroyed, his wives, his children, all that he loved most in the world consumed by the very power that was his glory. Imagine it. The mightiest king in the land, the man who commanded the thunder, standing in the smoking ruin of his own home, having killed with his own power the people he would have died to protect. There is no enemy in this story. The enemy is the power itself, and the man who could not master it."}, {"id": "orisa-sango-seven-thunderstones#s5", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "What followed is the strangest and most haunting part of the tradition, and there are, again, different tellings. In the version most often told, Sango, broken by grief and horror at what he had done, and perhaps deserted by his people who now feared the very power they had once cheered, left the city. He went out from Oyo, alone or nearly so, wandering, undone, and in the depth of his despair he took his own life, hanging himself, the histories say, from a tree in the forest at a place remembered ever after. The mighty king, the master of lightning, ended not on a battlefield in glory but alone in the bush, destroyed by his own hand after being destroyed by his own gift. It is one of the darkest ends in all the traditions, the fall of a titan not to any foe but to the impossible weight of his own nature."}, {"id": "orisa-sango-seven-thunderstones#s6", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "But the story does not end at the tree, and this is where the man becomes a god. His faithful followers, or so the tradition says, could not accept that their king was simply gone, dead by his own hand in the forest like any broken man. And a saying arose, and became a creed, and became a religion. Oba ko so, they said. The king did not hang. The king did not die. He did not end in shame in the forest. Rather, they declared, he had ascended, gone up into the sky, transformed, taken his rightful place as a power among the powers, no longer a man at all but an orisa, a god, the lord of the storm he had always commanded. And to prove it, the sky itself seemed to answer, for after his passing the thunder rolled and the lightning struck, and his people said, there, that is him, that is Sango, speaking from the heavens. His death was not an ending. It was a coronation in the sky."}, {"id": "orisa-sango-seven-thunderstones#s7", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "And here at last we come to the thunderstones, which give the tale its name and which tie the god forever to the earth he once ruled. When lightning strikes the ground in a great storm, it sometimes leaves behind, so the old belief holds, a stone, a smooth dark polished thing, often in truth an ancient stone axe-head from a forgotten age, found in the burnt earth where the bolt came down. These stones the Yoruba call the thunderstones, edun ara, the stones of Sango, and they are understood to be the very thunderbolts the god has hurled, fallen to earth and hardened into stone. They are sacred. The priests of Sango gather them and keep them in the shrines, upon the god’s altar, and they carry immense power, for they are pieces of the god’s own weapon, fragments of the lightning itself made solid and given into human hands."}, {"id": "orisa-sango-seven-thunderstones#s8", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "So the worship of Sango took root and spread, and it spread far beyond Oyo, far beyond Yorubaland itself. The priests of Sango, dressed in red, wielding the double-headed axe that is his emblem, the oshe Sango, carrying his thunderstones and dancing his fierce dances, became a fixture of Yoruba religious life, and when the terrible ships carried the Yoruba across the ocean in chains, Sango went with them, hidden and preserved, so that today he is worshipped under his own name and under others across the whole Atlantic world, in Cuba, in Brazil, in Trinidad, wherever the children of the Yoruba landed and held onto their gods. Few kings of any land, anywhere in history, have been remembered so long or so widely, and none in quite this way, worshipped as a living power thousands of miles and hundreds of years from the throne he once held."}, {"id": "orisa-sango-seven-thunderstones#s9", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "And the meaning the elders draw from all of it is as sharp as the lightning that is his sign. Sango is power, and power is the theme of his whole story, from the throne to the storm to the god. But the lesson is not simply that power is glorious. It is that power without mastery destroys, and that it destroys most surely the one who holds it and the ones he loves. Sango could summon the lightning, but he could not rule his own rage, and so the lightning ruled him, and burned his house, and drove him to the tree. He is worshipped, yes, and feared, and honored, but the truest worshippers of Sango have always understood that to stand before that power is to stand before something that can bless and can annihilate, and that the whole point of the priesthood, the discipline, the taboos, the careful handling of the thunderstones, is the mastery that the king himself never fully achieved. Sango is the god of a force we all know in smaller forms, the force of our own strength and our own anger, and his story asks each of us the question his life answered so terribly. You have power. Can you rule it? Or will it, in the end, rule you?"}]}, {"id": "orisa-orunmila", "emoji": "⚡", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#150020,#350050)", "category": "orisa", "rating": 9.6, "duration": "11 min", "title": {"en": "Orunmila: The Witness to Destiny", "yo": "Orunmila"}, "desc": {"en": "The orisa of wisdom and divination who witnessed each soul choose its fate, and whose Ifa is the great book of the Yoruba.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Orisa", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Orisa, the story of the orisa of wisdom and divination, who was present when each soul chose its fate, and whose Ifa is the great book of Yoruba knowledge)", "segments": [{"id": "orisa-orunmila#s1", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Tonight, my child, we speak softly, for we come to the wisest of all the orisa, and wisdom does not shout. Last night we had thunder. Tonight we have the quiet click of the palm nuts passing between a diviner’s hands, which is a smaller sound than thunder, and yet, I tell you, it has guided our people through more storms than any lightning ever lit the way through. Tonight we speak of Orunmila, the orisa of wisdom, of knowledge, of destiny and divination, the one whom we also call Ifa, or who is bound so closely to Ifa that the two names are spoken almost as one. And to understand Orunmila you must first understand the deepest of all our beliefs, the belief in ori, in destiny, in the fate each of us chose before we were ever born."}, {"id": "orisa-orunmila#s2", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "For we believe, my child, that before you came into this world, before your mother ever held you, your soul stood in heaven before Olodumare the Almighty, and there, in that place before life, you chose your ori, your inner head, your destiny. You knelt and you selected the fate you would carry into the world: the length of your days, the work you would do, the joys and the sorrows you would meet, the whole shape of the life ahead. And then you crossed into the world and, in the crossing, you forgot. Every one of us forgets. We come into life carrying a destiny we chose and cannot remember, walking a road whose map is sealed inside our own foreheads, hidden even from ourselves."}, {"id": "orisa-orunmila#s3", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "And here is why Orunmila matters above almost all. For Orunmila was there. When each soul knelt before Olodumare and chose its destiny, Orunmila was present, the witness, the one who saw what each of us selected. He alone among the orisa attended every choosing, watched every soul pick its fate, and remembers what each of us has forgotten. He is called eleri ipin, the witness to destiny, the witness to the choosing of lots. He knows what is written on your ori because he was standing there when it was written, and you were the one writing it, though you no longer recall the hand that held the pen."}, {"id": "orisa-orunmila#s4", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Do you see now why we go to Orunmila? We go to him to be reminded of ourselves. We go to recover the map we sealed inside our own heads and forgot. When a Yoruba faces a great decision, a marriage, a journey, a sickness, the naming of a child, the founding of a business, the choosing of a path, they go to the babalawo, the priest of Ifa, the father of secrets, who serves Orunmila, and through the sacred system of divination they ask Orunmila to look upon their destiny and tell them what it holds and how to walk it well. Not to change fate, understand, but to understand it, to align with it, to avoid the pitfalls the soul agreed to and to reach the good it chose."}, {"id": "orisa-orunmila#s5", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Now the system by which Orunmila speaks is Ifa, and Ifa, my child, is the greatest treasure of our people’s mind, and I must tell you what it is, for it is unlike anything the strangers brought. Ifa is not a simple casting of lots, not a mere fortune-telling. Ifa is a vast, deep, immense body of knowledge, a whole library held for centuries in the memories of the babalawo, without a single written page. It is organized into two hundred and fifty-six chapters, called the odu, and each odu contains a great store of verses, of poems, of proverbs, of myths, of remedies, of histories, of moral teachings, so many verses that no single babalawo can hold them all, and a diviner may study for decades and still be learning. It is, in truth, the encyclopedia of Yoruba civilization, our philosophy and our medicine and our history and our ethics and our poetry, all bound together in the memory of the priests of Orunmila."}, {"id": "orisa-orunmila#s6", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "When the babalawo divines, he manipulates the sacred palm nuts, the ikin, sixteen of them, passing them between his hands, or he casts the divining chain, the opele, and by the pattern that falls he arrives at one of the two hundred and fifty-six odu, and then he recites the verses of that odu, and within those verses, the ancient stories and their outcomes, lies the answer to the seeker’s question, and the sacrifice or the action that Orunmila advises. It is a system so sophisticated, so deep, so vast, that the whole world has come to recognize it as one of the great intellectual and spiritual achievements of humankind, a body of wisdom to stand beside any philosophy of any people anywhere."}, {"id": "orisa-orunmila#s7", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "Let me tell you a small tale of Orunmila’s wisdom, for his wisdom is not the cold cleverness that wins arguments but the deep understanding that saves lives. It is said that Orunmila’s power came not from being the strongest or the fiercest of the orisa, for he was neither. In the councils of the orisa, where Ogun brought iron and Sango brought fire and the others brought their mighty forces, Orunmila brought only knowledge, only understanding, only the ability to see what was truly happening and to know what should truly be done. And again and again it was Orunmila’s quiet wisdom, not the others’ loud power, that solved the problems that power could not touch."}, {"id": "orisa-orunmila#s8", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "For there are troubles in this world, my child, that no sword can cut and no fire can burn and no flood can wash away, troubles that yield only to understanding. When a sickness has a hidden cause, when a family is torn by a wrong no one will name, when a person walks a road of endless misfortune and cannot see why, when the very destiny a soul chose has become tangled and the person has lost their way upon it, then all the iron of Ogun and all the fire of Sango are useless, and only Orunmila can help, only the one who sees the hidden pattern, who remembers the forgotten choice, who knows the verse that names the trouble and the sacrifice that heals it. Wisdom, my child, is the power that works where all other powers fail."}, {"id": "orisa-orunmila#s9", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "And here is a thing about Orunmila that I want you to carry above all others, for it is gentle and it is wise and it is a comfort. Orunmila’s whole purpose, the whole purpose of Ifa, is not to trap us in a fate we cannot escape. It is the very opposite. It is to help us. Understand this: even the destiny you chose in heaven can be helped or hindered by how you live. A good destiny can be spoiled by bad character or bad choices in the world. A hard destiny can be softened, its worst dangers avoided, its hidden goods reached, by wisdom and by right action and by the sacrifices Ifa advises. Orunmila does not tell you your fate so that you may despair of it. He tells you so that you may cooperate with the good in it and steer around the bad. He is the friend of humankind, the orisa who took pity on us for forgetting our own destinies, and who consented to be our guide, our reminder, our counselor on the dark road."}, {"id": "orisa-orunmila#s10", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "This is why he is beloved, and why his priests, the babalawo, held such honour among our people, for they were the doctors and the counselors and the wise elders and the keepers of all knowledge, the ones you went to in your deepest trouble, who knew the ancient verse for every human sorrow because they had memorized the accumulated wisdom of a thousand years. To sit before a true babalawo, my child, was to sit before the whole remembered mind of your people, speaking to your single small trouble with the voice of ten centuries."}, {"id": "orisa-orunmila#s11", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "His worship is quieter than the others, fitting for wisdom. His colours are green and yellow, or brown, the colours of the palm and the earth. His sacred number is sixteen, for the sixteen palm nuts and the sixteen principal odu. His devotees keep his shrine with the ikin, the sacred nuts, and the divining tray, the opon Ifa, carved with the face of Esu at its edge, for remember, my child, that Esu the messenger works alongside Orunmila, carrying between the diviner and the divine, so that the two are partners, the wisdom and the road it travels on. And the greeting of Ifa’s people is aboru aboye, a prayer that the sacrifice may be accepted and bring life."}, {"id": "orisa-orunmila#s12", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So this is Orunmila, the witness to destiny, the keeper of the great wisdom, the gentle guide of the forgetful. And what he asks of you, my child, is not that you should live in fear of a fixed and frozen fate, but that you should seek wisdom, always, in every trouble; that you should remember you chose your own road and can walk it well or badly; that you should honour knowledge and the ones who keep it; and that you should know, when you face a trouble no strength can solve, that there is a deeper power than strength, the power of understanding, and that it was placed in the world by an orisa who loved us enough to remember, on our behalf, the very selves we had forgotten."}, {"id": "orisa-orunmila#s13", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "Tomorrow, if the ancestors permit, we will return to fire and iron, but from a different door, and I will tell you of Ogun’s own great festival at the town of Ire, and of the day the lord of iron is honoured with the dog and the palm frond and the blood of the hunt, and of what that festival still means to the smiths and the drivers and the soldiers of today. But tonight belongs to wisdom, to the quiet click of the palm nuts, and we will let Orunmila’s calm counsel carry us gently down into sleep."}]}, {"id": "orisa-ogun-festival-ire", "emoji": "⚡", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#150020,#350050)", "category": "orisa", "rating": 9.6, "duration": "11 min", "title": {"en": "Ogun’s Festival at Ire: The Day the Iron Is Fed", "yo": "Odun Ogun"}, "desc": {"en": "The yearly festival at Ire where the lord of iron is fed.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Orisa", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Orisa, the story of the great yearly festival of Ogun at the town of Ire, where the lord of iron is honoured with the dog, the palm frond, and the blood of the hunt, and what it still means today)", "segments": [{"id": "orisa-ogun-festival-ire#s1", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Come, my child, for tonight I take you not to the beginning of an orisa but to a place, and to a day, for some of the deepest truths of a people are found not in the old myths but in what the people still do, year after year, with their own hands and feet and voices. Tonight I take you to the town of Ire, and to the festival of Ogun, for there is an old saying among us, Ogun onire, Ogun the lord of Ire, and the town of Ire holds a special and ancient place in the worship of the lord of iron. I told you already the great tale of Ogun, of how he cut the road from heaven and of how his rage destroyed those he loved. Tonight I tell you how the living honour him still, and why the festival matters as much as the myth."}, {"id": "orisa-ogun-festival-ire#s2", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "For you must understand, my child, that our religion was never only a matter of old stories told at fires like this one. It is a living thing, done and danced and drummed and eaten and offered, every year, in the towns and the shrines, by people who are alive today. The myths are the roots, but the festivals are the tree still bearing fruit. And of all the festivals, the festival of Ogun is among the most powerful, the most necessary, and, I will be honest with you, the most fierce, for you cannot honour the lord of iron with soft and pretty things. You honour fire with fire."}, {"id": "orisa-ogun-festival-ire#s3", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "The festival of Ogun comes each year, and in the towns where the smiths and the hunters and the warriors have always lived, it is a great matter, a holy obligation. For think, my child, who depends upon Ogun. The blacksmith, whose every tool and whose whole livelihood is iron. The hunter, whose spear and whose knife and whose very life in the dangerous forest depend upon the faithfulness of his iron and the favour of its lord. The farmer, whose hoe and cutlass are iron. The warrior and the soldier. And in our own day, the driver, the mechanic, the welder, everyone whose life or living touches metal and machine. All of these are Ogun’s people, and once a year they must feed the iron, must honour its lord, must renew the covenant between themselves and the fierce power that both serves them and could destroy them."}, {"id": "orisa-ogun-festival-ire#s4", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "To feed the iron. That is the heart of it, and I will explain it plainly. On the festival day, the objects of iron are gathered, the tools, the weapons, the implements of every trade that uses metal, and they are cleaned and adorned, often with palm fronds, the young pale fronds called mariwo that are sacred to Ogun and hung wherever he is honoured, and offerings are made upon them and to them. For the belief is that the iron, like the orisa himself, must be fed, must be given its portion, so that it will remain faithful, so that the blade will cut true and not turn in the hand, so that the vehicle will run and not crash, so that the tool will serve and not betray. An iron that is honoured serves its master. An iron that is neglected, like an orisa that is neglected, may turn dangerous."}, {"id": "orisa-ogun-festival-ire#s5", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Now I must tell you of the sacrifice, and I will not hide it from you, for a true telling does not flinch, and you are old enough to hear it. The creature most sacred to Ogun, and most often offered to him, is the dog. This may seem strange or even hard to you, but hear the reason, for there is a reason, and it is beautiful in its own fierce way. The dog is the hunter’s companion, the faithful one who goes with the hunter into the dangerous forest, who faces the beast alongside its master, who is loyal unto death. The dog belongs to Ogun’s world of the hunt and the wild and the courage that faces danger. And so the dog, the most faithful and courageous of animals, is given to the most fierce and demanding of the orisa, and in the giving the people ask Ogun to be as faithful to them as the dog is faithful, and to grant them the dog’s own courage in facing the dangers of their lives and their work."}, {"id": "orisa-ogun-festival-ire#s6", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "Palm wine is poured, though, remember, my child, that Ogun’s own thirst for palm wine once brought his catastrophe, so there is always in this a note of solemn warning within the celebration. Roasted yam and beans and oil are offered. Snails, sometimes, for their coolness, to cool the hot orisa. And the blood of the offering is poured upon the iron, feeding it, renewing it, binding once more the covenant between the people and the power. And through it all the drums beat the fierce rhythms of Ogun, and his devotees dance the hard, jerking, powerful dance of iron and war, and some, taken by the orisa, may handle iron and even fire and take no harm."}, {"id": "orisa-ogun-festival-ire#s7", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "But let me tell you what I have seen with my own eyes, my child, for I have stood at the festival of Ogun, and it is a thing that stays in the marrow. There is a moment when the whole fierce energy of the town gathers, the smiths and the hunters and the drivers, the young men especially, and there is a wildness in the air, a heat, a sense of a great and dangerous power drawn very near. It is not a gentle festival like the sweet river-worship of Oshun. It is loud and hot and a little frightening, as Ogun himself is loud and hot and a little frightening. And yet, at the center of all that fierce energy, there is a deep and serious reverence, a real awe, for the people know exactly what they are dealing with. They are honouring the power that clears the road and cuts the enemy and does the hard dangerous work no one else will do, and they know it must be kept fed, kept honoured, kept in covenant, or it will turn."}, {"id": "orisa-ogun-festival-ire#s8", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "And here, my child, is the wisdom of the festival, the reason it is not mere old superstition but a deep truth dressed in ancient clothes. The festival of Ogun is a yearly reminder that the powerful and dangerous forces we depend upon, the iron, the fire, the machine, the weapon, the very tools of our civilization, must be respected, tended, honoured, kept in right relationship, or they will destroy us. Is this not true, my child, even in the world of today? The car that carries you can kill you if you do not respect it. The machine that feeds you can maim you if you grow careless before it. The fire that cooks your food can burn down your house. The very power that built our world can end it. The festival of Ogun says, once a year, in blood and drum and palm frond: remember that you live among mighty and dangerous powers, and treat them with the respect they demand, and they will serve you; grow careless and neglectful and disrespectful, and they will turn in your hand."}, {"id": "orisa-ogun-festival-ire#s9", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "There is a particular tie to the town of Ire, as I said, for Ogun is called onire, the lord of Ire, and in the old telling it was at or near Ire that some of the great events of Ogun’s earthly wandering took place, that he came to that region and became its lord, and it was near there, in some accounts, that his final catastrophe and his descent into the earth occurred. And so the people of Ire hold his worship as a special inheritance and a special burden, tending the covenant with the fierce orisa on behalf, you might say, of all who use iron everywhere."}, {"id": "orisa-ogun-festival-ire#s10", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "And on the driver’s festival days that grew from this ancient root, you will still see, on any road in Yorubaland, the lorries and the taxis decorated, palm fronds tied to them, offerings made, the drivers pouring a little something to Ogun and asking the lord of iron to keep the machine faithful and the road open and the journey safe. It is the festival of Ire, my child, come down the centuries and out onto the modern highway, the same covenant, the same feeding of the iron, the same fierce respect for the dangerous power we cannot live without."}, {"id": "orisa-ogun-festival-ire#s11", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "So when you next see a lorry hung with palm fronds, or hear the fierce drums of a festival, or even simply pick up a knife or ride in a car, remember the festival of Ogun at Ire. Remember that our people learned, long ago, a truth the whole world is still learning: that power must be honoured, that the forces which serve us can also destroy us, and that the wise do not take their dangerous servants for granted but feed them, tend them, respect them, and keep the covenant. Feed the iron, my child, in whatever form the iron takes in your own life, and it will serve you. Neglect it, and one day it will turn."}, {"id": "orisa-ogun-festival-ire#s12", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "Tomorrow, if the ancestors are willing, I will tell you of a strange and beautiful thing, of the night when the orisa themselves are said to walk among us, of the masks and the darkness and the powers that move when the drums call them down. But tonight belongs to the lord of iron and his fierce festival, and we will bank the fire carefully, with respect, as one does all fire, and let it carry us to sleep."}]}, {"id": "orisa-night-orisa-walk", "emoji": "⚡", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#150020,#350050)", "category": "orisa", "rating": 9.6, "duration": "11 min", "title": {"en": "The Night Orisa Walk: When the Masks Come Out", "yo": "The Night Orisa Walk: When the Masks Come Out"}, "desc": {"en": "The sacred night when the powers walk among the living and the masks come out.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Orisa", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Orisa, the story of the sacred night when the powers are said to walk among the living, of the masquerades and the darkness and the awe that binds a people to its ancestors)", "segments": [{"id": "orisa-night-orisa-walk#s1", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Lower your voice tonight, my child, and do not point, for tonight I tell you of the night the orisa walk, of the sacred hour when the powers come out from the groves and move among the living, and of the masks that carry them, and though we are only speaking of it and not living it, it is wise, always, to speak of such things with respect, for the old people say that what you speak of with your mouth may turn its face toward you. So sit still, and listen, and learn one of the deepest mysteries of our people: the mystery of the masquerade, of the night walk, of the moment when the wall between the living and the unseen grows thin."}, {"id": "orisa-night-orisa-walk#s2", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "There are certain nights, my child, in the round of the sacred year, when it is said the boundary between our world and the world of the spirits, the world of the orisa and the ancestors, grows thin as worn cloth, and the powers cross over and walk among us. On such nights, in the old towns, an order would go out, and the women and the uninitiated would go indoors and shutter their windows and put out their lamps, and the streets would empty and fall silent, and then, in the deep dark, you would hear it beginning: the drums, far off, and a strange voice that was not quite a human voice, and the soft or heavy tread of something walking that was no longer only a man."}, {"id": "orisa-night-orisa-walk#s3", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "For this is the mystery of the masquerade, and I must teach it to you carefully, for it is easy to misunderstand. When the mask comes out, when the great costume of cloth and raffia and carved wood moves through the dark street, we do not say, that is a man wearing a costume. We say, the ancestor has come. The spirit is present. The one who walks is not the man beneath the mask, for the man beneath the mask has, for that sacred time, ceased to be himself and become the vessel, the horse, the carrier of a power greater than himself. The mask does not represent the spirit. The mask, when the rites are done and the drums have called and the power has descended, is the spirit, present and walking, for that holy while."}, {"id": "orisa-night-orisa-walk#s4", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Do you understand the depth of this, my child? It is one of the most profound ideas our people ever held. Through the masquerade, through the egungun that carries the ancestors and the great orisa masks that carry the powers, the dead are not gone and the gods are not distant. Once a year, on the night they walk, the boundary opens, and the great-grandfather you never met, and the founding ancestors of your whole line, and the very powers that shaped the world, come back into the streets of the living town and move among their descendants, blessing, warning, judging, remembering. Death did not take them away forever. It only moved them behind a thin curtain, and on the sacred nights the curtain opens and they return."}, {"id": "orisa-night-orisa-walk#s5", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "I will tell you what it is like, for I have felt it, and there is nothing else in all of life quite like it. You are in your house in the deep dark, the lamp out, the window shuttered, and you hear the drums coming closer, and the voice, that unearthly voice that raises the hair on your arms because it is not the voice of any living throat you know. And you hear the tread coming down your own street, and you know, you know in your body and not just your mind, that something is passing outside your wall that is older and greater and stranger than yourself, that the ancestors are abroad, that the powers are loose in the world tonight. And a great awe falls upon you, my child, a holy fear, not the fear of a threat but the fear of standing very near to something vast, the way you might feel standing at the edge of the sea in a storm, or beneath the sky when the lightning walks. It is the feeling the whole festival exists to give, and it changes a person to feel it, even once."}, {"id": "orisa-night-orisa-walk#s6", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "And then, in the morning, the sun comes up, and the streets are ordinary again, and the drums are silent, and the mask has gone back to the grove, and the man is a man again. But you are not quite the same. You have been reminded, in your very flesh, that you are not alone in the world, that you stand in a long line of the living and the dead, that the ones who came before have not abandoned you, and that there are powers and presences beyond the small bright circle of the everyday. And a person who has been reminded of that, my child, lives differently. They walk more humbly. They remember they are watched, and accompanied, and held, by more than they can see."}, {"id": "orisa-night-orisa-walk#s7", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "Now I must tell you of the different kinds of walkers in the night, for they are not all the same. There are the egungun, the masquerades of the ancestors, who come robed head to foot in layers of rich cloth, so that no part of the man beneath is seen, for the ancestor must be wholly covered, wholly other, and they whirl and spin so that their many-coloured cloths fly out, and it is said that to touch an egungun, or to be touched by it carelessly, is a grave and dangerous thing, for you are touching the very presence of the dead. These carry the ancestors, the family dead, the founders of the lineage, come back to visit and to bless their descendants."}, {"id": "orisa-night-orisa-walk#s8", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "And there are the great orisa masks and processions, when the powers themselves, Obatala the cool, Sango the fierce, and the others, are carried in procession, embodied in their priests who have gone into possession, dancing the dance of the god, speaking with the voice of the god, blessing and judging with the authority of the god. On these occasions the orisa is present in the street, in the body of the possessed priest, as truly present, the people believe, as ever the orisa was present in the age of myth. The god walks. The god dances. The god speaks. And the people crowd to receive the blessing and the word of a power come down among them."}, {"id": "orisa-night-orisa-walk#s9", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "And there are darker and more secret walkers too, my child, that I will only mention and not describe, the powers that enforce and that judge, that move in the deepest dark and are not for the eyes of the uninitiated at all, whose business is with the hidden law of the community and the punishment of the secret wrong. Of these I will say only that our people knew there must be, in any community, forces that see what is hidden and answer what the daylight courts cannot reach, and they gave these forces mask and mystery and the authority of the night. Enough of them. We speak of them softly and we pass on."}, {"id": "orisa-night-orisa-walk#s10", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "But hold onto the great meaning of it all, my child, for beneath the drums and the masks and the awe there is a truth as deep as any our people ever knew, and it is a truth of comfort. It is this: that we are not alone, and that the dead are not lost. In the world the strangers brought, death is often a wall, a final ending, a going into silence and separation forever. But in the world of the night walk, death is a thin curtain, not a wall, and the ones who pass through it are not gone but only moved a little way off, and they return, they return every year, they come back down the street of the living and let their descendants feel their nearness. Your ancestors are watching you, my child. They have not forgotten you. And one sacred night each year, they put on the cloth and the raffia and the carved wood, and they come back to walk among the ones they love, to remind us all that the family is not broken by death, that the line holds, that the ones who went before still care what becomes of us."}, {"id": "orisa-night-orisa-walk#s11", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "So when you hear, someday, the far-off drums on a night when the streets have gone strangely quiet, and that voice that is not quite a living voice, do not be merely afraid, though awe is right and proper. Remember the night orisa walk. Remember that the boundary has grown thin, and that the ancestors and the powers are abroad, and that they walk not to harm you but to remind you: that you are held in a great company of the living and the dead, that you are watched over, that you are never as alone as the daylight world would have you believe. Shutter your window if the custom says to, and be still, and feel the awe, and let it make you humble and make you grateful, for you have been visited, my child, by everyone who ever loved you into being."}, {"id": "orisa-night-orisa-walk#s12", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "Tomorrow, if the ancestors are willing, and having felt their nearness tonight, we will speak of another of the great powers, of the fierce lady of the storm winds, of the guardian of the gates of the dead, the warrior queen Oya, wife of Sango, who carries the whirlwind in her hands. But tonight belongs to the walkers in the dark, and we will keep the fire low, and speak softly, and be grateful for the company we cannot see."}]}, {"id": "orisa-oya", "emoji": "⚡", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#150020,#350050)", "category": "orisa", "rating": 9.6, "duration": "12 min", "title": {"en": "Oya: The Lady of the Whirlwind", "yo": "Oya"}, "desc": {"en": "The warrior queen of the whirlwind, wife of Sango and guardian of the gate of the dead, who carries change in her hand.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Orisa", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Orisa, the story of the fierce warrior queen of the storm winds, wife of Sango, guardian of the gate of the dead, who carries change in her hands)", "segments": [{"id": "orisa-oya#s1", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Feel the wind rising tonight, my child, how it bends the tops of the trees and scatters the ashes at the edge of our fire? Good. That is fitting, for tonight we speak of the lady of the wind, of the fiercest of all the women among the orisa, of the warrior queen who carries the whirlwind in her two hands and the gate of the dead upon her shoulder. Tonight we speak of Oya. And I will tell you at once, so that you carry it through the whole telling: Oya is change. She is the great and terrible force of change itself, the storm that tears down the old so that the new may stand, and change, my child, is the one thing in all the world that no one can escape and few can love, and Oya is its queen."}, {"id": "orisa-oya#s2", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Oya is the orisa of the strong winds, of the storm, of the tornado and the whirlwind, of the sudden violent tempest that comes up out of a calm sky and rearranges the whole face of the land in an hour. She is the orisa of the river Niger, the great river we call Odo Oya, whose floods can rise and change everything in their path. She is a warrior, a fighter fiercer than most of the male orisa, who goes to battle at the side of her husband and is more terrible in the fight than he. And she is the guardian of the gate between the living and the dead, the one who stands at the boundary of the cemetery, at the very threshold of the great change from life into death. Wind, war, and the door of death: these are Oya’s, and you can see already that she is not a gentle orisa, and does not pretend to be."}, {"id": "orisa-oya#s3", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "She is the wife of Sango, the thunder king, and theirs is the great stormy marriage of the orisa, and you cannot fully know either of them without the other. For think, my child: what comes with the thunder and the lightning? The wind. The great rushing wind that goes before the storm, that bends the trees and darkens the day and announces that Sango is coming. That wind is Oya. She goes before her husband into battle and into storm, clearing his path, driving his enemies before her, and it is said that Sango’s power is doubled when Oya fights beside him, for she brings the wind that fans his fire and the storm that carries his thunder. Fire needs wind to become an inferno, my child, and Oya is Sango’s wind. Alone, each is mighty. Together, they are the whole tempest, and nothing stands before them."}, {"id": "orisa-oya#s4", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "There is a tale that shows her fierce devotion. It is said that Oya was so bound to Sango, so much a part of him, that when Sango met his end and passed from the world, when the king hanged, as his enemies cried, and ascended to become the thunder, Oya would not be parted from him. She followed him. She too left the world of the living and passed into power, so that they might remain together, storm and wind, thunder and tempest, forever. Some say she became the river, some say she became the wind that always accompanies the thunder, but all agree that she would not let even death divide her from the one she loved and fought beside. There is a fierce faithfulness in Oya, my child, the faithfulness of the storm to the fire, and it is one of her deepest qualities beneath all her terror."}, {"id": "orisa-oya#s5", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "But now I must tell you of Oya as the guardian of the gate of the dead, for this is her most solemn office and her deepest mystery. Oya stands at the threshold of the cemetery, at the boundary between the living and the dead, and she is the one who governs that great passage. When a person dies, it is Oya’s wind that carries the spirit across the threshold, Oya who stands at the gate and manages the mighty change from life into whatever lies beyond. And because of this, she is deeply connected to the egungun, to the ancestral masquerades I told you of, to the whole traffic between the living and the dead, for she is the keeper of the door they must all pass through, in both directions."}, {"id": "orisa-oya#s6", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "Think of what this means, my child. The fiercest, most warlike, most storm-wild of the orisa is also the tender guardian of the passage of death, the one who stands at the gate and sees each soul across. And is this not right? For death is the greatest storm, the greatest change, the greatest tearing-down of the old so that something new may be, and who should govern the greatest change but the queen of change herself? The same power that sends the tornado to level the forest sends the wind that carries the soul across the final threshold. Destruction and passage, ending and crossing-over, these are one thing seen from two sides, and Oya rules them both."}, {"id": "orisa-oya#s7", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "Now here is the wisdom of Oya, the teaching hidden in her terror, and it is a teaching this age of the world needs as much as any. We fear change, my child. We fear it more than almost anything. We build our lives and our houses and our certainties and we want them to stand forever, unchanged, and when the wind of change comes and tears them down, we grieve and we rage and we call it disaster. But Oya teaches a harder and a truer thing. She teaches that change is not the enemy of life but its very engine, that the storm which tears down the old and dead makes room for the new and living, that the field must be cleared before it can be planted, that the forest must sometimes burn before it can grow again fresh and green, that even death, the greatest change, is a gate and not a wall, a passage and not an ending."}, {"id": "orisa-oya#s8", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Oya is the wind that clears away. And though we fear her, though her coming is violent and frightening and full of loss, we cannot live without her, for a world without change would be a world without growth, without renewal, without birth, a stagnant and finally a dead world. The old must pass for the new to come. The dead leaf must fall for the bud to open. And when your own life is torn by some great change, some loss, some ending, some storm that levels what you had built, it is Oya who is passing through, my child, and hard as it is to believe in the moment, she is not only destroying. She is clearing. She is making room. She is carrying you across a threshold to something you cannot yet see. The one who can learn to trust Oya, to bend before the wind of change instead of breaking against it, to believe that the tearing-down is also a making-room, that one has learned a secret that makes the whole of life easier to bear."}, {"id": "orisa-oya#s9", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Her worship reflects her fierce nature. Her colours are the dark reds and purples and browns of the storm sky, and the many colours mixed together, for the whirlwind gathers up everything. Her number is nine, and she is called Oya of the nine, for the nine tributaries of her river, or the nine children she is said to have borne. She loves the buffalo, the wild and untameable buffalo, and can take its shape, charging and unstoppable. Her devotees dance the wild, whirling, wind-driven dance of the storm, and they carry the flywhisk, the horsetail switch, with which she clears the air and drives away all that is stagnant and dead. And she is honoured especially in the rites of the dead, for she governs that gate, and no funeral among her people is complete without acknowledging the lady who stands at the threshold and carries the soul across."}, {"id": "orisa-oya#s10", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "She is not to be trifled with, my child. Of all the orisa, Oya is among the most powerful and the most volatile, quick to rise like the sudden storm, and her anger, when it comes, comes like the tornado, without warning, leaving the landscape changed forever. But she is also, to those who honour her and understand her, a mighty protector and a fierce mother, for the same storm-power that destroys her enemies shelters her children, and the same wind that levels the wicked carries the faithful across every threshold to safety. She is fierce, but she is not cruel. She is the storm, and the storm has no malice, only power, and the power falls where it will."}, {"id": "orisa-oya#s11", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "So when the wind rises, my child, as it is rising now around our little fire, do not only shelter from it. Feel it. Remember Oya, the lady of the whirlwind, the warrior queen, the wife of the thunder, the guardian of the gate of the dead. Remember that she is change itself, the one force none of us can escape, and that she is not only to be feared but to be understood, and even, in time, to be trusted. For she will come to your life as surely as she comes to every life, with her storm and her tearing-down and her terrible clearing-away, and when she comes, you can break against her in rage and grief, or you can learn to bend, and to trust the wind, and to believe that beyond the storm she is clearing a field for a planting you cannot yet imagine, and carrying you across a threshold toward a morning you cannot yet see."}, {"id": "orisa-oya#s12", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "And with Oya, my child, we come to a resting place in our telling of the great orisa, though there are many more, as many as the winds, and we will return to them, to the other powers and their mysteries, on other nights. But we have walked now with the shaper of heads and the lord of iron, with the sweet river and the great sea, with the keeper of the crossroads and the king who became the thunder, with the witness to destiny and the walkers in the night, and now with the lady of the storm. Let the wind she sends carry you gently, tonight, across the small nightly threshold of sleep, and bring you safe again to the morning. Rest now, my child. The fire is low, and the wind is only Oya, passing by, on her way to clear some far field for a planting still to come."}]}, {"id": "orisa-osanyin", "emoji": "⚡", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#150020,#350050)", "category": "orisa", "rating": 9.6, "duration": "12 min", "title": {"en": "Osanyin: The One-Legged Lord of the Leaves", "yo": "Osanyin"}, "desc": {"en": "The one-legged orisa of herbs and healing, master of every leaf in the forest.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Orisa", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Orisa, consolidates the tellings of Osanyin across the collection, the story of the orisa of herbs and healing, master of every leaf in the forest, without whose knowledge no medicine works)", "segments": [{"id": "orisa-osanyin#s1", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Come, my child, but first look about you at the darkness beyond our little fire, at the great forest breathing out there in the night, and know that every single leaf on every tree in all that darkness has a name, and a power, and a use, and that there is one orisa who knows them all, every last one, and without him not one of them will heal you. Tonight we speak of Osanyin, the lord of the leaves, the orisa of herbs and healing and the deep green medicine of the forest, the one-legged master of all growing things. And I promise you, my child, that by the end of this telling you will never again walk past a common weed by the roadside without wondering what secret it holds, for that is the first lesson of Osanyin: there is no such thing as a useless plant. There are only plants whose gift we have not yet learned."}, {"id": "orisa-osanyin#s2", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Osanyin is the orisa of the forest’s healing power. Every herb, every leaf, every root and bark and flower that can cure a sickness, ease a pain, mend a wound, calm a troubled mind, or, yes, harm an enemy, for the same knowledge that heals can also hurt, all of it belongs to Osanyin, and all of it obeys him. He is the great herbalist, the master of the green pharmacy of the forest, the keeper of the knowledge of what each plant can do. And here is a thing you must understand deeply, my child, for it is the heart of our healing: among our people, medicine is never only the plant. The leaf alone does not heal. It is the leaf together with the knowledge, the word, the incantation, the right gathering and the right speaking, and all of that knowing belongs to Osanyin. The herbalist, the onisegun, who gathers and prepares the medicine, works always as the servant of Osanyin, and must honour him, or the medicine, however correct the leaf, will not work."}, {"id": "orisa-osanyin#s3", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Now I must tell you why Osanyin has but one leg, for this is his great and strange tale, and it teaches something you would not expect. Osanyin is most often described as having a single leg, one arm, and one eye, and speaking in a thin, high, whistling voice like the wind through the reeds, a small strange being, not grand and beautiful like Oshun or mighty like Sango, but odd, lopsided, easy to overlook. And yet this small strange one-legged being holds a power that all the other orisa need and none of them can do without, for who among them does not, sooner or later, need healing?"}, {"id": "orisa-osanyin#s4", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "There is a tale of how the great orisa, and even Orunmila himself, the lord of wisdom, came to depend on Osanyin. For it happened, the tale says, that Orunmila recognized that Osanyin held the secret of the leaves, the knowledge of the medicines that could make his divination truly effective, for it is one thing to know through Ifa what sacrifice or what cure a person needs, and another thing to know the exact leaf that carries the healing. And so a great partnership grew between wisdom and medicine, between Orunmila who knows what is wrong and Osanyin who knows the leaf that mends it. The two work together, my child, the diagnosis and the cure, and to this day the babalawo and the herbalist are partners in the healing of the people."}, {"id": "orisa-osanyin#s5", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "But the leaves nearly caused a catastrophe, and this is the most important tale of Osanyin, so listen well. It is said that Osanyin kept all the secrets of the leaves to himself, hoarded in his own strange head, so that no one could heal without coming to him, and this gave him great power but also made him a single point on which all healing depended, which is a dangerous thing. In some tellings, Orunmila, wishing that this vast knowledge should not be lost or hoarded by one being alone, arranged for the secrets of the leaves to be scattered, spread out, distributed, so that the knowledge of medicine would belong to many and not be locked in one strange head that might one day be lost."}, {"id": "orisa-osanyin#s6", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "And it is told that there came a great wind, a mighty storm, that shook the tree in which Osanyin kept his gourds and calabashes full of the powdered secrets of every leaf, and the storm scattered them, blew them out across the whole forest, so that the knowledge of the leaves was spread everywhere, into every corner of the bush. And as the powders scattered on the wind, the leaves themselves cried out, each one calling its own name and its own power: I am the leaf for this fever, I am the root for that wound, I am the bark that calms the troubled heart. And the herbalists who were wise enough to listen learned the names and powers of the leaves from the leaves’ own crying in that great scattering, and so the knowledge of medicine spread among many healers and was not lost."}, {"id": "orisa-osanyin#s7", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "Do you see the wisdom, my child? The tale is teaching that knowledge, especially healing knowledge, is too precious and too necessary to be hoarded by any single one, that it must be scattered, shared, spread among many, so that it cannot be lost and cannot be held hostage. And yet, and here is the balance, the knowledge still belongs to Osanyin, still requires his blessing, so that the sharing does not become a careless free-for-all in which the dangerous power of the leaves, for remember they can harm as well as heal, falls into unworthy or ignorant hands. It is a delicate teaching about knowledge: that it must be shared so it is not lost, and yet held sacred so it is not abused. The leaves are scattered to all, but Osanyin still watches over how they are used."}, {"id": "orisa-osanyin#s8", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "For this is the shadow-side of Osanyin’s domain, and an honest telling must name it: the same forest that holds every cure holds every poison. The leaf that heals in one dose kills in another. The knowledge that mends a wound can, in a wicked hand, work a curse. Osanyin governs both, the medicine and the poison, the healing and the harming, for they are the same knowledge turned to different ends, and this is why his power is approached with such care and such respect, and why the true herbalist is bound by so many oaths and taboos, that the great green power of the leaves shall be used to heal and to help and not to harm."}, {"id": "orisa-osanyin#s9", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Now let me tell you what this means for us, still, today, for Osanyin is not a dead god of an old forest but a living truth. Our people, my child, developed over the long centuries an immense knowledge of the healing power of plants, a true and effective medicine, a green pharmacy of the forest that could treat a great range of the sicknesses that afflict us. This was not superstition. This was observation, and experiment, and accumulated wisdom, passed down through the herbalists, the onisegun, generation after generation, all of it held under the patronage of Osanyin. Long before the strangers came with their medicines, our herbalists could set a bone, dress a wound so it would not fester, bring down a fever, ease a birth, calm a madness, treat a hundred ailments with the leaves and roots and barks that Osanyin governs. And much of that knowledge is real, and true, and is only now being confirmed by those who once dismissed it, for the world is slowly learning that the lord of the leaves knew things worth knowing."}, {"id": "orisa-osanyin#s10", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "And so Osanyin stands, my child, as the guardian of a great truth we are in danger of forgetting: that the forest is a pharmacy, that the green world around us is full of healing we have only begun to understand, that the knowledge of the leaves is a treasure to be honoured and preserved and not paved over and lost. When the forest is cut down, Osanyin’s library burns, and cures we never learned are lost forever. When the old herbalists die without passing on their knowledge, gourds of Osanyin’s secrets are lost on the wind again, but this time with no one wise enough to listen and learn the leaves’ names. To honour Osanyin, today, is to honour and protect and learn from the green wisdom of the earth before it is gone."}, {"id": "orisa-osanyin#s11", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "His worship suits his nature: quiet, green, secret, tied to the forest. His shrine is often marked by the iron staff topped with sixteen birds and a central bird, the opa Osanyin, a beautiful and mysterious emblem. His devotees are the herbalists and healers, and they gather the leaves with prayer and with the proper words, for remember, the leaf without the word does not heal. His voice, the thin high whistling voice, is imitated in his rites. And he is honoured wherever healing is sought through the green medicine of the forest, which is to say, wherever our people have kept faith with the old knowledge."}, {"id": "orisa-osanyin#s12", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So when you next walk past a common weed by the roadside, my child, or brush through the bush at the forest’s edge, remember Osanyin, the one-legged lord of the leaves. Remember that there is no useless plant, only unknown gifts. Remember that the forest is a great library of healing, written in green, and that we are losing the ability to read it. And remember the deepest teaching of the scattered gourds: that healing knowledge must be shared so that it is never lost, and yet held sacred so that it is never abused, and that the one who holds the power to heal holds also the power to harm, and must be bound, always, by the oath to use the leaves for life."}, {"id": "orisa-osanyin#s13", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "Tomorrow, if the ancestors are willing, we will speak of a much more fearsome power, of the hot orisa of the earth, the lord of the dreaded sickness, the one whose very name we do not speak aloud but call by soft praise-names so as not to summon him, the terrible and yet strangely merciful Sopona. But tonight belongs to the healer, to the green lord of the leaves, and we will let his cool medicine carry us gently down into a healing sleep."}]}, {"id": "orisa-sopona", "emoji": "⚡", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#150020,#350050)", "category": "orisa", "rating": 9.6, "duration": "11 min", "title": {"en": "Sopona: The Hot Lord of the Earth", "yo": "Sopona"}, "desc": {"en": "The feared orisa of the earth and smallpox, whose name is not spoken aloud.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Orisa", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Orisa, the story of the feared orisa of the earth and the dreaded sickness, whose name is not spoken aloud, and who teaches both terror and a strange mercy)", "segments": [{"id": "orisa-sopona#s1", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Now I must ask you, my child, to be very still, and I will speak softly, and I will not say the great name aloud more than I must, for tonight we speak of a power before whom even the boldest of our people lower their voices. Tonight we speak of the hot lord of the earth, the orisa of the dreaded sickness, the one whose true name we so fear to summon that we call him instead by soft and flattering praise-names: the hot one, the lord of the open earth, the king, the one who owns the world. Some call him Sopona, some Sakpata, some Obaluaye, king and lord of the world, or Babaluaye, father and lord of the world, and these gentle titles are used precisely so as not to speak his true name and draw his hot attention. Tonight we speak of him, respectfully, quietly, and we ask him, as we begin, to pass by our door in peace."}, {"id": "orisa-sopona#s2", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "He is the orisa of the earth, of the ground itself, and of the terrible sicknesses that once swept through the land like fire through dry grass, above all the dreaded pox that scarred and blinded and killed, that came without warning and emptied whole compounds and whole towns. In the old days, my child, before the medicines of this present age, such a sickness was the most terrifying thing that could befall a people, for it could not be fought, could not be reasoned with, could not be stopped, and it fell upon the innocent and the guilty alike, the child and the elder, the good and the wicked, without mercy and without explanation. And our people, needing to understand a terror they could not control, gave it a lord, and named him, softly, the hot one, the king of the earth."}, {"id": "orisa-sopona#s3", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Now you might think, my child, that such a power would be simply hated, simply feared, an enemy and nothing more. But here is where our people showed their deep wisdom, and where the tale grows subtle, so attend closely. Sopona was not seen as a mere monster, an evil to be only cursed. He was seen as a king, a great and powerful orisa, terrible, yes, but also just in his own hot way, and even, strangely, merciful, and worthy of the deepest respect. For why do we honour and even court the power we most fear? Because the one who can send the sickness is also the one who can withhold it. The lord who owns the dreaded pox is the very lord who can spare you from it. And so, rather than only cursing him, our people honoured him, praised him with sweet names, made him offerings, kept his taboos, and asked him, as a subject asks a stern king, for mercy and for protection."}, {"id": "orisa-sopona#s4", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "There is a profound truth here, my child, about how the wise face what they cannot control. You do not master the storm by cursing it. You do not stop the sickness by hating it. What you can do is honour the power, keep right relationship with it, observe the precautions the elders have learned, and ask for mercy. And so Sopona became not only the sender of the sickness but, when properly honoured, its withholder, the protector against the very thing he governs. His priests were both feared and needed, for they knew the taboos, the precautions, the offerings that could turn the hot one’s face away from a household or a town."}, {"id": "orisa-sopona#s5", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "And here is a thing that will surprise you, my child, and that shows how much real knowledge was folded inside the worship of Sopona. His priests, the ones who served the hot lord of the earth, were also, in many places, the keepers of a real and practical knowledge about the dreaded sickness: how it spread, how the afflicted must be isolated so the sickness would not leap to others, how the survivors, marked and scarred but alive, were now safe and could tend the sick without falling ill themselves. The taboos and the sacred rules around Sopona were not only religious. Folded inside them was hard-won practical wisdom about containing a plague, knowledge our people had learned at terrible cost over many generations, preserved in the sacred customs of the hot one’s worship. The religion carried the medicine, my child, hidden inside the reverence, the way a hard seed carries the tree."}, {"id": "orisa-sopona#s6", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "It is even told, and this is a remarkable thing, that our people practiced a kind of early protection against the dreaded pox, a deliberate and careful means by which a person might take a small and survivable touch of the sickness in order to be spared the deadly full measure of it later, all done within the rites and knowledge of Sopona’s priests. Whether every telling of this is exact I cannot swear to you, for it is old and much of the knowledge was lost or driven underground, but the pattern is clear: the worship of the terrible hot lord carried within it real, practical, life-saving knowledge about the very sickness he governed. The god of the plague was also, through his priests, the keeper of the wisdom that fought the plague. There is no contradiction in this, my child. It is exactly how a wise people survives a terror it cannot defeat: it wraps its hard knowledge in reverence and passes it down as sacred law."}, {"id": "orisa-sopona#s7", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "I must tell you, too, of a sorrowful chapter, for an honest telling does not hide the hard parts. Because Sopona was so feared, and because his priests held such power, and because in some times and places that power was abused, there came an age when the worship of the hot lord was suppressed, forbidden, driven underground by the authorities of the day, who feared that his priests might spread the very sickness they claimed to control, using terror for their own ends. And so the open worship of Sopona was banned in some places, and much was lost, and the great god of the earth was pushed into the shadows. This is a sorrow, my child, for whatever abuses there may have been, a great store of real knowledge and real reverence was scattered and lost in that suppression, gourds of Osanyin’s kind of wisdom blown away on the wind with too few left to catch the names. Powers driven underground do not disappear. They only grow harder to know and to honour rightly."}, {"id": "orisa-sopona#s8", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "But the deepest teaching of Sopona, my child, the one I most want you to carry, is about the earth, and about humility. For Sopona is the lord of the earth itself, the ground, the very soil beneath your feet, into which every one of us, king and beggar, saint and sinner, will one day be laid down. And the dreaded sickness he governs is the great leveler, the power that spares no one for their wealth or their rank or their goodness, that takes the child in the palace and the child in the hut with the same hot hand. And so Sopona teaches the hardest and most necessary humility of all: that we are all, every one of us, mortal, vulnerable, made of the earth and returning to the earth, equal before the powers that can end us, and that all our pride and rank and wealth are nothing before the hot lord of the ground."}, {"id": "orisa-sopona#s9", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "This is a bitter medicine, my child, but a true one, and a healthy one for the proud heart. Remember Sopona, and you will not despise the poor, for the sickness that could take you could take them, and you are made of the same earth. Remember Sopona, and you will not grow arrogant in your health and strength, for the hot one can visit any door, and the strong body of today is the earth of tomorrow. Remember Sopona, and you will treat the ground beneath your feet, and the poor and the sick and the vulnerable, and your own mortal self, with the humility of one who knows that we are all only borrowing our breath, and that the great king of the earth is patient, and will, in the end, receive us all."}, {"id": "orisa-sopona#s10", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "His worship is solemn and careful and hedged with taboo. His colours are the reds and blacks and whites of the hot earth, and he is associated with the grains of the earth, with sesame and popcorn and dry things, with the broom that sweeps and the covering that hides. His devotees keep strict rules, for one does not approach the hot one carelessly. And always, always, his true name is kept soft in the mouth, spoken behind the flattering titles, king of the world, father and lord of the world, so as to praise him and not to summon him, to honour him and not to draw his hot attention."}, {"id": "orisa-sopona#s11", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "So we too, my child, will speak of him no more tonight, having honoured him and asked his mercy. We will thank him for the humility he teaches, and for the hard knowledge his worship carried down to us, and we will ask him, softly, to pass our door in peace, and to let all under this roof rise well in the morning. That is the right way to end a telling of the hot lord: not with fear alone, but with respect, and with a prayer for mercy, and with gratitude for the terrible lessons only he can teach."}, {"id": "orisa-sopona#s12", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "Tomorrow, if the ancestors are willing and the hot one passes us by, we will speak of a gentler and more ancient power, of the orisa of peace and of the old kingship, the cool and dignified Obalufon, patron of bronze and of cloth and of the spoken word. But tonight belongs to the lord of the earth, and we will lie down upon that same earth, humbly, as his subjects and his children, and ask him for the mercy of a peaceful sleep."}]}, {"id": "orisa-obalufon", "emoji": "⚡", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#150020,#350050)", "category": "orisa", "rating": 9.6, "duration": "11 min", "title": {"en": "Obalufon: The Cool King of Bronze and Speech", "yo": "Obalufon"}, "desc": {"en": "The orisa-king of peace, patron of bronze-casters, weavers, and speech.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Orisa", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Orisa, the story of the ancient orisa-king of peace, patron of the bronze-casters, the weavers, and the gift of speech, whose famous mask still gazes across the centuries)", "segments": [{"id": "orisa-obalufon#s1", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "After the heat of last night, my child, let us cool ourselves, for tonight we speak of one of the coolest and most dignified of all the powers, an orisa who was also a great and ancient king, the patron of peace and of the finest crafts of our people, the giver, some say, of the very gift of speech. Tonight we speak of Obalufon, and if ever you have seen, or come to see, one of the great bronze masks of old Ife, that serene and perfect face gazing out at you across seven hundred years, calm as still water, so lifelike you feel it might speak, then you have already met Obalufon, for it is his face, and it is one of the most beautiful things our people ever made."}, {"id": "orisa-obalufon#s2", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Obalufon was, in the old telling, an early king, an oba of ancient Ife, the sacred city where the world began, a successor in the line of Oduduwa himself, of whom I will tell you another night. And he was so great a king, so wise and so beneficent, so bound up with the finest achievements of his people, that after his earthly reign he was numbered among the orisa, honoured as a divine ancestor and a power. Remember, my child, that among us the line between the great king and the orisa is thin, as we saw with Sango. A ruler of sufficient greatness does not merely die; he passes into the ranks of the powers and is honoured forever. Obalufon is such a one, the good king become a god."}, {"id": "orisa-obalufon#s3", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "And what was he the king and the god of? Of peace, first of all, and this is precious. For we have spoken of fierce powers, of Ogun’s sword and Sango’s fire and Oya’s storm, the mighty destroying forces. But a people cannot live on war and fire alone. A people needs peace, needs the cool times, the settled times, the times of building and making and trading and creating, when the sword is hung on the wall and the hands turn to finer work. And Obalufon is the lord of those good cool times, the patron of peace and good governance, of the wise and gentle rule under which crafts flourish and beauty is made and people prosper. Where Ogun clears the road by force, Obalufon builds the peaceful city that the road leads to. Both are needed, my child, but blessed above all is the people whose Obalufon-times are long and whose Ogun-times are few."}, {"id": "orisa-obalufon#s4", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "He is the patron, especially, of the great crafts, of the makers of beautiful things, and above all of the bronze-casters. For you must know, my child, and know it with pride, that our ancestors of old Ife were among the greatest artists the world has ever produced. In an age long past, seven hundred years and more ago, the bronze-casters of Ife created works of such beauty, such perfect and lifelike naturalism, such consummate skill, that when strangers from far away first saw them in later centuries, they could not believe that Africans had made them, and invented all manner of foolish theories to explain them away, so unwilling were they to grant that such perfect art had come from our own ancestors’ hands. But it had. The serene bronze heads of Ife, the perfect faces, the crowned kings and queens rendered so truly you feel their breath, these are the work of our people, made under the patronage of Obalufon, the lord of the bronze-casters’ art, and they stand among the supreme artistic achievements of all humankind."}, {"id": "orisa-obalufon#s5", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Think on that, my child, and let it straighten your back. In the very age when it is sometimes pretended our ancestors made nothing of worth, they were casting bronze portraits so lifelike and so refined that the world, when it finally saw them, could scarcely believe human hands, and certainly not African hands, had made them. That art was ours. That genius was ours. And Obalufon is its divine patron, the orisa under whom that supreme skill was practiced and honoured, and to remember him is to remember the height of our ancestors’ achievement, the proof, cast in eternal bronze, of what our people can do when the Obalufon-times of peace give their hands the freedom to create."}, {"id": "orisa-obalufon#s6", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "And he is the patron of the weavers too, of cloth, of the making of beautiful textiles, another great art of our people, the strip-woven cloths and the patterned fabrics whose designs carry meaning and beauty together. Where there is fine craft, fine making, the patient creation of beautiful and useful things, there is Obalufon, blessing the work."}, {"id": "orisa-obalufon#s7", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "But the gift of Obalufon that I most want you to marvel at, my child, is stranger and greater than bronze or cloth, for it is said that Obalufon is connected with the very gift of speech itself. It is told in some traditions that Obalufon gave to humankind the power of articulate speech, the ability to shape sound into meaning, to speak, to name, to communicate mind to mind through the miracle of words. And is that not, when you truly think on it, the greatest craft of all, my child, the master-craft beneath all the others? For without speech there is no passing-on of knowledge, no teaching of the bronze-caster’s art from master to apprentice, no telling of tales at the fire like this one, no naming of the leaves, no reciting of the odu of Ifa, no law, no poetry, no praise-song, nothing of all that makes us human rather than beasts. Speech is the craft that makes all other crafts possible, the making that makes all making. And it too, this tradition says, is a gift of Obalufon, the cool king, the lord of all fine and civilizing arts."}, {"id": "orisa-obalufon#s8", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Do you see the beautiful unity of his domain, my child? Peace, and the crafts, and speech. For they are all one thing, really. They are civilization. They are what human beings build in the cool times when the wars are stilled: the beautiful object, the fine cloth, the ordered word, the well-governed city. Obalufon is the orisa of civilization itself, of the height of human making and speaking and living-together in peace, and his serene bronze face is the very image of that ideal, calm, dignified, perfected, gazing out at us across the centuries as if to say: this is what your people made, in peace; remember it; return to it."}, {"id": "orisa-obalufon#s9", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "There is a tale, too, that ties Obalufon to the deep matters of kingship and its sacred emblems, for it is said he was connected with the beaded crown, the ade, the great veiled crown of the Yoruba kings whose curtain of beads hides the oba’s face, for the oba, once crowned, is no longer merely a man but the vessel of the sacred authority, and must not show his ordinary human face to the people. And Obalufon, the artist-king, the patron of all fine making, is bound up with the making and the meaning of these sacred crowns, the supreme craft-objects that carry the very soul of kingship. So he presides not only over the beauty of the bronze and the cloth but over the deepest sacred art of all, the crafting of the emblems of divine kingship itself."}, {"id": "orisa-obalufon#s10", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "So let me gather the teaching of Obalufon for you, my child, for it is a hopeful one, a cool clear draught after the hot lord of last night. Obalufon teaches us to honour peace, and to use the times of peace well, for war and fire and storm will come and cannot always be helped, but the cool times are the times of making, and a people is remembered not for the wars it fought but for the beauty it created in the peace between them. He teaches us to honour the crafts, the makers, the artists, the weavers and casters and builders and, yes, the tellers of tales and the speakers of true words, for these are the ones who build the civilization that the warriors only defend. He teaches us pride in what our ancestors made, that supreme bronze art that silenced, in the end, all who doubted us. And he teaches us to honour the gift of speech, the master-craft, and to use it as he would have it used: to name truly, to teach faithfully, to make peace, to shape beauty, and never to wound or deceive, for the tongue that was given to build the world can also, misused, tear it down."}, {"id": "orisa-obalufon#s11", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "His worship is cool and dignified, as befits him. His colours are the cool ones, and bronze and white. He is honoured wherever the fine crafts are practiced, and his great festival at Ife recalls the ancient artist-king. And his serene bronze face, that supreme masterpiece, remains, gazing calmly across the centuries, the truest icon of the cool king of peace and of all the civilizing arts."}, {"id": "orisa-obalufon#s12", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So when you next hear a beautiful word well spoken, or see a beautiful thing well made, or enjoy the blessing of a season of peace, my child, remember Obalufon, the cool king of bronze and speech. Remember that these gentle things, the fine word, the fine craft, the settled peace, are not lesser than the mighty deeds of war, but greater, for they are what all the fighting is finally for. And remember the serene bronze face of Ife, and let it teach you what your ancestors knew: that the highest greatness of a people is not the enemies it destroys but the beauty it creates."}, {"id": "orisa-obalufon#s13", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "Tomorrow, if the ancestors are willing, we will go back to the very beginning, to the descent of the first of us, to the founding of the sacred city, to the great ancestor from whom the kings and the crowns and the whole Yoruba nation descend, to Oduduwa and the making of Ife. But tonight belongs to the cool artist-king, and we will let his serene and dignified peace carry us gently down into sleep."}]}, {"id": "orisa-oduduwa", "emoji": "⚡", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#150020,#350050)", "category": "orisa", "rating": 9.6, "duration": "11 min", "title": {"en": "Oduduwa’s Descent: The Founding of the World at Ife", "yo": "Oduduwa"}, "desc": {"en": "The great progenitor who came down to found the world at Ife and fathered the Yoruba kingdoms.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Orisa", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Orisa, consolidates the tellings of Oduduwa across the collection, the story of the great progenitor, founder of Ife and father of the Yoruba nation, whose descent began the world and whose sons became kings)", "segments": [{"id": "orisa-oduduwa#s1", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Tonight, my child, we go back to the very beginning, further back than any tale I have yet told you, to the founding, to the descent, to the making of the world at the sacred navel of the earth. For every people has a beginning, a place and a moment from which they say, here we started, here the first of us set foot, and for us, for the whole Yoruba nation, that place is Ile-Ife, the city of the source, and that moment is the descent of Oduduwa. Tonight we speak of the great progenitor, the founder, the father of us all, from whom the kings descend and the crowns come down, the one who began the world. We speak of Oduduwa."}, {"id": "orisa-oduduwa#s2", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Now I told you long ago, when we spoke of Obatala, that there is a great matter, an ancient dispute, over who truly made the land, and that I would not settle it, and I will not settle it tonight either, for it has divided the wise for a thousand years. But I will tell you the telling of Oduduwa’s people, the royal telling, the one the kings tell, and you may hold it beside the telling of Obatala’s people and honour both, for both are true in the way that great foundational stories are true, which is a deeper truth than mere fact."}, {"id": "orisa-oduduwa#s3", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "In the beginning, as I told you, there was only the sky above, where Olodumare the Almighty reigned, and below there was only water, endless water, with no land at all. And in the royal telling, it was Oduduwa whom Olodumare charged, or who took it upon himself, to descend and make the dry land. Whether because Obatala had drunk the palm wine and fallen asleep and dropped the task, as some say, or by Olodumare’s own choosing, it was Oduduwa who came down on the long chain from the sky, carrying the shell of loose earth, the five-toed hen or the sacred hen, and the palm nut."}, {"id": "orisa-oduduwa#s4", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "And Oduduwa descended, and at a certain place upon the wide waters he poured out the loose earth from the shell, and he set down the hen, and the hen began to scratch, as hens do, scratching and spreading the earth this way and that across the face of the waters, and wherever the earth was scratched and spread, dry land appeared and grew and spread wider, until there was a great expanse of solid ground where before there had been only water. And the place where Oduduwa first poured the earth, the very spot where the spreading began, became Ile-Ife, which means, in one reading, the house of spreading, the place from which the earth spread out, the source, the origin, the navel of the whole world. That is why Ife is sacred above all other places to our people, my child. It is not merely an old and important city. It is the literal beginning of the world, the point from which all dry land, and all life, and all of us, spread out. When a Yoruba speaks of Ife, they speak of the source of creation itself."}, {"id": "orisa-oduduwa#s5", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "And then Oduduwa planted the palm nut, and it grew into a great palm tree with many branches, and this, in the telling, is a sign and a symbol of what was to come, for Oduduwa was to be the root of a great and spreading tree of kings and peoples. For Oduduwa became the first oba, the first king, ruling from Ife over the new-made world, and he had children, and his children and grandchildren, the tellings say, went out from Ife in every direction to found the great kingdoms and cities of the Yoruba, each becoming the crowned king of a new domain."}, {"id": "orisa-oduduwa#s6", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "This is the deepest meaning of Oduduwa for our people, my child, and you must understand it well, for it is the very foundation of who we are. From Oduduwa, seated at Ife, the source, went out the founders of all the great Yoruba kingdoms. His sons and descendants became the first kings of Oyo, of Ketu, of Benin in some tellings, of Sabe, of Popo, of Owu, of the great crowned cities, each carrying with them a beaded crown, the ade, the sacred emblem of descent from Oduduwa, the sign that this king was of the true royal line and had the right to wear the veiled crown. To this day, my child, the crowned kings of the Yoruba, the true obas who may wear the beaded crown with the veil that hides the face, trace their descent and their right to rule back to Oduduwa at Ife. The crown itself is the proof of the bloodline that begins with the founder. Ife is the source, and the Ooni of Ife, the king of Ife, holds a special and senior place among all Yoruba kings as the keeper of the sacred origin, the one who sits closest to the beginning."}, {"id": "orisa-oduduwa#s7", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "Do you feel now, my child, how this single ancestor binds a whole nation together? For however far apart the Yoruba kingdoms grew, however much they warred and rivaled and went their separate ways, and they did, bitterly, as I have told you in the tales of the Aare and the fall of Oyo, they all knew themselves to be one people, children of one founder, branches of one palm tree planted by one hand at one sacred source. Oduduwa is the reason a Yoruba from far Ketu and a Yoruba from distant Oyo and a Yoruba from the coast could all say, we are one, we are the children of Oduduwa, we spring from Ife. He is the root that makes the many branches one tree. In an age when our people were often divided into warring kingdoms, the memory of Oduduwa was the memory of a deeper unity beneath all the division, and it is no small thing, my child, that a people torn by a hundred wars still knew, always, that they were finally one blood, one origin, one nation of the descendants of the founder."}, {"id": "orisa-oduduwa#s8", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Now let me speak honestly, as I always do with you, about the two tellings, Obatala’s and Oduduwa’s, for the honest naming of a hard thing is part of a true education. There are those who hold that Obatala was the true creator, charged first by Olodumare, and that Oduduwa either took the task through Obatala’s fault, when he drank the wine, or even usurped it, and that a great and ancient rivalry, even a war, lies buried in the double telling, the rivalry perhaps of an older people and a newer, of one dynasty and another, remembered now as a quarrel of the gods over who made the land. This may well be so, my child. Beneath our sacred stories often lie the real and tangled histories of real peoples, of migrations and conquests and mergings, dressed now in the robes of myth. But here is the wisdom of our people: they did not force the two tellings into one and destroy the other. They kept both. They honour Obatala the shaper and Oduduwa the founder, both, and let the tension between the tellings stand, as a living thing, an honest acknowledgment that beginnings are complicated and that more than one people and more than one truth went into the making of the nation. There is a maturity in that, my child, a refusal to flatten a complicated origin into a single simple lie, that I want you to admire."}, {"id": "orisa-oduduwa#s9", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "So Oduduwa stands, my child, as the great ancestor, the founder, the root of the royal tree, the one from whom the crowns descend and in whom the whole scattered nation finds its unity. He is honoured not quite as the other orisa are honoured, for he is more the divine ancestor than the nature-power, more the founding father raised to divinity than the primordial force. But he is honoured deeply, at Ife above all, and in the reverence every crowned oba owes to the source of his crown, and in the pride every Yoruba may take, however humble their own station, in knowing that they spring from an ancient and mighty root, that their people did not begin as scattered nothing but as the children of a founder who made the very land beneath their feet."}, {"id": "orisa-oduduwa#s10", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "His teaching, then, is a teaching of unity and of origin. Oduduwa says to us, across all the centuries and all the divisions: remember that you are one. Remember the source. Remember that beneath every quarrel between Yoruba and Yoruba lies a deeper kinship, one blood, one founder, one sacred beginning at Ife. Honour the root, and the many branches, however far they spread and however they may sometimes clash, will remember that they are one tree. And in an age, my child, when peoples everywhere are torn apart by the forgetting of their common origin, that is a teaching worth carrying in your heart: know your source, honour your root, and remember, always, the deeper unity beneath the surface divisions."}, {"id": "orisa-oduduwa#s11", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "So when you hear the name of Ife, my child, or see the beaded crown of an oba with its veil that hides the sacred face, remember Oduduwa. Remember the descent on the golden chain, the pouring of the earth, the scratching of the hen, the spreading of the land from the sacred navel of the world. Remember that you stand upon land that, in the telling of your people, was made by the hand of your own founding ancestor, and that you spring from the palm tree he planted, one branch among many, all of one root. It is a proud inheritance, my child, and a unifying one, and it is yours."}, {"id": "orisa-oduduwa#s12", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "Tomorrow, if the ancestors are willing, we will speak of fire and volcano and the deep hot places of the earth, of the fierce orisa Aganju, the wilderness and the molten heart of the world, brother or father or ferryman of the thunder king. But tonight belongs to the founder, to the descent, to the making of the world at Ife, and we will rest upon the good dry land that, the old ones say, Oduduwa made, and let the sacred source cradle us into sleep."}]}, {"id": "orisa-aganju", "emoji": "⚡", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#150020,#350050)", "category": "orisa", "rating": 9.6, "duration": "10 min", "title": {"en": "Aganju: The Molten Heart of the Earth", "yo": "Aganju"}, "desc": {"en": "The orisa of the volcano, the wilderness, and the deep fires of the earth.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Orisa", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Orisa, the story of the fierce orisa of the volcano, the wilderness, and the deep fires of the world, ferryman of the great river, kin to the thunder king)", "segments": [{"id": "orisa-aganju#s1", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Put another log on the fire, my child, a good big one, and watch the flames take it, and think, as you watch, that deep beneath the very ground you sit upon there is a fire so vast and so hot that it makes our little blaze here look like a single spark, a fire in the deep heart of the earth that now and then breaks through and pours out molten upon the land. Tonight we speak of the lord of that deep fire, of the orisa of the volcano and the wilderness and the untamed places, the fierce and mighty Aganju. And I tell you at the start, my child, that Aganju is one of the least gentle and most elemental of all the powers, a raw force, the very heat at the center of the world, and there is a lesson in his fierceness that the tame and comfortable heart most needs to learn."}, {"id": "orisa-aganju#s2", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Aganju is the orisa of the volcano, of the molten fire deep within the earth, of the desert and the wilderness and the wild untamed lands where no farm grows and no town stands, of the fierce sun overhead and the fierce fire below. He is heat itself, the raw elemental heat of creation and destruction. Where his kinsman Sango is the fire of the sky, the lightning, Aganju is the fire of the earth, the volcano, the two of them together holding the fire above and the fire below. In many tellings they are close kin, brothers, or father and son, or bound in some deep way, the two great fire-lords of the Yoruba, and their worship is often intertwined."}, {"id": "orisa-aganju#s3", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "There is a tale, my child, that Aganju was the ferryman of a great river, and it is a strange and powerful tale, so let me tell it. It is said that Aganju kept the ferry across a mighty river, carrying travelers from one bank to the other, and that he was fierce and hard in this work, and demanded of those he ferried a great respect. And there came one day a certain traveler, a woman, who wished to cross, and she had no payment that Aganju would accept, and there was a great contest of wills between them at the water’s edge. In some tellings this woman was an orisa herself, and the meeting of the fierce ferryman and the determined traveler became a tale of the clash and the joining of two great powers at the crossing-place of the river."}, {"id": "orisa-aganju#s4", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "And the deep meaning of the ferryman, my child, is this: Aganju stands at the crossing, at the place where one must pass from one state to another, from one bank of life to the other, and he governs the fierce and difficult passages, the crossings that cannot be made easily or cheaply, that demand everything of the one who would cross. For there are passages in a life, my child, that are like crossing a great and dangerous river with a fierce ferryman who will not be cheated: the passage into manhood, the passage through grief, the passage through the wilderness of a hard season, the passages that burn away everything soft and easy in us and demand that we give all we have to reach the far bank. Those fierce crossings are Aganju’s, and he is the lord who governs them, hard and demanding, but the one who gets you across, if you will pay the fierce price the crossing costs."}, {"id": "orisa-aganju#s5", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "Now I want you to think hard, my child, about why a people would honour the volcano, the desert, the wilderness, the raw destroying heat, why they would make a god of the very fire that could burn them and the very wilderness that could kill them. It is the same wisdom we found in Sopona the hot lord and in Oya the storm: our people did not pretend the fierce and dangerous forces of the world away, did not imagine a soft and safe creation with all the danger edited out. They looked the volcano in its molten eye and they said, this too is a power, this too is holy, this too must be honoured, for it is part of the real world, and only children and fools build their understanding on the pretense that the world is safe."}, {"id": "orisa-aganju#s6", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "And there is more, for Aganju’s fierce heat is not only destruction. Think, my child: the volcano destroys, yes, it pours out molten fire and buries the land, but what is left behind, after the fire cools, is the richest soil in all the world, ground so fertile that things grow there as they grow nowhere else. The wilderness, the untamed land, harsh and empty as it seems, is the reservoir of the wild, the place from which new life and new possibility come, the necessary wildness without which the tame and cultivated world would grow stale and die. Aganju’s fierce fire is the fire of creation as much as destruction, the heat that forged the very earth in the beginning and that renews it still. To honour Aganju is to honour the truth that the fiercest forces are often the most creative, that destruction and creation are the two faces of one great heat, and that a world without the volcano’s fire would be a cold dead world indeed."}, {"id": "orisa-aganju#s7", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "This is the lesson of Aganju that I most want you to carry, my child, for it runs against everything the soft and comfortable world would teach you. We are taught, in the easy times, to fear and avoid all that is fierce, all that is hard, all that is wild and untamed and dangerous, to wrap ourselves in comfort and safety and to flee every heat. But Aganju teaches that there is a fire we need, a fierceness that forges and renews, a wilderness that keeps the soul from growing tame and stale, a hard crossing that makes us who we are meant to become. The one who flees all fire never becomes tempered steel; they remain soft iron, useless, easily bent. The one who avoids every wilderness never discovers what they are truly made of. The one who will not pay the fierce ferryman never crosses to the far bank where their real life is waiting. There is a heat, my child, that you must walk into, not away from, if you are ever to become the whole and hardened and fruitful thing you are meant to be. That heat is Aganju’s, and to honour him is to find the courage to enter the fire that forges rather than always fleeing to the comfort that softens."}, {"id": "orisa-aganju#s8", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "His worship is fierce and raw, as befits him. His colours are the dark reds and browns of the molten earth and the volcanic rock, and the deep purples of the fire’s heart. His domain is the wild and the high and the hot, the mountain, the desert, the untamed bush. He is honoured alongside Sango, the two fires, and his devotees know him as a power not to be trifled with, elemental, vast, the very heat of the world’s deep heart. He does not have the wide and tender following of sweet Oshun or motherly Yemoja, for he is not a comfortable god to love, but among those who know the necessity of the fierce forge and the wild crossing, he is honoured with a deep and knowing respect."}, {"id": "orisa-aganju#s9", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "So the next time you feel the earth warm beneath the sun, or see a fire take a log, or stand at the edge of some wild and untamed place, or face some fierce and burning passage in your own life that demands everything of you, remember Aganju, the molten heart of the earth. Remember that the fiercest fire is also the most creative, that the volcano’s destruction leaves the richest soil, that the wilderness holds the wild renewing life the tame world needs, and that there are crossings in a life that can only be made by paying the fierce ferryman his full and terrible price. Do not flee every fire, my child. Some fires you must walk into, for they are the only forge that can make you what you are meant to be."}, {"id": "orisa-aganju#s10", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "Tomorrow, if the ancestors are willing, we will cool ourselves once more, and speak of a very different and very beautiful thing, of the great masked festival in which the very power and the very secrets of women are honoured and celebrated, of the spectacle and the deep meaning of Gelede. But tonight belongs to the fierce lord of the deep fire, and we will bank our own little blaze with respect for its great and molten cousin at the heart of the world, and let its warmth carry us into sleep."}]}, {"id": "orisa-gelede", "emoji": "⚡", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#150020,#350050)", "category": "orisa", "rating": 9.6, "duration": "10 min", "title": {"en": "Gelede: The Spectacle That Honours the Mothers", "yo": "Gelede"}, "desc": {"en": "The masked festival that honours and placates the powerful mothers.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Orisa", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Orisa, the story of the great masked festival that celebrates and placates the powerful mothers, the awon iya wa, and the deep respect our people hold for the hidden power of women)", "segments": [{"id": "orisa-gelede#s1", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Tonight, my child, we speak of something wonderful, a great festival of colour and music and dance and laughter and carved masks, one of the most beautiful and famous spectacles our people ever created, so famous that the whole world has come to honour it as a treasure of humankind. We speak of Gelede. But do not be fooled by its beauty and its laughter into thinking it is merely a pretty show, for beneath the dazzling spectacle of Gelede lies one of the deepest and most serious truths our people ever grasped: the truth of the hidden and formidable power of women, of the mothers, and the wisdom of honouring that power rather than fearing or suppressing it."}, {"id": "orisa-gelede#s2", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Gelede is a festival held in honour of the mothers, the awon iya wa, our mothers, a phrase that means far more than it seems. For the mothers, my child, in the deep understanding of our people, are not only the women who bore us. They are a great spiritual force, the collective power of womankind, especially of elderly women, of the female principle itself, a power that is life-giving and nurturing beyond all others, and yet also, if disrespected or provoked, formidable and even dangerous. Our people understood that women hold a power men do not: the power to bring forth life itself, the deep mystery of creation carried in the female body, and they understood that such a power, the very power of life, is not a small or a safe thing, but vast, and must be honoured."}, {"id": "orisa-gelede#s3", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Now I must explain something subtle, my child, that the strangers who came often misunderstood and slandered. Our people spoke of the mothers as holding a power that could be used for great good, for blessing, for fertility, for the protection and nurturing of the whole community, but that could also, if the mothers were angered, disrespected, or wronged, turn to great harm. The strangers heard this and cried witchcraft, and made it something evil and shameful. But that is a slander and a misunderstanding, my child, and I want you to unlearn it. What our people were really saying, in the language of their own wisdom, is a profound truth: that the power of women, the life-giving power, is real and great and must not be trifled with; that a society which honours its women, its mothers, its elder women, will be blessed with life and fertility and peace; and that a society which disrespects, oppresses, or wrongs its women is courting disaster, for the very power that gives life can, when wronged, withhold it. That is not superstition, my child. That is one of the wisest social truths any people ever grasped, dressed in the robes of the sacred."}, {"id": "orisa-gelede#s4", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "And so Gelede exists to honour and to celebrate and, yes, to placate the mothers, to keep the community in right and joyful relationship with the great female power, so that it will be turned toward blessing and not toward harm. It is, at its heart, a great and beautiful act of respect toward women, performed by the whole community, year after year, an entire festival dedicated to saying to the mothers: we see your power, we honour it, we celebrate it, we do not take it for granted or trample it, and so we ask you to turn your great life-giving force toward our blessing."}, {"id": "orisa-gelede#s5", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Now let me tell you what the festival is actually like, my child, for it is a marvel. Gelede is famous above all for its masks, its headdresses, elaborate carved wooden creations worn atop the head, and these are among the greatest achievements of our carvers’ art. And here is a wonderful thing: the Gelede masks are not solemn and terrifying like some sacred masks. They are often witty, satirical, full of humour and social commentary. A Gelede headdress might portray a scene of everyday life, a trader, an animal, a foolish person behaving badly, a comment on the events and the follies of the day, all carved with great skill and great wit atop the dancer’s head. For Gelede uses laughter, my child, uses satire and spectacle and delight, both to entertain and to instruct, to gently mock the community’s follies and teach it better ways, all in the joyful service of honouring the mothers."}, {"id": "orisa-gelede#s6", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "And the dancers, my child. The Gelede dancers are men, who wear the masks and elaborate costumes, and here is a beautiful thing: the male dancers dress as women, honouring the female by embodying her, and they dance with tremendous skill and precision, their movements matched exactly to the intricate rhythms of the drums, ankle rattles marking every beat, so that the drummer and the dancer are locked in a dazzling conversation of rhythm and motion. It is a spectacle of extraordinary artistry, the carved masks, the flowing costumes, the precise and powerful dancing, the witty social commentary, the driving drums, all woven together into one great joyful offering to the power of women. The world has recognized Gelede as a masterpiece of the heritage of all humankind, and rightly so, for there are few festivals anywhere that combine such beauty, such artistry, such wit, and such deep meaning."}, {"id": "orisa-gelede#s7", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "And there is a special part of Gelede, my child, that comes in the night, called Efe, which is more solemn and more serious than the daytime spectacle. In the night ceremony, a particular masked singer comes out, and he has a special license, a sacred freedom, to sing the truth, to speak openly of the wrongs and follies and injustices of the community, to name the powerful who have done wrong, to voice the grievances the ordinary person dares not speak aloud in daylight. Under the mask, in the night, protected by the sacred occasion, the Efe singer becomes the voice of the community’s conscience, saying what must be said, criticizing even the mighty, calling the whole town to account. Is that not a wonderful thing, my child? A sacred institution by which truth may be spoken to power, by which the grievances of the humble may be voiced, by which even the great may be called to account, all under the protection of the mask and the festival of the mothers. Our people built the freedom of honest criticism right into their sacred festival, and gave it the protection of the very power of women."}, {"id": "orisa-gelede#s8", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "So gather now, my child, the deep meaning of Gelede, for it is rich. First, it teaches the honouring of women, of mothers, of the great life-giving female power, and the wisdom that a community which respects its women is blessed and one which wrongs them is cursed. Second, it teaches the use of beauty and wit and laughter and art in the service of the sacred and the social, that instruction and correction need not always be grim, that a people can dance and laugh its way toward wisdom. Third, through the Efe night song, it teaches the necessity of truth-telling, of a protected space in which even the powerful may be criticized and the grievances of the humble voiced. And beneath all of it lies that one profound and unfashionable truth that our ancestors grasped so clearly: that the power of women is real, and great, and formidable, and that to honour it is life, and to trample it is ruin."}, {"id": "orisa-gelede#s9", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "I ask you to carry that truth especially, my child, into whatever age you live in. Wherever you see women honoured, celebrated, their power acknowledged, their voices heard, there you will find a community that is blessed with life. And wherever you see women disrespected, oppressed, their power denied and trampled, there, our ancestors would tell you, a society is courting the anger of the mothers, and cutting off the very source of its own life. Gelede is a whole festival built to remember this, to celebrate the mothers, to keep the great female power turned toward blessing. Remember it, and honour the mothers, all your days."}, {"id": "orisa-gelede#s10", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "Its colours are many and bright, for it is a festival of joyful spectacle, and its art is the carved mask and the flowing costume, and its voice is the driving drum and the truth-telling night song, and its heart is respect for women. And though I have never seen a thing more purely joyful than Gelede in full daytime spectacle, the masks bobbing, the dancers’ ankles thundering in time with the drums, the crowd roaring with delight at each witty headdress, I have never seen a thing more purely serious than the Efe singer in the night, calling the whole town to account under the protection of the mothers. Joy and seriousness, beauty and truth, laughter and reverence, all in one festival. That is Gelede, my child. That is the genius of your people."}, {"id": "orisa-gelede#s11", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "Tomorrow, if the ancestors are willing, we will speak of the most intimate power of all, of the one that sits, quite literally, upon your own shoulders, of the orisa of your own inner head, your own personal destiny and character, of Ori, the god that is most truly your own. But tonight belongs to the mothers, and to the great joyful spectacle that honours them, and we will let the memory of the drums and the dancing carry us, smiling, down into sleep."}]}, {"id": "orisa-ori", "emoji": "⚡", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#150020,#350050)", "category": "orisa", "rating": 9.6, "duration": "11 min", "title": {"en": "Ori: The God That Is Your Own Head", "yo": "Ori"}, "desc": {"en": "The most personal of the powers: the inner head and destiny each person carries.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Orisa", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Orisa, the story of the most personal of all the powers, the inner head, the personal destiny and divinity that each person carries, whom even the great orisa must honour)", "segments": [{"id": "orisa-ori#s1", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Come very close tonight, my child, closer than usual, for tonight I speak to you of the most intimate power in all our understanding, a power that is not far off in the sky or deep in the sea or hidden in the forest, but that sits, right now, this very moment, upon your own shoulders and within your own skull. Tonight we speak of Ori, of the inner head, of your own personal destiny and your own personal divinity, the orisa that is most truly and only your own. And I will tell you at the very start the most astonishing truth of our whole religion, the one that surprises everyone who first truly grasps it: that of all the powers, great and small, the one you must honour most, the one that most decides your life, the one that even the mighty orisa themselves must bow to, is your own Ori. Your own head. Your own self."}, {"id": "orisa-ori#s2", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Now I have spoken to you before, when we told of Orunmila, of ori, of the inner head, of the destiny each soul chose in heaven before birth and then forgot. Tonight we go deeper, for Ori is not only the destiny you chose; Ori is a power, a divinity, an orisa in its own right, and it is the personal one, the one that belongs to you alone and to no one else in all the world. Every person, my child, carries their own Ori, their own inner head, their own personal god, and no two are alike, for no two destinies and no two selves are alike. Your Ori is you, the deepest truest you, the you that chose your fate and carries your character and steers your life. And it is holy. It is divine. You carry a god within your own head, and that god is your own truest self."}, {"id": "orisa-ori#s3", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Let me tell you the teaching that flows from this, for it is unlike anything the strangers brought, and it is one of the most empowering truths any people ever grasped. Because your Ori is the personal divinity that carries your own destiny, it follows that your Ori is, in the matters of your own life, more important to you than any of the great orisa. Sango may rule the thunder and Ogun the iron and Oshun the sweet waters, but none of them governs your particular destiny the way your own Ori does. And so there is a profound saying among us, my child: that a person’s Ori is their own personal orisa, and that before you pray to any of the great gods, you should first honour your own Ori, for it is your Ori that will finally determine whether your life goes well or ill."}, {"id": "orisa-ori#s4", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "And here is the most striking part, the part that astonishes. It is taught, my child, that even the great orisa must consult and honour Ori. There is a famous teaching that says none of the orisa can bless a person whose own Ori does not agree to it, that even the mightiest god cannot make your life prosper if your own inner head, your own destiny and self, is set against it. Ori goes before the orisa. Ori must approve. For all the power of all the gods cannot override the destiny you yourself chose and the self you yourself are. Do you feel the weight of that, my child? Your own head, your own self, your own chosen destiny, is a power that even the gods must reckon with. There is no more empowering teaching in all the world, and no more sobering one, for it means that the final responsibility for your life rests, in the deepest place, with your own Ori, with your own self."}, {"id": "orisa-ori#s5", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Now this raises a great and difficult question, my child, and our people, being wise, did not shrink from it. If your destiny was chosen before birth and is carried by your Ori, then are you simply fated, helpless, unable to change anything, a leaf carried on a river you did not choose? And the answer of our wisdom is subtle and beautiful: no. For there are two aspects of Ori, the old ones teach. There is the destiny you chose, yes, the broad shape of your fate. But there is also your character, your iwa, your conduct, the way you actually live and choose and behave day by day, and this too is bound up with your Ori, and this you can shape."}, {"id": "orisa-ori#s6", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "A good destiny, the old ones say, can be ruined by bad character. And a difficult destiny can be greatly improved, its worst dangers avoided and its hidden goods reached, by good character and wise conduct and the honouring of one’s Ori. So it is not simple fate, my child. It is a partnership between the destiny you chose and the character you build, between the Ori you were born with and the way you choose to live. Your Ori sets the field; your iwa, your character, plays the game upon it. And this is why our people say, again and again, that iwa, good character, is the most beautiful thing, iwa l’ewa, character is beauty, for it is by good character that a person cooperates with the good in their destiny and softens the hard, and by bad character that a person ruins even a fine destiny. The head you were given is your fate; the way you carry it is your freedom."}, {"id": "orisa-ori#s7", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "So how do we honour Ori, my child? We honour it in a rite called the feeding of the head, ibori, the propitiation of one’s own inner head. A person may, with the proper rites, honour and feed and strengthen their own Ori, asking their own destiny and their own truest self to be favourable, to guide them well, to steer their life toward the good they chose and away from the pitfalls. It is a beautiful and intimate act, my child, this honouring of one’s own head, this taking-seriously of one’s own destiny and one’s own divine inner self. It is a way of saying: I honour the god within me, I take responsibility for the self I am and the destiny I carry, I ask my own truest nature to lead me well."}, {"id": "orisa-ori#s8", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "And there is a deep humility and a deep dignity in it together. Humility, because you acknowledge that there is a destiny greater than your daily wanting, an inner head that knows more than your surface mind, a chosen fate you must reckon with. And dignity, because you acknowledge that you carry a god within you, that your own self is holy, that your own head is divine and worthy of honour. What a balance our people struck, my child: to be humble before your destiny and yet to honour the divinity of your own self, both at once. That is the wisdom of Ori."}, {"id": "orisa-ori#s9", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "Let me tell you a small teaching-tale of Ori, for it captures the whole matter. It is said that once all the parts of the body and all the potential companions set out on a great journey to a far and prosperous place, and that many wished to go, the strong ones, the clever ones, the beautiful ones, each boasting of what they would contribute. But it was taught that only the one who carried a good Ori, a good destiny and a good head, actually arrived at the prosperous place, while the others, for all their strength and cleverness and beauty, failed along the way, because they lacked what Ori alone provides. The meaning, my child, is that strength and cleverness and beauty and all the gifts of the surface are not enough; it is the inner head, the destiny and the character together, that finally determines whether a person arrives at the good life or fails short of it. Choose good character, honour your Ori, and you will arrive. Trust in mere gifts without character or the honouring of your destiny, and you may fail with all your gifts intact."}, {"id": "orisa-ori#s10", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "So gather the teaching of Ori into your heart, my child, and hold it above almost all the others, for it is the most personal and the most practical of all. You carry a god within your own head. Your own destiny and your own truest self are holy and are yours alone. Honour that inner self, take it seriously, feed it, consult it, do not betray it. Know that even the great gods must reckon with your Ori, that the final responsibility for your life rests, in the deepest place, with your own head and your own character. And know that though your broad destiny was chosen before you were born, your character, your iwa, is yours to build day by day, and that it is by good character that you cooperate with the good in your fate and rise to the fullness of the self you chose to be. Honour your Ori, build a beautiful character, and you will arrive, my child, at the prosperous place you set out for before you were ever born."}, {"id": "orisa-ori#s11", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "Of all the tellings I have given you of the orisa, this one, of Ori, is the one I most want you to keep, for the others tell you of powers outside yourself, but this one tells you of the power within, the god you carry, the self you are, the destiny you must honour and the character you must build. Honour your own head, my child. It is holy. It is divine. It is you. Tomorrow, if the ancestors are willing, we will speak of the ancestors themselves, of the honoured dead who return in the masks, of the great institution of Egungun by which the departed remain present among the living. But tonight belongs to your own inner head, to your own destiny, to your own truest and most divine self, and I ask your Ori, and mine, to grant us a peaceful and a favourable sleep."}]}, {"id": "orisa-egungun", "emoji": "⚡", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#150020,#350050)", "category": "orisa", "rating": 9.6, "duration": "11 min", "title": {"en": "Egungun: The Return of the Honoured Dead", "yo": "Egungun"}, "desc": {"en": "The ancestral masquerade through which the honoured dead return to bless the living.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Orisa", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Orisa, consolidates the tellings of the ancestral masquerade, the story of the great institution by which the departed return among the living, robed in cloth, to bless and to guide their descendants)", "segments": [{"id": "orisa-egungun#s1", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "I told you once, on the night of the walkers in the dark, of the masks that come out when the boundary between the worlds grows thin, and tonight, my child, I return to that mystery to tell it more fully, for it deserves a telling of its own. Tonight we speak of Egungun, of the honoured dead who come back robed in glorious cloth to walk among their descendants, of the great and ancient institution by which our people declared, and still declare, that death is not the end of belonging, that the ones who go before us do not leave us, that the family is not broken by the grave but only stretched across the boundary of the two worlds. Tonight we speak of the return of the ancestors."}, {"id": "orisa-egungun#s2", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "For this, my child, is perhaps the deepest and most comforting of all our beliefs: that the dead are not gone. When a person dies, in the understanding of our people, they do not simply vanish into nothing, nor do they go off to some far place from which they never return. They pass into the company of the ancestors, the honoured dead, and from there, if they lived well and are properly honoured, they remain present to their family, watching over their descendants, blessing them, guiding them, and, on the sacred occasions, actually returning, visibly, embodied, to walk once more among the living. Egungun is the great festival and the great institution of that return."}, {"id": "orisa-egungun#s3", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "· · ·aw toward the close of our great cycle of the orisa, and I will tell you of the year of the babalawo, of the sacred calendar and the great yearly divination by which the priests of Ifa read the fortune of the whole people for the year to come. But tonight belongs to the sacred twins, to the double blessing, to the little carved figures and the love that keeps them, and we will sleep in the joy of new life and the comfort that love outlasts even death."}, {"id": "orisa-egungun#s4", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Let me tell you what the Egungun is. It is a masquerade, a masked figure, but unlike any ordinary costume, for the Egungun represents, and in the deepest understanding is, an ancestor come back. The Egungun is robed entirely, head to foot, in layers upon layers of rich and beautiful cloth, panels of costly fabric of every colour and pattern, often the finest cloth the family can provide, sewn into a magnificent flowing costume that covers the wearer completely, so that not one inch of the living person beneath is seen. This complete covering is essential, my child, for the ancestor must be wholly other, wholly hidden, wholly transformed; the man beneath must disappear entirely so that the ancestor alone is present."}, {"id": "orisa-egungun#s5", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "And when the Egungun dances, it whirls, spinning so that the many panels of cloth fly out in a great blossoming circle of colour, a breathtaking sight, the layers of costly fabric spreading and swirling. The finer and more numerous the cloths, my child, the greater the honour to the ancestor, so that a great family’s Egungun may wear a fortune in beautiful fabric, spinning it out in glory before the admiring crowd, each panel a gift of respect to the returned dead."}, {"id": "orisa-egungun#s6", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Now here is a solemn matter I must impress upon you. The Egungun, when it walks, is not to be touched. It is dangerous, it is said, for an ordinary person to touch or be touched by the Egungun, for you would be touching the very presence of the dead, the power of the other world, and it could bring harm. And so the Egungun is often accompanied by attendants who carry switches and guide it and keep the crowd at a respectful distance, clearing the way, ensuring that the sacred figure is not touched by careless hands. There is awe in this, my child, a holy fear, the same awe I told you of, the awe of standing near to something vast and other, to the very presence of the departed come back among the living."}, {"id": "orisa-egungun#s7", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "For make no mistake about what the people believe they are seeing. They are not watching a man in a costume pretending to be grandfather. They are watching grandfather, come back, present, here, for this sacred while. The Egungun speaks, often in a strange, deep, unearthly voice, not the voice of the living man beneath, and through that voice the ancestor may bless the family, may give guidance, may settle disputes, may pronounce on matters of the lineage, may praise or may warn. The living crowd around the returned ancestor to receive the blessing, to hear the guidance, to feel the presence of the one they loved and lost, come back to them for a little while across the great boundary."}, {"id": "orisa-egungun#s8", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Think, my child, of what this means for a family, for a person. Imagine that your beloved grandmother, who died when you were small, is not simply gone forever into silence and absence. Imagine that once a year, on the sacred festival, she comes back, robed in glory, spinning her beautiful cloths, speaking in the voice of the other world, to bless you and your children and to remind you that she has not forgotten you, that she watches over you still from among the ancestors. Imagine the comfort of that, the healing of that, the strength of that. This is what Egungun gives our people, my child: the felt, embodied, visible assurance that death does not break the bonds of family, that the dead remain part of the living community, that we are held and watched and loved by a great company of ancestors who return to us, and who wait for us, and to whom, one day, we ourselves will belong, and return in our turn to bless our own descendants."}, {"id": "orisa-egungun#s9", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "For that is the other half of it, my child, and it is a great comfort too. One day you will die, as all must. But if you live well, and are honoured by those who come after you, you too will pass into the company of the ancestors, and you too will not be gone, but will watch over your children and your children’s children, and you too, on the sacred festivals, may return, robed in glory, to bless the ones you leave behind. Death, in the world of Egungun, is not a wall but a doorway into a new kind of belonging, a promotion, you might almost say, into the honoured ranks of those who guide and bless the living. There is no terror of oblivion in this understanding, my child, for no one is forgotten and no one is truly lost. The line holds. The family endures across the boundary of death itself."}, {"id": "orisa-egungun#s10", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Now I must add a serious word, for Egungun is not only comfort; it carries authority and law. In the old society, the Egungun, as the voice of the ancestors, held real power in the community. The ancestors, through the masquerade, could pronounce judgment, enforce the customs, settle disputes, and even, in the most solemn societies, execute the deepest justice of the community, for who could argue with the voice of the ancestors themselves? The Egungun institution was thus not only a festival of comfort but a pillar of the social and moral order, the ancestors reaching from beyond the grave to hold the living to the ways of the family and the community, to say: this is how it has always been done, these are the values of your lineage, walk in them, or answer to us who made you. There is a great power in a community that feels itself watched and guided and held to account by its own honoured dead, my child, a continuity, a rootedness, a moral seriousness, that a people who believe the dead simply vanish can never quite know."}, {"id": "orisa-egungun#s11", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "The great festival of the ancestors comes yearly, and it is one of the most important in the sacred calendar, a time when the whole community’s dead return, when the streets fill with the glorious spinning masquerades, when the drums call the ancestors back across the boundary and the living gather to receive them. Each important lineage may have its own Egungun, its own ancestral masquerade carrying its own honoured dead, so that the festival becomes a great gathering of all the community’s ancestors at once, a reunion, you might say, of the living and the dead of the whole town."}, {"id": "orisa-egungun#s12", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So when you see, my child, in a photograph or with your own eyes someday, the great Egungun spinning its layered cloths in a blossom of colour, surrounded by its attendants and its awed and joyful crowd, do not see merely a colourful custom. See the profound faith beneath it: that the dead are not gone, that the family endures across the grave, that we are watched and blessed and guided by a great company of ancestors who return to us on the sacred days and who wait to welcome us when our own day comes. Honour your ancestors, my child, the ones whose blood you carry and whose name you bear, for they are not gone; they are only a little way beyond the boundary, watching, and on the sacred nights, they come back, robed in glory, to bless the ones they never stopped loving."}, {"id": "orisa-egungun#s13", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "Tomorrow, if the ancestors are willing, and having spoken tonight of those who have gone before, we will speak of new life and its double blessing, of the most beloved of all births among our people, of the sacred twins, of Ibeji, and the special joy and the special power that twin children carry. But tonight belongs to the honoured dead, to the ancestors who return, and we will sleep in the good company of all who went before us and watch over us still."}]}, {"id": "orisa-ibeji", "emoji": "⚡", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#150020,#350050)", "category": "orisa", "rating": 9.6, "duration": "10 min", "title": {"en": "Ibeji: The Sacred Twins and Their Double Blessing", "yo": "Ibeji"}, "desc": {"en": "The sacred twins: why the Yoruba became the people of twins, and the small carved figures that keep them.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Orisa", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Orisa, the story of the beloved twin children, why the Yoruba became the people of twins, and the joy and the mystery and the small carved figures that keep the twins forever)", "segments": [{"id": "orisa-ibeji#s1", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Tonight, my child, we turn from the honoured dead to the most joyful of new life, for tonight we speak of the sacred twins, of Ibeji, and there is no telling in all our store that carries more tenderness, more joy, and, I will tell you honestly, more of a mother’s fierce love reaching even past death. And I begin with a fact that will surprise you and that should fill you with a certain wonder: of all the peoples of the whole wide earth, my child, the Yoruba are among those who bear the most twins. It is true. Twin births are more common among our people than almost anywhere else in the world, and our ancestors, noticing this, this strange and marvelous abundance of doubled births, understood it as a sacred sign, and they built around the twins one of the most beautiful and beloved of all our traditions."}, {"id": "orisa-ibeji#s2", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "The twins are called Ibeji, and the twin children themselves are considered sacred, special, marked, carriers of a particular power and a particular blessing, and also, it must be said, of a particular mystery and a particular danger, for the sacred is never simple. To bear twins, among our people, is to be visited by something holy, and a mother of twins holds a special and honoured place, and the twins themselves are treated with a special reverence all their lives."}, {"id": "orisa-ibeji#s3", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Now the first-born of the twins, my child, has a special name, Taiwo, and the second-born a special name, Kehinde, and there is a beautiful teaching folded into these names, so let me unfold it for you. You might think the first-born, Taiwo, is the elder. But our people teach it the other way, and here is the lovely logic of it. Kehinde, the second to come out, is considered the elder, the senior, the more mature. And Taiwo, the first to come out, is considered the younger, the junior. Why? Because, the old ones reason, Kehinde, the senior and wiser twin, sent Taiwo out first, as an elder sends a junior ahead to go and see whether the world outside is good, whether it is fit and ready to be entered. Taiwo goes first as the scout, the taster of the world, sent by the senior Kehinde who waits behind to hear the report. Taiwo’s very name means, come and taste the world, go and see if life is sweet. And only after Taiwo has gone ahead does Kehinde, the elder, follow. Is that not a beautiful piece of wisdom, my child, hidden in the names of twins, that the one who comes first is the junior sent ahead by the senior who follows?"}, {"id": "orisa-ibeji#s4", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Now I must tell you of the deep and tender heart of the Ibeji tradition, and it touches on sorrow even as it speaks of joy, for it concerns what our people did when a twin died, and it is one of the most moving customs in all our culture. In the old days, my child, and even now, the death of a child was common, and the death of one twin was a particular grief, made worse by a particular fear: for the twins were understood to be so deeply bound to each other, two halves of one sacred whole, that it was feared the surviving twin might follow the dead one out of the world, might sicken and die from the broken bond, unable to live with half of itself gone."}, {"id": "orisa-ibeji#s5", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "And so our people did a thing of extraordinary tenderness and wisdom. When a twin died, a small wooden figure was carved to represent the departed twin, a little standing statue, a doll, called an ere ibeji, an image of the twin. And this small carved figure was not thrown away, not hidden in grief, but kept, and cared for, and honoured, as if it were the living child. The mother would keep the little figure of her dead twin, and she would wash it, and oil it, and clothe it, and adorn it with beads, and she would feed it, symbolically, when she fed the living twin, and carry it, and care for it, exactly as she cared for the surviving child. The dead twin was kept present, kept in the family, kept beloved, in the form of the little carved image, so that the bond would not be broken, so that the surviving twin would not be left half-whole, so that the departed child would never be abandoned or forgotten."}, {"id": "orisa-ibeji#s6", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "Do you feel the beauty and the heartbreak of that, my child? A mother who has lost one of her twins, carving or commissioning a small wooden image of the child she lost, and then keeping it, feeding it, washing it, dressing it, loving it, for years, for a lifetime, so that her dead child remains present and cared for and part of the family still. It is grief transformed into an act of continuing love. It is a refusal to let death sever the bond of a mother and her child. It is the same faith we found in Egungun, that the dead are not gone, made intimate and personal and heartbreaking in the small carved figure a bereaved mother keeps and tends. These little ere ibeji figures, my child, thousands upon thousands of them, carved over the centuries, each one representing a lost twin, each one lovingly tended by a grieving mother, are among the most poignant objects our people ever made, and they have become treasured all over the world, though few who admire them in the museums understand the depth of a mother’s love that each one holds."}, {"id": "orisa-ibeji#s7", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "And notice, my child, the wisdom hidden in the custom, for our people’s tenderness was always practical too. The keeping of the little figure gave the grieving mother a way to carry her grief, a ritual, an action, a continuing relationship with the child she lost, rather than only an empty absence. It is, if you think on it, a very wise way to grieve, to have a small honoured object to tend and love, a way of keeping the bond alive while slowly, over the years of tending it, learning to live with the loss. Our ancestors understood the human heart, my child, understood that grief needs somewhere to go, something to do, someone still to love, and they built into the Ibeji tradition exactly that gift for the mothers of twins."}, {"id": "orisa-ibeji#s8", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "The twins who live, my child, are honoured and, it is said, are watched over by a special power, and they are believed to bring blessing and prosperity to the family that honours them well, and, it is warned, misfortune to any who would mistreat or scorn them. There are special songs for the twins, special foods, especially beans and palm oil, which are associated with them, special observances. Mothers of twins are known to dance and sing in the market and among the people, celebrating and drawing blessing, and there is a special joy that surrounds the twins wherever they are honoured. For the twins are a doubled gift, a doubled blessing, two sacred children where one was expected, and our people receive them with a doubled joy."}, {"id": "orisa-ibeji#s9", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "So gather the teaching of Ibeji into your heart, my child, for it is rich and tender. It teaches the sacredness of children, and the special wonder of twins, that marvelous doubled blessing our people bear more than almost any other on earth. It teaches the beautiful wisdom of the twins’ names, the junior sent ahead to taste the world and the senior who follows. And most of all, it teaches, through the little carved figure a grieving mother keeps and tends, that love does not end at death, that the bond between a mother and her child, between the two halves of a sacred pair, is not to be severed by the grave, and that grief, at its most profound, can become an act of continuing and faithful love, a keeping-present of the one who is gone."}, {"id": "orisa-ibeji#s10", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "There is so much in this, my child, of what is most beautiful in our people: the joy in new life, the reverence for children, the wisdom about the human heart, the tenderness toward grief, the refusal to let death have the final word over love. When you see, someday, one of those small carved twin-figures, worn smooth by a mother’s tending hands, remember what it is: not a mere doll, not a mere idol, but a lost child, kept beloved, a bond that death could not break, a mother’s love made wood and carried through the years. That, my child, is Ibeji. Tomorrow, if the ancestors are willing, we will dr"}]}, {"id": "orisa-babalawos-year", "emoji": "⚡", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#150020,#350050)", "category": "orisa", "rating": 9.7, "duration": "12 min", "title": {"en": "The Babalawo’s Year: The Great Divination That Reads the Fortune of a People", "yo": "Babalawo"}, "desc": {"en": "The yearly Ifa reading in which the priests of Orunmila divine the fortune of a whole people.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Orisa", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Orisa, the story of the sacred calendar and the yearly reading of Ifa, when the priests of Orunmila divine the fortune of the whole community for the year to come)", "segments": [{"id": "orisa-babalawos-year#s1", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "We come now, my child, near to the close of our great cycle of the orisa, and it is fitting that we close where in a sense it all comes together, in the work of the babalawo, the father of secrets, the priest of Orunmila, and in the great yearly divination by which the whole fortune of a people is read for the year ahead. For I have told you of many powers, of the shaper and the iron-lord and the sweet river and the thunder king and the witness to destiny and the lord of leaves and the mothers and the ancestors and the twins and your own inner head. Tonight I gather them, in a way, into the turning of the sacred year, and I show you how our people, through the babalawo, sought each year to know what was coming and how to meet it well."}, {"id": "orisa-babalawos-year#s2", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "You remember Orunmila, my child, the witness to destiny, and Ifa, his great system of divination, the two hundred and fifty-six odu, the vast library of wisdom held in the memory of the priests. And you remember that a person facing a great decision goes to the babalawo to consult Ifa, to learn what their destiny holds and how to walk it well. Now understand this: what a single person does for their own life, a whole community does, once a year, for its collective life. There is a great yearly divination, a reading of Ifa not for one person but for the entire people, to discover the character of the coming year, its dangers and its opportunities, and the sacrifices and the conduct by which the community may steer toward the good and away from the ill."}, {"id": "orisa-babalawos-year#s3", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "This great yearly reading, my child, in the sacred cities, above all at Ife, the source, is one of the most solemn and important events of the whole calendar. The babalawo gather, the most senior and learned priests of Orunmila, and they perform the great divination for the year, and from it emerges the ruling odu of the year, the particular chapter of Ifa that will govern the coming year, along with its guidance: what the year holds, what forces are ascendant, what dangers loom, what goods are possible, and, crucially, what the people must do, what sacrifices they must make, what conduct they must observe, to meet the year well. It is, if you like, the spiritual forecast for the entire nation, delivered not by guesswork but by the ancient and sophisticated system of Ifa, read by the most learned keepers of the tradition."}, {"id": "orisa-babalawos-year#s4", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "And then this guidance is proclaimed to the people, so that all may know the character of the year they are entering and how they are counseled to live it. Imagine it, my child: a whole people, at the turning of the year, gathering to hear from their wisest elders, read through the ancient sacred system, what the coming year holds and how they should meet it, together, as one community. It is a way of facing the future not with blind anxiety but with counsel, with preparation, with a shared understanding, with the accumulated wisdom of the ancestors brought to bear on the year ahead. There is a deep steadiness in it, my child, a way of entering the unknown future with a map, however imperfect, rather than with mere fear."}, {"id": "orisa-babalawos-year#s5", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Now I want you to understand the deep purpose of this, for it is easy to misunderstand it as mere fortune-telling, as a superstitious peering into what is fixed and cannot be changed. But that is not its heart at all, and I have taught you why: remember what Orunmila’s wisdom truly is. The divination does not reveal a fixed and unchangeable fate that the people must simply suffer. It reveals the character and the tendencies of the coming year so that the people may cooperate with its goods and guard against its ills, may make the sacrifices and adopt the conduct that will steer their shared destiny toward the best possible outcome. It is guidance for action, not a sentence of doom. It says: this is the shape of the year that is coming; here are its dangers; here is what you must do to meet them; here are its opportunities; here is how to reach them. It puts the future, to a real degree, back into the hands of the people, by telling them how to act well within it."}, {"id": "orisa-babalawos-year#s6", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "And notice, my child, how this weaves together everything I have taught you. The yearly divination often calls for particular sacrifices and observances, and these may involve many of the orisa: an offering to Ogun for the safety of the roads and the work of iron, to Oshun for fertility and increase, to Sango for justice, to the mothers through Gelede for the blessing of the female power, to the ancestors through the honouring of Egungun. The babalawo’s year gathers all the powers into a single ordered response to the coming time, so that the whole rich world of the orisa is brought to bear, in a coordinated way, on the shared task of living the year well. It is the crown, you might say, of the whole system, the place where all the powers are marshaled together in the service of the community’s collective destiny."}, {"id": "orisa-babalawos-year#s7", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "Let me tell you what the babalawo himself is, my child, for he is the keeper of all this, and he was among the most honoured and important figures in all our society. The babalawo, the father of secrets, was not merely a fortune-teller. He was the repository of the accumulated wisdom of the people, the keeper of the vast Ifa corpus, the doctor and the counselor and the historian and the philosopher and the guide, all at once. To become a babalawo required decades of study, the memorization of an immense body of sacred verse, the mastery of the divination itself, the learning of the medicines and the sacrifices and the meanings. A true babalawo held in his trained memory a library that in other cultures would fill many shelves, and he could bring it to bear, verse by verse, on the troubles of individuals and the fortunes of the whole people. He was, my child, a walking university of his people’s wisdom, and to sit before him was to consult ten centuries of accumulated understanding, speaking to your present need."}, {"id": "orisa-babalawos-year#s8", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "And so the honour paid to the babalawo, and the solemnity of the yearly divination, reflect the deep value our people placed on wisdom, on knowledge, on the guidance of the learned and the tested, on facing the future with counsel rather than with recklessness or blind fear. A people who begin each year by gathering to hear the wisdom of their most learned elders, read through the ancient sacred system, about how to live the coming year well, are a people who honour wisdom, my child, who take the future seriously, who believe that how we meet the year matters and can be guided. There is much in that worth admiring, and much worth keeping, whatever system of wisdom one turns to."}, {"id": "orisa-babalawos-year#s9", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "So gather the teaching of the babalawo’s year, my child, as we draw our great cycle toward its close. It teaches that the future, though unknown, need not be met with blind fear, but may be approached with counsel, with preparation, with the accumulated wisdom of those who came before. It teaches that destiny, whether of a person or a people, is not a fixed doom but a field within which wise action matters, and that the purpose of all divination is not to make us passive before fate but to help us act well within it. It teaches the honouring of wisdom and of the learned keepers of a people’s knowledge. And it gathers all the powers, all the orisa, all the rich world I have spent these many nights unfolding to you, into a single ordered response to the turning of the year, a whole people, guided by its wisest, marshaling all its sacred resources to meet the coming time as well as it can be met."}, {"id": "orisa-babalawos-year#s10", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "That, my child, is the crown of the whole system, and a fitting place to draw near the end of our long telling of the orisa. For I have shown you a people who looked at the whole of existence, the making of the world and the shaping of humankind, the fire and the water and the iron and the storm, the healing and the sickness, the mothers and the ancestors and the twins and the inner head, the crossroads and the destiny and the turning of the very year, and who found in all of it not chaos but order, not blind fate but a world of powers that could be known, honoured, and lived with in wisdom. That is the achievement of your people’s understanding, my child, and it is a mighty one, worthy to stand beside the great wisdoms of any people anywhere on earth."}, {"id": "orisa-babalawos-year#s11", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "We have walked far together in this cycle, my child, from the cool white shaper of heads to the great yearly reading of the fortune of a people. We have met the powers of the sky and the earth and the water and the fire, the powers of healing and of sickness, of the crossroads and of the crown, of the mothers and the ancestors and the twins, and, most intimately, the power of your own inner head. And through all of it I have tried to show you not a collection of quaint old superstitions, but a great, coherent, profound, and beautiful understanding of the world, wrought by your ancestors over uncounted generations, an understanding that honoured the fierce and the gentle alike, that faced the hardest truths without flinching and yet found in them lessons of mercy and humility and wisdom, and that gave a whole people a way to live with dignity, with reverence, with joy, and with courage, in a world of great and mysterious powers."}, {"id": "orisa-babalawos-year#s12", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "Carry it, my child. Carry these tellings in your heart, and tell them, one day, to your own children by a fire much like this one, so that the great understanding of your people is not lost, so that the orisa are still honoured and the ancestors still remembered and the wisdom still passed on. For that is how a people lives forever: not in stone and not in gold, but in the tellings passed from the old to the young, night after night, by the fire, from the beginning of the world until its end."}, {"id": "orisa-babalawos-year#s13", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "There. The great cycle of the orisa is told, or the greatest part of it, for the powers are as many as the leaves and no telling can hold them all. Rest now. And tomorrow, if the ancestors are willing, we will turn to new matters, to the great ones of our people who walked in more recent days, but tonight belongs to all the orisa together, and to the wisdom that binds them, and we will sleep in the good order of a world well understood."}]}, {"id": "orisa-osun-osogbo", "emoji": "⚡", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#150020,#350050)", "category": "orisa", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "8 min", "title": {"en": "Osun-Osogbo: The Goddess, the Grove, and the Pact by the River", "yo": "Osun-Osogbo"}, "desc": {"en": "Osogbo's founding pact with the river goddess, the sacred grove, and the festival that renews it every year.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Orisa", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Oba, from “Osun-Osogbo”)", "segments": [{"id": "orisa-osun-osogbo#s1", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "There are towns founded by warriors and towns founded by kings, and then there are towns founded by a friendship between a people and a goddess, and Osogbo is one of these. Its story does not begin with a battle or a crown, but with thirsty travelers, a great river, and a voice that rose out of the water. And it is a story still living, for the pact that was made by that river in the deep past is renewed every single year, in one of the greatest festivals the Yoruba world has ever kept, so that Osogbo is not merely a town with an old legend, but a town whose legend walks its streets and sings by its water to this very day."}, {"id": "orisa-osun-osogbo#s2", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "The elders tell that in the old time a group of settlers, led by a hunter and his people, came wandering through the forest looking for a place to build. They were led by Olutimehin the hunter, and behind him came the man who would be their king, Larooye, and they were tired and dry and looking for water, for no settlement can stand where there is no water. They came at last to the banks of a great river running clear through the forest, and they rejoiced, for here was water enough for a whole city, and they began at once to cut the trees and clear the ground to build. But as they felled a great tree it crashed down into the river, and from the water rose a voice, not angry exactly, but reproachful, crying out that they had broken her dye-pots, that they were spoiling the home of the river and her children. This was Osun, the goddess of the river, the owner of those waters, and the settlers had blundered into her sacred place."}, {"id": "orisa-osun-osogbo#s3", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Now here is the founding pact, the heart of the whole matter. The settlers did not flee, and the goddess did not destroy them. Instead the two sides came to an understanding, and it is one of the most beautiful bargains in all the old stories. Osun told them they could not build there, on her very banks, but that they should move a little away, up to higher ground, and there build their town. And in return for the room they gave her, and the respect they showed her water, she would be their protector. She would give them children, for Osun is the giver of children above all, the goddess to whom barren women pray and go home carrying life. She would give them prosperity and safe water and long life. And they, for their part, would honor her forever, would keep her grove, and would come down to her each year with gifts and worship. So the people moved up and founded Osogbo, and the name itself holds the story, tied to the wizard or the spirit of the place, Oso Igbo, the sorcerer of the forest, and the deal was struck. The goddess kept the river and the sacred grove around it, and the people kept the town, and between them ran a promise that was never to be broken."}, {"id": "orisa-osun-osogbo#s4", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "And the extraordinary thing, the thing that makes Osogbo unlike almost any other place, is that the promise really was kept, across centuries, across the coming of new religions, across colonial rule and independence and all the changes of the modern world. Every year the people of Osogbo keep the festival of Osun, and it is a spectacle to shake the heart. The whole town pours down toward the river and the grove. At the center of it moves the Arugba, the sacred votary maiden, a young virgin of the royal house who carries upon her head the covered calabash of sacrifice, and she must carry it in a trance of concentration, for she bears the prayers of the whole people to the goddess, and the crowds press around her but must not let her stumble. Drums thunder, praise singers chant the old praises of Osun, the priestesses in white move through the throng, and the king and his chiefs process in their full splendor. Down at the river the offerings are given, the goddess is petitioned for children and health and prosperity for another year, and the ancient bargain by the water is renewed once more, exactly as it was first struck, so that the deep past and the living present meet on the riverbank and become one thing."}, {"id": "orisa-osun-osogbo#s5", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "But there is a later chapter to this story, and it is a strange and wonderful one, for it tells how the goddess found an unlikely champion in the modern age. Through the long years the sacred grove along the river, the forest that Osun had kept for herself in the old bargain, had suffered. As the town grew and the new faiths spread and the old worship weakened, the grove was encroached upon, its shrines neglected, its trees cut, and it might have shrunk away to nothing as so many sacred places have. Then, in the middle of the twentieth century, there came to Osogbo an Austrian woman, an artist named Susanne Wenger, who fell so deeply in love with the Yoruba gods and the Yoruba way of seeing the world that she gave the rest of her life to them. She became a devotee of the orisa, was initiated into the priesthood, and took as her life’s work the saving and the rebuilding of the sacred grove of Osun. With local artists and craftsmen she raised great new sculptures and shrines among the trees, massive and dreamlike forms in earth and iron that seem to grow out of the forest itself, and she fought off the loggers and the developers and the doubters, and slowly the grove came back to life, protected, honored, filled with art and worship once more."}, {"id": "orisa-osun-osogbo#s6", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "And so the old bargain was kept yet again, in a way the first settlers could never have imagined, by a stranger from a far country who loved the goddess enough to spend her life defending the goddess’s home. Because of that work, and because of the unbroken festival, and because the grove is one of the last great sacred forests of its kind left in all the land, the world itself took notice. The sacred grove of Osun-Osogbo was named a World Heritage Site, recognized as a treasure not merely of the Yoruba or of one nation but of all humanity, a place the whole earth agreed was worth protecting forever. Think of the distance the story has traveled. It began with a hunter and a felled tree and a reproachful voice in the water, and it arrived at the whole world standing guard over a goddess’s forest."}, {"id": "orisa-osun-osogbo#s7", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "That is why Osogbo matters, and why the elders tell it with such love. It is proof that a promise can outlast empires. Kingdoms that seemed mightier than Osogbo have fallen and been forgotten, but the little pact struck by a river, that we will honor you and you will bless us, has been renewed without fail through every storm of history, and is renewed still, every year, when the Arugba carries the calabash down to the water and the whole town holds its breath. The goddess kept her side, the people kept theirs, and the river still runs, and the children still come, and the drums still sound by the water. When you feel that nothing lasts, the elders say, remember Osogbo, and remember that some promises, faithfully kept, can be more durable than stone."}]}, {"id": "orisa-yewa", "emoji": "⚡", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#150020,#350050)", "category": "orisa", "rating": 9.4, "duration": "4 min", "title": {"en": "Yewa: The Orisa of the River of the Dead", "yo": "Yewa"}, "desc": {"en": "Yewa, the orisa of the river of the dead.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Orisa", "secondary": [], "source_note": "", "segments": [{"id": "orisa-yewa#s1", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Narrator opens: There is a river in the Yoruba cosmos that runs in the opposite direction from Osun's life-giving waters. Where Osun flows toward the living — toward fertility, toward honey, toward the sweetness of embodied existence — Yewa flows toward what comes after. She is the Orisa of the river of the dead, the keeper of the boundary between the living world and the world beyond it, the one who accompanies the soul on the final crossing. Yewa is the daughter of Yemoja — a daughter of the water, moving through the world in a specific direction that her mother's broader domain does not fully reach. She is associated with the cemetery, with the ancestors, with the quiet dignity of death approached without fear. Her color is pink and white — not the red of danger or the indigo of the deep ocean, but the pale colors of the threshold, neither fully alive nor fully dead."}, {"id": "orisa-yewa#s2", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "She is a virgin Orisa in the specific sense that she is not associated with earthly sexuality or reproduction — her domain is what comes after the body's productive life, the preparation for the crossing. This quality of untouchability — of being beyond ordinary human desire and beyond ordinary human claim — is central to her character. She is not cold. She is simply not available to the grasping that characterizes the living world. Her festival is quiet. Her worshippers approach her with restraint, without the ecstatic drumming and dancing that characterizes Sango's festival or Osun's. She receives her offerings with a dignity that asks the same in return. She is the reminder that the way a person dies matters — that the crossing deserves attention and care."}, {"id": "orisa-yewa#s3", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "In the diaspora, Yewa's tradition is less widely preserved than some of the more prominent Orisa. But in specific communities — in certain Candomble terreiros, in some Lucumi houses — her presence is maintained. She travels with the dead wherever the dead go. She is always present at the crossing, waiting."}]}, {"id": "orisa-orisa-401", "emoji": "⚡", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#150020,#350050)", "category": "orisa", "rating": 9.4, "duration": "5 min", "title": {"en": "The 401 Orisas: A Field Guide to the Yoruba Divine", "yo": "The 401 Orisas: A Field Guide to the Yoruba Divine"}, "desc": {"en": "A field guide to the 401 orisa of the Yoruba divine.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Orisa", "secondary": [], "source_note": "", "segments": [{"id": "orisa-orisa-401#s1", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Narrator opens: The Yoruba say there are 401 Orisas. The number is both literal and figurative — it means \"innumerable,\" the number that exceeds the largest round number and therefore implies that the divine is too large to fully catalog. It is a statement of sacred infinity. But within that infinity, there are the major Orisas whose stories are widely known, whose rituals are actively practiced, whose presence in the Yoruba world — and in the diaspora — is most visible. Let me introduce them, one by one, so that when their names come up in the stories of Aroba, you know who you are meeting. Oduduwa: The progenitor of the Yoruba people, the one who descended from heaven and created the earth at Ile-Ife. He is both ancestor and Orisa — the bridge between the human and the divine at the very origin of things. His festival — the Edi festival — is celebrated in Ile-Ife."}, {"id": "orisa-orisa-401#s2", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Obatala: The arch-divinity, the sculptor of human forms, the white Orisa of purity and creation. He is associated with the sky, with chalk, with patience and wisdom. His festival is one of the most solemn in the Yoruba calendar. Orunmila: The Orisa of wisdom and divination, the keeper of Ifa. He was present when each soul chose its destiny and helps humans remember their purpose through the Ifa oracle. His priests, the Babalawo, are among the most important figures in Yoruba religious life. Ogun: The Orisa of iron, of roads, of hunting, of war. He is the pioneer and the patron of all who work with metal. His energy is fierce and necessary. Sango: The fourth Alaafin of Oyo who became the Orisa of thunder and lightning. He is associated with justice — lightning strikes the liar. He is the most dramatically realized of the Orisas, with a vast ritual tradition and worldwide diaspora presence."}, {"id": "orisa-orisa-401#s3", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "Osun: The Orisa of the Osun River, of sweetness, fertility, love, and beauty. She is the diplomatic principle among the Orisas, the reminder that the feminine must be included for the world to work. Oya: The Orisa of the storm wind and the keeper of the dead. She is associated with change, transformation, and the fierce independent feminine. Yemoja: The Orisa of the Ogun River and, in the diaspora, of the ocean. She is the mother Orisa, the one whose children are as numerous as fish in the sea. Esu: The divine trickster and messenger, the keeper of the crossroads, the first to be acknowledged in any ceremony. He is the medium through which all spiritual communication passes. Soponna (Obaluaiye): The Orisa of the earth, of disease and healing. He is associated with smallpox historically and with infectious disease generally. His energy is feared and respected — he reminds humans of their vulnerability to the natural world."}, {"id": "orisa-orisa-401#s4", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Orisa Oko: The Orisa of agriculture, of the farm, of planting and harvest. His sacred groves are in Ira, in Osun State. Erinle (Eyinle): The Orisa of the river and the forest, associated with abundance and hunting. Aje: The Orisa of wealth and prosperity, often associated with female commercial power. Ososi: The Orisa of hunting, often paired with Ogun and popular in the Cuban Lukumi tradition. Omolu: Associated with smallpox and healing, particularly prevalent in Brazil. Nana Buruku: An ancient Orisa associated with the primordial waters before creation, venerated particularly in the Dahomey-influenced traditions. This is not a complete list. It cannot be. The Yoruba divine world is as diverse and complex as the human world. Each Orisa has their own personality, their own domain, their own sacred objects and foods and colors and days. Each has a vast body of mythology, of Ifa verses, of ritual practice associated with them."}, {"id": "orisa-orisa-401#s5", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "To know the Orisas is not simply to know a list of gods. It is to know a cosmology — a sophisticated way of understanding how different forces in the universe relate to each other, how the human and the divine interact, how individual destiny connects to universal purpose. The 401 Orisas. The immeasurable divine. The sacred ecology of the Yoruba universe. You cannot learn all of them in a single sitting. But you can begin. Begin with the one whose name called to you first."}]}, {"id": "orisa-oba-wife-of-sango", "emoji": "⚡", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#150020,#350050)", "category": "orisa", "rating": 9.8, "duration": "17 min", "title": {"en": "Oba: The Wife Who Loved Too Much", "yo": "Oba"}, "desc": {"en": "Oba, Sango's devoted wife, deceived by Osun into the sacrifice that became a river.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Orisa", "secondary": [], "source_note": "", "segments": [{"id": "orisa-oba-wife-of-sango#s1", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "[Full Mythological Biography | Genre: Sacred Myth + Tragedy | Mood: Intimate, Tender, Devastating | Duration: ~18 mins] WHO IS OBA? Oba is not a minor figure in the Yoruba pantheon. She is an Orisa in her own right — a river deity, the personification of the Oba River that flows through present-day Oyo and Osun States in southwestern Nigeria. Before she ever encountered Sango, before she was a wife or a rival or a tragic figure in someone else's story, Oba existed. She was a force of nature with her own domain, her own worshippers, her own sacred responsibilities. Her name — Oba — is the Yoruba word for king or ruler, but when applied to her, it takes on a different meaning. She is sometimes called Obara-oro, \"the one who speaks with authority.\" She is the river that has its own voice. She was always more than the wife she became in myth. WHERE DOES OBA COME FROM?"}, {"id": "orisa-oba-wife-of-sango#s2", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "The Oba River originates in the Ekiti highlands and flows westward, eventually joining the Osun River. The confluence of the Oba and Osun Rivers — at a place near Iwo and Oshogbo — is one of the most sacred hydrological sites in Yorubaland. Local tradition says the turbulent waters at this confluence are Oba and Osun still fighting — still quarreling centuries after the events of Sango's household. In her mythological origins, Oba is said to have emerged from the earth itself at the source of the Oba River — one of the primordial forces that Olodumare placed in the landscape to sustain life. Where Osun is sweet water that flows smoothly, Oba is the river that sometimes floods, that has power without prettiness, that nourishes through abundance rather than through elegance. Her primary shrine town is Oba-Ile — meaning \"Town of Oba\" — near Akure in Ondo State. Here, her priests maintain her worship in its most ancient forms."}, {"id": "orisa-oba-wife-of-sango#s3", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "OBA'S FAMILY AND LINEAGE In the Orisa cosmology, Oba is one of the daughters of Yemoja — the great mother of waters. This makes her a divine sibling of Sango and Ogun. The family relationships within the Orisa pantheon are complex, but Oba's identification as a daughter of Yemoja places her firmly within the water family of Orisas — associated with fertility, abundance, and the life-giving power of rivers. She is sometimes said to be a sister to Osun — in some traditions they are sisters, in others simply rivals. If they are sisters, then the later betrayal between them is even more devastating: not merely two women competing for a man's attention, but two sisters who should have been each other's support and instead became each other's wound. BEFORE SANGO: OBA IN HER OWN RIGHT"}, {"id": "orisa-oba-wife-of-sango#s4", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Before the marriage, Oba had her own community of worshippers and her own ritual life. She was venerated by farmers who depended on the Oba River for irrigation, by fishermen who worked its waters, by women seeking fertility who came to her river banks with offerings. She had her own festivals, her own priests and priestesses, her own moral teachings. The teaching of Oba — independent of the Sango story — is about generous love and the abundance that comes from the earth. She is associated with the flood plain: areas the river covers in high season, leaving behind nutrient-rich soil that makes agriculture possible. She represents love that gives abundantly, that covers and nourishes everything in its path — but that can also overwhelm when it overflows its banks."}, {"id": "orisa-oba-wife-of-sango#s5", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "She was a power — a river deity accustomed to worship and abundance — before she ever met Sango. Her falling for him was not weakness. It was a catastrophic mismatch between two enormous energies. HOW OBA AND SANGO MEET Sango, before his ascension to the Alaafin's throne, was traveling through the territories where the great rivers ran. He was not yet king — he was a prince, a warrior, a man whose power was still raw and uncontained. He encountered Oba at her river, and what he saw was not simply a beautiful woman but a presence — the kind of dignified, steady, abundant presence that a man of Sango's intensity finds both attractive and challenging."}, {"id": "orisa-oba-wife-of-sango#s6", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Sango was drawn to her because she was different from him. Where he was fire, she was water. Where he was explosive and unpredictable, she was steady and deep. There is a theological complementarity between fire and water in the Yoruba cosmology — they need each other, they balance each other — and this was the foundation of their attraction. In some traditions, Oba's family arranged the marriage — she was given to Sango as a bride appropriate to his station. In others, Sango pursued her and she accepted. What all traditions agree on is that she came to the marriage with complete commitment. Whatever Sango was — and even then, before the kingship, he was complicated — Oba was his. THE MARRIAGE: THREE WIVES, ONE COMPOUND"}, {"id": "orisa-oba-wife-of-sango#s7", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "When Oba entered Sango's household, she was the first wife — the Iyaale, the senior wife. She brought everything: exceptional household management, loyalty, skilled administration of the royal compound. She was described as \"beautiful like the flood plain\" — rich, fertile, full. Then Sango acquired two more wives: Oya (the fierce storm wind) and Osun (the sweet river). Each represented a different relationship to the thunder king. Oya was his equal — fierce, independent, matching his energy. Osun was his delight — the sweetness and honey that softened his intensity. Oba had seniority, but she had neither Oya's wild independence nor Osun's captivating sweetness. As Sango spent more time with Osun, Oba's seniority felt hollow. DID OBA AND SANGO HAVE CHILDREN?"}, {"id": "orisa-oba-wife-of-sango#s8", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Different traditions diverge here in ways that are themselves meaningful. Some say Oba and Sango had no children — that despite the completeness of her devotion, the marriage was not blessed with offspring. In this version, her childlessness deepens her vulnerability: without heirs who were Sango's children, her position in the household was less secure. Without the bond of shared parenthood, the marriage was more fragile."}, {"id": "orisa-oba-wife-of-sango#s9", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "Other traditions say they had at least one son together — though that child is not named with the prominence of children associated with other unions. The theological weight of the childless version suggests it may be older: Oba's fertility — her characteristic divine gift as a river deity — was somehow blocked within the marriage. The river that nourished the entire land could not nourish the one household she most wanted to fill. This is the precise kind of cosmic irony that Yoruba mythology, with its sharp eye for suffering's patterns, would not miss. THE FATAL DECEPTION: OSUN'S LIE IN FULL Oba went to Osun for advice. This was an act of profound vulnerability — going not to her own allies but to her rival, with an honest admission: I am losing him. Tell me what I am missing."}, {"id": "orisa-oba-wife-of-sango#s10", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "Osun had choices in that moment. She could have been kind. She could have said the truth: that Sango's preferences were a matter of his own personality and that no recipe would change what was in his heart. She could have been a sister. She chose the lie. Perhaps it was mischief — Osun's trickster dimension. Perhaps it was calculated cruelty — knowing that if Oba acted on the lie, she would remove herself as competition. Perhaps it was something in between. \"I cut off my own ear,\" Osun told Oba, \"and I cook it in the soup. That is why Sango cannot resist my food.\" And then — the dark genius of the deception — she covered her ear with her hair or a head tie. Oba searched for the evidence of Osun's sacrifice and could not find its absence. The lie was sealed. THE ACT ITSELF"}, {"id": "orisa-oba-wife-of-sango#s11", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "Oba went home. She made her decision alone, in the privacy of her own conviction. She had been told that love required the ultimate proof — and she believed it, because she already knew she was willing to give everything. The ear was just the latest thing on a list of everything she had already given. She cut off her own ear. She cooked it in the soup. She prepared the meal with the love of a woman who believed this act would restore what she had lost — who believed that Osun's secret was real and that this sacrifice would finally, finally, make Sango see her. She served it to Sango. And Sango saw the ear in his bowl. SANGO'S REACTION AND OBA'S FLIGHT"}, {"id": "orisa-oba-wife-of-sango#s12", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "Sango's reaction is described across traditions with varying degrees of horror. He was revolted. He was horrified. In the most nuanced versions, there were multiple horrors: the physical fact of the ear in his food, the realization of what Oba had done to herself, the terrible question of what she had been told and by whom, and the unbearable sight of his first wife — wounded, waiting, still hoping. He rejected the food. He rejected Oba. And Oba, seeing her sacrifice received with horror, broke. She did not stay to explain. She did not wait for compassion. She ran — from the compound, from the city, from the life she had tried to build and could not hold. She ran weeping, and her weeping became a river. She ran until she dissolved into the landscape. She became the Oba River. THE CONFLUENCE: WHERE OBA AND OSUN MEET FOREVER"}, {"id": "orisa-oba-wife-of-sango#s13", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "The Oba River and the Osun River meet at their confluence — and the meeting is not peaceful. The waters churn. The currents conflict. Eddies form and spin. The Yoruba say this is Oba and Osun still fighting. Even as rivers, they cannot make peace. Oba's grief and Osun's guilt and Sango's horror — all of it persists in the turbulent water at their meeting point. When you stand at this confluence and watch the water churn, you are not watching hydraulics. You are watching unresolved feeling written into the landscape. You are watching what happens when a lie is never corrected, when a wound is never healed, when two ancient divine figures carry their quarrel for centuries without resolution. WHAT HAPPENED TO OBA AFTER SANGO?"}, {"id": "orisa-oba-wife-of-sango#s14", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "After her transformation into a river, Oba did not cease to exist or to feel. In the Yoruba understanding of Orisa existence, transformation is not death — it is a change of form. She continued as a river, but she was still Oba, still the same consciousness, still carrying the same wound. Some traditions say Sango eventually learned the truth — that Osun had lied to Oba, that Oba's act was not madness but desperate love. And that when he understood what had been done to her, his grief was profound. But by then, she was already a river. The timing of understanding and the timing of presence were not the same."}, {"id": "orisa-oba-wife-of-sango#s15", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "In the river form, Oba has continued to receive the devotion she was always owed. Women who have been deceived by rivals, women who have given too much for too little, women whose households have been taken over by others with more charm — they come to Oba's river. They pour their grief into her water. And she holds it. OBA'S FESTIVALS AND LIVING WORSHIP Oba's primary festivals are observed at Oba-Ile in Ondo State and at communities along the Oba River's course. Her devotees dress in white and yellow — the colors of the flood plain's sandy soil. The ritual foods brought to Oba's shrines are prepared with deliberate care — soups made with love, offerings that acknowledge the story's central tragedy and transform it. The act of bringing Oba a beautiful, lovingly prepared meal is itself a form of repair: here is food made for you in honesty. Here is what you deserved."}, {"id": "orisa-oba-wife-of-sango#s16", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "Her priests and priestesses — the Oba-ologun — maintain the oral tradition of her story, the sacred objects at her shrines (specific cooking bowls, ladles), and the healing ceremonies for women who come seeking her comfort. THE MORAL TEACHINGS OF OBA'S STORY The tradition draws several ethical teachings from Oba's story: First: Never ask your rival for advice about how to keep your beloved. The person who benefits from your failure is not the person who will tell you how to succeed. Second: Even the most beloved sacred figures are capable of cruelty. Osun — sweet, radiant Osun — lied in a way that destroyed another woman's life. The tradition does not erase this. It preserves it as a reminder that complexity is everywhere, that the river of sweetness can also be the source of harm."}, {"id": "orisa-oba-wife-of-sango#s17", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "Third: Love that asks you to destroy yourself for the beloved's attention is not the love being asked for. Sango did not ask for Oba's ear. He was horrified by it. The sacrifice was made for an audience of one — and that one person's response was revulsion, not recognition. Fourth: The honest, devoted partner deserves better than what Oba received. Her loyalty, her household management, her complete commitment — all of these were genuine virtues. The tragedy is not that she was wrong to have these qualities. It is that the context did not honor them. OBA IN THE DIASPORA In Cuba's Lukumi tradition, Oba is venerated with her ear story intact. She is associated with Saint Catherine of Alexandria — another woman who suffered for something she believed in. Her sacred secrets and ritual protocols are maintained in the initiated tradition."}, {"id": "orisa-oba-wife-of-sango#s18", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "In Brazil's Candomble, she is less central but present in the Ketu Nation. Her story survives in the memory of communities that crossed the Atlantic with their Orisas held against loss. In contemporary African American and diaspora spiritual practice, Oba has found new relevance. She is the Orisa of women healing from relationships where they gave everything and received harm. She is the patron of those who finally understand — sometimes decades late — that the love they offered was real, the sacrifice was real, and the person who received it did not deserve it. THE RIVER TODAY The Oba River still flows through Ondo and Oyo and Osun States. It still churns at its confluence with the Osun. The communities along its banks still maintain their relationship with the river as a sacred presence."}, {"id": "orisa-oba-wife-of-sango#s19", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "The river faces modern threats — deforestation, pollution, agricultural runoff. But the devotees of Oba understand that protecting the river is an act of religious responsibility. She is not a symbol of the river. She is the river. Her well-being and the river's well-being are inseparable. To honor Oba is to keep her water clean. To keep her water clean is to honor the earth that sustains all of us. The ecological and the spiritual are not separate languages for the same thing — they are the same thing. WHAT OBA MEANS TODAY Oba asks one of the hardest questions in human experience: at what point does devotion become self-destruction? When does loyalty to someone who does not see you become a betrayal of yourself?"}, {"id": "orisa-oba-wife-of-sango#s20", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "She does not have an easy answer. She is the Orisa of those who learned the hard way — who cut off the ear, metaphorically or literally, and found that the act brought horror rather than love. She holds that experience without shame. She does not demand that you have made better choices. She asks only that you come to the river, pour your grief into the water, and let it flow."}]}, {"id": "folk-tortoise-feast-of-birds", "emoji": "📖", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#0a1a00,#1a3a00)", "category": "iwe", "rating": 9.7, "duration": "13 min", "title": {"en": "The Tortoise and the Feast of the Birds", "yo": "Ijapa"}, "desc": {"en": "The tortoise's trick to join the birds' feast in the sky, and why his shell is cracked.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Folktales / Literary", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Apagbe, consolidates “The Tortoise and the Feast of the Birds” and “The Tortoise and the Sky King’s Feast”)", "segments": [{"id": "folk-tortoise-feast-of-birds#s1", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Ah, now we come to a different kind of story, my children. Put away the crowns and the war-drums for a while, and let us laugh a little, and let us learn the way our people have always taught the young: through the doings of Ijapa the tortoise, that cunning, greedy, clever, foolish creature who is all of us at our worst and, sometimes, at our funniest. Gather close, for tonight Ijapa is going to fly. Yes, fly. And you will see what his cleverness earns him."}, {"id": "folk-tortoise-feast-of-birds#s2", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "You must understand first who Ijapa is, for he is the hero and the villain of a thousand of our tales. The tortoise is small and slow and weak. He has no claws to speak of, no speed, no great strength. What he has, and what he has in abundance, is cunning. Wit. The sharp and slippery cleverness of the one who must live by his brains because his body cannot save him. In this, my children, Ijapa is a teacher, for many of the weak of this world survive by their wits. But Ijapa also has a great flaw, and the flaw is this: his greed and his pride are even larger than his cleverness. And so again and again his own tricks turn in his hand and cut him. Watch now, and see it happen."}, {"id": "folk-tortoise-feast-of-birds#s3", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "It happened that in a certain season there was hunger on the earth. The rains had been poor, the farms were thin, and Ijapa the tortoise, who loved to eat more than he loved anything in this world, grew lean and desperate. His belly rumbled like distant thunder. His wife scolded him for bringing home nothing. And Ijapa, lying on his back in the heat of the day, turned his sharp mind this way and that, looking for a way to fill his stomach."}, {"id": "folk-tortoise-feast-of-birds#s4", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Now word came, as word does, carried on the wind and on the gossip of the compound, that up in the sky the birds had been invited to a great feast. The sky people, the beings of the heavens, were holding a celebration, and all the birds of the earth had been asked to come and eat their fill. And oh, what a feast it was said to be. Mountains of pounded yam. Rivers of egusi soup thick with meat and fish. Every delicacy the heart could desire, spread out in the courts of the sky, free for the eating. When Ijapa heard this, the water ran in his mouth until he nearly drowned in it. A feast in the sky. Endless food. And here he lay, starving on the earth. It was not to be borne."}, {"id": "folk-tortoise-feast-of-birds#s5", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "But there was a difficulty, and it was a large one. Ijapa could not fly. He was a tortoise. He was bound to the earth, heavy and slow, while the birds had wings to carry them up into the heavens. How was a tortoise to reach a feast in the sky?"}, {"id": "folk-tortoise-feast-of-birds#s6", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "A lesser creature would have sighed and gone hungry. But not Ijapa. His cunning went to work. He went among the birds, and to each one he spoke with honey on his tongue. My friends, he said, my dear friends the birds, I have heard of this great feast in the sky, and I am so happy for you. But I am sad too, for I cannot come, having no wings. Yet I would so love to share in your joy. Would each of you, out of your great kindness, lend me one feather? Just one feather each, and I could make myself wings and come along and celebrate with you, my beloved friends."}, {"id": "folk-tortoise-feast-of-birds#s7", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Now the birds, my children, were kinder than they were wise. One by one, moved by his sweet words, each bird plucked a feather and gave it to Ijapa. And Ijapa, clever Ijapa, bound all these feathers together and made himself a fine suit of wings, bright with every colour of every bird in the world. And when he put it on, he could fly. The tortoise rose into the air on his borrowed feathers, and his heart swelled with triumph and with greed. But listen now, for here is where the cleverness curdles into something worse. As the great flock rose together toward the sky, Ijapa flew among them, and his sharp mind kept working, and it turned now to a wicked scheme. For Ijapa did not wish merely to share the feast. Ijapa wished to have it all."}, {"id": "folk-tortoise-feast-of-birds#s8", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "And so, as they flew, Ijapa spoke to the birds again. My friends, he said, when we arrive at a great feast such as this, it is the custom, the proper and polite custom, for everyone to take a new name for the occasion. It is a mark of respect to our hosts. And since it was I who thought of this, allow me the honour of a fine name. And Ijapa announced that his name for the feast would be All of You. That is my feast-name, he said. All of You. Remember it well. The birds, still more kind than wise, thought this a harmless bit of nonsense and agreed."}, {"id": "folk-tortoise-feast-of-birds#s9", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "So they came at last to the courts of the sky, and oh, my children, the feast was everything that had been promised and more. The pounded yam rose in white mountains. The soups steamed in great pots, rich and red and fragrant. The meat, the fish, the sweet things, all of it spread out in endless plenty. The hungry birds’ eyes went wide, and Ijapa’s widest of all. The sky people, the generous hosts, brought out the food and set it down, and they said graciously, welcome, welcome, our friends. This feast, all of this food, is prepared for all of you. It is for all of you. And Ijapa leapt forward. For all of you? He cried. Did you hear, my friends? Did you hear our kind hosts? This feast is for All of You. And I am All of You. They have said it themselves. This feast is mine."}, {"id": "folk-tortoise-feast-of-birds#s10", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "And the wretched, greedy tortoise fell upon the food and began to devour it, guarding it with his borrowed wings spread wide, shouting that the hosts had given it all to him, to All of You, and that the birds must keep back. The birds, astonished and then furious, could only watch as Ijapa gorged himself on the feast that had been meant for every one of them. His trick had worked. He ate and ate while his friends went hungry. Now, my children, you know already that a story does not end at the moment of a cheat’s triumph. That would not be a Yoruba story. Watch what the birds do."}, {"id": "folk-tortoise-feast-of-birds#s11", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "The birds were angry, angrier than Ijapa in his greed had bothered to notice. And their anger was a cold and a clever thing, for even birds can learn cunning when a tortoise teaches it to them so cruelly. They said nothing. They let him eat. And when the feast was ending and it was time to return to the earth, each bird stepped forward, quietly, and took back its feather. One feather, and another, and another, plucked from Ijapa’s borrowed wings, until the suit of wings fell apart entirely and there was nothing left. And the birds, their own wings whole, rose up and flew away home, and left Ijapa the tortoise standing at the edge of the sky with no way down."}, {"id": "folk-tortoise-feast-of-birds#s12", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "There stood Ijapa, his belly grotesquely full, his wings gone, the earth impossibly far below. His cleverness had carried him up. His greed had stranded him at the top. And now, my children, the cunning that had served his greed had to serve his survival instead, and desperately. He called down toward the earth, toward his own house, hoping his wife might hear. Wife, he cried, wife, gather all the soft things in our house. Gather the cloths, the rags, the soft grasses, and pile them on the ground below to catch me, for I must jump, and I will surely die if I strike the bare earth. Tell her, he begged of any bird still near, tell my wife to lay out the soft things."}, {"id": "folk-tortoise-feast-of-birds#s13", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "But there was a bird still near, one who had heard everything, and that bird carried the message. Yet the bird, out of malice or out of justice, changed it. The bird flew to Ijapa’s wife and told her that her husband said to gather all the hard things, the stones, the hoes, the cutlasses, the broken pots, and pile them on the ground. And the wife, believing the message true, did exactly that. She laid out a bed of stones and iron and jagged broken things, and waited for her husband to descend. And Ijapa jumped. Down and down and down he fell from the sky, his heavy body plummeting, until he struck the ground, the ground that he thought would be soft and was hard as anger. He crashed onto the stones and the iron and the broken pots with a terrible sound."}, {"id": "folk-tortoise-feast-of-birds#s14", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "He did not die, my children. Ijapa never quite dies, for he must be back tomorrow to be foolish again. But his shell, which had been smooth and whole and beautiful before that day, was shattered by the fall into many pieces. It cracked all across into a map of broken lines. And to this very day, when you pick up a tortoise and look at the pattern on its back, you will see it: the shell is not smooth, but broken into many small plates, cracked all over into pieces that healed back together crookedly. That, my children, is the mark of Ijapa’s greed. Every tortoise since carries on its very back the memory of the day the tortoise cheated his friends at the feast in the sky and fell to the earth for it."}, {"id": "folk-tortoise-feast-of-birds#s15", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "So what does Ijapa teach us tonight, my children? He teaches us more than one thing, for a good story is like a good soup, with many things in the pot. He teaches us that cleverness without goodness will turn against you. Ijapa was clever enough to make wings and reach the sky. But because his cleverness served only his greed, that same cleverness led him to a scheme that lost him all his friends and stranded him at the top of the world. He teaches us that greed is never satisfied and always overreaches. Ijapa could have shared the feast and eaten well and flown home happy on his borrowed wings. But he wanted all of it, and in grabbing for all of it he lost even the little he might have had."}, {"id": "folk-tortoise-feast-of-birds#s16", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "And he teaches us that those we cheat will remember. The birds seemed kind and foolish, and Ijapa took them for fools. But every one of them took back its feather, and the wrong he did them came home to roost, as wrongs so often do, upon his own cracked back. Remember Ijapa, my children, when your own cleverness whispers to you a plan to take what is not fairly yours. Remember the tortoise standing at the edge of the sky with no wings, because he was too greedy to leave the other birds theirs. And remember to be, always, a little kinder than you are clever. For cleverness will lift you up, but only goodness will give you a soft place to land."}, {"id": "folk-tortoise-feast-of-birds#s17", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "Tomorrow, my children, we will hear how it was that stories came to belong to us at all, in the days when all the tales in the world were locked away in the house of the sky god, and it was Ijapa, of all creatures, who set them free."}]}, {"id": "folk-stories-of-the-sky-god", "emoji": "📖", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#0a1a00,#1a3a00)", "category": "iwe", "rating": 9.6, "duration": "11 min", "title": {"en": "When Stories Belonged to the Sky God", "yo": "When Stories Belonged to the Sky God"}, "desc": {"en": "How the tortoise won the world's stories from the sky god.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Folktales / Literary", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Apagbe, the tale of how the tortoise won the world’s stories and why we may tell them at all)", "segments": [{"id": "folk-stories-of-the-sky-god#s1", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Sit down, my children, and settle yourselves, for tonight I owe you an answer to a question you have never thought to ask. You come to this fire, and I open my mouth, and stories pour out of it, tale after tale of kings and warriors and tricksters and gods. But have you ever wondered where the stories came from? Who owned them first? For everything in this world was owned by someone in the beginning, and stories are a thing, a precious thing, and in the oldest days the stories did not belong to us at all. They belonged to the sky god. And the tale of how they came down to us, so that I may tell them and you may hear them, is itself one of the finest stories of all. So let me tell you the story of the stories."}, {"id": "folk-stories-of-the-sky-god#s2", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "In the beginning of things, all the stories in the world belonged to Olodumare, or in some tellings to a great sky king who kept them, and they were locked away in the heavens. There were no tales told on the earth in those days. Imagine it, my children, a world with no stories. The evenings were silent. The children grew up hearing nothing of the ancestors, nothing of the orisa, nothing of the clever tortoise or the proud king. The people ate and worked and slept, and when the darkness came they had nothing to gather around, for there were no tales to tell. It was a poorer world, a hungrier world, hungry in the spirit even when the belly was full. For a people without stories, my children, is a people without a mirror, a people who cannot see themselves."}, {"id": "folk-stories-of-the-sky-god#s3", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "And on the earth, it was the tortoise, Ijapa, that same cunning creature you met last night, who longed for the stories most of all. For Ijapa loved cleverness, and what is a story but cleverness set down and shared? Ijapa went up, in the way that creatures of tales can go up, and stood before the sky god, and asked to be given the stories, so that they might be told on the earth."}, {"id": "folk-stories-of-the-sky-god#s4", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "The sky god looked down at the small, slow, weak creature before him and was amused. You? He said. You wish to own all the stories of the world? Very well. I will sell them to you. But the price is not gold, for I have no need of gold. The price is a set of tasks, tasks that no creature has ever been able to accomplish. Bring me three things that cannot be brought, and the stories are yours. Fail, and you are a fool who reached too high."}, {"id": "folk-stories-of-the-sky-god#s5", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Now the tasks the sky god set were these, and hear how impossible they were. First, Ijapa must bring the sky god a live python, a great serpent, captured and carried up alive. Second, he must bring a swarm of biting hornets, gathered and delivered without being stung to death. And third, in some tellings, he must bring the fiercest and most cunning of the great beasts, delivered helpless into the sky god’s hands. The sky god named these tasks laughing, certain that no one, least of all a tortoise, could ever perform them. The python would crush the catcher. The hornets would sting the gatherer to death. The great beast would devour the hunter. It was, the sky god thought, a safe and mocking bargain."}, {"id": "folk-stories-of-the-sky-god#s6", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "But the sky god had forgotten one thing, my children, the same thing the birds forgot in last night’s tale. He had underestimated the cunning of the weak. For Ijapa had no strength. But Ijapa had his wits, and this time, thank the ancestors, his wits were bent toward a good purpose, toward winning the stories for all of us. So watch now how the tortoise did the impossible, not with muscle but with mind."}, {"id": "folk-stories-of-the-sky-god#s7", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "To catch the python, Ijapa did not fight it. He would have died in a moment. Instead he walked back and forth near the great serpent’s home, muttering to himself, loudly, as if in an argument. He is longer than the pole. No, he is not. He is certainly longer than the pole. No, the pole is longer than he is. On and on Ijapa argued with himself about whether a certain long pole he carried was longer than the python or shorter. And the python, my children, being proud, as the great and strong so often are, could not bear to hear his length doubted. I am far longer than that pole, hissed the python. Ijapa pretended not to believe it. Prove it, he said. Stretch yourself out straight along the pole, and let me measure, for I am sure you are shorter. And the vain python stretched himself out full length along the pole to prove his greatness, and while he lay there stretched and still, Ijapa quickly bound him to the pole, round and round, until the great serpent was trussed and helpless. So the python was caught, not by strength, but by its own pride, played upon by a clever tongue."}, {"id": "folk-stories-of-the-sky-god#s8", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "To gather the hornets, again Ijapa used no force, for force was useless. He took a great gourd, and he filled it partly with water, and he came to where the hornets swarmed. And he poured some water over himself and some over the swarm, and then he called out to the hornets in a worried voice. Oh, my friends the hornets, the great rains are coming, the flood is upon us, look how the water falls already. You will all be drowned in the open air. Come, come quickly, take shelter here inside my dry gourd, and be saved from the flood. And the hornets, seeing the water and fearing the coming rain, flew gratefully into the shelter of the gourd, the whole swarm of them, to escape the flood that was only Ijapa’s trick. And when they were all inside, Ijapa stopped up the mouth of the gourd, and so the hornets were caught, not by strength, but by their own fear, played upon by a clever tongue."}, {"id": "folk-stories-of-the-sky-god#s9", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "And so it went with the third task also. By wit, by cunning, by playing upon the pride and the fear and the appetites of the strong, the weak little tortoise accomplished every impossible thing the sky god had set. And at last he carried them all up into the heavens and laid them before the astonished sky god, the trussed python, the trapped hornets, the humbled beast, all the things that could not be brought, brought."}, {"id": "folk-stories-of-the-sky-god#s10", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "The sky god looked upon these things, and he was amazed, and then he laughed a great laugh, but this time it was a laugh of respect, not of mockery. Tortoise, he said, I set you tasks that no creature of strength could ever perform, and you have performed them all, not with strength, which you do not have, but with cunning, which you have in great measure. You have shown that wisdom is greater than strength, that the clever mind can overcome what no muscle can. And since it is cunning and wisdom that make the best stories, it is right that you, the most cunning of all, should be their keeper. The stories are yours. Take them down to the earth. Let them be told among all peoples. And let them bear your name."}, {"id": "folk-stories-of-the-sky-god#s11", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "And so, my children, the stories came down to the earth. Ijapa carried them down from the sky, and scattered them among the peoples of the world, and from that day the evenings were silent no longer. From that day the children grew up hearing of the ancestors and the orisa and the kings and the tricksters. From that day the people had their mirror, and could see themselves in the tales, and could teach their young without ever seeming to teach, letting the story do the work that a scolding never could."}, {"id": "folk-stories-of-the-sky-god#s12", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "And in many lands, my children, to this very day, the folktales are called by the tortoise’s name, or the spider’s name, for in some places it was Anansi the spider who won them, but it is the same tale and the same truth. The stories belong to the clever weak one who won them from the sky, and through that one, they belong to all of us. So what is the lesson, my children? There are two, and both are precious."}, {"id": "folk-stories-of-the-sky-god#s13", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "The first is the lesson the sky god himself spoke: that wisdom is greater than strength. The python was mighty and the tortoise was weak, but the tortoise bound the python. The hornets were deadly and the tortoise was soft, but the tortoise caged the hornets. Again and again in this world, my children, you will face things stronger than you, and you will not be able to overcome them by force. Learn then from Ijapa to overcome them by wit, by patience, by understanding the pride and the fear of the strong and turning it against them. The mind is the weapon of the weak, and it is a mightier weapon than any spear."}, {"id": "folk-stories-of-the-sky-god#s14", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "The second lesson is quieter and deeper. It is that stories are precious, precious enough that a great god kept them locked in heaven, precious enough that the tortoise did the impossible to win them. Never think that a story is a small thing, a mere amusement for children. The stories are how a people remember who they are. They are the mirror and the memory and the teacher all at once. When Ijapa brought the stories down, he brought the earth a treasure greater than gold. And when I tell you these tales at this fire, and when you tell them one day to your own children, we are all keeping alive the gift that the tortoise won from the sky. Do not let it fall. Do not let the evenings go silent again."}, {"id": "folk-stories-of-the-sky-god#s15", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "Tomorrow, my children, we leave the tortoise for a while and hear a stranger and more haunting tale, of a hunter who went into the forest and married a woman who was not entirely a woman, but was, in her deepest nature, a river."}]}, {"id": "folk-hunter-married-a-river", "emoji": "📖", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#0a1a00,#1a3a00)", "category": "iwe", "rating": 9.6, "duration": "12 min", "title": {"en": "The Hunter Who Married a River", "yo": "The Hunter Who Married a River"}, "desc": {"en": "A hunter, a secret, and the woman who was a river.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Folktales / Literary", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Apagbe, the haunting tale of a hunter, a secret, and the woman who was a river)", "segments": [{"id": "folk-hunter-married-a-river#s1", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Draw close tonight, my children, and let your laughter grow quiet, for this is not a laughing tale like the tortoise stories. This is one of the old, deep, haunting tales, the kind that our people tell in a lower voice, the kind that leaves a coolness on the skin like the mist that rises from a river at dawn. It is the tale of a hunter who went too deep into the forest, and found a wife who was more than a wife, and lost her through the one thing that destroys more love than any other in this world: the failure to keep a promise, and the cruelty of a careless tongue."}, {"id": "folk-hunter-married-a-river#s2", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "There was once a hunter, a fine hunter, skilled and brave, who lived at the edge of a great forest. He was a solitary man, as hunters often are, for the forest teaches a kind of patient loneliness. Day after day he went deep among the trees, farther than other men dared, into the green places where the light falls in scattered coins and the great silence hums with unseen life. And it was in the deepest part of the forest, by a wide and quiet river, that he first saw her."}, {"id": "folk-hunter-married-a-river#s3", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "She was a woman of surpassing beauty, standing by the water in the early morning, and the hunter had never seen her like. Her skin held the sheen of water under moonlight. Her movements had the smoothness of a current. When she spoke, her voice had in it the low music that a river makes over stones. The hunter fell in love at once, deeply and completely, the way a solitary heart falls when it has waited long."}, {"id": "folk-hunter-married-a-river#s4", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "He courted her, and she was willing, but she came to him with one condition, spoken gravely by the water’s edge. I will be your wife, she said, and I will love you truly, and I will bring you good fortune such as you have never known. But you must swear to me one oath, and keep it all your life. You must never, never, reveal to anyone where I came from. You must never tell any living soul what I truly am. My origin is my secret, and it must remain forever between us alone. Swear it, and I am yours. Break it, and you lose me forever."}, {"id": "folk-hunter-married-a-river#s5", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "The hunter, drunk on love, swore the oath gladly. What did he care where she came from? He had her, and that was everything. He would guard her secret to his dying breath. And so they were married, and he brought her home to his compound at the edge of the forest. And oh, my children, how she blessed his life. From the day she came, everything the hunter touched prospered. His hunting had never been so rich, the game seeming almost to offer itself to his spear. His farm, which he now had reason to tend, flourished beyond all his neighbors’. His compound grew. Children came, strong and beautiful children with something of their mother’s water-sheen about them. Wealth flowed to him like a stream in the rainy season. Where he had been a lonely, struggling hunter, he became a man of substance, respected, envied, blessed in every corner of his life."}, {"id": "folk-hunter-married-a-river#s6", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "And all of it, my children, all of it flowed from her, from the mysterious woman of the river, whose secret he had sworn to keep. She was the source of his good fortune as surely as a river is the source of the life along its banks. Remember that, for it is the heart of the tale: she was his fountain, and a man should never poison his own fountain."}, {"id": "folk-hunter-married-a-river#s7", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "But prosperity, my children, does a strange and terrible thing to some men. It makes them forget the source of their blessing. It makes them proud. And it loosens the tongue, especially the tongue that has been warmed by palm wine. For the hunter, now a big man, took to drinking with his friends and his new admirers, and pride swelled in him season by season, and the old oath by the river began to feel to him like a small and distant thing, the whim of a woman, easily set aside among men."}, {"id": "folk-hunter-married-a-river#s8", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "And there was another poison at work, the oldest poison in any compound. For the hunter, being now wealthy, had taken other wives, as prosperous men did. And these other wives were jealous of the first wife, the river woman, whom the hunter still loved best and who was still, though they did not know it, the true source of everything they all enjoyed. The other wives whispered and probed. Who is she really? Where did she come from? She gives herself such airs. Why will he never speak of her family? There is something strange about her, something hidden. And their whispering worked upon the hunter’s wine-loosened pride."}, {"id": "folk-hunter-married-a-river#s9", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "Now the day came, as such days always come in these tales, the day of the broken oath. There was a quarrel in the compound, a bitter squabble among the wives, and the river woman, in the right of it, stood her ground against the others. And the other wives turned on the hunter and taunted him. See how she defies you. She thinks herself too good for us. Who is she, anyway? You have never even told us where this woman came from. Perhaps you do not know. Perhaps she is nobody, a stray thing you found in the bush."}, {"id": "folk-hunter-married-a-river#s10", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "And the hunter, his pride stung, his head hot with wine and anger, forgot the water, forgot the oath, forgot the source of all his blessing in a single reckless moment. He rose up in his fury and he shouted, so that the whole compound could hear, the thing he had sworn never to say. You dare to mock her origin? She is no nobody. She came from the river. She IS the river. I took her from the deep water of the forest, and she is a spirit of the river, and it is she who has given us everything we have. The words were out. Into the open air, before many witnesses, the secret was spoken. And in the silence that fell after his shout, my children, the hunter felt his heart turn to cold stone, for he knew, too late, what he had done."}, {"id": "folk-hunter-married-a-river#s11", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "The river woman grew very still. She looked at her husband, and in her eyes there was no anger, only a deep and ancient sorrow, the sorrow of water that must always, in the end, return to its source. You have broken your oath, she said quietly. You have told what you swore never to tell. I gave you my love, and my secret, and every blessing you have known, and I asked only this one thing of you, and you could not keep it. Now I must go. The water cannot stay where its secret has been spilled."}, {"id": "folk-hunter-married-a-river#s12", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "And she turned, and she walked out of the compound, and the hunter and all his household ran after her, wailing, the hunter begging, weeping, clutching at her, crying out that he was sorry, that it was the wine, that it was his pride, that he would give anything to unsay the words. But some words, my children, can never be unsaid. She walked on, through the compound, through the fields that her presence had made rich, into the trees, back toward the deep forest and the wide quiet river from which she had come."}, {"id": "folk-hunter-married-a-river#s13", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "And at the river’s edge, before his eyes, she changed. Her form flowed and softened and lost its human shape, and she became water, pure water, and poured herself down the bank and into the river, and was gone. Merged back into the current. Vanished into the source. And where there had been a woman, there was only the river, running on as it had run since the beginning of the world, indifferent now, closed, her secret returned to the deep."}, {"id": "folk-hunter-married-a-river#s14", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "And from that day, my children, everything the hunter had gained began to drain away, as water drains from a broken pot. The game vanished from his traps. His farms withered. His wealth melted season by season. The children of the river woman, some say, grew wan and strange and were drawn always toward the water. The compound that had been the envy of the district fell into poverty and silence. For the source had been offended and had withdrawn itself, and a man who poisons his own fountain must live with the dry ground that follows. The hunter lived out his days a broken and a mourning man, going often to the river’s edge, calling to the water, weeping, but the river never answered him again. It only ran on, cool and beautiful and forever closed to him, carrying his lost wife and his lost fortune and his lost happiness away toward the sea."}, {"id": "folk-hunter-married-a-river#s15", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "Now gather the lessons, my children, for this old tale is heavy with them. The first is about the keeping of a promise. The river woman asked one thing, only one, and it cost the hunter nothing to keep, only silence. Yet he could not keep it. How many blessings in this life, my children, ask of us only that we hold to a single promise, guard a single trust, keep a single silence? And how often does pride, or drink, or a moment’s anger, make us break the small promise and lose the great blessing? Guard your oaths. Guard especially the small oaths that cost you nothing, for those are the ones a careless heart most easily forgets, and their breaking can cost you everything."}, {"id": "folk-hunter-married-a-river#s16", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "The second lesson is about the tongue. The whole ruin came from words, spoken in anger, in wine, in wounded pride, that could never be taken back. Our people say that the tongue has no bone, and yet it can break what no bone can mend. The hunter’s spear killed game, but his tongue killed his own happiness. Learn to hold your tongue, my children, especially when the wine is in you and the pride is up and the others are taunting you. In that hot moment lies the power to destroy the very thing you most love."}, {"id": "folk-hunter-married-a-river#s17", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "And the third lesson is about the source. The hunter forgot where his blessing came from. Grown proud and comfortable, he treated the source of everything as a thing to be exposed and shouted about in a quarrel. Never forget your source, my children, whatever it may be, the parent, the mentor, the spouse, the community, the God who blesses you. Honor it. Protect it. Keep its trust. For the one who forgets and dishonors the source of their blessing may find, like the hunter at the empty river, that the blessing can flow away as silently as it came. Tomorrow, my children, we return to lighter ground, and to the tortoise once more, and to a certain magical drum that Ijapa found, a drum that would play a feast out of the air itself, and what his greed did with it."}]}, {"id": "folk-ijapa-drum", "emoji": "📖", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#0a1a00,#1a3a00)", "category": "iwe", "rating": 9.6, "duration": "10 min", "title": {"en": "Ijapa and the Drum That Played Itself", "yo": "Ijapa"}, "desc": {"en": "The magic drum, the tortoise's greed, and the second drum that answered it.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Folktales / Literary", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Apagbe, the tale of the magic drum, the tortoise’s greed, and the second drum that answered it)", "segments": [{"id": "folk-ijapa-drum#s1", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Come back to the fire, my children, and let us laugh again, for the tortoise is here once more, and where Ijapa goes, foolishness and greed and a hard lesson are never far behind. Tonight I will tell you of the drum that played a feast out of the empty air, and of what the tortoise’s greed made of such a gift. It is a tale about kindness rewarded and greed punished, and about how the same magic can bring you food or bring you beating, depending on what is in your heart when you hold it."}, {"id": "folk-ijapa-drum#s2", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "You remember that in the old tales there was often hunger on the land, and it was so again in this story. Ijapa the tortoise and his family were starving. The farms had failed, the rains had been cruel, and Ijapa, who loved his food above all earthly things, wandered far from home in his desperation, searching the bush for anything at all to fill the empty bellies of his household."}, {"id": "folk-ijapa-drum#s3", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "And in his wandering, my children, Ijapa came by chance to a place he had never seen, and there he met a spirit, or in some tellings a small strange being of the forest, who saw his hunger and his need. Now here, at the beginning, Ijapa was pitiable rather than wicked, only a hungry creature far from home, and the spirit took pity on him. Perhaps too, in the way of these tales, the spirit was testing him. The spirit gave to Ijapa a gift: a drum. But not an ordinary drum."}, {"id": "folk-ijapa-drum#s4", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "This is a magic drum, the spirit told him. When you are hungry, beat upon it, and food will appear, a whole feast, spread before you out of the air. It will feed you and your household and never fail you. But hear my warning, tortoise, and keep it well. This drum will serve you only so long as you use it with a modest heart. Take from it what you need, and share, and be content, and it will feed you always. But if ever you grow greedy, if ever you abuse the gift and demand too much and think only of yourself, the magic will turn. Do you understand? Use it modestly, and it blesses. Abuse it with greed, and it will curse. Ijapa, overjoyed, swore that he understood, that he would be modest, that he would be grateful and content. And he took the drum and hurried home."}, {"id": "folk-ijapa-drum#s5", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "At first, my children, all was well, and it was wonderful. Ijapa brought the drum into his hungry compound, and he beat upon it, and behold, out of the empty air appeared a feast. Pounded yam and rich soup, meat and fish and sweet things, spread out before his astonished family. They fell upon it and ate and were filled, for the first time in many months. And the next day Ijapa beat the drum again, and again the feast appeared, and again they ate their fill. The starvation was over. Ijapa’s family blessed him, and for a little while the tortoise was, if not modest, at least too happy and too full to be greedy. But you know Ijapa, my children. You know what lives in his heart. And you can feel already what is coming."}, {"id": "folk-ijapa-drum#s6", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "As the hunger faded and the fear of starving passed, the old greed woke in Ijapa and stretched itself and grew. It was no longer enough to feed his family. He began to think bigger. Why should he only feed his own household? Why not invite the whole village, and be praised as a great and generous host, and bask in everyone’s admiration? And so he called a great gathering, and before all the people he beat the magic drum, and the feast appeared, and the whole village ate, and marveled, and praised Ijapa to the skies. And the tortoise swelled with pride."}, {"id": "folk-ijapa-drum#s7", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "And now greed had him fully in its grip. He began to beat the drum more and more, not from hunger, for he was never hungry now, but from greed and from vanity, demanding feast after feast, more and grander, showing off, wasting, gorging, throwing food about, treating the modest gift as a bottomless well to be plundered for his own glory. He forgot the spirit’s warning entirely. Modesty was far from his heart. There was nothing in him now but appetite and pride. And so, my children, the magic turned, exactly as the spirit had warned that it would."}, {"id": "folk-ijapa-drum#s8", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "One day, in the midst of his greed, Ijapa beat upon the drum expecting another lavish feast, and no feast appeared. Instead, out of the drum there came a crowd of fierce beings, whips and cudgels in their hands, who fell upon Ijapa and beat him soundly. They thrashed him from one end of his compound to the other, a beating for every feast he had greedily demanded, a blow for every waste and every vanity. The drum that had fed him now flogged him, for he had abused the gift and turned its blessing into a curse by the greed of his own heart. And when the beating was done, the drum was silent and dead and would give him nothing more, not food, not anything. Its magic was spent, its gift withdrawn, exactly as he had been warned."}, {"id": "folk-ijapa-drum#s9", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Battered and hungry once more, my children, Ijapa should have learned. But this is Ijapa, and Ijapa’s lessons never quite take. His mind, ever cunning, ever greedy, began to work. He remembered where he had met the spirit. And he thought: the spirit gave me one drum. Perhaps if I go back, and pretend to be humble, I can get another, an even better one. And so, shameless as ever, Ijapa journeyed back to the place of the spirit, hoping to trick or beg his way into a second magic gift."}, {"id": "folk-ijapa-drum#s10", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Now in some tellings the spirit, knowing the tortoise’s heart, gave him exactly what his greed deserved. Ijapa asked for another drum, and the spirit gave him one, saying only, use it as you see fit. And Ijapa, greedy and impatient, did not even wait to get it home. He beat upon the new drum at once, hungrily, demanding a feast. And out of this second drum too came only the fierce beings with their whips, who beat him even more terribly than before. For the greedy heart draws punishment to itself. The tortoise had gone seeking a second helping of blessing, and because nothing in his heart had changed, he received a second helping of the beating instead."}, {"id": "folk-ijapa-drum#s11", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "And so Ijapa limped home, bruised in body and, for the moment at least, in pride, with no magic drum, no feast, and no one to blame but himself and the greed he would not master. He had been given a gift that could have fed his family forever, if only he had held it with a modest heart. And he had turned that endless blessing into a beating, twice over, by the plain and simple fault of wanting too much. Now the lessons, my children, for this tale is generous with them."}, {"id": "folk-ijapa-drum#s12", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "The first is the plainest: greed turns blessing into curse. The very same drum gave food to the modest and beatings to the greedy. The magic did not change. Ijapa changed, or rather, Ijapa let his worst nature take command. And so a gift that was pure blessing became, in greedy hands, a punishment. How many good things in this life, my children, food, money, power, love, are blessings when held modestly and curses when seized greedily? The gift is often the same. It is the heart that holds it that decides whether it feeds you or flogs you."}, {"id": "folk-ijapa-drum#s13", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "The second lesson is about contentment and its warning. The spirit’s rule was simple: take what you need, share, and be content. That is a recipe for a blessed life, my children, and it is written into the very magic of the drum. The one who takes what they need and is content will be fed forever. The one who is never content, who always wants more and more and grander and grander, will beat and beat upon the drum of their own life until at last it turns and beats them back."}, {"id": "folk-ijapa-drum#s14", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "And the third lesson is about learning, or the failure to learn. Ijapa was beaten once, and did he change his heart? No. He went straight back for more, unchanged, and was beaten again. It is not enough, my children, to suffer the consequence of a fault. You must let the suffering change the heart. The one who is punished and learns nothing, who goes back to the same greed hoping for a different result, will simply be punished again. Do not be twice-beaten like the tortoise. When life flogs you for your greed, do not go seeking a bigger drum. Go and mend your heart. Tomorrow, my children, we leave the tortoise and turn to a tale of deep sorrow turned to unexpected life, the tale of a girl whose tears were so many and so bitter that from them, watered by her endless weeping, there grew a whole forest."}]}, {"id": "folk-girl-forest-tears", "emoji": "📖", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#0a1a00,#1a3a00)", "category": "iwe", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "5 min", "title": {"en": "The Girl Who Grew a Forest from Tears", "yo": "The Girl Who Grew a Forest from Tears"}, "desc": {"en": "A grief so deep it became a forest.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Folktales / Literary", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Apagbe, the tale of a grief so deep it became a forest, and the life that sorrow can water)", "segments": [{"id": "folk-girl-forest-tears#s1", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Sit close and let the fire warm you, my children, for tonight’s tale is a tender one, a tale of sorrow, and of how even the deepest sorrow can, in the strange way of this world, become a kind of life. It is the story of a girl whose tears were so many and so bitter that from the wet ground where she wept, a whole forest grew."}, {"id": "folk-girl-forest-tears#s2", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "There was once a girl, gentle and good, who suffered a great loss. In some tellings it was her mother who died and left her alone in a hard household. In some it was a beloved she was torn from. In all of them the shape is the same: this girl was left with a grief too large for her small body to hold, a sorrow that had no bottom and no shore. And she was alone in it, for those around her did not share her tenderness, and told her to stop her weeping, to be silent, to swallow her pain and be useful."}, {"id": "folk-girl-forest-tears#s3", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "But the grief would not be swallowed. Every day the girl went to a certain place, a bare and empty patch of ground beyond the compound, and there she wept. She wept in the morning and she wept in the evening. She wept until, the old people say, her tears soaked deep into the dry earth, day after day, season after season, watering the bare ground with the salt water of her endless sorrow."}, {"id": "folk-girl-forest-tears#s4", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "And here, my children, is the strange and beautiful turning of the tale. For the bare ground where the girl wept did not stay bare. Watered by her tears, something began to grow there. First a small green shoot, then another, then many. The tears that seemed so useless, so wasted, so much a sign of weakness in the eyes of those who scolded her, were feeding the earth. And out of that grief-watered ground there rose, over time, a forest. A whole green forest, thick and living, where before there had been only dry and empty dust."}, {"id": "folk-girl-forest-tears#s5", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "And the forest was a place of life. Birds came to nest in it. Animals came to shelter in it. It gave fruit and shade and firewood and healing leaves. The very sorrow that the girl had been told to be ashamed of had become, without her ever intending it, a gift to her whole community, a living forest that fed and sheltered them all."}, {"id": "folk-girl-forest-tears#s6", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Now the tellings differ in how they end, as old tales do. In some, the girl is at last honored, her grief understood, the forest named for her, and she finds a measure of peace in seeing what her sorrow had made. In some, the forest becomes a sacred grove, and the girl a kind of spirit of it, watching over the green life her tears had raised. But in every telling the heart is the same: that her grief was not wasted, that her tears were not weakness, that out of the deepest sorrow there grew the deepest life."}, {"id": "folk-girl-forest-tears#s7", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "What does it teach us, my children? It teaches us, first, not to be ashamed of our sorrow, and not to shame the sorrow of others. Those around the girl told her to stop weeping, to swallow her grief, as if grief were a fault. But her grief was real, and it was the measure of her love, for we grieve most for what we have loved most. To tell a grieving heart to simply stop is a cruelty. The girl’s tears were not weakness. They were love with nowhere left to go."}, {"id": "folk-girl-forest-tears#s8", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "And it teaches us, second, the deep and hopeful mystery that sorrow can become life. Not always, and not quickly, and never in the way we plan. But the tears that seem so wasted may be watering a ground we cannot see. Grief carried honestly, wept fully, can soften the hard earth of a heart and make it able to grow things it never could have grown while it stayed dry and proud. Many of the most fruitful people you will ever meet, my children, are people whose forests grew from tears. Their kindness, their depth, their ability to shelter others, grew out of ground that was once watered by their own great sorrow. So do not fear your grief, my children, and do not despise it in others. Weep when you must weep. And trust that even the bitterest tears may, in a season you cannot yet see, raise up a forest that shelters many."}, {"id": "folk-girl-forest-tears#s9", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "Tomorrow, we go down to the water again, to the tale of a fisherman and the daughter of the sea-king, and a bargain made in the deep."}]}, {"id": "folk-fisherman-sea-king", "emoji": "📖", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#0a1a00,#1a3a00)", "category": "iwe", "rating": 9.6, "duration": "9 min", "title": {"en": "The Fisherman and the Sea-King’s Daughter", "yo": "The Fisherman and the Sea-King’s Daughter"}, "desc": {"en": "A bargain with the deep and the keeping and breaking of a sacred word.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Folktales / Literary", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Apagbe, the tale of a bargain with the deep, and the keeping and breaking of a sacred word)", "segments": [{"id": "folk-fisherman-sea-king#s1", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Come and sit by the fire, my children, and hear a tale of the deep water, for our people who lived by the lagoons and the sea told many tales of the beings who dwell beneath the waves, and of what happens to a mortal who makes a bargain with them. Tonight it is the tale of a poor fisherman, and the daughter of the king of the sea, and a promise made in desperation that would test his heart for the rest of his life. There was once a fisherman who was very poor. Day after day he cast his net into the water, and day after day he drew it up empty, or nearly so, a few small fish that hardly kept his family alive. He was a good man, hardworking and honest, but fortune had turned her face away from him, and he was sliding toward ruin and hunger."}, {"id": "folk-fisherman-sea-king#s2", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "One day, in his despair, the fisherman rowed out farther than he had ever gone, into the deep water where the older fishermen warned no one should go, for that was the domain of the sea-king, the great spirit who ruled the waters and all that lived in them. And there, in the deep, the fisherman cast his net one last time, praying to any power that would listen."}, {"id": "folk-fisherman-sea-king#s3", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "And the net came up heavy. So heavy he could barely draw it. His heart leapt, thinking of a great catch of fish that would save his family. But when he hauled the net over the side, it was not fish that lay in it. It was a woman, a woman of astonishing beauty, with hair like sea-foam and eyes like deep green water, and about her there hung the power and the strangeness of the deep. She was, she told him, a daughter of the sea-king himself, caught by accident in his net. The fisherman was terrified, for to harm or hold a child of the sea-king was to invite destruction upon himself and all his line. He made to release her at once, to lower her gently back into the water. But she stayed his hand."}, {"id": "folk-fisherman-sea-king#s4", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Wait, said the sea-king’s daughter. You have caught me, and by the law of the deep, that gives you a claim, if you are bold enough to press it. I will make you a bargain, fisherman. Take me for your wife, and I will bring you such fortune from the sea as you have never dreamed. Your nets will never again come up empty. Your family will prosper. But you must swear to me one thing and keep it faithfully. On certain days, the sacred days of the sea, I must return to my father’s kingdom beneath the waves. On those days you must let me go to the water alone, and you must never follow me, never spy upon me, never seek to see what I do in the deep. Grant me this freedom, keep this one trust, and I am yours, and fortune is yours. Break it, and I return to the sea forever."}, {"id": "folk-fisherman-sea-king#s5", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "The fisherman, amazed and grateful, swore the oath. And so he took the sea-king’s daughter for his wife, and brought her home. And everything she had promised came true. From that day his nets were always full, heavy with fish, the finest catches in all the village. His poverty melted away. His family grew fat and prosperous and happy. And his wife was good to him, loving and wise, and bore him fine children, and asked of him only that one thing: that on the sacred days she be allowed to go down to the water alone, unfollowed, unwatched, to visit her father’s kingdom and return. And for a long time the fisherman kept his word. On the sacred days his wife would go down to the shore alone, and vanish into the water, and return by evening, and he asked no questions, and all was well."}, {"id": "folk-fisherman-sea-king#s6", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "But you will guess, my children, for you have heard the tale of the hunter and the river woman, how such stories go. Over the years, the fisherman’s gratitude cooled, and in its place crept curiosity, and suspicion, and the small mean voice that asks: what is she really doing down there? Who does she see? What does she keep from me? And the neighbors, jealous of his fortune, fed the voice. She goes to the water alone, they whispered. What woman does that? What is she hiding? A man should know what his wife does. Are you not the master of your own house? And at last, on a certain sacred day, the fisherman’s suspicion overcame his oath. When his wife went down to the water, he crept after her, quietly, hiding among the rocks, to spy upon the secret he had sworn never to seek."}, {"id": "folk-fisherman-sea-king#s7", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "And he saw her. He saw her go to the water’s edge and shed her human form, and become a being of the deep, radiant and strange, and he saw the waters open, and the shapes of the sea-people rising to greet her, and the glory of the sea-king’s kingdom shining beneath the waves. For a moment he beheld the sacred thing he had promised never to look upon. And in that moment, she knew. She turned, and saw her husband hidden among the rocks, his eyes upon the forbidden mystery, his oath broken."}, {"id": "folk-fisherman-sea-king#s8", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "And the same deep sorrow came into her face that came into the river woman’s, the sorrow of the water that must return to its source. You have followed me, she said. You have looked upon what you swore never to see. You have broken the one trust I asked of you. I gave you my love and all my father’s fortune, and asked only for this one freedom, this one unwatched hour, and you could not grant it. Now I must go back to the sea, and I cannot return."}, {"id": "folk-fisherman-sea-king#s9", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "And she went down into the water, and the waves closed over her, and she was gone, back to her father’s kingdom in the deep, and the fisherman never saw her again. And with her went the fortune of the sea. His nets grew empty once more. His prosperity ebbed away like the tide going out. And he lived out his days a poorer and a lonelier man than before he ever rowed into the deep water, haunted by the shore where he had lost, through his own broken word, the wife and the fortune the sea had given him. Now you will see, my children, that this tale rhymes with the tale of the hunter and the river woman, and that is no accident, for our people told many versions of this deep truth, and told it often, because it is a truth we forget again and again. So let me name the lessons plainly."}, {"id": "folk-fisherman-sea-king#s10", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "A marriage, a friendship, any love, needs its unwatched hour. The sea-king’s daughter asked not for much, only for one freedom, one sacred space that was hers alone, one trust that she would not be followed and spied upon. And this the fisherman could not grant. Learn, my children, that to love someone is not to own every hour of them, not to pry into every secret, not to follow them into every deep. Love needs room to breathe. The one who must watch and control and know everything will, in the end, drive away the very one they cannot bear to lose."}, {"id": "folk-fisherman-sea-king#s11", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "And again, as with the hunter, the lesson of the kept and broken word. The fisherman swore an oath, and his fortune rested entirely upon his keeping it, and he broke it for nothing, for mere suspicion, mere curiosity, mere neighbors’ gossip. Guard the trust that your blessing rests upon, my children. And beware the whisperers, the jealous voices that urge you to break faith, to pry, to suspect. They do not have your good at heart. They fed the fisherman’s suspicion until it swallowed his oath and his fortune both. Tomorrow, my children, a lighter and a stranger tale: of the elephant, the greatest of beasts, and how it came to have the long memory that our people say it carries to this day."}]}, {"id": "folk-elephants-first-memory", "emoji": "📖", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#0a1a00,#1a3a00)", "category": "iwe", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "6 min", "title": {"en": "The Elephant’s First Memory", "yo": "The Elephant’s First Memory"}, "desc": {"en": "How the elephant came to carry the longest memory.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Folktales / Literary", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Apagbe, the tale of how the greatest of beasts came to carry the longest memory)", "segments": [{"id": "folk-elephants-first-memory#s1", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Gather round, my children, and let me tell you why the elephant, the great erin, the mightiest of all the animals of the bush, is said by our people to have the longest memory of any creature that walks the earth. For the elephant never forgets, the old ones say. An elephant remembers a kindness for a lifetime, and remembers a wrong for a lifetime, and remembers the paths and the water-holes of a country it walked as a calf even in its old age. And there is a tale of how this came to be."}, {"id": "folk-elephants-first-memory#s2", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "In the early days of the world, when the animals were still being given their natures and their gifts, the elephant was already the largest and the strongest of all the beasts. No lion could bring it down. No river could sweep it away. It walked through the forest like a moving hill, and all the other animals gave way before it. But being strong, my children, is not the same as being wise, and in those first days the elephant, for all its size, was a forgetful creature, as forgetful as any other beast, living only in the present moment, remembering nothing of yesterday."}, {"id": "folk-elephants-first-memory#s3", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "And because it forgot, the elephant suffered. It would forget where the sweet water lay, and go thirsty in a season of drought though it had drunk from a good spring only a moon before. It would forget which fruits had made it sick, and eat them again and sicken again. It would forget the animals who had helped it and the hunters who had hunted it, and so it could neither repay a kindness nor guard against a danger. For all its strength, the forgetful elephant stumbled through life making the same mistakes over and over, because it carried nothing forward from one day to the next."}, {"id": "folk-elephants-first-memory#s4", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "Now the tellings differ on how the change came, my children, as the old tales do, but the heart of it is this. There came a time of great trial, a terrible drought, when the rivers shrank and the water-holes dried and death stalked the parched land. And in that trial, the elephant’s forgetfulness nearly killed it, for it wandered in circles, unable to remember where any water still remained, growing weaker and weaker under the merciless sun."}, {"id": "folk-elephants-first-memory#s5", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "And in its extremity, the elephant received help. In some tellings it was a small creature, a mouse or a bird, that the elephant had once, almost by accident, spared or aided, and that now led the great beast to a hidden spring and saved its life. In some tellings it was a spirit of the bush, or Olodumare himself, who took pity on the suffering giant. But in all of them, the elephant was given, in that hour of trial, a great gift: the gift of memory. From this day, it was told, you shall remember. You shall carry the past with you. You shall forget nothing."}, {"id": "folk-elephants-first-memory#s6", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "And oh, my children, how that gift changed the elephant. Now it remembered where every spring and water-hole lay, across all the wide country, and never went thirsty again even in the hardest drought, for it could walk straight to water it had found seasons before. Now it remembered which plants healed and which harmed, and grew strong and long-lived. Now it remembered the small creature that had saved it, and repaid the kindness, and remembered the hunter who had wounded it, and kept far from that danger. The memory made the elephant not only strong but wise, and it became the elder of the animals, the one the others came to for knowledge of the old paths and the old ways, because the elephant alone carried the past."}, {"id": "folk-elephants-first-memory#s7", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "And from that day to this, my children, the elephant remembers. It remembers its dead, and is said to grieve over their bones. It remembers the paths of its ancestors. It remembers every kindness and every cruelty. The gift given in the hour of the great drought has never been taken back, and the erin walks the earth carrying the longest memory of any beast. Now what does this teach us, my children? It teaches us the great value of memory, and why our people prize it so. The forgetful elephant, for all its strength, stumbled and suffered and made the same mistakes forever. It was memory that made it wise. And so it is with a person, and with a people. Strength alone is not enough. It is memory that turns strength into wisdom, that lets us find the water we found before, avoid the poison that sickened us before, repay the kindness and guard against the harm."}, {"id": "folk-elephants-first-memory#s8", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "This is why, my children, we tell these stories at the fire. This is the elephant’s gift, given to a whole people. For a people who remember their past, who carry forward the lessons and the kindnesses and the warnings of those who came before, walk wisely and find water in the drought. But a people who forget, who let the stories die and the past slip away, are like the elephant of the early days, strong perhaps, but stumbling, making the same mistakes over and over, thirsty in a land where their ancestors always knew where the springs lay. Be like the elephant that was given memory, my children. Remember. Carry the past forward. It is the gift that turns strength into wisdom. Tomorrow, we lift our eyes to the sky, and hear why it is that the moon forever follows the sun across the heavens, and never quite catches him, and never quite gives up the chase."}]}, {"id": "folk-moon-follows-sun", "emoji": "📖", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#0a1a00,#1a3a00)", "category": "iwe", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "7 min", "title": {"en": "Why the Moon Follows the Sun", "yo": "Why the Moon Follows the Sun"}, "desc": {"en": "The endless chase across the sky and a quarrel that could not be mended.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Folktales / Literary", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Apagbe, the tale of the endless chase across the sky, and a quarrel that could not be mended)", "segments": [{"id": "folk-moon-follows-sun#s1", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Lift your eyes to the sky tonight, my children, and look at the moon riding there among the stars. Have you ever wondered why the sun and the moon are never in the sky together at their full strength? Why the sun rules the day and departs, and only then does the moon come out to rule the night? Why the moon seems always to be following the sun across the heavens, rising as he sets, chasing after him, but never, ever catching him? Our people have a tale for this, and like many of our tales it is a story of a quarrel, and of pride, and of a separation that could never be healed."}, {"id": "folk-moon-follows-sun#s2", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "In the beginning of the world, the old ones say, the sun and the moon were close companions. Some tellings say they were husband and wife, some say brother and sister, some say the dearest of friends. But all agree that once, long ago, the sun and the moon dwelt together in the sky and shone together, side by side, and the world had no separate day and night as we know them, only the shared golden light of the two of them together. And it was beautiful, my children, that first togetherness of the sun and the moon."}, {"id": "folk-moon-follows-sun#s3", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "But between them there arose a quarrel. The tellings differ on its cause, as the causes of quarrels are always disputed long after the quarrel itself. Some say the sun grew proud of his greater brightness and mocked the gentler moon, saying she was pale and weak beside his glory. Some say the moon was vain and cold and wounded the warm-hearted sun. Some say it was a matter of their children, the stars, and a dispute over them. But in every telling, harsh words were spoken between the sun and the moon, words of pride and anger, the kind of words that, once spoken, cannot easily be unspoken."}, {"id": "folk-moon-follows-sun#s4", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "And the quarrel grew bitter, and neither would yield, neither would be the first to soften, neither would say the small humble word that might have healed it. Pride held them both. And so at last they parted. The sun declared that he would no longer share the sky with the moon. And the moon, wounded and proud in her turn, declared that she would no longer dwell beside the sun."}, {"id": "folk-moon-follows-sun#s5", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "And so they were separated, and the world was divided into day and night. The sun took the day for his own, and rules it in his fierce bright glory, and while he reigns the moon must keep away. And the moon took the night, and rules it in her cool and gentle light, and comes out only when the sun has gone. Where once they shone together, now they are forever parted, the one ruling while the other is banished, taking turns at the sky they once had shared."}, {"id": "folk-moon-follows-sun#s6", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "But here, my children, is the tender heart of the tale. The separation did not kill the old love entirely. For look at the moon. Each evening, as the sun sinks down in the west, the moon rises in the east, following after him, chasing across the sky the companion she quarreled with and lost. All night she pursues the path he took, moving always toward the place where he went down, as if trying to catch him, to reach him, to mend the old quarrel at last. And sometimes, at dawn or at dusk, you may see them both in the sky at once for a little while, the pale moon and the risen or the setting sun, almost together, almost close, the moon having nearly caught the sun."}, {"id": "folk-moon-follows-sun#s7", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "But she never quite catches him. He is always just ahead, just gone, sinking as she rises, rising as she sinks. The chase goes on forever, night after night, age after age, the moon forever following the sun across the heavens, forever almost reaching him, forever failing. The quarrel is never mended. But the following never stops. Now the lessons, my children, and this gentle tale carries a heavy one."}, {"id": "folk-moon-follows-sun#s8", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "It teaches us, first, the terrible cost of a quarrel that pride will not let us mend. The sun and the moon loved each other. Their quarrel could have been healed by a single humble word from either one. But pride held them both, and neither would speak that word, and so they lost each other forever, and were divided for all time. How many people, my children, how many brothers and sisters, husbands and wives, dear friends, have parted this way over a quarrel that a little humility could have healed, and spent the rest of their lives, like the moon, chasing after a love that pride drove away? Do not let this be you. When you quarrel with one you love, be the one who speaks the humble word. Do not be so proud that you become like the sun and the moon, forever parted, forever chasing, never reunited."}, {"id": "folk-moon-follows-sun#s9", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "And it teaches us, second, something tender about love that outlasts a quarrel. For the moon still follows. The love did not die, even when the togetherness did. And there is a sad beauty in that, my children, but also a warning: how much better to have mended the quarrel and kept the companion, than to spend eternity following after them across a lonely sky. Let the moon’s endless chase remind you: mend your quarrels while you still can, while the loved one is still beside you in the sky, before pride divides you into separate days and nights, and leaves you only the following, and never again the being together. Tomorrow, my children, we come down from the sky to a humble little bird, the weaver, and learn from its patient building of its wonderful hanging nest a lesson about patience that the swift and the clever too often forget."}]}, {"id": "folk-weaver-bird", "emoji": "📖", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#0a1a00,#1a3a00)", "category": "iwe", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "7 min", "title": {"en": "The Weaver Bird’s Lesson in Patience", "yo": "The Weaver Bird’s Lesson in Patience"}, "desc": {"en": "The little bird whose patient work outlasted the boasts of the swift.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Folktales / Literary", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Apagbe, the tale of the little bird whose patient work outlasted the boasts of the swift)", "segments": [{"id": "folk-weaver-bird#s1", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Come close, my children, and look, if you can picture it, at the nest of the weaver bird, that little brown bird you have seen a thousand times, hanging its wonderful woven nest from the tip of a slender branch. Have you ever truly looked at that nest? It is a marvel. Knotted and woven strand by strand, blade of grass by blade of grass, hung out over the empty air where no snake can reach it, a little hanging house more cleverly made than many a thing that men build with their hands. And there is a tale of how the weaver bird came to build so well, and it is a tale about patience, that quiet virtue the swift and the clever so often despise."}, {"id": "folk-weaver-bird#s2", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "In the days when the animals and birds were learning their crafts, there was a great deal of boasting among them, as there always is among the gifted and the young. The birds especially were proud of their various gifts. The eagle boasted of his mighty wings and his height of flight. The swift boasted of his speed, faster than any other through the air. The bright birds boasted of their colours, the loud birds of their songs. And they all looked down upon the weaver bird, that small plain brown creature with no great speed, no bright feathers, no mighty wings, no wonderful song. What can you do? They mocked him. You are small and dull and slow. You have no gift at all."}, {"id": "folk-weaver-bird#s3", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "The weaver bird did not boast back, for he had nothing to boast of, and he knew it. But he had noticed something the others had not. He had noticed that when the great storms came, the fast birds and the bright birds and the strong birds all suffered, for their nests were rough and hasty things, a few sticks thrown together, easily torn apart by the wind and rain, easily robbed by snakes and other climbers. The proud birds, for all their gifts, built poorly, because they were too proud and too impatient to take pains over so humble a task as a nest."}, {"id": "folk-weaver-bird#s4", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "So the weaver bird set himself to learn to build well. And here, my children, is where his one true gift showed itself, a gift the others had overlooked because it makes no show and wins no admiration: the gift of patience. The weaver bird was patient. He did not try to build his nest all at once in a proud hurry. He gathered his materials carefully, one strand at a time. He learned to knot and to weave, and when a knot was wrong he undid it and did it again, and again, without complaint, without giving up, until it was right. Strand by strand, knot by knot, day by day, he wove."}, {"id": "folk-weaver-bird#s5", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "The other birds mocked him for it. Look at the foolish weaver, they laughed, fussing over his nest for days and days, tying and untying, when we finish ours in an hour. What a waste. What a slow and joyless little creature. And they flew off to show their speed and their colours, and left the patient weaver to his slow and humble work."}, {"id": "folk-weaver-bird#s6", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "But when the work was done, my children, oh, when the work was done. The weaver bird had made something that none of the proud, swift, clever birds could make: a nest of surpassing strength and beauty, tightly woven, snug and safe, hung out over the empty air on the thinnest of branches where no snake could climb and no thief could reach, so well made that the fiercest storm could not tear it, so cleverly knotted that it held together through wind and rain and season after season. His patient weaving had produced a marvel, while the hasty nests of the proud birds were rough scraps that fell apart at the first hard wind."}, {"id": "folk-weaver-bird#s7", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "And when the great storms came, the weaver bird’s little ones were safe and dry and snug in their strong hanging house, while the eggs and chicks of the swift and the eagle and the bright loud birds were tumbled out of their careless nests and lost to the wind and the rain and the climbing snakes. The patient builder had protected what mattered most, and the proud hasty builders had lost it."}, {"id": "folk-weaver-bird#s8", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "And from that day, my children, the weaver bird was mocked no longer. The other birds, who had laughed at his slow and patient labour, now looked with wonder at his marvelous nest, and some of them, the wiser ones, came humbly to learn from the little brown bird they had despised. For it turned out that his patience, the gift they had all overlooked, was worth more in the end than all their speed and colour and strength. And to this day the weaver bird builds its wonderful hanging nest, strand by patient strand, a marvel of the bush, a lesson hanging from every branch for anyone with the eyes to read it."}, {"id": "folk-weaver-bird#s9", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "Now the lesson is plain, my children, but it is one you will need to hear again and again all your lives, because the world is always shouting the opposite. The world admires the swift, the flashy, the quick, the ones who finish in an hour and fly off to show themselves. And the world overlooks the patient, the careful, the ones who take pains, who do a thing slowly and rightly, who undo the crooked knot and tie it again until it is true. The world calls such patience slowness, dullness, a waste of time."}, {"id": "folk-weaver-bird#s10", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "But hear the weaver bird, my children. It is patient, careful work that builds the things that last. The hasty nest falls in the first storm. The patiently woven one shelters the little ones through every storm for seasons on end. Whatever you would build in your life that you wish to last, a skill, a marriage, a home, a good name, a community, build it like the weaver bird: patiently, carefully, one true knot at a time, undoing your mistakes and doing them again until they are right, not caring that the swift and the flashy mock you for your slowness. When the storms come, and they always come, you will be glad you built like the weaver and not like the boastful birds whose careless work the wind tore away."}, {"id": "folk-weaver-bird#s11", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "Tomorrow, my children, we come to the last of these folktales, and to a beloved old figure, Baba Ologbon, the father of wisdom, and to the riddle season, when the old people would test the wits of the young with the deep and playful riddles of our people."}]}, {"id": "folk-baba-ologbon", "emoji": "📖", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#0a1a00,#1a3a00)", "category": "iwe", "rating": 9.6, "duration": "9 min", "title": {"en": "Baba Ologbon and the Riddle Season", "yo": "Baba Ologbon"}, "desc": {"en": "The father of wisdom and the season when the old tested the wits of the young.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Folktales / Literary", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Apagbe, the tale of the father of wisdom and the season when the old tested the wits of the young)", "segments": [{"id": "folk-baba-ologbon#s1", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Come close, my children, for this is the last of our folktales for now, and it is fitting that we end with a tale about the very thing we have been doing all these nights: the passing of wisdom from the old to the young. Tonight I will tell you of Baba Ologbon, whose name means the father of wisdom, and of the riddle season, that beloved time in the old villages when the elders would gather the children around the fire and test their wits with the deep and playful riddles of our people."}, {"id": "folk-baba-ologbon#s2", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "You must understand, my children, that among our people wisdom was never taught the dry and dusty way, with lectures and scoldings and long solemn speeches. Our ancestors were far too clever for that. They knew that a child’s mind is like a slippery fish: grab at it directly and it darts away, but move gently and patiently and you may catch it. And so they taught wisdom through stories, as I teach you, and through proverbs, those little pots of meaning our people love, and through riddles, the alo, the playful puzzles that sharpened a child’s mind while seeming only to play with it."}, {"id": "folk-baba-ologbon#s3", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "And there was a season for the riddles, in the old way of life. After the harvest was gathered, when the hard work of the farms was done and the evenings were long and cool, that was the season of tales and riddles. The whole compound would gather after the evening meal, and the elders would begin, and the children, tired but happy, would match their young wits against the accumulated cleverness of generations."}, {"id": "folk-baba-ologbon#s4", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "And in the village of this tale, the greatest of the elders at this game was Baba Ologbon, the father of wisdom. He was very old, so old that his beard was white as cotton and his skin was creased like the bark of an ancient tree. But his eyes, my children, his eyes were bright and young and dancing, for a mind kept sharp by a lifetime of thinking does not grow old the way the body does. And Baba Ologbon loved nothing in this world more than the riddle season, and loved nothing more than to sit among the children and match his ancient wit against their young and eager minds."}, {"id": "folk-baba-ologbon#s5", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Now you might think, my children, that an old man so wise would use the riddles to show off his own cleverness and to make the children feel foolish. But that was not Baba Ologbon’s way, and here is where the true wisdom of the father of wisdom showed itself. He did not use the riddles to defeat the children. He used them to teach the children to think."}, {"id": "folk-baba-ologbon#s6", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Hear how he did it. Baba Ologbon would pose a riddle, one of the deep old riddles of our people. He might say: I have a little house with no door and no window, and yet inside it there is a precious yellow treasure. What is my house? And the children would shout their guesses, wild and wrong and laughing, a pot, a box, a buried thing. And Baba Ologbon would not simply give the answer and shame their wrong guesses. He would guide them. He would say, think, my children. A house with no door and no window, that you cannot open, and yet something rich and yellow is inside. Where have you seen such a thing? In the bush? On a tree? And slowly, with his gentle guiding, he would lead them to see it themselves, until one bright child would cry out, an egg! It is an egg, Baba! The little house with no door, and the rich yellow yolk inside. And Baba Ologbon would laugh with delight, not because he had stumped them, but because they had found it, because he had led them to think their way to the answer."}, {"id": "folk-baba-ologbon#s7", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "And that, my children, was his secret. He did not want to win. He wanted them to learn how to think. The riddle was never really about the answer. It was about the thinking, the turning of the mind this way and that, the looking at a familiar thing, an egg, a shadow, a river, from a new and surprising angle. Baba Ologbon knew that a child who learns to think through a riddle learns to think through the harder puzzles that life will set before them later, the puzzles that have no easy answer and no kind elder to guide them."}, {"id": "folk-baba-ologbon#s8", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "And there is a tale told of Baba Ologbon’s last riddle season, when he was very old and knew his time was near. The children gathered as always, and he posed his riddles as always, and led them gently to the answers as always. And at the end of the night, one child, a thoughtful one, asked him: Baba, when you are gone, who will give us the riddles? Who will teach us to think?"}, {"id": "folk-baba-ologbon#s9", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "And the old man smiled his bright young smile and said: why, you will, my child. Do you not see? All these seasons I have not been filling your heads with answers, which you would forget. I have been teaching you how to find answers, which you will never forget. And one day you will sit where I sit, with white in your own beard, and you will pose these same riddles to the children of that day, and lead them gently to think as I have led you. The riddles do not die with me. They pass to you, as they passed to me from the old ones before. That is how wisdom lives forever among a people: not by being poured into one head, but by being passed from head to head, season after season, forever."}, {"id": "folk-baba-ologbon#s10", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "And so it was, my children. Baba Ologbon passed on, as all the old must. But the riddle season did not end. The children he had taught grew old in their turn, and gathered the children of their day, and posed the old riddles, and led young minds gently to think, exactly as the father of wisdom had led them. And so it has continued, from generation to generation, down all the long years, to this very fire where you sit tonight. Now the lesson, my children, is the deepest of all the lessons of these tales, for it is the lesson of the tales themselves."}, {"id": "folk-baba-ologbon#s11", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "Baba Ologbon teaches us that the highest kind of teaching does not fill the mind with answers, but teaches the mind to think. Answers can be forgotten, and answers can fail you when life sets you a puzzle no one has solved before. But a mind that has been taught to think, to turn a problem this way and that, to look at the familiar from a new angle, that mind can find its own way through any darkness. Seek teachers like Baba Ologbon, my children, who make you think rather than merely making you memorize. And when your turn comes to teach, teach that way yourself."}, {"id": "folk-baba-ologbon#s12", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "And he teaches us, finally, how a people’s wisdom lives forever. Not in one great head, however wise, for that head must one day grow still. But in the passing, the endless handing-on from the old to the young, season after season, fire after fire. This is why I tell you these tales, my children. Not so that you will simply know them, but so that one day you will tell them, to children not yet born, at fires not yet lit, and so keep the long chain of our people’s wisdom unbroken, as Baba Ologbon kept it, as the old ones before him kept it, as now I place it, tonight, into your keeping."}, {"id": "folk-baba-ologbon#s13", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "Guard it well. Pass it on. That is the whole meaning of the riddle season, and of the storyteller’s fire, and of everything the ancestors have given us. And so we close the tales, for now, my children, but the fire is not out, and the stories are not ended, for as long as you remember them and tell them again, they can never end at all."}]}, {"id": "folk-ajantala", "emoji": "📖", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#0a1a00,#1a3a00)", "category": "iwe", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "8 min", "title": {"en": "Ajantala: The Difficult Child Who Refused to Be Ordinary", "yo": "Ajantala"}, "desc": {"en": "The wonder-child born grown, and the trouble and truth of one who will not be tamed.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Folktales / Literary", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Apagbe, the tale of the wonder-child who was born grown, and the trouble and the truth of the one who will not be tamed)", "segments": [{"id": "folk-ajantala#s1", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Sit close, my children, and hold onto your laughter, for tonight I bring you Ajantala, and there is no stranger child in all our tales than he. Every people has its stories of the wonder-child, the child who is born already able to talk, already able to walk, already, alas, able to argue. Among our people that child is Ajantala, the terrible, marvelous, impossible child who refused from the very moment of his birth to be an ordinary baby, and whose story is both a comedy and a deep and troubling riddle about the ones among us who simply will not be tamed."}, {"id": "folk-ajantala#s2", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "They say that Ajantala was trouble before he was even properly born. Most babies, my children, come into the world small and helpless and squalling, knowing nothing, able to do nothing, utterly dependent on their mothers. Not Ajantala. Ajantala was born already grown in mind and in tongue, and from the very first moment he was a rebel and a marvel."}, {"id": "folk-ajantala#s3", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "When the women gathered to help at his birth, as our women do, tending to the mother and preparing to wash and swaddle the newborn, Ajantala would have none of it. He refused to be washed. He refused to be swaddled. He rejected the breast. And, most shocking of all, he spoke, this newborn thing spoke, in a full and grown voice, scolding the astonished women, telling them he needed none of their help, insulting them, ordering them about. Imagine the terror, my children, imagine the women fleeing the birth-hut in fright from a newborn baby who cursed at them like a quarrelsome old man."}, {"id": "folk-ajantala#s4", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "And so it went from there, worse and worse. Ajantala would not obey his mother. He would not obey his father. He would not obey any elder, any custom, any rule of the household or the village. Where an ordinary child learns by copying and by being corrected, Ajantala rejected all correction and all authority. He ate what he was told not to eat and refused what he was told to eat. He went where he was forbidden and refused to go where he was sent. He answered every instruction with an argument and every rebuke with a worse insult. He was, in the plainest terms, the impossible child, the child no one could manage, the child who broke every rule simply because it was a rule."}, {"id": "folk-ajantala#s5", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "The tellings pile up his outrages with a kind of horrified delight, my children, for there is something darkly funny in it. Ajantala fought with everyone. He caused chaos wherever he went. He was so difficult, so wild, so utterly ungovernable, that at last his own family could bear him no longer, and in one telling they carried him out and abandoned him in the forest, hoping to be rid of the trouble he brought. But even the forest could not tame Ajantala. He strode through it as its master, defeating the very spirits and beasts that terrified grown hunters, for he feared nothing and bowed to nothing, not even the powers of the deep bush."}, {"id": "folk-ajantala#s6", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "Now here, my children, is where the tale of Ajantala turns from mere comedy into something deeper, something our wise elders meant us to sit with rather than simply laugh at. For what are we to make of such a child? It would be easy to say only: here is the lesson of the bad child, be not like Ajantala, obey your elders. And that lesson is in the tale, truly. The child who respects no one, who breaks every bond of family and community, who insults those who would help him and rejects all correction, brings chaos and ends by being cast out, alone in the forest. There is a real warning there against pure rebellion, against the pride that will accept no guidance, against becoming so ungovernable that even those who love you can no longer keep you. A person who is all defiance and no belonging ends, like Ajantala, cast out and alone."}, {"id": "folk-ajantala#s7", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "But our elders were subtle, my children, and there is a second face to Ajantala, and the wise storytellers knew it. For notice what else is true of him. Ajantala is fearless. Ajantala cannot be intimidated. Ajantala walks into the deep forest that terrifies grown men and masters the very spirits. Ajantala refuses to be an ordinary, obedient, easily-led creature. And there is a strange power in that, a power the tale does not entirely condemn. For the one who will not be tamed is a trouble to every household, yes, but such a one is also sometimes the only one brave enough to face what everyone else flees, strong enough to break a rule that ought to be broken, stubborn enough to stand alone when standing alone is right."}, {"id": "folk-ajantala#s8", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "The riddle of Ajantala, my children, is the riddle of every strong-willed and difficult person you will ever know, perhaps the riddle of some of you sitting here tonight. The very fire that makes such a person impossible to manage is also the fire that makes them impossible to conquer. The stubbornness that breaks the good rule is the same stubbornness that could break a bad one. The fearlessness that terrifies the birth-women is the same fearlessness that masters the forest. So what did the old ones want us to take from Ajantala? Not a simple thing, my children, for it is not a simple tale. They wanted us to laugh, first, at the sheer outrageous comedy of the impossible child, for laughter too is wisdom. But then they wanted us to think."}, {"id": "folk-ajantala#s9", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "To the wild and difficult ones among us, Ajantala is a warning: that fearlessness and strong will, if they are joined to no respect, no love, no belonging, no willingness ever to yield or to learn, lead only to chaos and to the lonely forest of the cast-out. Strength without wisdom, courage without kindness, will without any bond to others, these do not make a hero. They make an Ajantala, marveled at, feared, and finally abandoned."}, {"id": "folk-ajantala#s10", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "But to those who would deal with the difficult ones, the tale offers a subtler thought. Do not imagine that the strong-willed child is simply bad, to be broken or cast out. That fire is dangerous, yes, but it is also precious, and the task, the hard task, is not to extinguish it but to give it something worthy to burn for. The fearlessness that masters the forest could defend a whole people, if only it could be joined to love. The stubbornness that breaks every rule could stand against a tyrant, if only it could be turned toward justice."}, {"id": "folk-ajantala#s11", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So when you meet an Ajantala, my children, and you will, do not simply wish them tamed into an ordinary obedient creature, for the world needs some of its fearless and unmanageable ones. But pray that their great fire finds a great and good purpose, and is joined to love and wisdom, lest it consume them and everyone around them and leave them at last alone in the forest, mastering the spirits, and loved by no one. Tomorrow, my children, a gentler and a spookier tale: of a certain gourd that would sing in the night, and the greed and the wonder it stirred in the people who heard it."}]}, {"id": "folk-gourd-sang-at-night", "emoji": "📖", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#0a1a00,#1a3a00)", "category": "iwe", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "9 min", "title": {"en": "The Gourd That Sang at Night", "yo": "The Gourd That Sang at Night"}, "desc": {"en": "The singing gourd and the difference between the one who listens and the one who grabs.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Folktales / Literary", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Apagbe, the tale of the singing gourd, and the difference between the one who listens and the one who grabs)", "segments": [{"id": "folk-gourd-sang-at-night#s1", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Gather close, my children, and let the night grow quiet, for this is a tale to be told in a low voice, a tale of a strange and lovely magic and of two very different hearts that met it. It is the story of a gourd that sang in the night, and of the poor kind man who found it, and the greedy man who tried to steal its magic, and how the same wonder brought blessing to the one and ruin to the other."}, {"id": "folk-gourd-sang-at-night#s2", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "There was once a poor man, gentle and good, who had very little in this world but his kindness. He lived at the edge of the village, and he was the sort of man who shared his last morsel with a hungry stranger, who spoke softly to children and to old people, who never passed a suffering creature without stopping to help. He was poor in goods, my children, but rich in heart, and though the world had given him little, he had never let it make him hard."}, {"id": "folk-gourd-sang-at-night#s3", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "One evening, walking home through the dusk, this poor man heard something that stopped him still on the path. Singing. A soft, sweet, unearthly singing, coming from somewhere near the ground at the forest’s edge. He followed the sound, gently, quietly, and found that it came from a gourd, an ordinary-looking calabash lying among the leaves, and yet from within it came this music of surpassing beauty, a song like nothing he had ever heard, a song that seemed to hold all the sweetness and all the sorrow of the world."}, {"id": "folk-gourd-sang-at-night#s4", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Now mark how the kind man behaved, my children, for it is the whole heart of the tale. He did not grab the gourd. He did not seize it and think at once how to profit from it. He knelt down beside it, softly, and he listened. He listened with wonder and with respect to the beautiful singing, and when at last he spoke, he spoke gently to the gourd, thanking it for its lovely song, asking humbly if he might take it home to hear its music, promising to treat it with care and kindness."}, {"id": "folk-gourd-sang-at-night#s5", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "And the gourd, it seemed, judged his heart and found it good. It let him take it up gently in his hands, and it sang for him sweetly all the way home, and in his poor little house it became his treasure and his companion. And here the tellings agree that the gourd blessed the kind man. In some, its singing drew people from all around who paid to hear the wonder, and so the poor man prospered. In some, the gourd granted him wishes, or poured out riches, or brought him great good fortune. But in all of them, the kind man, because he had approached the magic with respect and gentleness and gratitude, was richly blessed by it. His kindness had prepared him to receive the wonder rightly, and so the wonder became a blessing in his hands."}, {"id": "folk-gourd-sang-at-night#s6", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "But of course, my children, word of the poor man’s sudden fortune spread through the village, as such word always does. And in the village there lived another man, a greedy man, hard and grasping, who heard of the magic singing gourd and was consumed at once with envy. Why should that soft foolish pauper have such a treasure? He thought. I will have a singing gourd too, and a better one, and I will grow far richer than he. And the greedy man went out to the forest’s edge to find himself a gourd of magic."}, {"id": "folk-gourd-sang-at-night#s7", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "Now the tellings differ in the details, but the shape is always the same. Either the greedy man went and rudely demanded that the kind man tell him where the gourd was found, and then rushed to that place, or he simply searched until he too heard, one evening, the sweet singing coming from a gourd among the leaves. And there, my children, the difference between the two hearts showed itself with terrible clarity."}, {"id": "folk-gourd-sang-at-night#s8", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "For where the kind man had knelt and listened and spoken gently and asked humbly, the greedy man did the opposite in every way. He did not kneel. He did not listen. He did not wonder or give thanks or ask. He heard the singing, and his only thought was: mine. He lunged at the gourd and snatched it up roughly, greedily, already counting the riches it would bring him, already boasting in his mind of how he would outshine the kind man. He grabbed the magic as a thief grabs, with no respect and no gratitude, thinking only of what he could get."}, {"id": "folk-gourd-sang-at-night#s9", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "And the magic, my children, judged his heart too, and found it wanting. In his rough grasping hands the gourd’s sweet song changed. In some tellings it fell silent and would sing no more for him, mock him as he might. But in most tellings, and these are the ones the children love to shiver at, the gourd turned on him. Its sweet singing became a torrent of abuse, or it summoned a swarm of stinging things, or it poured out not riches but filth and misfortune, or it grew and swallowed his wealth, or it brought down calamity upon his greedy house. The very magic that had blessed the kind man cursed the greedy one, because he had grabbed at it with a grabbing heart."}, {"id": "folk-gourd-sang-at-night#s10", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "And so the greedy man, who had gone out to seize a fortune, came home with ruin, beaten or shamed or impoverished by the very wonder he had tried to steal. And the kind man kept his blessing, and the village had a new tale to tell at the fire about the singing gourd, and about the two men, and about why the same magic had blessed the one and cursed the other. Now the lesson, my children, and it is one of the deepest and truest our people know. The gourd was the same gourd. The magic was the same magic. What differed was the heart that met it, and the manner of the meeting."}, {"id": "folk-gourd-sang-at-night#s11", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "The kind man approached the wonder with respect, with patience, with gratitude, with a listening heart. He knelt. He listened. He asked. He gave thanks. And the wonder blessed him. The greedy man approached the very same wonder with greed, with haste, with disrespect, with a grabbing heart. He lunged. He snatched. He demanded. He gave no thanks. And the very same wonder cursed him."}, {"id": "folk-gourd-sang-at-night#s12", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "This is a law of life, my children, as true as any law of the harvest. The blessings of this world, the good fortunes, the gifts, the wonders, they come to us all, but they become blessing or curse in our hands according to the heart with which we receive them. The one who receives good fortune with gratitude and humility and respect is blessed by it. The one who grabs at it with greed and pride and entitlement is often cursed by the very same thing. Two people may receive the identical gift, and to one it is a joy and to the other a ruin, and the whole difference is in the heart."}, {"id": "folk-gourd-sang-at-night#s13", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So when a wonder comes to you, my children, and wonders will come, do not grab. Kneel. Listen. Give thanks. Receive it as the kind man received the singing gourd, gently and gratefully, and it will sing sweetly in your house for all your days. Grab at it like the greedy man, and do not be surprised if the sweetest song turns to stinging in your hands. Tomorrow, my children, a lighter tale, and a colourful one: how the chameleon, who once was as plain as any other lizard, came to wear all the colours of the world and to change them as he pleases."}]}, {"id": "folk-chameleon-colours", "emoji": "📖", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#0a1a00,#1a3a00)", "category": "iwe", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "7 min", "title": {"en": "How the Chameleon Got Its Colours", "yo": "How the Chameleon Got Its Colours"}, "desc": {"en": "How the wise, slow chameleon received its many colours.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Folktales / Literary", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Apagbe, the tale of the wise slow lizard and the gift of many colours)", "segments": [{"id": "folk-chameleon-colours#s1", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Come and sit, my children, and let us have a lighter and a brighter tale tonight, for we have had some heavy ones. Look, if you can find one, at the chameleon, that strange slow lizard that walks so carefully along the branches, rocking back and forth with each deliberate step, its odd eyes swiveling this way and that, and its skin changing colour, now green, now brown, now almost the very colour of whatever it sits upon. How did the chameleon come to have this gift of many colours, when its cousins the common lizards are stuck with the one plain skin they were born in? Our people have a tale."}, {"id": "folk-chameleon-colours#s2", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "In the early days, when the creatures were being given their natures, the chameleon was a plain and unremarkable little lizard, no more colourful than any other. But the chameleon had one quality that set it apart, and it was not a flashy quality, for the chameleon was, and is, the slowest and most careful of creatures. Where other lizards darted and scrambled and rushed about, the chameleon moved with enormous, deliberate slowness, placing each foot with care, looking carefully in every direction before it moved, never hasty, never rash, thinking, it seemed, before every single step. The other animals mocked the chameleon for this. Look at the ridiculous slow thing, they laughed, taking an hour to cross a branch that we would cross in a heartbeat. What a timid, foolish, dawdling creature. And they rushed on past it, quick and proud, leaving the chameleon to its patient, careful, endless creeping."}, {"id": "folk-chameleon-colours#s3", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "But the chameleon’s slowness, my children, was not foolishness. It was wisdom. For the chameleon watched, and the chameleon thought, and the chameleon understood things that the quick and heedless creatures rushed straight past. And there came a day when this careful wisdom was tested and proved."}, {"id": "folk-chameleon-colours#s4", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "The tellings vary, as they do, but the heart is this. There came some great challenge or contest or trial set by Olodumare or by a great spirit, and all the swift proud animals rushed at it heedlessly and failed, precisely because they were too hasty, because they did not stop to look and think and understand. And the chameleon, coming slowly along behind them all, taking its careful time, observing everything, thinking through the problem step by patient step, succeeded where all the swift ones had failed. In one beloved telling there was a race, and it was not the swift who won it but the chameleon, who by a clever and patient trick, or simply by steady care while the swift ones grew careless, came through in the end. The slow, careful, thinking creature triumphed over all the quick and heedless ones."}, {"id": "folk-chameleon-colours#s5", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "And the great power who set the trial, whether Olodumare or a spirit of the bush, was so pleased with the chameleon, so delighted that a creature had shown the rare wisdom of care and patience and thought, that he gave the chameleon a wonderful gift. Because you alone did not rush, because you alone looked carefully and thought before you acted and adapted yourself wisely to what you saw, said the great power, I give you a gift to match your nature. I give you the colours of the world. From this day you shall be able to change your skin to any colour you see, to blend with any place you find yourself, to adapt your very body to your surroundings as your wise mind adapts to every situation. Let your changing colours be the mark and the reward of your careful, adaptable wisdom."}, {"id": "folk-chameleon-colours#s6", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "And so the plain little lizard became the chameleon we know, wearing all the colours of the world, changing them at will, blending with the leaf and the branch and the bark. And it kept, too, its careful slowness, its swiveling watchful eyes, its habit of looking in every direction and thinking before each step. For the colours were given precisely because of that careful nature, and the two go together to this day. Now the lesson, my children. The world, as I have told you before and will tell you again, admires the swift and mocks the slow. The quick lizards laughed at the careful chameleon. But it was the chameleon’s very slowness, its care, its habit of watching and thinking before acting, that won it the greatest gift of all."}, {"id": "folk-chameleon-colours#s7", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "There is a deep wisdom here about the value of care and patience and observation. The one who rushes heedlessly through life, who acts before thinking, who never stops to look carefully in every direction, will stumble into a hundred pits that the careful, watchful person steps around. And there is a second wisdom here too, about adaptability, for that is the chameleon’s special gift. The chameleon can change to match wherever it finds itself, and this too is a kind of wisdom, the wisdom to adapt, to fit yourself thoughtfully to your circumstances, to be flexible rather than rigid. The creature that could adapt was the creature that was given the power to adapt even its own skin."}, {"id": "folk-chameleon-colours#s8", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So learn from the chameleon, my children. Do not be ashamed to be careful, to be slow, to look in every direction and think before you step, even when the quick and heedless ones mock you for it. And learn to adapt, to change wisely with your changing circumstances, to blend and to fit yourself to where you are, rather than crashing rigidly through the world. The careful, watchful, adaptable creature was given the colours of the whole world. The swift and heedless ones stayed as plain as they began. Be a chameleon. Tomorrow, my children, a tale of a great and noisy quarrel between two mighty forces of nature, the river and the rain, each claiming to be the greater, and what their argument teaches us about pride and about how things truly depend upon each other."}]}, {"id": "folk-river-argued-rain", "emoji": "📖", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#0a1a00,#1a3a00)", "category": "iwe", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "8 min", "title": {"en": "The River That Argued with the Rain", "yo": "The River That Argued with the Rain"}, "desc": {"en": "The proud quarrel between the water above and the water below.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Folktales / Literary", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Apagbe, the tale of the proud quarrel between the water above and the water below)", "segments": [{"id": "folk-river-argued-rain#s1", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Listen, my children, to the sound of the rain on the roof, and think of the river running down to the sea, and hear tonight a tale of the great and foolish quarrel that once broke out between these two, the rain and the river, when each grew proud and each claimed to be the greater, and neither would remember that they were, in truth, the very same water."}, {"id": "folk-river-argued-rain#s2", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "In a certain season, the tellings say, the river grew very great and very proud. The rains had been heavy, and the river had swelled until it was mighty, overflowing its banks, rushing along in a broad brown flood, carrying away everything in its path. And the river, drunk on its own power, began to boast. Behold my greatness, roared the river. See how I flood the fields and feed the crops. See how the people and the animals come to me to drink, how the fish live in me, how the canoes travel upon me, how the whole land depends upon my waters. I am the giver of life to this country. I am the greatest of all. Without me, everything would wither and die."}, {"id": "folk-river-argued-rain#s3", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Now the rain, hearing this boast from the clouds above, was offended, and answered proudly in its turn. You, greatest? Said the rain. You foolish, boasting river? And where, pray, do you imagine your waters come from? Look up, proud river. It is I, the rain, who fill you. When I fall, you swell and boast. When I withhold myself, you shrink to a muddy trickle and then to cracked mud and then to nothing at all. It is I who give life to the land, I who fill the river and the well and the leaf and the root. You are nothing but the water I have already given. I am the greater. I am the source."}, {"id": "folk-river-argued-rain#s4", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "And so the quarrel was joined, my children, a great and noisy quarrel between the river below and the rain above, each boasting, each claiming to be the true giver of life, each insisting it was the greater and the other merely a lesser thing that depended upon it. The river pointed to all the life along its banks. The rain pointed to the clouds and said, but I fill you. The river said, but the people come to me, not to you. The rain said, but I come to them everywhere, on every field, while you stay in your one channel. Back and forth the proud argument raged, water above and water below, each too proud to yield."}, {"id": "folk-river-argued-rain#s5", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "Now the tellings end this quarrel in more than one way, my children, but they all arrive at the same truth. In some, a wise elder, or Olodumare himself, or an old tortoise passing by, hears the ridiculous argument and laughs, and stops to teach the two proud waters a lesson. In some, the quarrel simply plays itself out until the truth becomes plain to both. But the truth they all arrive at is this, and it is a truth so simple that only the proud could fail to see it: the rain and the river are the same water."}, {"id": "folk-river-argued-rain#s6", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "For where does the rain come from, my children? The rain comes from the clouds. And where do the clouds come from? The clouds rise up from the water of the rivers and the lakes and the sea, drawn up into the sky by the sun as a mist, gathering into clouds, until they fall again as rain. And the rain runs down into the rivers, and the rivers run down to the sea, and the sea rises again as mist into the clouds, and falls again as rain, round and round, forever. The rain becomes the river. The river becomes the cloud. The cloud becomes the rain. They are not two rivals. They are one water, moving through its endless circle, taking now the form of rain, now the form of river, now the form of cloud and mist and sea."}, {"id": "folk-river-argued-rain#s7", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "When the proud rain and the proud river at last understood this, my children, their quarrel died in their mouths, and they were ashamed. For each had been boasting against the other as though they were enemies and rivals, when in truth they were the same thing, utterly dependent on each other, each becoming the other in the great circle of the water. The river could not boast of feeding the land without the rain that filled it. The rain could not boast of filling the land without the rivers and seas that rose to become it. Neither was the greater. Neither was the source. They were partners in one endless dance, and their quarrel had been as foolish as a right hand quarreling with a left, or a foot boasting against the leg it is joined to."}, {"id": "folk-river-argued-rain#s8", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "Now the lesson, my children, is one our people never tire of teaching, because human beings never tire of forgetting it. The rain and the river quarreled over which was the greater, each claiming to be the true source of life, when the truth was that they depended upon each other completely and were, in the deepest sense, one thing."}, {"id": "folk-river-argued-rain#s9", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "How often, my children, do people quarrel exactly this way? The farmer boasts that he feeds the nation, and the trader boasts that without her the farmer’s yams would rot unsold, and the two despise each other, when in truth each depends utterly on the other and neither could feed anyone alone. The husband and the wife quarrel over whose work holds up the household, when the household stands only because of both. The old boast against the young and the young against the old, when each becomes the other in the circle of the years, the young growing old, the old having once been young. Everywhere, my children, proud people quarrel over who is the greater, who is the true source, when the truth is almost always that they need each other, that they are joined in one circle, that neither could stand alone."}, {"id": "folk-river-argued-rain#s10", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "When you feel that quarrel rising in you, my children, the proud urge to boast that you are the greater, that you are the source, that the other merely depends on you, remember the rain and the river. Remember that you are almost certainly more dependent on the other than your pride wishes to admit, and that you are, more than you know, the same water. Then let the foolish boast die in your mouth, as it died in the mouths of the rain and the river, and honor the one you depend on, who also depends on you. Tomorrow, my children, a small and stubborn tale, the tale of a single seed that refused to give up, and what its stubbornness, unlike Ajantala’s, was able to build."}]}, {"id": "folk-stubborn-seed", "emoji": "📖", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#0a1a00,#1a3a00)", "category": "iwe", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "7 min", "title": {"en": "The Stubborn Seed", "yo": "The Stubborn Seed"}, "desc": {"en": "The small seed that would not give up and the great tree it became.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Folktales / Literary", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Apagbe, the tale of the small seed that would not give up, and the great tree it became)", "segments": [{"id": "folk-stubborn-seed#s1", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Gather close, my children, for tonight’s tale is small, as small as a seed, and as great as the tree a seed becomes. It is the story of a single stubborn seed, and it teaches the difference between the wrong kind of stubbornness, the kind we saw in Ajantala, and the right kind, the kind that changes the world."}, {"id": "folk-stubborn-seed#s2", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "There was once a tiny seed, one among many, that fell to the ground at the edge of a field. And it was a hard place where this seed fell, my children, a poor place. The ground was dry and stony. There was little water. The sun beat down mercilessly. The other seeds that fell nearby looked at that hard ground and, in the way of tales, gave up before they had even begun. Why should we struggle here? They said. This ground is too hard. There is not enough water. The sun is too fierce. We will never grow. And they lay in the dry earth and did nothing, and came to nothing."}, {"id": "folk-stubborn-seed#s3", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "But the stubborn seed was different. The stubborn seed looked at the same hard ground, the same little water, the same fierce sun, and it made a different choice. I will grow, said the stubborn seed. It is hard here, yes. But I will grow anyway. I will send my root down as far as it must go to find water. I will push my shoot up however difficult it is. I will not give up."}, {"id": "folk-stubborn-seed#s4", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "And oh, my children, it was hard. It was so hard. The stubborn seed sent down a tiny root, and struck stone, and had to turn and go around, and struck more stone, and turned again, pushing, straining, reaching down and down through the difficult earth in search of water. Above, it pushed up a tiny fragile shoot, and the sun scorched it, and the dry wind battered it, and still it pushed, a little higher, a little higher. Every day was a struggle. Every finger’s width of growth was won against resistance. There was no ease for the stubborn seed, no soft rich earth, no gentle rain, only the daily hard labour of growing where growing was almost impossible."}, {"id": "folk-stubborn-seed#s5", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Many times, the old ones say, the stubborn seed might have given up, as its neighbors had. The struggle was so long and so hard, and the results so slow. But each day it made again the same choice: not yet. I will not give up. I will grow a little more. And a little more. And a little more."}, {"id": "folk-stubborn-seed#s6", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "And the seasons passed, my children. And the root that had struggled so hard to go down deep found, at last, deep beneath the stony surface, a hidden vein of water, and drank, and was strong. And the shoot that had pushed so hard against the scorching sun grew into a stem, and then into a sapling, and then, over the long years, into a tree. A great tree. A mighty tree, with deep roots that no drought could kill, for they reached the deep water, and a broad crown that gave shade over all that hard ground where nothing else had grown."}, {"id": "folk-stubborn-seed#s7", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "And here is the wonder, my children. Because the stubborn seed had grown in such a hard place, and had struggled so, its roots went deeper than the roots of any tree that grew in soft and easy ground. The very hardship that might have killed it had, because it refused to give up, made it stronger than it could ever have become in comfort. The deep struggle made deep roots. And in time, birds nested in its branches, and travelers rested in its shade, and its seeds scattered and grew, and the hard stony place that had been barren became, because of one stubborn seed, a place of green life. And where were the seeds that had given up? They had come to nothing, my children, and were long forgotten, dust in the dry ground. Only the stubborn one, the one that refused to quit, became the great tree that changed the whole place around it."}, {"id": "folk-stubborn-seed#s8", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Now the lesson is plain, my children, but plain things are sometimes the ones we most need to hear. The stubborn seed teaches us the power of not giving up. It fell in a hard place, harder than most of us will know, and by the simple, daily, stubborn choice to grow a little more and not to quit, it became a mighty tree where others became dust."}, {"id": "folk-stubborn-seed#s9", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "And notice, my children, the deep thing the tale says about hardship. We think hardship is only our enemy. But the stubborn seed’s roots went deep precisely because the ground was hard. Had it grown in soft easy earth, its roots would have stayed shallow, and the first great drought would have killed it. It was the struggle itself, faced and not fled, that made it strong enough to endure. So too with a person, my children. The one who struggles hard in a hard place, and does not give up, may grow deeper roots than the one who had everything easy, and may stand through storms that topple the comfortable."}, {"id": "folk-stubborn-seed#s10", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "Now remember, I told you when we spoke of Ajantala that stubbornness can be a curse, the stubbornness that respects nothing and breaks every good bond. But here is stubbornness as a blessing, and the difference is everything. Ajantala was stubborn against others, against love, against all bond and belonging, and it made him a lonely ruin. The seed was stubborn against hardship, against despair, against giving up, and it made it a great and sheltering tree. Be stubborn like the seed, my children, not like Ajantala. Set your stubbornness not against the people who love you, but against the hard ground and the fierce sun and the whispering voice that says give up. Refuse to quit. Send your roots down deep. And in time, where others became dust, you may become a tree in whose shade many will rest."}, {"id": "folk-stubborn-seed#s11", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "Tomorrow, my children, a tale of the mighty lion, the king of beasts, and the hard and humbling day he learned that even a king must sometimes do the one thing his pride hated most: ask for help."}]}, {"id": "folk-lion-learned-to-ask", "emoji": "📖", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#0a1a00,#1a3a00)", "category": "iwe", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "8 min", "title": {"en": "The Lion That Learned to Ask", "yo": "The Lion That Learned to Ask"}, "desc": {"en": "The proud king of beasts learns the humbling wisdom of asking for help.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Folktales / Literary", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Apagbe, the tale of the proud king of beasts and the humbling wisdom of asking for help)", "segments": [{"id": "folk-lion-learned-to-ask#s1", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Come close, my children, for tonight the king of beasts himself, the mighty lion, the kiniun, comes to our fire, and he comes to be humbled, and to learn the one lesson his great pride had never let him learn: that even the strongest sometimes must ask for help, and that there is no shame in it, but wisdom."}, {"id": "folk-lion-learned-to-ask#s2", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "The lion, my children, was the king of the animals, and he knew it, and he never for one moment let anyone forget it. He was mighty. His roar shook the forest. His strength was greater than any other beast’s. And because he was so strong, the lion had come to believe that he needed no one. He looked down upon all the other animals as lesser creatures, weak things beneath his notice. He never asked for help, for asking for help, he believed, was for the weak, and he was the strong. To ask, to the lion’s proud mind, was to admit a lack, and the king of beasts would admit no lack in himself. I am the lion, he would say. I need no one. I depend on no one. My own strength is enough for anything."}, {"id": "folk-lion-learned-to-ask#s3", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "And the other animals, the small clever ones, the ones who lived by cooperation and by helping one another, the lion despised. Look at them, he sneered, these weak creatures who must band together and help each other because none of them is strong enough alone. How pitiful. I alone need no one’s help."}, {"id": "folk-lion-learned-to-ask#s4", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "But pride, my children, always meets its lesson, and the lion met his. There came a day when the mighty lion, in all his strength, fell into a trouble that his strength could not solve. The tellings vary. In some, he fell into a hunter’s pit, a deep trap from which, for all his mighty muscles, he could not leap out, for the walls were too high and too steep and there was nothing to spring from. In some, he was caught in a hunter’s net or snare, and the more his great strength strained against it, the tighter it bound him. In some, a thorn or a bone lodged deep in his paw or his throat, a small thing, but one his mighty strength could do nothing to remove, and it festered and weakened him. In every telling, the lesson is the same: the mighty lion was brought low by a trouble that raw strength could not fix, and for the first time in his life he was helpless."}, {"id": "folk-lion-learned-to-ask#s5", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "And there the great king lay, trapped or bound or suffering, and his strength availed him nothing. He roared, and roared, but a roar cannot lift you from a pit or untie a net or pull a thorn. He strained with all his mighty muscles, and it did no good, or made things worse. Slowly, terribly, it dawned upon the proud lion that his own strength, the strength he had boasted of all his life, the strength he had believed made him need no one, was not enough. He was going to die there, alone, brought down by a small trouble, because he was too strong to save himself and too proud ever to have made a friend who might help him."}, {"id": "folk-lion-learned-to-ask#s6", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "Now here, my children, is the turning, and it is a hard and a humbling one for a king. The lion had to make a choice. He could hold onto his pride and die, roaring defiance to the end, needing no one, and be very dead and very proud. Or he could do the thing he had despised all his life. He could ask for help. And at last, in his extremity, the great lion humbled himself and asked. When a small creature passed by, a mouse in some tellings, a little bird, a passing animal he had always scorned, the lion, swallowing the pride of a lifetime, called out not with a command but with a request. Please, he said, and oh, how the word must have burned in his royal throat, please, help me. I am trapped and I cannot free myself. I have been proud, and I scorned you, and I was wrong. Please, help me."}, {"id": "folk-lion-learned-to-ask#s7", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "And the small creature helped him. In the most beloved telling, it was the tiny mouse, whom the lion had once been about to eat and had, in a rare moment, let go free. And now the tiny mouse, so small the lion had laughed at the very idea that it could ever be of use, came and with its little sharp teeth gnawed through the ropes of the net that all the lion’s strength could not break, and set the king of beasts free. The creature the lion had despised as useless saved his life, precisely by doing what the mighty lion could not: a small quick clever thing, gnawing a rope, where great strength was helpless."}, {"id": "folk-lion-learned-to-ask#s8", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "And so the lion was freed, and lived, and was never again quite so proud. He had learned, at the very edge of death, the lesson his strength had hidden from him all his life: that no one, not even the king of beasts, is truly strong enough to need no one; that everyone, however mighty, meets troubles that their own strength cannot solve; and that to ask for help in such an hour is not weakness but the plainest wisdom, and often the only road to life."}, {"id": "folk-lion-learned-to-ask#s9", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "Now the lesson, my children, is for the strong among you especially, though it is for everyone. The lion believed that his strength meant he needed no one, and that asking for help was beneath him. And that pride nearly killed him. For here is a truth that pride hides from the strong: everyone, no matter how mighty, will one day face a trouble that their own strength cannot solve. The strongest man cannot lift himself from some pits. The cleverest woman cannot untie some knots alone. And in that hour, the one who has been too proud to ask, and too scornful to make friends, will lie helpless like the trapped lion, brought down by a small thing, because greatness without humility is a trap of its own."}, {"id": "folk-lion-learned-to-ask#s10", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "There is no shame in asking for help, my children. Hear me, for the world will tell you otherwise. The world will tell you, as the lion believed, that to ask is to be weak, that the strong stand alone. It is a lie, and it is a lie that has killed many a proud lion. The truly wise, even the truly strong, know how to ask. They know that the small despised creature may hold the very help that all their strength cannot supply. They know that we are made to help one another, the strong and the weak together, and that the one who scorns this and stands proudly alone has not understood the first thing about strength."}, {"id": "folk-lion-learned-to-ask#s11", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So be strong, my children, if you can. But never be too strong to say the two words that saved the king of beasts: please, help. And never be too proud to be kind to the small creatures, for it may be the mouse you spared, and not your own great strength, that one day gnaws you free. Tomorrow, my children, a quiet and a beautiful tale, of why it is that the old people, and only the old people, know the true names of all the rivers, and what that knowing really means."}]}, {"id": "folk-old-people-rivers", "emoji": "📖", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#0a1a00,#1a3a00)", "category": "iwe", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "8 min", "title": {"en": "Why Old People Know the Names of Rivers", "yo": "Why Old People Know the Names of Rivers"}, "desc": {"en": "The knowledge only long living gives, and the honour owed to the old.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Folktales / Literary", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Apagbe, the tale of the deep knowledge that only long living can give, and the honor owed to the old)", "segments": [{"id": "folk-old-people-rivers#s1", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Sit close, my children, and let me tell you a quiet and a beautiful tale, one that is really about the old people you see every day, the grandmothers and grandfathers, the elders who sit in the shade, and about a kind of knowledge that only they possess. For our people say that it is the old ones, and only the old ones, who truly know the names of all the rivers. And there is a tale in that saying, and a deep honor."}, {"id": "folk-old-people-rivers#s2", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "In a certain village, the tellings say, there were young people who had grown impatient with their elders, as the young sometimes do. What use are the old people? The young ones grumbled among themselves. They are slow. They cannot farm as we farm or run as we run or work as we work. They sit in the shade and eat the food we grow. They tell the same old stories again and again. Of what use are they to us now? Perhaps we would be better off without carrying them. And there is a dark version of this tale, my children, told among many peoples, in which a foolish young generation actually decides to be rid of the burden of the old, and drives out or abandons its elders, keeping only the strong young workers. Hold that grim idea in your mind, for the tale will use it."}, {"id": "folk-old-people-rivers#s3", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Now it happened, as trouble always happens, that a great difficulty came upon that village, a difficulty that the strong young people, for all their strength, could not solve. In some tellings it was a terrible drought, and the young ones could not find water, for the familiar rivers and streams had dried up, and they did not know where the older, deeper, hidden sources lay. In some it was a riddle or a challenge set by a powerful stranger or a spirit, a question about the old ways, the old boundaries, the old names, that no young person could answer. In some it was a matter of a disputed farmland or a broken custom, where the truth lay in what had happened long ago, before any young person was born."}, {"id": "folk-old-people-rivers#s4", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "And the young people, my children, were helpless. For all their strength, they did not know. They did not know where the hidden water lay in the time of drought. They did not know the answer to the old riddle. They did not know the ancient boundaries or the old agreements or the names of the rivers that had run dry and might run again. This knowledge was not in them, because they had not lived long enough to gather it, and they had scorned the very ones who held it."}, {"id": "folk-old-people-rivers#s5", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "And here comes the heart of the tale. For there was, in most tellings, one old person who had been saved, hidden and cared for by a loving grandchild who could not bear to see them driven out, or simply an elder the village had, after all, kept. And when the great difficulty came and the young ones stood helpless, it was that old one who knew the answer. The old one remembered where the hidden spring lay, the deep old water source that never failed, from a drought suffered fifty years before, when they themselves were young. The old one knew the answer to the ancient riddle, because they had heard it from their own grandparents, long ago. The old one knew the names of the rivers, all of them, the ones running and the ones dried up and the ones that only flowed in the great rains, and knew the boundaries and the old agreements and the buried truths."}, {"id": "folk-old-people-rivers#s6", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "And by that old knowledge, the village was saved. The hidden spring was found, and the people drank. The riddle was answered, and the danger passed. The truth was remembered, and the dispute was settled. The old one, whom the young had thought useless, held in their long memory the very knowledge that saved everyone, knowledge that could not be found in any strong young back or any swift young leg, but only in a mind that had lived long and gathered much and forgotten nothing."}, {"id": "folk-old-people-rivers#s7", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "And from that day, my children, the young people of that village honored their elders as they had never honored them before. For they had learned, at the very edge of disaster, why the old people know the names of the rivers, and why that knowing is precious beyond all the strength of youth. The old know the names of the rivers because they have lived beside those rivers for a long lifetime, have seen them flood and seen them dry, have learned in their long years a thousand things that no young person can possibly know, because knowing them takes time, and time is the one thing the young do not yet have."}, {"id": "folk-old-people-rivers#s8", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "Now the lesson, my children, is one I hold especially dear, being old myself. It is this: do not despise the old, and do not despise the knowledge that only long living can give. The strength and speed of youth are good things, real things, and the world needs them. But they are not the only things, and they are not the deepest things. There is a kind of knowledge that cannot be hurried, that cannot be taught quickly to the young, that comes only from having lived a long time and watched and remembered. The names of the rivers. Where the hidden water lies. What happened in the drought of fifty years ago and how the people survived it. The old boundaries, the old agreements, the old ways that still hold the answers to new troubles. All of this lives in the old people, and in them alone, and a people who throw away their old ones throw away their own memory and their own survival."}, {"id": "folk-old-people-rivers#s9", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "Honor your elders, my children. Sit with them. Listen to their same old stories, for the same old stories are the names of the rivers, and one day, in a drought you cannot yet foresee, one of those stories may be the only thing that saves you. And know that you too, if you are fortunate, will grow old, and will hold in your own long memory the names of rivers that the young of that day will not know, and will be tempted to scorn you for your slowness, not understanding, until their own hour of trouble comes, why the old people know the names of the rivers. This, my children, is also why I keep you here at the fire, night after night, telling you these tales. I am teaching you the names of the rivers. Remember them."}, {"id": "folk-old-people-rivers#s10", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "Tomorrow, a stranger tale, of a girl born with a fearful and a wonderful gift, the gift of hearing the future before it came, and the loneliness and the wisdom that such a gift can bring."}]}, {"id": "folk-girl-hear-future", "emoji": "📖", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#0a1a00,#1a3a00)", "category": "iwe", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "8 min", "title": {"en": "The Girl Who Could Hear the Future", "yo": "The Girl Who Could Hear the Future"}, "desc": {"en": "A child born with the fearful gift of hearing what is to come.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Folktales / Literary", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Apagbe, the tale of the child born with a fearful gift, and the burden and the wisdom of knowing what is to come)", "segments": [{"id": "folk-girl-hear-future#s1", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Draw close, my children, and let your voices fall quiet, for tonight’s tale is a strange and a haunting one, about a girl who was born with a gift that most people think they would want, until they see what it truly costs. She was a girl who could hear the future. And her story teaches us hard and gentle things about knowledge, and loneliness, and what we do with what we know."}, {"id": "folk-girl-hear-future#s2", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "There was once a girl, born like any other, except that from her earliest years it became clear that she was not like any other at all. For this girl could hear the future. It came to her, the tellings say, like a whispering on the wind that only she could hear, or like voices under the ordinary sounds of the world, telling her of things that had not yet happened. She would hear the coming of the rains before any cloud appeared. She would hear of a traveler’s arrival days before he came. She would hear, sometimes, of sorrows approaching, a sickness, a death, a hard season, long before anyone else could know."}, {"id": "folk-girl-hear-future#s3", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "At first her family thought it a wonderful thing, a blessing, a mark of favor. And in some ways it was. When the girl heard that a drought was coming, the village could store grain. When she heard that raiders were on the road, the people could prepare or flee. Her gift saved lives and softened blows. She was, you might think, the most fortunate of children, to carry such a power."}, {"id": "folk-girl-hear-future#s4", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "But, my children, the gift was also a heavy and a lonely burden, and this is the true heart of the tale. For to hear the future is not only to hear the good. The girl heard the sorrows too, and could not stop hearing them. She would know, sometimes, that a beloved person was going to die, long before it happened, and would have to carry that terrible knowledge alone, watching them laugh and live, unable to change what was coming, unable even always to speak of it. She would sit at a joyful feast and hear, under the laughter, the whisper of the grief that was coming to that very house, and could not join the joy, because she alone knew."}, {"id": "folk-girl-hear-future#s5", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "And the people, my children, grew afraid of her, as people so often grow afraid of what they do not understand. It is one thing to be glad of a warning. It is another to live beside someone who knows your future, who may know your death, who carries knowledge you cannot share. The villagers began to keep their distance. They came to her when they wanted to know something, but they did not sit with her as a friend. They used her gift, but they did not love her for it, and some feared and even blamed her, as though by hearing a coming sorrow she had somehow caused it. So the girl who could hear the future grew up profoundly alone, set apart by the very gift that might have seemed a blessing, carrying knowledge no one else could bear and no one wished to share."}, {"id": "folk-girl-hear-future#s6", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "Now the tellings take the girl’s story in more than one direction, but the wisest of them turn on a choice she had to make about what to do with her terrible gift. For she learned, over her lonely years, some hard truths about knowing the future. She learned, first, that she could not change most of what she heard. The future she heard largely came to pass, whatever she did. And this taught her a strange peace, and a strange grief. She could not save everyone. She could not stop every sorrow. Some things were coming and would come, and her knowing them did not give her the power to prevent them, only the burden of foreseeing them."}, {"id": "folk-girl-hear-future#s7", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "But she learned, second, and this was her wisdom, that even when she could not change what was coming, she could change how people met it. She could not stop the drought, but she could help the people prepare, so that it did not destroy them. She could not always prevent a death, but she could, sometimes, help a family treasure their last good days together, without ever needing to speak the hard thing aloud, simply by gently urging them toward one another while there was still time. She could not change the future, but she could help people live wisely in the present that the future was rushing toward."}, {"id": "folk-girl-hear-future#s8", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "And so the girl who could hear the future became, in the wisest tellings, not a mere oracle to be used and feared, but a kind of quiet, sorrowful, loving guardian of her people, using her lonely gift as best she could, to soften what could be softened, to prepare what could be prepared, and to carry in silence the many things that could not be changed and should not be spoken. She never escaped the loneliness of her gift entirely. To know what others cannot know is always to be somewhat alone. But she made of her burden a kind of service, and in that service she found what peace was possible for one who hears too much. Now the lessons, my children, for this deep tale holds several."}, {"id": "folk-girl-hear-future#s9", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "It teaches us, first, that knowledge is not always a simple blessing. We imagine we would love to know the future, to know everything. But the girl’s gift shows us the weight of such knowing, the loneliness of carrying what others cannot carry, the grief of foreseeing sorrows you cannot prevent. Be a little careful, my children, of wishing to know everything. Some knowledge is heavy, and the ones who carry great knowledge often carry great loneliness with it. When you meet a person who seems to know much, do not only envy them. Consider, too, the weight they may be carrying, and be kind."}, {"id": "folk-girl-hear-future#s10", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "It teaches us, second, a wisdom about what we can and cannot change. The girl could not change most of the future she heard, and her peace came only when she accepted this, and turned her energy not to the impossible task of preventing everything, but to the possible task of helping people meet what came with wisdom and preparation and love. There is deep wisdom here for all of us, who cannot hear the future but who all know that hard things are coming, for hard things always come. We cannot prevent all sorrow. But we can prepare. We can treasure the good days while we have them. We can turn toward one another while there is still time. That is a wisdom you do not need to hear the future to practice, my children. You already know that sorrows will come. Live, then, like the wise girl: preparing what can be prepared, treasuring what can be treasured, and meeting what cannot be changed with as much love and courage as you can."}, {"id": "folk-girl-hear-future#s11", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "Tomorrow, my children, the last of tonight’s tales but one, a tale of music and of struggle, of how the drum, that heart of all our celebrations, first found its voice."}]}, {"id": "folk-drum-found-voice", "emoji": "📖", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#0a1a00,#1a3a00)", "category": "iwe", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "6 min", "title": {"en": "How the Drum Found Its Voice", "yo": "How the Drum Found Its Voice"}, "desc": {"en": "How the drum, heart of Yoruba music, first learned to speak.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Folktales / Literary", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Apagbe, the tale of how the drum, the heart of our music, first learned to speak)", "segments": [{"id": "folk-drum-found-voice#s1", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Listen, my children. Listen to the drum. When the drummer’s hands fall upon the skin of the talking drum, the dundun, and it rises and falls and speaks almost like a human voice, calling the praises of a king or the rhythm of a dance, have you ever wondered how the drum first found its voice? For the drum, my children, does not merely make noise. Among our people the drum speaks. It talks. It says words, real words, that those who know can understand. And there is a tale of how this came to be."}, {"id": "folk-drum-found-voice#s2", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "In the early days, the tellings say, there was no drum, or there was a drum but it had no voice, only a dull dead thud, a mere noise, saying nothing. And the people longed for music that could do more than make a flat sound. They longed for a music that could speak to the heart, that could carry words and feelings, that could call the village together and lift the dancer’s feet and praise the ancestors and mourn the dead. But the drum they had could not do this. It only thudded, empty and voiceless. Now the tellings differ on how the drum found its voice, my children, but the deepest of them agree on this: the voice did not come easily. It came through struggle, and through sacrifice, and through the giving of something precious."}, {"id": "folk-drum-found-voice#s3", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "In one telling, there was a maker, a craftsman or a musician, who was determined to give the drum a voice, and who labored long and hard at it. He tried this wood and that wood, this skin and that skin, this shape and that shape, failing and failing, for the true voice would not come. Many would have given up. But he persisted, through long labor and much failure, until at last he learned the secrets, the right wood hollowed just so, the right skin stretched just so, the cords that could tighten and loosen the skin so that its pitch could rise and fall like a speaking voice. And through all that patient struggle, the drum at last found its voice, and could speak. The voice was the fruit of long, hard, faithful work, and of a refusal to give up until the dead noise became living speech."}, {"id": "folk-drum-found-voice#s4", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "In a deeper and a sadder telling, my children, the drum’s voice came through a sacrifice. For the skin of the drum, the part that speaks, must come from a living creature that gave its life, and the spirit or the voice within the drum is, in this telling, understood to be a kind of gift or sacrifice, a life given so that the drum might speak. In some versions a beloved animal, in some a spirit, gave itself, and its voice passed into the drum, so that when the drum speaks, it is in some sense the voice of that sacrifice still singing. And this is why, the old ones say, the drum can touch us so deeply, because there is a true voice in it, a life in it, given at a price."}, {"id": "folk-drum-found-voice#s5", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "And once the drum had found its voice, my children, everything changed for the people. Now the drum could speak. Now it could call the names and the praises of the kings, beating out the very words of the oriki, the praise-poetry, so that a skilled drummer could make the drum say a chief’s whole lineage and history. Now it could call the village together from far off, sending messages across the miles in the drum-language that carried words no shout could carry. Now it could lift the dancers, and lead the festivals, and praise the ancestors, and mourn the dead with a grief deeper than any voice. The drum became the very heartbeat of the people’s life, present at every birth and marriage and death and festival, speaking for the whole community in a voice that all could feel. Now the lessons, my children, and this musical tale carries good ones."}, {"id": "folk-drum-found-voice#s6", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "It teaches us, first, whichever telling we hold, that the things of true value and true beauty come through struggle and through sacrifice, not easily and not for free. The drum’s voice came either through long, patient, failing, persistent labour, or through a life given in sacrifice, or both. It did not come cheaply. And so it is, my children, with everything that has a true voice, everything that can touch the heart. The beautiful song, the deep skill, the thing made well, the love that lasts, all of these come through struggle and cost something dear. Do not expect the things of real value to come easily. The drum found its voice the hard way, and that is the only way the deep voices are ever found."}, {"id": "folk-drum-found-voice#s7", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "And it teaches us, second, to hear rightly. When you hear the drum speak, my children, now you know that you are hearing the fruit of struggle and sacrifice, a voice won at a price. Listen to it with the respect that such a thing deserves. And more widely, when anything in this world speaks to you with a voice that touches your heart, a music, a poem, a great work, a deep kindness, remember that behind that voice there is almost always a hidden struggle, a hidden sacrifice, a hidden cost that someone paid so that this beauty could speak. Honor the cost. Hear the sacrifice in the voice. The drum can teach you to listen that deeply, if you let it."}, {"id": "folk-drum-found-voice#s8", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "Tomorrow, my children, the last of these tales for now, a strange and a solemn one, about a man who took from the forest without asking, and so came to owe the forest a debt, and how such debts are always, in the end, collected."}]}, {"id": "folk-man-owed-forest", "emoji": "📖", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#0a1a00,#1a3a00)", "category": "iwe", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "8 min", "title": {"en": "The Man Who Owed the Forest", "yo": "The Man Who Owed the Forest"}, "desc": {"en": "The taker who did not ask, and the debt the forest always collects.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Folktales / Literary", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Apagbe, the tale of the taker who did not ask, and the debt the forest always collects)", "segments": [{"id": "folk-man-owed-forest#s1", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Come close, my children, and let the fire burn low, for the last of tonight’s tales is a solemn one, a tale from the deep green shadow of the forest, about a man who took and took and never asked and never gave thanks, and about the debt that such a man builds up without knowing it, a debt that the forest, in its own slow time, always comes to collect."}, {"id": "folk-man-owed-forest#s2", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "You must understand first, my children, how our ancestors regarded the forest, the igbo, the great bush that surrounded every village. The forest was not to them a mere pile of wood and a hunting ground to be stripped as one pleased. The forest was alive. It was full of spirits, the ebora and the imale, the powers of the trees and the streams and the hidden places. It gave the people everything, the game they hunted, the herbs that healed them, the wood for their fires and their houses, the fruits and the nuts and the honey. And because it gave so much, the forest was to be approached with respect. A hunter, before he took, would ask. He would give thanks. He would leave offerings. He would take what he needed and no more, and he would honor the spirits of the place. For our people understood a deep truth: that you cannot simply take and take from what sustains you, without respect and without limit, and expect no reckoning."}, {"id": "folk-man-owed-forest#s3", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "But there was a man in this tale who did not understand this, or who understood it and scorned it. He was a greedy and a heedless man, and he looked at the forest and saw only things to be taken. He hunted more than he needed, killing animals he left to rot, for the mere pleasure or pride of killing. He cut down trees wastefully. He took the herbs and the honey and the fruit without ever asking, without ever giving thanks, without ever leaving the smallest offering or speaking the smallest word of respect to the spirits of the place. He treated the living forest as a dead storehouse to be plundered at his will. I take what I want, he said. Why should I ask a tree? Why should I thank a bush? The forest is here for my use, and I will use it as I please."}, {"id": "folk-man-owed-forest#s4", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "And for a time, my children, it seemed he suffered nothing for it. He grew fat and prosperous on what he stripped from the forest. He laughed at the old people who still asked and gave thanks before they took. Superstitious fools, he called them. See how I take freely and prosper, while you waste your time bowing to trees. But the forest was keeping count, my children. The forest always keeps count. Every animal wantonly killed, every tree needlessly felled, every gift taken without asking or thanks, was written down in the slow deep memory of the green places, and the man’s debt grew and grew, though he never felt it growing. He thought he was taking for free. In truth he was borrowing, at a terrible rate, against a debt he would one day have to pay."}, {"id": "folk-man-owed-forest#s5", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "And the day of reckoning came, as it always comes in these tales, for the forest does not forget and does not forgive the one who never asks. The tellings differ in exactly how the debt was collected. In some, the man went into the forest one day as he always had, to take as he always took, and the forest closed around him. The paths he knew twisted and vanished. The spirits he had scorned led him in circles. And he was lost in the deep bush, the very bush he thought he had mastered, and it swallowed him, and he was never seen again, taken by the forest to pay at last the debt he had built up. In some tellings the animals he had wronged, or the spirits of the trees he had felled, came against him. In some, a great sickness or misfortune fell upon him and all his ill-gotten prosperity rotted away as suddenly as it had come, the forest reclaiming all it had lent him. But in every telling, the debt was collected in full. The man who had taken and taken and never asked and never given thanks was made, in the end, to pay everything he owed, and the payment was terrible."}, {"id": "folk-man-owed-forest#s6", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "And the people, my children, saw, and remembered, and told the tale, so that the young would learn what the greedy man had refused to learn: that you cannot simply take and take from what sustains you, without respect and without limit, and walk away free. The debt is always recorded. The reckoning always comes. Now the lesson, my children, is old, but it has never been more needed than in your own day, so hear it well. The forest gives us everything, and our ancestors knew that everything given must be honored, asked for, thanked, and taken only in measure. The greedy man treated the living source of his life as a dead thing to be plundered without respect or limit, and the debt he built up in his heedlessness destroyed him in the end."}, {"id": "folk-man-owed-forest#s7", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "This is true of the forest, my children, and it is true, you will find, of everything that sustains you. The land that feeds you, if you strip it without care and give nothing back, will one day fail you, and the debt will be collected in hunger. The water, the animals, the very earth, all of these give to us, and all of these keep count, and a people who take and take without respect or limit or thanks build up a debt that their children, if not they themselves, will be made to pay. Our ancestors knew this. They asked before they took. They gave thanks. They took in measure and left offerings and honored the spirits of the living world. We may smile at their bowing to trees, my children, but there was a deep and true wisdom in it, a wisdom your own age is only now, too late, beginning to relearn: that we do not own what sustains us, we only borrow it, and every debt to the living world is, in the end, collected in full."}, {"id": "folk-man-owed-forest#s8", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "So be not like the man who owed the forest, my children. Whatever sustains you, the land, the water, the people who love you, the community that holds you, do not simply take and take from it without respect or thanks or limit. Ask. Give thanks. Take in measure. Give back. Honor the source. For the forest always keeps count, and the reckoning, though it may be slow, always comes."}, {"id": "folk-man-owed-forest#s9", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "And so, my children, we close this round of tales. We have laughed with the tortoise and shivered with the river woman, marveled at Ajantala and wept with the girl whose tears grew a forest, learned patience from the weaver bird and humility from the lion and respect from the man who owed the forest and never paid until the end. Carry these tales with you. Tell them again. For as long as they are told, the fire is not out, and the wisdom of the ancestors is not lost, and you, my children, are their keepers now."}]}, {"id": "folk-girl-refused-to-cry", "emoji": "📖", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#0a1a00,#1a3a00)", "category": "iwe", "rating": 9.7, "duration": "15 min", "title": {"en": "The Girl Who Refused to Cry", "yo": "The Girl Who Refused to Cry"}, "desc": {"en": "A moral tale of grief, pride, and the healing that comes only when the heart is allowed to break.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Folktales / Literary", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Aroso, a folktale of Yorubaland about grief, pride, and the healing that only comes when the heart is allowed to break)", "segments": [{"id": "folk-girl-refused-to-cry#s1", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Come and sit. The fire is young tonight, and the moths have not yet found it, so we have a little quiet before the noise of the night begins. Good. Let me tell you about a girl whose name the old people still say when a child holds sorrow too long inside a stiff little chest. Her name was Aduke, and it is said that she was born on a morning so bright that even the roosters forgot to crow, for they thought the sun had already risen twice."}, {"id": "folk-girl-refused-to-cry#s2", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Aduke was the only daughter of a woman named Iya Ronke, a dyer of cloth in a town at the edge of the forest, where the indigo pots stood in rows like fat sleeping elders and the air smelled always of that deep blue that stains the fingers and will not wash away for a week. Iya Ronke was a widow, and Aduke was her whole harvest, her only field, the single lamp in a house that would otherwise have been dark. And because the mother poured all her love into the child, the child grew up believing that love was a thing that could not be taken away, the way a foolish person believes the river will always be kind because it has never yet swallowed him."}, {"id": "folk-girl-refused-to-cry#s3", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Now, Aduke had one strangeness about her, and the neighbours spoke of it the way people speak of a beautiful mask that has one crack across the eye. The girl did not cry. Not as a baby, when the midwife slapped her small back and waited, and waited, and finally the child simply opened her eyes and looked at the woman as though asking what all the fuss was about. Not as a toddler, when she fell into the cooking ashes and burned her palm and only pressed her lips together and stared at the blister as if it belonged to someone else. Not when the other children mocked her for having no father. Aduke had decided, somewhere in the deep hidden root of herself, that crying was a kind of surrender, and she was not a girl who surrendered."}, {"id": "folk-girl-refused-to-cry#s4", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Iya Ronke worried about this the way a farmer worries about a sky that will not rain. “A child who does not cry,” said the old dyer women, dipping their cloth, “is a pot that is filling with water and has no crack to let it out. One day it will not spill. One day it will simply burst.” But Iya Ronke would laugh and say her daughter was strong, and pride is a sweet wine that lets a mother swallow a warning without tasting it. The years turned, as years do, like a woman turning cloth in the dye. Aduke grew tall and clever. She could count faster than the market women, she could weave a pattern her mother had never taught her, and she carried herself with a straight back that some called grace and others called stubbornness, and both, my child, were telling the truth, for those two are often the same tree seen from two sides."}, {"id": "folk-girl-refused-to-cry#s5", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Then came the season of the long fever. You know how such seasons come, quiet as a thief, taking one old man here, one small baby there, and everyone says it is nothing, it is the ordinary sorrow of living, until suddenly it is in your own compound, sitting by your own fire, warming its cold hands. The fever came for Iya Ronke."}, {"id": "folk-girl-refused-to-cry#s6", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Aduke nursed her mother for eleven days. She boiled the bitter leaf, she called the herbalist and paid him with the last of the indigo cloth, she pressed cool water to the burning forehead through nights so long they seemed to have swallowed the very idea of morning. And through all of it, Aduke did not cry. She set her jaw and she worked, because she believed, as the young believe, that if she was strong enough, if she refused hard enough, the thing she feared could not happen. As if death were a debtor who could be frightened away by a fierce enough face."}, {"id": "folk-girl-refused-to-cry#s7", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "On the twelfth morning, Iya Ronke opened her eyes, found her daughter’s hand, held it, and said, “Aduke, my own, when the water rises in you, let it rise. Do not dam a river that only wants to reach the sea.” And then, gently, the way a lamp goes out when the oil is finished and not before, Iya Ronke left. And Aduke did not cry."}, {"id": "folk-girl-refused-to-cry#s8", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "The neighbours came. They wailed and rolled on the ground the way we do, for among us grief is not a private thing to be swallowed but a public river we all step into together, so that no one drowns alone. The women beat their chests. The men stood with their heads bowed. And in the middle of all that honest, loud, healing sorrow stood Aduke, dry as a road in harmattan, straight as a post, saying to everyone who tried to hold her, “I am well. There is much to do. Who will help me prepare her for the ground?” The old dyer women looked at one another. “The pot,” one of them whispered. “The pot with no crack.”"}, {"id": "folk-girl-refused-to-cry#s9", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "They buried Iya Ronke under the iroko at the edge of the indigo yard, where the good blue smell would keep her company. And Aduke went back to work the very next day, her hands in the dye, her back straight, her eyes dry, and the whole town marvelled at her strength and pitied it in the same breath, the way you might admire a beautiful house and notice, only as you leave, that no smoke rises from its kitchen. Now here is where the folktale opens its second door, my child, for our stories never let a thing be simple."}, {"id": "folk-girl-refused-to-cry#s10", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "There was in that forest a certain spirit, an old and patient one, whom the people called the Weeping Woman of the stream, Arugbo Omi, the ancient one of the water. She was not wicked. Understand this, because the young always want a story to have a monster, and life so rarely gives them one. Arugbo Omi was the keeper of the town’s tears. It was her work, from the beginning of things, to gather the sorrow the people let flow and to carry it down to the great sea, where all grief is finally dissolved and made light. Every tear ever cried in that town had passed through her hands and been carried gently away. That was the order of the world."}, {"id": "folk-girl-refused-to-cry#s11", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "But a tear that is never cried does not disappear. Oh no. It has nowhere to go. And so, without anyone knowing it, every tear Aduke refused to weep, from the burned palm of her babyhood to the fresh unbearable loss of her mother, had been collecting, drop by drop, year by year, in a hidden clay vessel deep in the roots of the forest, filling and filling. And on the night after the burial, that vessel, at last, was full to the very lip."}, {"id": "folk-girl-refused-to-cry#s12", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "Arugbo Omi came up out of her stream in the grey hour before dawn, when the world holds its breath. She was not frightening to look at. She looked like the oldest woman you have ever seen, with a face folded like cloth put away too long, and eyes that were kind and endlessly, endlessly tired. She came to Aduke’s compound and she sat by the cold ashes of the fire, and she waited until the girl came out to fetch water."}, {"id": "folk-girl-refused-to-cry#s13", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "“Daughter,” said the old spirit, “I have carried this town’s sorrow since before your grandmother’s grandmother drew breath. And in all that time, I have never once had trouble with a single soul. Until you.” She lifted her hand, and in it was the clay vessel, and it was trembling and dark and heavy, and Aduke could hear, faintly, a sound coming from it, the sound of a river that has been told for eighteen years that it may not move. “This is yours,” said Arugbo Omi. “Every drop. It is time to give it to me, so that I may carry it to the sea and you may be light again.” And Aduke, my child, being who she was, lifted her straight back straighter still and said, “I do not weep. Take your pot elsewhere, old mother. I have work.”"}, {"id": "folk-girl-refused-to-cry#s14", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "The old spirit did not argue. Spirits rarely argue; they simply wait, for they have all the time that we do not. She set the trembling vessel down beside the cold fire and she said only this: “A river dammed does not stop being a river. It only decides, in the end, to make a new path. And the new path a dammed river makes, daughter, is never gentle.” Then she went back into her stream, and Aduke, stubborn to the bone, kicked cool ashes over the clay pot as if to bury it, and went to fetch her water, and told herself she felt nothing, nothing at all, her heart a fist closed so tight it had forgotten it was ever a hand. That night the dam broke. But it did not break as tears."}, {"id": "folk-girl-refused-to-cry#s15", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Aduke woke in the black middle of the night to find that she could not move her arms. When she looked, in the dim light of the low moon through the doorway, she saw that her fingers had begun to turn the deep unmoving blue of the indigo dye, and the blue was climbing, slow as sap, up her wrists, up toward her elbows. The river, denied the soft path of tears, had found a harder one. All that unwept sorrow was turning her, quite literally, into stone the colour of the dye her mother had loved. She was becoming a statue of her own refusal. She tried to scream and could not. She tried to rise and could not. And in that terror, at last, at long last, something in Aduke understood what she had spent her whole life not understanding: that strength which cannot bend is not strength at all, but only a slower kind of breaking."}, {"id": "folk-girl-refused-to-cry#s16", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "With the very last of her free breath, Aduke did the one thing she had sworn since babyhood she would never do. She thought of her mother’s hand growing cold in hers. She thought of the burned palm no one had kissed. She thought of the father she had never wept for, of every mockery she had swallowed, of the long eleven nights and the twelfth morning and the four words, let it rise, my own. And she let it rise. One tear. Just one, out of one blue-turning eye. But eighteen years were in that single tear."}, {"id": "folk-girl-refused-to-cry#s17", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "It fell, and where it struck the earthen floor, the clay vessel beside the cold fire cracked clean across, the way the dyer women had said it would, all those years ago. The pot with no crack, at last, had a crack. And out of it poured the river, all of it, and Aduke wept as no one in that town had ever wept before or since, wept until the neighbours ran in with lamps, wept until the blue drained back out of her arms and her fingers were her own warm brown again, wept until the grey dawn came, and then wept a little more, for the good measure of a heart learning what it was for."}, {"id": "folk-girl-refused-to-cry#s18", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "And here is the strange and beautiful thing, my child, the thing that makes this a story worth carrying to you across all these years. When Aduke’s tears at last flowed down through the ground to the stream, Arugbo Omi gathered them and carried them to the sea, as was her work. But she found among them so much long-held love, so much fierce faithful devotion pressed into sorrow, that she did not let it dissolve like ordinary grief. Instead she planted it. And on the bank of that stream there grew, overnight, a single indigo tree, the only one that has ever grown wild, its leaves the exact blue of the dye pots, and it is said that its leaves make the truest blue in all of Yorubaland, a blue with a little silver in it, the silver of tears finally allowed to fall."}, {"id": "folk-girl-refused-to-cry#s19", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "Weavers still search the forests for that tree. And the old dyer women, when they teach a stiff, proud, hurting child, will touch the little one’s chest and say, gently, “Do not be the pot with no crack. Let it rise. There is a whole tree waiting on the other side of your weeping.” So. If ever you feel the water rising in you and some hard proud voice tells you to dam it, to be strong, to swallow it and keep your back straight, remember Aduke. Remember that the bravest thing is not to refuse the river but to let it reach the sea. Grief that is wept becomes, in time, a tree that gives shade. Grief that is swallowed becomes stone."}, {"id": "folk-girl-refused-to-cry#s20", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "Now the moths have found the fire, you see, and it is time. Tomorrow, if the sky is kind and the yams hold out, I will tell you of another blue, the blue of a grandmother’s cloth and the year it saved a whole village. But that, my child, is a tale for another night."}]}, {"id": "folk-grandmothers-indigo", "emoji": "📖", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#0a1a00,#1a3a00)", "category": "iwe", "rating": 9.6, "duration": "10 min", "title": {"en": "Grandmother’s Indigo", "yo": "Grandmother’s Indigo"}, "desc": {"en": "A dyer's cloth, a hungry season, and the wealth hidden inside patience.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Folktales / Literary", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Aroso, a folktale of Yorubaland about a dyer’s cloth, a hungry season, and the wealth that hides inside patience)", "segments": [{"id": "folk-grandmothers-indigo#s1", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Draw a little nearer, for this next tale is a soft one, and soft tales must be told low. It carries on from where we left the blue tree growing by the stream, for you asked me about a grandmother’s cloth, and there is no better night than tonight to tell it. This is the story of Iya Agba Duro, whose name means the grandmother who stands, and stands, and stands, and it is a story about the difference between a thing that is slow and a thing that is worthless, which the young so often confuse, and the old so rarely do."}, {"id": "folk-grandmothers-indigo#s2", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "In a town whose name I will not tell you, because it belongs now to the ancestors, there lived an old woman who was the last true indigo dyer of her people. Her hands were blue to the elbow, blue past all washing, blue the way the evening sky is blue just before it becomes night, and the children called her Iya Aro, mother of the dye. She was poor in the way that the truly skilled are often poor, for she would not hurry her craft, and the world pays quickly for quick things and slowly, so slowly, for the things that are worth waiting for."}, {"id": "folk-grandmothers-indigo#s3", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Now you must understand something about true indigo, my child, for the whole story turns on it. The blue does not come easily. The dyer does not simply dip the cloth and pull it out blue, the way a foolish person imagines. No. When the cloth first comes out of the indigo pot it is green. An ugly, sickly, disappointing green, like a fruit that will never ripen. And only when the air touches it, only when it is lifted and hung and allowed to breathe, does the green slowly, slowly turn, before your patient eyes, into that deep and holy blue. The blue is a gift the air gives to the cloth, but only if the cloth is willing to wait in the ugliness of the green long enough to receive it."}, {"id": "folk-grandmothers-indigo#s4", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Iya Aro knew this in her bones. She had dipped and lifted, dipped and lifted, for sixty years. And she had a granddaughter, a bright quick girl named Sola, who lived with her because the girl’s parents had gone to the city to seek their fortune and, as often happens, the city had swallowed them whole and given back only silence. Sola was clever and Sola was impatient, which is the most dangerous marriage of qualities a young person can carry, for cleverness gives you a hundred shortcuts and impatience makes you take every one of them. She loved her grandmother, but she did not understand her. “Iya Agba,” she would say, watching the old woman lift green cloth from the pot for the fortieth time, “why do you not simply buy the ready-made powders from the traders? They make blue in an hour. Everyone uses them now. You work for a week to make what they make before breakfast.”"}, {"id": "folk-grandmothers-indigo#s5", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "And the old woman would only smile her folded smile and say, “Their blue and my blue are called by the same name, child, the way a river and a puddle are both called water. Wait, and you will learn the difference. Wait, and the air will teach you.” But Sola did not want to wait. That is the whole trouble with this story, and with half the troubles of this world. Then came the hungry season."}, {"id": "folk-grandmothers-indigo#s6", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "You who have never known a true hungry season cannot imagine it, and I pray you never learn. The rains failed. The yams shrivelled in the ground like old men’s fingers. The market emptied. Money became a memory people spoke of the way they speak of a friend who has travelled far. And in that season, the quick traders with their quick powders did a roaring business, for a chief in a far city had ordered a great quantity of blue cloth for a festival, and he was paying, paying well, and everyone in the dyeing towns threw themselves at the work. The traders came to Iya Aro too. “Old mother,” they said, “use the powders this once. The chief needs the cloth in ten days. Your slow way cannot make enough in ten days, and your granddaughter is thin. Look at her. Do the quick blue, take the quick money, and feed the child.”"}, {"id": "folk-grandmothers-indigo#s7", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "And Sola, hungry, frightened, watching her grandmother’s steady blue hands, cried out, “Iya Agba, please. Just this once. Do it their way. I am so hungry.” The old woman was silent for a long time. Then she took her granddaughter’s face in her two blue hands and said, “Sola. Listen to me now as you have never listened. A hungry belly is a loud counsellor, and it gives bad advice in a loud voice. Trust me for ten days. Only ten. And if at the end of them we are worse off, I will never ask you to wait for anything again as long as I live.” Sola did not understand. But she loved her grandmother more than she trusted her own hunger, which is a rarer and finer thing than you know, and so she said yes."}, {"id": "folk-grandmothers-indigo#s8", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "And Iya Aro did not rush. While the whole town churned out quick powder-blue by the cartload, the old woman worked at her own slow pace, dipping and lifting, dipping and lifting, letting the green become blue in the breathing air, and she made only a small stack of cloth, so small that Sola wept to see it beside the mountains the others were making. But oh, my child, that blue. It was the blue of the indigo tree by the stream, the blue with a little silver in it. It was a blue you could fall into. On the tenth day the chief’s man came to buy. And here the story turns, as our stories love to turn, on a thing no one expected."}, {"id": "folk-grandmothers-indigo#s9", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "The quick powder-blue, it happened, had been made too fast, in too much of a hurry, by too many frightened hands. When the great pile of it was unrolled in the sun before the chief’s man, they all watched in horror as the colour ran, streaked, and faded before their eyes, blotching like a sickness, for a blue that is rushed has no roots in the cloth and cannot hold. Cartloads of it, ruined. The traders wailed. Their quick money turned to quick ruin, for the world had paid them fast, and now, just as fast, it took it all back."}, {"id": "folk-grandmothers-indigo#s10", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Then the chief’s man saw the small stack of Iya Aro’s cloth. He unrolled one length in the sun, and it did not run. It did not fade. It only deepened, glowing, as though it were drinking the light and giving it back richer. The man went very quiet. Then he asked how much she had. And when she told him it was only this small stack, he said, “Then name your price, old mother, for cloth like this comes into the world once in a lifetime, and a chief who wears it will be remembered a hundred years.”"}, {"id": "folk-grandmothers-indigo#s11", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "Iya Aro fed her granddaughter that night, and the next, and the year after, and the year after that, for the price she named, and the man paid gladly, was more than all the ruined cartloads together would have earned. The slow small blue had outweighed the fast great green, as the patient thing so often outweighs the hurried thing, once the air has had its say. But that is not the lesson, my child. Do not think the lesson is only about money, for money is the smallest thing in any true story. The lesson came later, when Sola was herself an old woman with blue hands to the elbow, teaching her own grandchild, and the child asked the same impatient question she herself had once asked: why not use the quick way?"}, {"id": "folk-grandmothers-indigo#s12", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "And old Sola took the child’s face in her two blue hands and said what her grandmother had said to her: “Wait, and the air will teach you. There is a green that every good thing must pass through on its way to becoming blue. The ones who cannot bear the green never see the blue. But you, my own, you will wait. And you will be the richer for every hour you did not hurry.”"}, {"id": "folk-grandmothers-indigo#s13", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So it is with cloth, and so it is with grief, and so it is with love, and so it is with wisdom, and so it is with you, sitting there wanting the story to reach its end faster than a story is willing to go. Every worthy thing has its green season, its ugly, patient, unripe middle, when nothing looks like it will ever come right. Do not run from the green. Hang it in the air of your patience. The blue is coming. The blue is always coming, for those who can bear to wait for it."}, {"id": "folk-grandmothers-indigo#s14", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "The fire has settled to embers now, good and red. Tomorrow, if the ancestors permit, I will take you to a market on a strange bright morning at a place called Oje, where something happened that the traders still swear by and the priests still argue over. But the green of tonight has become its blue, and it is time to rest."}]}, {"id": "folk-market-day-miracle-oje", "emoji": "📖", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#0a1a00,#1a3a00)", "category": "iwe", "rating": 9.6, "duration": "13 min", "title": {"en": "The Market Day Miracle at Oje", "yo": "Oje"}, "desc": {"en": "Honesty, a widow's small coin, and the strange arithmetic of generosity.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Folktales / Literary", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Aroso, a folktale of Yorubaland about honesty, a widow’s small coin, and the strange arithmetic of generosity)", "segments": [{"id": "folk-market-day-miracle-oje#s1", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Now sit close, because this next one is a market tale, and market tales are the liveliest of all, full of noise and haggling and the smell of smoked fish and the sudden hush when something happens that no one can explain. This is the tale of the market day miracle at Oje, and whether it is true, my child, I will let you decide, for the priests still argue over it and the traders still swear by it, and when the priests and the traders agree that a thing is worth arguing about, you know there is something in it."}, {"id": "folk-market-day-miracle-oje#s2", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Oje was, and is, one of the greatest markets in all Yorubaland. It met, in the old telling, every sixteen days, and on the eve of Oje market day the roads for a hundred miles around would fill with people carrying goods on their heads, driving goats, rolling barrels, singing to keep their feet moving through the dark. By the morning the great open ground beneath the ancient trees would be a living river of colour and voice, a place where you could buy anything under the sun, a needle or a horse or a secret or a curse, if you knew whom to ask."}, {"id": "folk-market-day-miracle-oje#s3", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "In this tale there was a widow named Yetunde, and she was poor even by the measure of the poor. Her husband had died leaving nothing but debts, and she had a small son, Kunle, who was often hungry, and she made her living by selling roasted groundnuts, wrapped in twists of leaf, walking up and down the great market from the first cool of morning to the last red of evening, crying her wares in a thin brave voice."}, {"id": "folk-market-day-miracle-oje#s4", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "On the day of this tale, Yetunde woke before dawn with only enough groundnuts left for one last basket, and only one small coin to her name, a single cowrie, worn smooth as a pebble. She roasted her groundnuts, she wrapped them in their little leaf twists, she left her sleeping son with the neighbour, and she walked to Oje with her basket on her head and her one coin knotted in the corner of her wrapper, and she prayed, as the poor pray, not for riches but only for enough. Only for enough to last another sixteen days. Now on that same morning, three other people were walking to Oje, and you must hold all four of them in your mind at once, the way a good storyteller holds four eggs, for the tale is going to juggle them."}, {"id": "folk-market-day-miracle-oje#s5", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "The first was a rich cloth merchant named Alhaji Bako, a man so wealthy he had forgotten the taste of hunger, and forgetting hunger, he had also forgotten mercy, for the two are more closely related than the rich like to admit. The second was a thief, a quick-fingered young man named Ganiyu, who came to Oje not to buy or sell but to take, sliding through the crowds like a fish through reeds. And the third was a stranger. No one knew the stranger. He was an old man in a plain white cloth, with dust on his feet as though he had walked a very long way, and eyes that seemed to look at each person a moment too long, as though he were reading something written on the inside of them."}, {"id": "folk-market-day-miracle-oje#s6", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "The market opened. The river of voices rose. And Yetunde walked her rounds, crying her groundnuts, and by the middle of the hot day she had sold almost her whole basket, twist by twist, cowrie by cowrie, until she had a small knotted handful of coins, enough, just enough, and her thin heart was light with the joy of enough. It was then that the thief Ganiyu, working the crowd, saw the knot in her wrapper and judged it an easy taking. He slid close, and with his quick fingers he cut the knot, and the widow’s whole day, her entire enough, dropped into his palm, and he melted back into the crowd before she even felt the lightness at her waist."}, {"id": "folk-market-day-miracle-oje#s7", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "When Yetunde reached for her coins and found them gone, she did not scream. The very poor often do not scream, my child; they have learned that screaming changes nothing and only entertains the cruel. She simply stood very still in the middle of the roaring market, and two tears ran down her dusty face, and she thought of her small hungry son, and she felt the world go grey."}, {"id": "folk-market-day-miracle-oje#s8", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "Now here is the first turn. The stranger in white had been watching. He had seen the thief’s hand and the widow’s tears, though he was too far to have stopped it. And he did a curious thing. He did not run after the thief. Instead he walked to the widow, and he bought from her the last twist of groundnuts in her basket, the one she had thought she would take home to her son, and he paid her with a coin. But when Yetunde looked at the coin in her palm, she gasped, for it was gold, a single piece of gold such as she had never held, worth more than she might earn in a whole year of walking and crying her wares. “Baba,” she stammered, “this is too much. This is far, far too much for one twist of groundnuts. I cannot take it. You have made a mistake.”"}, {"id": "folk-market-day-miracle-oje#s9", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "And the stranger looked at her with those eyes that read the inside of people, and he said, “It is not a mistake. But it comes with one condition. You must spend the whole of it, today, before the market closes, and you must spend it honestly. Do you agree?” And the widow, bewildered, agreed. Now watch, my child, how one honest coin travels through a market, for this is the true miracle, and it is a miracle you can work yourself, which is more than can be said for most."}, {"id": "folk-market-day-miracle-oje#s10", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "Yetunde took her gold and went first to the yam seller, an old man whose yams were good but whose trade was slow that day, and she bought yams for her son, more than she needed, and she said, “Sell the rest cheaply to the next hungry mother who comes, for I have been shown a kindness today and I would pass it on.” And the yam seller, moved, did as she asked, and the next hungry mother wept with relief. With her change she went to the cloth seller, not the rich Alhaji Bako but a struggling young weaver beside him, and she bought a length of good cloth to make her son a garment, and she paid the honest price and a little over, and the young weaver, who had sold nothing all day and had been about to weep, blessed her name."}, {"id": "folk-market-day-miracle-oje#s11", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "And so the coin broke into smaller coins, and the smaller coins ran out into the market like water finding every dry root, and everywhere the widow spent, she spent honestly and a little generously, and everywhere she spent, the sellers she blessed grew lighter of heart and, catching the feeling, became a little kinder to their next customer, and that kindness spread again, so that by the late afternoon there was a warmth moving through the market of Oje that no one could name but everyone could feel."}, {"id": "folk-market-day-miracle-oje#s12", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Now for the thief. Ganiyu, with the widow’s stolen coins heavy and hot in his pocket, had gone to buy palm wine to celebrate his cleverness. But the coins gave him no joy. Every mouthful of wine tasted of the widow’s grey still face. He had stolen many times and never felt this. And it happened, by the strange arithmetic of that day, that the palm wine seller he bought from was the very yam seller’s daughter, who told him, laughing with the joy of it, how a widow had come by that morning and shown such kindness, buying extra yams so a stranger could eat, all because someone had shown her kindness first."}, {"id": "folk-market-day-miracle-oje#s13", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "And Ganiyu understood, with a lurch of his whole soul, that the widow he had robbed was the very fountain of the goodness that was now flowing all around him and would not, would not, let him enjoy his wine. He was the one man in all of Oje standing outside the warmth, and he had put himself there. The thief found the widow near the close of market. He knelt in the dust before her, which is no small thing for a proud young man to do, and he pressed her stolen coins back into her hands, and more besides, all he had, saying, “Mother, forgive me. I took your day. I did not know I was taking it from the one person in this market too good to be robbed. Take it back, and take mine with it, only let me out of the cold I have put myself in.”"}, {"id": "folk-market-day-miracle-oje#s14", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "And Yetunde, who had already been given gold and had already given it all away and lost nothing, looked at the trembling thief and did the thing that made this a story worth a hundred years. She gave him back half. “Keep this,” she said. “A man with nothing steals. Start again with something, and steal no more.” And she closed his fingers over the coins with her own dusty hand."}, {"id": "folk-market-day-miracle-oje#s15", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "When the market closed and the great river of people flowed back out along the roads, the widow looked for the stranger in white to thank him, or to return what she had not managed to spend, though in truth she had spent it all and more. But he was gone. No one had seen him leave. Some said he was never there. But the yam seller had his gold, and the young weaver had his blessing, and the thief had his second chance, and the widow had her son fed for a year, and none of that was nothing."}, {"id": "folk-market-day-miracle-oje#s16", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "And the priests of Oje, when they heard the tale, said the stranger had been Orunmila himself, the orisa of wisdom, walking the market in a plain white cloth to see what people would do. And the traders said no, he was only a kind old man with more money than sense. And I say, my child, that it does not matter which, for the miracle was never the gold. The miracle was what one honest coin did as it ran through honest hands. The gold was only the seed. The generosity was the harvest, and any of us can plant that seed, on any market day, with any coin at all."}, {"id": "folk-market-day-miracle-oje#s17", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So remember, when someone shows you a kindness, that a kindness is not a thing to be kept, like a coin in a knot at your waist, where any thief may cut it loose. A kindness is a thing to be spent, quickly, honestly, and a little generously, and spent again by the one you spend it on, until the whole market of your life is warm with it and even the thieves cannot bear to stand outside. The embers are grey now, my child, and the roads of sleep are calling. Tomorrow, if we are spared, I will tell you of a prince who threw away a crown to pick up a hoe, and whether he was a fool or the wisest man in the kingdom. But the market of Oje has closed for tonight, and so must we."}]}, {"id": "folk-prince-became-farmer", "emoji": "📖", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#0a1a00,#1a3a00)", "category": "iwe", "rating": 9.6, "duration": "11 min", "title": {"en": "The Prince Who Became a Farmer", "yo": "The Prince Who Became a Farmer"}, "desc": {"en": "A crown laid down, a hoe taken up, and where true kingship lives.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Folktales / Literary", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Aroso, a folktale of Yorubaland about a crown laid down, a hoe taken up, and where true kingship really lives)", "segments": [{"id": "folk-prince-became-farmer#s1", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Come, the night is deep and the fire is good, and this is a tale for a deep-night fire, for it asks a question that has kept wiser heads than mine awake until the roosters crowed. The question is this: what is a king? And the answer, my child, is not the one you think, and by the end of this tale you may find you have to give up an idea you did not even know you were holding."}, {"id": "folk-prince-became-farmer#s2", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "There was once a kingdom, small but old, ruled by an oba who had three sons. The eldest was Adeyemi, whose name means the crown befits me, and he was everything a first son of a palace is raised to be, tall and proud and skilled with the sword, quick to command and slow to doubt. The second son was cunning and hungry for the throne. The third was too young to matter yet. And the old oba, feeling his years, began to think about which son should follow him, for among the Yoruba the crown does not always pass simply to the eldest; the kingmakers weigh a man, and Ifa is consulted, and the throne is given to the one who can carry it, not merely the one born first."}, {"id": "folk-prince-became-farmer#s3", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Now the old oba was wise, and he had watched his eldest son Adeyemi with a father’s love and a king’s cold eye, and he had seen something that troubled him. The boy could command, yes. He could win the loud loyalty of soldiers. But the oba had never once seen Adeyemi kneel, never seen him do a small thing for a small person, never seen him get his hands dirty in the actual soil of another’s need. And a king who has never served, thought the old man, is a king who does not know what he is the king of."}, {"id": "folk-prince-became-farmer#s4", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "So the old oba devised a test, and it was a hard one, and some said a cruel one, though the cruelest tests are often only the truth arriving early. He called Adeyemi and said, “My son, before you can wear the crown, you must go out from this palace and live for three full years as a common farmer in a distant village where no one knows your face. You will take no gold, no servant, no sword. You will work the land with your own hands and eat only what you grow. If at the end of three years you still wish to be king, come home, and the throne is yours. This is my word.”"}, {"id": "folk-prince-became-farmer#s5", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Adeyemi was furious, as you can imagine. To send a prince to grub in the dirt like a peasant. But he wanted the crown more than he hated the test, and so he swallowed his rage and went, telling himself he would endure the three years the way a soldier endures a siege, hating every day, changing nothing inside himself, and returning to take what was his and never touch a hoe again. He came to a poor village in the far hills, and he told them he was a traveller fallen on hard times, and they, being poor themselves and therefore generous, for it is so often the poor who share and the rich who hoard, gave him a corner of land to farm and a broken hut to sleep in and showed him, laughing at his softness, how to hold a hoe."}, {"id": "folk-prince-became-farmer#s6", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "The first year nearly killed his pride, and pride, my child, is the hardest thing in the world to kill; it has more lives than a bush rat. His hands blistered and bled. His crops failed, because he was too proud to ask how to plant and thought a prince’s cleverness would be enough. He went hungry. He, who had never known hunger, lay in his broken hut with his belly howling and cursed his father and cursed the village and cursed the very yams for refusing to grow for a man of royal blood."}, {"id": "folk-prince-became-farmer#s7", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "But somewhere in that first terrible year, something began to happen that Adeyemi did not notice, the way you do not notice a child growing until one day the clothes no longer fit. His neighbours, poor as they were, kept helping him. When his crops failed, an old farmer named Baba Iranlowo, whose name means the father who helps, shared his own small harvest without being asked, though he had little enough for himself. When Adeyemi fell sick with fever, the village women nursed him, a stranger, for nothing. And slowly, so slowly, the proud prince began to feel a thing he had never felt on any throne or battlefield: he began to feel that he owed something to people who had no power to make him pay."}, {"id": "folk-prince-became-farmer#s8", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "The second year he learned to farm. He swallowed his pride and asked Baba Iranlowo to teach him, truly teach him, and the old man did, and Adeyemi’s yams grew fat and his cassava spread green, and the day he harvested his first full crop with his own two hands he wept, and did not know exactly why, except that he had made a living thing come out of the ground by his own labour and his neighbours’ kindness, and it was the first thing in his whole life he had truly earned."}, {"id": "folk-prince-became-farmer#s9", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "The third year he became something more than a farmer. There came a hard season, a flood that washed out the low fields, and hunger stalked the village. And Adeyemi, who now had a good harvest stored, did a thing that would have been unthinkable to the prince who arrived. He opened his store and shared it. He, who had cursed old Baba Iranlowo for sharing, now understood why the old man had done it, and he did the same, and more, for he had learned to organize and to lead, and he used those palace-taught gifts not to command but to serve, rallying the village to share fairly, to rebuild the washed-out fields, to see that no child went to sleep with an empty belly. And the village, who had known him as a soft proud stranger, came to love him as the truest man among them."}, {"id": "folk-prince-became-farmer#s10", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "At the end of the three years, a messenger came from the palace, as the old oba had arranged, to bring the prince home to take the crown. And here, my child, is the turn on which the whole tale rests, so lean close. Adeyemi stood at the edge of the village he had come to hating and had come to love, and the messenger held out the fine robe and the sandals of a prince, and Adeyemi looked at them, and then he looked back at the huts, at Baba Iranlowo grown very old now, at the fields he had planted, at the children who called him uncle, and he found that his heart was split clean in two."}, {"id": "folk-prince-became-farmer#s11", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "He went home. He did not refuse the crown, for that would have been its own kind of pride, the pride that pretends to be humility. But when he stood before his father and the kingmakers and the assembled town, the old oba asked him the question of the test: “My son, after three years, do you still wish to be king?”"}, {"id": "folk-prince-became-farmer#s12", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "And Adeyemi answered, “Father, I wish it, but not as I once wished it. I came to your test wanting the crown so that people would serve me. I return knowing that the crown is only worth wearing so that I may serve them. If you give me the throne, I will sit on it as I stood in that village: with my hands ready to get dirty, my store ready to open, and my ear bent low to the smallest person in the kingdom. And if that is not the kind of king you want, then give the crown to another, and give me back my hoe, for I have learned I can be happy with it.”"}, {"id": "folk-prince-became-farmer#s13", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "The old oba wept then, for the second son had wanted the throne to be served, and only the eldest, broken and remade in the far hills, had learned that the throne exists to serve. And the kingmakers looked at one another, and Ifa was consulted, and the crown was placed on Adeyemi’s head. And it is said he was the greatest oba that kingdom ever knew, for he ruled with the hands of a farmer and the heart of a man who had once been hungry, and in all his long reign no child in his kingdom ever slept with an empty belly if he could prevent it."}, {"id": "folk-prince-became-farmer#s14", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "But do you know what they say he kept, all his years, hung on the wall behind his throne where any visitor could see it? His old hoe from the village. The broken-handled hoe he had learned to farm with. And when foreign kings came in their gold and asked him, laughing, why a great oba kept a peasant’s tool where a lesser king would hang a sword, Adeyemi would say, “That is the tool that made me a king. The crown only confirmed it.”"}, {"id": "folk-prince-became-farmer#s15", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So when you hear people speak of greatness, my child, and imagine it is a matter of who commands and who is obeyed, who is served and who serves, remember the prince who became a farmer and was only then fit to become a king. True kingship is not a chair you sit in above others. It is a hoe you take up alongside them. The one who has never served does not know what he would be the king of. And the smallest person, faithfully served, is worth more than the loudest crowd, obeying."}, {"id": "folk-prince-became-farmer#s16", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "The fire is nearly out, and my old bones say it is time. Tomorrow, if the ancestors are willing, I will tell you the last of these tales for a while, the story of two brothers and a bag of wishes, and how the same gift can be a blessing in one hand and a ruin in the other. But the prince has hung up his hoe for the night, and so must we lay down ours."}]}, {"id": "folk-two-brothers-bag-of-wishes", "emoji": "📖", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#0a1a00,#1a3a00)", "category": "iwe", "rating": 9.6, "duration": "11 min", "title": {"en": "Two Brothers and the Bag of Wishes", "yo": "Two Brothers and the Bag of Wishes"}, "desc": {"en": "Two hearts, one gift, and the truth that a wish reveals more than it grants.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Folktales / Literary", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Aroso, a folktale of Yorubaland about two hearts, one gift, and the truth that a wish reveals more than it grants)", "segments": [{"id": "folk-two-brothers-bag-of-wishes#s1", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "This is the last of the fireside tales I will tell you for a while, my child, so let it settle deep, for it is the kind of story you will understand differently at every age of your life, the way you see new things in a river each time you cross it. It is the tale of two brothers and a bag of wishes, and it teaches the oldest lesson there is, which is that a gift does not change a person, it only reveals the person who was there all along, waiting to be seen."}, {"id": "folk-two-brothers-bag-of-wishes#s2", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "There were once two brothers, born of the same mother, raised at the same fire, fed from the same pot. The elder was named Taiwo and the younger Kehinde, as is proper among us for the first and second of a certain birth. And though they came from the same root, they had grown into two very different trees. Taiwo, the elder, was open-handed and warm, but careless, a man who gave freely and thought little. Kehinde, the younger, was careful and clever, but tight-fisted, a man who counted everything twice and gave nothing without weighing what he would get in return. Neither was wholly good nor wholly bad, my child, for real people never are, and this is a tale about real people wearing the thin cloth of a folktale."}, {"id": "folk-two-brothers-bag-of-wishes#s3", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "The brothers were farmers, and one dry season, when the work was slow, they went together into the deep forest to gather firewood and honey. And in a part of the forest where they had never been, in the hollow of a tree so old it must have been standing when the first oba was crowned, they found a bag."}, {"id": "folk-two-brothers-bag-of-wishes#s4", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "It was a plain thing, a small leather bag, worn and old, but when Taiwo picked it up, a voice came from it, dry and ancient as the tree itself, and it said: “You have found the bag of wishes. I hold three wishes, and no more, to be shared between the two of you as you decide. But hear my warning, for every bag of wishes carries one. I do not grant what you say. I grant what you are. Speak your wishes, and they shall be filled according to the true shape of the heart that speaks them. Choose well, for the bag closes at sunset and will never open for you again.” The two brothers sat down beneath the ancient tree to decide, and at once you could see the two different trees they had grown into, standing in the shade of the one old tree that had held the gift."}, {"id": "folk-two-brothers-bag-of-wishes#s5", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Kehinde, the careful one, wanted to divide the wishes cleanly. “There are three wishes and two of us,” he said, already counting. “It does not divide evenly. So let us say I take two, being the cleverer, and you take one, being the elder and needing less, since you give everything away regardless.” And he smiled the smile of a man who thinks he has been fair because he has been quick."}, {"id": "folk-two-brothers-bag-of-wishes#s6", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Taiwo, the open-handed one, only laughed and said, “Take all three if you like, brother. I have enough. What would I do with wishes? I have my farm and my two hands and my belly is full more days than not.” And he meant it, which was his virtue, but he also had not thought for one moment about their old mother at home, or the poor of their village, or anything at all beyond his own easy contentment, which was his fault, for carelessness is a fault even when it wears the clothing of generosity. Now remember the bag’s warning, my child. I do not grant what you say. I grant what you are."}, {"id": "folk-two-brothers-bag-of-wishes#s7", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "Kehinde, greedy for the most and the best, made his first wish, and he made it carefully, cleverly, wishing for a great heap of gold, enough to make him the richest man in the whole region. And the bag granted it according to the shape of his heart. The gold appeared, yes, a great gleaming heap of it, right there in the forest clearing. But because Kehinde’s heart was a closed fist that could hold but never open, the gold came with the exact nature of his own soul: it was heavy beyond moving. He could not lift a single coin. He could not carry it home. He could not share it, for he would not, and he could not spend it, for he could not move it, and there it sat, a fortune he could only crouch beside and guard, exactly as his tight heart had always crouched over and guarded every small thing it owned. The bag had granted him not gold, but the truth of himself in gold: a wealth that could not be enjoyed, only hoarded, uselessly, forever."}, {"id": "folk-two-brothers-bag-of-wishes#s8", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "Kehinde wailed and clutched at the coins and could not move them, and in his panic he made his second wish, quick and furious, wishing simply to be able to carry his gold. And the bag, granting what he was and not what he said, made him strong enough to carry the gold, yes, but it made the wanting of it grow in him at the same terrible rate, so that the more he could carry the more he needed to have, and he found himself scrabbling to gather every last coin, then digging for more that was not there, never satisfied, his hunger growing exactly as fast as his strength, which is the true and secret curse of all greed, that it can never, ever, arrive at enough. Two wishes gone, both turned to ash in the taking, and Kehinde crouched weeping over a fortune that was slowly, you might say, eating him alive. One wish remained."}, {"id": "folk-two-brothers-bag-of-wishes#s9", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "And now Taiwo, the careless elder brother, who had wanted nothing and asked for nothing, looked at his younger brother’s suffering, and here at last his true heart showed itself, and it showed itself better than his careless words had promised, for that is the other half of the bag’s lesson: sometimes a person is better than they seem, just as Kehinde was worse."}, {"id": "folk-two-brothers-bag-of-wishes#s10", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Taiwo did not use the last wish for gold, or for power, or for himself at all. He looked at his brother trapped in the prison of his own hunger, and he was moved, not by cleverness, for he had none, but by love, which he had in careless abundance. And he said to the bag, “My last wish is this: take back the gold, all of it, and with it take back the hunger it has planted in my brother, and let him be as he was before he found this bag, only, if you can, let him remember what he felt when he could not lift the gold, so that the memory may soften him. That is all I wish. I want nothing for myself.”"}, {"id": "folk-two-brothers-bag-of-wishes#s11", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "And the bag was silent for a long moment, my child, as though even an ancient spirit could be surprised. Then it said, “In all the ages I have granted wishes to the greedy and the frightened and the proud, none has ever spent his only wish to unmake another’s ruin and ask nothing back. You have wished according to the true shape of your heart, and your heart, careless as it is, is open all the way to the bottom. So it shall be. And because you asked nothing for yourself, hear this: the one who wishes only for another’s good has already got the one thing worth wishing for, and cannot be made poorer by any gold or richer by any wish. You were rich when you walked in. You simply did not know it until now.”"}, {"id": "folk-two-brothers-bag-of-wishes#s12", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "The gold vanished. The terrible hunger drained out of Kehinde like water from a cracked pot, and he came back to himself, gasping, freed, and he looked at his brother who had spent everything to save him and had asked for nothing, and Kehinde wept, and the memory the bag had left him stayed in him like a small good scar, so that from that day he was never again quite the closed fist he had been. The two brothers walked home through the darkening forest with empty hands and, for the first time in their lives, matched hearts, for Taiwo’s carelessness had been burned into true generosity by the choosing, and Kehinde’s greed had been cracked open by the losing, and each had become, through the same gift, a better version of the tree he already was."}, {"id": "folk-two-brothers-bag-of-wishes#s13", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "And when their old mother asked what they had found in the forest, they looked at each other and said only, “Firewood, mother. And a lesson.” And they never told anyone else the tale, for a while, and it came down to us only because Kehinde, grown old and gentle and open-handed at last, told it to his own grandchildren by a fire much like this one, saying always at the end the words the bag had said to his brother, which he had made the rule of his whole changed life: the one who wishes only for another’s good has already got the one thing worth wishing for."}, {"id": "folk-two-brothers-bag-of-wishes#s14", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So there it is, my child, the last of these tales. When you dream of what you would ask for, if ever a bag of wishes fell into your two hands, remember the warning that no wish can escape: you will not be granted what you say, but what you are. A wish does not add to a person. It reveals them, the way lifting a lamp reveals what was always standing in the dark corner of the room. So do not spend your life polishing your wishes. Spend it shaping your heart, for it is the heart, and not the wish, that decides whether gold becomes a blessing or a chain."}, {"id": "folk-two-brothers-bag-of-wishes#s15", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "And with that, the fire is out, and the tales are done for now. Rest well. When the season turns and we gather again, I will not tell you folktales but something older still: the doings of the orisa, the great ones, the shining ones, who walked the earth before the tales began. But that, my child, is for another and a greater night. Sleep now."}]}, {"id": "folk-spider-weaves-at-night", "emoji": "📖", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#0a1a00,#1a3a00)", "category": "iwe", "rating": 9.4, "duration": "4 min", "title": {"en": "Why the Spider Weaves at Night", "yo": "Why the Spider Weaves at Night"}, "desc": {"en": "Why the spider does its weaving at night.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Folktales / Literary", "secondary": [], "source_note": "", "segments": [{"id": "folk-spider-weaves-at-night#s1", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Long before the first human being opened their eyes to the world, the creatures of the earth had their own councils and their own mysteries. Chief among the quiet ones — the watchers, the builders — was Spider. Not the flashy kind of creature. No bright feathers, no thunderous roar. Just eight careful legs and a mind that never stopped working. Spider had a gift from Olodumare at the beginning of things: the ability to make something from nothing. From her own body she drew thread, and from thread she made structures of impossible delicacy. She could bridge two leaves across a stream. She could build a home that held morning dew like jewels in a net. She was, in her way, as close to the divine act of creation as any animal could be."}, {"id": "folk-spider-weaves-at-night#s2", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "But Spider had an enemy: the sun. Not because the sun was cruel — the sun shone equally on all things — but because daylight brought the birds. And birds, as Spider had learned through bitter experience, had sharp eyes and sharper beaks. A web built in the morning was often destroyed by noon. A web built in the afternoon rarely survived till evening. So Spider went to Ogun, the god of iron and craft, and asked for counsel. Ogun listened — he was a practical deity, not given to long speeches — and told her simply: \"Work when your enemies sleep.\" It was not a glamorous answer. But it was the right one."}, {"id": "folk-spider-weaves-at-night#s3", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "And so Spider moved her work to the night. While everything else slept, she built. Her webs caught the moonlight and the dew. They survived till morning, and even the birds, when they woke and saw the glistening structures, sometimes paused in admiration before they destroyed them. The lesson Spider learned is the one that the Yoruba elders have passed down: do not waste your gifts fighting the conditions. Change the conditions. And if you cannot change them, find the hour when they work in your favour. Spider still weaves at night. And her webs are still the most beautiful things the morning finds."}]}, {"id": "folk-boy-spoke-to-rivers", "emoji": "📖", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#0a1a00,#1a3a00)", "category": "iwe", "rating": 9.4, "duration": "4 min", "title": {"en": "The Boy Who Spoke to Rivers", "yo": "The Boy Who Spoke to Rivers"}, "desc": {"en": "The boy who could speak to rivers.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Folktales / Literary", "secondary": [], "source_note": "", "segments": [{"id": "folk-boy-spoke-to-rivers#s1", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "His full name was Oluwafemi Adeyemi, but the village called him simply Omi — water — because from the time he could walk, rivers seemed to call to him in a way they did not call to other children. He would sit at the bank of the Osun for hours, head tilted, lips moving slightly, as if in conversation. His mother thought it was harmless daydreaming. His grandfather suspected something more. It was the grandfather, old Baba Adeyemi, who first asked the boy directly: \"What does the river say?\" And Femi — he preferred Femi to Omi — answered without embarrassment: \"It says the rains are coming three days before the priests expect them. And it says the bridge on the north path has a weak stone that will break if the blacksmith's cart passes over it.\""}, {"id": "folk-boy-spoke-to-rivers#s2", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "Baba told the priests. They were sceptical — what does a child know of rain? But they checked the bridge, and the weak stone was real. The cart was rerouted. The bridge held for another season. After that, people stopped dismissing Femi. The river, he explained when pressed, didn't speak in words exactly. It spoke in the way light moves through water, in the rhythm of current against stone, in the way fish cluster before a storm. He had simply learned to read what was there. Anyone could, he thought, if they were patient enough to listen."}, {"id": "folk-boy-spoke-to-rivers#s3", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "As he grew older, Femi became an apprentice to the Osun shrine — not as a devotee, exactly, but as something between a guardian and an interpreter. The priests, who were learned in their own ways, found they needed him whenever the river's behaviour became unusual: too high, too still, too discoloured. He would go to the bank, sit quietly, and after a time come back with answers. He never claimed magic. He claimed only attention. \"The world speaks constantly,\" he said once, to a younger boy who wanted to learn. \"We are simply very bad at listening.\" The boy asked how to get better. Femi pointed at the river. \"Start there,\" he said. \"And be patient. Rivers are generous teachers, but they move at their own pace.\""}]}, {"id": "folk-esu-painted-hat", "emoji": "📖", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#0a1a00,#1a3a00)", "category": "iwe", "rating": 9.4, "duration": "4 min", "title": {"en": "Esu and the Painted Hat: A Story About Perspective", "yo": "Esu"}, "desc": {"en": "Esu's two-coloured hat sets two friends quarrelling: a lesson that where you stand decides what you see.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Folktales / Literary", "secondary": [], "source_note": "", "segments": [{"id": "folk-esu-painted-hat#s1", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Esu Elegbara does not play fair, and he will tell you so himself. He is the Orisa of crossroads, of communication, of the gap between intention and outcome — and his favourite teaching tool is disruption. He does not make life difficult because he is malicious. He makes it difficult because comfort makes people stop paying attention, and Esu has no patience for inattention. The story of the painted hat is perhaps his most famous teaching story, and it travels well. Two friends — lifelong companions who have farmed side by side for years — are working their fields when a figure walks down the path between them. The figure wears a striking hat. One farmer sees it clearly: the hat is red. The other farmer sees it equally clearly: the hat is white. They argue. The argument becomes heated. A lifelong friendship begins to strain at the seams."}, {"id": "folk-esu-painted-hat#s2", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "The figure — who is, of course, Esu — turns around and comes back. Now each farmer sees what the other had described. The hat is red on one side and white on the other. Esu smiles — not unkindly, but with the satisfaction of a teacher whose lesson has landed. \"You were both right,\" he says. \"And both wrong. You saw from where you stood. But you forgot that your friend was standing somewhere else.\" The story is deceptively simple, but Esu does not trade in simple things. The lesson is not merely that \"everyone has a perspective.\" It is sharper than that. It is that you can be completely confident in your own perception and still be missing half the picture. This is why Esu stands at crossroads: the crossroads is the place where perspectives intersect, where what seemed obvious from one direction suddenly becomes complicated."}, {"id": "folk-esu-painted-hat#s3", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "He is called a trickster in the literature. The word is a rough translation, and it flattens something more complex. Esu is a teacher who will not make it easy for you. He is the universe's refusal to be simple. And his lesson — that reality is bigger than any one vantage point — is perhaps the most important lesson that a human being can learn."}]}, {"id": "folk-ogboju-ode", "emoji": "📖", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#0a1a00,#1a3a00)", "category": "iwe", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "8 min", "title": {"en": "Ogboju Ode ni Igbo Irunmale: Freedom and Forest", "yo": "Ogboju Ode ninu Igbo Irunmale"}, "desc": {"en": "Fagunwa's great hunter's tale of Akara-ogun in the Forest of a Thousand Spirits, and why the free hunter meant so much to a people menaced by the slave roads.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Folktales / Literary", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Ode, consolidates “Ogboju Ode ni Igbo Irunmale: Freedom and Forest”)", "segments": [{"id": "folk-ogboju-ode#s1", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Gather close, my children, and let the forest come alive around this fire, because tonight I take you into the deep bush, into Igbo Irunmale, the Forest of a Thousand Spirits, where the brave hunter goes and where a whole people learned to speak of freedom in the language of the wild. This is a story about a story, for our people gave the world a great book about a hunter who journeyed into that forest, and I will tell you both the tale itself and the deeper thing it carries, which is why, in the years after the trade and the wars, the Yoruba imagination turned so hungrily toward the figure of the hunter alone in the forest, master of himself, answerable to no chain. To understand that hunger, you must first understand what the forest meant to a people who had watched the open roads turn into roads of captivity."}, {"id": "folk-ogboju-ode#s2", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "First, the tale, for it is one of the treasures of our tongue. In the days when a man called Daniel Fagunwa took up his pen, in the years before the middle of the last century, he set down in Yoruba a book whose name means the brave hunter in the forest of the thousand spirits, and it is counted the first great novel written in our language. But do not imagine Fagunwa invented it out of nothing, for he was doing what griots have always done, gathering the old hunters’ tales, the ones told at night in the hunters’ guild and around fires like this one, and weaving them into a single glittering journey. The book tells of Akara-ogun, whose name means compound of charms, a hunter born of a strange and gifted father and a mother who was a witch, a man who carries the old world in his very blood."}, {"id": "folk-ogboju-ode#s3", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Akara-ogun goes into the forest of the thousand spirits, and what a forest it is, my children. It is no ordinary bush. It is the whole invisible world made into trees and shadow. In it he meets Agbako, the embodiment of trouble and disaster, a creature so terrible that to describe him is to shiver, with limbs where limbs should not be and a single eye and a hunger for the ruin of men. He meets the ghommid folk, the spirits of the wild, some helpful and some deadly. He meets a king of the forest, and a woman named Helpmeet who becomes his companion, and dewdrops and demons and talking beasts and temptations without number. He journeys to a place called Mount Langbodo, the far city at the edge of the spirit world, on a mission that will test everything he is."}, {"id": "folk-ogboju-ode#s4", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "And through all these wonders, my children, notice what the hunter carries and what he does not. He carries no master. He goes into the forest alone, or with companions he has chosen, and every trial he meets he meets as a free man, by his own courage, his own cunning, his own charms and prayers and strength. When Agbako seizes him, no one will free him but himself. When temptation offers him ease, no owner commands his choice, he must choose. The whole shape of the hunter’s tale is the shape of a self that belongs to itself, tested to the utmost, and answerable only to its own courage and its own gods. And that, my children, is precisely why our people loved it so, in the century when the roads had become dangerous and the open country had become a place where a person could be seized and sold. The forest, terrible as it was, was the one place the slave-catcher’s logic did not rule. In the forest, a man was measured by his own worth, not priced by a dealer’s hand."}, {"id": "folk-ogboju-ode#s5", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "Think on this deeply, for it is the marrow of tonight’s telling. When the wars raged and the trade fed on captives, the open savannah roads, once the arteries of proud commerce, became roads of dread, for on them the coffles moved and on them a traveller might be taken. But the forest hunter, the ode, moved through country no coffle could cross, knew paths no raider could follow, lived by skills no market could buy. The hunter became, in the wounded imagination of the people, the very image of the free man, the one who could not be caught, the one who answered to himself."}, {"id": "folk-ogboju-ode#s6", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "And there is more in the hunter’s tale than escape, for that alone would make it only a story of running away. The forest of a thousand spirits is also a place of testing, where a person discovers what they are truly made of. Akara-ogun does not merely survive the forest, he is refined by it. He learns wisdom from the spirits who help him and hard lessons from the ones who trick him. He learns that courage without wisdom is folly, that greed is the doorway through which the demons enter, that a companion chosen well is worth more than any charm, that even the bravest hunter must sometimes bow his head and pray. The journey to Mount Langbodo is not only a physical road, it is the road of becoming a full and worthy person, and the sage who waits at the end sends the travellers home not with treasure but with wisdom, with counsel on how to live and how to govern and how to hold a community together. The tale that begins as an adventure ends as a teaching on how to be good."}, {"id": "folk-ogboju-ode#s7", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "Do you see now why our people treasured this so, and why Fagunwa’s book became a foundation of our written literature, and why the young Wole Soyinka would one day carry it across into English so the whole world could walk that forest? It is because in the figure of the brave hunter, our people found a way to speak of everything the trade had threatened. Freedom, in the hunter who answers to no master. Dignity, in the man measured by his courage and not his price. Wisdom, in the journey that makes a boy into a sage. Community, in the counsel he brings home from the mountain. The forest that the slave-catcher feared, the Yoruba storyteller claimed, and made it the very throne room of the free and testing spirit."}, {"id": "folk-ogboju-ode#s8", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So when you walk past a stretch of bush and feel the old town-dweller’s unease at the wildness of it, remember Akara-ogun, and remember what the forest meant to your ancestors in their hardest century. It was danger, yes. But it was also the country of the unconquered, the place where a person belonged to themselves, the green cathedral of Yoruba freedom. Our people did not only mourn what the trade took. They also imagined, in glorious and terrifying detail, a world where the human spirit went armed and free through a forest of a thousand spirits and came home wiser than it went. That is the story of the brave hunter. Sleep now, and when you dream, do not dream of the coffle on the open road. Dream of the ode in the deep forest, his charms about his neck, his courage in his chest, answerable to no one, walking free."}]}, {"id": "folk-fagunwa", "emoji": "📖", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#0a1a00,#1a3a00)", "category": "iwe", "rating": 9.4, "duration": "4 min", "title": {"en": "D.O. Fagunwa: The Father of Yoruba Literature", "yo": "D.O. Fagunwa"}, "desc": {"en": "D.O. Fagunwa, the father of Yoruba literature.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Folktales / Literary", "secondary": [], "source_note": "", "segments": [{"id": "folk-fagunwa#s1", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Narrator opens: Before there was Gabriel Garcia Marquez, before there was Toni Morrison, before magical realism had been named and celebrated in Western literary circles, Daniel Olorunfemi Fagunwa was writing in Yoruba — and in his prose, the forest was alive, the mountains had names and personalities, the spirits walked among men, and death was not an end but a doorway. Fagunwa was born around 1903 in Oke-Igbo, in what is now Osun State. He was educated in mission schools and became a teacher. He wrote in Yoruba — only in Yoruba, always in Yoruba — and produced six novels that are among the most extraordinary works of African literature in any language."}, {"id": "folk-fagunwa#s2", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "His first novel, Ogboju Ode Ninu Igbo Irunmale — published in 1938, translated into English by Wole Soyinka as \"The Forest of a Thousand Daemons\" — tells the story of Akara-ogun, a great hunter who enters a fearful forest and encounters a series of supernatural beings. The forest — Igbo Irunmale — is not merely a setting. It is a world unto itself, populated by creatures of extraordinary variety: some monstrous, some wise, some terrifying, some heartbreakingly beautiful. Akara-ogun must navigate this world with courage, with wit, and with the protection of Ogun, the deity of iron and hunters."}, {"id": "folk-fagunwa#s3", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "The novel works on multiple levels simultaneously. It is an adventure story — thrilling and episodic, each chapter a new encounter. It is a moral tale — each encounter teaches something about how to live. It is a cultural encyclopaedia — the creatures of the forest, the prayers and incantations, the ethical codes of the hunter's world, all reflect actual Yoruba spiritual and cultural traditions. And it is a masterpiece of prose style — Fagunwa's Yoruba is of a richness and musicality that translators have consistently struggled to capture."}, {"id": "folk-fagunwa#s4", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Wole Soyinka, who translated the first novel and was deeply influenced by Fagunwa, has said that encountering Fagunwa's work was like encountering his own cultural inheritance made fully conscious. That is exactly what Fagunwa achieved: he took the oral traditions of the Yoruba — the folktales, the hunter's lore, the Orisa cosmology, the proverbs and praise songs — and transformed them into prose literature, giving them the permanence of the written word while preserving their oral energy. His five subsequent novels — Igbo Olodumare (The Forest of God), Ireke-Onibudo (The Sugarcane of the Keeper), Igbo Elegbeje (The Forest of Four Hundred Demons), Irinkerindo Ninu Igbo Elegbeje (Expedition in the Forest of Four Hundred Demons), and Adiitu Olodumare (The Mystery of God) — continued in the same vein, each a forest adventure, each populated by different supernatural encounters, each teaching through story rather than lecturing through direct moralizing."}, {"id": "folk-fagunwa#s5", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "Fagunwa died in 1963, drowned in the Niger River — a death that seems almost literary in its symbolism, a great teller of stories claimed by the water that runs through all of Yoruba cosmology. He was 60 years old. His six novels had established a tradition that would shape every subsequent Yoruba writer. His influence extends beyond Yoruba literature. Amos Tutuola — who adapted some of Fagunwa's forest world into English in \"The Palm-Wine Drinkard\" — brought aspects of the tradition to international attention. But Tutuola was, in important ways, a popularizer of what Fagunwa had created. The depth is in Fagunwa's Yoruba originals."}, {"id": "folk-fagunwa#s6", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "To truly understand Yoruba literary culture — its richness, its depth, its supernatural imagination — you must read Fagunwa. And if you read him in Yoruba, with the tones and the musicality intact, you will understand something that no translation fully captures: that this is a literary tradition of world-class importance, that the Yoruba people's relationship with language is not merely communicative but aesthetic, not merely functional but sacred. The forest calls. Will you enter?"}]}, {"id": "folk-tutuola", "emoji": "📖", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#0a1a00,#1a3a00)", "category": "iwe", "rating": 9.4, "duration": "4 min", "title": {"en": "Amos Tutuola: The Palm-Wine Drinkard and the World That Saw Him", "yo": "Amos Tutuola"}, "desc": {"en": "Amos Tutuola, The Palm-Wine Drinkard, and the bewildered world that read him.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Folktales / Literary", "secondary": [], "source_note": "", "segments": [{"id": "folk-tutuola#s1", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Narrator opens: In 1952, a Yoruba man who had not completed secondary school, who worked as a government messenger, who had written a manuscript in English that his Yoruba-speaking friends found strange and his English-speaking acquaintances found stranger — sent that manuscript to the publishing house of Faber and Faber in London. They published it. The international literary world went mad with admiration. And a man named Amos Tutuola became one of the most discussed African writers of the 20th century — celebrated by Dylan Thomas in London, analyzed by scholars in Paris, debated in Lagos. Tutuola was born in 1920 in Abeokuta, to a Yoruba family with roots in the Ekiti region. He was educated in mission schools but did not progress beyond standard six before circumstances forced him to leave school. He worked various jobs before finding employment as a government messenger in Lagos. In his spare time, he wrote."}, {"id": "folk-tutuola#s2", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "\"The Palm-Wine Drinkard and His Dead Palm-Wine Tapster in the Deads' Town\" — to give the novel its full and marvelous title — is the story of a man who drinks palm wine at a rate that would alarm even the most committed celebrant. When his palm-wine tapster dies, the Drinkard embarks on a journey to the Deads' Town — the land of the dead — to bring him back. Along the way, he encounters creatures, situations, and transformations of extraordinary strangeness. The novel is written in what critics have called \"bizarre English\" — a creative blend of formal English, Yoruba sentence structures, Yoruba folk tale conventions, and Tutuola's own idiosyncratic imagination. Sentences run in ways that English grammar does not sanction. Creatures do things that physics does not permit. The narrative leaps between episodes with the logic of a dream rather than a plot."}, {"id": "folk-tutuola#s3", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "This was, for many Western readers, exactly what made it brilliant. Tutuola's \"Yorubaglish\" was not a failure of English — it was a creative construction of something new, a language that could carry the freight of Yoruba oral tradition into written English. Critics like Dylan Thomas recognized immediately that this was not a naive production but a sophisticated one — naive in its apparent simplicity, profoundly sophisticated in its cultural depth. But in Nigeria, the initial reception was more complicated. Educated Yoruba readers were embarrassed by Tutuola's English — they felt it reflected poorly on Nigerian literacy, that it would confirm Western stereotypes of African linguistic incompetence. There was a fierce debate in Nigerian newspapers about whether Tutuola should have been celebrated or corrected."}, {"id": "folk-tutuola#s4", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "This debate reveals something important: the question of how Africa presents itself to the world, of who gets to tell African stories and in what language, was — and remains — deeply contested. Tutuola, who was writing from genuine experience and genuine imagination without concern for how he was perceived, succeeded precisely because he was not performing for anyone's approval. His Palm-Wine Drinkard is what Yoruba oral culture sounds like when it pours through an imperfect but genuine vessel. Subsequent novels — \"My Life in the Bush of Ghosts\" (1954), \"Simbi and the Satyr of the Dark Jungle\" (1955), \"The Brave African Huntress\" (1958) — continued in the same vein. Each is a forest or spirit-world adventure. Each is unmistakably Yoruba in its cultural DNA and unmistakably Tutuola in its wild, careening energy."}, {"id": "folk-tutuola#s5", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "He died in 1997, in Ibadan. By then, he had been translated into more than a dozen languages and was recognized as one of the foundational figures of African literature in English. The embarrassment of the early Nigerian reception had given way to pride. He was claimed, properly, as a genius. The Palm-Wine Drinkard. A Yoruba man talking to the dead, seeking to bring back what was lost — and in the seeking, creating something that the world had never seen before."}]}, {"id": "folk-faleti", "emoji": "📖", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#0a1a00,#1a3a00)", "category": "iwe", "rating": 9.4, "duration": "4 min", "title": {"en": "Adebayo Faleti: The Voice of Yoruba Literary Journalism", "yo": "Adebayo Faleti"}, "desc": {"en": "Adebayo Faleti, voice of the Yoruba language in literature, journalism, and broadcasting.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Folktales / Literary", "secondary": [], "source_note": "", "segments": [{"id": "folk-faleti#s1", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Narrator opens: There are those who write, and there are those who speak, and there are those in whom writing and speaking become the same thing — whose written word carries the rhythm of a voice, and whose spoken word has the precision of a carefully chosen text. Adebayo Faleti was one of these people. And for generations of Yoruba speakers who grew up listening to the radio, his voice was the sound of the Yoruba language at its most beautiful — composed, musical, full of the kind of deliberate pleasure in words that reminds you why language is something to be treasured rather than merely used."}, {"id": "folk-faleti#s2", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Faleti was born in 1921 in Oyo State and died in 2017, aged 95 — one of the last of the great generation of Yoruba literary and cultural figures who built the tradition in the colonial and immediate post-independence period. He worked for the Nigerian Broadcasting Corporation, where he became one of the most prominent Yoruba-language broadcasters. He wrote plays, he wrote poetry, he was deeply involved in the Yoruba theater tradition. But his most lasting contribution may be the Yoruba dubbing of \"Mosebolatan\" — the Yoruba translation and adaptation of a dramatic performance that brought the Yoruba theater tradition to new audiences. Faleti's Yoruba was prized for its purity and its precision — he was deeply conversant with classical Yoruba, with orikì, with proverb, and he used this knowledge to enrich everything he wrote and spoke."}, {"id": "folk-faleti#s3", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "He is also remembered for \"Omo Olokun Esin\" — one of the significant Yoruba-language novels of the later 20th century — and for his contributions to the development of Yoruba orthography: the systematic rules for writing Yoruba with all its tones and special characters. Faleti lived to see the Yoruba language transition from a primarily oral tradition, through the first generation of writers, through the development of Yoruba-language broadcasting and theater, and into the digital age. He witnessed the entire arc of Yoruba's transition from voice to page to screen. And he was proud of it. He said, in various interviews over his long life, that the Yoruba language was one of the great languages of the world — and that the Yoruba people had an obligation to develop it, to teach it, to insist on its vitality in a world that constantly pressured African languages toward extinction."}, {"id": "folk-faleti#s4", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "The voice that spoke Yoruba beautifully. The man who insisted that beauty in language is not vanity but duty."}]}, {"id": "folk-isola", "emoji": "📖", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#0a1a00,#1a3a00)", "category": "iwe", "rating": 9.4, "duration": "4 min", "title": {"en": "Akinwumi Isola: The Historian Who Wrote Stories", "yo": "Akinwumi Isola"}, "desc": {"en": "Akinwumi Isola, the historian-storyteller who gave Yoruba women a voice in literature.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Folktales / Literary", "secondary": [], "source_note": "", "segments": [{"id": "folk-isola#s1", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Narrator opens: While Fagunwa created forests of supernatural imagination and Tutuola wandered through spirit worlds, Akinwumi Isola built his literary world from the material of Yoruba history — the documented lives of real people, the verified events of the past, transformed by his novelist's imagination into narratives of great beauty and emotional depth. Isola was born in 1939 in Ede, in Osun State. He was educated at the University of Ibadan and went on to an academic career as a professor of Yoruba language and literature. His academic work gave him an exceptionally deep knowledge of Yoruba oral and written traditions, and this knowledge shaped everything he wrote."}, {"id": "folk-isola#s2", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "His most celebrated work is \"Efunsetan Aniwura\" — a play (and later a novel) about the powerful Iyalode of Ibadan, the female chief who was eventually killed by the Balogun of Ibadan in the 1870s after a confrontation over the treatment of her slaves and her refusal to free them for military service. The work is extraordinary — a politically complex portrait of a woman of enormous power and significant moral failures, neither condemned nor idealized, but fully realized as a human being in a specific historical moment."}, {"id": "folk-isola#s3", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "The Efunsetan play became one of the most widely performed works in Yoruba theater. It was adapted for film. It has been the subject of more academic analysis than almost any other piece of Yoruba-language literature. And central to its power is Isola's refusal to simplify: Efunsetan is both an early exemplar of female economic power in Yorubaland and a woman who owned and mistreated enslaved people. Both things are true. The play holds both. His other major works include \"Madam Tinubu\" (about the merchant queen of Lagos) and various novels and plays that draw on Yoruba historical material. Each work demonstrates his central conviction: that Yoruba history contains stories of sufficient drama, complexity, and human interest to sustain a literature of world-class quality, and that these stories do not need to be imported from European traditions or adapted to European conventions to achieve literary greatness."}, {"id": "folk-isola#s4", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "Isola has also been deeply committed to the teaching and development of Yoruba as a literary language — the belief that Yoruba fiction and drama deserve to be taught in schools and universities, that Yoruba children should grow up reading great works in their own language, that the literary tradition Fagunwa began must be continued and expanded. He is one of the last of a generation that is passing. His students carry the tradition forward. And his works — available in Yoruba and in English translation — are among the finest achievements of 20th-century African literature. Akinwumi Isola. The scholar who wrote beautifully. The historian who honored his subjects by imagining them fully alive."}]}, {"id": "folk-duro-ladipo", "emoji": "📖", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#0a1a00,#1a3a00)", "category": "iwe", "rating": 9.4, "duration": "4 min", "title": {"en": "Duro Ladipo: The Man Who Put Sango on Stage", "yo": "Duro Ladipo"}, "desc": {"en": "Duro Ladipo, the dramatist who put Sango on the stage.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Folktales / Literary", "secondary": [], "source_note": "", "segments": [{"id": "folk-duro-ladipo#s1", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Narrator opens: In 1964, at the Berlin Theatre Festival, an audience of Europeans who had never been to Nigeria and who knew almost nothing about the Yoruba world sat in the dark and watched a performance that shook them. A man in royal regalia, backed by the thundering rhythms of Yoruba talking drums and the soaring voices of a chorus, commanded a stage with a physical and vocal power that needed no translation. The performance was \"Oba Koso\" — the Yoruba opera about Sango, the king who became lightning. The man who wrote it, composed the music, directed it, and played Sango himself was Duro Ladipo."}, {"id": "folk-duro-ladipo#s2", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Duro Ladipo was born in 1931 in Osogbo — the sacred city of Osun, the town that has long been one of the creative centers of Yoruba cultural life. He trained in the Anglican church tradition, learned music through the choir, and was a church organist as a young man. But what he heard beneath the church music — the rhythms of Yoruba drumming, the melodies of Yoruba praise singing, the theatrical energy of the Egungun masquerade — was pulling at him with a force that the church's respectable walls could not contain."}, {"id": "folk-duro-ladipo#s3", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "In 1961, he broke from the church and founded his own theatre company, the Duro Ladipo National Theatre. His innovation was to create a form that did not exist before him: Yoruba opera, in which the classical storytelling of the Yoruba tradition was presented in a theatrical format with original compositions drawing on Yoruba musical modes, full professional staging, and the emotional intensity of the operatic tradition without its European musical vocabulary. \"Oba Koso\" — which means \"the king did not hang,\" referring to the tradition that Sango did not die by hanging but transformed into lightning — was his masterpiece. It told the story of Sango with a theatrical power that had audiences across West Africa and Europe weeping and standing in ovation. The music he composed for the show entered the Yoruba cultural consciousness so deeply that some of the melodies are now indistinguishable, to many listeners, from actual traditional material."}, {"id": "folk-duro-ladipo#s4", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "He also wrote \"Eda\" — based on Everyman, the medieval European morality play, transposed into the Yoruba spiritual world with Yoruba characters and Yoruba theological stakes. \"Moremi\" — about the woman who saved Ile-Ife. \"Oba Waja\" — a political drama set in a fictional Yoruba kingdom that was so sharp in its commentary on post-independence governance that it drew official attention. He died in 1978, at forty-seven, while he was still at his creative peak. His company survived him under the leadership of his wife, Afolabi Ladipo, who kept the tradition alive for decades. His music has been recorded, studied, performed, and imitated. The Yoruba musical theatre form he created has influenced every subsequent generation of Yoruba performing artists."}, {"id": "folk-duro-ladipo#s5", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "He put Sango on stage. He made thunder audible to audiences who had never stood in a Yoruba rainstorm. He gave the world a window into the Yoruba spiritual cosmos that no amount of academic writing could have opened as effectively as one performance of \"Oba Koso.\" That is what artists do at their best. That is what Duro Ladipo did."}]}, {"id": "folk-osofisan", "emoji": "📖", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#0a1a00,#1a3a00)", "category": "iwe", "rating": 9.4, "duration": "4 min", "title": {"en": "Femi Osofisan: The Marxist Who Loved Yoruba Myth", "yo": "Femi Osofisan"}, "desc": {"en": "Femi Osofisan, the radical dramatist who loved Yoruba myth.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Folktales / Literary", "secondary": [], "source_note": "", "segments": [{"id": "folk-osofisan#s1", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Narrator opens: There is a tension at the heart of Femi Osofisan's work that makes it among the most interesting in Nigerian literature: he is a Marxist who cannot stop returning to Yoruba myth. A rationalist who sets his plays in the world of the Orisa. A man who believes that the social structures that oppress working people must be dismantled, who chooses as the primary language for that argument the aesthetic vocabulary of a traditional world he simultaneously wants to transform. This tension is not a contradiction. It is a method. Osofisan understood — long before it became fashionable in critical theory — that the most effective way to speak truth to power is to speak in the language the audience already loves, using the forms they already recognize as valuable, and to turn those forms subtly but irreversibly toward a critical purpose."}, {"id": "folk-osofisan#s2", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Femi Osofisan was born in 1946 in Erunwon, in Ogun State. He studied at Ibadan and in Paris, absorbing Brechtian theatre theory and Marxist analysis alongside deep immersion in Yoruba oral tradition. He joined the University of Ibadan's English department and for decades was simultaneously an academic and one of Nigeria's most prolific playwrights."}, {"id": "folk-osofisan#s3", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "His most celebrated works include \"Morountodun\" — which takes the Moremi story and retells it from a contemporary class-conscious perspective, asking whose interests the legend of sacrifice actually served. \"Once Upon Four Robbers\" — a play about four armed robbers who acquire magical powers that allow them to escape capture, which raises questions about the relationship between law, poverty, and criminality that Nigerian theatre had not previously dared to ask in such direct terms. \"Birthdays Are Not for Dying\" — a meditation on political violence. \"Wesoo Hamlet\" — a reworking of Shakespeare that places the Hamlet problematic in a Yoruba court, with Yoruba dramaturgy replacing the Elizabethan. His plays are performed. They are not museum pieces — they are working theatrical texts that directors return to because they are structurally sophisticated, because the characters are fully realized, because the language (which moves between English and Yoruba registers) is precise and alive."}, {"id": "folk-osofisan#s4", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "Osofisan represents a specific position in Yoruba intellectual history: the generation that came after Soyinka, that was shaped by the Biafran War and by the disappointments of post-independence governance, that chose critique over celebration, that demanded that culture answer for its complicity in the structures of oppression rather than simply celebrate the beauty of the heritage. His work is not comfortable. It is necessary. And it grows from the same Yoruba earth that produced everything else in this collection — because the tradition contains, within itself, the tools for its own critique."}]}, {"id": "folk-oba-in-fiction", "emoji": "📖", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#0a1a00,#1a3a00)", "category": "iwe", "rating": 9.4, "duration": "4 min", "title": {"en": "Chieftaincy and Literature: The Oba in Yoruba Fiction", "yo": "Chieftaincy and Literature: The Oba in Yoruba Fiction"}, "desc": {"en": "Chieftaincy and literature: the Oba as a figure in Yoruba fiction.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Folktales / Literary", "secondary": [], "source_note": "", "segments": [{"id": "folk-oba-in-fiction#s1", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Narrator opens: One of the most persistent figures in Yoruba literature — across the novel, the play, the oral tradition, and the film — is the Oba. The king. The one who sits on the throne, wears the beaded crown, speaks from behind the veil of royal authority, and is simultaneously the most powerful and the most constrained figure in any Yoruba community. The Oba in Yoruba literature is rarely simple. He is not the fairy-tale king of European childhood stories — good by definition, wise by right, just by nature. The Yoruba literary tradition has always known that power corrupts, that the man who puts on the crown changes, that the institution of kingship requires critique as much as celebration."}, {"id": "folk-oba-in-fiction#s2", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Soyinka's engagement with the Oba figure is central to his drama. In \"Death and the King's Horseman,\" the Alafin's authority — expressed posthumously, through the obligation his death places on his horseman — is portrayed with genuine reverence and genuine tragedy. The king's power is real, and its claims on the living are legitimate, even when those claims cost everything. In \"Kongi's Harvest,\" Soyinka moves to a fictional contemporary African state and shows the same royal power corrupted into tyranny — the Oba who refuses to surrender his sacred yam ceremony to the dictator becomes the symbol of authentic cultural authority against the fake authority of the strongman. Osofisan's Oba figures are more ambivalent. In \"Morountodun\" and other plays, the king is as likely to be an instrument of class oppression as a symbol of cultural legitimacy. The crown does not ennoble the man who wears it. It reveals him."}, {"id": "folk-oba-in-fiction#s3", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "The TV and film industry's Yoruba-language productions — the Yoruba home video tradition that emerged from the early 1990s — made the Oba one of its central characters. Thousands of Yoruba-language films have featured palace intrigue, contested successions, evil chiefs, noble kings, queens who rule from behind the throne. This enormous body of popular culture is the contemporary form of the Oba story — the way the Yoruba people continue to think through the questions of authority, legitimacy, and the relationship between the ruler and the ruled."}, {"id": "folk-oba-in-fiction#s4", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "The figure persists because the questions persist. Who has the right to rule? What makes authority legitimate? When does the king serve the people and when does he serve himself? These are not historical questions. They are the questions of every election, every government, every institution that claims authority over a community. The Yoruba literary tradition — from the oldest oral narratives to the newest film — has been working through these questions for centuries. It has not finished. It will not finish. The Oba keeps appearing because the questions keep mattering."}]}, {"id": "folk-hubert-ogunde", "emoji": "📖", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#0a1a00,#1a3a00)", "category": "iwe", "rating": 9.4, "duration": "4 min", "title": {"en": "Hubert Ogunde: The Father of Nigerian Theatre", "yo": "Hubert Ogunde"}, "desc": {"en": "Hubert Ogunde, the father of Nigerian theatre.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Folktales / Literary", "secondary": [], "source_note": "", "segments": [{"id": "folk-hubert-ogunde#s1", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Narrator opens: Every tradition needs a first person — the one who was there before there was a tradition, who invented the form out of necessity and possibility, who created the stage on which everyone who came after would perform. In Nigerian theatre, that person is Hubert Ogunde. He built Nigerian theatre before anyone had agreed that Nigerian theatre was possible, and he kept building it for five decades, through colonial prohibition, through post-independence politics, through military censorship, never stopping because stopping was not something he knew how to do."}, {"id": "folk-hubert-ogunde#s2", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Hubert Adedeji Ogunde was born in 1916 in Ososa, in what is now Ogun State. He worked initially as a police officer and as a teacher, but the theatre called to him with a force that no day job could contain. In 1944, he founded the African Music Research Party — later the Ogunde Concert Party — and began producing theatrical shows that drew on Yoruba musical and oral tradition while engaging directly with the political realities of colonial Nigeria. His early plays were political in the most direct sense. \"Strike and Hunger\" (1945) dramatized the general strike of that year against the colonial administration. \"Bread and Bullet\" addressed colonial violence. \"Tiger's Empire\" criticized the British Empire's extraction economy. The colonial authorities did not find this amusing. Ogunde was banned from performing in certain regions, his shows were suppressed, his passport was confiscated at various points."}, {"id": "folk-hubert-ogunde#s3", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "He kept producing. He moved from subject to subject — plays about Yoruba spiritual tradition, plays about contemporary social issues, plays celebrating Nigerian independence, plays critiquing post-independence governance — with the versatility of an artist who understood that the theatre's job was to hold a mirror to the community's face, whatever that face was showing in any given moment. In the 1970s, he moved into film — becoming one of the first Nigerian filmmakers and producing Yoruba-language features that were commercially successful and technically sophisticated by the standards of their era. His film \"Aiye\" (1980) was a sensation — a Yoruba-language film about witchcraft and community that drew enormous audiences across Nigeria and demonstrated definitively that Yoruba-language film could compete in the market."}, {"id": "folk-hubert-ogunde#s4", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "He died in 1990. He had spent forty-six years building Nigerian theatre from nothing. The institutions he created, the performers he trained, the aesthetic standards he established, the audiences he built — these survived him and formed the foundation of everything that Nigerian theatre and film has become. The father of Nigerian theatre. Not a metaphor. A fact."}]}, {"id": "women-moremi-ajasoro", "emoji": "👸", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#2a0018,#500030)", "category": "obinrin", "rating": 9.8, "duration": "18 min", "title": {"en": "Moremi Ajasoro: The Woman Who Loved Ife More Than Herself", "yo": "Moremi Ajasoro"}, "desc": {"en": "The noblewoman of Ife who let herself be captured to learn the raiders' secret, and the terrible price she paid to keep her vow.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Great Yoruba Women", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Consolidates: “The Woman Who Loved Ife More Than Her Son” and “The Sacrifice Behind the Victory”)", "segments": [{"id": "women-moremi-ajasoro#s1", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Long ago, before the roads were tarred and before the white man’s ships had learned the shape of our coast, there was a city that the whole world of the Yoruba turned toward the way a plant turns toward the sun. That city was Ile-Ife. The elders say it is the place where the earth was first gathered out of water, the navel of the world, the town from which all crowns descend. To be born in Ife in those days was to be born at the center of everything. But even the center of the world can be made to tremble, and this is the story of the woman who stood between Ife and its ruin, and of the terrible price she agreed to pay so that her people could sleep without fear."}, {"id": "women-moremi-ajasoro#s2", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Her name was Moremi. Moremi Ajasoro. She was a woman of great beauty, yes, the kind of beauty that made market traders forget their change and made old men remember their youth. But beauty was the smallest of the things she carried. She had a mind like a well-honed blade and a heart that could hold both tenderness and iron at the same time, and it is those two things, not her face, that the story remembers."}, {"id": "women-moremi-ajasoro#s3", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "In those years, Ife lived under a shadow that came not from the sky but from the forest. There was a people the Ife called the Ugbo, though some tell it as the Igbo of the forest, a folk who lived in the thick country beyond the farms. Every year, at the season when the yams were fattening in the ground and the town should have been at its happiest, the raiders came. But these were no ordinary raiders. They came wrapped head to foot in raffia and dried grass, in leaves and bark and strange fibers, so that when they burst out of the treeline they did not look like men at all. They looked like spirits. They looked like the bush itself had stood up and grown teeth."}, {"id": "women-moremi-ajasoro#s4", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "And the people of Ife, who feared the gods more than they feared any human army, looked upon these grass-covered figures and their hearts turned to water. Who can fight a spirit? Who lifts a cutlass against the messengers of the other world? So the warriors of Ife, brave men who would have stood against any human foe, dropped their weapons and ran. And the raiders walked into the town unopposed, took the yams from the barns and the cloth from the looms, and worst of all, took the people. Men, women, and children were bound and led away into the forest, never to be seen again. Year after year this happened, until Ife, the navel of the world, the mother of cities, began to empty like a calabash with a crack in its bottom."}, {"id": "women-moremi-ajasoro#s5", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "You must understand what this did to a proud people. It is one thing to be defeated. It is another thing to be humiliated season after season and to believe, in your deepest heart, that the gods themselves have taken the side of your enemy. The children of Ife grew up flinching at the sound of rustling leaves. Mothers stopped singing at the grinding stone. The drummers forgot their brightest rhythms. A town can lose a war and recover, but a town that has lost its courage is already half in the grave."}, {"id": "women-moremi-ajasoro#s6", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Now Moremi watched all of this. She was married to a man of standing in Ife, and by some tellings her husband was close to the Ooni himself, the sacred king; by other tellings she was of noble blood in her own right. Either way, she was a woman with a seat near the center of things, a woman whose voice could reach the ears of the powerful. And she was tired. Not the tiredness of the body, but the deeper tiredness of watching your people be broken a little more each year while the men argue in circles and the priests offer sacrifices that change nothing."}, {"id": "women-moremi-ajasoro#s7", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "She began to ask a question that no one else was asking. Everyone assumed the raiders were spirits because they looked like spirits. But Moremi looked harder. Spirits, she reasoned, do not need to carry away yams. Spirits do not covet cloth. A true creature of the other world has no use for a barn of food or a captured child to sell. Whatever these things were, they ate, they hoarded, they profited. And anything that eats and hoards and profits, she concluded, is not a spirit at all. It is a man. It is only a man, hidden inside a clever and terrifying costume."}, {"id": "women-moremi-ajasoro#s8", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "But to know a thing in your heart is not the same as being able to prove it, and it is a long way still from proving it to defeating it. Moremi understood that if Ife was ever to be free, someone had to go inside the enemy, learn the secret of the costumes, learn where they came from and how they could be beaten, and bring that knowledge home. And she understood, with a cold clarity that must have frightened even her, that the only way inside was to allow herself to be captured."}, {"id": "women-moremi-ajasoro#s9", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Before she did anything, Moremi went to the river. Not to any river, but to the spirit of the waters that the people of Ife knew and honored, the one they called Esimirin. There, at the edge of the flowing water, she made a vow, and the vow was this: if the spirit of the river would grant her success, if it would let her enter the enemy’s country and learn their secret and return alive to save her people, then she would offer in return whatever the spirit demanded. Anything. She did not set a price. She left the price open, which is the most dangerous kind of promise a person can make, because it means you have agreed to pay before you know what you owe. The water accepted her vow. And Moremi went home and waited for the season of raids."}, {"id": "women-moremi-ajasoro#s10", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "When the raiders came again, wrapped in their grass and their menace, and the men of Ife scattered as they always did, Moremi did not run. She stood. She let the leaf-covered figures take her, bind her, and lead her away into the forest with the other captives. Imagine the courage of that single act. Everyone she had ever known was fleeing in one direction, and she walked calmly in the other, into the arms of the very terror that had emptied her city. She went as a prisoner, but she went as a spy, and only she knew the difference."}, {"id": "women-moremi-ajasoro#s11", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "The raiders carried her far into their own land, and there the first veil fell from her eyes. These were not spirits. She had been right. They were people, ordinary people, with a town of their own, with cooking fires and quarrels and children and chiefs. The grass and raffia that had terrified Ife for a generation were nothing more than costumes, elaborate and frightening, but costumes all the same, woven and dried and put on before a raid the way a hunter puts on his charms. The whole terror of Ife had been built on a trick of the eye and a trick of the mind."}, {"id": "women-moremi-ajasoro#s12", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "Now, the tellings differ on what happened next, and a good storyteller does not pretend to know what he cannot know. Some say Moremi’s beauty and bearing were so remarkable that she was brought before the king of the Ugbo himself, and that he was so taken with her that he made her his wife, or kept her in his household in a place of honor. However it happened, she rose from captive to confidante. She won their trust. She learned their language, their ways, and above all she learned the one thing she had come for: the secret of how to break them."}, {"id": "women-moremi-ajasoro#s13", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "And the secret was almost laughably simple, once you knew it. The costumes that made the raiders seem invincible were made of dried grass and leaves and fiber. And dried grass, as every child who has watched the harmattan fires knows, does one thing above all others. It burns. The men who had ruled Ife through fear could be undone by a single torch. If the warriors of Ife would only stand their ground and carry fire, the “spirits” would go up like a field in the dry season, and the men inside them would be revealed as men, and men can be fought, and men can be beaten."}, {"id": "women-moremi-ajasoro#s14", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "Now Moremi carried a secret worth more than gold, and a secret is only useful to the one who can deliver it. So she waited, and she watched, and when the moment came she slipped away from the enemy’s town and made the long, dangerous journey back through the forest to Ife. Picture her walking those miles alone, a woman moving through hostile country, carrying in her head the one piece of knowledge that could save a nation, knowing that if she were caught she would die with the secret still locked inside her. But she was not caught. She reached the gates of Ife, and the people who had mourned her as lost gathered around her as though she had returned from the land of the dead, which in a way she had."}, {"id": "women-moremi-ajasoro#s15", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "She went before the Ooni and the elders and the warriors and she told them everything. The raiders are men. Their power is a costume. Their costume is grass. Grass burns. Stand your ground next season, carry torches, and set them alight."}, {"id": "women-moremi-ajasoro#s16", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "When the season of raids returned and the grass-covered figures came howling out of the forest expecting the people of Ife to scatter as they always had, they met a town transformed. The warriors of Ife did not run. They came forward with fire in their hands, and they thrust their torches into the dry raffia, and the terrible spirits of the forest went up in roaring flame. The costumes burned away, and where there had been monsters there were only frightened, blistering men, stumbling and screaming, their trick exposed for all to see. Ife fell upon them and broke them utterly. The raids ended. The barns stayed full. The children of Ife could sleep again without listening for the sound of leaves. Moremi had done what no army and no sacrifice had been able to do. She had freed her people."}, {"id": "women-moremi-ajasoro#s17", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "And now we come to the part of the story that the drums slow down for, the part that turns a tale of clever victory into something that aches in the chest. Because Moremi had made a vow. She had promised the spirit of the river Esimirin that she would give whatever was asked, and she had left the price open. Now that the victory was won, she went back to the water to pay."}, {"id": "women-moremi-ajasoro#s18", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "She offered goats. The river was not satisfied. She offered sheep, cattle, the fattest and finest of her wealth. The river refused them all. She offered every kind of sacrifice a person could think to offer, and each time the answer came back the same: no, this is not the price. And slowly, terribly, Moremi came to understand what the river wanted. It wanted the one thing she treasured above her own life. It wanted her only son. His name, the tellings tell us, was Oluorogbo."}, {"id": "women-moremi-ajasoro#s19", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "Think of what she faced. She had saved thousands of children so that they could grow up in a free city, and the payment for that salvation was her own child. She had walked willingly into slavery, she had walked willingly out of it, she had walked the whole road of the hero, and here at the end was a road she did not want to walk at all. But Moremi was not a woman who broke her word, even a word given to the water. A vow is a vow. She had promised before she knew the cost precisely so that she would not be able to bargain her way out of it when the cost proved to be more than she could bear. She gave up her son."}, {"id": "women-moremi-ajasoro#s20", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "The grief of Ife when they learned what their salvation had cost is beyond the reach of words, so the story does not try to describe it. It only tells what the people did. They wept, and then they resolved that the sacrifice would never be forgotten. Some of the old tellings say that all the people of Ife came together and pledged to be as sons and daughters to Moremi in place of the child she had lost, that the entire city adopted her grief and made itself her family, so that she would never again be a mother without children. A whole town became her household. That is how the Yoruba answer an unbearable debt: not by pretending it away, but by sharing it until it can be carried."}, {"id": "women-moremi-ajasoro#s21", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "And they gave Oluorogbo his own memory. In some tellings the boy did not simply vanish; they say he ascended, that he climbed to the heavens on a rope or was taken up, and that a shrine was raised in his name. To this day in Ile-Ife there are festivals and sacred places that keep the memory of Moremi and her son alive, and the Edi festival is spoken of as growing from these very events, a yearly reckoning with sacrifice and cleansing that the town has kept for centuries. The story did not end when Moremi died. It became part of the calendar of the city. It became something the people do, not just something they say."}, {"id": "women-moremi-ajasoro#s22", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "Now, why does this story last? Why, out of all the tales of clever tricks and won wars, does Moremi’s endure so that mothers still name their daughters after her, so that a university bears her name, so that a great statue of her now rises in Ile-Ife where anyone can stand at her feet and feel small?"}, {"id": "women-moremi-ajasoro#s23", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "It lasts because it refuses to lie to us about the price of great things. A lesser story would have ended with the burning of the raiders, with the triumph, with the town saved and everyone happy. But this story knows something truer and harder. It knows that the people who save us often pay in a currency we would not have the courage to spend. Moremi’s genius was not only her cunning, though she was cunning enough to see through a terror that had fooled an entire nation. Her greatness was that she understood, from the very first moment at the river when she left the price open, that real salvation is never free, and she agreed to it anyway. She loved Ife more than her comfort, more than her safety, and in the end more than her own motherhood."}, {"id": "women-moremi-ajasoro#s24", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "There is a temptation, when we tell this story to children, to make Moremi only brave and only clever, a heroine with a happy ending. But the elders never told it that way, and neither will I. They told it with the ache left in, because the ache is the lesson. To be great is to be willing to lose what you cannot bear to lose. Moremi knew that. She walked into slavery with her eyes open, walked out of it with a nation’s freedom in her hands, and then walked to the river and paid a price that still makes us lower our voices generations later. That is Moremi Ajasoro. Not the woman who loved Ife more than her son, as the old title had it, but the woman who loved her people so completely that she was willing to be broken for them, and who kept her word to the water even when the water asked for everything. When you next hear her name, remember that behind the beauty and the cleverness there was a mother who paid, and remember that the freedom of Ife was bought not with a clever trick alone but with the most expensive thing a human being can offer. That is why we still say her name softly. That is why we still say it at all."}]}, {"id": "women-efunsetan-aniwura", "emoji": "👸", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#2a0018,#500030)", "category": "obinrin", "rating": 9.6, "duration": "11 min", "title": {"en": "Efunsetan Aniwura: The Iyalode Whose Wealth Rivalled Kings", "yo": "Efunsetan Aniwura"}, "desc": {"en": "The formidable Iyalode of Ibadan whose wealth and armed household rivalled kings, and whose clash with the war-chiefs ended in her fall.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Great Yoruba Women", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Awon Obinrin Nla, consolidates the tellings of Efunsetan across the collection, the story of the great and formidable merchant queen of Ibadan, mighty in wealth and power, and the tragedy of her clash with the warlords)", "segments": [{"id": "women-efunsetan-aniwura#s1", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Tonight, my child, we come to a very different kind of great woman from the ancient queen Moremi, a woman not of the misty age of legend but of more recent and better-remembered days, of the great city of Ibadan in the eighteen hundreds, in the very age of the warlords and the Aare Ona Kakanfo of whom I have told you. Tonight we speak of Efunsetan Aniwura, the Iyalode of Ibadan, one of the wealthiest and most powerful women our people ever produced, a merchant queen whose riches rivalled those of kings, whose power was felt throughout the land, and whose story ends in a tragedy that has been argued over ever since. Hers is a tale of a woman’s power in a world of men, and of what that power cost her."}, {"id": "women-efunsetan-aniwura#s2", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "First you must understand what an Iyalode was, my child, for it is a title of great importance and it shows you something fine about our people. The Iyalode was the highest chieftaincy title a woman could hold, the recognized leader of all the women of a town, a great chief in her own right with a voice in the councils of the city, a woman who represented the interests and the power of women in the governance of the whole community. It was not a mere honorary title, my child. The Iyalode was a genuine power, a chief among chiefs, and it shows you that our people, whatever their faults, made real room for the power and the voice of women at the very highest levels of society. And of all the women who ever held that title, none was greater or more formidable than Efunsetan Aniwura of Ibadan."}, {"id": "women-efunsetan-aniwura#s3", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Efunsetan was, above all, a merchant of staggering success, my child. She built a commercial empire. She traded in every valuable thing, in agricultural produce, in goods brought up from the coast, in arms and ammunition, which in that age of constant warfare were worth more than gold, in every commodity that moved through the great markets of the age. She was a master of trade, shrewd, bold, tireless, and she accumulated wealth on a scale that few men, let alone women, ever matched. Her second name, Aniwura, means one who possesses gold, or one who has gold, and it was well earned, for her riches were legendary."}, {"id": "women-efunsetan-aniwura#s4", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "And her wealth translated into power, my child, real and formidable power. She had a vast household. She had, it is said, hundreds of servants and workers and slaves, for this was that age, and I will speak honestly of that shadow in a moment. She had her own considerable stores of weapons. She financed the wars of Ibadan, supplying the armies with the arms and provisions they needed, so that the warlords themselves depended upon her wealth to fight their campaigns. She was, in effect, one of the pillars on which the whole military power of Ibadan rested, and as Iyalode she was a great chief with real authority. A woman, my child, whose wealth and power rivalled and even exceeded that of many of the great men of her age. In a world dominated by warriors and kings, Efunsetan Aniwura stood as a mighty power in her own right, bowing to no man in wealth and to few in influence."}, {"id": "women-efunsetan-aniwura#s5", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Now let me be honest with you about the shadow, my child, for I never hide the hard truths. Efunsetan lived in a brutal age, the age of the wars I told you of, when the whole society was organized around warfare and when slavery was woven through everything, and she was a great slave-owner, as were the warlords and the wealthy of that age. Her great household and her plantations were worked by many slaves, and the tales tell that she could be a hard and even a cruel mistress, and some of the darkest stories told about her concern her harsh treatment of those she held in bondage. I will not clean this away, my child, for it was real, and it is part of the honest picture of that hard age, in which even the greatest figures were bound up in the brutal system of their time. Efunsetan was a mighty and formidable woman, but she was a woman of her age, and that age had great darkness in it, and she was not free of it."}, {"id": "women-efunsetan-aniwura#s6", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Now to the tragedy, my child, to the clash that ended her, for her story is finally a tragedy, and one that people have argued over ever since. Efunsetan’s rise to such power and wealth brought her, inevitably, into tension with the ruling warlord of Ibadan, the Aare of that day, a mighty and ruthless man named Latoosa, of whom I have told you in the cycle of the Aare Ona Kakanfo. And here, my child, two great powers collided: the formidable Iyalode with her wealth and her arms and her independence, and the ambitious Aare who wished to bend all of Ibadan to his will and could not tolerate so mighty and independent a power, especially a woman’s power, standing beside his own."}, {"id": "women-efunsetan-aniwura#s7", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "The tellings of exactly what happened between them differ, my child, and this is where the arguing begins that has never ended. Some say Efunsetan defied the Aare, refused to support one of his campaigns, or failed to accompany him to war as custom or his command required, or otherwise stood against his authority. Some say the Aare, resentful and threatened by her independent power, sought a pretext to bring her down and seize her wealth. Some emphasize her own harshness and the enemies she had made. What is clear is that a bitter enmity grew between the great Iyalode and the great Aare, a collision of two mighty and unbending wills, and that in such a collision, in that violent age, one of them would be destroyed."}, {"id": "women-efunsetan-aniwura#s8", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "And it was Efunsetan who fell, my child. The Aare moved against her. She was stripped of her position, besieged in her own household, and in the end she met a violent death, killed, the tellings say, in her own home, some say by her own slaves or servants set on or bribed to it, in a plot connected to the Aare’s enmity. The mightiest woman of her age, the merchant queen whose wealth rivalled kings, the Iyalode who had bowed to no man, was brought down and killed, and her great wealth scattered, and her power ended. So fell Efunsetan Aniwura."}, {"id": "women-efunsetan-aniwura#s9", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Now what are we to make of this tragedy, my child, for it is not simple, and the arguing over it reflects its difficulty. On one reading, Efunsetan is a tragic heroine, a woman of extraordinary power and independence in a man’s world, brought down precisely because she was too powerful and too independent for the men around her to tolerate, destroyed by a jealous and ruthless warlord who could not abide a mighty woman standing beside him as an equal power. On this reading her fall is an injustice, the crushing of a great woman by the machinery of male power, and there is much truth in it, my child, for it is certain that her being a woman of such independent might made her a target as a man of equal power might not have been."}, {"id": "women-efunsetan-aniwura#s10", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "On another reading, more complicated, she was a figure of her hard age, entangled in its brutalities, a great slave-owner with her own cruelties, brought down in the ordinary violent way that mighty figures, men and women alike, were brought down in that age of constant power-struggle and war. On this reading her fall is less a special injustice against a woman than the common fate of the powerful in a violent time. And the truth, my child, as so often, likely holds something of both: a genuinely extraordinary and formidable woman, whose being a woman made her power both more remarkable and more resented, brought down in a collision of wills in a merciless age, her real greatness and her real hardness both part of the tragic whole."}, {"id": "women-efunsetan-aniwura#s11", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "And that complexity, my child, is exactly why I tell you her story, and why I honour our people’s memory for keeping it in all its difficulty rather than flattening it into a simple tale. For remember the lesson I have taught you again and again, the lesson of Afonja, that we must never flatten a great figure into a single simple thing. Efunsetan was not simply a heroine, and not simply a villain, but a great, formidable, complicated, powerful, flawed, and finally tragic human being, one of the mightiest women our people ever produced, who rose to a height of wealth and power almost unimaginable for a woman of her age, and who was destroyed in the merciless collisions of that age. To honour her truly is to hold all of it: her greatness and her hardness, her power and her fall, the injustice done to her and the injustices she herself did, the triumph of her rise and the tragedy of her end."}, {"id": "women-efunsetan-aniwura#s12", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "Above all, my child, remember what her life proves: that a woman could rise, in the world of our ancestors, to a height of wealth and power that rivalled kings and warlords, could hold the highest office open to women, could finance armies and shape the destiny of a great city. Whatever the tragedy of her end, the fact of her rise stands as a mighty testimony to what a woman of our people could achieve. Efunsetan Aniwura, the Iyalode of Ibadan, the one who possessed gold, the merchant queen whose wealth rivalled kings. Remember her."}, {"id": "women-efunsetan-aniwura#s13", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "Tomorrow, if the ancestors are willing, I will tell you of another great and powerful woman of the same age, another mighty merchant and power-broker, of Madam Tinubu, whose name a great market and a great square still bear, and who shaped the destiny of Lagos and Abeokuta. But tonight belongs to the Iyalode of Ibadan, and we will sleep in memory of the formidable Efunsetan, in all her greatness and all her tragedy."}]}, {"id": "women-madam-tinubu", "emoji": "👸", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#2a0018,#500030)", "category": "obinrin", "rating": 9.6, "duration": "12 min", "title": {"en": "Madam Tinubu: The Merchant Queen Who Made and Unmade Kings", "yo": "Efunroye Tinubu"}, "desc": {"en": "The trader and kingmaker of Lagos and Abeokuta, morally complex, whose name a great square still carries.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Great Yoruba Women", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Great Yoruba Women — “Tinubu of Abeokuta: The Trader Who Moved Markets”)", "segments": [{"id": "women-madam-tinubu#s1", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "There are women whose names survive because they were gentle, and there are women whose names survive because they were impossible to ignore. Efunroye Tinubu belonged to the second kind. When you say her name today in Lagos, you are also naming a square in the very heart of the city, a place where traffic roars and traders shout and the whole restless commerce of the country seems to pass through in a single afternoon. That is fitting, because commerce was her element the way water is the element of a fish. But do not let the noise of the marketplace fool you into thinking she was only a trader. Madam Tinubu bought and sold, yes, but the goods she truly dealt in were power and influence, and the customers who came to her table included kings."}, {"id": "women-madam-tinubu#s2", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "She was born in the early years of the nineteenth century in Abeokuta, the town under the great rock, in the country of the Egba. This was a hard and unsettled time in Yorubaland. The old empire of Oyo had collapsed, and the collapse had thrown the whole country into a long season of war. Towns rose and fell. Refugees streamed from ruined settlements to new ones. Abeokuta itself was a town of the displaced, a gathering of Egba people who had fled the wars and built themselves a stronghold beneath the rock called Olumo. Into this world of upheaval and opportunity, Tinubu was born, and she learned early the two lessons that would define her whole life: that nothing is permanent, and that the person who controls the flow of goods controls a great deal more than goods."}, {"id": "women-madam-tinubu#s3", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "She began, as so many great traders begin, with almost nothing. She sold small things. Salt, tobacco, whatever could be carried and turned over for a little profit. But she had the eye. She could see where a thing was cheap and where the same thing was dear, and she understood that the distance between those two places was where a fortune lived. Slowly she built her trade, and as she built it she built something rarer than money: a network. She knew people. She knew who could be trusted and who could not, who owed her and who she owed, which roads were safe and which were not. A market, she understood, is not really about goods at all. It is about relationships, and she cultivated hers like a careful farmer."}, {"id": "women-madam-tinubu#s4", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "The turning of her fortune came when she moved to Lagos. In those days Lagos was a small island kingdom, but it sat in a position of enormous importance, because it was a door. Through Lagos passed the trade between the interior of Yorubaland and the wide Atlantic world beyond, and whoever controlled that door grew rich. Tinubu understood doors better than anyone. She settled in Lagos and built her trade to a scale that dwarfed anything she had done before, dealing in salt, in tobacco, in palm oil, in guns, and, in the fashion of that grim age, in the trade in human beings that the Atlantic economy was built upon. This is not a thing to hide or soften. She was a trader of her time, and the trade of her time had blood in it. Her later years would see her turn against that very traffic, but honesty requires that we place her fully in her age before we praise how she rose above parts of it."}, {"id": "women-madam-tinubu#s5", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "What set Tinubu apart from every other wealthy merchant was that she did not stop at wealth. She understood that money on its own is fragile, that a rich person without power is simply a person waiting to be robbed by a powerful one. So she reached for power, and she reached for it in the most direct way imaginable: she got close to the throne of Lagos itself. When a king named Oba Akitoye was struggling to hold or regain his seat, it was Tinubu’s wealth and Tinubu’s backing that helped put him where he needed to be. She financed loyalty. She fed fighters. She turned her fortune into a political weapon, and when the dust settled, the king of Lagos owed his position in no small part to a woman from the Abeokuta market."}, {"id": "women-madam-tinubu#s6", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Think about what that means. In a world that gave women no formal seat in the councils of kings, Tinubu made herself indispensable to kings. She could not sit on the throne, so she made sure the throne sat on her. When Akitoye died and his son Dosunmu came to power, Tinubu’s influence only grew. She became the power behind the palace, the one whose word could raise a man or ruin him, the one the ambitious came to court before they dared approach the king. They called her, in time, a kingmaker, and it was not flattery. It was a plain description of what she did."}, {"id": "women-madam-tinubu#s7", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "But the age was changing under her feet, and the change wore a British uniform. The English were pressing into Lagos, first as traders and anti-slavery patrols, then as something far more ambitious. They wanted the door for themselves. And here the story of Tinubu becomes the story of a woman who saw, earlier and more clearly than most, that these newcomers did not come to trade as equals but to rule. She resisted them. She threw her weight, her wealth, and her fierce will against the growing British grip on Lagos. She stirred opposition. She made herself a problem the colonizers could not simply buy off, because she already had more money than they could tempt her with, and more pride than they could bend."}, {"id": "women-madam-tinubu#s8", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "For this, she paid. The British and their local allies moved against her, and Tinubu, the kingmaker of Lagos, was driven out of the city she had helped to shape. Exile is a bitter cup for anyone, but for a woman whose whole power was built on being at the center of things, being cast out to the edge must have felt like a kind of death. And yet here is the measure of her: she did not fold. She did not fade into a quiet retirement to nurse her losses. She went back to Abeokuta, the town under the rock where she had begun, and she began again."}, {"id": "women-madam-tinubu#s9", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "In Abeokuta she rose a second time, and this second rising is in some ways more impressive than the first, because it was the rising of a woman who had already lost everything once and refused to accept that the loss was final. She rebuilt her wealth and her influence among the Egba. And when the Egba faced their own threats, from Dahomey to the west whose armies came raiding, and from the ever-encroaching Europeans, Tinubu was in the thick of the defense, financing the war effort, supplying the fighters, using every instrument of her trade to keep her people free. The Egba honored her for it. They gave her the title of Iyalode, the highest office a woman could hold, the recognized leader and voice of all the women of the town, a chief in her own right with a seat in the councils of the land. The market girl who had sold salt had become, by the sheer force of her will, one of the most powerful figures in the whole of Yorubaland."}, {"id": "women-madam-tinubu#s10", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Madam Tinubu died in Abeokuta in the year 1887, an old woman by then, having lived through the collapse of one world and the violent birth of another. She had begun her life in the chaos that followed the fall of Oyo and ended it on the threshold of the colonial age she had fought so hard to hold back. She did not stop the tide. No single person could have. But she made the colonizers work for every inch, and she showed a watching country that a woman could stand in the road of empire and refuse to move."}, {"id": "women-madam-tinubu#s11", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "What did she leave behind? Look at a map of Lagos and you will find your answer written in the ground. The great open square at the center of the island, where the life of the whole city seems to gather, bears her name: Tinubu Square. For generations it has been a place of gathering, of protest, of commerce, of the restless energy she embodied. There is a statue, there are streets, there is the simple fact that more than a century after her death, ordinary people who could not tell you a single date from her life still know that Tinubu was somebody, that she mattered, that she was not to be trifled with."}, {"id": "women-madam-tinubu#s12", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "But her deeper legacy is not carved in stone. It is the idea of her. In a society that told women where their power ended, Tinubu simply refused to believe it. She did not ask permission to be powerful. She did not wait for a system to make room for her; she made her own room and dared anyone to take it back. She was not a saint, and this story has not tried to make her one. She traded in things we now find terrible, she could be ruthless, she made enemies as easily as she made money. But she was undeniable, and in her time and place, for a woman to be undeniable was itself a kind of revolution."}, {"id": "women-madam-tinubu#s13", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "The elders say that when you want to describe a woman who cannot be pushed aside, a woman whose yes means yes and whose no can stop a king in his tracks, you can simply say she is like Tinubu, and everyone will understand. That is the truest monument of all, greater than the square and the statue: to have your name become a word for a certain kind of strength. Efunroye Tinubu, the market girl who became a merchant queen, who made kings and unmade them, who was exiled and rose again, who stood in the doorway of Lagos and told an empire to wait. When you pass through Tinubu Square in the heat of a Lagos afternoon, in all that noise and striving and buying and selling, know that you are standing in the shadow of a woman who understood before almost anyone that the marketplace and the palace are the same place, and who made herself queen of both."}]}, {"id": "women-emotan", "emoji": "👸", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#2a0018,#500030)", "category": "obinrin", "rating": 9.6, "duration": "11 min", "title": {"en": "Emotan: The Market Woman Whose Loyalty Saved a King", "yo": "Emotan"}, "desc": {"en": "The Benin market woman whose loyalty protected a prince and preserved a dynasty, and whose shrine still stands.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Great Yoruba Women", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Awon Obinrin Nla, the story of the faithful market woman of Benin whose courage and loyalty protected a prince and preserved a dynasty, and whose memory a great landmark still honours)", "segments": [{"id": "women-emotan#s1", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Tonight, my child, we turn to a woman whose greatness was not built on wealth or armies or the making of kings, but on something quieter and, in its way, even more admirable: on loyalty, on faithfulness, on the plain and steadfast courage of an ordinary woman who did an extraordinary thing at the moment when everything depended on it. Tonight we speak of Emotan, the market woman of Benin, whose loyalty saved a king and preserved a dynasty, and who is remembered and honoured to this day, so that a great landmark in the heart of Benin City stands in her memory. And I tell you this tale, my child, among the great Yoruba women, for the histories of Benin and of the Yoruba are deeply interwoven, bound together since the ancient days when, in the old tellings, the founding line of Benin itself reached back to Ife and Oduduwa, so that the great figures of Benin belong, in a real sense, to our shared inheritance."}, {"id": "women-emotan#s2", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Emotan was not a queen, my child, not a chief, not a woman of rank or riches. She was a market woman. She traded in the great market of Benin, and she was known for two things above all: for her care of children, for it is said she kept a kind of nursery, looking after the little ones of the market women while they traded, one of the first such carers our histories remember, a woman of deep tenderness toward children; and for her absolute, unshakeable loyalty and integrity. She was, in short, an ordinary good woman, faithful, hardworking, kind, trusted by all who knew her. And it is precisely from among such ordinary good people, my child, that the greatest heroes sometimes step forward, when the moment demands it."}, {"id": "women-emotan#s3", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Now the times were dangerous, my child. There was a struggle over the throne of Benin, as there so often was in the great kingdoms, and a rightful heir, a prince who should by right have become the Oba, was in grave danger. A rival, a usurper who had seized or coveted the throne, sought the prince’s life, for a rival claimant is always a threat to one who holds a throne unlawfully, and the surest way to secure a stolen throne is to destroy the rightful heir. And so this prince, the true heir, whose name in the tellings was Ogun, was hunted, in peril of his life, forced into hiding and into exile, a rightful king reduced to a fugitive, his life hanging by a thread."}, {"id": "women-emotan#s4", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "And here, my child, Emotan the market woman enters the great history. For Emotan was loyal to the rightful heir. She knew who the true king was, and where her loyalty belonged, and she did not waver. And from her position in the market, this ordinary trader became something extraordinary: she became the eyes and ears, the protector, the faithful ally of the hunted prince. The market, you see, is where all the news of a city passes, where everyone comes and goes, where every plot and every movement is eventually spoken of. And Emotan, faithful and watchful in her market stall, gathered intelligence, learned of the movements and plots of the usurper and his men, and passed warnings to the endangered prince, and helped to protect him, at the risk of her own life, for to be discovered aiding the hunted heir would have meant her death."}, {"id": "women-emotan#s5", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Think of the courage of this, my child. An ordinary market woman, with no army, no wealth, no rank to protect her, choosing to place herself in mortal danger out of pure loyalty to the rightful king, becoming his secret protector and informant in the very heart of the city where his enemies ruled. She had everything to lose and, in the worldly sense, nothing to gain, for she was not doing this for reward or advancement, but simply because it was right, because her loyalty was true, because she knew who the rightful king was and would not betray him no matter the danger. That, my child, is the purest kind of courage there is: the courage of the ordinary person who does the right and dangerous thing not for glory or gain but simply because it is right."}, {"id": "women-emotan#s6", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "And through her faithfulness and her watchfulness and her courage, my child, the prince survived. Emotan’s warnings and her protection helped keep him alive through the dangerous years of his exile and hiding, until at last the tide turned, and the rightful heir was able to return, and to reclaim his throne, and to become the Oba of Benin, the true king restored to his place. The dynasty was preserved. The rightful line continued. And it was preserved, in no small part, my child, by the loyalty and courage of an ordinary market woman who had risked everything to protect her king."}, {"id": "women-emotan#s7", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "Now here is the sorrow and then the honour, my child, for the tale holds both. It is told that Emotan did not live to enjoy the full triumph of the king she had saved, that she died, in some tellings even giving her life in a final act of service to his cause. But the king, restored to his throne, did not forget what the market woman had done for him. He did not do what so many of the powerful do, my child, which is to forget the humble ones who helped them once they no longer need them. No. This king honoured Emotan, and honoured her greatly, and in a way that has kept her memory alive for centuries."}, {"id": "women-emotan#s8", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "For on the very spot in the market where Emotan had traded and watched and served, the king ordered that her memory be honoured, and a sacred tree, an uruhe tree, was planted or consecrated there in her honour, and it became a revered landmark, a place of reverence, where Emotan was remembered and, it is said, even venerated, her faithfulness held up as an example to all. And when in later ages that tree was replaced, a great bronze statue of Emotan was raised on the spot, in the heart of Benin City, so that to this day, my child, the Emotan statue stands there, one of the most beloved and honoured landmarks of the city, a monument not to a king or a warrior or a wealthy chief, but to an ordinary market woman whose loyalty and courage saved a king and preserved a dynasty. Think of that, my child. In the heart of a great city, a statue honouring the faithfulness of a market woman, standing for centuries, teaching all who pass it the value of loyalty and courage and integrity."}, {"id": "women-emotan#s9", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "What a fine thing our ancestors did in honouring Emotan so, my child, and what a fine lesson it teaches. For most monuments in most places honour the mighty, the kings and the conquerors and the rich. But here a people raised their most beloved monument to an ordinary woman whose only greatness was her faithfulness, her courage, and her integrity, saying to all the generations to come: this is what we honour, this is what we hold highest, not wealth, not power, not conquest, but the plain steadfast loyalty and courage of a good woman who did the right and dangerous thing when everything depended on it. That, my child, is a people with its values in the right place."}, {"id": "women-emotan#s10", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "So let Emotan teach you, my child, and let her teaching go deep. She teaches that greatness is not reserved for the wealthy and the powerful and the high-born, that an ordinary market woman can be as great a hero as any king or warrior, greater even, for her courage was purer, seeking no reward. She teaches the supreme value of loyalty and faithfulness and integrity, the steadfast holding to what is right even at mortal risk, even when there is nothing to gain by it but the knowledge that one has done right. She teaches that the humble and faithful service of ordinary good people is often what preserves kingdoms and dynasties and all the great things, though the histories usually forget them, and that it is right and just to remember and honour them. And she teaches, through the king who did not forget her, the nobility of gratitude, of the powerful remembering and honouring the humble ones who served them."}, {"id": "women-emotan#s11", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "Be like Emotan, my child, in your own life. Be faithful. Be loyal to what is right and to those who deserve your loyalty. Have the courage to do the right and dangerous thing when the moment demands it, not for reward, but because it is right. And if you should ever rise to power or good fortune, be like the king who honoured her, and never forget the humble ones who helped you on the way. For a statue to a market woman still stands in the heart of a great city, centuries later, teaching these things to all who pass, and there is no finer monument in all our history."}, {"id": "women-emotan#s12", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "Tomorrow, if the ancestors are willing, I will tell you of a great woman of more recent days, a fierce champion of women and of freedom, of the mighty Funmilayo Ransome-Kuti, the lioness of Abeokuta, who led the women against injustice and shook the very thrones of the powerful. But tonight belongs to Emotan, to the faithful market woman whose loyalty saved a king, and we will sleep in honour of the quiet, steadfast courage of the ordinary good."}]}, {"id": "women-funmilayo-ransome-kuti", "emoji": "👸", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#2a0018,#500030)", "category": "obinrin", "rating": 9.6, "duration": "11 min", "title": {"en": "Funmilayo Ransome-Kuti: The Lioness Who Shook the Thrones", "yo": "Funmilayo Ransome-Kuti"}, "desc": {"en": "The champion of women and freedom who organized the women of Abeokuta and deposed a king.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Great Yoruba Women", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Awon Obinrin Nla, the story of the mighty champion of women and of freedom, who organized the women of Abeokuta, deposed a king, and became a voice for justice across the land)", "segments": [{"id": "women-funmilayo-ransome-kuti#s1", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Tonight, my child, we come to a woman of more recent days, within the memory of people still living when I first heard her story told, a woman so mighty, so fierce, so utterly fearless in the cause of women and of justice and of freedom, that she has been called the lioness, the mother of many, and by titles of honour too many to count. Tonight we speak of Funmilayo Ransome-Kuti, the great champion of the women of our people, who organized the market women into a force that shook the very thrones of the powerful, who fought for the rights of women and the freedom of her country, and who stands as one of the mightiest figures, man or woman, in the whole modern history of our people. Sit up straight for this one, my child, for she would have expected nothing less."}, {"id": "women-funmilayo-ransome-kuti#s2", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Funmilayo was born in Abeokuta, that great city we have spoken of so often, in the closing years of the eighteen hundreds, and she was, my child, a woman of the new age, educated, formidably intelligent, one of the first women of her land to receive a full modern education, even travelling abroad to study. She became a teacher, and she married a great educator and clergyman, and together they were a formidable pair, both dedicated to the uplift of their people. But it is not for her education alone, nor for her marriage, that we honour her, my child. It is for what she did with her gifts: for she turned all her intelligence, all her education, all her formidable will, into a weapon in the service of the women of her people and the freedom of her land."}, {"id": "women-funmilayo-ransome-kuti#s3", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Let me tell you of her great campaign, my child, the one that shook the thrones, for it is one of the most stirring stories of the courage of women in all our history. In Abeokuta, the ordinary women, above all the market women, the traders who were the backbone of the whole economy, were suffering under heavy burdens: unjust taxes laid upon them, arbitrary and harsh treatment by the authorities, seizure of their goods, and a whole system that took from them and gave them no voice. These were the women who fed the city through their trade, my child, and they were being ground down by taxes and injustices they had no power to resist, and no one had ever stood up for them."}, {"id": "women-funmilayo-ransome-kuti#s4", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Until Funmilayo. She organized them, my child. She took the scattered, powerless, individually helpless market women, and she forged them into a mighty organized union, the women of Abeokuta gathered into one great body, tens of thousands strong, disciplined, coordinated, and formidable. She taught them their rights. She gave them a voice, and then she gave that voice a mighty volume, for tens of thousands of women speaking as one cannot be ignored as one poor market woman can. And she led them, fearlessly, against the injustices they suffered, above all against the unjust taxation of women, and against the local authority, the traditional ruler, the Alake of Abeokuta, and the colonial system behind him, that imposed these burdens."}, {"id": "women-funmilayo-ransome-kuti#s5", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "And here is the mighty thing, my child, the thing that rings down the years. Funmilayo and her organized women did not merely petition politely and go home. They protested. They demonstrated. They gathered in their thousands outside the palace, singing, chanting, refusing to disperse, camping there, day and night, an immovable mass of determined women. They mocked and shamed the powerful in songs. They withheld their cooperation. They made the ordinary functioning of the city impossible until their grievances were heard. They deployed the full power of organized, determined, fearless womanhood against the thrones of the mighty, and, my child, the thrones shook."}, {"id": "women-funmilayo-ransome-kuti#s6", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "For the campaign of Funmilayo and the women of Abeokuta was so powerful, so relentless, so impossible to ignore or suppress, that it achieved what had seemed unthinkable: the unjust taxation of the women was ended, and, most astonishingly of all, the very ruler, the Alake, the king himself, was forced from his throne, compelled to abdicate, to leave his own palace, brought down by the organized power of the market women led by one fearless woman. Think of it, my child. A king, deposed, by women. By the organized courage of the market traders, led by Funmilayo Ransome-Kuti. There are few moments in all our history that show more powerfully what the organized courage of ordinary women can achieve against even the mightiest and most entrenched power."}, {"id": "women-funmilayo-ransome-kuti#s7", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "But Funmilayo’s greatness did not stop at Abeokuta, my child, nor at the cause of women alone, though that would have been enough for ten ordinary lives. She became a national figure, a leader in the great struggle for the freedom of her whole country from foreign rule, one of the important voices in the movement for independence, standing shoulder to shoulder with the great men of that struggle, and often putting them to shame with her greater courage and her fiercer will. She travelled the world, my child, speaking for her people, for the women of her land, for freedom and justice, meeting with the powerful of the earth, a woman from Abeokuta carrying the voice of her people onto the world stage. She fought for the vote for women, for the education of girls, for the rights and the dignity of women in every sphere, and for the freedom of her nation, tirelessly, fearlessly, for the whole of her long life."}, {"id": "women-funmilayo-ransome-kuti#s8", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "She was, my child, a thorn in the side of every unjust power, colonial and local alike, feared and hated by the powerful precisely because she could not be bought, could not be frightened, could not be silenced. They tried everything against her, and none of it worked, for she had a lioness’s heart and would not be cowed. And she raised, my child, a family as remarkable as herself, sons who became great figures in their own right, in medicine, in activism, in music, one of them among the most famous musicians the world has ever known, whose fierce spirit of resistance to injustice came, everyone agrees, straight from his mighty mother. The lioness raised lions."}, {"id": "women-funmilayo-ransome-kuti#s9", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Now I must tell you, my child, that her long life of fearless struggle ended in tragedy and injustice, as the lives of many great fighters for freedom have done, and I will tell it to you honestly, for you are old enough, and because it must not be forgotten. In her old age, this mighty woman, this mother of the nation, was gravely wronged in a violent assault upon her son’s compound by soldiers, an assault in which she, an elderly and revered woman, was terribly injured, thrown, the accounts say, from an upper window, and from the injuries of that assault she never recovered, and in time she died of them. That so great and so revered a woman, who had given her whole long life to the service of her people, should meet such an end at the hands of the very powers that should have honoured her, is one of the deep injustices and sorrows of our recent history, my child, and I do not soften it, for the truth must be told, and her memory demands it."}, {"id": "women-funmilayo-ransome-kuti#s10", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "But the injustice of her death, my child, terrible as it was, could not diminish the greatness of her life, and could not silence what she had achieved. For she had shown, once and forever, what the organized courage of women could do; she had deposed a king; she had helped to free a nation; she had fought for the rights of women across a whole land and onto the world stage; and she had raised a family of fighters who carried her fierce spirit forward. Her enemies could injure her body in her old age, but they could not touch what she had done or what she had become in the memory of her people: the lioness, the mother of many, the mightiest champion of women our modern history has produced."}, {"id": "women-funmilayo-ransome-kuti#s11", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "So let Funmilayo Ransome-Kuti teach you, my child, and let her teaching burn in you like the fire she carried all her life. She teaches that ordinary women, organized and led with courage, are a power that can shake and topple even the mightiest thrones, that there is no injustice so entrenched it cannot be resisted by the determined and united courage of those it oppresses. She teaches fearlessness, the refusal to be bought or frightened or silenced by any power however great. She teaches that the fight for the rights and dignity of women is a noble and necessary fight, worthy of a whole life’s devotion. She teaches that the struggle for freedom, for justice, for the uplift of one’s people, is the highest calling there is. And her death, terrible and unjust as it was, teaches the price that the bravest fighters for justice have so often paid, and lays upon us the duty to remember them, and to honour them, and to carry their fight forward."}, {"id": "women-funmilayo-ransome-kuti#s12", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "Be a little like the lioness, my child, in whatever measure you can. Do not be silent before injustice. Do not let the ordinary and powerless be ground down without lifting your voice. Have the courage to stand, and to organize, and to resist, and to speak, even against the mighty, even at cost to yourself. For one fearless woman from Abeokuta organized the market traders and deposed a king and helped free a nation, and if she could do that, my child, then none of us has any excuse to be silent before the injustices of our own day."}, {"id": "women-funmilayo-ransome-kuti#s13", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "Tomorrow, if the ancestors are willing, I will tell you of a mighty queen of the ancient interwoven history of Benin and the Yoruba, of the warrior-mother Queen Idia, who raised armies and worked mighty powers to secure her son’s throne, and whose carved ivory face has become known across the whole world. But tonight belongs to the lioness, to Funmilayo Ransome-Kuti, and we will sleep in fierce admiration of the woman who shook the thrones, and in sorrow and honour for how her great life ended."}]}, {"id": "women-queen-idia", "emoji": "👸", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#2a0018,#500030)", "category": "obinrin", "rating": 9.6, "duration": "12 min", "title": {"en": "Queen Idia: The Warrior Mother Whose Face Conquered the World", "yo": "Idia"}, "desc": {"en": "The warrior queen mother of Benin who raised armies for her son, and whose ivory face became famous worldwide.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Great Yoruba Women", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Awon Obinrin Nla, the story of the mighty queen mother of Benin, warrior and mystic, who raised armies to secure her son’s throne, and whose carved ivory face became a symbol known across the whole earth)", "segments": [{"id": "women-queen-idia#s1", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Tonight, my child, we close our cycle of the great women with one of the mightiest of them all, a queen of the ancient interwoven history of Benin and the Yoruba, a woman who was warrior and mother and mystic and kingmaker all at once, and whose face, carved in ivory five hundred years ago, has become one of the most famous images of African greatness in all the world, known and honoured across every continent. Tonight we speak of Queen Idia, the warrior mother of Benin, and I tell you her story among the great Yoruba women because, as I told you with Emotan, the histories of Benin and of the Yoruba are woven together from the ancient days, and her greatness belongs to our shared inheritance."}, {"id": "women-queen-idia#s2", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Idia lived around five hundred years ago, my child, in the great and powerful kingdom of Benin, in the age when Benin was one of the mightiest states in all of West Africa, a kingdom of magnificent art and formidable armies and a great and feared Oba. And Idia was the mother of one of the greatest of all the Obas of Benin, a mighty warrior-king named Esigie, who reigned in that age and expanded and strengthened his kingdom. But Idia was no mere mother-in-the-background, my child. She was a power in her own right, and the throne her son held, he held in no small part because of her."}, {"id": "women-queen-idia#s3", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "For when the old Oba died, my child, there was a struggle over the succession, a bitter and dangerous struggle, as there so often was, between rival claimants to the throne. Esigie faced a powerful rival, and the kingdom was in danger of being torn apart in the fight, and Esigie’s hold on the throne was far from secure. And it was his mother, Idia, who secured it for him. Idia threw all her formidable gifts into the fight to place and keep her son upon the throne, and her gifts, my child, were formidable indeed."}, {"id": "women-queen-idia#s4", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "For Idia was, the histories tell us, a warrior and a mystic both. She was skilled, it is said, in the arts of war and in the deeper arts of the unseen, in medicine and in the mystical powers that our peoples have always known, and she used all of these in her son’s cause. She raised armies. She organized and financed and even, in the tellings, helped to lead the military campaigns that defeated her son’s rivals and enemies. And she worked her mystical powers, her medicines and her spiritual arts, to protect her son and to secure his victories, so that the people believed, and Esigie himself believed, that it was his mother’s mighty powers, martial and mystical alike, that won him his throne and his greatest victories. Idia was, in effect, my child, the power behind and beside the throne, a warrior-mother who fought, by arms and by mystic craft, to make and keep her son a king."}, {"id": "women-queen-idia#s5", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "And Esigie, my child, unlike so many of the powerful who forget those who raised them up, honoured his mother greatly for all she had done. But he faced a difficulty, for in the custom of Benin, there had been no formal title or office for the mother of the Oba, no recognized place of power for the queen mother. So Esigie, in gratitude and in recognition of his mother’s mighty role, created one. He established the title and the office of the Iyoba, the queen mother of Benin, a great and honoured position with its own palace, its own domain, its own chiefs and power, a formal recognition that the mother of the Oba was a mighty personage in the kingdom. And Idia was the first Iyoba, my child, the first queen mother of Benin, and the office she was given endured for centuries after her, so that she founded not only her son’s secure reign but a whole institution of female power in the kingdom that outlasted her by ages."}, {"id": "women-queen-idia#s6", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Think of that, my child. A woman whose greatness was so undeniable that a king created an entire new office of state simply to honour and contain her power, an office that then endured for centuries, giving formal recognized power to the queen mothers of Benin ever after. That is greatness that reshapes the very structure of a kingdom. Idia did not merely wield power in her own lifetime; she created a permanent place for the power of women at the height of the state, for all the queen mothers who would come after her."}, {"id": "women-queen-idia#s7", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "But now, my child, I must tell you the most extraordinary part, the part that has carried Idia’s face and Idia’s greatness across the whole world and down all the centuries to our own day. For Esigie, to honour his mighty mother, had her likeness carved, and among the carvings made in her honour were ivory masks, pendant masks of astonishing beauty and craftsmanship, carved from ivory by the master artists of Benin, whose art, like the bronze art of Ife I told you of, ranks among the supreme artistic achievements of all humankind. These ivory masks portray the serene, dignified, powerful face of Queen Idia, the warrior mother, and they are, my child, among the most beautiful and famous objects ever made by African hands."}, {"id": "women-queen-idia#s8", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "And here is the bitter and the proud thing together, my child, for the story of these masks carries in it the whole later history of our peoples. In a later age, the great kingdom of Benin was attacked and conquered and terribly sacked by the British, its treasures, the magnificent bronzes and ivories of Benin, looted and carried away across the sea, scattered into the museums and private collections of the foreigners, where a great many of them remain to this day, a wound and an injustice that our peoples still feel and still seek to right, for those treasures were stolen, my child, and their return is still demanded and still, too often, refused. And among the looted treasures were the ivory masks of Queen Idia."}, {"id": "women-queen-idia#s9", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "But the face of Idia, my child, even in exile, even looted, could not be kept from becoming a symbol of African greatness. For in our own age, when the peoples of Africa gathered to celebrate their arts and their cultures and their unity, in a great festival of Black and African arts and culture, it was the face of Queen Idia, that serene and mighty ivory face carved five hundred years ago, that was chosen as the very symbol and emblem of the whole festival, the image that represented the greatness and the beauty and the dignity of all of African art and culture to the whole watching world. The warrior mother’s face, my child, became the face of African cultural greatness itself, known and reproduced and honoured across every continent, a symbol of what Africa was and is and can be."}, {"id": "women-queen-idia#s10", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "So the injustice of the looting was answered, in a way, by a greater triumph, my child. The foreigners could steal the ivory masks, but they could not steal what the face upon them meant, and in the fullness of time that face rose to become the very emblem of African greatness, gazing out at the whole world with the serene and mighty dignity of the warrior queen who first wore it in life. Idia’s face conquered the world, my child, not by armies, but by its beauty and its dignity and by all that it had come to stand for: the greatness of African art, the power of African women, the unbreakable dignity of a people whose treasures could be stolen but whose greatness could not."}, {"id": "women-queen-idia#s11", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "So let Queen Idia teach you, my child, as we close our cycle of the mighty women. She teaches that a woman could be warrior and mystic and kingmaker and mother all at once, could raise armies and win wars and secure thrones and reshape the very structure of a kingdom to make a permanent place for the power of women. She teaches the greatness of the mother who fights for her child, the fierce maternal power that is among the mightiest forces in all the world. She teaches, through the office of Iyoba that she founded, that true greatness does not merely win power for itself but builds lasting structures that lift up those who come after. And she teaches, through the long journey of her ivory face from the palace of Benin to the looter’s ship to the emblem of African greatness before the whole world, that the dignity and the greatness of a people, and of its women, cannot finally be stolen or suppressed, but will rise, in the end, to be honoured before all the earth."}, {"id": "women-queen-idia#s12", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "We have walked now, my child, through the whole cycle of the great women: Moremi who gave her son to save her people; Efunsetan the formidable Iyalode whose wealth rivalled kings; Tinubu the merchant queen who fell and rose again and shaped two cities; Emotan the faithful market woman whose loyalty saved a king; Funmilayo the lioness who shook the thrones and helped free a nation; and now Idia, the warrior mother whose face conquered the world. Let no one ever tell you, my child, that the history of our people was made by men alone. These mighty women, and countless others whose names the tellings have not kept, shaped our whole story, in courage and wisdom and power and sacrifice, and they are owed the same honour as any king or warrior, and more, for they achieved their greatness so often against the added weight of a world that did not expect greatness from women, and rose to it anyway."}, {"id": "women-queen-idia#s13", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "Tomorrow, if the ancestors are willing, we will turn to a new cycle, to the living legends of our own age, to the mighty ones of recent days whose names are known across the whole world, in music and letters and art and every field of human greatness. But tonight belongs to the warrior mother, to Queen Idia, and to all the mighty women of our people, and we will sleep in honour of the mothers and queens and warriors and builders who made us, and whose greatness gazes out at the world still, serene and unconquered, from a face carved in ivory five hundred years ago."}]}, {"id": "women-wuraola-esan", "emoji": "👸", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#2a0018,#500030)", "category": "obinrin", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "6 min", "title": {"en": "Chief Mrs Wuraola Esan: The First Woman in the Senate", "yo": "Wuraola Esan"}, "desc": {"en": "The first woman to sit in the Nigerian Senate.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Great Yoruba Women", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Great Yoruba Women — consolidates “Chief Mrs Wuraola Esan: The First Woman in the Senate”)", "segments": [{"id": "women-wuraola-esan#s1", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Every locked door has a first person to walk through it, and the walking is rarely easy, because the door was locked precisely to keep people like her out. This is the story of the woman who became the first female member of the Nigerian Parliament, the first woman to sit in the Senate of a country that had never imagined a woman there. Her name was Chief Mrs Wuraola Adepeju Esan, and though her name is quieter today than it should be, she belongs in the front rank of the women who forced open the rooms where the future of the nation was decided."}, {"id": "women-wuraola-esan#s2", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "She was born in Ibadan in 1909, in the years when Western education was still a narrow and precious thing, and narrower still for a girl. But Wuraola came from people who valued learning, and she seized it with both hands. She trained as a teacher, which in that era was one of the very few professions that gave an educated African woman a foothold of independence and respect. And she did not stop at being a teacher who works for someone else. She became a founder. She established her own girls’ school, Ibadan People’s Girls’ Grammar School, because she understood, as the truly far-sighted always do, that the surest way to change the future is to educate the girls who will build it. Every girl she taught was a small act of defiance against a world that expected little of women."}, {"id": "women-wuraola-esan#s3", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "From the schoolhouse she moved into the wider work of public life. She threw herself into the women’s organizations that were flowering across Yorubaland in the mid-twentieth century, the unions and societies through which women who had no formal power built informal power of their own, organizing, petitioning, raising their collective voice. She became a leader among these women, respected for her learning, her judgment, and her tireless work for the welfare of women and children. She was a bridge between the world of the market woman and the world of the educated elite, at home in both, trusted by both, and that made her formidable."}, {"id": "women-wuraola-esan#s4", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "When Nigeria moved toward independence and built the institutions of self-government, the question arose of who would sit in its new chambers of power. In 1960, the very year the nation was born, Wuraola Esan was appointed to the Senate, becoming the first Nigerian woman ever to hold a seat in the federal legislature. Sit with the weight of that for a moment. In the entire long history of that vast territory, through all its kingdoms and empires and colonial decades, no woman had ever held such a seat, and now, at the dawn of the independent nation, one did, and her name was Wuraola Esan."}, {"id": "women-wuraola-esan#s5", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "She used her position exactly as you would hope a pioneer would use it, not merely to enjoy the honor of being first, but to pull others through the door behind her. In the Senate she was a voice for the things that the all-male chambers were inclined to forget: the education of girls, the welfare of women and children, the health of families, the dignity of the ordinary people whose lives the grand decisions of government touched most directly. She proved, in the plainest possible way, that a woman in the legislature was not an experiment or an ornament but a necessity, that half the nation had been governed without a voice, and that this woman’s voice made the chamber wiser than it had been without her."}, {"id": "women-wuraola-esan#s6", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Chief Mrs Wuraola Esan died in 1985, having lived long enough to see the trail she blazed widen into a road, to see other women follow her into the councils of the nation, into ministries and legislatures and high office. She did not live to see the road finished, for it is not finished yet, but she lived to see that it existed, and that she had made it."}, {"id": "women-wuraola-esan#s7", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "Here is why her story matters even though it is quieter than the tales of warrior queens and market rebels. The firsts are the ones who pay the highest price for the smallest visible reward. A woman who topples a king has a dramatic story. A woman who simply walks, with dignity and competence, into a room where no woman has ever been allowed, and does her work so well that the room can never again pretend women do not belong there, changes the world just as surely, but changes it in a way that is easy to overlook precisely because she made it look natural. That was her genius: to make the unprecedented look inevitable."}, {"id": "women-wuraola-esan#s8", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So remember Wuraola Esan, the teacher who built a school for girls, the organizer who gave women a collective voice, and above all the first woman to sit in the Senate of Nigeria. Every woman who has since risen to high office in that country walks through a door that Wuraola Esan was the first to open, and most of them will never know her name. Now you do. Say it, and honor the one who went first."}]}, {"id": "women-ogundipe-leslie", "emoji": "👸", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#2a0018,#500030)", "category": "obinrin", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "6 min", "title": {"en": "Omolara Ogundipe-Leslie: Writing Women Into the Story", "yo": "Omolara Ogundipe-Leslie"}, "desc": {"en": "The scholar and poet who wrote women into the story.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Great Yoruba Women", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Great Yoruba Women — consolidates “Omolara Ogundipe-Leslie: Writing Women Into the Story”)", "segments": [{"id": "women-ogundipe-leslie#s1", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Some warriors fight with cutlasses and some with armies, but there is a kind of warrior who fights with ideas, and her weapons are words on a page that change the way a whole generation thinks. Omolara Ogundipe-Leslie was that kind of warrior. She was a scholar, a poet, and a critic, one of the sharpest African minds of her generation, and she spent her life fighting a battle that most people did not even realize was being fought: the battle over who gets to be included in the story a people tells about itself."}, {"id": "women-ogundipe-leslie#s2", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "She was born Molara Ogundipe in Lagos in 1940, into a family that prized education and the life of the mind. She was brilliant from the start, the kind of student who makes teachers sit up, and she rose through the best schooling available to become one of the first women to reach the heights of African academic life. She studied literature, mastered it, and joined the faculty of universities in Nigeria and around the world, standing in lecture halls in an age when a woman at the front of a university classroom was still a rare and sometimes resented sight. She was a first, and like all firsts she had to be twice as good to be counted as equal."}, {"id": "women-ogundipe-leslie#s3", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Now here is what made her more than just a successful academic. As she read the great African literature that was pouring out in the years around independence, the novels and poems that were teaching the world what Africa was, she noticed something that troubled her deeply. In story after story, the women were in the background. They were mothers and wives and symbols, they were the earth and the nation and the beautiful thing to be won, but they were rarely the ones whose inner lives mattered, rarely the thinkers and the doers and the centers of their own stories. Even in the work of the great male writers she admired, women were being written as scenery rather than as people. And Omolara decided that this would not stand."}, {"id": "women-ogundipe-leslie#s4", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "She became one of the founding voices of African feminist literary criticism, though she was careful and particular about that word. She did not simply borrow Western feminism and apply it to Africa, because she saw that Western feminism carried assumptions that did not always fit African lives. Instead she built her own framework. She coined a term, stiwanism, from the phrase Social Transformation Including Women in Africa, precisely so that the conversation would not be about importing foreign ideas but about the African social transformation that must include its women fully. It was a characteristically brilliant move: she took control of the very language of the debate, and in doing so insisted that African women would define their own liberation in their own terms."}, {"id": "women-ogundipe-leslie#s5", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "Her scholarship was formidable and fearless. She dissected the way women were represented in African writing, praising what deserved praise and cutting without mercy where writers, even celebrated ones, had reduced women to shadows. She insisted that you cannot tell the true story of a people while leaving out half of them, that a literature that cannot see its women clearly is a literature that cannot see clearly at all. And she wrote poetry of her own, spare and powerful, that gave voice to the very interiority she found missing elsewhere. She was, at once, the critic pointing at the gap and the artist filling it."}, {"id": "women-ogundipe-leslie#s6", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Her influence spread far beyond Nigeria. She taught and lectured across the world, in America and Europe and across Africa, and a generation of scholars, both women and men, learned to read differently because of her. The writers who came after, the daughters of the tradition like Chimamanda Ngozi Adichie and so many others who now place complex, fully human African women at the very center of their fiction, write in a world that Omolara Ogundipe-Leslie helped to clear for them. They may not always cite her, but they walk on ground she broke. That is the quiet fate of the critic and the theorist: to change everything and be thanked by few."}, {"id": "women-ogundipe-leslie#s7", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "She lived a long, full, and combative life, never softening her edges to make herself more comfortable to those in power, whether that power was colonial, patriarchal, or academic. She died in 2019, an elder of African letters, mourned by scholars across the continent and the diaspora who understood that a foundational voice had gone silent. But her ideas did not die with her. Stiwanism is still taught. Her essays are still assigned. Her insistence that African women must be at the center of the story of African transformation has become, for a whole field, simply common sense, which is the highest thing that can be said of any thinker, that her once-radical idea became the air a discipline breathes."}, {"id": "women-ogundipe-leslie#s8", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So remember Omolara Ogundipe-Leslie, and remember what she teaches, which is that representation is not a small or vain thing. Who gets to be the subject of a story, rather than its scenery, shapes what a whole society believes is possible. When she fought to write women into the story, she was not asking for a favor or a courtesy. She was insisting on the truth, because the truth is that women were always there, always thinking and acting and shaping events, and a literature that could not see this was simply lying. She spent her life correcting that lie. The next time you read a novel in which an African woman is fully, gloriously herself, the center of her own world, know that a scholar-poet named Omolara fought so that such a woman could appear on the page at all."}]}, {"id": "women-iyalode-subuola", "emoji": "👸", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#2a0018,#500030)", "category": "obinrin", "rating": 9.4, "duration": "5 min", "title": {"en": "Iyalode Subuola: The First Voice in the Council", "yo": "Subuola"}, "desc": {"en": "The Iyalode who was the first woman's voice in the council.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Great Yoruba Women", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Great Yoruba Women — consolidates “Iyalode Subuola: The First Voice in the Council”)", "segments": [{"id": "women-iyalode-subuola#s1", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "To understand this story you must first understand a title, because the title is the point. Among the Yoruba there is an office called the Iyalode, and it is one of the most remarkable political institutions any society ever invented for its women. The word means, roughly, the mother of the outside world, the mother whose domain is public affairs, and the Iyalode was no mere ceremonial figure. She was a chief in her own right, with a seat in the councils of the town, a voice in matters of war and peace and trade, and the recognized leader and advocate of all the women of the community. In an age when the wider world was locking women out of every chamber of power, the Yoruba had built a chair for a woman at the very table where the town’s fate was decided. This is the story of one who held that chair, Subuola, and of what it means to be the first voice for those who have never had a voice."}, {"id": "women-iyalode-subuola#s2", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Subuola was, by the tellings, a woman of the market and of the town, risen through the ordinary channels by which a Yoruba woman gained standing: through trade, through wealth honestly built, through the trust and loyalty of other women, and through a wisdom that made people bring their disputes to her rather than to the courts. For among the Yoruba, a woman did not become Iyalode by birth or by a king’s whim alone. She earned it by first becoming a leader among women in fact, so that when the formal title came, it merely recognized a power she had already built from the ground up. The women chose their champion, and the town confirmed her."}, {"id": "women-iyalode-subuola#s3", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Picture her at work. When a dispute broke out in the market, between traders over a debt, over a stall, over a broken agreement, it was to the Iyalode that the women brought it, and her judgment carried the weight of law. When the town’s chiefs gathered to weigh some great decision, a war to be fought, a tax to be levied, a treaty to be signed, the Iyalode sat among them, and she spoke not for herself but for the tens of thousands of women whose labor fed the town and whose trade filled its coffers. If the men were minded to make a decision that would fall hardest on the women, on the traders and the farmers and the mothers, it was Subuola’s duty to rise and say so, and to make them reckon with the half of the population they might otherwise forget."}, {"id": "women-iyalode-subuola#s4", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "This was real power, and it rested on a real foundation. The women of a Yoruba town were not dependents; they were the economy. They controlled the markets, they moved the goods, they held wealth of their own. And because they had economic power, their representative had political power. Subuola’s authority was not a gift graciously handed down by men. It was the political expression of a truth the whole society understood: that you cannot govern a town against the will of the women who feed it and finance it. The Iyalode was the institution that turned that truth into a seat at the table."}, {"id": "women-iyalode-subuola#s5", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "The deepest lesson of Subuola’s story is not about any single dramatic deed, but about the wisdom of a system that had a place for her at all. It is fashionable to imagine that the idea of women in leadership is a modern, foreign import, something that came with the colonizers or after them. The office of the Iyalode gives that idea the lie. Long before any of that, the Yoruba had already worked out that a healthy town needs the voice of its women in its councils, and they built a permanent, respected, powerful office to guarantee it. When later women like Tinubu and Efunsetan and Funmilayo rose to lead, they were not inventing something new. They were stepping into a chair that Yoruba society had carved for women generations before."}, {"id": "women-iyalode-subuola#s6", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So remember Iyalode Subuola, and remember the office she held. She stands for every woman who has ever spoken in a room where decisions are made and spoken not for herself alone but for all the voiceless behind her. Her greatness was not in conquering an enemy but in doing, faithfully, the quiet and essential work of representation, of being the one who rises when the powerful forget, and says: and what of the women? What of us? A society that keeps a chair at the table for that question is a wiser society than most the world has ever built, and the Yoruba kept that chair for centuries. Subuola sat in it, and spoke."}]}, {"id": "women-iyalaje", "emoji": "👸", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#2a0018,#500030)", "category": "obinrin", "rating": 9.4, "duration": "5 min", "title": {"en": "The Iyalaje: The Women Who Owned the Economy", "yo": "Iyalaje"}, "desc": {"en": "The market mothers who ran the Yoruba economy, and the power they held over it.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Great Yoruba Women", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Great Yoruba Women — consolidates “The Iyalaje: Women Who Owned the Economy”)", "segments": [{"id": "women-iyalaje#s1", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "If you had walked into any great Yoruba market two hundred years ago, at Ibadan or Oyo or Abeokuta, you would have seen a sight that would have astonished a European traveler of the same era: a vast, roaring, brilliantly organized economy run, almost entirely, by women. Row upon row of stalls, thousands of traders, goods moving from the farms of the interior to the ports of the coast and back again, credit extended, debts collected, prices set, quarrels judged, and behind it all, at the head of it all, women. This is not the story of a single heroine. It is the story of a whole class of them, the Iyalaje, the mothers of wealth, the women who owned the Yoruba economy and, through it, held a power that the men who wore the crowns could never ignore."}, {"id": "women-iyalaje#s2", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Among the Yoruba, trade was women’s work, and this did not mean it was small work. It meant the opposite. The market was the beating heart of the town, and the women who commanded it commanded the lifeblood of the whole society. A successful trader, an Iyalaje, might control a network of buyers and sellers stretching across hundreds of miles. She might employ scores of people. She might extend credit to farmers before the harvest and to other traders across the region, functioning, in effect, as a bank in a society that had no banks. She dealt in cloth, in salt, in kola, in palm oil, in beads, in everything that moved, and the wealth she built was her own, held in her own name, passed down through her own line."}, {"id": "women-iyalaje#s3", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "The market itself was a marvel of self-government, and the women ran that too. Each trade had its guild, its association of sellers, with its own rules, its own leaders, its own way of settling disputes and setting fair practice. Over the whole market presided the great female chiefs, up to the Iyalode herself, so that a woman with a grievance never had to go begging to a man for justice; she took it to the women’s own courts, run by women, according to rules the women had made. The market was, in a real sense, a female republic operating inside the male kingdom, and the two needed each other, for the kingdom could not eat without the market, and the market could not be safe without the kingdom."}, {"id": "women-iyalaje#s4", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "And here is the thing that gave the Iyalaje their teeth. Because they controlled the flow of goods, they could stop it. If a king or a chief pushed the women too far, taxed them too heavily, insulted them too gravely, the market women had a weapon more powerful than any spear: they could close the market. They could refuse to sell. And a town whose market has shut its gates is a town where the fires go cold and the powerful grow hungry along with everyone else. The threat did not have to be used often, because everyone understood it was there. The economic power of the women was, quietly and constantly, a political power, a check on the throne itself."}, {"id": "women-iyalaje#s5", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "When we tell the stories of the great named women, the Tinubus and the Efunsetans, we must remember that they did not rise from nothing. They rose from this. They were the tallest trees in a whole forest of trading women, the most successful of a class that already held enormous power. Tinubu was an Iyalaje before she was anything else; her political kingmaking was built on the foundation of her market wealth. To understand any of the great Yoruba women, you must first understand the ground they grew from, and that ground was the market, ruled by the mothers of wealth."}, {"id": "women-iyalaje#s6", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So remember the Iyalaje, the women who owned the economy, even though most of their names are lost to us. They are the reason that Yoruba women were never the powerless dependents that women were made to be in so many other places. They held the purse of the whole society, and a people that lets its women hold the purse cannot help but let them hold a share of the power. When you next pass through a great Nigerian market and see the traders, overwhelmingly women, presiding over their goods with the confidence of people who know their own worth, you are looking at the living descendants of the Iyalaje, an unbroken line of women who have owned the marketplace, and through it a piece of the world, for longer than anyone can remember."}]}, {"id": "women-women-who-carried-news", "emoji": "👸", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#2a0018,#500030)", "category": "obinrin", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "5 min", "title": {"en": "The Women Who Carried the News: The Female Messengers of Old Yorubaland", "yo": "The Women Who Carried the News: The Female Messengers of Old Yorubaland"}, "desc": {"en": "The female messengers who carried news across old Yorubaland.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Great Yoruba Women", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Great Yoruba Women — consolidates “The Women Who Carried the News: Female Messengers”)", "segments": [{"id": "women-women-who-carried-news#s1", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "In a world without telephones, without radio, without a single printed page for most of its history, how does word travel? How does a king in one town learn what a king in another is plotting? How does a warning of an approaching army outrun the army itself? How does a market three days’ walk away know today’s price of cloth? The answer, in old Yorubaland, was often a woman on a road, walking fast, carrying nothing that would mark her as important, holding in her memory a message worth a kingdom. This is the story of the women who carried the news, the messengers whose names are almost never written but without whom the whole delicate machinery of the Yoruba world could not have functioned."}, {"id": "women-women-who-carried-news#s2", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Think about why a woman was, in many cases, the ideal messenger. A market woman moving between towns was the most ordinary sight in the world. She had every reason to be on the road, a basket on her head, moving from one market day to the next. No one stopped her, no one suspected her, because women traders crossed borders and territories all the time in the ordinary course of trade. An armed man moving between rival towns was a provocation; a woman with goods was simply commerce. And so the very invisibility that the wider world imposed on women, the assumption that they were unimportant, became, in the hands of a clever society, a weapon. The person no one watches is the perfect person to carry a secret."}, {"id": "women-women-who-carried-news#s3", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "The messenger’s craft was a serious discipline. A message of importance could not be written down, for writing could be intercepted and read, and in any case most of the world was not lettered. So the messenger carried the words in her head, and she carried them exactly, for a single altered word could start or stop a war. She learned to memorize long and precise messages, to repeat them back faultlessly, to deliver them only to the right ears and to swallow them, if need be, before the wrong ones. Some carried tokens as well, a particular bead, a knotted cord, a small carved object, that proved to the receiver that the message was genuine and came from who it claimed to come from. It was a whole system of trust and secrecy, run on human memory and human loyalty."}, {"id": "women-women-who-carried-news#s4", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "And the danger was real. A messenger caught carrying word between enemies could be killed, or worse. To walk the forest roads alone, through territories at war, carrying knowledge that powerful men would kill to possess or to suppress, took a courage every bit as real as a soldier’s, though it won none of a soldier’s glory. These women went out quietly and came back quietly, and the kingdom turned on their reliability, and almost no one sang their names."}, {"id": "women-women-who-carried-news#s5", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "There is a particular kind of greatness in the ones history forgets, and the female messengers are its very emblem. We remember Moremi because she saved a city with a single dramatic sacrifice. We do not remember the countless women who saved their towns a hundred quieter times, by carrying a warning through the night, by getting the true word to the right chief before a disaster, by being trusted with a secret and keeping it unto death. Their heroism was distributed across thousands of ordinary journeys, and so it left no single monument. But it was heroism all the same."}, {"id": "women-women-who-carried-news#s6", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So remember the women who carried the news, the nameless messengers of old Yorubaland. Remember that the flow of knowledge, which is the flow of power, ran through the memories and the loyalty and the tireless feet of women whom the powerful thought too unimportant to watch. They turned invisibility into strategy and ordinariness into a shield, and they held together a world that could not have held together without them. The next time you send a message across the world in an instant and think nothing of it, spare a thought for the woman on the forest road, carrying a kingdom’s fate in her head, walking fast, watching the treeline, saying the words over and over to herself so that not one of them would be lost. She was a keeper of the word, and the word was everything."}]}, {"id": "women-akinke", "emoji": "👸", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#2a0018,#500030)", "category": "obinrin", "rating": 9.4, "duration": "5 min", "title": {"en": "Akinke: The Female Warrior of Ijesa", "yo": "Akinke"}, "desc": {"en": "The female warrior of Ijesa.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Great Yoruba Women", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Great Yoruba Women — consolidates “Akinke: The Female Warrior of Ijesa”)", "segments": [{"id": "women-akinke#s1", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "We have spoken of women who ruled markets and women who carried secrets, women who theorized and women who governed. Now let us speak of a woman who did the thing the world least expected of a woman: she took up arms and fought. Her name, in this telling, is Akinke, a warrior of the Ijesa country in the hard years of the nineteenth-century wars, and though the drums of history beat loudest for the male generals, there were women on those battlefields too, and Akinke stands for them all."}, {"id": "women-akinke#s2", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "The nineteenth century in Yorubaland was a century of almost ceaseless war. The old empire of Oyo had fallen, and in the long violence that followed, towns rose and fell, armies marched and countermarched across the land, and no family was untouched by the fighting. The Ijesa, a proud people of the eastern Yoruba country, were in the thick of it, defending their independence against the great war camp of Ibadan and fighting in the long coalition struggles that consumed the age. In such a time, when the survival of a whole people hangs on its ability to fight, old rules about who may and may not take up a weapon come under enormous pressure, and sometimes they break."}, {"id": "women-akinke#s3", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Akinke, the tellings say, was a woman who would not stay behind the walls while the men went out to decide the fate of her town. Some say she came from a family of warriors and learned the arts of war at her father’s or her brother’s side. Some say the death of the men in her family thrust the defense of her household onto her, and she rose to it. However she came to it, she learned to fight, to handle weapons, to move and think on a battlefield, and she earned a place among fighters who had every reason to doubt a woman could hold one and who came to know better."}, {"id": "women-akinke#s4", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Understand how hard-won such respect would have been. A war camp is a world of men, built on codes of male honor, deeply suspicious of anything that disturbs its order. For a woman to be accepted there, not as a camp follower or a burden but as a fighter whose courage and skill the men relied on, she would have had to be not merely as good as the men around her but visibly, undeniably better, brave past the point where doubt was possible. Akinke, by the memory of her people, was exactly that. She fought where the fighting was worst. She did not flinch. And the men who fought beside her stopped seeing a woman and started seeing a warrior, which in that world was the highest promotion there was."}, {"id": "women-akinke#s5", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "The deeper truth her story carries is that the line between what women could do and what they were allowed to do was always drawn by circumstance, and war redrew it. In peace, a society can afford its comfortable rules about the proper place of women. In a fight for survival, it cannot; it needs every hand that can hold a weapon and every heart that will not break, and it quickly discovers that courage was never the property of one sex. Akinke did not have to prove that a woman could be brave; the women of Yorubaland had been proving that in a thousand ways for centuries. She proved something narrower and, to the men around her, more startling: that a woman could be brave in exactly their way, on their ground, at the sharp and bloody edge of battle, and be their equal there."}, {"id": "women-akinke#s6", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So remember Akinke, the female warrior of Ijesa, and remember that she stands for all the women whose names the war chronicles left out, the ones who fought and bled and sometimes died defending their towns while the songs afterward remembered only the men. She took up the weapon they said was not for her, walked into the place they said was not hers, and held her ground so well that they could not deny her. Her greatness is the greatness of the one who breaks a rule that deserved to be broken, and who does it not with an argument but with her own unanswerable courage, on the one field where courage cannot be faked. When the Ijesa needed every warrior they had, Akinke was a warrior, and no one who saw her fight ever again asked whether a woman could be one."}]}, {"id": "women-kemi-midwife", "emoji": "👸", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#2a0018,#500030)", "category": "obinrin", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "5 min", "title": {"en": "Kemi: The Midwife Who Held the Town Together", "yo": "Kemi"}, "desc": {"en": "The midwife who held the town together.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Great Yoruba Women", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Great Yoruba Women — consolidates “Kemi: The Midwife Who Held the Town Together”)", "segments": [{"id": "women-kemi-midwife#s1", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "There is a woman in every old Yoruba town whose hands were the first thing almost every living person in that town ever touched. Before the mother’s face, before the father’s voice, before the light of the sun, there were her hands, catching the newborn as it came into the world. She was the midwife, the agbebi, the one who caught the child, and though she wore no crown and held no title in the men’s council, she may have been, in the truest sense, the most important person in the town. This is the story of such a woman, called Kemi here, and of the quiet, immense power of the ones who stand at the doorway between the unborn and the living."}, {"id": "women-kemi-midwife#s2", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "In a world without hospitals, without doctors as we know them, without any of the machinery of modern medicine, birth was the most dangerous journey a woman would ever make, and the midwife was her only guide through it. Kemi’s knowledge was vast and entirely unwritten, passed down from midwife to apprentice across untold generations: how to turn a child that lay wrong in the womb, how to ease a hard labor, which leaves and roots would slow a dangerous bleeding or bring down a fever, how to read the signs that told her whether all was well or whether she must fight through the night for two lives at once. She was a physician, a pharmacist, and a surgeon, all in one person, working by firelight with her hands and her memory and her nerve."}, {"id": "women-kemi-midwife#s3", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "But Kemi’s role went far beyond the moment of birth, and this is where the title of her story becomes clear, for she held the town together in ways that reached into every corner of its life. Because she attended the births, she knew every family’s secrets. She knew who was truly whose child, who had struggled to conceive, who had lost baby after baby and needed gentleness rather than gossip. She was a keeper of the town’s most intimate history, and she kept it faithfully, for a midwife who could not hold a confidence would soon have no one willing to call her. She was, in a sense, the town’s memory of its own beginnings, family by family."}, {"id": "women-kemi-midwife#s4", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "She was also its counselor. A woman in trouble, in her marriage, in her health, in her heart, would go to the midwife before she went to anyone else, because the midwife had already seen her at her most vulnerable and had proven she could be trusted. Kemi settled quarrels between wives, advised young mothers, comforted the grieving, and spoke hard truths to those who needed to hear them. She moved freely between households that were otherwise at odds, because everyone needed her and everyone trusted her, and in a divided town such a person is a thread that keeps the whole cloth from tearing."}, {"id": "women-kemi-midwife#s5", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "And when death came, as it does, she was often there too, for the one who knows how to ease a body into the world often knows best how to ease it out. She sat with the dying. She prepared the dead. She stood at both doorways of life, the entrance and the exit, and in doing so she carried a spiritual weight that the whole community felt even if it rarely spoke of it. The men might hold the visible power, the titles and the councils and the wars, but the midwife held something deeper: the trust of every family at the hours when trust matters most, and the knowledge that could mean the difference between a living child and a grave."}, {"id": "women-kemi-midwife#s6", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So remember Kemi, the midwife, and remember all the agbebi whose names are gone but whose hands shaped the town. Her greatness was not loud. It was measured in lives, one at a time, in the dark, with no one watching but the exhausted mother and the god she prayed to. Every person who lived owed her their first breath. Every family owed her its continuance. She held no title, and she needed none, for she held something greater: she held the very life of the town in her two hands, night after night, and she did not drop it. When we count the great women, we must count her, the one who caught us all as we fell into the world, and who held the town together simply by being, for everyone, the one who could be trusted at the door of life."}]}, {"id": "women-iyalode-abiye", "emoji": "👸", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#2a0018,#500030)", "category": "obinrin", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "5 min", "title": {"en": "Iyalode Abiye of Egba: The Voice That Voted Before There Were Votes", "yo": "Abiye"}, "desc": {"en": "The Egba Iyalode whose voice counted before there were votes.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Great Yoruba Women", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Great Yoruba Women — consolidates “Iyalode Abiye of Egba: The Voice That Voted Before There Were Votes”)", "segments": [{"id": "women-iyalode-abiye#s1", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "We in the modern world are fond of telling ourselves a certain story about progress. We say that once, long ago, women had no say in how they were governed, and that only recently, after long struggle, did they win the vote and a voice in public affairs. It is a true story for much of the world. But it is not the whole story, and in the Egba country of the Yoruba it needs an important correction, because there was a woman whose voice counted in the governing of her people long before any ballot box was ever set down on African soil. She held the title of Iyalode of the Egba, and in this telling her name is Abiye, and her story is a reminder that the vote is only one way, and a late one, of doing something the Yoruba had done for centuries: giving women a real voice in power."}, {"id": "women-iyalode-abiye#s2", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "The Egba were a people forged in adversity. Their great town of Abeokuta was built by refugees who had fled the collapse and the wars, gathering under the protection of the rock called Olumo and building there a new home and a new kind of government. Because Abeokuta was assembled from many scattered communities, its politics were unusually consultative; power was shared among many chiefs and councils rather than concentrated in one absolute king, and decisions were reached through argument and negotiation among the many holders of authority. And among those holders of authority, with a genuine seat and a genuine voice, was the Iyalode."}, {"id": "women-iyalode-abiye#s3", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Abiye’s power was not symbolic and it was not soft. As Iyalode of the Egba, she led the women of one of the most commercially powerful communities in all of Yorubaland, women who controlled the trade that made Abeokuta rich and who financed, when it came to it, the wars that kept Abeokuta free. When the councils of the Egba deliberated on the great questions, on how to deal with the kingdom of Dahomey whose armies came raiding from the west, on how to handle the British and the missionaries pressing in from the coast, on matters of war and taxation and law, the Iyalode’s voice was in the room, and it carried the weight of the thousands of women behind her. When she spoke, she was not asking a favor. She was exercising a recognized right."}, {"id": "women-iyalode-abiye#s4", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Consider what this meant in practice. When the men of the council leaned toward a course that would harm the traders, the Iyalode could oppose it, and her opposition was not easily brushed aside, because everyone knew that the women could withhold their wealth and their cooperation, and that a decision the women would not support was a decision that would fail. She could deliver the support of the market women to a policy, or withhold it, and in a community that lived by trade, that was a power close to a veto. She voted, in every way that matters, on the fate of her people, in an age when the very idea of a woman voting was unthinkable in the lands of the strangers who would later come to rule."}, {"id": "women-iyalode-abiye#s5", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "There is a bitter irony that her story lays bare. When the British came, bringing with them their claim to be the bearers of civilization and progress, they brought a system in which women had no political voice at all, in which the very notion of a woman chief with a seat in council was strange and even offensive to them. And so the coming of colonial rule, sold as progress, in many ways diminished the political power that Yoruba women like Abiye had long enjoyed. The strangers who thought they were civilizing the Egba were, in this one crucial respect, dragging them backward, replacing a system that gave women a real voice with one that silenced them, and then, generations later, congratulating themselves when they finally granted women a fraction of what the Iyalode had held all along."}, {"id": "women-iyalode-abiye#s6", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So remember Iyalode Abiye of the Egba, the voice that voted before there were votes. Remember her the next time someone tells you that the political empowerment of women is a modern, Western gift to the rest of the world. The Egba had a woman in their councils with a real voice and a real veto long before that gift was ever offered. She stands for a truth worth guarding: that our ancestors, in their own way and in their own language, had already understood that no people can govern itself well while ignoring the voice of its women, and had built that voice a seat at the table. Abiye sat in that seat, and she was heard, and the fate of a people turned in part on what she said."}]}, {"id": "women-herbalist-women", "emoji": "👸", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#2a0018,#500030)", "category": "obinrin", "rating": 9.4, "duration": "5 min", "title": {"en": "The Herbalist Women of Old Yorubaland", "yo": "The Herbalist Women of Old Yorubaland"}, "desc": {"en": "The herbalist women who kept the healing knowledge of old Yorubaland.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Great Yoruba Women", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Great Yoruba Women — consolidates “The Herbalist Women of Old Yorubaland”)", "segments": [{"id": "women-herbalist-women#s1", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Walk into the forest with one of them and you would understand within an hour that you were in the presence of a scholar. She would move among the trees and the undergrowth the way a lettered person moves among the shelves of a great library, for the forest was her library, and every leaf and root and bark was a page she had learned to read. These were the herbalist women, the keepers of the healing knowledge of the Yoruba, the ones who knew which plant would break a fever and which would stop a wound from festering, which would ease a woman’s pains and which, mishandled, would kill. They held in their memories a pharmacy vaster than any building, and they held it for the good of everyone, and this is their story."}, {"id": "women-herbalist-women#s2", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Yoruba medicine was old, deep, and remarkably sophisticated, and much of it was in the hands of women. The knowledge was not written; it lived in the trained memory, passed from an old woman to a young one across a lifetime of apprenticeship, so that to become a true herbalist was the work of decades, not years. A girl chosen for it would follow her teacher into the forest again and again, season after season, learning to identify hundreds upon hundreds of plants, to know each one at every stage of its growth, to understand not just what it did but how to prepare it, in what quantity, combined with what, for whom, and when. A single mistake could be fatal, and so the discipline was exacting and the respect it commanded was immense."}, {"id": "women-herbalist-women#s3", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "The herbalist woman’s craft joined body and spirit in a way that the modern world has largely forgotten. She did not treat only the flesh. She understood illness as something that could arise in the body, in the mind, in the tangle of a person’s relationships, or in the unseen world, and she treated the whole person accordingly. Her remedies came with words, with prayers and incantations, the ofo and the ayajo, spoken over the medicine to awaken its power, for the Yoruba understood that the healer’s intention and the patient’s belief were themselves part of the cure. In this she was physician and priest at once, and modern medicine, with its growing understanding of how mind and body speak to each other, is only now catching up to what she always knew."}, {"id": "women-herbalist-women#s4", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Her authority in the community was real and it was independent. People came to her, high and low, king and commoner, because sickness respects no rank and everyone, sooner or later, needs a healer. This gave her a standing that no one could take away, and an income of her own, and a web of gratitude that ran through every family she had ever helped. A woman who has saved your child from a fever is a woman you do not lightly cross. And because she dealt in powerful and dangerous knowledge, she was also, quietly, a little bit feared, for the same hand that heals with a plant could, everyone knew, do the opposite. That edge of fear only deepened the respect."}, {"id": "women-herbalist-women#s5", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "There is a hard chapter here too, for when the Europeans came with their own medicine and their own religion, they often dismissed the herbalist women as ignorant, as superstitious, even as witches, and much of the deep knowledge these women had guarded for centuries was scorned, suppressed, and in places lost. Precious pages of that great forest library were torn out and burned by people too arrogant to read them. And yet the knowledge did not die entirely. It survived in the villages, in the memories of stubborn old women who kept teaching their apprentices whatever the strangers said, and today, as the world rediscovers the value of traditional plant medicine and researchers comb the old remedies for the cures of tomorrow, the herbalist woman is being vindicated. She was right about the plants all along."}, {"id": "women-herbalist-women#s6", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So remember the herbalist women of old Yorubaland, the scholars of the forest, the healers who held a nation’s medicine in their memories. Their greatness was a greatness of knowledge, patiently gathered and faithfully kept and freely given, across so many generations that no one can count them. They asked no throne and wrote no books, yet they carried a science, and they used it to keep their people alive. When you next take a medicine and are grateful to be well, remember that long before the laboratories, there were women who walked into the forest, learned to read every leaf, and came back with the power to heal, and gave it to whoever knocked at their door. That is a kind of greatness the world is only now learning again to honor."}]}, {"id": "women-abebi", "emoji": "👸", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#2a0018,#500030)", "category": "obinrin", "rating": 9.4, "duration": "5 min", "title": {"en": "Abebi: The Girl Whose Name Meant the World Wanted Her", "yo": "Abebi"}, "desc": {"en": "The girl whose name meant the world wanted her.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Great Yoruba Women", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Great Yoruba Women — consolidates “Abebi: The Girl Whose Name Meant the World Wanted Her”)", "segments": [{"id": "women-abebi#s1", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Among the Yoruba, a name is not a label. A name is a prayer, a history, a hope, sometimes a whole story folded into a single word. When a child is born, the family does not reach for a name at random; they choose one that says something about the circumstances of the birth, the state of the family, the wish they carry for the child. And so this story begins with a name, Abebi, which means, in its heart, we asked and we were given, we begged for her and she came, the world wanted her. It is a name given to a daughter who was longed for, prayed for, waited for through hard years, and it carries a truth worth pausing on: that a girl, in the Yoruba world, could be a child so wanted that her very name announced it to all who heard it."}, {"id": "women-abebi#s2", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "We are told a certain story about old societies, that they wanted sons and merely tolerated daughters, that a girl was a disappointment and a burden. And it would be false to pretend that no such attitudes ever existed anywhere. But the name Abebi, and the many names like it, complicate that easy story, because you do not give a child a name that means the world wanted her unless the world, in fact, wanted her. Think of a family that had struggled to have children, that had known the grief of empty arms, that had prayed and sacrificed and waited. When at last a child came, a daughter, and lived, was she a disappointment? The name says otherwise. The name says she was a miracle, an answered prayer, and it hung that meaning on her for her whole life to hear."}, {"id": "women-abebi#s3", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Consider what it does to a person to grow up carrying such a name. Every time someone called to her across the compound, every time her mother summoned her to a task, every time a friend greeted her in the market, the word that reached her ears carried its buried meaning: wanted, longed for, the answer to a prayer. A name like that is a quiet, lifelong act of love, repeated a thousand times a day by everyone she meets, most of whom are not even thinking of the meaning. The Yoruba understood something profound about this. They understood that we grow, in part, into the words that are spoken over us, and so they were careful, when a child was longed for, to speak that longing into her very name, so that she would carry the knowledge of being wanted in the most intimate way possible, folded into the sound of her own self."}, {"id": "women-abebi#s4", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "This is why Abebi belongs in a collection of great women, though she may be less a single historical person than the emblem of a whole way of loving daughters. Her greatness is the greatness of being cherished, and of what a cherished child can become. A girl who grows up knowing, in her bones and in her name, that she was wanted, walks through the world with a particular steadiness. She does not have to spend her life proving she deserves to exist. That work was done for her at her naming. She is freed to become something, to build and to lead and to dare, because the foundation, the knowledge that she is loved and was always meant to be here, was laid before she could even speak."}, {"id": "women-abebi#s5", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "There is a lesson in Abebi for our own time, when so many people, and so many girls in particular, grow up starved for exactly the assurance her name gave freely. The Yoruba naming tradition is a piece of ancient wisdom that says: tell the child, from the very first day, who she is to you. Do not leave her to guess. Do not make her earn it. Write your love for her into the word she will hear more than any other, and let that word do its quiet work across all the years of her life."}, {"id": "women-abebi#s6", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So remember Abebi, and remember what her name means. She stands for every wanted daughter, every girl who was prayed for and celebrated and told, in the deepest language a people has, that the world was glad she had come. Her greatness is not written in the chronicles of wars and thrones. It is written in a single word, spoken with love over a cradle and carried for a lifetime: we asked, and we were given. The world wanted her. May every girl know that she, too, was wanted like Abebi, for a child who knows she was longed for can grow up to long for great things, and to reach them."}]}, {"id": "women-alaga-idana", "emoji": "👸", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#2a0018,#500030)", "category": "obinrin", "rating": 9.6, "duration": "11 min", "title": {"en": "The Alaga Idana: The Women Who Officiate the Wedding", "yo": "Alaga Idana"}, "desc": {"en": "The women who officiate the Yoruba traditional wedding.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Great Yoruba Women", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Great Yoruba Women — consolidates “The Alaga Idana: Women Who Officiate Weddings”)", "segments": [{"id": "women-alaga-idana#s1", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Go to any Yoruba wedding today, the traditional engagement that the people call the idana, and you will meet her within the first few minutes, because she is running the entire affair. She stands at the front, microphone in hand, wrapped in the finest cloth, and she is at once the master of ceremonies, the comedian, the judge, the negotiator, and the keeper of tradition. She makes the groom’s family kneel and prostrate. She teases and tests them. She reads the letter, receives the gifts, hands over the bride, and blesses the union, and she does it all with such wit and command that the entire gathering hangs on her every word. She is the Alaga Idana, the mistress of the engagement, and she is one of the most delightful and underappreciated figures of authority in Yoruba life."}, {"id": "women-alaga-idana#s2", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "The word Alaga means, roughly, the one who owns the chair, the presiding one, the chairwoman, and Idana refers to the engagement ceremony itself. So the Alaga Idana is, quite literally, the woman in the chair, the one who presides. And presiding is exactly what she does, for the traditional Yoruba wedding is an elaborate piece of theater with generations of custom behind it, and someone has to run it, someone who knows every step of the ancient choreography and can guide two families, often nervous and unfamiliar with each other, through it smoothly and joyfully. That someone is always a woman, and she is a professional, skilled and often paid, sought after for her eloquence, her humor, her knowledge, and her authority."}, {"id": "women-alaga-idana#s3", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "To understand what the Alaga Idana does, you have to understand the engagement ceremony itself, the introduction, the day when two families meet formally and agree, before God and before witnesses, that their children will marry. In the old days this was the real wedding, the traditional one, and even now, when church weddings and court registries have been added on top, it remains the ceremony that carries the deepest weight. It is where the families actually come together. And because two families are strangers to each other, and because pride runs high on both sides, and because the whole thing is meant to be joyful rather than tense, you cannot simply let the two groups sit and stare at each other. You need someone to stand between them and turn the meeting into a performance. That is the Alaga."}, {"id": "women-alaga-idana#s4", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "There are usually two of them, in fact. There is the Alaga Ijoko, the one who sits, who represents the bride’s family and hosts the event. And there is the Alaga Idana, the one who stands, who comes with the groom’s family and speaks on their behalf. The two women face each other across the gathering, and the entire ceremony becomes a kind of duel between them, a witty, elaborate, affectionate contest, with the two families as the audience and the marrying couple as the prize. Watching two skilled Alaga go back and forth is one of the great pleasures of Yoruba social life. People come partly for the couple and partly for the show."}, {"id": "women-alaga-idana#s5", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "The Alaga Idana arrives leading the groom’s party. And the first thing she must do is get her people through the door, which the bride’s side, playing their part, will pretend to make difficult. There is a letter to be read, a formal proposal letter from the groom’s family asking for the bride’s hand. The Alaga reads it aloud, performing it, and the bride’s Alaga will find fault, will demand it be read again, will tease that the handwriting is poor or the words too plain, and the crowd laughs, and money changes hands to smooth the way. This is the theater. Everyone knows the marriage is agreed. The negotiation is ritual, not real. But it is played with total commitment, because the playing is the point."}, {"id": "women-alaga-idana#s6", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "Once the groom’s people are inside and seated, the real theater begins, and its most famous scene is the prostration. The Alaga Idana will call the groom and his friends forward, and she will command them to prostrate, to lie flat on the ground before the bride’s parents and elders. This is dobale, the full prostration of respect that a Yoruba man offers to those senior to him, and on this day it carries enormous meaning. The young man who wants to take a daughter out of this family must first put his face to the earth before them. And the Alaga does not let him do it once and rise. She makes him do it again. She says it was not low enough, not humble enough, not held long enough. She teases him. Are you sure you want this wife? Then down again. His friends go down with him, a row of young men flat on the floor, and the crowd roars, and the bride’s mother wipes her eyes, and somewhere the bride is laughing behind her veil. It is funny and it is serious at the same time, which is the genius of the whole ceremony."}, {"id": "women-alaga-idana#s7", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Then come the gifts. The groom’s family brings the engagement list, the eru iyawo, the trousseau, which traditionally includes things heavy with meaning. A Bible or a Quran, for the couple’s faith. Honey, so the marriage will be sweet. Salt, so it will never lack flavor. Kola nuts and bitter kola, for long life and blessing. Sugar, palm oil, a suitcase of clothing, jewelry, and money. The Alaga Idana presents each item, and the bride’s Alaga inspects each one, and there is more teasing, more negotiation, more laughter. Is this all the honey? For our daughter, this small jar? And more is produced, or more money is given, and the two women spar over every item while the families delight in the spectacle. Through it all the Alaga Idana must be quick, funny, gracious, and unshakably in control. A dull Alaga can sink a whole ceremony. A brilliant one can make a modest engagement feel like a royal coronation."}, {"id": "women-alaga-idana#s8", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "And when the sparring is done, the mood shifts. Because the Alaga is not only a comedian. She is also the one who blesses. She calls the bride forward, and the bride comes veiled, and the Alaga tests the groom one more time, making him identify his wife among a group of covered women, or find her, or prove his love in some small performed way. And then the veil is lifted, and the couple kneels, and the elders lay their hands on them, and the Alaga leads the prayers. Here her voice changes completely. The joking stops. She speaks blessing over the marriage, over children to come, over long life and prosperity and peace between the two families now joined. The same woman who had the whole room laughing a moment ago now has them weeping. That is her range. That is her craft."}, {"id": "women-alaga-idana#s9", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "It is worth pausing on what this role means, because it is easy to laugh at the Alaga and miss her significance. In a culture where formal authority so often sat with men, with the kings and the chiefs and the elders on their stools, here is a woman standing at the center of the most important social contract two families ever make, and running it entirely. She decides when the groom rises and when he stays down. She decides whether a gift is enough. She sets the pace, controls the mood, and speaks the blessing that seals the union. No marriage in the traditional way is complete without her. The families defer to her. The elders wait for her cue. For the length of that ceremony, she is the most powerful person in the room, and everyone knows it and everyone accepts it, because tradition placed this authority in a woman’s hands and kept it there."}, {"id": "women-alaga-idana#s10", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "And the knowledge she carries is real knowledge. A good Alaga holds in her head the full choreography of the ceremony, the order of events, the meaning of every item on the list, the proverbs and songs and prayers for each moment, the delicate etiquette of two proud families, and the timing that turns a routine formality into an unforgettable day. She has served at hundreds of engagements. She has seen every kind of family, every awkwardness, every joy, and she knows how to handle all of it. She is, in the truest sense, a keeper of tradition, one of the people through whom the old ways are carried forward and handed on. When a young woman apprentices to an experienced Alaga, learning the craft, watching, practicing the calls and the jokes and the blessings, a whole body of cultural knowledge passes from one generation to the next, woman to woman."}, {"id": "women-alaga-idana#s11", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So the next time you sit at a Yoruba engagement and watch the woman in the beautiful cloth make the groom prostrate for the third time, and the whole room dissolves in laughter, look a little closer. You are not just watching entertainment. You are watching one of the oldest offices in the culture being performed by one of its most skilled practitioners. You are watching a woman hold two families in her hands and weave them together with wit and ceremony and blessing. The Alaga Idana does not wear a crown. She does not sit on a throne. But she presides, and she is obeyed, and when she lifts her voice to bless the couple at the end, she is speaking with an authority as old and as deep as any in the land. That is her chair. And she owns it completely."}]}, {"id": "women-first-headmistress", "emoji": "👸", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#2a0018,#500030)", "category": "obinrin", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "8 min", "title": {"en": "The First Headmistress: The Women Who Built the Schools", "yo": "The First Headmistress: The Women Who Built the Schools"}, "desc": {"en": "The women who built Yorubaland's schools.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Great Yoruba Women", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Great Yoruba Women, consolidates “The First Headmistress” and the women who built schools)", "segments": [{"id": "women-first-headmistress#s1", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "There is a particular kind of woman who does not appear in the songs of the drummers or the praises of the kings, and yet without her a whole society would look different today. She is the woman who taught the children. Not one child, not her own children only, but hundreds of them, thousands over a lifetime, sitting in rows on wooden benches under a tin roof, chanting their letters and their numbers, learning to read and write and count, learning that the world was larger than the compound they were born into. She was the first headmistress, and in town after town across Yorubaland, when the schools came, she was the woman who ran them, and generations of leaders, doctors, lawyers, writers, and mothers passed through her hands."}, {"id": "women-first-headmistress#s2", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "To understand her you have to remember what education meant when it first arrived, and how rare it was, and how fiercely it was fought over. In the old Yoruba world knowledge was passed down by mouth and by hand. A child learned farming from the father, trading from the mother, the drum from the drummer, medicine from the herbalist, the histories from the elders around the fire. It was a complete and sophisticated system of learning, but it did not include the thing that came with the missionaries and the traders and, later, the colonial government, which was the book. Reading and writing arrived as a strange and powerful new magic, and at first many families were suspicious of it, and some were hostile, and it took a special courage to embrace it and stranger courage still to teach it."}, {"id": "women-first-headmistress#s3", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "And it took more courage for a girl. Because when schools first opened, the assumption in many quarters was that they were for boys. A boy would go out into the new world of clerks and catechists and colonial offices, and so a boy should be trained. A girl would marry and keep a home, and what did she need with letters? This idea sat heavily on the land, and every girl who got an education got it because someone, a father, a mother, a missionary, a determined aunt, decided to fight that assumption on her behalf. And the girls who were educated and then chose to turn around and teach, to become the ones who opened the door for the next generation of girls, those were among the quiet revolutionaries of Yoruba history."}, {"id": "women-first-headmistress#s4", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Picture one of them. Call her, as many were called, simply the headmistress, though of course she had a name, and in her own town everyone knew it and said it with respect. She had been one of the lucky girls, sent to a mission school as a child, perhaps in Abeokuta or Ibadan or Lagos where the missionaries had established their stations early. She had shown quickness, and the teachers had marked her out, and instead of leaving after the first years she had gone on, to a teacher training college, one of those institutions that produced the backbone of educated West Africa. There she had learned not only her letters and her arithmetic but how to stand before a room of restless children and hold them, how to make a lesson land, how to be firm and kind at once. And when she finished, she went home, or to a town that needed a school, and she began."}, {"id": "women-first-headmistress#s5", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "The early schools were humble beyond anything a modern child would recognize. A single room, sometimes just a shelter of poles and thatch, later a building of mud brick and then of cement blocks with a corrugated iron roof that roared when the rain came so loud that lessons had to stop. Benches worn smooth by generations of small bodies. A blackboard, a stick of chalk hoarded like treasure. Slates for the youngest children, who wrote and wiped and wrote again. And the headmistress at the front, chalk in hand, leading the chant that carried across the whole town in the mornings, the letters and the times tables sung out in unison, a sound that meant the future was being built one recitation at a time."}, {"id": "women-first-headmistress#s6", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Her authority in that room was total, and it had to be, because she was doing something difficult. She was teaching children whose parents often could not read, in a language of instruction that was frequently not their mother tongue, with almost no materials, and she was expected to produce results, to send pupils on to the higher schools, to justify the whole strange enterprise of formal education to a community still deciding whether it trusted it. She disciplined with a firm hand, as was the way then. She also fed children who came hungry, nursed those who fell sick, counseled the ones whose homes were troubled, and quietly paid the school fees of the bright poor ones out of her own small salary. She was teacher, matron, nurse, counselor, and mother to the whole school. The word headmistress hardly captures it."}, {"id": "women-first-headmistress#s7", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "And then think of what came out of those rooms. The pupil who could barely hold a slate pencil in the first year grew up to be a schoolteacher himself, or a nurse, or a clerk who rose to become a permanent secretary, or a market woman who could now read her own contracts and could not be cheated, or a mother who taught her own children their letters at home before they ever reached a school. The girls especially. Every girl who sat in that room and learned to read was a small crack in the old wall of the idea that letters were not for women, and enough cracks, over enough years, brought that wall down. The women who later became doctors and lawyers and professors and ministers, the Funmilayo Ransome-Kutis and the ones who followed her, did not spring from nowhere. Somewhere behind each of them stood a headmistress who had taught her mother, or taught her, or built the school she first walked into."}, {"id": "women-first-headmistress#s8", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "The tragedy is how little of this is remembered. The kings have their praise poems. The warriors have their drums. But the woman who taught three generations of a town to read, who shaped more lives than any single chief, is remembered, if at all, only by her old pupils, growing fewer each year, who still say her name softly and straighten a little when they say it, as though she might still be watching to see that they behave. Ask an old man or woman in a Yoruba town about their headmistress and watch what happens to their face. Sixty, seventy years later, they still remember her, the sound of her voice, the sting of her cane, the day she praised them in front of the class, the way she believed they could be something. That is a monument, even if no stone was ever carved."}, {"id": "women-first-headmistress#s9", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So we should say it plainly, since so few others do. The women who built the schools built the country. Every literate Yoruba person, every professional, every writer including whoever set down these very words, stands on a foundation laid by a headmistress in a hot little room with a piece of chalk and an unreasonable faith that these children, her children, could learn anything and become anyone. She asked for no throne and got none. She asked only that the children come to school, that they sit still, that they try. And because they did, because she made them, a whole people learned to read. There are few greater gifts one generation can give another, and few givers so completely, and so unjustly, forgotten."}]}, {"id": "women-mama-agba", "emoji": "👸", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#2a0018,#500030)", "category": "obinrin", "rating": 9.6, "duration": "9 min", "title": {"en": "Mama Agba: The Grandmother as an Institution", "yo": "Mama Agba"}, "desc": {"en": "The grandmother as an institution in Yoruba family life.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Great Yoruba Women", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Great Yoruba Women, consolidates “Mama Agba, the grandmother as institution”)", "segments": [{"id": "women-mama-agba#s1", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "In every Yoruba family there is, or there was, a Mama Agba. The words mean, simply, the elder mother, the grand old woman, the grandmother. But to translate it as grandmother and leave it there is to miss almost everything, because Mama Agba is not merely an old woman who happens to be related to you. She is an institution. She is a court of appeal, a library, a hospital, a bank, a school, and a shrine, all housed in one small, bent, unhurried body sitting on a low stool in the shade of the compound. When the family gathers, she is its center. When it quarrels, she is its judge. When it forgets who it is, she is the one who remembers. Long after the men who ran the town are gone and forgotten, the family still speaks of Mama Agba, and lowers its voice a little to do so."}, {"id": "women-mama-agba#s2", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "She was old, of course, that was the whole point, but her oldness was not weakness in the way the modern world often treats age. Her oldness was her authority. She had lived through everything. She had seen wars and famines and epidemics, colonial officers come and go, the old kings die and new ones crowned, the coming of the motorcar and the radio and the electric light. She had buried a husband, and sometimes children, which is the heaviest sorrow there is, and she had survived it and kept walking. All of that living had turned into a deep, unshakable knowledge of how the world actually works, of what matters and what does not, of how people behave and why. When Mama Agba spoke, she spoke from the far side of experiences the young ones had not yet had, and they knew it, and so they listened."}, {"id": "women-mama-agba#s3", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Her power in the family was real, though it was almost never formal. No one appointed her. She held no title necessarily, though some Mama Agbas were also chiefs or priestesses in their own right. Her authority came from position and from person, from being the oldest surviving root of the family tree, and from the accumulated respect of a life lived in full view of everyone. A word from Mama Agba could stop a fight that all the shouting men could not stop. Her blessing was sought before any journey, any marriage, any major undertaking, because a grandmother’s blessing was believed to carry weight in the unseen world, and her curse, though she rarely spoke one, was a thing to be feared above almost anything."}, {"id": "women-mama-agba#s4", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Consider all the things she was, one by one. She was the family’s library. In a culture where so much was carried in memory rather than in books, Mama Agba was where the memory lived. She knew the family tree going back generations, who was descended from whom, which lineages had married into which, where the family had come from before it came to this town, what the founding ancestor had done, which praises belonged to the family and which taboos it kept and why. When a child asked who am I, where do we come from, it was Mama Agba who answered, and her answer was the family’s whole identity handed down. Lose her without asking, and you lost a book that had never been written and could never be recovered."}, {"id": "women-mama-agba#s5", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "She was the school. The small children were often left with her while the parents worked the farm or the market, and in those long days in the compound she taught them the things a person needs that are not taught in any classroom. She told them the folktales in the evenings, the tortoise stories with their sly lessons, and through the stories she taught them right from wrong, cleverness from foolishness, the values of the people. She taught them the proverbs, those compressed packages of wisdom that a Yoruba person carries for life. She corrected their manners, taught them how to greet, how to kneel or prostrate for elders, how to speak and when to be silent. A child raised at the feet of a good Mama Agba came out shaped, rooted, and mannered in a way no school could match."}, {"id": "women-mama-agba#s6", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "And she was the hospital and the pharmacy too, at least the first line of it. She knew the leaves. She knew which one to crush for a fever, which to boil for a cough, which to bind on a wound, which tea to give a woman after childbirth and which was dangerous and must never be touched. Generations of medical knowledge, tested by long use, sat in her head and her hands. When a child burned with fever in the night, before there was any thought of a distant clinic, it was Mama Agba who was woken, who felt the forehead, who knew what to do. She was the bank as well, in her quiet way, the one who kept a little put by, who could produce the small sum that rescued a grandchild in trouble, and the one whose approval you needed before the family spent on anything large. In one old woman on a low stool, a whole society’s institutions were gathered and kept alive."}, {"id": "women-mama-agba#s7", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "And then, one day, Mama Agba dies. It is in the nature of the elder mother that her story ends this way, in old age, surrounded if she is fortunate by the great crowd of descendants she has produced and shaped. Her death is not like other deaths. When a young person dies it is a wound, a thing against nature, and the grief is sharp and bitter. But when a very old woman dies full of years, having seen her children’s children’s children, the Yoruba do something that surprises outsiders. They mourn, yes, but they also celebrate. They dance. They call it a good death, a completed life, and they send her off with drumming and feasting, because she ran her whole race and finished it, and now she goes to join the ancestors, where she becomes something even greater than she was in life, a spirit who watches over the family from the other side."}, {"id": "women-mama-agba#s8", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "But even in that celebration there is a real loss, and the wise ones know it. When Mama Agba dies, the library closes. The songs she never taught anyone die with her. The corner of the family tree that only she remembered goes dark. The recipe for the medicine, the name of the ancestor, the reason for the taboo, the exact way the festival was done in the old days, all of it, if it was not passed on, is simply gone, as if a whole building had quietly burned down in the night with no one watching. The old Yoruba understood this, which is why they said that when an old person dies, a library burns to the ground. It is one of the truest things ever said about human memory, and it was said about her."}, {"id": "women-mama-agba#s9", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "Which is why, in the end, the story of Mama Agba is not only a story about the past. It is a warning and an instruction to the present. Sit with the old woman while she is still here. Ask her the questions. Learn the stories, the songs, the family history, the names, before the chance is gone forever, because it goes without warning and it does not come back. Every family that still has its Mama Agba is holding a treasure it may not fully see, and every one that has lost hers knows the particular ache of the questions that can never now be answered. She was the grandmother, yes. But she was also the memory of the whole people, kept alive in one patient woman at a time, on a low stool, in the shade, waiting for the children to come and sit and listen. Blessed is the family that listened while it still could."}]}, {"id": "women-princess-adunola", "emoji": "👸", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#2a0018,#500030)", "category": "obinrin", "rating": 9.4, "duration": "4 min", "title": {"en": "Princess Adunola: The Daughter Who Held Abeokuta", "yo": "Adunola"}, "desc": {"en": "The chief's daughter whose market and intelligence networks held Abeokuta through siege.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Great Yoruba Women", "secondary": [], "source_note": "", "segments": [{"id": "women-princess-adunola#s1", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Narrator opens: Not all of history's great women are recorded in formal archives. Some are remembered in the traditions of the communities they served — in oral histories, in the praise names that have been passed down, in the specific ways a community holds the memory of those who made its survival possible. Princess Adunola was the daughter of one of the senior Egba chiefs of Abeokuta and operated during the most dangerous period of Abeokuta's nineteenth-century history — the years of sustained military pressure from Dahomey and from Ibadan, when the city built under the Olumo Rock was repeatedly tested and when its survival required contributions from every sector of its population."}, {"id": "women-princess-adunola#s2", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Adunola's role was that of the Iyaloja — the market leader, the woman whose authority extended over the commercial life of Abeokuta. This was not a ceremonial title. The Iyaloja controlled the flow of goods in and out of the city — food, trade items, information. In a period of siege, this control was literally the difference between survival and starvation. Adunola organized the market women of Abeokuta with the precision of a logistics commander, ensuring that food supplies reached the defenders, that trade routes were maintained through the chaos of war, that the city's commercial life continued to function even as military pressure mounted."}, {"id": "women-princess-adunola#s3", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "She also organized women's intelligence networks — the invisible information systems that market women have always maintained, where knowledge travels from stall to stall faster than any official communication channel. She knew where Dahomean scouts were moving, which trade routes were compromised, which chiefs were wavering in their loyalty. She brought this information to the men's council because she had access to it and they didn't. The story of Adunola is partly the story of all market women in crisis — the women whose contribution to military campaigns is not counted in the histories that record battles and generals, but without whom the battles could not have been fought. Armies eat. Armies need information. Armies need morale management. The women who provided all of this are too often invisible in the historical record."}, {"id": "women-princess-adunola#s4", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "Adunola is their representative name. Her story stands for all the named and unnamed women of Abeokuta who held the city together from inside while the men fought from outside. Let her name be remembered."}]}, {"id": "women-yoruba-women-in-professions", "emoji": "👸", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#2a0018,#500030)", "category": "obinrin", "rating": 9.4, "duration": "4 min", "title": {"en": "Olubunmi Akerele-Okoye: Yoruba Women in the Professions", "yo": "Olubunmi Akerele-Okoye"}, "desc": {"en": "Olubunmi Akerele-Okoye and Yoruba women in the professions.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Great Yoruba Women", "secondary": [], "source_note": "", "segments": [{"id": "women-yoruba-women-in-professions#s1", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Narrator opens: The story of great Yoruba women does not end in the nineteenth century with Efunsetan and Moremi and Tinubu. It continues, with different weapons and different battlegrounds, into the twentieth and twenty-first centuries. In laboratories and hospitals and law courts and lecture halls, Yoruba women have been making their mark in ways that the women's historians of the nineteenth century might have dreamed but could not have anticipated."}, {"id": "women-yoruba-women-in-professions#s2", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "Dr. Olubunmi Akerele represents a generation of Yoruba women who took the promise of education — the free primary education that Awolowo launched in 1955, the mission school tradition that the Saro built in the nineteenth century — and carried it to its professional conclusion. She trained as a physician, rose to senior positions within the World Health Organization's traditional medicine program, and spent decades building the international case for the scientific engagement with African traditional medicine — the knowledge of Osanyin's herbs that had been dismissed by colonial medicine as superstition and was increasingly being recognized as a source of genuine pharmacological insight. Her work bridges the story of Yoruba women in history with the contemporary moment. She carries the legacy of the women who organized the markets and the protests and the intelligence networks, translated now into the language of international public health policy and medical research."}, {"id": "women-yoruba-women-in-professions#s3", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "She is one of thousands. There are Yoruba women surgeons, Yoruba women engineers, Yoruba women mathematicians, Yoruba women jurists, Yoruba women architects, Yoruba women entrepreneurs in technology, in agriculture, in finance. They are continuing, in the language of their era, the same argument that Efunsetan made with her trading empire and Funmilayo made with her mass organization: Yoruba women are not supporting characters in anyone else's story. They are principals. They have always been principals. The tradition is intact. The names have changed. The work continues."}]}, {"id": "women-women-of-abeokuta", "emoji": "👸", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#2a0018,#500030)", "category": "obinrin", "rating": 9.4, "duration": "4 min", "title": {"en": "The Women of Abeokuta: The Town That Women Saved", "yo": "The Women of Abeokuta: The Town That Women Saved"}, "desc": {"en": "How the women of Abeokuta helped turn back Dahomey's armies in 1851 and 1864.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Great Yoruba Women", "secondary": [], "source_note": "", "segments": [{"id": "women-women-of-abeokuta#s1", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Narrator opens: The story of Abeokuta is, at its core, a story about what women do when the city they have built is under threat. The Egba people, who founded Abeokuta in the 1830s as a refuge from the Yoruba civil wars, built their new city under the Olumo Rock with the organizational discipline of people who had survived the worst. And when Dahomey came for them — in 1851 and again in 1864 — it was partly the women of Abeokuta who turned the attacks back."}, {"id": "women-women-of-abeokuta#s2", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "The Dahomey Kingdom under King Gezo and then King Glele was one of the most powerful military forces in West Africa, and it was specifically organized to conduct raids on Yoruba towns. Its military — including the famous all-female regiment of the Dahomey Agojie, the women warriors who would later become the subject of global fascination — conducted raids into Yoruba territory to capture enslaved people for the Atlantic trade and to extend Dahomey's sphere of influence. When Dahomey attacked Abeokuta in 1851, the city was alert and organized. The women of the town — who had been watching from the walls and from the elevated positions near the Olumo Rock — participated actively in the defense. They carried ammunition. They brought food to the fighters. They organized the evacuation of children to safe positions. They communicated between different sectors of the walls. And some of them fought."}, {"id": "women-women-of-abeokuta#s3", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "The attack was repelled. Dahomey's army — massive, well-organized, experienced — was turned back by Abeokuta's defense. The missionaries at Abeokuta, including the returned Yoruba Christian Samuel Ajayi Crowther, reported the event with wonder. The city had held. The women had played a role that the conventional military histories would systematically understate. In 1864, Dahomey came again — with a larger army, greater firepower, and the determination to finish what the 1851 campaign had begun. Again, Abeokuta held. Again, women were among the defenders. The Olumo Rock, which served as both fortification and spiritual anchor, gave the city a psychological advantage that no military calculation could fully capture: these people believed their city was sacred ground, and that belief made them fight differently."}, {"id": "women-women-of-abeokuta#s4", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "The women of Abeokuta are not recorded as individual heroes in most histories. They are recorded as a collective — the women who organized, who supplied, who informed, who sometimes fought. But that collective is one of the most important facts of Abeokuta's survival. No individual hero's action explains why the city held against the most powerful raiding force in the region. What explains it is organized community defense in which women were not observers but participants. The women of Abeokuta saved their city. Twice. Their names were not put in the histories the British administrators wrote. But the city they defended still stands, and the Olumo Rock is still there, and people still climb it and look out over the red earth and remember that this place has been held before by people who refused to let it go."}]}, {"id": "women-margaret-ekpo", "emoji": "👸", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#2a0018,#500030)", "category": "obinrin", "rating": 9.4, "duration": "4 min", "title": {"en": "Chief (mrs) Margaret Ekpo: The Mother of Nigerian Female Politics", "yo": "Chief (mrs) Margaret Ekpo: The Mother of Nigerian Female Politics"}, "desc": {"en": "Margaret Ekpo, mother of Nigerian female politics.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Great Yoruba Women", "secondary": [], "source_note": "", "segments": [{"id": "women-margaret-ekpo#s1", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Narrator opens: The conversation about Yoruba great women rightly centers on Yoruba women. But the Nigerian women's political tradition that Funmilayo Ransome-Kuti helped build was not confined to Yorubaland. And among the women from other Nigerian traditions who shaped the national conversation in ways that directly affected Yoruba women's lives and the broader Nigerian feminist political landscape, Chief Margaret Ekpo deserves specific mention. Born in 1914 in Itu, Cross River State — an Ibibio woman — Margaret Ekpo was one of the most politically significant women in Nigerian history. She organized market women in the Calabar region in the 1940s and 1950s with the same methodology that Funmilayo was using in Abeokuta: going to the markets, sitting with the traders, understanding their specific grievances, building an organization from their collective power."}, {"id": "women-margaret-ekpo#s2", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "She was elected to the Eastern House of Assembly in 1954 — one of the first women elected to any Nigerian legislative body. She served in the Eastern Region government during the critical period of transition to independence and the early post-independence years. She was a participant in the pan-Nigerian women's political networks that connected the work being done by women across the country's different regions and ethnic communities. The connection to the Yoruba story is direct: the women's political movements that Funmilayo and Ekpo built were explicitly connected to each other. The women of the Abeokuta Women's Union and the women of the Calabar-Ogoja-Rivers women's organizations recognized each other as part of the same struggle — women demanding political representation in a newly independent country that was systematically excluding them from formal governance."}, {"id": "women-margaret-ekpo#s3", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "Margaret Ekpo's life demonstrated that the struggle was not Yoruba-specific. It was pan-Nigerian, and the strategies that worked in Abeokuta — mass organization, market-based mobilization, the assertion of women's economic power as a political instrument — worked in Calabar too. The sisterhood across ethnic lines was real, hard-won, and consequential. She died in 2006, at ninety-two. She had seen Nigerian independence, military rule, civilian governance, and the long struggle to get women from 3% of the legislature to a still-inadequate but slowly growing proportion. The work is not finished. Her example is not finished either."}]}, {"id": "women-iyalode-institution", "emoji": "👸", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#2a0018,#500030)", "category": "obinrin", "rating": 9.4, "duration": "4 min", "title": {"en": "The Iyalode Institution: Women's Authority in Yoruba Governance", "yo": "Iyalode"}, "desc": {"en": "The Iyalode institution and women's authority in Yoruba governance.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Great Yoruba Women", "secondary": [], "source_note": "", "segments": [{"id": "women-iyalode-institution#s1", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Narrator opens: Before Western feminism arrived in Africa with its vocabulary of women's liberation and gender equality, before the Universal Declaration of Human Rights, before the Nigerian constitution's formal provisions for equal political participation — the Yoruba world had the Iyalode. This was not a courtesy title or a decorative office. It was a substantive governing position, carrying real authority and real responsibilities, embedded in the traditional political structure of Yoruba communities. The Iyalode — the \"mother who speaks for all women,\" or more literally \"the one who handles affairs of the outside world on behalf of women\" — was a senior chief in every major Yoruba town. She was appointed through a process that recognized demonstrated leadership: the woman who became Iyalode was typically the most successful female trader, the woman with the largest network of market relationships, the one whose organizational capacity had already proven itself in commerce before being elevated to political office."}, {"id": "women-iyalode-institution#s2", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "Her responsibilities were comprehensive. She represented women's interests in the traditional council of chiefs — the male-dominated governing body that made decisions about town affairs. She was their voice when the men were discussing matters that affected women's lives: taxation, market regulations, security, land access, the organization of festivals and ceremonies. She had the right to speak, and in communities with strong Iyalode traditions, her voice was not ceremonial — it was effective. She also had direct authority over women's affairs: resolving disputes between women, organizing women's collective labor for community projects, managing the women's sections of major ceremonies, controlling access to certain women-specific spiritual practices. She was, in effect, the parallel chief of the female half of the community."}, {"id": "women-iyalode-institution#s3", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "The most famous Iyalode — Efunsetan of Ibadan, Tinubu of Abeokuta, the various Iyalode of different Yoruba towns — used their positions to exercise power that extended well beyond the conventional limits of their office. They became economic powers, political powers, figures whose authority was acknowledged by the men's councils because ignoring them was not politically viable. The Iyalode institution still exists in Yoruba communities today. The Iyalode of Ibadan, of Ile-Ife, of Abeokuta, of Ijebu-Ode, of Ondo — these are living offices, filled by living women, carrying the weight of a tradition that is at least five centuries old. In an era when women's political representation is still being contested at every level of Nigerian governance, the Iyalode is a reminder that the argument has already been settled — by the Yoruba tradition, at least. Women belong in governance. They always have. The institution that guarantees it is older than the nation that is still debating it."}]}, {"id": "women-sarah-forbes-bonetta", "emoji": "👸", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#2a0018,#500030)", "category": "obinrin", "rating": 9.4, "duration": "4 min", "title": {"en": "Princess Sarah Forbes Bonetta: The Yoruba Princess Who Became Queen Victoria's Goddaughter", "yo": "Aina"}, "desc": {"en": "The captive Yoruba girl who became Queen Victoria's goddaughter.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Great Yoruba Women", "secondary": [], "source_note": "", "segments": [{"id": "women-sarah-forbes-bonetta#s1", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Narrator opens: She was born around 1843, in a Yoruba town in the kingdom of Oke-Odan. She was the daughter of a chief. And then the Dahomey army came on a raid, and her parents were killed, and she was taken as a captive to the court of King Gezo of Dahomey. She was five years old. Her name, as far as history records, was Aina. What happened next is one of the most extraordinary stories in Victorian history — and in the history of the Yoruba diaspora in a direction no one anticipated: not west to Brazil or Cuba, but north to England, to the very court of Queen Victoria."}, {"id": "women-sarah-forbes-bonetta#s2", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "In 1850, Captain Frederick Forbes of the Royal Navy arrived in Dahomey on a diplomatic mission. He encountered the child Aina at Gezo's court — recognized her evident intelligence and her desperate situation — and asked Gezo to give her to him, as a gift for Queen Victoria. Gezo, in the political calculations of the moment, agreed. Forbes took the child. He named her Sarah Forbes Bonetta — Sarah for the queen's name (not quite — Victoria's second name was not Sarah, but the intention was clear), Forbes for his own surname, Bonetta for the ship that carried her."}, {"id": "women-sarah-forbes-bonetta#s3", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "She arrived in England in 1850 and was presented to Queen Victoria. What happened at that meeting — what the queen saw in the seven-year-old Yoruba girl — can be read in Victoria's diaries, which describe the child as \"sharp and intelligent\" and as \"a great pet\" at court. Victoria took personal interest in her. She paid for her education. She became, in every functional sense, Sarah's patron — and the relationship that developed between the Yoruba orphan and the British queen was one of the more remarkable of the Victorian era. Sarah was educated at the Church Missionary Society Female Institution in Freetown, Sierra Leone — joining the community of liberated Africans and returning Yoruba that was building modern African Christianity from that base. She was musically gifted, speaking multiple languages, and possessing an intelligence that impressed every teacher who encountered her."}, {"id": "women-sarah-forbes-bonetta#s4", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "She returned to England periodically, staying with the queen. She returned to West Africa. She eventually married James Pinson Labulo Davies — a Yoruba man of the Sierra Leonean educated community, a successful merchant. They had a daughter, Victoria, named for the queen who had been Sarah's patron. Sarah Forbes Bonetta Davies died in Madeira in 1880, of tuberculosis. She was approximately thirty-seven. Queen Victoria noted her death in her diary with genuine grief."}, {"id": "women-sarah-forbes-bonetta#s5", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "What do we make of this story? A Yoruba girl, orphaned by Dahomey's slave raids, taken to England, educated by Victorian philanthropy, brought into the domestic circle of the most powerful monarch in the world. Extraordinary opportunity? Undeniable. Complete erasure of her original identity and culture? Also present. The Yoruba child who became the queen's goddaughter is not a simple story of rescue. It is a story of the complicated ways in which the violence of the slave trade and the responses to it created lives that fit no simple narrative. What we can say with certainty: she was Yoruba. Her name was Aina before it was Sarah. She lived and died with a Queen's attention on her and the memory of a mother who was killed by Dahomey's army somewhere in the background of every extraordinary moment of her extraordinary life."}]}, {"id": "women-queen-oronsen", "emoji": "👸", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#2a0018,#500030)", "category": "obinrin", "rating": 9.4, "duration": "4 min", "title": {"en": "Queen Oronsen: The Warrior Queen of Okun Yoruba", "yo": "Oronsen"}, "desc": {"en": "Queen Oronsen, the warrior queen of the Okun Yoruba.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Great Yoruba Women", "secondary": [], "source_note": "", "segments": [{"id": "women-queen-oronsen#s1", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Narrator opens: The Okun Yoruba — the Yoruba-speaking communities of Kogi State, the eastern edge of Yorubaland — have their own traditions of great women, and among them stands Queen Oronsen: a female ruler of the Yagba community whose leadership during a period of sustained external threat is still remembered in the oral tradition of her people. The Yagba are one of the Okun Yoruba sub-groups, occupying the northeastern stretch of Yoruba territory where the culture meets the traditions of neighboring peoples. Their location — at the border of multiple zones of influence — meant that they faced threats from multiple directions, and their political tradition accordingly developed leaders who were defined by crisis management rather than ceremonial governance."}, {"id": "women-queen-oronsen#s2", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "Oronsen came to power — through the specific succession mechanisms of Yagba tradition — at a moment when external raiding was threatening the community's survival. She took the position of leadership not as a regent or a temporary stand-in but as the ruling authority, recognized by the community's traditional structures as the person most capable of organizing the defense and managing the political relationships that would determine whether the community survived. Her military leadership was direct: she organized the community's defense, directed the placement of fighters, managed the logistics of maintaining a community under pressure. She was not a distant symbol of authority — she was present in the decisions and, according to the tradition, present in the field."}, {"id": "women-queen-oronsen#s3", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "She is remembered in Yagba oral tradition as one of the founding figures of the community's identity — a woman who held what needed holding at a moment when the holding was genuinely difficult. Her name is part of the praise tradition of the Yagba, invoked alongside the male leaders of comparable eras as a ruler of equivalent standing. The Okun Yoruba traditions of female leadership are less widely known than those of the western Yoruba communities — less documented, less celebrated in national narratives, less studied by the scholars who have shaped the public understanding of Yoruba history. But they are there. Oronsen is there. Waiting to be found by those who look east in the Yoruba world, where the great traditions continue in communities that the major histories have not always reached."}]}, {"id": "women-women-warriors", "emoji": "👸", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#2a0018,#500030)", "category": "obinrin", "rating": 9.4, "duration": "4 min", "title": {"en": "The Women Warriors: Female Soldiers in the Yoruba Tradition", "yo": "The Women Warriors: Female Soldiers in the Yoruba Tradition"}, "desc": {"en": "Female soldiers in the Yoruba tradition.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Great Yoruba Women", "secondary": [], "source_note": "", "segments": [{"id": "women-women-warriors#s1", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Narrator opens: When people speak of female warriors in West Africa, the conversation usually moves immediately to the Agojie — the Dahomey kingdom's all-female military regiment, now globally known through film and scholarly attention. The Agojie were extraordinary and deserve every word written about them. But the focus on Dahomey has left a less-told story in its shadow: the presence and the role of women soldiers in the Yoruba military tradition. The Yoruba tradition did not have an institutionalized all-female military regiment in the Agojie mode. But it had women who fought — individually, in small groups, and in specific institutional roles within the war camp and the palace military establishment. These women are recorded in oral histories, in oriki, in the traditions of specific lineages, and in the accounts of nineteenth-century observers who encountered them with various degrees of surprise."}, {"id": "women-women-warriors#s2", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "In the Yoruba palace system, the Iyalode — the senior woman's title — commanded the loyalty and the organizational capacity of all women in the community, including in times of war. The Iyalode's network was not separate from the war effort — it was integral to it. She organized food supply, she managed intelligence from the market networks, she coordinated the evacuation of non-combatants. In this sense, every Iyalode was a military commander, even those who never personally carried a weapon. In Ibadan's tradition, there are specific accounts of women who participated in combat — women of warrior families who trained alongside the men, who accompanied war camps, and who fought when the situation required it. Their names are less well-recorded than their male counterparts, but they appear in the oral histories of specific lineages as fighters, as intelligence gatherers, as commanders of the sections of the war camp that were specifically organized and maintained by women."}, {"id": "women-women-warriors#s3", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "The defense of Abeokuta against Dahomey in 1851 and 1864 — already discussed in this collection — included women who took active roles in the defense. The Olumo Rock's defensive positions included female fighters who used whatever weapons were available. These women are not individually named in most histories, but their collective contribution is acknowledged in the oral tradition. Efunsetan Aniwura — the Iyalode of Ibadan — had her own warriors. Not a regiment, but a personal guard and military capacity that was genuinely functional. She was not a symbolic female chief. She was a woman with armed men and the ability to deploy them."}, {"id": "women-women-warriors#s4", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "The broader picture is of a military culture that was predominantly but not exclusively male — one in which women with the capacity and the will to participate in warfare found institutional roles and personal opportunities to do so. The Yoruba tradition did not celebrate this as the Dahomey tradition celebrated the Agojie. But it existed. And the women who lived it deserve to have their contribution named, even where their names were not recorded."}]}, {"id": "legends-sade-adu", "emoji": "🌟", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#00201a,#003a30)", "category": "legends", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "7 min", "title": {"en": "Sade Adu: The Voice That Chose Its Own Register", "yo": "Helen Folasade Adu"}, "desc": {"en": "Sade Adu, the Ibadan-born singer whose voice chose its own register and conquered the world quietly.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Living Legends", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Great Yoruba Women — consolidates “Sade Adu: The Voice That Chose Its Own Register”)", "segments": [{"id": "legends-sade-adu#s1", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "In a world that shouts, there is a particular kind of power in a voice that refuses to raise itself and yet commands the whole room to lean in and listen. That is the power of Sade. Born Helen Folasade Adu, known to the world by a single name, she became one of the best-selling and most beloved musicians to ever come out of Africa or Britain, and she did it entirely on her own terms, in a soft, smoky, unhurried voice that never once begged for anyone’s attention and received all of it anyway. Her story is a lesson in the strength of restraint, in a Yoruba girl who conquered the world without ever pretending to be anything other than exactly what she was."}, {"id": "legends-sade-adu#s2", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "She was born in 1959 in Ibadan, in western Nigeria, to a Nigerian father, an economics lecturer of the Yoruba people, and an English mother, a nurse. Folasade, the name her father gave her, carries the Yoruba blessing that honor or a crown has arrived, and it would prove more prophetic than most names do. When her parents’ marriage ended, she moved as a small child with her mother to England, and she grew up there, in a modest English setting, a girl with a Yoruba name and a Yoruba face making her way in a country that did not always know what to make of her. That double inheritance, African and English, never resolved into one thing or the other; it stayed double, and that doubleness became part of the cool, self-possessed distance that would define her art."}, {"id": "legends-sade-adu#s3", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "She did not set out to be a singer. She studied fashion design in London, and for a time it seemed she might make her life in clothes rather than in music. But she began singing with a band on the side, backup at first, and something happened when she stepped to the microphone that could not be ignored. People stopped. That quiet voice, paired with a stage presence that was all stillness and poise, was magnetic in a way that the loud performers around her were not. She formed a band, which she also named Sade, and in 1984 they released an album called Diamond Life, and the world changed for her overnight."}, {"id": "legends-sade-adu#s4", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Diamond Life was a phenomenon. It sold in enormous numbers across the world, won her a major music award as best new artist, and announced a sound that was entirely her own, a smooth, jazz-inflected soul that did not sound like anything else on the radio. The song Smooth Operator became a signature that people who could not name another of her songs still knew. And through all of it, she remained composed, elegant, and strikingly reluctant to play the game of celebrity. She let the music do the talking, and the music was more than enough."}, {"id": "legends-sade-adu#s5", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "What sets Sade apart, and what makes her a fitting subject for this collection of great women, is the fierce independence of how she managed her fame. In an industry that devours artists, that demands they tour endlessly, release constantly, and expose every corner of their private lives, Sade simply refused. She released albums years and sometimes more than a decade apart, appearing when she had something to say and vanishing when she did not. She guarded her privacy so completely that she became almost mythical, a superstar who might be your neighbor, who was famously seen living quietly, raising her daughter, tending a garden, entirely uninterested in the machinery of celebrity that others chased so desperately."}, {"id": "legends-sade-adu#s6", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "And here is the astonishing thing: it did not diminish her. It deepened her. Each time she returned, the world was waiting, and her records still sold in the millions to a devoted global audience that had never stopped loving her. She proved that you do not have to sell your privacy to sustain your art, that mystery can be a kind of loyalty to your audience rather than a betrayal of it, and that a woman gets to decide for herself how much of her belongs to the public. In refusing the terms the industry tried to impose, she quietly rewrote them."}, {"id": "legends-sade-adu#s7", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Over the decades the honors piled up, awards and record sales in the tens of millions and a place in the halls that enshrine the greats of popular music, and eventually recognition from the British crown itself. Younger artists across soul, hip hop, and R&B cite her constantly, sampling her songs and naming her as the template for a whole mood of music, cool and sensual and adult, that she more or less invented. There is an entire register of contemporary music that is, whether it knows it or not, singing in the key of Sade."}, {"id": "legends-sade-adu#s8", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "Now, some might ask why a British-based pop star belongs in a collection of great Yoruba women alongside warrior queens and market rebels. The answer is that the thread never broke. She carries a Yoruba name given by a Yoruba father, and she has carried it, unchanged and unanglicized, to the very top of global fame, so that hundreds of millions of people who have never been to Nigeria have a Yoruba word, Folasade, living in their mouths and their playlists. In a small but real way, every time the world says her name, it says a piece of Yoruba, and it says it with love. That is its own kind of cultural victory, quiet and lasting, exactly like the woman herself."}, {"id": "legends-sade-adu#s9", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So remember Sade Adu, and remember the particular strength she stands for. Not every powerful woman conquers by force or by confrontation. Some conquer by refusing to be rushed, by holding their own counsel, by choosing their own register and never once straining to be louder than they wish to be. She took a Yoruba blessing of a name, a name that promised a crown, and she wore that crown lightly, on her own terms, for forty years and counting. The girl from Ibadan who moved to England and was not sure the world had a place for her ended up teaching the world how to listen. That is the voice that chose its own register, and the world has been leaning in ever since."}]}, {"id": "legends-ngozi-okonjo-iweala", "emoji": "🌟", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#00201a,#003a30)", "category": "legends", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "7 min", "title": {"en": "DR. Ngozi Okonjo-Iweala: The Woman at the Head of the Table", "yo": "Ngozi Okonjo-Iweala"}, "desc": {"en": "The economist who took the head of the table at the World Trade Organization, and the Yoruba thread in her story.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Living Legends", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Great Yoruba Women — consolidates “Dr. Ngozi Okonjo-Iweala and the Yoruba Thread”)", "segments": [{"id": "legends-ngozi-okonjo-iweala#s1", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "A note of honesty belongs at the head of this story, the kind of honesty the elders always insisted upon. Ngozi Okonjo-Iweala is not, strictly, a Yoruba woman; she is of Igbo origin, from the Delta country. But she is included in this collection, and rightly so, because her life is woven tightly into the shared Nigerian story that the Yoruba world is part of, because she made her career and much of her life in Lagos and the wider country, and because the greatness this collection honors, the greatness of women who walk into rooms that were closed to them and refuse to leave, knows no single ethnic border. Her story is a Nigerian story and an African story, and it belongs here as an honored guest."}, {"id": "legends-ngozi-okonjo-iweala#s2", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "She was born in 1954 in the Delta, the daughter of a royal family and of a father who was both a king and a professor of economics. Learning and leadership were her inheritance from both sides. She was educated in Nigeria and then abroad at the very summit of the academic world, earning her degrees from Harvard and from the Massachusetts Institute of Technology, one of the finest economic educations available anywhere on earth. She did not do this in an age that made it easy for an African woman to sit in those halls. She did it by being undeniable, by mastering her field so thoroughly that the doors that might have stayed shut had to open."}, {"id": "legends-ngozi-okonjo-iweala#s3", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "She rose through the World Bank, that vast global institution that shapes the fortunes of nations, climbing over twenty-five years to become one of its most senior figures, a managing director trusted with enormous responsibility. But the chapter that made her a household name in Nigeria was her return home to serve as her country’s Minister of Finance, a post she held twice under different presidents. And she took it on at a moment when the job was less an honor than a battlefield."}, {"id": "legends-ngozi-okonjo-iweala#s4", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Nigeria’s public finances were a tangle of debt, opacity, and corruption. Okonjo-Iweala went to work with the cold nerve of a surgeon. She led the negotiations that won Nigeria an enormous debt relief deal, wiping away tens of billions of dollars of crushing obligations and giving the country room to breathe. She introduced transparency where there had been darkness, publishing what the government was actually spending and sending to the states, so that ordinary citizens could for the first time see the flow of their own money. She fought the entrenched interests who grew fat on the old darkness, and she made powerful and dangerous enemies doing it."}, {"id": "legends-ngozi-okonjo-iweala#s5", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "The price of that courage came in the most brutal way imaginable. In 2012, as she pressed her reforms and battled corruption, her elderly mother was kidnapped, a naked act of intimidation meant to break her will and force her to back down. Sit with the horror of that: her fight against corruption was answered by criminals seizing her aged mother. And yet she did not yield. Her mother was eventually freed, and Okonjo-Iweala kept fighting. That refusal to be intimidated, when the cost was pressed against the person she loved most, is the truest measure of the steel in her."}, {"id": "legends-ngozi-okonjo-iweala#s6", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "She wrote about her battles with corruption with unusual frankness, because she believed that the fight had to be brought into the open to be won, that secrecy was the corrupt man’s best friend and that sunlight was the reformer’s sharpest tool. She was not always popular; serious reformers rarely are, because reform means taking something away from someone who was comfortable with the way things were. But she was respected, even by her enemies, as someone who could not be bought and would not be scared."}, {"id": "legends-ngozi-okonjo-iweala#s7", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Then, in 2021, came the crowning recognition. Ngozi Okonjo-Iweala was chosen to lead the World Trade Organization, becoming the first woman and the first African ever to hold that office, one of the most powerful economic positions on the entire planet, the referee of the rules by which the whole world trades. Think of the arc of that life: from a girl in the Delta, through Harvard and MIT, through the trenches of Nigerian finance where they kidnapped her mother to try to stop her, to the very head of the table where the nations of the earth sit down to do business. And she got there in the depths of a global pandemic, taking the helm of world trade at a moment of crisis, an African woman telling the assembled powers of the world how the game would be run."}, {"id": "legends-ngozi-okonjo-iweala#s8", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "So why does her story belong in this collection, and what does it teach? It teaches that the greatness of the Yoruba warrior queens, the courage of Moremi, the defiance of Tinubu, the stubborn nerve of Funmilayo, did not end with the old world. It lives on in a new form, in the boardrooms and the ministries and the great global institutions, wherever an African woman walks in and takes the seat that no one like her was ever meant to occupy. Okonjo-Iweala did with spreadsheets and treaties what the old heroines did with armies and market unions: she refused to accept the place she was assigned, and she made the powerful reckon with her."}, {"id": "legends-ngozi-okonjo-iweala#s9", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "Remember her, then, not only as an economist or a diplomat, but as one more woman in a long and unbroken line, stretching back through the centuries of this collection, of women who were told that certain rooms were not for them and who walked in anyway and sat down at the head of the table. She is Igbo by birth and Nigerian by life and African to the bone, and she stands here among the great women of the Yoruba world as kin, because courage recognizes courage, and the fight she fought is the same fight, only the weapons have changed. From the Delta to the head of world trade, she carried the same simple, revolutionary refusal: I will not be moved, and I will not be bought, and I will sit exactly where my work has earned me the right to sit."}]}, {"id": "legends-fela", "emoji": "🌟", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#00201a,#003a30)", "category": "legends", "rating": 9.6, "duration": "11 min", "title": {"en": "Fela: The Man Who Sang Against the Storm", "yo": "Fela Anikulapo-Kuti"}, "desc": {"en": "The father of Afrobeat, who carried death in his pouch and sang against the storm of military rule.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Living Legends", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Alaaye, the story of Fela Anikulapo-Kuti, the one who carried death in his pouch, father of Afrobeat, the coffin-bearer, the thorn in the side of soldiers)", "segments": [{"id": "legends-fela#s1", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Gather close, children, for tonight I do not sing of a king who wore beads in the time before roads. Tonight I sing of a man some of you may have seen with your own eyes, or heard from a speaker in your father’s parlour, a man whose voice is still living breath, and whose name means the very thing he was. Fela. Fela Anikulapo-Kuti. Anikulapo. It means, I have carried death in my pouch. A name he chose for himself, because he said the old name he was given, Ransome, was a slave name, and no man born free should carry the mark of another man’s rope. But let me begin where all people begin, at the mouth of the mother."}, {"id": "legends-fela#s2", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "He was born Olufela Olusegun Oludotun Ransome-Kuti, in the town of Abeokuta, under the great rocks, in the year 1938. Do not let the music fool you into thinking he came from nothing, scraping for his supper. No. Fela was born into one of the proudest, most learned families the Yoruba have produced. His father, the Reverend Israel Oludotun Ransome-Kuti, was a clergyman and a schoolmaster, a stern man of hymns and grammar, the first president of the Nigeria Union of Teachers. His mother, ah, his mother."}, {"id": "legends-fela#s3", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "His mother was Funmilayo Ransome-Kuti. Yes. The same lioness of Abeokuta I told you of not long ago, the woman who gathered ten thousand market women and made a king abdicate, the first woman in this country to drive a car, the one who crossed the world speaking for the ones without a voice. That was the breast Fela was suckled at. That was the courage poured into him before he could walk. When people wondered where Fela found the nerve to stand before soldiers and generals and spit in the face of their power, the elders only smiled and said, have you forgotten whose son he is? The apple, they say, does not travel far from the tree that dropped it, and this apple fell into a fire."}, {"id": "legends-fela#s4", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "The house was full of famous cousins too. Wole Soyinka, who would one day win the world’s greatest prize for letters, was of this same clan. Learning ran in the blood like water in the Ogun river. They sent him to London in 1958, to become a doctor, as good families did with their bright sons. But the boy had another god pulling at his ribs, and that god was music. He enrolled instead at Trinity College of Music, and he broke his parents’ careful plan the way a river breaks a fence. He studied the trumpet, the piano, he swallowed the theory of European classical music whole, and then he went looking for something of his own. He formed a band. He married a London woman named Remilekun. He played highlife and jazz in cold English clubs, a young African finding his fingers."}, {"id": "legends-fela#s5", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "But it was not until he crossed the water to America, in 1969, that the lightning struck him. In America, in that year of fists raised in the air and cities burning with a long-held anger, Fela met a woman named Sandra Smith. She was of the Black Panther movement, and she put books in his hands. Malcolm X. The words of men who taught that the black man was not a beggar at the world’s table but a king robbed of his throne. Fela had gone to America a musician. He came back a prophet with a drum."}, {"id": "legends-fela#s6", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "He looked at his own music and found it too soft, too borrowed. So he built something new, something that had never existed before. He took the highlife of West Africa, the jazz of black America, the deep talking drums of the Yoruba, the call and the answer of the shrine, and he welded them into one long, hypnotic, unstoppable groove. He called it Afrobeat. Not the light thing that borrows the name today. The real thing. Songs that ran twenty, thirty minutes, building like a storm coming over the rocks, the horns stabbing, the shekere hissing, and over it all his voice, half sung, half preached."}, {"id": "legends-fela#s7", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "And he stopped singing in the fine English of his father’s schoolhouse. He sang in Pidgin, the broken tongue of the streets, the language every hawker and taxi driver and market woman could understand. Because he did not want to sing for the few who dined with forks. He wanted to sing for the many who ate with their hands, and he wanted them to hear exactly what he was saying about the men who ruled over them. He built his own compound in Lagos and named it the Kalakuta Republic, and he declared it independent of Nigeria, a free country inside a country. He built a nightclub, the Shrine, and there, night after night, he played and he preached, and the people came in their thousands."}, {"id": "legends-fela#s8", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "Now hear me, children, for this is the marrow of the story. Fela did not sing about love. He sang about power, and about the abuse of it. When soldiers ruled Nigeria with the boot and the whip, Fela named them in his songs. Zombie, he called the soldier who obeys without thinking, marching where he is pointed, killing where he is aimed, a dead man walking with a gun. The whole country sang Zombie in the streets, and the soldiers heard their own people laughing at them, and they did not forgive it. He sang of the woman who bleaches her skin and apes the white man’s ways. He sang of the government stealing while the people starved. He sang of the colonial mentality that stayed in the mind long after the colonizer sailed home. He held up a mirror to the powerful, and the powerful hate a mirror above all things."}, {"id": "legends-fela#s9", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "So they came for him. Again and again they came. They arrested him more than two hundred times in his life. They beat him. But the worst day, the day the elders speak of with lowered voices, came in 1977. A thousand soldiers surrounded the Kalakuta Republic. They burned it to the ground. They beat Fela nearly to death. And they threw his mother, Funmilayo, the old lioness of Abeokuta, from an upper window. She was near eighty years old. The fall broke her, and from those injuries she died the following year. They had killed his mother to punish his song."}, {"id": "legends-fela#s10", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "A lesser man would have folded like wet paper. Fela took his mother’s coffin, and he carried it to the barracks of the head of state, and he laid it at the gate of Dodan Barracks so that the ruler would have to look at what his soldiers had done. Then he wrote two of the fiercest songs he ever made, Coffin for Head of State, and Unknown Soldier, mocking the government’s lie that an unknown soldier, and not the army itself, had burned his home. He did not carry death in his pouch as an empty boast. He looked death in the eye and sang louder."}, {"id": "legends-fela#s11", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "I will not paint him as a saint, children, for a griot who lies to you is worse than a thief. Fela had shadows as long as his light. In one single day he married twenty-seven women, many of them his dancers and singers, and though he later said he did it to protect them from the police who called them prostitutes, it is a thing the wise still argue over. He preached freedom yet ruled his own compound like an old king. He scorned some of the modern medicine that might have saved him. He was a man of enormous appetites and enormous contradictions, generous and impossible, tender and tyrannical, all in the one skin."}, {"id": "legends-fela#s12", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "But this is the Afonja lesson I have taught you before. Do not flatten a great man into a single smooth thing. Hold his courage and his flaws in the two palms of your hands at once, and weigh them honestly. That is respect. That is history. He died in 1997, of the sickness that was sweeping the world in those years, the one he had refused to name or fear. And when he died, Lagos, the city that the soldiers ruled, stopped. More than a million people poured into the streets to walk with his body. A million. For a man who had never held an office, never worn a crown, never commanded an army. Only a saxophone, a piano, a microphone, and a courage that would not bend."}, {"id": "legends-fela#s13", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "They could arrest the man two hundred times. They could burn his house and kill his mother and beat him bloody. But they could never once make him sing their song instead of his own. That, children, is what it means to carry death in your pouch. It does not mean you cannot die. It means you have already made your peace with dying, so that no man can rule you with the fear of it. When they had nothing left to threaten him with, they had nothing left at all. His sons carry the horn still. His Shrine still fills with the faithful. And somewhere in the long groove of every drum that refuses to be quiet, Fela is still preaching to the ones who eat with their hands."}, {"id": "legends-fela#s14", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "Tomorrow I will tell you of another son of this soil who fought with a different weapon, not the saxophone but the pen, a man who was locked in a cell for twenty-two months with no paper, and who wrote anyway, on toilet roll and between the lines of smuggled books, and who carried our stories all the way to the highest table in the world."}]}, {"id": "legends-wole-soyinka", "emoji": "🌟", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#00201a,#003a30)", "category": "legends", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "8 min", "title": {"en": "Wole Soyinka: The Man the Tiger Could Not Silence", "yo": "Wole Soyinka"}, "desc": {"en": "Kongi, the first African Nobel laureate in literature, whom no tyrant could silence.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Living Legends", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Alaaye, consolidates the accounts of Akinwande Oluwole Babatunde Soyinka, Kongi, the first African and first black man to win the Nobel Prize in Literature, playwright, poet, prisoner of conscience)", "segments": [{"id": "legends-wole-soyinka#s1", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Children, last night I told you of Fela, who fought the tiger with a saxophone. Tonight I tell you of his kinsman, a man of the same proud Abeokuta clan, who fought the tiger with a pen and a stubbornness so deep that not even a solitary prison cell could reach the bottom of it. His name is Akinwande Oluwole Babatunde Soyinka. The world calls him Wole. And he once said a thing that tells you his whole spirit in a single breath. A tiger, he said, does not proclaim its tigritude. It pounces."}, {"id": "legends-wole-soyinka#s2", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "He was born in 1934, in Abeokuta, under those same great rocks that raised Fela and Funmilayo. His father, Samuel Ayodele Soyinka, was a schoolmaster, the headmaster of a mission school, a man of chalk and discipline whom the family called Essay, after his initials, S.A. His mother, Grace Eniola, was a shopkeeper and a force of the church, and because of her fervent faith the children called her the Wild Christian. From these two, the schoolmaster and the Wild Christian, came a boy of ferocious curiosity, who wrote a whole enchanted book about his childhood among the spirits of the parsonage and the tree that he was sure the aeroplanes were tangled in. He was Yoruba to the marrow. Not the church god alone but Ogun, the god of iron, of the road, of creation and destruction both, became the deep well he drew his art from all his life."}, {"id": "legends-wole-soyinka#s3", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "He studied at Ibadan, then at the University of Leeds in England, where the great tongue of Shakespeare became a tool in his own hand. He worked in the London theatres. And then, in the very year Nigeria became free of Britain, in 1960, he came home, carrying a scholarship to travel the country and study its own drama, its masquerades, its festivals, the deep theatre that had been performed in the marketplaces of his people long before any European built a stage. And he began to write. Plays that mixed the Yoruba masquerade with the Greek chorus, poems dense as forests, a language so rich that lazy readers cursed him and patient readers wept."}, {"id": "legends-wole-soyinka#s4", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "But do not think him only a man of the study and the stage. Here is where the tiger pounces. In 1965, it is said, he walked into a radio station in the west of Nigeria at the time of a stolen election, and forced them at the point of an argument to broadcast the truth instead of the government’s lie. And when the great war came, the war over Biafra that starved children by the hundred thousand, Soyinka did not stay safe and silent. He travelled to try to broker a peace, to stop the killing. For that, the government of General Gowon threw him in prison. And they did not merely jail him. They held him for twenty-two months, and most of it in solitary confinement, alone in a cell, denied books, denied paper, denied the company of a single human voice."}, {"id": "legends-wole-soyinka#s5", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "Think on it, children. They took a man whose entire soul was made of words, and they tried to starve him of words as you would starve a body of bread. They thought the silence would break him. It did not. He made ink from what he could find. He wrote on toilet paper, between the printed lines of the few books smuggled to him, on cigarette packets, in the margins of everything. He composed whole poems in his head and held them there in the dark. And when at last they let him out, he carried that prison out with him in a book he called The Man Died, and its title came from his own hard saying, that the man dies in all who keep silent in the face of tyranny. The man dies in all who keep silent. Remember that line. It is the whole of him."}, {"id": "legends-wole-soyinka#s6", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "He went into exile more than once when the soldiers ruled and put a price on his head. Under the worst of the tyrants, the one called Abacha, Soyinka fled the country on the back of a motorcycle through the bush, they say, to the border, and a military court sentenced him to death while he was away. He kept speaking from abroad. He never once traded his tongue for his safety. And in 1986, the news came that changed how the whole world looked at the literature of Africa. Wole Soyinka was awarded the Nobel Prize in Literature. The first African. The first black man anywhere on the earth to receive it. He did not use his moment of glory to preen. He dedicated the honour to Nelson Mandela, who was still then rotting in a South African cell, and he turned the world’s eye toward the crime of apartheid."}, {"id": "legends-wole-soyinka#s7", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Now, the Afonja lesson, children, always. Soyinka is not a simple hero to all. His writing is hard, and some of his own people have called him distant, a man who wrote for professors more than for market women, the very opposite of what Fela chose. He has been fierce and unforgiving in his quarrels, quick to scorn those he thinks are fools or cowards, and he has made enemies among people who ought to have been his friends. Greatness of his kind comes with sharp edges, and a griot who files them off is lying to you. But hold the two palms together and weigh him. On one side, a difficult, proud, uncompromising man. On the other, sixty years of refusing to let any government, foreign or homegrown, tell his people what they were allowed to say. Weigh it honestly, and the scale comes down hard on the side of the free tongue."}, {"id": "legends-wole-soyinka#s8", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "He is old now, the white hair standing about his head like a crown he grew himself, and still he speaks, still he scolds the powerful, still he writes. He has taught in the great universities of the world, and he has always come home. What I want you to carry from him is not the prize, though the prize was sweet. It is the cell. It is the man alone in the dark with no paper, writing anyway. Because that is the thing the tiger never understood. You can lock away the body. You cannot lock away the sentence a man is composing in his own head. The word outlasts the wall."}, {"id": "legends-wole-soyinka#s9", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "Tomorrow I will lay down the pen and the saxophone, and I will bring you the talking drum and the guitar, for I will tell you of a king of a different kind, the Minister of Enjoyment, the man who took the sound of the Yoruba drum and made the whole world dance to it."}]}, {"id": "legends-king-sunny-ade", "emoji": "🌟", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#00201a,#003a30)", "category": "legends", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "7 min", "title": {"en": "King Sunny Ade: The Minister of Enjoyment", "yo": "Sunday Adeniyi Adegeye"}, "desc": {"en": "KSA, the king of Juju music, who carried the talking drum to the world.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Living Legends", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Alaaye, consolidates the accounts of Sunday Adeniyi Adegeye, KSA, the King of Juju music, the Chairman, the man who carried the Yoruba talking drum to the ears of the whole world)", "segments": [{"id": "legends-king-sunny-ade#s1", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Children, put away the heavy things now. Dry the eyes that wept for the prison cell and the burned house. Tonight the story dances, because tonight I sing of the man they call the Minister of Enjoyment, the Chairman, the King of Juju. His name is Sunday Adeniyi Adegeye, and the whole world knows him as King Sunny Ade. He was born in 1946, in Ondo, into a family of the Akure royal line, a family of standing. His mother was a church organist, so music sat in the house from his first breath. His people had a clear road planned for him, the road of books and a respectable profession. They sent him to school expecting a doctor or a lawyer to come out the other end."}, {"id": "legends-king-sunny-ade#s2", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "But the boy heard the guitar the way Fela heard the horn and Soyinka heard the word. It called him, and he could not un-hear it. So he did a thing that broke his parents’ hearts for a season. He left his studies in Lagos, told them a small lie about where he was going, and disappeared into the world of the working musicians, the palm-wine bars and the party circuit, to learn his craft from the bottom up. A prince who ran away to play the guitar. His family thought he had thrown his life in the gutter."}, {"id": "legends-king-sunny-ade#s3", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "He played in other men’s bands first, paying his dues, learning the trade. And then in the mid nineteen sixties he formed his own band, the Green Spots, later the African Beats. And he took the music called Juju, the guitar music built on the foundation of the Yoruba talking drum, and he did to it what a master weaver does to ordinary thread. He made it glorious."}, {"id": "legends-king-sunny-ade#s4", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Hear what Juju is, children, for it is a marvel of our people. At its heart beats the dundun, the talking drum, the hourglass drum squeezed under the arm so that it rises and falls and actually speaks the tones of the Yoruba language. In the hands of Sunny Ade’s drummers, that drum did not merely keep time. It spoke proverbs. It called the names of the wealthy guests at the party. It praised, it teased, it prayed, all in rhythm. And over it he layered guitars, many guitars, weaving bright interlocking lines like sunlight through leaves, and the pedal steel guitar crying like a human voice, and voices answering voices in the old call-and-answer of the shrine."}, {"id": "legends-king-sunny-ade#s5", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "His songs were long, flowing rivers of sound that could carry a party from dusk until the birds sang again. He did not shout down governments the way his kinsman Fela did. That was not his war. His gift was joy. He gave the Yoruba people the sound of their own celebration, their weddings, their naming ceremonies, their chieftaincy parties, made beautiful and grand. To be sprayed with money while King Sunny Ade’s band played your praise name on the talking drum, ah, that was to have arrived in life. But do not mistake joy for smallness. To lift a whole people’s spirit, to give them a music that says you are worthy of celebration, that is its own kind of freedom, and it is not a lesser one."}, {"id": "legends-king-sunny-ade#s6", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Then came the moment that made him more than a local king. In the early nineteen eighties, when the world outside was hungry for a new sound from Africa, the doors opened, and King Sunny Ade walked through carrying his talking drums. He signed with a label that took him across the oceans. His album Juju Music, and the ones that followed, played in the great halls of Europe and America. Critics who had never heard a dundun in their lives sat with their mouths open. They called him the successor to the world’s biggest reggae star as the voice of a continent. He toured the world with a band twenty strong, a moving kingdom of drummers and guitarists in flowing agbada, and he made foreigners who did not know a word of Yoruba dance until they sweated."}, {"id": "legends-king-sunny-ade#s7", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "He took the talking drum, the very instrument our ancestors used to send messages across the hills, and he made it speak to the whole earth. Now the honest palm, children, the Afonja lesson. Sunny Ade’s road to the world was not a straight triumph. The great international fame did not last as long as some hoped. Tastes changed, deals soured, and he came home from the mountain of world stardom to be, once more, the king of his own house rather than the emperor of all. Some whispered that he had reached and not fully grasped."}, {"id": "legends-king-sunny-ade#s8", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "But hear the wisdom in how he took it. He did not break. He came home and he kept playing, for fifty years and more he kept playing, and he built things that outlast a hit song. He set up a foundation for musicians. He fought for the rights of performers so that the young ones coming after him would not be cheated as musicians of old were cheated. He became an elder, a chief, a giver of advice, the Chairman that the whole industry looks up to. The prince who ran away to play the guitar became a king in truth, not of a town but of a whole music. And the parents who wept when he abandoned his studies lived to see the world bow to their runaway son."}, {"id": "legends-king-sunny-ade#s9", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "That is the lesson I leave with you tonight. There are many ways to serve your people. One man serves with defiance, one with the difficult truth, and one, this one, with joy, with beauty, with the pure gift of making the talking drum sing your name so sweetly that you remember, in the middle of your dancing, that it is a fine and blessed thing simply to be alive and Yoruba and here. Tomorrow I will cross the water again, to the new voices, the young ones who carry our sound now, the daughters and sons who sing in the tongue of the whole world yet never forget the drum underneath."}]}, {"id": "legends-chinua-achebe", "emoji": "🌟", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#00201a,#003a30)", "category": "legends", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "7 min", "title": {"en": "Chinua Achebe: The Man Who Gave Africa Back Its Own Story", "yo": "Chinua Achebe: The Man Who Gave Africa Back Its Own Story"}, "desc": {"en": "The father of the modern African novel, who gave Africa back its own story.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Living Legends", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Alaaye, the story of Albert Chinualumogu Achebe, father of the modern African novel, the elephant of Ogidi, the man who taught the world that until the lion learns to write, the tale will always glorify the hunter)", "segments": [{"id": "legends-chinua-achebe#s1", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Children, tonight I must be honest with you before I begin, for a griot who deceives is worse than a thief. The man I tell you of tonight was not Yoruba. He was Igbo, of the eastern forests, of the town of Ogidi. But I bring him to this fire anyway, and gladly, because his fight was our fight, and because he said the very thing that gives a griot his reason to exist. He said that until the lions have their own historian, the history of the hunt will always glorify the hunter. That, children, is why I sit at this fire every night. So that our lions will have their historian."}, {"id": "legends-chinua-achebe#s2", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "He was born Albert Chinualumogu Achebe in 1930, in Ogidi, in the east of what would become Nigeria. His father, Isaiah Okafor Achebe, was one of the early converts to the church, a catechist who travelled teaching the new faith. So the boy grew up standing with one foot in two worlds, the world of the church and the English book, and the world of his uncle’s compound down the road where the old gods and the old masquerades still lived and breathed. He was a bright child, so bright they gave him a scholarship, and he shed the borrowed English name Albert and took up his own name, Chinua. He went to study at Ibadan, here among us in the west, expecting to become a doctor, but literature seized him instead, as it seizes all its chosen ones."}, {"id": "legends-chinua-achebe#s3", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Now hear why this man matters to every African child, Yoruba, Igbo, Hausa, all of us. In the school in those days, the books that spoke of Africa were written by Europeans. And in those books, our grandfathers were savages in the bush, without reason, without history, without souls worth the ink. A famous English writer had written of the great river as a place of darkness and grunting shadows. And the young Achebe, reading these books, made a discovery that turned his stomach. He realized that in the story the white man told, he, Achebe, the reader, was not meant to be one of the people on the ship. He was meant to be one of the shadows on the shore. And he decided, this young man, that he would answer. Not with a spear. With a book."}, {"id": "legends-chinua-achebe#s4", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "In 1958 he published a novel called Things Fall Apart. It is, perhaps, the most important book to come out of the whole of Africa. He wrote of a man named Okonkwo, a proud wrestler and farmer of an Igbo village, in the days when the missionaries and the district officers first came. And he wrote of that village not as a place of darkness but as a whole living civilization, with its own laws, its own poetry, its own gods and courts and comedies and cruelties, a society as complex and complete as any in Europe. And then he showed how the coming of the stranger made it all fall apart. The title itself he borrowed from a European poet, and in doing so he turned the master’s own tools back upon him."}, {"id": "legends-chinua-achebe#s5", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "That book has been read by tens of millions, in more than fifty languages, in every corner of the earth. For countless people who are not African, it is the first true window they ever had into an African village seen from the inside, by one of its own sons, and not gawked at from the deck of a passing ship. He wrote in English, the colonizer’s tongue, and for this some criticized him, saying a true African should write in his mother tongue. But Achebe answered wisely. He said he would take the English language, this thing left behind, and he would bend it, and season it with Igbo proverbs and rhythms and the smell of the palm-wine hut, until it could carry the full weight of African experience. He would make the master’s language serve the servant’s story. Proverbs, he taught us, are the palm oil with which words are eaten."}, {"id": "legends-chinua-achebe#s6", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "The Afonja lesson, always, children. Achebe’s life carried deep sorrow and hard choices. When the terrible war came, the war of Biafra, he took the side of his own eastern people who sought to break away, and he served their cause and grieved as his homeland starved and burned. He carried that wound all his life, and late in his years he wrote a hard, mournful book about it that stirred old angers, for the wounds of that war are not fully healed even now. He was a man of his people, and that means he was inside the quarrel, not floating above it. And in his later years, a car accident on a bad Nigerian road left him unable to walk, and he lived and taught abroad, in America, a giant carried in a chair, still writing, still teaching, never silent."}, {"id": "legends-chinua-achebe#s7", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "He turned down honours from his own government more than once, in protest at how the country was misruled, refusing to let a title from corrupt hands paper over the suffering of the ordinary people. He died in 2013, far from Ogidi, but they carried him home to the eastern earth to rest. Why do I, a Yoruba griot, honour this Igbo son at our fire? Because he did for the written page what I try to do at this fire. He refused to let strangers be the only ones who tell us who we are. He picked up the pen the way a warrior picks up a blade, and he wrote our humanity back into the record of the world."}, {"id": "legends-chinua-achebe#s8", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "Until the lion has its own historian, the story will always praise the hunter. Achebe was our lion’s historian, and because of him, and the many he inspired, no one can ever again say that Africa had no story of its own until the white man came to write it. Tomorrow I will bring you a daughter of this same lineage of storytellers, a young woman of our own time who took Achebe’s torch in her hand and carried it further than even he could have dreamed, all the way to the ears of the whole modern world."}]}, {"id": "legends-chimamanda-adichie", "emoji": "🌟", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#00201a,#003a30)", "category": "legends", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "7 min", "title": {"en": "Chimamanda Ngozi Adichie: The Keeper of the Many Stories", "yo": "Chimamanda Ngozi Adichie: The Keeper of the Many Stories"}, "desc": {"en": "Achebe's torch-bearer, who warned the world of the danger of a single story.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Living Legends", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Alaaye, the story of Chimamanda Ngozi Adichie, the daughter who took Achebe’s torch, the woman who warned the world of the danger of a single story)", "segments": [{"id": "legends-chimamanda-adichie#s1", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Children, last night I told you of Achebe, the lion’s historian, and I promised you a daughter who would carry his torch further than he could dream. Here she is. Her name is Chimamanda Ngozi Adichie. Like Achebe she is Igbo, not Yoruba, and again I bring her to our fire without shame, because the story she guards belongs to all of us, and because she is of our time, walking the earth now as I speak, a young elder we are blessed to share the world with."}, {"id": "legends-chimamanda-adichie#s2", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "She was born in 1977, in the city of Enugu, and she grew up in the university town of Nsukka. And hear this wonder, children. The very house her family lived in on that campus had once been the home of Chinua Achebe himself. She grew up, literally, in the great man’s old house, walking the floors he had walked. It is as if the ancestors themselves arranged the passing of the torch from one hand to the next, and set the stage before she was even born. Her father, James Nwoye Adichie, was a professor of statistics, the first professor of that subject in the country. Her mother, Grace Ifeoma, was the first woman to be the registrar of that same university. So she came, like so many of our finest, from a house of books and firsts, a house where a girl was expected to think."}, {"id": "legends-chimamanda-adichie#s3", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "They started her, too, on the road toward medicine, the road our bright children are always pushed down. She studied medicine and pharmacy for a while at Nsukka. But the words were calling, as they always call the chosen, and at nineteen she left for America to study, and there she followed the pen instead of the scalpel, taking degrees in communication and political science, and later in writing itself. And she began to write, and the world sat up."}, {"id": "legends-chimamanda-adichie#s4", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Her first novel, Purple Hibiscus, told of a girl under the roof of a father who was a pillar of the church in public and a tyrant behind his own door. Her second, Half of a Yellow Sun, returned to the terrible war of Biafra that had wounded Achebe, and told it through the eyes of ordinary people caught in it, the lovers and the servants and the children, so that a new generation who had only heard the war as a rumour could feel it as a human grief. That book won her the great Orange Prize and set her name among the finest novelists alive. Then she wrote Americanah, the story of a Nigerian woman in America discovering that in that land she had become, for the first time in her life, a black woman, a thing she had never had to think about at home, and all that this discovery meant."}, {"id": "legends-chimamanda-adichie#s5", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "But children, if you take one thing from this woman, take the small talk she gave that flew around the whole world. She stood before a crowd and spoke of what she called the danger of a single story. Hear her wisdom, for it is the wisdom of every griot who ever lived. She said that if you hear only one story about a person, or a people, or a place, again and again, you come to believe that the single story is the whole truth. If the only story you ever hear of Africa is a story of famine and war and helplessness, you will believe that is all Africa is. If the only story you hear of a poor family is their poverty, you will never see their laughter, their art, their genius, their love. The single story robs people of their fullness. It makes one thing the only thing."}, {"id": "legends-chimamanda-adichie#s6", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "That, children, is the very sickness Achebe fought with Things Fall Apart, now named plainly and handed to the whole world by his daughter in the torch. The cure for the single story is many stories. It is exactly what I do here, night after night, refusing to let any one tale become the only tale of who we are."}, {"id": "legends-chimamanda-adichie#s7", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "She spoke, too, and boldly, on why we should all be feminists, saying that we do a disservice to our sons and our daughters both when we raise them by narrow, unequal rules. Her words on this were woven into music heard by millions and printed in a small fierce book that traveled the world. And here comes the Afonja lesson, children, for she is not without her storms. She has spoken words on some tender matters that divided even her admirers, and made enemies of some who once cheered her, and she has not backed down from the quarrels. She is a proud, sharp, independent mind, and such minds do not float serenely above the fights of their age. They wade in."}, {"id": "legends-chimamanda-adichie#s8", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "Hold the two palms. On one side, a woman who will not soften herself to please a crowd. On the other, a body of work that has given millions of readers the dignity of the many stories over the single one. Weigh it honestly. She divides her life between America and Nigeria, and she never forgets the road home. She has used her fame to lift up the young writers coming behind her, running workshops in Lagos to teach a new generation to guard their own stories. She wraps her hair, she wears the styles of her people with pride, and she carries herself into the finest rooms in the world without once pretending to be anything other than an Igbo woman of Nsukka."}, {"id": "legends-chimamanda-adichie#s9", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "That is her gift to you, children. She proved that a young African woman, in our own lifetime, need not choose between the world and her roots. She can stand at the very summit of world letters with her feet planted firmly in the red earth of home, telling not the single story that strangers wrote for us, but the many, many true stories that are ours to tell. Tomorrow I will leave the world of the book, and I will bring you the sound that the youth of our age carry across every border on the earth, the new music, born of Fela’s fire and Sunny Ade’s drum, that has made the whole world, once again, dance to a beat from this soil."}]}, {"id": "legends-wizkid", "emoji": "🌟", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#00201a,#003a30)", "category": "legends", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "6 min", "title": {"en": "Wizkid: The Star Boy from Surulere", "yo": "Ayodeji Balogun"}, "desc": {"en": "Star Boy from Surulere, who carried the sound of Lagos to the world.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Living Legends", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Alaaye, the story of Ayodeji Ibrahim Balogun, Star Boy, Big Wiz, the son of two faiths who carried the sound of Lagos to the whole singing world)", "segments": [{"id": "legends-wizkid#s1", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Children, the ink of the great writers is dry on the page now, and the horn of Fela has cooled. But the sound did not die. It went down into the young, the way a river goes underground in the dry season and rises again as a fresh spring somewhere new. Tonight I bring you the first of the new springs. His name at home is Ayodeji Ibrahim Balogun. The world screams a different name at him across stadiums. Wizkid."}, {"id": "legends-wizkid#s2", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "He was born in 1990, in Surulere, in the belly of Lagos, the twelfth and last child of a large family. And here is the first beautiful thing about him, children, a thing that is a small parable of this whole land. His father was a Muslim. His mother was a Christian, a devout one, of the deep-singing Pentecostal kind. And in that one house the two great faiths of Nigeria lived side by side under one roof, and the boy grew up moving between the mosque and the church without the walls that grown men build. He sang first in his mother’s church choir. That is where the voice was born, in the house of worship, as so many voices are."}, {"id": "legends-wizkid#s3", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "He was a small, slight boy, they say, and he started young, recording with a group of church boys when he was barely eleven, calling himself little names before he found the one that stuck. He came up in the hard, hungry Lagos music scene of the two thousands, where a boy with talent and no money waits at studio doors and sleeps on other men’s floors. He caught the eye of an established star who signed him and gave him his platform, and in 2011 he released a song and an album that changed the weather over Lagos. Holla at Your Boy. Then the album Superstar. Suddenly every radio, every danfo bus, every party, every phone in the country carried the light, sweet, unmistakable voice of the boy from Surulere."}, {"id": "legends-wizkid#s4", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "Now hear what he did, children, for it is not small. He took the highlife and the Afrobeat that Fela and the elders had built, and the fuji and juju in his bones, and the American hip-hop and R and B the young ones loved, and he blended them into something light as air and impossible to stop moving to. They call this new thing Afrobeats, with the s, the child and grandchild of Fela’s Afrobeat. And Wizkid became its brightest young face."}, {"id": "legends-wizkid#s5", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "But he did not stop at the borders of Nigeria, as the stars before him mostly had. He reached across the water. He sang on a song with one of the biggest musicians on the entire planet, a Canadian, a song called One Dance, and that song went to the very top of the charts in country after country after country, the first of its kind to do so, played a billion times and more. A boy from Surulere, on the number one song in the world."}, {"id": "legends-wizkid#s6", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Then he made his own album, Made in Lagos, and named it after his home so that no one could ever mistake where the sound came from. And a song from it, Essence, did what no Nigerian song had done before it. It climbed the American charts on its own strength, and the American giants lined up to sing beside him. He filled the great halls of London and New York, twenty thousand voices singing his Yoruba and pidgin words back to him in the cold northern night. He carried the name of Lagos, written on the album cover, into every corner of the earth."}, {"id": "legends-wizkid#s7", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "The Afonja lesson, children, for I flatter no one. Wizkid is a private, sometimes moody star. He can be careless with his words and his promises, slow to release what he owes, quarrelsome with rivals, and his personal life has been tangled and much gossiped over. He is not a saint and does not pretend to be one. He is a young man who became a god to millions before his character was fully cured, and that is a heavy, dangerous gift, and it has bruised him as it bruises many. Hold the two palms. On one side, the flaws of a young man raised too fast into fame. On the other, a boy from a crowded Surulere house who kicked open a door in the wall of the world so wide that a whole generation of African singers walked through behind him. Weigh it fairly."}, {"id": "legends-wizkid#s8", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "That is the thing I want you to keep. He did not merely become rich and famous. He widened the road. Because Wizkid crossed over, the ones who came after him did not have to beg to be let in. The world was already listening for the next voice from Lagos. He turned the sound of our streets into a passport that any gifted child of this soil could now carry. The Star Boy from Surulere, son of the mosque and the church both, proved that the light music of our parties was strong enough to be heard around the entire spinning world. Tomorrow I will bring you his great rival and brother in the same fight, a very different man, prouder, angrier, more openly the grandchild of Fela, who took the Afrobeat and made it once again a music of meaning, and who won for this soil a prize the whole world watches."}]}, {"id": "legends-burna-boy", "emoji": "🌟", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#00201a,#003a30)", "category": "legends", "rating": 9.4, "duration": "6 min", "title": {"en": "Burna Boy: The African Giant and the Grandmother’s Prophecy", "yo": "Damini Ogulu"}, "desc": {"en": "The self-named African Giant and the grandmother's prophecy.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Living Legends", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Alaaye, the story of Damini Ebunoluwa Ogulu, Burna Boy, the self-named African Giant, grandson of Fela’s own manager, who won for this soil the great world prize of song)", "segments": [{"id": "legends-burna-boy#s1", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Children, last night I promised you Wizkid’s great rival, and here he stands, and he is a different animal altogether. Where the Star Boy is light and cool as evening rain, this one is loud and hot and proud as the noonday sun. He named himself the African Giant, and he dared the whole world to call him arrogant, and then he went out and made the name true. His mother named him Damini Ebunoluwa Ogulu. The world calls him Burna Boy."}, {"id": "legends-burna-boy#s2", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "He was born in 1991, in Port Harcourt, in the oil country of the south. And listen to the marrow of his lineage, children, for it is the kind of thing that makes a griot’s eyes shine. His grandfather was Benson Idonije. And who was Benson Idonije? He was the very first manager of Fela Anikulapo-Kuti. The man who guided the father of Afrobeat in his early years. So the sound of Fela was not a thing Burna Boy discovered on a record. It was the air of his grandfather’s house. It was in the family blood, handed down across three generations like an heirloom drum. His mother, Bose Ogulu, a woman of learning who worked with languages, became his manager, guiding his career with a fierce mother’s hand, and she stood beside him at the highest moment of his life, as you will hear."}, {"id": "legends-burna-boy#s3", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "He was a restless, rebellious boy, sent to study abroad in London and back, dropping out, chasing the music that his grandfather’s world had planted in him. He came up slowly, harder than Wizkid, with a big early song called Like to Party, and then years of grinding, of being known but not yet crowned, of watching others rise faster. And then he did the thing that set him apart from every other young star. He decided the music must mean something again. He looked back past the light party sound to his grandfather’s old boss, to Fela, and he took up the fallen mantle openly. He called what he made not just Afrobeats but Afro-fusion, and he filled it with the old spirit, songs about the state of Africa, about the pride of the black man, about history and injustice and the long memory of a wounded continent, all riding on a groove that still made you dance."}, {"id": "legends-burna-boy#s4", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "He named an album African Giant, and when a great foreign festival printed his name too small on their poster, he did not swallow it quietly. He roared. He made his size, his worth, his refusal to be printed small, into a whole philosophy. Some called it ego. He called it the simple demand that Africa be printed large on the poster of the world. Then came the album Twice as Tall, guided by his mother and shaped with the help of a legendary American producer, and it carried him to the mountaintop. He won the great American Grammy award for the best global album. And when the moment came, it was his mother, Bose, who had stood at a world stage before on his behalf and declared, with the whole earth listening, that every black person should remember that they were the grandchildren of those who could not be sold. Africa printed large indeed."}, {"id": "legends-burna-boy#s5", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "And he did a thing the party-stars rarely dared. He filled the great stadiums, the enormous halls of London and New York and beyond, as the headline act, the main name, the giant on the poster at last, tens of thousands of people from every nation singing African songs of meaning back at him. The Afonja lesson, always, children, and with this one it is loud. Burna Boy’s pride is real and it has cut him. His mouth has landed him in quarrels beyond number. He has been tangled in ugly incidents, accused of things in his younger, wilder days that sit uneasily beside his greatness, feuds with other artists, hot words he could not take back. The very fire that makes his music burn also scorches the people around him. He is not a gentle man, and he would not thank you for calling him one."}, {"id": "legends-burna-boy#s6", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "Hold the two palms. On one side, arrogance, temper, the scars of a hot-blooded youth. On the other, the grandson of Fela’s manager who refused to let the meaning drain out of the music, who forced the world to take African pride seriously again, and who carried the great prize home to this soil. Weigh it honestly, and remember that his own grandfather guided the very first Giant of them all. What I want you to carry, children, is the inheritance in it. Nothing in this land is ever truly lost. Fela’s fire, banked down low after his death, was carried quietly in an old man’s house in Port Harcourt, breathed in by a rebellious grandson, and blown back into a blaze that lit the whole world stage. The grandmother’s pot, they say, is never empty for long. What the elders plant, the children harvest, sometimes three generations on."}, {"id": "legends-burna-boy#s7", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "Tomorrow I will bring you the daughter among these giants, a quiet, deep-voiced young woman who needed no roaring to conquer, who wrote from her own pain in her own bedroom, and who became the first of our daughters to stand in that same winning circle of the world’s great prize."}]}, {"id": "legends-tems", "emoji": "🌟", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#00201a,#003a30)", "category": "legends", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "6 min", "title": {"en": "Tems: The Voice from the Quiet Room", "yo": "Temilade Openiyi"}, "desc": {"en": "The deep-voiced Lagos singer who sang softly and conquered loudly.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Living Legends", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Alaaye, the story of Temilade Openiyi, Tems, the deep-voiced daughter of Lagos who sang softly and conquered loudly, first Nigerian woman to top the American chart and to win the great world prize of song)", "segments": [{"id": "legends-tems#s1", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Children, I have given you the roaring men, the Star Boy and the African Giant. Now let me lower my voice, because the last of the singers I bring you tonight conquered not with a roar but with a hush. She has a voice deep and dark and smoky as the inside of a talking drum, a voice you lean in to hear, and the whole loud world went quiet to lean in. Her name is Temilade Openiyi. The world calls her Tems. She was born in 1995, in Lagos, though part of her early childhood was spent abroad before her mother brought her home. Her parents parted when she was small, and she was raised largely by her mother, a strong woman who worked hard and carried the household. So Tems grew up a girl watching a woman hold a family together alone, and something of that quiet strength went into her."}, {"id": "legends-tems#s2", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "She was, by her own telling, a shy child, not the loud performing kind. She loved music but hid it, singing where no one could hear, too self-conscious to push herself forward. She studied, she worked ordinary jobs, she taught herself the craft in private, learning to produce her own sound on a computer in her room, because she could not yet afford to pay others to shape her voice. That detail matters, children. She built her sound with her own two hands, in a quiet room, owing nothing to anyone."}, {"id": "legends-tems#s3", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "She put out her own songs slowly, on her own terms. A song called Try Me announced her, a warning to any who underestimated the quiet girl. Her voice was like nothing else on the Lagos radio, low and mournful and rich, more like a deep river than the bright chatter around it. She refused to be pushed into the mould of the dancing pop girl. She would be still, and deep, and strange, and she would make the world come to her. And the world came. First, her own giants noticed her. Wizkid, the Star Boy himself, put her on that song Essence, the one that climbed the American charts, and her deep voice floating over it became the part the whole world hummed. Suddenly the quiet girl from the private room was on the song of the summer across the ocean."}, {"id": "legends-tems#s4", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "Then came the moment that broke a ceiling for every daughter of this soil. One of the biggest musicians alive, an American, reached across the water and asked her to write and sing with him for the soundtrack of a great film about an African kingdom. The song was called Lift Me Up. It rose to the very top tier of the American charts. And when the awards of that land were counted, Tems held in her hands the Grammy, and later the honours of the film world circled that same song. A shy girl who once hid her singing became the first Nigerian woman ever to reach the summit of the American chart, and to stand in that highest winning circle. She did it without ever once raising her voice."}, {"id": "legends-tems#s5", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "The Afonja lesson, children, though with this young woman the shadows are fewer and gentler, for she is still early in her road and has kept her life quiet and clean. Her struggle has been of a different kind. She has spoken of the hard fight to be taken seriously as a woman who writes and produces her own music, in an industry that too often wants a woman only to be pretty and to sing what a man has written for her. She was once even briefly jailed on a trip abroad in a confused matter that was later cleared, a frightening thing for a young woman far from home. She has had to guard her stillness fiercely against a world that wanted her louder and faster."}, {"id": "legends-tems#s6", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "Hold the two palms. On one side, a young career still being written, its full measure not yet taken. On the other, a daughter who proved that a woman does not have to shout, or bend herself into another’s shape, to conquer the whole listening world. Weigh it, and lean toward hope, for she is ours and she is only beginning. Here is why I set her beside the roaring men, children. Fela taught that a voice from this soil could carry meaning to the world. Wizkid widened the road. Burna Boy carried the fire and the great prize. And Tems proved the last thing that needed proving, that a Nigerian woman, on her own terms, in her own deep and unhurried voice, building her sound with her own hands in a small quiet room, could stand at that very same summit and be crowned."}, {"id": "legends-tems#s7", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "The quiet room, children. Never despise the quiet room where a shy person builds something true in private. Sometimes the softest voice, when it finally opens its mouth, is the one the whole world holds its breath to hear. Tomorrow I will leave the world of song entirely, and I will bring you a different kind of giant, the ones who built not melodies but empires, who employ hundreds of thousands, who feed and bank and move a whole continent. For greatness wears many garments, and not all of them sing."}]}, {"id": "legends-aliko-dangote", "emoji": "🌟", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#00201a,#003a30)", "category": "legends", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "7 min", "title": {"en": "Aliko Dangote: The Boy Who Sold Sweets and Built an Empire", "yo": "Aliko Dangote: The Boy Who Sold Sweets and Built an Empire"}, "desc": {"en": "The Kano trader's son who sold sweets as a boy and built an industrial empire.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Living Legends", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Alaaye, the story of Aliko Dangote, the trader from Kano who became the richest black man on the earth, the builder of cement and sugar and salt and oil, who chose to make things rather than merely sell them)", "segments": [{"id": "legends-aliko-dangote#s1", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Children, dry the song from your ears now, for tonight greatness wears a plain kaftan and speaks softly of numbers. I bring you a man who never sang a note, never wrote a novel, never stood on a stage. And yet his name is known in every market from Lagos to Lusaka, because he built things you can touch. His name is Aliko Dangote. I must be honest at the fire, as always. He is not Yoruba. He is a Hausa man of Kano, in the far north. But I bring him among these legends without apology, because he employs the children of every tribe in this land by the tens of thousands, Yoruba among them, and because the lesson of his life belongs to every young person of this soil who dreams of building something real."}, {"id": "legends-aliko-dangote#s2", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "He was born in 1957, in Kano, into a family that already knew trade. His great-grandfather had been one of the wealthiest merchants of the region, a dealer in groundnuts and commodities. So business, like music in Sunny Ade’s house, was the air the boy breathed. But hear this small story, for the elders love it. Even as a small child in school, young Aliko would buy cartons of sweets, of sugar candy, and sell them one by one to his schoolmates for a small profit. A boy at school, already a trader, already counting his little gains. The seed of the empire was in the sweet-seller."}, {"id": "legends-aliko-dangote#s3", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "When he came of age, an uncle lent him a small sum of money to begin. He did not squander it. He traded in commodities, cement and sugar and rice and salt, importing them and selling them across the country, and he repaid the loan quickly and grew his trade with a discipline that never left him. For years he was simply a very successful trader, a man who bought cheap and sold dear and did it better than others. But then, children, he made the decision that separates a rich man from a great builder. He grew tired of merely importing what other lands had made. He asked himself a harder question. Why should a country this large, with this many mouths, import its cement and its sugar from abroad forever? Why not make it here, on this soil, with these hands?"}, {"id": "legends-aliko-dangote#s4", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "So he turned from trading to building. He raised enormous factories. Cement plants that rose out of the bush like grey mountains, until the country that once begged cement from foreigners began to make its own and sell the surplus to its neighbours. Sugar refineries. Flour mills. Salt works. He built and built, employing tens of thousands directly and hundreds of thousands more in the trades that fed off his factories. And he became, by this making of things, the richest black person on the entire face of the earth, a title he has held year after year. Not by selling oil that God put in the ground. By building factories that turn raw earth into the things people need every single day."}, {"id": "legends-aliko-dangote#s5", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "Then he dared his greatest gamble, a thing so large that wise men called him mad. This land floats on oil, yet for decades it shamefully sent its crude abroad to be refined and bought back the petrol at a painful price. Dangote resolved to build a refinery, one of the largest in the entire world, on the edge of Lagos, so that the country could at last refine its own oil. It cost a sum so vast the mind struggles to hold it, and it took years longer and far more money than promised, and many nights the whole enterprise seemed it might swallow him. But he built it. The giant refinery rose."}, {"id": "legends-aliko-dangote#s6", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "The Afonja lesson, children, for no giant is without shadow. A fortune of that size in a country such as ours is never made in a spotless room. Critics say his empire grew tall partly because governments gave his companies protections and favours that smaller men never enjoyed, tariffs and waivers that shut out competitors, so that some of his kingdoms became near-monopolies where the ordinary person had little choice but to buy from him. There are hard questions about how close great wealth sits to great power in this land, and Dangote sits very close to power indeed. A griot who hides this from you is a flatterer, not a teacher."}, {"id": "legends-aliko-dangote#s7", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "Hold the two palms. On one side, the favours, the monopoly power, the closeness to the mighty. On the other, a man who chose to build factories in a country where it is far easier and safer to simply import and sell, who employs a multitude, who kept his fortune working on this soil instead of hiding it abroad, and who gives large sums to fight disease and hunger through his foundation. Weigh it honestly. Here is the lesson I press into your hands, children. There are two kinds of rich men. One buys low and sells high and grows fat, and when he dies his cleverness dies with him and nothing remains. The other takes his gains and builds something that stands, a factory, a refinery, a thing that goes on feeding families and making goods long after he is gone. Dangote chose to be the builder. He chose to make rather than merely to move."}, {"id": "legends-aliko-dangote#s8", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "The boy who sold sweets one by one at his school desk understood a thing very early, that wealth is only truly wealth when it puts bread in another family’s pot. Remember the sweet-seller, children, when someone tells you that you are too small, or your beginning too humble, to build something that will one day cast a shadow across a continent. Tomorrow I will bring you another builder of empires, but one whose great cause is not cement but the young themselves, a banker who declared that the future of this whole continent would be built by its own entrepreneurs, and who put his own fortune behind that belief."}]}, {"id": "legends-tony-elumelu", "emoji": "🌟", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#00201a,#003a30)", "category": "legends", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "7 min", "title": {"en": "Tony Elumelu: The Banker Who Bet on the Young", "yo": "Tony Elumelu: The Banker Who Bet on the Young"}, "desc": {"en": "The banker who revived a dying bank and bet on Africa's young entrepreneurs.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Living Legends", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Alaaye, the story of Anthony Onyemaechi Elumelu, the man who revived a dying bank, who named a whole philosophy Africapitalism, and who vowed to fund ten thousand African entrepreneurs with his own gold)", "segments": [{"id": "legends-tony-elumelu#s1", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Children, last night I promised you a builder whose cause is not cement but the young themselves. Here he is. Where Dangote built factories of iron and stone, this man decided to build people, to take his fortune and plant it in the dreams of thousands of young Africans he would never meet. His name is Anthony Onyemaechi Elumelu. He is Igbo, of the Delta, not Yoruba, and once more I bring him to our fire gladly, for his gift falls on the young of every tribe, and Yoruba children by the thousand have eaten from the harvest he sows."}, {"id": "legends-tony-elumelu#s2", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "He was born in 1963, in Jos, on the cool high plateau of the middle of the country, one of several children of parents who were themselves entrepreneurs and strivers. His mother in particular was a businesswoman, and the boy learned early that money was a thing you made move and grow, not a thing you merely waited to receive. He studied economics, took his degrees, and went into banking as a young man, learning the trade of money from the inside."}, {"id": "legends-tony-elumelu#s3", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Now hear the deed that first made his name, for it is a tale of daring. While still a young man in his early thirties, he gathered a small group of investors and did an audacious thing. He took over a small, failing, nearly dead commercial bank, a bank circling the drain, and he seized its wheel. Men of his age did not do such things. But Elumelu and his partners turned that dying little bank around, breathed life back into it, made it healthy and profitable."}, {"id": "legends-tony-elumelu#s4", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "And then he did something even bolder. He used that revived small bank to take over a far larger, older, more famous bank, a minnow swallowing a whale, and merged them into one great institution, the United Bank for Africa. Under his hand it spread across the whole continent, into some twenty African countries, a bank flying an African flag in nation after nation, so that an African need not always bow to a foreign bank to move his money across borders."}, {"id": "legends-tony-elumelu#s5", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "But it is not the bank alone that earns him a place at this fire. It is the idea he carried out of it. He looked at his continent and at the endless foreign aid poured into it for generations, the charity that seemed only to keep Africa forever on its knees with its palm out. And he declared that this was the wrong road entirely. Africa would not be lifted by the charity of strangers, he said. Africa would be lifted by African business, by African capital planted in African soil, by profit that stays home and builds. He gave this belief a name. Africapitalism. The idea that the private wealth of Africans, invested with purpose in their own continent, is the true engine of its rising."}, {"id": "legends-tony-elumelu#s6", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "And he did not merely preach it, children, which is the test of every fine word. He put his own gold where his mouth was. Through his foundation he made a vow that stunned the continent. He would identify ten thousand young African entrepreneurs, across every one of the fifty-four countries, and he would give each of them seed money of their own, training, mentoring, and a network, to start or grow a business. Not a loan to be squeezed back with interest. Seed capital, a true beginning, spread across the whole continent without regard for tribe or border or which language a young dreamer spoke. Think of it. A young woman in a village with an idea and no father’s wealth behind her, given the first true chance of her life by a stranger’s foundation. Multiply her by ten thousand. That is a harvest no factory of cement can equal."}, {"id": "legends-tony-elumelu#s7", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "The Afonja lesson, always, children. Elumelu is a titan of banking, and the world of high finance in this land is a world of the powerful dealing with the powerful. His empire reaches into oil and power stations as well as banks, and such reach always draws whispers about closeness to government and about deals the ordinary person cannot see. Some ask, too, whether ten thousand seeds, wonderful as they are, can truly turn the tide against the vast unemployment of a whole continent, or whether they are a bright lamp against a very great darkness. These are fair questions, and I do not hide them."}, {"id": "legends-tony-elumelu#s8", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "Hold the two palms. On one side, a financier of enormous power moving in the shadowed rooms where money and government meet. On the other, a man who chose to spend his own fortune betting on the poorest young dreamers of every African nation, and who changed the very conversation from begging for aid to building with our own hands. Weigh it honestly. Here is the marrow for you, children. It is one thing to grow rich. It is another thing entirely to look at a young stranger with nothing but an idea and to say, I will back you, I will plant my own gold in your dream, and I ask only that when you rise, you turn and lift the one behind you. That is a chief’s heart in a banker’s suit."}, {"id": "legends-tony-elumelu#s9", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "The old proverb says that the one who plants a tree under whose shade he knows he will never sit understands the meaning of life. Elumelu planted ten thousand trees across a continent, and he will never sit under most of them. But the children of this soil will, long after his name has passed into the mouths of griots like me. Tomorrow I will carry you across the ocean, to a son of this soil who went out into the coldest, sharpest markets of the wider world and beat the foreigners at their own hardest game, and who came to own the very roads of the sky and sea through which the world’s goods travel."}]}, {"id": "legends-adebayo-ogunlesi", "emoji": "🌟", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#00201a,#003a30)", "category": "legends", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "7 min", "title": {"en": "Adebayo Ogunlesi: The Son Who Bought the Roads of the Sky", "yo": "Adebayo Ogunlesi"}, "desc": {"en": "The Yoruba lawyer-banker who conquered global finance and came to own great airports.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Living Legends", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Alaaye, the story of Adebayo O. Ogunlesi, the Yoruba lawyer-banker who conquered the sharpest markets of the world and came to own great airports and the very highways of trade)", "segments": [{"id": "legends-adebayo-ogunlesi#s1", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Children, tonight I bring you home to our own, a true son of Yorubaland, and I bring you a tale that will stretch your imagination, for this man did not sell music or build cement. This man went out into the coldest, most cutting markets on the face of the earth, the great money-houses of America, where they eat the unprepared alive, and he beat them at their own hardest game. And then he did a thing so large you will think I am spinning a folktale. He came to own airports. Actual airports, where the aeroplanes land. His name is Adebayo Ogunlesi."}, {"id": "legends-adebayo-ogunlesi#s2", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "He was born in 1953, in Makun, in Sagamu, in Ogun State, deep in the Yoruba heartland. And here is a thing the elders love, children, for it shows that greatness is often watered in a learned home. His father, Theophilus Ogunlesi, was no ordinary man. He was one of the first, if not the very first, Nigerian to become a professor of medicine, a pioneering physician. So the boy grew up in a house where excellence was not a hope but an expectation, where you were not praised merely for passing but measured against the highest bar in the land. He was schooled here, at the finest schools, then he went abroad, and he did a rare and formidable thing. He took degrees from the greatest universities of the wider world in two hard fields at once, both the law and business, arming himself with a double blade few men carry."}, {"id": "legends-adebayo-ogunlesi#s3", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "He began in the law, and so sharp was his mind that he clerked for one of the highest judges in America, a rare honour for anyone, let alone a young African. But the world of high finance called, and he crossed into investment banking, into one of the most famous and ruthless money-houses in all the world. And there, over decades, this son of Sagamu rose and rose, until he sat at the very top of that institution’s dealings across the whole earth, advising governments and giants, one of the most powerful bankers on the planet, a black African man giving orders in the whitest, coldest boardrooms of the West. He did not merely survive that world. He commanded it."}, {"id": "legends-adebayo-ogunlesi#s4", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "But hear the deed that sets him apart, children. Many men grow powerful working inside another man’s great house. Fewer dare to walk out and build their own. Ogunlesi did the harder thing. He left the towering firm, and he founded his own, to do a special kind of work, the buying and building of what they call infrastructure. Not shares of paper that vanish, but the great solid bones of the modern world. Airports. Seaports. Pipelines. Power stations. The very roads, on the ground and in the sky and on the sea, along which all the goods and people of the world must travel."}, {"id": "legends-adebayo-ogunlesi#s5", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "His firm came to own major airports in great cities, including famous ones in Britain, the very gateways through which millions pass. Think on it, children. A boy from Sagamu came to hold in his hands the airports of the people who once came to rule over us. The traveller landing in a foreign capital was, unknowing, setting his foot on ground owned in part by a son of Ogun State. And his mastery grew so respected that one of the very largest money-managers in the entire world, a firm that holds the savings of half the earth, brought his whole enterprise into their house and set him high among their leaders, so that he now sits near the summit of global finance itself, advising on where the world’s great rivers of money shall flow."}, {"id": "legends-adebayo-ogunlesi#s6", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "The Afonja lesson, children, and here it takes a gentler, subtler form. Ogunlesi’s triumph is real and clean of scandal, a rare and beautiful thing. But wise elders ask a quiet question of such sons. He gave his genius to the markets of the wider world. He built and owned the airports of foreign lands. And some ask, with love and not with malice, what might this soil have become had that same towering genius been poured first and fully into building the ports and power and airports of his own country, which needs them so desperately. It is not a rebuke. It is the ache of a people who watch their finest children shine most brightly on distant shores. He has counselled his homeland and invested here too, but the greater share of his monuments stand abroad."}, {"id": "legends-adebayo-ogunlesi#s7", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Hold the two palms. On one side, a giant whose grandest works serve other lands. On the other, a Yoruba son who proved beyond all argument that the mind born in Sagamu can master and command the very hardest arena the world has to offer, and who lit a lamp of possibility for every ambitious child of this soil. Weigh it honestly, and with pride."}, {"id": "legends-adebayo-ogunlesi#s8", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "Here is what I press into your hands, children. There is a lie whispered to African children, sometimes by strangers and sometimes, shamefully, by our own, that we are fit only to sing and to dance and to dig, that the cold high citadels of finance and law and global power are not for the likes of us, that we may visit but never rule there. Adebayo Ogunlesi took that lie and broke it across his knee. He walked into the very throne room of world capital and he sat down at the head of the table. Remember his father, the pioneering professor, and remember the son who bought the roads of the sky. In that one family, across two generations, is the whole answer to anyone who ever tells you that there is a summit in this world too high for a child of this soil to climb."}, {"id": "legends-adebayo-ogunlesi#s9", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "Tomorrow I will bring you a banker of a different kind, not a conqueror of foreign markets but a quiet, steady builder here at home, a man who took a bank and made it a thing of trust and light in the very hand of the ordinary customer."}]}, {"id": "legends-segun-agbaje", "emoji": "🌟", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#00201a,#003a30)", "category": "legends", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "7 min", "title": {"en": "Segun Agbaje: The Banker Who Made Money Simple", "yo": "Segun Agbaje"}, "desc": {"en": "The banker who made money simple for the ordinary customer.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Living Legends", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Alaaye, the story of Oluwasegun Julius Agbaje, the steady steward who took a proud bank and turned it toward the ordinary customer, and who taught money to live inside a small telephone)", "segments": [{"id": "legends-segun-agbaje#s1", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Children, last night I gave you Ogunlesi, who conquered the cold markets far away. Tonight I bring you a banker of the opposite kind, a man whose whole great work was done right here, quietly, patiently, aimed not at the giants of the world but at you, the ordinary person with a small account and a phone in your pocket. His name is Oluwasegun Julius Agbaje. He was born in 1964, a Lagos man, a Yoruba son of the city. He was schooled well and went abroad to study, taking his learning in accounting and business across the ocean, and he became a chartered accountant, a master of the careful reading of numbers. He could have stayed abroad, as so many of our bright ones do. He came home."}, {"id": "legends-segun-agbaje#s2", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "And he joined, as a young man near its very beginning, a new bank that a group of ambitious young Nigerians were building, a bank called Guaranty Trust. He was not the founder. He was one of the early hands, a young officer among others, and he simply began to work, and to prove himself, year upon steady year. Hear the shape of his rise, children, for there is a quiet lesson in it that the loud world forgets. He did not leap from bank to bank chasing a bigger title, as the restless do. He stayed. He gave his working life to that one house, rising slowly through every level, learning every corner of it, from the small desk to the high office, until in time the leadership of the whole bank was placed in his hands. The patient man who masters one house completely, the elders say, ends up owning the keys to every room in it."}, {"id": "legends-segun-agbaje#s3", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Under his stewardship that bank became one of the most respected and profitable in the whole country, famous for a thing rare in our banking, a kind of style and cleanliness and modern ease, a bank that felt less like a dusty government office and more like a bright, well-run thing of the new age. But here is the deed that touches your own life, children, whether you know his name or not. Agbaje understood, earlier and more clearly than most, that the future of money in this land was not in grand marble banking halls. It was in the small telephone in the hand of the market woman, the student, the okada rider, the trader. He drove his bank hard toward this future, toward banking that lived inside the phone, so that a person in a far village with no branch for a hundred miles could still send money, receive money, pay for goods, all from a cheap handset."}, {"id": "legends-segun-agbaje#s4", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "He pushed to make the moving of money simple and cheap and quick for the ordinary Nigerian, to tear down the old walls that made banking a lordly favour granted to the few, and to make it instead a plain daily tool in the hand of the many. When you send a few naira to your mother from your phone in a heartbeat, you are walking on a road that men like Agbaje paved."}, {"id": "legends-segun-agbaje#s5", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "And he did not stop at banking. He led the building of a wider house, a group that reached into other services beyond the bank alone, always with the same eye, how to make the modern tools of money and commerce simpler and closer to the reach of the everyday person. He also gave the bank a name for its great free food fairs and fashion and enterprise fairs, gathering small traders by the thousand and giving them, without charge, a stage to sell their wares to the whole city. The mighty bank stooping to lift the smallest trader. That, too, is his signature."}, {"id": "legends-segun-agbaje#s6", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "The Afonja lesson, children, for even the steady steward casts a shadow. The world of banking in this land is not spotless, and a bank that grows so large and so profitable is always fairly asked whether its profits come from serving the customer well or from fees and charges that quietly bleed the small account-holder. Banks of this country have faced hard questions and hard investigations over the years about how they treat the ordinary customer and how close they sit to power, and no great banker rises to the top without navigating those murky waters. Agbaje is a titan of a system that many ordinary Nigerians find costly and frustrating to deal with, and honesty requires me to say so."}, {"id": "legends-segun-agbaje#s7", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "Hold the two palms. On one side, a lord of a banking system that can squeeze the small saver. On the other, a patient, clean-handed builder who gave his whole life to one house, made it excellent, and bent it, more than most, toward putting the power of money into the ordinary citizen’s own pocket. Weigh it honestly. Here is what I leave with you, children. The world praises the loud conqueror and forgets the patient steward. But a house is not built by the man who lays the loudest brick. It is built by the one who returns every single day, for thirty years, to lay the next quiet brick true and level. Agbaje is that steward. He did not roar. He did not conquer distant lands. He stayed home, mastered one great house completely, and used it to put a bank into the pocket of the market woman."}, {"id": "legends-segun-agbaje#s8", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "Do not despise the long, patient, faithful road, children. In a world drunk on the quick and the loud, the one who stays, who masters, who serves the many rather than dazzles the few, often builds the thing that lasts the longest and touches the most lives. Tomorrow, for the last of these living giants, I will gather the ones who served not with song or gold but with the pure light of the mind and the open hand of service, and then I will close this long telling of our legends with a blessing."}]}, {"id": "legends-lamp-bearers", "emoji": "🌟", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#00201a,#003a30)", "category": "legends", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "7 min", "title": {"en": "The Lamp-Bearers: Those Who Served with Mind and Clean Hands", "yo": "The Lamp-Bearers: Those Who Served with Mind and Clean Hands"}, "desc": {"en": "Ngozi Okonjo-Iweala, Philip Emeagwali, Christopher Kolade and others who served with mind and clean hands.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Living Legends", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Alaaye, consolidates the honours of Ngozi Okonjo-Iweala, Philip Emeagwali, Christopher Kolade and their kind, the ones who added value not with fortune or fame but with the pure light of the mind and the open hand of service)", "segments": [{"id": "legends-lamp-bearers#s1", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Children, gather close, for tonight I gather several lamps into one lantern. Not every giant sings, and not every giant grows rich. Some serve their people with the mind and the clean hand, and their monuments are not mansions but institutions, ideas, and the plain fact of an honest life lived in a dishonest age. I will not tell each of these lives in full tonight, for the hour grows late and the telling is long, but I will not let them pass without their names spoken at this fire. And again I say, some of these are not Yoruba, but their light fell on us all, and a griot who honours only his own tribe is only half a griot."}, {"id": "legends-lamp-bearers#s2", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Hear first of Ngozi Okonjo-Iweala. She was born in 1954, an Igbo woman of the Delta, daughter of a royal-house professor, and she grew into one of the finest economic minds this continent has produced. She served two times as the one who held the nation’s purse, the finance minister, in seasons when the treasury was a den of thieves, and she fought, not always winning, to plug the holes through which the nation’s wealth leaked away. She helped negotiate the forgiveness of a crushing mountain of foreign debt that had chained the country for a generation, freeing billions to be spent on the living instead of on old interest."}, {"id": "legends-lamp-bearers#s3", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "And then she rose to a height no African, and no woman, had ever reached before her. She was chosen to lead the World Trade Organization, the very body that governs the commerce of the whole earth, the first woman and the first African ever to sit in that chair. A daughter of this soil, refereeing the trade of all nations. And through it all she carried a reputation, fiercely defended and dearly paid for, as one who would not be bought. They even, it is said, kidnapped her own aged mother to try to bend her while she guarded the treasury, and still she would not bend."}, {"id": "legends-lamp-bearers#s4", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Hear next of Philip Emeagwali. Born in 1954 in the east, a boy so poor that the war of Biafra drove his family into a refugee camp and forced him out of school, a boy who taught himself by walking to libraries and devouring everything. That self-taught refugee boy grew into a computer scientist who, it is celebrated, harnessed thousands of small processors to work as one, performing a calculation at a speed that helped light the path toward the way great computers speak to one another today. Whatever the exact measure of his feats, and the learned still debate them, he became for a generation of African children the living proof that a mind born in a refugee camp, with no shoes and no school fees, could still astonish the world of science. The dream he planted in young heads is itself a monument."}, {"id": "legends-lamp-bearers#s5", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "And hear, for our own Yoruba honour, of Christopher Kolade. Born in 1932, a son of an Anglican clergyman, he became the very picture of the incorruptible public servant, a thing so rare in this land that the young scarcely believe it exists. He ran the national broadcasting house, he led great companies with a spotless hand, he served as the nation’s envoy abroad, and late in life a government asked him to oversee the honest spending of a vast public fund, precisely because his name was a byword for integrity. In a marketplace where everyone expected a cut, Christopher Kolade was the man who would not take one, and he mentored generations of the young in the strange, old-fashioned art of the clean hand."}, {"id": "legends-lamp-bearers#s6", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "The Afonja lesson, children, even here among the lamp-bearers. None of them was perfect. The economist served governments that failed the poor in a hundred ways, and some blame her for policies that pinched the very people she meant to help. The scientist’s grandest claims are argued over by scholars to this day. The incorruptible servant worked within systems that, for all his personal cleanliness, ground on corruptly around him. To honour a good person is not to pretend they were a saint or that their every choice was wise. It is to weigh the two palms, and to see the light come down heavier than the shadow."}, {"id": "legends-lamp-bearers#s7", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "And there are more, children, so many more that one fire cannot hold them, the doctors who stayed when they could have fled, the teachers who taught for forty years on a wage that shamed the nation, the judges who ruled justly when a bribe was on the table, the nurses, the farmers who fed cities, the market women who banked a whole informal economy on trust alone. I cannot name them all. But know that the true giants of a people are not only the ones whose names reach the ends of the earth. They are also the ten thousand quiet lamp-bearers whose names never leave their own street, and yet without whom the whole house would fall into darkness."}, {"id": "legends-lamp-bearers#s8", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "And so, children, I come to the end of the telling of the living legends, and to the end of this long, long journey we have walked together, from the first Alaafin in the mist of the beginning, through the war-generals and the folktales, the orisa and the gods, the ones scattered across the water, the great women, and now these giants of our own age."}, {"id": "legends-lamp-bearers#s9", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "Hear my blessing, and then let the fire burn low. May you sing like Fela, without fear of the powerful. May you write like Soyinka, refusing to be silenced even in the dark. May you build like Dangote, making things that outlast you. May you lift the young like Elumelu, planting trees whose shade you will never sit in. May you climb like Ogunlesi, believing no summit too high for a child of this soil. May you serve like Kolade, with a hand that will not take what is not yours. And whatever gift the ancestors have folded into your own two hands, may you carry it, as all these did, not for yourself alone, but for your people. The fire is low now, children. The stories are told. Go and become the ones your own grandchildren will gather to hear about, at a fire not yet lit, in a night not yet born."}]}, {"id": "legends-obafemi-awolowo", "emoji": "🌟", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#00201a,#003a30)", "category": "legends", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "5 min", "title": {"en": "Obafemi Awolowo: The Sage of the West", "yo": "Obafemi Awolowo"}, "desc": {"en": "Obafemi Awolowo, the Sage of the West and architect of free education.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Living Legends", "secondary": [], "source_note": "", "segments": [{"id": "legends-obafemi-awolowo#s1", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Narrator opens: There is a political figure in Nigerian history who is spoken of by Yoruba people with a combination of admiration, grief, and the special fury of those who feel that a great man was denied his rightful destiny by forces smaller than himself. That figure is Obafemi Awolowo. He is called \"Awo\" by his admirers. He is called \"the best President Nigeria never had\" by those who study the history. And in Yorubaland, he is simply called the Sage. Awolowo was born in 1909 in Ikenne, in what is now Ogun State, to a Yoruba family of modest means. His father died when he was a child, and the story of his early life is one of poverty navigated through exceptional intelligence and extraordinary determination. He educated himself through correspondence courses, through part-time study while working, through the sheer will of a man who understood that knowledge was the only ladder available to him."}, {"id": "legends-obafemi-awolowo#s2", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "He qualified as a lawyer in London in 1947 — at the age of 38, a decade later than most of his contemporaries achieved similar credentials, because he had been earning and saving and studying simultaneously for years. He returned to Nigeria and threw himself immediately into politics. Awolowo co-founded the Action Group — the dominant political party of the Western Region — and became the first Premier of the Western Region when Nigeria moved toward internal self-government in the 1950s. His record as Premier is one of the most remarkable in African governance history. In the Western Region under Awolowo, universal primary education was introduced — free primary schooling for every child in the region, funded from regional oil revenues and agricultural taxes. This was in 1955. Universal free primary education did not come to most of the United States until the early 20th century. Awolowo was doing it in colonial Nigeria in 1955."}, {"id": "legends-obafemi-awolowo#s3", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "He established the first television station in Africa — the Western Nigeria Television Service, launched in 1959, before independence. He built hospitals. He built roads. He invested in agriculture. He created a regional government that functioned — that actually delivered services to its citizens, that showed what African governance could look like when led by someone with vision and competence. His political philosophy — which he elaborated in several books, including \"Path to Nigerian Freedom\" and \"Thoughts on Nigerian Constitution\" — was a form of social democracy shaped by the specific conditions of Nigeria: a federal structure that gave real power to the regions, coupled with a developmental state that invested in its citizens. He called it \"democratic socialism\" and he meant it. Then came 1964, and the federal political crisis. Then came 1966, and the military coup. And then came the accusation — the treason charge that would put Awolowo in prison for ten years."}, {"id": "legends-obafemi-awolowo#s4", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "The charge of treasonable felony was brought by the federal government of Tafawa Balewa, and many of Awolowo's supporters — then and now — believed it was politically motivated, designed to remove the most effective opposition leader from the field. The trial was conducted by the Supreme Court and resulted in a ten-year sentence. Awolowo was imprisoned from 1963 to 1966, when he was released by the military government of Gowon. He served as Federal Commissioner for Finance during the civil war — playing a key role in the federal government's economic strategy. And after the war, he returned to civilian politics and contested the presidency twice — in 1979 and 1983 — and lost both times in elections that his supporters characterized as rigged. He died in 1987, never having been President of Nigeria."}, {"id": "legends-obafemi-awolowo#s5", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "The Yoruba mourn this. Not with the passive mourning of resignation but with the active mourning of those who feel that something was taken that should not have been taken — that the best-qualified man of his generation was denied the opportunity to lead his country, and that Nigeria was the poorer for it. Awolowo's legacy lives in the free schools that educated a generation of Yoruba professionals. It lives in the roads that connected the villages of the West. It lives in the institutions — the Western Nigeria Broadcasting Service, the Cocoa House in Ibadan — that were built during his stewardship. And it lives in the memory of what was possible when governance was taken seriously. The Sage of the West. The best President Nigeria never had. He deserved better. So did Nigeria."}]}, {"id": "legends-gani-fawehinmi", "emoji": "🌟", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#00201a,#003a30)", "category": "legends", "rating": 9.4, "duration": "4 min", "title": {"en": "Gani Fawehinmi: The Lawyer Who Fought for the People", "yo": "Gani Fawehinmi"}, "desc": {"en": "Gani Fawehinmi, the lawyer who fought for the people.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Living Legends", "secondary": [], "source_note": "", "segments": [{"id": "legends-gani-fawehinmi#s1", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Narrator opens: There are lawyers who serve the law. And there are lawyers who use the law to serve justice — even when those are not the same thing. Gani Fawehinmi was unambiguously in the second category. He was, for four decades, Nigeria's most consistently combative human rights lawyer, using the courts as a battlefield in the fight against military rule, official corruption, and the systematic violation of the rights of ordinary Nigerians. Olawole Ganiyu Fawehinmi was born in 1938 in Ondo, in what is now Ondo State. He studied law in Nigeria and England, and returned to practice in Lagos in the 1960s. He became a Senior Advocate of Nigeria — the highest designation in the Nigerian legal profession — and used that standing to take on cases that other lawyers avoided."}, {"id": "legends-gani-fawehinmi#s2", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "His method was aggressive and unrelenting. He filed suit after suit — challenging military decrees, seeking habeas corpus for political prisoners, suing government officials for corruption and abuse of power. The suits were often unsuccessful in the short term — courts under military governments were not always free to rule against the government — but Fawehinmi's strategy was not purely legal. It was publicity. Every suit filed, every court appearance, every press conference was another moment in the public record — another documentation of what was being done and who was doing it. He was arrested multiple times. He was detained under military detention laws that allowed the government to hold people without charge or trial. He was beaten by security forces. He was harassed. And every time, he emerged and filed another suit. The pattern was consistent over forty years of activism."}, {"id": "legends-gani-fawehinmi#s3", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "His most famous cases include his pursuit of justice for Dele Giwa — the journalist killed by a letter bomb in 1986, in what was widely believed to be a government-ordered assassination. Fawehinmi filed multiple suits seeking an independent investigation, naming government officials, making the case publicly and in court that Giwa's death was not random violence but political murder. The cases were never fully resolved in court, but Fawehinmi kept them alive in the public consciousness for decades. He was also a champion of student rights, of press freedom, of the rights of the poor and marginalized. He provided legal representation to protesters, to union organizers, to ordinary people caught up in the machinery of a government that did not always respect the rights of its citizens."}, {"id": "legends-gani-fawehinmi#s4", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "He ran for president twice — in 2003 and 2007 — under the National Conscience Party, a political party he founded. He knew he would not win. The elections were not free enough, the conditions not fair enough. But he used the campaigns as platforms — to say what needed to be said, to put on the official record what official Nigeria preferred to ignore. He was awarded the Senior Advocate of Nigeria designation, and then had it stripped from him by the legal authorities. He appealed. He got it back. He wore it as a badge of what he had endured. He died in 2009 of chronic leukemia. His funeral was attended by thousands — activists, lawyers, market women, students, journalists, ordinary Nigerians who had been touched by his battles. The National Human Rights Commission named its annual lecture after him."}, {"id": "legends-gani-fawehinmi#s5", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "Gani Fawehinmi. The legal warrior. The man who used the tools of the oppressor against the oppressor and refused to stop even when the tools were bent back against him. Nigeria needed him. Nigeria needs people like him."}]}, {"id": "legends-dele-giwa", "emoji": "🌟", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#00201a,#003a30)", "category": "legends", "rating": 9.4, "duration": "4 min", "title": {"en": "Dele Giwa: The Journalist Who Died for the Truth", "yo": "Dele Giwa"}, "desc": {"en": "Dele Giwa, the journalist killed by a parcel bomb for the truth.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Living Legends", "secondary": [], "source_note": "", "segments": [{"id": "legends-dele-giwa#s1", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Narrator opens: On the morning of October 19, 1986, Dele Giwa was having breakfast at his home in Lagos when a package arrived. It appeared to be official correspondence — the kind of thing that journalists and editors receive regularly. He opened it. The package contained a bomb. It killed him instantly. He was 39 years old. He was the founding editor of Newswatch magazine — the most important news magazine in Nigeria at the time, known for its investigative journalism, its political independence, and its willingness to publish what the government preferred suppressed. He was, by the account of everyone who knew him, one of the most gifted journalists Nigeria had produced."}, {"id": "legends-dele-giwa#s2", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "The bomb came in a package that appeared to bear the seal of the State Security Service — the Nigerian equivalent of a domestic intelligence agency. This detail made the assassination unmistakable in its source, even as the government denied any involvement. No one has ever been officially charged with Giwa's murder. The case remains officially unsolved. What was Giwa working on when he was killed? Investigations suggest he had been pursuing a story about drug trafficking that allegedly involved highly placed individuals in the Babangida government. The story, if published, could have been politically explosive. It was never published. Dele Giwa's death was a message. It was sent to Nigerian journalists in the clearest possible terms: there are limits to what you can investigate, and those limits are enforced not in court but in the bodies of those who exceed them."}, {"id": "legends-dele-giwa#s3", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "The message was received. Some journalists became more cautious. Others — including those who worked with Giwa at Newswatch — became more defiant. The magazine continued to publish. The press in general continued to push against the boundaries that military governments tried to enforce. Gani Fawehinmi, as we have told, pursued justice for Giwa through the courts for decades. The struggle for accountability in Giwa's murder became one of the defining battles of the Nigerian human rights movement. Giwa's legacy is the practice of journalism he embodied: rigorously researched, fearlessly published, committed to the public interest over the interests of power. Nigerian journalism schools teach his career. Young journalists speak his name with the reverence reserved for martyrs of the profession."}, {"id": "legends-dele-giwa#s4", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "He died for the truth. The truth he was pursuing died with him, in the sense that it was never published. But the commitment to truth-telling that he represented has outlived both the bomb and the government that sent it. Dele Giwa. The journalist who died on a Sunday morning opening his mail. The bomb was meant to be a full stop. It became instead an ellipsis — the beginning of a sentence that the Nigerian press has been writing ever since."}]}, {"id": "legends-beko-ransome-kuti", "emoji": "🌟", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#00201a,#003a30)", "category": "legends", "rating": 9.4, "duration": "4 min", "title": {"en": "Beko Ransome-Kuti: The Doctor Who Prescribed Democracy", "yo": "Beko Ransome-Kuti"}, "desc": {"en": "Beko Ransome-Kuti, the doctor who chose democracy over comfort.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Living Legends", "secondary": [], "source_note": "", "segments": [{"id": "legends-beko-ransome-kuti#s1", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Narrator opens: The Ransome-Kuti family deserves its own category in the history of Nigerian activism. The patriarch Israel was a Christian minister and educator. His wife Funmilayo was the feminist pioneer who led women's marches. Their son Fela was the musician who used Afrobeat to challenge power. And their other son, Beko Ransome-Kuti, was a medical doctor who became one of the most important human rights activists in Nigerian history. Beko was born in 1940. He trained as a doctor and practiced medicine — but from early in his career, he was also deeply involved in civil society activism, particularly around health policy and human rights. He helped found the Campaign for Democracy — one of the most important civil society organizations in Nigeria — and served as its chairman during the crucial years of the fight against military rule."}, {"id": "legends-beko-ransome-kuti#s2", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "During the Abacha years, when the Campaign for Democracy and allied organizations were organizing protests, strikes, and civil disobedience against the military government, Beko was among the most visible and most exposed leaders. He was arrested. He was tried before a military tribunal. He was sentenced to life imprisonment — later reduced to 15 years — by the Abacha government. He spent three years in prison, from 1995 to 1998 — released after Abacha's death, like many other political prisoners. His health had deteriorated in prison. He had been denied proper medical care during his detention. After his release, he continued his activism. He continued to be a voice for democracy, for accountability, for the rights of ordinary Nigerians. He died in 2006, aged 65 — relatively young, the toll of his years of activism and imprisonment visible in his body even if not in his spirit."}, {"id": "legends-beko-ransome-kuti#s3", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "The Ransome-Kuti family. Four generations of people who said \"no\" to power when \"no\" was dangerous. Israel, who insisted on African dignity in the church. Funmilayo, who insisted on women's rights in the market. Fela, who insisted on truth in the music. Beko, who insisted on democracy in the hospital and the courtroom and the prison cell. What was in the water in Abeokuta? What produced this family? Perhaps it was simply the Yoruba tradition — the tradition that says the community matters, that the elder's obligation is to speak truth, that the person who sees wrongdoing and keeps silent is complicit. The Ransome-Kutis did not keep silent. At whatever cost."}]}, {"id": "legends-wande-abimbola", "emoji": "🌟", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#00201a,#003a30)", "category": "legends", "rating": 9.4, "duration": "4 min", "title": {"en": "Professor Wande Abimbola: The Keeper of Ifa for the Modern World", "yo": "Wande Abimbola"}, "desc": {"en": "Professor Wande Abimbola, scholar and keeper of Ifa for the modern world.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Living Legends", "secondary": [], "source_note": "", "segments": [{"id": "legends-wande-abimbola#s1", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Narrator opens: There are scholars who study a tradition from the outside, producing knowledge about it that the tradition itself does not recognize or validate. And there are scholars who are the tradition — initiated practitioners who also have academic training, who bring the inside knowledge and the outside methodology simultaneously, and whose work serves both the scholarly community and the living tradition they represent. Professor Wande Abimbola is in the second category — and he is without question the most important figure in the modern transmission of Ifa knowledge to both the academic world and the diaspora communities that have reclaimed the tradition."}, {"id": "legends-wande-abimbola#s2", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Born in Lagos in 1932, Abimbola trained in Yoruba studies and became one of the most distinguished Yoruba language scholars of his generation. But his work went beyond the academic study of Ifa — he was also a practicing Babalawo, initiated into the tradition in the orthodox manner, carrying the knowledge of the Odu corpus not just as intellectual material but as lived spiritual responsibility. His academic work on Ifa — his analyses of the Odu verses, his studies of the corpus's internal structure and philosophical content, his arguments for Ifa's recognition as a sophisticated world philosophical tradition — brought scholarly legitimacy to a body of knowledge that the colonial education system had consistently dismissed as primitive superstition. His books, including \"Ifa: An Exposition of Ifa Literary Corpus\" and \"Awon Oju Odu Mereerindinlogun,\" remain foundational texts in the academic study of Yoruba religion."}, {"id": "legends-wande-abimbola#s3", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "His international work took Ifa beyond Nigeria. He served as the Awise Agbaye — the world spokesperson for Ifa — a role that involved him in the global Ifa community: the Brazilian Candomble practitioners, the Cuban Santeria Babalawo, the Trinidadian Orisha community, the American practitioners who had reclaimed the tradition. He built bridges between these communities and the Nigerian Ifa source, helping to resolve disputes about practice and to ensure that the diaspora traditions remained in living dialogue with the African original. He was twice elected a senator in the Nigerian Senate, giving him a political platform to advocate for the recognition and protection of traditional Yoruba culture at the level of national policy."}, {"id": "legends-wande-abimbola#s4", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "He has given Ifa a voice in the modern world without requiring Ifa to become something other than what it is. That is the rarest and most valuable kind of scholarly work — not translation into another framework, but presentation on its own terms, in a language the world can hear."}]}, {"id": "legends-bola-tinubu", "emoji": "🌟", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#00201a,#003a30)", "category": "legends", "rating": 9.3, "duration": "4 min", "title": {"en": "Bola Tinubu: Politics as Yoruba Power", "yo": "Bola Tinubu: Politics as Yoruba Power"}, "desc": {"en": "Bola Tinubu and Yoruba political power.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Living Legends", "secondary": [], "source_note": "", "segments": [{"id": "legends-bola-tinubu#s1", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Whatever you think of Bola Tinubu as a political figure — and in Nigeria, strong opinions are unavoidable — his career represents something significant about how Yoruba political culture operates in the modern Nigerian context. The emphasis on coalition-building. The management of patronage networks that extend across ethnic and religious lines. The patience with which positions are prepared over years before they are claimed."}, {"id": "legends-bola-tinubu#s2", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "These are recognisable Yoruba political strategies, adapted to the context of the Nigerian federal state. Lagos under his political influence became what it is: the commercial capital of West Africa, a city of genuine complexity and genuine dysfunction, which is also what complex cities tend to be. The governance record is contested. The achievement of turning Lagos into something resembling a functional state is not."}, {"id": "legends-bola-tinubu#s3", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "Yoruba political culture has always valued the politician who can sustain a coalition — not through fear but through the constant, laborious work of making each faction feel its interests are considered. Whether any particular holder of power honours this tradition is a different question from whether the tradition exists. It exists. Tinubu is one data point in a very long series."}]}, {"id": "legends-olumide-akpata", "emoji": "🌟", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#00201a,#003a30)", "category": "legends", "rating": 9.4, "duration": "4 min", "title": {"en": "Olumide Akpata: The Lawyer Who Said Enough", "yo": "Olumide Akpata: The Lawyer Who Said Enough"}, "desc": {"en": "Olumide Akpata, the lawyer who said enough.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Living Legends", "secondary": [], "source_note": "", "segments": [{"id": "legends-olumide-akpata#s1", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "In 2020, Nigerian lawyers elected Olumide Akpata as President of the Nigerian Bar Association — the youngest person to hold the position in the organisation's history. He ran on a platform of institutional reform, of making the bar function as a professional body rather than a social club, of taking seriously the role that lawyers should play in defending rule of law in a country where that rule is constantly under"}, {"id": "legends-olumide-akpata#s2", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "pressure. He is Yoruba. He is from the generation that grew up watching every Nigerian institution fail — education, healthcare, infrastructure, the justice system — and chose, for reasons that probably mix idealism and strategy, to try to fix one institution from the inside. He serves the legal community with the energy of someone who has not yet been made tired by the work, which is its own kind of resource."}, {"id": "legends-olumide-akpata#s3", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "His story is not complete. He is still writing it, which is what makes him interesting. The completed stories are in the history books. The ones being written now are in the daily news, in the court filings, in the meetings that happen in boardrooms and chambers that most people never see."}]}, {"id": "legends-tunde-kelani", "emoji": "🌟", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#00201a,#003a30)", "category": "legends", "rating": 9.4, "duration": "4 min", "title": {"en": "Tunde Kelani: The Filmmaker Who Chose Yoruba", "yo": "Tunde Kelani"}, "desc": {"en": "Tunde Kelani, the filmmaker who chose to tell stories in Yoruba.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Living Legends", "secondary": [], "source_note": "", "segments": [{"id": "legends-tunde-kelani#s1", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "In an era when Nigerian filmmakers were making their names primarily in English or in Igbo-language Nollywood, Tunde Kelani chose Yoruba. His films — Ti Oluwa Nile, Thunderbolt, Saworoide, Arugba — are grounded in Yoruba culture with a specificity that goes beyond costume and location. The moral frameworks in his films are Yoruba. The humour is Yoruba. The way characters relate to elders, to the sacred, to community obligation — all of"}, {"id": "legends-tunde-kelani#s2", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "it is drawn from deep inside a tradition he clearly loves and understands. He is based in Ibadan, which is itself a statement. He did not move to Lagos to be closer to industry. He stayed where the culture is thick and where his particular kind of work makes the most sense. His production company, Mainframe Film and Television, has produced work that holds its own with anything made on the continent."}, {"id": "legends-tunde-kelani#s3", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "He is making the case, through the work itself, that Yoruba storytelling does not need to apologise for being Yoruba to find an audience. He is right. His films are watched and loved from Ibadan to London. The Yoruba-ness is not despite that. It is because of it."}]}, {"id": "legends-bayo-akomolafe", "emoji": "🌟", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#00201a,#003a30)", "category": "legends", "rating": 9.4, "duration": "4 min", "title": {"en": "Dr. Bayo Akomolafe: The Philosopher Who Slows Down", "yo": "Dr. Bayo Akomolafe: The Philosopher Who Slows Down"}, "desc": {"en": "Bayo Akomolafe, the philosopher who invites the world to slow down.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Living Legends", "secondary": [], "source_note": "", "segments": [{"id": "legends-bayo-akomolafe#s1", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Bayo Akomolafe is a Yoruba thinker whose work is genuinely difficult to categorise, which is perhaps the most interesting thing about him. He draws on Yoruba cosmology, on quantum physics, on postcolonial theory, on the specific kind of slow attention that the Yoruba concept of itan — story, history, becoming — requires of anyone who takes it seriously. His central provocation, repeated across"}, {"id": "legends-bayo-akomolafe#s2", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "essays and lectures and his book These Wilds Beyond Our Fences, is that the pace of modern life — the insistence on solutions, on clarity, on moving forward — may itself be part of the problem. That some things require slowness. That the sacred emerges in the margins, in the cracks, in the places where the efficient logic of modern life does not reach."}, {"id": "legends-bayo-akomolafe#s3", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "This is recognisably Yoruba in its roots — the tradition that understands that the crossroads is where power lives, that the moment of hesitation contains more information than the moment of certainty. Akomolafe has taken that traditional insight and applied it to the questions of our current moment: ecological crisis, racial justice, institutional failure. The old thought, in the new fire. It is still burning clearly."}]}, {"id": "legends-dele-momodu", "emoji": "🌟", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#00201a,#003a30)", "category": "legends", "rating": 9.3, "duration": "4 min", "title": {"en": "Dele Momodu: The Man Who Documented a Generation", "yo": "Dele Momodu: The Man Who Documented a Generation"}, "desc": {"en": "Dele Momodu, the publisher who documented a generation.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Living Legends", "secondary": [], "source_note": "", "segments": [{"id": "legends-dele-momodu#s1", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Dele Momodu founded Ovation magazine in 1996 and spent the next two decades documenting the social life of the African elite with a thoroughness and a flair that no one else had thought to apply to this particular subject. Ovation’s pages were full of weddings, funerals, birthday parties, and the spectacular celebrations that wealthy West Africans throw at significant moments of their lives. It"}, {"id": "legends-dele-momodu#s2", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "was social journalism. It was also, in its way, anthropology. What Momodu understood was that these occasions — the celebrations that the serious press ignored or covered condescendingly — were where the culture was actually visible. Where families made statements. Where alliances were formed and broken. Where the values of the community were performed in cloth and food and music and the positioning of chairs."}, {"id": "legends-dele-momodu#s3", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "He is Yoruba, from Ondo State, and his eye for the ceremony, for the social meaning embedded in surface detail, is recognisably Yoruba. A culture that has elevated occasion to an art form needed someone who understood occasions as texts. Momodu could read them. He published what he read. A generation read along."}]}, {"id": "legends-seun-kuti", "emoji": "🌟", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#00201a,#003a30)", "category": "legends", "rating": 9.3, "duration": "4 min", "title": {"en": "Seun Kuti: The Third Generation of Fire", "yo": "Seun Kuti: The Third Generation of Fire"}, "desc": {"en": "Seun Kuti, the third generation of the Kuti fire.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Living Legends", "secondary": [], "source_note": "", "segments": [{"id": "legends-seun-kuti#s1", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Fela Kuti’s youngest son Seun inherited his father’s band — Egypt 80 — at the age of fifteen, when Fela died in 1997. He was seventeen when he first led the band on stage. He has been leading it ever since, playing music that is explicitly political, explicitly Afrobeats, and explicitly in conversation with his father’s legacy while also being his own. The inheritance was not simple. Fela’s shadow is enormous. Any musician working in that shadow who"}, {"id": "legends-seun-kuti#s2", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "is also the man’s son is going to spend a significant portion of their career answering to comparisons they didn’t ask for. Seun has handled this with a combination of stubbornness and genuine artistic development: staying close to the Afrobeats form that his father established while pushing it into new territory, incorporating jazz and more explicit hip hop references, and saying things about Nigeria and Africa in 2024 that Fela said in 1977 and that are, depressingly, still necessary."}, {"id": "legends-seun-kuti#s3", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "He is the third generation of Yoruba political music. His grandmother was Funmilayo Ransome-Kuti. His father was Fela. He is carrying something that is heavier than most people are asked to carry, and he carries it standing up."}]}, {"id": "legends-mo-abudu", "emoji": "🌟", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#00201a,#003a30)", "category": "legends", "rating": 9.3, "duration": "4 min", "title": {"en": "Mo Abudu: The Woman Who Built a Media Empire", "yo": "Mo Abudu: The Woman Who Built a Media Empire"}, "desc": {"en": "Mo Abudu, the woman who built a media empire.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Living Legends", "secondary": [], "source_note": "", "segments": [{"id": "legends-mo-abudu#s1", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Mo Abudu’s Ebonylife Media has become one of the most significant media companies in Africa — producing films and series that reach Netflix and other global platforms, and doing so from Lagos rather than from a production hub that requires the creative vision to be exported and filtered through non-African sensibilities. She is Yoruba, from Lagos, and the trajectory of her career —"}, {"id": "legends-mo-abudu#s2", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "from human resources professional to television presenter to media executive — is recognisably Yoruba in its structure: the willingness to build laterally, to accumulate competence in adjacent domains before making the decisive move, to treat every role as preparation for the next one. This is not a Western business school model. It is the way market women have been building enterprises for centuries."}, {"id": "legends-mo-abudu#s3", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "What she built with Ebonylife is not just a business. It is an argument about where storytelling should happen and who should control it. African stories, told by Africans, produced in Africa, financed in Africa, reaching global audiences — this is not a romantic idea in her work. It is a production schedule. It is a business plan. It is working."}]}, {"id": "legends-tobi-ogedengbe", "emoji": "🌟", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#00201a,#003a30)", "category": "legends", "rating": 9.3, "duration": "4 min", "title": {"en": "Tobi Ogedengbe: Grassroots to Global", "yo": "Tobi Ogedengbe: Grassroots to Global"}, "desc": {"en": "A community health worker in Osogbo whose quiet thirty-year service is its own kind of legend.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Living Legends", "secondary": [], "source_note": "", "segments": [{"id": "legends-tobi-ogedengbe#s1", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Not every Yoruba legend is famous. Some of the most important people in any community are the ones who stay close to it, who put their skills back into the neighbourhood that produced them, who make the invisible work of sustaining a community visible by simply showing up and doing it for thirty years. Tobi Ogedengbe is a community health worker"}, {"id": "legends-tobi-ogedengbe#s2", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "in Osogbo who has spent three decades doing preventive medicine in communities where hospitals are distant and preventable diseases are common. She trained as a nurse, could have worked in a city hospital with better facilities and better pay. She chose to work in communities where the gap between what healthcare could do and what it was actually doing was largest."}, {"id": "legends-tobi-ogedengbe#s3", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "She has delivered babies, managed childhood malnutrition, tracked vaccination rates, trained community health volunteers, and argued — successfully, multiple times — with local government officials who wanted to cut the programmes she runs. She is not famous. She is necessary, which is better."}]}, {"id": "legends-akinnuoye-agbaje", "emoji": "🌟", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#00201a,#003a30)", "category": "legends", "rating": 9.4, "duration": "4 min", "title": {"en": "Adewale Akinnuoye-Agbaje: The Actor Who Carried Two Worlds", "yo": "Adewale Akinnuoye-Agbaje: The Actor Who Carried Two Worlds"}, "desc": {"en": "Adewale Akinnuoye-Agbaje, the actor who carried two worlds.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Living Legends", "secondary": [], "source_note": "", "segments": [{"id": "legends-akinnuoye-agbaje#s1", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Adewale Akinnuoye-Agbaje was born in London to Yoruba parents from Nigeria, was fostered at an early age with a white English family in a small English town, grew up between two cultures that had no particular mechanism for acknowledging each other, and became one of the most distinctive character actors in Hollywood — recognisable from Lost, from Game of Thrones, from a dozen films that required someone with genuine presence and genuine depth."}, {"id": "legends-akinnuoye-agbaje#s2", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "His 2018 film Farming, which he wrote and directed, tells his own story: a Nigerian boy fostered in an English town who joins a skinhead gang as a form of belonging. The film does not simplify this. It refuses to make sense of what cannot quite be made sense of — the particular violence of being placed between two cultures without the tools to navigate either, and the particular resilience of surviving that experience."}, {"id": "legends-akinnuoye-agbaje#s3", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "His career is proof that the Yoruba thread runs through unexpected lives in unexpected places — that you can grow up in an English farming town and still carry something in your body, in the quality of your presence, that goes back further than anything England gave you."}]}, {"id": "legends-funke-akindele", "emoji": "🌟", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#00201a,#003a30)", "category": "legends", "rating": 9.4, "duration": "4 min", "title": {"en": "Funke Akindele: Nollywood’s Most Bankable Director", "yo": "Funke Akindele: Nollywood’s Most Bankable Director"}, "desc": {"en": "Funke Akindele, Nollywood's most bankable director.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Living Legends", "secondary": [], "source_note": "", "segments": [{"id": "legends-funke-akindele#s1", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Funke Akindele is the most commercially successful filmmaker in Nigerian cinema right now, which means she is one of the most commercially successful filmmakers on the African continent. Her Omo Ghetto franchise, her A Tribe Called Judah — which became the highest-grossing Nollywood film of all time upon its release — are made with Lagos in their bones: the city’s humour, its violence, its warmth, its class dynamics, its particular genius for getting by when the systems that are"}, {"id": "legends-funke-akindele#s2", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "supposed to work do not work. She is Yoruba and she works in Yoruba-inflected Lagos stories, but her audience is all of Nigeria and much of the diaspora. This is what good local specificity does: it goes deep enough into a particular place and particular people that the universals emerge naturally, without having to be aimed at. She is not trying to make films that will travel. She is making films that are true. The travel is a consequence."}, {"id": "legends-funke-akindele#s3", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "Her success has also changed the argument about Nigerian cinema from “can it be taken seriously?” to “who is going to fund the infrastructure it now clearly deserves?” This is a better argument to be having."}]}, {"id": "legends-enoch-adeboye", "emoji": "🌟", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#00201a,#003a30)", "category": "legends", "rating": 9.3, "duration": "4 min", "title": {"en": "Pastor Enoch Adeboye: Faith as Yoruba Institution", "yo": "Pastor Enoch Adeboye: Faith as Yoruba Institution"}, "desc": {"en": "Pastor Enoch Adeboye and faith as a Yoruba institution.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Living Legends", "secondary": [], "source_note": "", "segments": [{"id": "legends-enoch-adeboye#s1", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Enoch Adejare Adeboye — Papa Adeboye, as his congregation calls him — is the General Overseer of the Redeemed Christian Church of God, one of the largest Pentecostal denominations in the world, with congregations on every continent. He holds a doctorate in mathematics. He became a Christian in his thirties and rose through the RCCG to become its leader, under whom the church’s membership grew"}, {"id": "legends-enoch-adeboye#s2", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "from a few hundred thousand to tens of millions. He is deeply Yoruba in his public manner: the quietness, the use of proverb in sermon, the patience that reads as humility but is also something more strategic, the capacity to be simultaneously accessible and authoritative. He tells stories in the Yoruba tradition — parables that work because they are rooted in observable reality, not abstraction."}, {"id": "legends-enoch-adeboye#s3", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "His influence goes well beyond theology. The Redeemed Christian Church’s redemption camp outside Lagos has become a city in its own right. His Holy Ghost Congress, held annually, draws millions of worshippers in what is one of the largest human gatherings on earth. He represents the specific way that Yoruba culture has adopted, adapted, and expanded Christianity — making it large enough to hold the whole of what Yoruba people need from a communal spiritual practice, while keeping it recognisably connected to the original."}]}, {"id": "legends-gani-adams", "emoji": "🌟", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#00201a,#003a30)", "category": "legends", "rating": 9.6, "duration": "9 min", "title": {"en": "Gani Adams of Arigidi-Akoko: The Sword of Our Own Day", "yo": "Gani Adams"}, "desc": {"en": "The activist who holds the Aare Ona Kakanfo title in our own day.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Living Legends", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Aare Ona Kakanfo, the story of the activist and defender of Yoruba custom who bears the ancient title in our own time, installed in the year two thousand and eighteen)", "segments": [{"id": "legends-gani-adams#s1", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Come close, my children, for tonight we reach the end of our long roll, the very last knot in a rope that began with Kokoro Igangan of Iwoye in the mist of ancient days and reaches now into our own time, into the years you yourselves are living through. Tonight we sit with the man who bears the sword of the Aare Ona Kakanfo in our own day: Chief Gani Adams of Arigidi-Akoko. He is the fifteenth in the great line, and he was installed as Aare Ona Kakanfo by the Alaafin of Oyo in the year two thousand and eighteen, in a grand ceremony that drew the whole Yoruba nation to witness the crowning of a new war-marshal for a new age."}, {"id": "legends-gani-adams#s2", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Before I tell his story, my children, let me speak one honest word, as I always do at this fire. Gani Adams is a living man, and his story is still being written in the world outside this circle of firelight. I tell you of him as he is known up to the edge of my own knowledge. But a living man’s story does not stand still. Deeds may be added, honors may change, the years may bring new chapters that I cannot see from where I sit. So take what I tell you as the truth of the man up to my own day, and know that for the very latest of his doings you must look to the living record of the world, to the news and the chronicles of your own time. An honest griot tells you where his certain knowledge ends, and about a living man, it ends at the present moment."}, {"id": "legends-gani-adams#s3", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Now, who is this man upon whom the ancient title has come? Gani Adams is a son of Arigidi-Akoko, in the Akoko country of the Yoruba, and he rose to prominence in a way quite different from the war-marshals of old, yet true to the deepest spirit of the office. He rose as an activist, a defender of the Yoruba people and their heritage, in the difficult years of the nineteen nineties and after, when Nigeria groaned under military rule and, as you heard in the story of Abiola, the stolen June twelfth mandate had set the whole Yoruba nation aflame with grievance."}, {"id": "legends-gani-adams#s4", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "In those years, movements arose to defend the rights and the interests of the Yoruba people within the troubled Nigerian nation, and Gani Adams became a prominent leader in that cause. He was, in his early days, a fierce and controversial figure, a militant voice for Yoruba self-determination in a dangerous time, a man the powerful watched with wary eyes. Remember the lesson, my children, that we never flatten a man to a single word: to some in those days he was a troubling militant, to others a brave defender of a people who felt themselves wronged and unheard. He was, like many who rise in hard times, a figure of fire and controversy."}, {"id": "legends-gani-adams#s5", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "But a man’s story unfolds, and over the years Gani Adams grew into a broader role as a champion and promoter of Yoruba culture, heritage, and unity. He became known as a defender of Yoruba tradition, a promoter of the festivals and the customs and the memory of the ancestors, a builder of cultural institutions meant to keep the flame of Yoruba identity burning in a modern age that so easily forgets. He worked to revive and celebrate the old ways, the very kind of remembering that these fireside stories themselves are part of. And it was this role, as a defender and promoter of the Yoruba heritage and a champion of the people, that made him fitting in the eyes of the Alaafin to receive the ancient title."}, {"id": "legends-gani-adams#s6", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "And think, my children, on how fitting it truly is, and how the meaning of the office has journeyed across the centuries. The first Aare Ona Kakanfo defended the Yoruba with cavalry and spear against the enemies of the empire. The last of the old fighting war-marshals, Latoosa, led great armies in the age of the guns. The modern bearers, Akintola and Abiola, fought for their people in the arena of politics and paid dearly for it. And now, in our own day, the war that the Aare must fight has changed again. The greatest threat to the Yoruba people in our time is not a foreign army or even a stolen election, but something quieter and in its way more dangerous: the slow forgetting of who we are. The fading of the language, the customs, the stories, the memory of the ancestors, under the great flood of the modern world."}, {"id": "legends-gani-adams#s7", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "And so it is deeply fitting that the Aare Ona Kakanfo of our own day should be a man whose great cause is the defense of Yoruba heritage and memory. For that is the front line now. The sword of the modern war-marshal is turned against forgetting itself. Gani Adams fights, in his way, the same fight that I fight at this fire when I tell you these stories: the fight to keep a people from losing their own soul."}, {"id": "legends-gani-adams#s8", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Let me draw the great lesson of the whole line now, my children, for with Gani Adams we have come to its end, and it is time to gather up its meaning. From Kokoro Igangan to Gani Adams, across all these centuries, what has the Aare Ona Kakanfo truly been? Strip away the changing weapons, the spear and the horse, the rifle, the ballot, the cultural festival, and what remains? This: the Aare Ona Kakanfo is the one who stands up for the Yoruba people. The champion. The defender. The one who takes upon himself the burden of the people’s safety and the people’s honor, and who is willing to spend himself for it."}, {"id": "legends-gani-adams#s9", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "The weapons changed with the ages, but the calling never changed. Oyabi defended his people with cavalry. Kurunmi defended a sacred law with his life. Latoosa defended Ibadan’s power to the bitter end. Abiola defended a stolen mandate unto death. And Gani Adams, in our own day, defends the very memory and identity of the Yoruba nation against the forgetting that would erase it. Fifteen men, one calling, across the rise and fall of empires and the coming and going of colonial masters and the birth of a new nation. That, my children, is the meaning of the great line we have walked together."}, {"id": "legends-gani-adams#s10", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "So we honor Chief Gani Adams of Arigidi-Akoko, the Aare Ona Kakanfo of our own day, the fifteenth bearer of the ancient sword, installed in two thousand and eighteen, the defender of Yoruba heritage in an age when heritage itself is under siege. And through him we honor the whole unbroken line, from the first war-chief of a young empire to the cultural champion of a modern people. The rope held, my children. From the very first knot to the very last, across all the centuries and all the storms, the rope held, and the sword passed from faithful hand to faithful hand, and the Yoruba people had, in every age, a champion to stand for them."}, {"id": "legends-gani-adams#s11", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "And now our long telling of the war-marshals is done. We have walked from Kokoro Igangan and Oyatope of Iwoye, through Oyabi of Ajase and the two sons of Jabata, Adeta and Oku, through the tragic Afonja of Ilorin and the war-chiefs of the fall, Toyeje and Edun and Amepo, through the lion Kurunmi of Ijaye and Ojo Aburumaku of Ogbomoso and the last great fighting marshal Latoosa of Ibadan, and on into the modern age with Akintola and Abiola, and here at last to Gani Adams of our own day. Fifteen champions of a people who would not be conquered and would not be forgotten."}, {"id": "legends-gani-adams#s12", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "Remember them, my children. Remember that a people who keep the memory of their champions can never truly be defeated, for as long as the stories are told at the fire, the ancestors are not dead, and the sword is not broken, and the Yoruba nation endures. That is why we tell these tales. That is why you must tell them, in your turn, to the children who will sit at your own fires when mine has long gone cold."}]}, {"id": "naija-forging-of-nigeria", "emoji": "🏛️", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#001a0a,#003a1a)", "category": "naija", "rating": 9.6, "duration": "11 min", "title": {"en": "The Forging of Nigeria: From Many Nations Into One", "yo": "The Forging of Nigeria: From Many Nations Into One"}, "desc": {"en": "The conquest, the 1914 amalgamation, and the rise of the nationalists: the making of Nigeria before independence.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Nigeria History", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Naijiria, the first telling, the making of the country in the age before independence, the conquest, the amalgamation of 1914, the rise of the nationalists, and the dawn of freedom in 1960)", "segments": [{"id": "naija-forging-of-nigeria#s1", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Children, tonight we turn a great corner in our telling. Until now I have told you the story of the Yoruba, our kings and our war-lords, our gods and our giants. But the Yoruba do not live alone in the world. We are one people among many who were gathered, in the age of our grandfathers and great-grandfathers, into a single country called Nigeria. And that country, its birth and its troubles and its long struggle to become itself, is now a part of our story too, for we are its children as surely as we are children of Oduduwa. So let me tell you, across several nights, the story of Nigeria itself, honestly, the glory and the shadow together, as a griot must."}, {"id": "naija-forging-of-nigeria#s2", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Begin, children, with a truth that you must hold at the very center of everything that follows. Nigeria was not a nation that grew up slowly from one people, the way the old Yoruba or the old Oyo grew. Nigeria was made. It was assembled, deliberately, by a foreign power, the British, out of hundreds of different peoples who had never before been ruled as one. The Yoruba in the west. The Igbo in the east. The Hausa and the Fulani in the north, with their great emirates and their long history of Islamic learning and rule. And hundreds of smaller peoples, the Efik, the Ijaw, the Tiv, the Kanuri, the Nupe, the Ibibio, and so many more, each with its own tongue, its own gods, its own kings, its own long memory."}, {"id": "naija-forging-of-nigeria#s3", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "These peoples had traded with one another, and warred with one another, and known one another across the centuries. But they had never been one country. It was the British who drew a line around them all on a map and said, this shall be a single thing, and we shall rule it. Hear how it happened. Through the last century, the British power crept inland from the coast, as I told you in the tale of Lagos, where their warships first planted the foreign flag. Piece by piece, war by war, treaty by treaty, and often by force and trickery both, they extended their grip. They took Lagos, then the Yoruba country, then the east, and in the north they conquered the great Sokoto Caliphate, that mighty Islamic empire, bringing its emirs under their control. By the early years of the last century, the British held sway over the whole vast territory."}, {"id": "naija-forging-of-nigeria#s4", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "At first they governed it as separate pieces, a Northern Protectorate and a Southern Protectorate, along with the Colony of Lagos. But in the year 1914, a British governor named Lugard joined the north and the south together into one single territory, one administration, and this is called the Amalgamation of 1914. That is the year, children, that many people mark as the true birth of Nigeria as one country. The very name Nigeria, it is said, was suggested by an Englishwoman, a journalist named Flora Shaw, who later married Lugard, taking it from the great River Niger that runs through the land."}, {"id": "naija-forging-of-nigeria#s5", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "So the country was named by a stranger, and joined together by a stranger, for the convenience of strangers. This is the birthmark Nigeria has carried ever since, and you must understand it to understand all the troubles that came after. The country was a container built by others, and only later did the peoples inside it have to work out whether they could become one nation in spirit as well as one country on the map. That work, children, is not finished even to this day."}, {"id": "naija-forging-of-nigeria#s6", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Now the British ruled through a system they called indirect rule, which was Lugard’s great idea. Rather than governing every village directly with British officers, of whom they had few, they ruled through the existing kings and chiefs and emirs, keeping the traditional rulers in place but making them serve British purposes, collect British taxes, enforce British law. In the north, where the emirs already had a strong system of governance, this worked smoothly and the British and the emirs made comfortable partners. In the west, among us Yoruba, the Obas were bent to this service too, though our people, being argumentative and proud, gave the British more trouble. In the east, among the Igbo, who in many places had no kings at all but governed themselves through councils and assemblies of the people, the British could not find kings to rule through, so they appointed warrant chiefs, men given authority by British warrant, and this artificial system bred deep resentment and, in time, revolt."}, {"id": "naija-forging-of-nigeria#s7", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "Understand the shadow here, children. Colonial rule brought some things that would matter greatly, the railways and roads that tied the country together, the English language that let a Yoruba and an Igbo and a Hausa speak to one another for the first time, Western schools that trained a new class of educated Africans, and the churches and mosques that spread. But it was rule for the benefit of the ruler, not the ruled. The wealth of the land, the palm-oil, the cocoa, the groundnuts, the tin, and later the oil, flowed outward to enrich Britain. The African was a subject, not a citizen, taxed and governed but denied real power in his own land. That is the hard truth of colonialism, and no railway can balance the scale against it."}, {"id": "naija-forging-of-nigeria#s8", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "But you cannot educate a people and then expect them to stay content as subjects forever, children. The very schools the British built raised up a generation of Nigerians, lawyers and doctors and journalists and teachers, who read the fine European words about liberty and self-government and asked, quite reasonably, why these should not apply to them. And so was born the nationalist movement, the long struggle for Nigerians to rule themselves."}, {"id": "naija-forging-of-nigeria#s9", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "The early giants of this struggle came often from the ranks of the educated and the press. Men like Herbert Macaulay, an early firebrand of Lagos, grandson of the very Bishop Ajayi Crowther I told you of in the diaspora tales, are remembered as fathers of Nigerian nationalism. And then rose the great figures who would lead the country to freedom and beyond. Nnamdi Azikiwe, the Igbo journalist and orator, Zik of Africa they called him, whose newspapers thundered against colonial rule and lit the fire of nationalism across the whole country. Obafemi Awolowo, our own Yoruba son, a lawyer of fierce discipline and vision, who organized the west and dreamed of a modern, educated, prosperous region. And Ahmadu Bello, the Sardauna of Sokoto, great-grandson of the founder of the Sokoto Caliphate, who led the north and guarded its interests with immense authority, along with his lieutenant Abubakar Tafawa Balewa, a schoolteacher of quiet dignity who would become the country’s first prime minister."}, {"id": "naija-forging-of-nigeria#s10", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Here, children, the seed of future trouble was already planted, and honesty requires that I name it. The nationalist leaders, great as they were, tended each to build his strongest base among his own people, Azikiwe among the Igbo and the east, Awolowo among the Yoruba and the west, Bello and Balewa among the Hausa-Fulani and the north. The political parties that grew up took on the colours of region and ethnicity. So even as Nigerians united to demand freedom from Britain, they were dividing along the very lines the country had been carelessly drawn across. The unity was real, but so were the fractures beneath it."}, {"id": "naija-forging-of-nigeria#s11", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Through the nineteen forties and fifties, the struggle advanced, not mainly by war but by argument, by constitutional conferences, by negotiation and agitation and the steady, undeniable pressure of a people determined to govern themselves. The British, weakened by the great world war and reading the direction of the age, agreed to a gradual handover. Region by region, Nigerians took more and more control of their own affairs, until the last step remained."}, {"id": "naija-forging-of-nigeria#s12", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "And so came the great day, children. On the first of October, in the year 1960, the British flag, the Union Jack, was lowered for the last time, and the green and white flag of Nigeria, green for the land and its forests and farms, white for peace, was raised in its place. Nigeria was free. An independent nation, the most populous in all of Africa, took its place among the nations of the world, full of hope, full of promise, rich in people and in resources, the great hope of the black race, many said, the country that would show the world what free Africans could build."}, {"id": "naija-forging-of-nigeria#s13", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "Abubakar Tafawa Balewa, the quiet schoolteacher, was the first prime minister. Nnamdi Azikiwe became the ceremonial head of state. The nationalists had won. The long night of colonial rule was over. There was dancing in the streets, children, real dancing, and a hope so bright it is almost painful to remember it now, knowing what came after."}, {"id": "naija-forging-of-nigeria#s14", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "Now hold the two palms, children, as we close this first telling. On one side, a country that was never a natural nation, assembled by strangers out of hundreds of peoples who had never chosen to be one, carrying from its very birth the fractures of region and tribe and the wounds of conquest. On the other, a genuine and glorious achievement, a great people who took the tool of self-rule and, through decades of disciplined, largely peaceful struggle, wrenched their freedom from one of the mightiest empires on earth, and stood up at last as masters in their own house, brimming with hope. Both are true, children. Nigeria was born flawed, and Nigeria was born hopeful. The whole story that follows, all the triumph and all the tragedy of the years to come, grows from the tension between those two truths, the deep fractures underneath and the great hope on top."}, {"id": "naija-forging-of-nigeria#s15", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "Tomorrow I will tell you what became of that shining hope, of the First Republic and how it fell, of the coming of the soldiers and the coups, and of the terrible war that nearly tore the young country apart before it had well begun."}]}, {"id": "naija-first-republic", "emoji": "🏛️", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#001a0a,#003a1a)", "category": "naija", "rating": 9.6, "duration": "10 min", "title": {"en": "The First Republic and the Breaking: Coups and a Terrible War", "yo": "The First Republic and the Breaking: Coups and a Terrible War"}, "desc": {"en": "The short First Republic, the coups of 1966, and the civil war that nearly broke the country.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Nigeria History", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Naijiria, the second telling, the short life of the First Republic, the coming of the soldiers in 1966, and the civil war of Biafra that nearly killed the young country)", "segments": [{"id": "naija-first-republic#s1", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Children, last night I left you in the year 1960, with the flag rising and the dancing in the streets and a hope so bright it hurt to remember. Tonight I must tell you how quickly and how terribly that hope was broken. For the First Republic of Nigeria, the free country the nationalists had built, lasted only a handful of years before it collapsed into violence, and out of that collapse came a war so dreadful that its wounds have never fully healed."}, {"id": "naija-first-republic#s2", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "When independence came, Nigeria was a federation of three great regions, and here, children, was the fatal flaw built into the house. The Northern Region was larger in land and population than the other two combined, so that the north could dominate the whole country by weight of numbers alone. The Western Region was the Yoruba heartland. The Eastern Region was the Igbo heartland. And each region was in truth the fortress of one great ethnic group and its leading politician. So politics became not a contest of ideas but a struggle of region against region, tribe against tribe, each fighting for its share and fearing domination by the others. In 1963, the country became a republic in full, cutting the last formal tie to the British crown, with Azikiwe as its first president, though real power lay with Prime Minister Balewa and the northern-led government. And almost at once, the young republic began to tear at itself."}, {"id": "naija-first-republic#s3", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Hear the troubles pile up, children, for they came fast. There was a bitter split in the Western Region, our own Yoruba house, between Chief Awolowo and his former deputy Akintola, a quarrel that turned violent and eventually saw Awolowo himself jailed on a charge of treason, which many believed was politically driven. There was a national census that everyone accused everyone else of rigging, because population meant power. There was an election in 1964 so marred by fraud and violence and boycott that it nearly broke the country then and there. And then, in the Western Region in 1965, came an election so brazenly rigged, so nakedly stolen, that the west erupted into a spasm of violence and arson and killing that people remembered with a shudder, a time simply called Operation Wetie, when thugs burned rivals and the region descended into chaos."}, {"id": "naija-first-republic#s4", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "The politicians, children, great as some of them had been in the freedom struggle, had failed at the harder task of governing together. They had let corruption fester, let elections be stolen, let the contest for power become a war of all against all. The people grew disgusted and afraid. And into that disgust and fear stepped the men with the guns. In January of 1966, a group of young army officers, many of them Igbo, staged Nigeria’s first military coup. They struck in the night, and they murdered. They killed Prime Minister Balewa, the quiet schoolteacher. They killed the Sardauna of Sokoto, Ahmadu Bello, the great lord of the north. They killed Akintola, the premier of the west. They killed senior military officers. It was a bloody stroke, meant, the plotters said, to sweep away the corrupt old order and save the nation."}, {"id": "naija-first-republic#s5", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "But hold the two palms even here, children. The plotters may have dreamed of a cleaner Nigeria. Yet look at who died and who did not. The murdered leaders were overwhelmingly northern and western. The eastern, Igbo leadership was largely spared. And the coup left an Igbo general, Aguiyi-Ironsi, as the new head of state. Whatever the plotters truly intended, it looked to the north and the west like an Igbo coup, a seizure of the country by one tribe over the corpses of the others. And in the poisoned, suspicious air of that young country, how a thing looks can matter as much as what it truly was."}, {"id": "naija-first-republic#s6", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "The reaction was horror upon horror. In July of that same year, 1966, northern officers struck back in a counter-coup, killing Ironsi and many Igbo officers, and putting a young northern officer named Yakubu Gowon at the head of the country. And then came the worst thing of all. In the north, mobs turned on the Igbo people living among them, the traders and workers and their families, and massacred them in their thousands. Ordinary Igbo men, women, and children, slaughtered in the streets and markets of northern towns in a terrible pogrom. The survivors fled east in their multitudes, back to the Igbo homeland, carrying their dead and their terror with them. After such a thing, children, how could the Igbo believe they had any safe place in Nigeria? Their leaders and their people asked, if we can be butchered like this and no one protects us, what is this country to us?"}, {"id": "naija-first-republic#s7", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "And so, in 1967, the military governor of the Eastern Region, an Igbo colonel named Odumegwu Ojukwu, declared that the east was leaving Nigeria. It would be a new and separate country, and he named it Biafra. The federal government under Gowon refused to let it go, for to lose the east was to lose much of the country including, crucially, most of the oil that was just then becoming the nation’s great wealth. And so came war. The Nigerian Civil War. The Biafran War. From 1967 to 1970, nearly three years of it. I will not glorify this war, children, for there was no glory in it, only sorrow. The federal side was far larger and better armed, and it slowly squeezed the breakaway Biafra into a shrinking pocket of land. And then the federal forces blockaded Biafra, cutting off food. And the children of Biafra began to starve."}, {"id": "naija-first-republic#s8", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "This is the image, children, that the whole world remembers of that war, and that we must never forget. The starving children of Biafra. Little ones with limbs like sticks and bellies swollen huge by the hunger-sickness, kwashiorkor, staring out of the photographs with old men’s eyes in babies’ faces. More than a million people died in that war, and most of them died not by the bullet but by hunger and disease, and a great many of them were children. It was one of the great humanitarian catastrophes of the age, and it happened here, among us, Nigerians killing Nigerians, or rather Nigerians and Biafrans both watching Biafran children starve as a weapon of war. In January of 1970, Biafra, broken and starving, surrendered. The war was over. Nigeria was one country again, held together by force."}, {"id": "naija-first-republic#s9", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "Now here, children, I must give credit where the honest hand requires it, for the peace was wiser than the war. When Biafra fell, many feared a great slaughter of the defeated Igbo in revenge. It did not come. Gowon, the federal leader, declared that there was no victor and no vanquished, only a Nigeria to be rebuilt, and he set a policy remembered by three words, Reconciliation, Reconstruction, Rehabilitation. The Igbo were, in principle, welcomed back into Nigeria. It was far from perfect, and deep bitterness remained, and many Igbo felt, and still feel, that they were never truly made whole, that property was lost and grievances left to fester. But the feared genocide of the vanquished did not happen, and for that restraint, in the ugly aftermath of so ugly a war, some credit is due."}, {"id": "naija-first-republic#s10", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "Hold the two palms, children, and hold them heavily, for this is the darkest telling so far. On one side, a catastrophe of failed leadership and tribal hatred, a stolen democracy, murderous coups, a pogrom against innocents, and a war that starved a million people including countless children, the young country very nearly strangled in its cradle. On the other side, a nation that, for all that horror, did not finally break apart, that survived its worst hour, and that in the end chose reconciliation over vengeance when vengeance was within its grasp."}, {"id": "naija-first-republic#s11", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "The deep lesson, children, is the terrible cost of what happens when a people let their differences of tribe and region curdle into hatred and mistrust, and when leaders reach for power by any means and count their own group’s advantage above the whole. The fractures the British built into Nigeria’s foundation, the fractures I warned you of in the first telling, here burst open into blood. Nigeria has spent every year since trying, and often failing, to heal them. Tomorrow I will tell you of the long age of the soldiers that this war ushered in, the coming of the oil wealth and the corruption that rode in with it, the brief and doomed Second Republic, and the hard men in uniform who ruled Nigeria for most of the decades that followed."}]}, {"id": "naija-soldiers-and-oil", "emoji": "🏛️", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#001a0a,#003a1a)", "category": "naija", "rating": 9.6, "duration": "9 min", "title": {"en": "The Age of the Soldiers and the Oil: The Second Republic and the Hard Men in Uniform", "yo": "The Age of the Soldiers and the Oil: The Second Republic and the Hard Men in Uniform"}, "desc": {"en": "The oil boom, the brief Second Republic, and the long rule of the men in uniform.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Nigeria History", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Naijiria, the third telling, the coming of the oil billions and the corruption that rode with them, the brief Second Republic, and the long rule of the military strongmen)", "segments": [{"id": "naija-soldiers-and-oil#s1", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Children, tonight I tell you of the long age when Nigeria was ruled mostly by soldiers, and of the great river of oil-wealth that flowed through the land in those years, blessing and cursing it in the same flood. For after the civil war, two things shaped Nigeria above all others. The gun, and the oil. And the story of these decades is the story of what those two things did to the young country."}, {"id": "naija-soldiers-and-oil#s2", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "First, the oil, children. Beneath the swamps and creeks of the Niger Delta, in the old Eastern and Mid-Western lands, lay one of the great oil deposits of the world. In the years around and after the civil war, the price of oil rose and rose, and suddenly Nigeria was awash in money, more money than its rulers had ever imagined, flowing in from selling crude oil to the world. This should have been the making of the country, children. It could have built the schools and hospitals and roads and industries that would lift a whole people out of poverty forever."}, {"id": "naija-soldiers-and-oil#s3", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Instead, it became a curse as much as a blessing, and this is one of the deepest tragedies of the Nigerian story. The oil-money did not have to be worked for, the way cocoa or groundnuts had to be grown and harvested by the sweat of farmers. It simply gushed up and flowed into the hands of the government. And money that comes without labour, children, is money that breeds theft. Whoever controlled the government controlled the oil-money, and so seizing the government became the greatest prize in the land, worth killing for, worth any crime. The oil turned politics into a scramble for a treasure-chest. Corruption, which had been a sickness in the First Republic, now became a plague, a way of life, as officials and generals and their friends siphoned off unimaginable sums while the ordinary people saw little of it. Nigerians have a bitter phrase for this. They call it the national cake, and everyone who reaches power scrambles for the biggest slice."}, {"id": "naija-soldiers-and-oil#s4", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Now the soldiers, children. The coup of 1966 had opened a door that would not close for thirty years, the door to military rule. Gowon, who led through the civil war, ruled until 1975, when he was overthrown in a coup while abroad, bloodlessly, and replaced by General Murtala Muhammed. Murtala was a fierce reformer who moved fast against corruption and won the people’s love in a matter of months, but he was assassinated in a failed coup in 1976, and the whole nation mourned him. Power passed then to his deputy, a Yoruba general named Olusegun Obasanjo, and remember that name, children, for he will return to this story in a most remarkable way."}, {"id": "naija-soldiers-and-oil#s5", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "And here Obasanjo did a rare and honourable thing. He kept the promise the soldiers had made to hand power back to civilians. In 1979, the military stepped aside, a new constitution was written modeled somewhat on the American system with an executive president, and elections were held. Nigeria returned to civilian rule. This was the Second Republic."}, {"id": "naija-soldiers-and-oil#s6", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "The man who won the presidency of the Second Republic was Shehu Shagari, a northern politician of gentle and dignified manner. And children, the Second Republic began in hope, as these things do, but it swiftly drowned in the very oil-money that should have saved it. These were years of staggering waste and corruption. The oil billions flowed in and vanished into private pockets and grand useless projects. Import licenses became tools of plunder. The phrase of the age was that money was not Nigeria’s problem, but how to spend it, and spend it they did, foolishly and corruptly, while the foundations of the country rotted. And then, cruelly, the oil price crashed, the money dried up, and the country was left with the debts and the waste and little to show for the vanished fortune."}, {"id": "naija-soldiers-and-oil#s7", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "The election of 1983 that returned Shagari to power was widely condemned as massively rigged, an echo of the frauds that had killed the First Republic. And the people, once again disgusted with corrupt and incompetent civilian politicians, once again did not much mourn when the soldiers came back."}, {"id": "naija-soldiers-and-oil#s8", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Come back they did, at the very end of 1983, in a coup that brought to power a stern, austere general named Muhammadu Buhari, and, note him too, children, for he also will return to this story decades later in an astonishing turn. Buhari launched a fierce campaign he called the War Against Indiscipline, trying to whip a chaotic and corrupt society into order by force, with soldiers making civil servants do frog-jumps for arriving late and jailing politicians of the fallen republic. Many Nigerians respected his personal honesty and his hatred of corruption, but his rule was harsh and rigid, and it did not last. In 1985 he was himself overthrown by yet another general."}, {"id": "naija-soldiers-and-oil#s9", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "This new ruler was General Ibrahim Babangida, a clever, smiling, cunning man who called himself not head of state but military president, and who ruled through the later nineteen eighties with a mixture of charm, manipulation, and ruthlessness. He was nicknamed Maradona, after the footballer, for his skill at dribbling, at feinting and dodging and never quite doing what he promised. He promised again and again to return the country to democracy, setting date after date and then moving them, running an endless, expensive transition program that always seemed designed never to actually end. Under him corruption reached new depths, and the country groaned under harsh economic medicine prescribed by foreign lenders that made the poor poorer."}, {"id": "naija-soldiers-and-oil#s10", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Now the two palms, children, for this whole long age of soldiers and oil. On one side, the deepest damage. Three decades in which the military taught the nation that power comes from the gun and not the ballot, in which the oil-wealth that could have transformed Nigeria was instead largely looted, in which corruption sank its roots so deep that they poison the country to this day, and in which the habit of democracy, the patient boring habit of arguing and voting and accepting the result, was never allowed to take hold."}, {"id": "naija-soldiers-and-oil#s11", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "On the other side, and honesty requires it, not every soldier was a mere thief, and not everything was ruin. Murtala’s brief flame of reform was real. Obasanjo’s handover in 1979 was a genuine and honourable return to civilian rule. Buhari’s hatred of corruption, however harshly expressed, was sincere. Roads were built, universities founded, a new capital city, Abuja, planned in the center of the country to belong to no single region. The country did not fall apart. But weigh it honestly, children, and the scale comes down hard on the side of the damage. The age of the soldiers and the squandered oil is the great lost opportunity of Nigeria, the decades in which a rich and hopeful nation was taught to steal from itself and to fear the gun more than it valued the vote."}, {"id": "naija-soldiers-and-oil#s12", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "The deep lesson, children, is about wealth and power both. Wealth that is not earned by labour, that simply gushes from the ground into the hands of whoever holds power, is a dangerous poison to a nation, for it turns the whole struggle of politics into a mere fight over who gets to loot the treasure. And power seized by the gun, however good the intentions of the man who seizes it, corrodes a country, because it teaches everyone that the way to the top is not to serve and persuade the people but to command the soldiers. Nigeria learned both these bitter lessons in these decades, and it is still trying to unlearn them."}, {"id": "naija-soldiers-and-oil#s13", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "Tomorrow I will tell you of the great crisis that ended the age of the soldiers, the freest election Nigeria had ever held and the wicked annulment that stole it, the martyrdom of the man who won it, the darkest tyrant of all, and the road that led at last, through much suffering, back to democracy."}]}, {"id": "naija-june-twelve", "emoji": "🏛️", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#001a0a,#003a1a)", "category": "naija", "rating": 9.6, "duration": "10 min", "title": {"en": "June the Twelfth and the Darkest Night: The Stolen Election and the Tyrant", "yo": "June the Twelfth and the Darkest Night: The Stolen Election and the Tyrant"}, "desc": {"en": "The freest election Nigeria ever held, its annulment, Abiola's martyrdom, and the Abacha terror.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Nigeria History", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Naijiria, the fourth telling, the freest election Nigeria ever held and its wicked annulment, the martyrdom of Abiola, the terror of Abacha, and the long road back to democracy)", "segments": [{"id": "naija-june-twelve#s1", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Children, tonight I tell you of the great wound and the great turning, the events that Nigerians mark above almost all others in their modern memory, gathered under one date that every Nigerian knows, June the Twelfth. It is the story of a democracy that was won and then stolen, of a man who died for the mandate the people gave him, and of the darkest tyranny the country ever endured, out of which, at last, came the democracy Nigeria has kept until this day."}, {"id": "naija-june-twelve#s2", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Remember, children, that the smiling general Babangida had promised endlessly to return the country to democracy, and endlessly delayed. But at last, in 1993, after years of his dribbling and his shifting dates, an election for president was finally held. And here is the marvel, children. That election, held on the twelfth of June, 1993, is remembered as the freest and fairest election in all of Nigeria’s history to that point, perhaps the cleanest the country had ever seen. And something happened in it that no one had dared to hope for."}, {"id": "naija-june-twelve#s3", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "The man who won, and won clearly, all across the country, was Moshood Kashimawo Olawale Abiola, known to all as M.K.O. Abiola, a Yoruba man, a fabulously wealthy businessman from Abeokuta, generous and beloved, a Muslim from the south. And here is the wonder. Abiola won not just in his own Yoruba west. He won in the north. He won in the east. He won across the ethnic and religious lines that had divided Nigeria since its birth. A southern Yoruba Muslim, with a northern Muslim running mate, swept the whole country. For one shining moment, June the Twelfth, 1993, Nigerians of every tribe and faith voted as one people for one man, and the old curse of tribe and region seemed, for a day, to be broken. It was, many still say, the most truly Nigerian moment the country had ever known."}, {"id": "naija-june-twelve#s4", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "And then, children, came the wickedness. Before the final results could even be fully declared, the military ruler Babangida annulled the election. Cancelled it. Threw away the freest vote in the nation’s history, the clear will of the whole people, and declared it null and void, for reasons he never honestly explained, though everyone understood it was because the powerful men around the throne, and elements of the north that did not wish to yield power, would not accept the result."}, {"id": "naija-june-twelve#s5", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Understand the enormity of this, children. The people had spoken, clearly, peacefully, as one nation across all its divisions. And a small clique of powerful men in uniform simply tore up that verdict because they did not like it. It was a theft not of money but of a whole nation’s voice, of the single most hopeful thing Nigeria had ever done. The country erupted. Protests, strikes, fury, especially in the Yoruba west, Abiola’s homeland, which felt the theft as a mortal insult. The whole edifice of military rule was shaken."}, {"id": "naija-june-twelve#s6", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Babangida, his authority shattered, was forced to step aside, handing power to a weak interim government, which was swiftly shoved out of the way. And into the vacuum stepped the darkest figure in all of Nigeria’s story, General Sani Abacha. And children, if the other soldiers had been hard men, Abacha was something worse. He was a true tyrant, cold, secretive, and merciless, and the years of his rule, from 1993 to 1998, were a reign of fear."}, {"id": "naija-june-twelve#s7", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "Abacha ruled by terror. He jailed and killed his opponents. He sent assassins after those who fled abroad. He locked up the old warhorse Obasanjo, the former head of state, on charges widely believed to be false, and Obasanjo very nearly died in that prison. He looted the treasury on a scale that stunned even a country used to corruption, stealing billions, salting them away in foreign banks, sums so vast that Nigeria was still recovering the stolen money from abroad decades later. Under him the country became a pariah, shunned by the world."}, {"id": "naija-june-twelve#s8", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "And two deaths from those dark years, children, must be spoken, for they are among the martyrs of the Nigerian story. The first is the writer and activist Ken Saro-Wiwa, a son of the Ogoni people of the oil-soaked Niger Delta, who led a brave, nonviolent campaign against the ruin the oil companies and the government were inflicting on his people’s land, the poisoned water, the flared gas, the wealth taken while the Ogoni got only pollution and poverty. Abacha’s regime seized him on trumped-up charges and, despite the outcry of the whole world, hanged him with eight of his comrades in 1995. The world recoiled in horror. Nigeria was cast out of the community of nations."}, {"id": "naija-june-twelve#s9", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "The second is Abiola himself, the winner of June the Twelfth. He had, after long hesitation, boldly declared himself the rightful president that the people had elected, and for that Abacha threw him into prison and kept him there, in harsh solitary confinement, for years. His wife, Kudirat Abiola, took up his cause and campaigned fiercely for his freedom, and for that she was assassinated, gunned down in Lagos, another martyr to June the Twelfth. Abiola himself never came out of prison alive."}, {"id": "naija-june-twelve#s10", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "And then, children, in 1998, the knot was cut by death. In June of that year, the tyrant Abacha suddenly died, in circumstances the tellings still whisper about. And barely a month later, still in detention, Abiola too died, on the very brink of what might have been his release, under circumstances that broke his supporters’ hearts and that many have questioned ever since. The winner of the great election never tasted the freedom, let alone the presidency, that the people had voted him. He died a prisoner, and a martyr, and his date, June the Twelfth, became sacred to Nigerian democracy."}, {"id": "naija-june-twelve#s11", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "But out of these two deaths came deliverance. The general who took over after Abacha, Abdulsalami Abubakar, did the thing the country needed. He did not cling to power. He organized elections quickly and honestly, and he handed the country back to civilian rule. In 1999, the soldiers left power, and this time, children, they did not come back. That handover in 1999 began what is called the Fourth Republic, the democratic era that has lasted, through all its troubles, from that day to this."}, {"id": "naija-june-twelve#s12", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "And in one of the most astonishing turns in all this story, the man chosen and elected as the first president of this new democratic era was Olusegun Obasanjo, the very man Abacha had thrown in prison to die. The prisoner walked out of his cell and, within a couple of years, into the presidential palace. It was as if the country, having stolen June the Twelfth, was trying in some clumsy way to make amends, and a Yoruba man, from Abiola’s own people, was placed at the head of the reborn democracy. The two palms, children, for this darkest and most pivotal telling. On one side, the deepest wickedness in the whole modern story, the theft of the freest election ever held, the murder of Saro-Wiwa and Kudirat Abiola, the martyrdom of Abiola himself, and the terror and vast plunder of the Abacha years, a genuine tyranny that made Nigeria a byword for misrule."}, {"id": "naija-june-twelve#s13", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "On the other side, the seed of redemption hidden inside the horror. June the Twelfth, for one day, showed Nigeria and the world that Nigerians could rise above tribe and religion and vote as one nation, and the memory of that day became a permanent rebuke to every force that would divide them. The martyrs gave the struggle for democracy its holy names. And the death of the tyrant opened the door, at last, to a democratic era that has now endured longer than any before it. Nigeria walked through its darkest night and came out, battered but unbroken, into the light of a lasting democracy."}, {"id": "naija-june-twelve#s14", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "The lesson, children, is a hard and a hopeful one at once. The powerful can steal an election, can murder the just, can rule by terror for a season. But they cannot kill the memory of what the people did on their best day, and they cannot rule forever. June the Twelfth was stolen, but it was never forgotten, and in the end the theft of it helped shame and shake the whole rotten structure of military rule until it fell. Years later, a Nigerian government would finally honour the truth, declaring June the Twelfth the country’s true Democracy Day and honouring Abiola at last as the president the people had elected. The mandate stolen in 1993 was, in memory and in honour, finally restored."}, {"id": "naija-june-twelve#s15", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "Tomorrow, in the last of these tellings, I will bring the story home to our own day, to the Fourth Republic and the leaders who have ruled it, the triumphs and the deep continuing troubles, up to the edge of the present time, as far as an honest teller can see."}]}, {"id": "naija-fourth-republic", "emoji": "🏛️", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#001a0a,#003a1a)", "category": "naija", "rating": 9.6, "duration": "12 min", "title": {"en": "The Fourth Republic: Democracy, Its Triumphs, and Its Unfinished Work", "yo": "The Fourth Republic: Democracy, Its Triumphs, and Its Unfinished Work"}, "desc": {"en": "The democratic era from 1999 to today, its presidents, triumphs, and unfinished work.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Nigeria History", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Naijiria, the fifth and last telling, the democratic era from 1999 to our own day, the presidents who have led it, and the great struggles that remain, told to the edge of the present)", "segments": [{"id": "naija-fourth-republic#s1", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Children, we come now to the last of these tellings, and to our own time, the age you yourselves are living in. This is the Fourth Republic, the democratic era that began in 1999 and has endured, through storm and trouble, to the present day. And I must tell you plainly, as an honest teller must, that my clear sight reaches only so far. I can tell you the story with confidence up to the last years I know well, up to about the opening of the year 2025. Beyond that edge, into the very freshest present, I will not pretend to a knowledge I do not have, for a griot who invents the news is no better than a liar. So I give you the story to that edge, and I trust you and your elders to carry it forward from there."}, {"id": "naija-fourth-republic#s2", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Remember where we ended, children. In 1999 the soldiers went back to their barracks and Olusegun Obasanjo, the Yoruba general whom Abacha had jailed, became the elected president of the reborn democracy. He served two terms, eight years, until 2007. And under him some real good was done. He worked to bring Nigeria back into the good graces of the world after the pariah years. His government secured a great relief of the crushing foreign debt that had chained the country, negotiated in large part by a brilliant economist I have told you of before, Ngozi Okonjo-Iweala, his finance minister. He set up new agencies to fight the monstrous corruption. Telephones, once a rare luxury of the powerful, spread suddenly to the ordinary people through the mobile phone, transforming daily life. The economy grew."}, {"id": "naija-fourth-republic#s3", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "But hold the two palms even here, children. Obasanjo’s rule was also high-handed and stained. Elections held under him, especially the one in 2007 that chose his successor, were badly flawed, marred by rigging and violence. And near the end he was widely believed to have tried to change the constitution so that he could rule for a third term, a dangerous echo of the old strongman hunger, and it was only when the National Assembly, the parliament, boldly refused him that the danger passed. That refusal, children, was itself a sign of something precious growing, a democracy learning to check its own leaders."}, {"id": "naija-fourth-republic#s4", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "The man that flawed election of 2007 brought to power was Umaru Musa Yar’Adua, a quiet, gentle, and by most accounts genuinely decent northern governor. He himself admitted openly that the election that brought him to power had been flawed, an honesty almost unheard of, and he promised reforms. But children, he was a sick man, gravely ill, and his health failed. He grew too ill to govern, and was flown abroad for treatment, and for a long and dangerous period the country drifted with no one clearly in charge, a hidden and absent president, and a constitutional crisis brewing over who held the reins. At last the parliament found a clever device to empower his deputy to act, and then, in 2010, Yar’Adua died in office."}, {"id": "naija-fourth-republic#s5", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "His deputy who took over was Goodluck Jonathan, a man from the Niger Delta, from the very oil-producing south whose people had so long felt cheated of the wealth pumped from under their feet. His rise, from a minority people of the Delta all the way to the presidency, was itself a kind of milestone. He went on to win the election of 2011 in his own right, in a vote widely judged the cleanest in a long while."}, {"id": "naija-fourth-republic#s6", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Under Jonathan there was some real progress, and the economy at one point was reckoned the largest in all of Africa. But his government was dogged by accusations of massive corruption, of billions vanishing, and by a grave new danger in the north, the rise of a monstrous extremist insurgency called Boko Haram, which waged a savage war of bombings and massacres and kidnappings against the Nigerian state and people. Its most infamous crime, which horrified the whole world, was the mass abduction of more than two hundred schoolgirls from a town called Chibok in 2014, a wound that has never healed and a cry, Bring Back Our Girls, that echoed around the earth."}, {"id": "naija-fourth-republic#s7", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "And then, children, came a moment of which Nigeria can be truly proud, one of the finest in this whole modern story. In the election of 2015, the sitting president, Goodluck Jonathan, was defeated at the ballot box by an opposition challenger. And rather than cling to power or unleash chaos, as the powerful so often had, Jonathan accepted his defeat, telephoned his rival to congratulate him, and stepped down peacefully. It was the first time in all of Nigeria’s history that a sitting president had lost an election and handed over power peacefully to the opposition. Think of how far the country had come, children, from the stolen votes and murderous coups of before, to this quiet, dignified handover. Jonathan won more honour in that one gracious act of defeat than many win in victory, and Nigerians rightly remember it with pride."}, {"id": "naija-fourth-republic#s8", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "The man who won that election, children, will astonish you, for he is an old friend of this story. It was Muhammadu Buhari. Yes, the very same stern general who had seized power in a coup back in 1983 and been overthrown in 1985. Thirty years later, the old soldier had remade himself as a democratic politician, running again and again for the presidency and losing, until at last, in 2015, the people elected him, drawn by his reputation, from those long-ago days, for personal honesty and hatred of corruption. The former military dictator returned, this time through the ballot box, a strange and telling turn."}, {"id": "naija-fourth-republic#s9", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Buhari served two terms, from 2015 to 2023. And the two palms must be held wide, children, for his rule divides opinion sharply. To his admirers, he was still the honest man, personally free of the greed that stained so many, who fought corruption and pushed back, with much struggle, against Boko Haram. To his critics, his government was slow, aloof, and often absent, he too fell ill and spent long periods abroad for treatment as Yar’Adua had, the economy suffered through recessions and hardship, insecurity spread with new dangers of banditry and kidnapping beyond even Boko Haram, and many felt he favoured his own northern region and did too little as ordinary people grew poorer. In one dark episode in 2020, young Nigerians rose in mass protests against a brutal police unit, the movement called EndSARS, and the security forces’ response, including a shooting of protesters at a Lagos tollgate, became a bitter symbol of a government at odds with its own frustrated youth."}, {"id": "naija-fourth-republic#s10", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "In 2023, another election was held, and it brought to the presidency, children, a man of our own, a Yoruba son, Bola Ahmed Tinubu, long the powerful political godfather of Lagos, credited by supporters with the transformation of Lagos into a better-run megacity, and accused by critics of amassing enormous power and wealth by murky means. His election was fiercely disputed, challenged in the courts, and left the country deeply divided, and he took office facing an economy in deep distress, with hard and painful reforms, the removal of the old fuel subsidy and the floating of the currency, that sent the cost of living soaring and inflicted real suffering on ordinary Nigerians even as economists argued the medicine was necessary. That, children, is roughly where my clear knowledge reaches, at the opening of 2025, with Tinubu in office and the country wrestling, as ever, with hardship and hope together. What has unfolded since then, you will know better than I, and I leave that telling to you."}, {"id": "naija-fourth-republic#s11", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "Now let me draw the two palms together for the whole of the Fourth Republic, children, and for the whole long story of Nigeria. On one side, the deep and unfinished troubles. Corruption still eats at the country like a worm in the yam. The old fractures of tribe and religion and region have never fully healed, and cynical leaders still exploit them. Insecurity, from extremist insurgency to banditry to kidnapping, has brought terrible suffering. The great oil-wealth is still too often looted rather than used to lift the people, and millions remain poor in a land of plenty, a scandal and a heartbreak. Elections are still too often marred by fraud and violence."}, {"id": "naija-fourth-republic#s12", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "But on the other side, children, hold this firmly, for it is real and it is precious. Nigeria has now sustained democratic, civilian rule, with regular elections, for longer than in all its previous history combined. Power has changed hands peacefully, even from a ruling party to the opposition, a thing once unthinkable. The press is loud and free and fierce. The courts have overturned stolen elections. The people protest, argue, vote, and demand better, and their leaders, however reluctantly, must answer to them. The soldiers have stayed in their barracks for over two decades. The habit of democracy, the patient boring precious habit that the age of coups never allowed to grow, is at last putting down roots."}, {"id": "naija-fourth-republic#s13", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "And so, children, we close the whole story of Nigeria, and I want you to carry from it the deepest lesson of all. Nigeria was born flawed, assembled by strangers, fractured by tribe, cursed and blessed by oil, brutalized by soldiers, and it has stumbled and bled and disappointed its people again and again. Anyone who tells you Nigeria is a finished triumph is a flatterer. But anyone who tells you Nigeria is only a failure is a fool, for they have forgotten June the Twelfth, and Jonathan’s gracious handover, and the courts that overturn theft, and the parliament that refused a third term, and the sheer, stubborn, unkillable determination of two hundred million people of every tribe and faith to live together, argue together, and build, however slowly, a country worthy of its own vast promise."}, {"id": "naija-fourth-republic#s14", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "The forging of Nigeria, children, is not finished. It is still on the anvil. And you, the children gathered at this fire, are the smiths who will hammer out what it becomes. The ancestors made you a country, flawed and unfinished. It is left to you to make it, at last, a nation. Go and do it better than we did. That is the whole point of my telling you where we succeeded and where we failed, so that you may keep the one and mend the other. That is the story of Nigeria, to the edge of today. The rest, my children, is in your hands."}]}, {"id": "naija-biafra", "emoji": "🏛️", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#001a0a,#003a1a)", "category": "naija", "rating": 9.8, "duration": "19 min", "title": {"en": "The War That Broke the House: The Story of Biafra", "yo": "The War That Broke the House: The Story of Biafra"}, "desc": {"en": "The Nigerian Civil War of 1967 to 1970, told with both palms open.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Nigeria History", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Naijiria, the Nigerian Civil War, 1967 to 1970, told with both palms open)", "segments": [{"id": "naija-biafra#s1", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Come closer, my children, and sit where the light of the fire can reach your faces, for tonight I will tell you the hardest story of all. It is not a story of one people against another, though many will try to tell it to you that way. It is the story of a house that was still new, still learning the names of its own rooms, and how that house caught fire and burned for thirty months, and how more than a million of its own children died in the burning. I will tell it to you with both my palms open, the way my father taught me, holding the grief of the East in one hand and the fear of the rest in the other, and refusing to close either fist. For a griot who tells you only one side of a war has not told you a war at all. He has told you a chant. And chants are for the drum, not for the truth."}, {"id": "naija-biafra#s2", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "·  ·  · Let me first set the ground, so you understand the house before it burned. When the British lowered their flag in 1960 and Nigeria stood up as its own country, it was not one people who stood up. It was many peoples tied together with the rope of a border that other men had drawn. In the North stood the Hausa and the Fulani, ruled long by emirs, many of them Muslim, cautious of the fast new ways of the South. In the West stood the Yoruba, my own people, with our old towns and kings and our love of argument. And in the East stood the Igbo, a people of a thousand villages who had governed themselves for centuries with no single king, deciding matters by the gathering of the whole town, a people who prized striving, learning, and the courage to leave home and make something of yourself in a far place."}, {"id": "naija-biafra#s3", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Now hear this, for it matters. When Western schooling and the new trades came, the Igbo took to them with a hunger that changed the country. They spread out across every region, to Kano and Kaduna in the far North, to Lagos and Ibadan in the West, and they became traders, clerks, railwaymen, teachers, soldiers. In many Northern cities they built their own quarters and prospered. And where one people prospers as strangers in another people’s land, my children, envy grows quiet and thick, like moss on the underside of a stone. Remember that moss. We will return to it. ·  ·  ·"}, {"id": "naija-biafra#s4", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "The first crack in the wall came over counting and over votes. Nigeria was a country where each region feared the others, and so every census, every election, became a war fought with paper. There was rigging, there was thuggery, there was a great crisis in the Western Region where the house of the Yoruba split against itself and the streets of the West ran with what the people called Operation Wetie, houses and men set alight. The young officers of the army watched all of this, the corruption, the rigging, the burning, and their stomachs turned. They were the first generation of Nigerian officers, proud, idealistic, impatient, and some of them decided that the politicians had failed the country and that soldiers must clean the house. ·  ·  ·"}, {"id": "naija-biafra#s5", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "So we come to January of 1966, the first blow. A group of majors, led by men whose names you should know, chief among them Major Chukwuma Kaduna Nzeogwu, rose in the night and struck. They killed the Prime Minister, Sir Abubakar Tafawa Balewa of the North. They killed the Premier of the North, Sir Ahmadu Bello, the Sardauna of Sokoto, the most powerful man in the country. They killed the Premier of the West, Chief Samuel Akintola. They killed senior soldiers, among them men of the North and the West. Nzeogwu himself spoke of wanting to end tribalism and corruption, and some of the plotters truly believed they were saving Nigeria."}, {"id": "naija-biafra#s6", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "But hear the wound in it, my children, and hear it plainly, for it is the seed of everything that followed. When the smoke cleared, the leaders who lay dead were mostly Northern and Western, and most of the officers who had done the killing were Igbo. Whether that was by design or by the accidents of who commanded which barracks, I cannot swear to you, and honest people still argue it to this day. But in the eyes of a frightened North, it did not look like a coup against corruption. It looked like a coup of one tribe against the others. And when the dust settled, the man who emerged to lead the country was General Johnson Aguiyi-Ironsi, the head of the army, himself an Igbo man. So even though Ironsi had not been part of the plot, and had in fact helped put it down, the North looked at the throne and saw an Igbo sitting on it after Northern leaders had been slain. The moss on the stone stirred."}, {"id": "naija-biafra#s7", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "·  ·  · Ironsi meant well and governed badly, as frightened new rulers often do. His gravest mistake was a decree that abolished the regions and made Nigeria one single unified state governed from the center. To you and me that may sound like unity. But to the North it sounded like a plan to let the better-schooled Southerners, and the Igbo above all, flood into every office in the land and rule over them forever. Rumor did the rest, for rumor is the fastest runner in any frightened country."}, {"id": "naija-biafra#s8", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "So in July of 1966, six months after the first coup, came the counter-coup, the second blow, and this one was drenched in blood and in tribe. Northern officers rose and killed Ironsi. They killed Igbo officers across the barracks in their hundreds. And out of that counter-coup rose a young Lieutenant Colonel from a small Middle Belt people, a Christian, only thirty-one years old, named Yakubu Gowon. Remember his name, for he will carry the federal side through the whole of this story. ·  ·  ·"}, {"id": "naija-biafra#s9", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Now came the darkest thing of all, and I will not soften it for you, because to soften it would be to lie. In the months that followed, mobs and soldiers in the Northern cities turned upon the Igbo who lived among them, the traders and clerks and railwaymen who had built their quarters and prospered. Tens of thousands were slaughtered, men, women, and children. Homes were burned. And a great river of survivors, perhaps a million or more, fled back east toward the homeland of their fathers, wounded, grieving, carrying their dead in their memory and sometimes in their arms. This, my children, is the moss grown into fire. When a people are hunted in the streets of a country that calls itself their own, something breaks in them that no speech can mend. The Igbo came home to the East no longer feeling like Nigerians. They felt like a people who had been told, in blood, that they were not wanted."}, {"id": "naija-biafra#s10", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "·  ·  · In the East stood their leader, a young military governor, handsome, proud, Oxford-schooled, the son of one of the richest men in Africa. His name was Chukwuemeka Odumegwu Ojukwu. He looked at the corpses coming home, and he looked at Gowon in Lagos whom he did not accept as his commander, and he asked the question that every hunted people eventually asks: if this country cannot keep us safe, why should we remain in it?"}, {"id": "naija-biafra#s11", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "There was one last attempt to hold the house together. Gowon and Ojukwu met in the town of Aburi, in Ghana, in January of 1967, and they reached an agreement, a loose arrangement that would have given the regions great power and kept Nigeria only barely joined. Both men left Aburi believing they had won. But when they returned home, the agreement fell apart in the arguing over what exactly had been promised, and each side accused the other of breaking faith. Aburi is the great might-have-been of this story, my children, the fork in the road not taken, and to this day men weep over it. ·  ·  ·"}, {"id": "naija-biafra#s12", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "Then Gowon played his strongest card. He carved the country into twelve states, and in doing so he split the Eastern Region into three, deliberately giving the minority peoples of the East, the Ijaw, the Efik, the Ibibio, and others, their own states away from Igbo rule, and placing the precious oil fields of the Niger Delta outside the Igbo heartland. It was a shrewd stroke, for it told the minorities of the East that their future lay with Nigeria, not with an Igbo nation. And it told Ojukwu that if he seceded, he might not even carry the oil with him."}, {"id": "naija-biafra#s13", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "Ojukwu answered on the thirtieth of May, 1967. He stood and declared the Eastern Region an independent nation, and he gave it the name of the great bight of water on its coast. He called it the Republic of Biafra. And Gowon answered that Nigeria would not be broken, that this was rebellion, and he called it not a war but a police action, expecting it to be over in a few weeks. He was wrong by nearly three years and by more than a million lives. ·  ·  ·"}, {"id": "naija-biafra#s14", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Let me now tell you the war itself, swiftly, for the fire burned hot and long. At first Biafra was daring. In August of 1967 Biafran troops crossed the Niger and drove clean into the Midwest, almost to the outskirts of Lagos itself, and for a moment the whole of Nigeria trembled. But they overreached, the thrust was thrown back, and from then on the war became a slow, grinding closing of the fist. Federal Nigeria, larger, richer, and armed by both Britain and the Soviet Union, two great powers who for once agreed on something, pressed in from every side. Biafra, blockaded by land and by sea, could import almost nothing."}, {"id": "naija-biafra#s15", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Now here is where the war stopped being about soldiers and became a horror against children, and this is the part the whole world remembers. Nigeria threw a blockade around Biafra and let almost no food pass. Whether starvation was a deliberate weapon of war or the cruel by-product of a tightening siege is argued to this day, and a federal official was quoted as saying that starvation was a legitimate weapon, words that have never stopped echoing. Whatever the intent, the result was the same. Inside Biafra the food ran out, and a sickness of hunger called kwashiorkor swept through the children, swelling their bellies and their feet while their limbs wasted to sticks and their hair turned rust-red. The photographs of those starving Biafran children flew around the world and seared the conscience of humanity. It was one of the first times, my children, that television carried a famine into the living rooms of distant nations, and the world has never quite been the same in how it sees African suffering. Relief flights ran by night into a single airstrip at Uli, dodging the guns, and priests and aid workers and the young Frenchmen who would later found the group called Doctors Without Borders were shaped forever by what they saw in Biafra."}, {"id": "naija-biafra#s16", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "·  ·  · Slowly, town by town, the fist closed. Enugu fell. Port Harcourt fell, and with it Biafra’s link to the sea and to its oil. The territory shrank and shrank until it was a small, starving, defiant island in the forest, still fighting, still refusing. Ojukwu’s people worked wonders in that shrinking pocket, building their own refineries from scrap, their own armored cars, even their own rockets, so that men still speak of Biafran ingenuity with wonder. But you cannot eat ingenuity, and you cannot feed a nation on defiance. In January of 1970 the end came. Ojukwu, seeing the cause was lost and perhaps to spare his people a final massacre, handed over power to his deputy, Philip Effiong, and flew into exile in Ivory Coast. And it was Effiong, not Ojukwu, who made the journey to Lagos to surrender. Biafra, after thirty months, laid down its arms. ·  ·  ·"}, {"id": "naija-biafra#s17", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "Now, my children, hear the thing that surprised the world, and hold it, for it is the one mercy in this whole burning. Many had expected that when Biafra fell, the victors would fall upon the Igbo and finish what the mobs of 1966 had begun. It did not happen. Gowon stood up and declared that there was no victor and no vanquished, and he spoke words that became the name of the whole peace, the policy of the three R’s, Reconciliation, Reconstruction, Rehabilitation. There were no war trials, no mass hangings of Biafran leaders, no parade of the conquered through the streets. For a civil war of that size and cruelty, the peace was, by the measure of history, astonishingly merciful, and Gowon deserves to be remembered for that as much as for the war itself. ·  ·  ·"}, {"id": "naija-biafra#s18", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "But do not let me leave you with too clean a peace, for that too would be a lie. The mercy of the ending did not undo the wound. When the war ended, the Igbo who had banked money in Nigerian banks before secession were given, no matter how much they had lost, only twenty pounds each to start their lives again. Twenty pounds, my children, to a people who had been merchants and men of substance. And policies that came after, in trade and in who could own the big businesses, fell heavily on a people already stripped bare. So even in a peace with no gallows, the Igbo rebuilt from almost nothing, with their own famous hard work, and many in the East to this day carry the quiet grief of feeling that they were welcomed back into the house but seated below the salt. This is why, generations later, you will still hear young men in the East speak the name of Biafra again, not always because they wish another war, but because the wound of belonging was never fully cleaned."}, {"id": "naija-biafra#s19", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "·  ·  · So let me now do what a griot must do at the end, and gather the lessons, though I hold them humbly, for the men who lived it are wiser than I. Hear them. The first lesson is the lesson of the moss on the stone. When a people prosper as strangers in another people’s land, and the rest grow to envy and to fear them, a wise country does not wait for the fire. It builds fairness before the fear can harden into hatred. Fairness, my children, is not softness. It is the cheapest wall a country can build against a war. The second lesson is that starving children is never a victory, whoever wins the war. A cause that must be fed on the bodies of its own children, or crushed by the starving of another’s, has already lost something that no surrender ceremony can give back."}, {"id": "naija-biafra#s20", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "The third lesson is Aburi, the fork in the road. Two proud young men, Gowon and Ojukwu, each convinced he was right, each unwilling to bend the last inch, let a agreement slip through their fingers, and a million people paid for that unbending with their lives. When the choice is between your pride and your people, my children, choose your people. Always choose your people. And the fourth lesson is Gowon’s mercy at the end. Wars are lost and won by soldiers, but nations are saved or broken by what the victor does the morning after. That a country so freshly and so terribly wounded did not fall upon the defeated is the small, precious ember I want you to carry out of this darkness. Nigeria did not do the peace perfectly, the twenty pounds and the policies after tell you that, but it did not do the worst, and in the affairs of men that is not nothing."}, {"id": "naija-biafra#s21", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "·  ·  · That is the story of the war that broke the house and did not quite break the country. Ojukwu came home from exile many years later and was received in peace, and lived out his days a Nigerian again, honored by many, and when he died the flags flew for him. Gowon lived long too, and became in his old age a man of prayer who traveled the country asking his own people to forgive one another. Two old men, once enemies, both grown gentle. Perhaps that is the last picture I want to leave in your eyes tonight. Not the starving child, though you must never forget the starving child. But the two old warriors, grown old in the same country they nearly tore in two, and the country still standing, wounded, argumentative, unfinished, but standing."}, {"id": "naija-biafra#s22", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "Remember, my children. A house that has burned once knows the smell of smoke forever. That memory can make it fearful, or it can make it wise. Which of the two Nigeria becomes is not for me to say. It is, as it has always been, in your hands."}]}, {"id": "naija-ijebu-expedition-1892", "emoji": "🏛️", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#001a0a,#003a1a)", "category": "naija", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "8 min", "title": {"en": "The Ijebu Expedition of 1892: The Day the Forest Wall Fell", "yo": "Ijebu"}, "desc": {"en": "The kingdom that closed its gates to Britain, and the 1892 expedition that forced them open.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Nigeria History", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Oba, from “The Ijebu Expedition of 1892”)", "segments": [{"id": "naija-ijebu-expedition-1892#s1", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Of all the Yoruba peoples, none guarded their independence more fiercely, or more cleverly, than the Ijebu. They were a proud and prosperous kingdom lying between the great inland towns and the sea, and their power rested on a single, brilliant fact of geography, that whoever wished to trade between the coast and the interior had to pass through Ijebu land, and the Ijebu made very sure that everyone paid for the privilege. They were the great middlemen of the western country, and they had grown rich and strong upon it, and they meant to keep things exactly as they were. This is the story of how that proud gatekeeping kingdom collided with the British Empire at the height of its hunger, and of the single short, terrible day that changed everything."}, {"id": "naija-ijebu-expedition-1892#s2", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "To understand the Ijebu you must understand what they controlled. The trade roads from the coast, where the European goods and later the guns came in, ran up through Ijebu territory to the great markets of the interior, to Ibadan and beyond, and back down came the palm oil and the produce of the inland country. The Ijebu sat astride these roads like a toll-keeper at a bridge, and they guarded their monopoly jealously. They restricted who could pass, they taxed the goods heavily, and above all they kept the Europeans themselves out, refusing to let the white traders and missionaries travel freely through their land into the interior. They had seen what happened elsewhere when the strangers were let in, how trade concessions became missions and missions became forts and forts became flags, and the Ijebu wanted none of it. Keep the strangers on the coast, control the roads, take the toll. It had worked for generations."}, {"id": "naija-ijebu-expedition-1892#s3", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "But the world was changing against them, and faster than they knew. The British at Lagos, growing ever bolder, wanted those inland roads open, wanted the trade flowing freely down to their port and the interior opened to their commerce and their influence. They pressed the Ijebu, cajoled them, made treaties with them that the Ijebu signed and then, sensibly, largely ignored, for why should a proud kingdom honor a bargain that meant its own undoing. The Awujale, the king of the Ijebu, and his chiefs held to the old policy. They would not throw open their land. And to the British, hungry for the interior and impatient with any African power that stood in the way, this refusal became the pretext they wanted. There were complaints of insults to a British envoy, of blocked roads, of treaties defied, and these were gathered up into a case for war. The truth beneath the diplomatic language was simple and brutal. The Ijebu had something the empire wanted, the keys to the interior, and the empire had decided to take them by force."}, {"id": "naija-ijebu-expedition-1892#s4", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "Now the Ijebu were not fools, and they were not weak. They were numerous, they were brave, they knew their own country, and they had guns, the trade muskets that had armed West African warfare for generations. They gathered a great army, some tellings say tens of thousands of men, and they took up a strong position to block the British advance, choosing their ground where the road crossed difficult country, a river and broken terrain that a defender could hold. By every reckoning of the old warfare, they should have been able to stop or at least badly maul the small British-led force coming against them, for the British column was tiny by comparison, a few hundred British and African troops. The Ijebu had the numbers, the courage, the ground, and the home advantage. What they did not have, and what they did not yet understand, was that the nature of war itself had just changed, and that the change was about to fall on them like the sky coming down."}, {"id": "naija-ijebu-expedition-1892#s5", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "The change was the machine gun. The British force carried with it a weapon the Ijebu had never faced, the Maxim gun, the first true modern machine gun, a weapon that could fire hundreds of rounds a minute, a scything, unceasing storm of bullets from a single mounting. When the armies met at the crossing, near the Yemoja river, the Ijebu did what brave warriors had always done, they came on in their masses, in the courageous frontal style of the old warfare, to overwhelm the small enemy force with numbers and valor. And they were met not by muskets they could trade shot for shot with, but by the Maxim. The gun cut them down in rows. No courage, no numbers, no knowledge of the ground could answer that mechanical slaughter, for the men fell faster than they could close the distance, and the massed bravery that had won a hundred older battles became, against the machine gun, only a way of dying in greater numbers. In a matter of hours it was over. The great Ijebu army, for all its size and spirit, was broken and scattered, and the road to the interior lay open. The forest wall that had stood for generations had fallen in a single dreadful day."}, {"id": "naija-ijebu-expedition-1892#s6", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "The consequences went far beyond Ijebu land, and this is why the day matters to the whole Yoruba story and not to the Ijebu alone. The defeat of the Ijebu, the strongest and proudest of the gatekeeper kingdoms, sent a message that every other Yoruba power heard and understood at once. If the mighty Ijebu, with their tens of thousands, could be shattered in an afternoon, then no one could stand against the new weapons. Resistance by the old means was finished. Within a few short years the other Yoruba states, Ibadan, the rest of them, came to terms with the British with far less fighting, for the lesson of the Yemoja river needed no repeating. The Ijebu Expedition of 1892 was the hinge on which the whole country turned from independence to colonial rule, the moment the modern world, in the form of a machine gun on a tripod, arrived in Yorubaland and announced that the old age of war was over."}, {"id": "naija-ijebu-expedition-1892#s7", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "And yet the Ijebu are not remembered as fools who threw their lives away, and the elders are careful about this, for it touches the deeper truth of the tale. The Ijebu were not defeated because they were cowards or because they were foolish. They were defeated because they met the future one day before they knew it had arrived, and no amount of the old courage could have saved them from a weapon that belonged to another age entirely. There is a terrible dignity in it, the brave men advancing in the honorable old way straight into the mechanical storm, not yet knowing that the rules had changed. So the elders tell the Ijebu Expedition as a warning wrapped in a lament, that the world does not wait for us to be ready, that a people can be strong and wise and brave by every standard they know and still be overtaken in an afternoon by a strength they never imagined, and that the only final defense is to never stop learning what the world has newly become. The Ijebu learned it at the cost of a generation of their bravest, on the banks of the Yemoja, on the day the forest wall fell."}]}, {"id": "naija-herbert-macaulay", "emoji": "🏛️", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#001a0a,#003a1a)", "category": "naija", "rating": 9.4, "duration": "4 min", "title": {"en": "Herbert Macaulay: The Grandfather of Nigerian Nationalism", "yo": "Herbert Macaulay: The Grandfather of Nigerian Nationalism"}, "desc": {"en": "Herbert Macaulay, grandfather of Nigerian nationalism.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Nigeria History", "secondary": [], "source_note": "", "segments": [{"id": "naija-herbert-macaulay#s1", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Narrator opens: Before there was an independent Nigeria, someone had to insist that there should be one. Someone had to look at the colonial order — the British governors, the Crown Agents, the legislative councils full of appointed Europeans and carefully selected Africans — and say: this is not good enough. We deserve self-determination. We deserve to govern ourselves. That someone was Herbert Macaulay. He was born in 1864 in Lagos, into the Saro community — the returned descendants of freed slaves, educated, Christian, connected to both Yoruba tradition and the British colonial system. His grandfather was Bishop Samuel Ajayi Crowther. His father was Thomas Babington Macaulay — a pastor and educator who had been a close colleague of Crowther. Herbert grew up in a family for whom intellectual and civic achievement were simply expected."}, {"id": "naija-herbert-macaulay#s2", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "He was educated in Lagos and then in England, where he trained as a civil engineer. He returned to Nigeria in the 1890s and worked for the colonial government briefly before deciding that his real work was elsewhere. His real work was opposition. Macaulay founded the Lagos Standard newspaper in 1903 — one of the most consistently anti-colonial publications in Nigerian history. Through the paper and through public agitation, he challenged the colonial government on land rights (particularly in the land dispute over the Lagos Lagoon that became one of the defining legal battles of early colonial Nigeria), on taxation, on the appointment of Africans to positions of authority, and on the fundamental question of who Lagos and Nigeria belonged to."}, {"id": "naija-herbert-macaulay#s3", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "He was a masterful agitator — combining legal challenges with public campaigns, newspaper editorials with street-level organizing, the language of British constitutional law with the political energy of ordinary Lagos people. He went to jail for his opposition (convicted of fraud in a case that his supporters argued was politically motivated) and emerged from jail with his reputation enhanced rather than diminished. In 1923, he co-founded the Nigerian National Democratic Party — the first political party in Nigeria organized for the purpose of advocating African interests. It won all three Lagos seats in the first elections to the Legislative Council, and Macaulay's movement had established the template for Nigerian nationalist politics."}, {"id": "naija-herbert-macaulay#s4", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "He continued working — writing, organizing, agitating — into his 70s and 80s. In 1944, at the age of 80, he co-founded the National Council of Nigeria and the Cameroons with the young Nnamdi Azikiwe — becoming the president of a broad-based nationalist movement that would carry Nigeria toward independence. He did not live to see independence. He died in 1946, three years before his dream was on the horizon and fourteen years before it was achieved. But the independence that came in 1960 was built on the foundation he had laid. The tradition of organized political opposition that he established — the newspapers, the parties, the legal challenges, the insistence on dignity and self-determination — was the inheritance that Azikiwe and Awolowo and the generation of independence leaders received from Herbert Macaulay."}, {"id": "naija-herbert-macaulay#s5", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "The grandfather of Nigerian nationalism. A man who spent his entire adult life in the service of a country that would not be fully born until after his death. He trusted that it would be born. He acted as if it would. And it was."}]}, {"id": "naija-mko-abiola", "emoji": "🏛️", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#001a0a,#003a1a)", "category": "naija", "rating": 9.6, "duration": "10 min", "title": {"en": "Moshood Kashimawo Olawale Abiola of Abeokuta: The Martyr of the Mandate", "yo": "Moshood Kashimawo Olawale Abiola"}, "desc": {"en": "The businessman and Aare Ona Kakanfo who won Nigeria's freest election and died for the mandate.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Nigeria History", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Aare Ona Kakanfo, the story of the great businessman and philanthropist of Abeokuta who won the freest election in his nation’s history and died for a mandate he was never allowed to claim)", "segments": [{"id": "naija-mko-abiola#s1", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Gather close tonight, my children, and let the fire burn low and solemn, for this is one of the most heartbreaking stories in all our telling, and it happened not in the mist of ancient days but within the memory of your own parents and grandparents. Tonight we sit with Chief Moshood Kashimawo Olawale Abiola of Abeokuta, whom the world knew as M.K.O., a man who wore the ancient title of Aare Ona Kakanfo, and who became, in the end, a martyr, a man who gave his very life for the right of his people to have their votes counted. His story is proof, my children, that the old title still meant what it had always meant: a champion who would fight for his people, even unto death."}, {"id": "naija-mko-abiola#s2", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Let me first tell you of the man before the tragedy, for M.K.O. Abiola was a giant long before the darkest chapter of his life. He was born in Abeokuta, that city of the Egba under the great rock, born into poverty so deep that our people still tell of the hardship of his childhood. And from that poverty he rose, by his own brilliance and tireless energy, to become one of the wealthiest and most successful businessmen in all of Africa. That rise alone would have made him a legend. But Abiola did not clutch his wealth to himself."}, {"id": "naija-mko-abiola#s3", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Remember the lesson, my children, that we never flatten a man to a single word, and Abiola was a man of many parts, almost too many to hold in one telling. He was a titan of business. He was one of the greatest philanthropists our land has ever known, a man whose generosity was legendary, who gave and gave, endowing schools, funding scholarships, building mosques and churches both, supporting sport and scholarship across the whole continent, so open-handed that he was called by many the great giver of our age. He was a champion of causes far beyond his own country, a leading voice in the call for reparations to Africa for the historical wrong of the slave-trade, that same trade whose long shadow we have followed through so many of these stories. He was a man of the whole continent, and yet also, deeply, a son of Abeokuta and of the Yoruba people. It was upon this remarkable man that the Alaafin bestowed the ancient title of Aare Ona Kakanfo."}, {"id": "naija-mko-abiola#s4", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Now I must tell you of the great and terrible turning of his life, the chapter that made him a martyr and broke a nation’s heart. In the early nineteen nineties, Nigeria had suffered long under military rule, and the people yearned for democracy, for the right to choose their own leaders. At last an election was arranged, and in June of nineteen ninety-three, the people of Nigeria went to the polls to choose a president. And they chose Abiola."}, {"id": "naija-mko-abiola#s5", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Hear me well, my children, for this is the heart of it. That election, the June twelfth election as our people call it and honor it to this day, is remembered as the freest and fairest election in the whole history of Nigeria. And what made it a wonder was this: Abiola, a Yoruba Muslim from the south-west, won votes across every region, every people, every faith. He won in the north and the south, among Muslims and Christians, among his own people and among peoples far from his own. For one shining moment, a divided nation had spoken with one voice and chosen one man. It was a victory not only for Abiola but for the very idea that Nigeria could be one people. It was, perhaps, the most hopeful moment in the nation’s life."}, {"id": "naija-mko-abiola#s6", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "And then, my children, came the crime that has never been forgotten and never been forgiven. The military ruler of that day, unwilling to hand power to the people’s choice, annulled the election. Cancelled it. Threw away the freest vote the nation had ever cast, as if it had never happened. The mandate that Abiola had won, clean and clear and across all the divisions of the land, was simply seized from him and torn up by the powerful."}, {"id": "naija-mko-abiola#s7", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "Imagine the anguish of it, my children. To be chosen by your whole people, fairly, in the light of day, and then to have that choice stolen by men with guns who feared the will of the people. It was a wound to the whole nation, and above all to the Yoruba people, who saw their son denied the highest office he had rightfully won. The land erupted in protest and grief and rage. And through it all, Abiola faced a terrible choice: to let the theft stand, or to insist, whatever the cost, that he was the rightful mandate of the people."}, {"id": "naija-mko-abiola#s8", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Abiola chose to stand. Here, my children, is where the ancient title of Aare Ona Kakanfo showed that it still meant what it had meant in the days of Kokoro Igangan and Latoosa: a champion who would fight for his people to the very end. Abiola declared himself the rightful President, the bearer of the people’s mandate. And for that declaration, for refusing to let the theft of his people’s votes go unchallenged, he was arrested by the military regime and thrown into prison."}, {"id": "naija-mko-abiola#s9", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "He was held for years, in harsh confinement, cut off, pressured, urged again and again to renounce his mandate and buy his freedom with surrender. And he would not. He would not abandon the mandate his people had given him. Like Kurunmi of Ijaye long before, who would not bend even when bending would have saved him, Abiola would not bend. He held to the people’s mandate as to a sacred trust, though it cost him his freedom, his health, and at last his very life."}, {"id": "naija-mko-abiola#s10", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "And the cost was indeed his life. During his long imprisonment, tragedy struck again and again. His wife Kudirat, who fought bravely and loudly for his freedom and for the restoration of the stolen mandate, was assassinated, gunned down for her courage, a martyr herself in the same great cause. And then, in nineteen ninety-eight, as the nation stood on the very edge of his possible release, M.K.O. Abiola died, suddenly, in detention, under circumstances that grieved and troubled the whole nation. He died a prisoner, still holding the mandate he had won, never allowed to sit in the office his people had chosen him for. He died a martyr."}, {"id": "naija-mko-abiola#s11", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "Now let us weigh him, my children, and there is less to complicate here than with many of our figures, for Abiola’s final chapter has the clean, terrible shape of true martyrdom. He was a man of enormous gifts and enormous generosity who rose from poverty to greatness. He won the love and the votes of his whole divided nation. And when that victory was stolen, he refused to surrender the people’s mandate, and he paid for that refusal with everything he had. His story is a wound that helped, in the end, to shame the military from power and to bring democracy back to Nigeria, for the memory of the stolen June twelfth election became a rallying cry, and years later that date was at last officially honored as the nation’s Democracy Day, and Abiola was at last officially recognized as the winner he had always been, and honored as a hero of the nation’s democracy."}, {"id": "naija-mko-abiola#s12", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "Do you see, my children? Even a martyr’s defeat can become, in time, a kind of victory. Abiola did not live to claim his office. But his sacrifice became a seed, and from that seed grew the restoration of the very democracy he had died for. He lost his life and won his people’s freedom. That is the deepest meaning of the ancient title he bore: the Aare Ona Kakanfo is the one who spends himself for his people. Abiola spent everything."}, {"id": "naija-mko-abiola#s13", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So we honor Chief Moshood Kashimawo Olawale Abiola of Abeokuta, the martyr of the mandate, the great giver, the man who won a nation’s heart and died holding the trust his people placed in him. In the long line of the Aare Ona Kakanfo, he stands among the very greatest in the meaning of his sacrifice, a modern war-marshal who fought his war not with the spear but with his own endurance and his own life, and who proved that the old title still named a man willing to die for his people. Let his name and the sacred date of June the twelfth never be forgotten among you. Tomorrow, my children, we come at last to the end of our long roll, to the man who bears the ancient title in our own day, Chief Gani Adams of Arigidi-Akoko, the activist and defender of Yoruba custom upon whose shoulders the sword of Kokoro Igangan now rests."}]}, {"id": "naija-ladoke-akintola", "emoji": "🏛️", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#001a0a,#003a1a)", "category": "naija", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "8 min", "title": {"en": "Samuel Ladoke Akintola of Ogbomoso: The Old Title in a New Age", "yo": "Samuel Ladoke Akintola"}, "desc": {"en": "The premier of Ogbomoso on whom the ancient war-title was reborn in modern politics.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Nigeria History", "secondary": [], "source_note": "(Itan Aare Ona Kakanfo, the story of the premier and politician of Ogbomoso upon whom the ancient war-title was reborn in modern times)", "segments": [{"id": "naija-ladoke-akintola#s1", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Come and sit, my children, for tonight our long story leaps across a great chasm of years. We have left behind the age of the massed armies and the cavalry and the great guns of Kiriji. The British came, as you heard, and for the better part of a century the Yoruba country lay under colonial rule, and the ancient office of the Aare Ona Kakanfo slept, for there were no more Yoruba armies for a war-marshal to lead. But an old and honored title does not die so easily among a people who remember. And when a new age came, the age of a Nigeria moving toward its own independence, the ancient title woke again, and it was laid upon the shoulders of a modern man, a man of Ogbomoso, Chief Samuel Ladoke Akintola."}, {"id": "naija-ladoke-akintola#s2", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Now here, my children, the very meaning of the title changes, and you must understand the change or you will not understand the man. In the old days, the Aare Ona Kakanfo was a war-marshal, a leader of armies, a man of the spear and the horse. But in the modern age there were no more such armies. So the title, when it was revived, became something new: a supreme honor, a mark of the highest distinction, bestowed by the Alaafin of Oyo upon a Yoruba man judged to be a great champion and defender of his people. The war had moved from the battlefield to the council chamber, from the spear to the word, from the cavalry charge to the political struggle. And so the new Aare Ona Kakanfo was not a general but a leader of his people in the new kind of contest that the modern age had brought: the contest of politics."}, {"id": "naija-ladoke-akintola#s3", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Samuel Ladoke Akintola was such a leader. He was a son of Ogbomoso, that same town that had given the old empire its war-marshals, and he rose in the age of Nigeria’s journey toward independence to become one of the foremost political figures of the western region and of the whole country. He was a brilliant man, a gifted orator, a lawyer, a politician of great skill and force. He became the Premier of the Western Region, one of the most powerful offices in the Nigeria of that day. And it was upon this modern champion of his people that the Alaafin bestowed the ancient title of Aare Ona Kakanfo, making him the war-marshal of a new and different kind of struggle."}, {"id": "naija-ladoke-akintola#s4", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Remember the lesson, my children, that we never flatten a great man into a single word, for Akintola’s story is one that our people still debate around their own fires, and I will give it to you honestly, with its greatness and its tragedy both. He was without question a man of enormous ability, one of the finest minds of his political generation, a champion of the western Yoruba in the fierce contests of the early Nigerian nation. But his career came in a time of bitter and dangerous political division, and he found himself at the very center of a terrible quarrel that would help plunge the whole young nation into crisis."}, {"id": "naija-ladoke-akintola#s5", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Let me tell you of that crisis, for it is the hinge of his tragic story. In the years after Nigeria gained its independence in nineteen sixty, the politics of the western region fell into a deep and bitter split. Akintola, the Premier, fell into a great and famous quarrel with Chief Obafemi Awolowo, the towering leader of the western political movement and the man who had been Akintola’s own leader and ally. This was not a small disagreement. It was a rupture that tore the western region in two, a feud between two giants that dragged the whole region into turmoil, into disorder, and at times into open violence in the streets and the assembly."}, {"id": "naija-ladoke-akintola#s6", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "The struggle grew so bitter, and the disorder so great, that the western region became known in that age as the wild west, a place of political chaos and burning and killing. Akintola held onto power through this storm, allied now with forces at the federal center, while his rival Awolowo was imprisoned. The bitterness deepened. The region became ungovernable. And this chaos in the west was one of the great cracks through which disaster entered the whole young nation."}, {"id": "naija-ladoke-akintola#s7", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "And now, my children, comes the tragic end, and I tell it to you plainly, for it is a matter of sober history. In January of nineteen sixty-six, a group of young army officers carried out Nigeria’s first military coup, a violent overthrow that struck down a number of the nation’s political leaders in a single bloody night. Among those killed in that coup was Samuel Ladoke Akintola, the Premier of the West, the Aare Ona Kakanfo. He died violently, cut down in the collapse of the first republic, one of the most prominent victims of that terrible night that ended Nigeria’s first experiment in democratic self-rule and began the long age of military coups and counter-coups and, before long, a dreadful civil war."}, {"id": "naija-ladoke-akintola#s8", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "So how shall we weigh him, my children? As always, with honesty and with the refusal to flatten. Akintola was a brilliant and powerful man, a great champion of his people in the political arena, worthy in his gifts of the ancient title he bore. And he was a tragic figure, drawn into a ruinous quarrel that helped tear his region apart and contributed to the fall of the first republic, and he paid for that age of division with his own life. His story, like Afonja’s long before him, carries the deep warning about the cost of division among a people. Where Afonja’s quarrel with his king helped bring down the old empire, Akintola’s quarrel with his rival helped bring down the young republic. The particulars are utterly different, the ages centuries apart, but the shape of the tragedy rhymes: brilliant men, bitter division, and a whole people paying the price."}, {"id": "naija-ladoke-akintola#s9", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "And there is a further, gentler truth to hold alongside the tragedy. Whatever the bitterness of his final years, Samuel Ladoke Akintola was a man of genuine gifts and genuine achievement, a builder as well as a battler, remembered by many for his contributions to the development of the western region in the hopeful early years before the great split. Our people do not remember him only for his tragic end. They remember also the height from which he fell, and heights are only tragic when they are real. He was a real height."}, {"id": "naija-ladoke-akintola#s10", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "So we honor Samuel Ladoke Akintola of Ogbomoso, the man upon whom the ancient sword-title was reborn as a modern honor, the war-marshal of the political age, brilliant and tragic, champion and cautionary tale both. In the long line of the Aare Ona Kakanfo, he marks the great transformation of the office from a leader of armies into a supreme honor for the champions of the Yoruba people in the modern world. And his violent death reminds us that the modern arena of politics, though it uses words and votes rather than spears and horses, can be every bit as deadly as the old battlefields of Kiriji."}, {"id": "naija-ladoke-akintola#s11", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "Tomorrow, my children, we sit with another giant upon whom this same modern title was laid, a man whose story is perhaps the most heartbreaking of all: Chief Moshood Kashimawo Olawale Abiola of Abeokuta, the great businessman and philanthropist who would win the freest election in his nation’s history and be denied its fruit, and who would die a martyr for a mandate he was never allowed to claim."}]}, {"id": "undecided-founding-of-lagos", "emoji": "👑", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#1a0a00,#4a2800)", "category": "itan-oba", "rating": 9.6, "duration": "10 min", "title": {"en": "The Founding of Lagos: A City Rises from the Water", "yo": "Eko"}, "desc": {"en": "How fishing villages on the lagoon became Lagos, under its early Obas, and passed to the British.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Itan Oba", "secondary": ["Itan Oba", "Nigeria History"], "source_note": "(Itan Oba, from “Oba Olua and the Founding of Lagos”)", "segments": [{"id": "undecided-founding-of-lagos#s1", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Today it is a giant, one of the largest cities on the face of the earth, a roaring sea of millions upon millions of people, a place of endless traffic and towering ambition and ceaseless noise, the beating commercial heart of a whole nation and much of a continent. But every giant was once small, and Lagos, before it was Lagos, was a scatter of fishing villages on a cluster of islands and lagoons, a quiet watery place where a few families lived by the water and cast their nets and knew nothing of the colossus their home would one day become. The story of how that quiet place became the great city, and of the kings who ruled it as it grew, is the story of Lagos, and it is a story of water and trade and the meeting of worlds, of a small settlement that sat at exactly the right place at exactly the wrong time and was changed forever by the ships that came across the sea."}, {"id": "undecided-founding-of-lagos#s2", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "The very land of Lagos shaped its destiny, for Lagos is a place of islands and water, a great lagoon opening through a channel to the Atlantic, protected islands set among the waterways, mangrove and creek and shifting sandbar. This was the appeal of it from the very beginning. It was a place you could reach by water and defend by water, a place of fishing and of trade along the lagoon, a natural meeting point where the goods of the interior could come down the waterways and the goods of the coast could come up. The early people of the area, connected to the wider Yoruba world and to their neighbors, settled these islands and lived from the water, and the place had names before it had the name the Portuguese would later give it, Lagos, meaning lagoons, the name of the water becoming the name of the town."}, {"id": "undecided-founding-of-lagos#s3", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "The kingship of Lagos, the line of the Oba of Lagos, grew up as the settlement grew, and its origins are tied, like so much in the region, to the great powers around it, especially to the kingdom of Benin, which extended its influence west along the coast and into the affairs of Lagos in the formative period, so that the traditions of the Lagos kingship carry the imprint of Benin as well as of the Yoruba world. The early Obas presided over a growing town, a place that was becoming something more than a fishing village, a trading center of increasing importance sitting at the mouth of a great lagoon system, positioned to become a gateway. And a gateway is exactly what it became, which was its glory and, for a long dark period, its curse."}, {"id": "undecided-founding-of-lagos#s4", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "For when the European ships came, first the Portuguese and then others, seeking trade along the West African coast, Lagos with its fine protected harbor and its position at the mouth of the lagoon became one of the points where the coast and the ocean met. And the trade that the ships increasingly wanted, in the terrible centuries of the Atlantic slave trade, was a trade in human beings. Lagos became a slave port, one of the significant ones on that stretch of coast, a place where captives taken in the wars of the interior were brought down to the water and held and sold and loaded onto the ships for the crossing that so many did not survive. The wealth that flowed through Lagos in this period was blood wealth, built on the suffering that passed through it, and the kingship and the merchants of Lagos were bound up in that trade, as the coastal powers of the whole region were bound up in it, profiting from the catastrophe that was emptying the interior."}, {"id": "undecided-founding-of-lagos#s5", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "It was this, in the end, that drew the British in and changed everything. By the middle of the nineteenth century Britain had turned against the slave trade and was using its navy to suppress it along the African coast, and Lagos, as an active slave port, became a target. The British involved themselves in a succession dispute over the throne of Lagos, backing one claimant against another, and in 1851 they bombarded the town in what is remembered as the reduction of Lagos, installing a ruler friendly to their aims and to the suppression of the slave trade. And then, ten years later, in 1861, they took the final step. Under pressure and the shadow of their guns, the Oba of Lagos was made to cede the territory, and Lagos became a British colony, the first foothold of formal British rule in what would become Nigeria, the seed from which the whole colonial structure would grow."}, {"id": "undecided-founding-of-lagos#s6", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "So Lagos passed under the flag of the empire, and its long transformation into the modern colossus began in earnest. Under colonial rule it became the administrative and commercial center, the port through which the trade of a vast region flowed, now in the goods of legitimate commerce, palm oil and produce and manufactured imports, rather than in human beings. It drew people from everywhere, Yoruba from all the towns of the interior, the returning freed captives from Sierra Leone and Brazil who brought new skills and new faith and new ambition, traders and clerks and laborers and dreamers from across the region and beyond. The islands filled and spilled onto the mainland. The population grew and grew. And when Nigeria became independent in 1960, Lagos was its capital, the face it showed the world, already straining at the seams with the energy that has never since stopped pouring into it."}, {"id": "undecided-founding-of-lagos#s7", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "And still it grew. Even after the capital of Nigeria was moved inland to Abuja, Lagos never stopped being the real center of gravity, the place where the money is made and the deals are done and the culture is created, the city that never sleeps and never stops arriving. It is now among the most populous cities on the planet, a place of staggering contrasts, of gleaming towers rising above teeming markets, of immense wealth and immense hardship pressed hard against each other, of unbearable traffic and unstoppable hustle, of music and film and fashion and art pouring out to conquer the continent and the world. The fishing villages on the islands have become a megacity that would be utterly unrecognizable to the people who first cast their nets in the lagoon. And yet, remarkably, the ancient kingship endures at the heart of it. There is still an Oba of Lagos, still a royal line, still a traditional throne carrying the memory of the old town beneath the roar of the new city, a thread of continuity binding the colossus back to the small watery place it once was."}, {"id": "undecided-founding-of-lagos#s8", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "This is the wonder the elders point to when they tell the story of the founding of Lagos, the sheer scale of the becoming, the almost unbelievable distance between the beginning and the now. But they tell the darker part too, and they do not let it be forgotten, because a city that does not remember how it grew cannot understand itself. Lagos grew great in part on the slave trade, on the suffering that passed through its waterside, and its fall under colonial rule came directly out of that trade, the British bombardment and annexation flowing from the effort to end the very commerce Lagos had profited from. The story holds both truths at once, the glory and the shame, the gateway that brought wealth and the gateway that carried away the stolen and the doomed, and the honest teller does not hide either."}, {"id": "undecided-founding-of-lagos#s9", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "What Lagos finally means, in the mouths of those who keep its story, is the astonishing power of place and time and human will to make something vast out of something small. The right position on the water, the collision of Africa and the wider world, the ceaseless arrival of people who came with nothing and were determined to become something, all of it compounded over generations into the giant that stands today. It is a story of transformation on the grandest scale, and it holds a truth about the Yoruba themselves and about the human spirit, that a people can take a scatter of fishing huts and, through trade and struggle and ambition and sheer relentless energy, build one of the great cities of the earth. Lagos rose from the water. It carries its whole hard history in its bones, the fishing villages and the slave ships and the colonial guns and the independence and the endless millions still arriving. And it is not finished. Whatever Lagos is today, it will be something larger and stranger tomorrow, still rising, still becoming, still the great restless city at the mouth of the lagoon."}]}, {"id": "undecided-abeokuta-refugees", "emoji": "⚔️", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#2a0a00,#5a1800)", "category": "ologun", "rating": 9.6, "duration": "11 min", "title": {"en": "Abeokuta and the Refugees: A City Built by the Displaced", "yo": "Abeokuta"}, "desc": {"en": "How Egba refugees of the wars found Olumo Rock and built Abeokuta, a city the slavers could not swallow.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Ologun / Warlords", "secondary": ["Ologun / Warlords", "Itan Eru"], "source_note": "(Itan Ilu, consolidates “Abeokuta and the Refugees” and “The Rock That Sheltered a People”)", "segments": [{"id": "undecided-abeokuta-refugees#s1", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Come closer, for tonight I will tell you how a people who had lost everything found a rock, and how under that rock they built a city that the slavers could not swallow. It is the story of Abeokuta, which means underneath the rock, and it is one of the few stories in this long season of sorrow where the ending is not grief but a kind of stubborn hope."}, {"id": "undecided-abeokuta-refugees#s2", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "You must understand first what had happened to the land. When the old Oyo empire broke apart, the towns of the savannah fell one after another. Owu fell. The great markets emptied. Whole peoples took to the roads with what they could carry on their heads, their children tied to their backs, walking south through the forest toward the sea, not knowing that the sea itself was where the danger waited. The Egba people, who had lived in scattered townships in the forest, found themselves hunted. Slave raiders moved through their farms like fire through dry grass. A woman would go to her cassava plot in the morning and never come home. A boy sent to fetch water would vanish, and weeks later a trader on the coast would sell him to a captain who did not know his name and did not ask it."}, {"id": "undecided-abeokuta-refugees#s3", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "In those days a man named Sodeke rose among the Egba. He was not a king in the old sense, for the Egba had never loved kings the way Oyo did. They governed themselves through the Ogboni, the elders of the earth, and through the leaders of their many townships, each proud, each jealous of the others. Sodeke was a war leader, a balogun, and he saw what the elders in their scattered villages could not yet see. He saw that a people divided into a hundred little towns would be eaten one town at a time. He saw that survival meant gathering, and gathering meant swallowing pride."}, {"id": "undecided-abeokuta-refugees#s4", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "So Sodeke walked from township to township. He sat with elders who had feuded for generations. He reminded them of the raiders. He reminded them of the empty markets and the vanished children. And slowly, town by town, he gathered them, and he led them south and west to a place he had marked, a great outcropping of grey granite rising from the forest, riddled with caves and overhangs, a natural fortress that a small band could defend against a large one. Underneath the rock. Abeokuta."}, {"id": "undecided-abeokuta-refugees#s5", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "They came in their thousands, then their tens of thousands. Each township that arrived did not dissolve into the mass. This was the genius of the thing, and the difficulty of it. Each brought its own name, its own drums, its own gods, its own quarter of the new city. Ake settled in one place, Owu refugees in another, Oke Ona in another, Gbagura in another. Abeokuta was not one town pretending unity. It was many towns agreeing, warily, to stand back to back against a common enemy, each keeping its own face while sharing a single wall."}, {"id": "undecided-abeokuta-refugees#s6", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "The rock gave them more than stone. It gave them a story about themselves. A people who had been prey now had a home that raiders feared to approach. The caves that had once sheltered hunters now sheltered families. From the heights, watchmen could see an approaching army while it was still a day away. And the Egba, who had been scattered leaves, began to feel themselves a forest again."}, {"id": "undecided-abeokuta-refugees#s7", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "But do not think the danger passed when the walls went up. The city of Abeokuta was born into war and lived its early life in war. To the west lay Dahomey, whose kings had built a state on the business of capture and sale, whose armies included the fearsome women soldiers the Europeans called Amazons, whose economy breathed in and out on the trade in human beings. Dahomey looked at Abeokuta and saw not a city but a warehouse, tens of thousands of people gathered conveniently in one place, and the king of Dahomey resolved to break the rock and carry off its people."}, {"id": "undecided-abeokuta-refugees#s8", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "Twice the armies of Dahomey came against Abeokuta. In 1851 they came in their thousands, the women soldiers in the front ranks, the muskets and the long knives, the war songs rolling across the fields toward the granite. And the Egba, fighting from behind their walls and their rocks, fighting for their children who were inside, threw them back. The attackers who had come to make captives became captives themselves or corpses on the field. It was one of the few times in that whole terrible century that an African city organized itself well enough to defeat a slaving power in open war and send it home broken."}, {"id": "undecided-abeokuta-refugees#s9", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "Now here is a turning that few expect in a slavery story. Abeokuta became not only a refuge but a homecoming. For across the water, in Sierra Leone, there lived thousands of people who had been captured in these very wars, marched to the coast, packed into ships, and then intercepted at sea by the British naval squadron that patrolled against the trade after Britain outlawed it. These recaptured people were landed at Freetown, a colony built for the liberated, and there they had learned to read, taken new trades, joined churches, and raised children. And among them were many Egba, many who remembered a homeland underneath a rock that they had been told now stood strong again."}, {"id": "undecided-abeokuta-refugees#s10", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "So they began to return. They came back across the water, these people who had been sold out of the country as captives and now came home as free men and women, carrying carpenter’s tools and printing type and Bibles and strange new ideas. They were called the Saro, from Sierra Leone, and they will have their own full telling in another night’s story, for their journey deserves it. But you cannot tell Abeokuta without them, because it was to Abeokuta above all that they returned, and they changed the city forever."}, {"id": "undecided-abeokuta-refugees#s11", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "One of them was a boy who had been captured as a child, sold, freed from a slave ship, educated in Freetown, and who came home to find his own mother still living, a reunion so unlikely it reads like a tale invented to make listeners weep. That boy became Samuel Ajayi Crowther, and he too will have his fuller telling. For now, know only that he stepped ashore at Abeokuta a free and lettered man, in the very country from which he had been stolen, and that his return was the return of thousands compressed into one astonishing life."}, {"id": "undecided-abeokuta-refugees#s12", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "The returnees brought change that cut in every direction. They brought literacy, and with it the first printing press in the interior, so that the Egba language and the Yoruba tongue could be set down in letters and read back. They brought new crops and new trades and connections to the merchants of the coast. They brought a mission church, and with it fierce arguments, for not everyone wished to abandon the orisa of their fathers for the god of the people who had, after all, been buying their children not so long before."}, {"id": "undecided-abeokuta-refugees#s13", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "And they brought a political idea that would matter enormously: the idea that Abeokuta might deal with the outside world as a state among states, writing letters to the Queen of England, negotiating, playing one European power against another, defending itself with diplomacy as well as with muskets. For a time in the middle of that century, Abeokuta was something rare, an African city that many outsiders spoke of as a model, a self governing Christian and traditional city that had stopped the slavers at its gates and was charting its own course into the new age."}, {"id": "undecided-abeokuta-refugees#s14", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "Do not let me paint it too bright. There were bitter quarrels between the returnees and the elders. There were quarrels between the mission and the Ogboni. There were seasons when the missionaries were driven out, when the presses were smashed, when the old suspicion flared that these lettered strangers were the forward scouts of a new kind of conquest, and in that suspicion there was wisdom, for the century that followed would prove that European friendship and European empire were often the same hand wearing two gloves. Abeokuta’s independence would not last forever. But that it lasted at all, that it was won at all, in the teeth of Dahomey and the slavers and the collapse of the old order, remains one of the proudest chapters a Yoruba grandmother can tell."}, {"id": "undecided-abeokuta-refugees#s15", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So when you hear the name Abeokuta, do not hear only a place on a map. Hear a decision. Hear Sodeke walking between feuding towns, saying we must gather or we will die apart. Hear the granite under the feet of frightened children who slept safe for the first time in years. Hear the war songs of Dahomey breaking against the walls and rolling back defeated. Hear the ships bringing home the stolen, the mother who thought her son was dead lifting her face to see him grown and lettered and free at the foot of the rock."}, {"id": "undecided-abeokuta-refugees#s16", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "In a century when the story of our people was mostly a story of being scattered, of being carried off, of markets built on the bodies of the taken, Abeokuta is the story of a gathering. It is proof that we were never only victims, that even in the worst of the storm our ancestors could plan, could build, could stand behind a wall and refuse to be carried away. Remember the rock. Underneath it, a people who had lost everything decided to become a nation again, and they did."}]}, {"id": "undecided-ogbomoso-town", "emoji": "⚔️", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#2a0a00,#5a1800)", "category": "ologun", "rating": 9.6, "duration": "12 min", "title": {"en": "Ogbomoso: The Town That Grew from a Hunter’s Camp", "yo": "Ogbomoso"}, "desc": {"en": "How a hunter's camp on the dangerous border became Ogbomoso, a wall the northern horse could not break.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Ologun / Warlords", "secondary": ["Ologun / Warlords"], "source_note": "(Itan Oba, from “Ogbomoso: The Town That Grew From a Hunter’s Camp”)", "segments": [{"id": "undecided-ogbomoso-town#s1", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Long before Ogbomoso was a city, before its markets spread for miles and its sons scattered to the far corners of the earth, it was only a stretch of high, hard ground where the forest thinned and the savannah began, and no one lived there at all. The land lay between the great powers of the age, south of the grasslands the horsemen loved, north of the deep forest where the Egba and the Ijebu built their towns, and because it sat on that border it was a dangerous place, a place armies passed through on their way to somewhere else, a place where a small settlement could be swallowed in an afternoon and no one would remember it had ever stood. That is the first thing to understand about Ogbomoso, that it was born in a hard country, on a road that everyone used and no one owned, and that everything the town became was built against those odds."}, {"id": "undecided-ogbomoso-town#s2", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "The elders tell it this way. In the days when Oyo was strong and its cavalry rode wherever it pleased, there lived a hunter, and his name has come down to us as Soun Ogunlola, though some tell it a little differently and every quarter of the town will argue for its own version. He was not a king’s son. He was not sent by an oracle with a crown in his baggage. He was a hunter, which in the old telling is a particular kind of man, one who lives at the edge of the settled world, who knows the forest paths and the animal trails, who is brave to the point of recklessness because his whole trade is walking alone into places other men fear. And this hunter, ranging far from his home in search of game, came upon that high ground between the grassland and the forest and saw that it was good."}, {"id": "undecided-ogbomoso-town#s3", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "There was water near, and the soil would take yam and maize, and the elevation let a man see trouble coming from a long way off, which for a hunter is worth more than gold. So he built a camp there, a small thing at first, a shelter and a fire and a place to dry his meat, and he hunted the country around it and prospered. And here the story turns, as the best founding stories do, on a single dramatic deed that gave the place its name.In those days the country was troubled by a man, or in some tellings a fearsome warrior, whose name was Elemoso, an emissary of the Alaafin, or in other versions simply a terror who preyed on travelers, and this Elemoso was so dreaded that no one dared face him. The hunter did. He met Elemoso and killed him, and then, so the story goes, he cut off the man’s head and carried it back, and when the people asked what had become of the terror, the answer was given in the old words, that here was the one who cut the mouth of Elemoso, ogun ti o gbe enu Elemoso, and from that deed, from that fierce shortening of a phrase, the town took its name, Ogbomoso, the place of the man who silenced the mouth of the dreaded one. Whether every detail is history or the shaping of memory, the meaning is plain. The town was founded on courage, on a single man’s refusal to be afraid of what everyone else feared, and it kept that reputation ever after."}, {"id": "undecided-ogbomoso-town#s4", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Word spread, as word does, that there was a brave man living well on the high ground, and others came to join him. A hunter’s camp became a hamlet, a hamlet became a village, and the village drew families fleeing the very dangers that made the borderland so perilous, refugees from wars, traders wanting a safe stop on the long road, farmers looking for land that no great king had already claimed. And because the founder was a hunter and not a prince, Ogbomoso grew up with a particular character that it never lost. It was a town of self-made people, of newcomers who had chosen it, a town proud of its own hands and suspicious of grand airs, and it built its own institutions rather than inheriting them ready-made from an ancient dynasty. The ruler of Ogbomoso took the title Soun, and the line of the Soun has continued from that first hunter’s day to this, one of the more remarkable unbroken threads in all the country, a chieftaincy that began not with a crown lowered from heaven but with a head carried home from the forest.· · ·"}, {"id": "undecided-ogbomoso-town#s5", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "Then came the great unmaking of the nineteenth century, the fall of Old Oyo, the collapse of the empire whose story is told elsewhere in these pages, and it is in that terrible age that Ogbomoso earned its greatest weight. For when Old Oyo fell and the horsemen of Ilorin, now Muslim and allied to the Sokoto power in the north, came sweeping south to press their advantage, they meant to carry their conquest deep into Yoruba country, and it was towns like Ogbomoso that stood in the road. This is the iconic moment the elders will not let anyone forget, that Ogbomoso, sitting on that hard borderland where the cavalry loved to fight, became one of the walls against which the northern tide broke. The town did not fall. Refugees from the ruined north poured into it, swelling it enormously, and rather than collapse under the weight or surrender to the raiders, Ogbomoso held. Its warriors fought, its walls were manned, and season after season, when the Ilorin horse came down to burn and to carry off captives, the town endured. Some tellings speak of desperate years, of siege and hunger and the country around laid waste, and of the town holding all the same, so that Ogbomoso became known as a place that the cavalry could raid but could not take, a stubborn stone in the river."}, {"id": "undecided-ogbomoso-town#s6", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "There is a reason for this that is worth the telling, because it shows how a town’s geography and its character work together. Ogbomoso sat at the very edge of the forest. To its north was open grassland where horses ran free and the Ilorin cavalry was terrible. But just to its south the forest thickened, and horses are of little use among close trees. So Ogbomoso stood exactly on the seam of the two worlds, and it learned to fight on that seam, to lure the horsemen toward the broken country where their advantage failed, to hold its walls through the dry season when the cavalry rode and to strike back in the wet season when the ground turned against the horse. The town became a shield for everything south of it. The Egba, the Ijebu, the peoples of the deep forest, all of them owed a debt they did not always acknowledge to the stubborn border towns, Ogbomoso chief among them, that soaked up the fury of the north and would not break. This is the weight of Ogbomoso’s story, not merely that a brave hunter founded it, but that the descendants of that hunter’s courage held a frontier for generations and saved a whole country’s south from the fate of its north."}, {"id": "undecided-ogbomoso-town#s7", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "· ·"}, {"id": "undecided-ogbomoso-town#s8", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "When the wars at last cooled and the British peace settled over the country, hard and unwanted as it was, Ogbomoso turned the same energy it had spent on survival toward growth, and it flourished. The missionaries came, and here is another of the town’s proud chapters, for Ogbomoso became one of the great centers of the Baptist mission in all of Yorubaland. A famous hospital rose there, and schools, and the town that had once been a hunter’s camp on a dangerous road became a place people traveled to for healing and for learning. The old character held true even in this. Ogbomoso took what the newcomers offered, the medicine and the letters and the new faith for those who wanted it, and made these things thoroughly its own, so that in time it was Ogbomoso people teaching, Ogbomoso people healing, Ogbomoso people carrying the mission forward. The town had never been one to sit and wait for a great man to save it, and it did not start now."}, {"id": "undecided-ogbomoso-town#s9", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "And it kept growing, and its people did a thing that borderland people often do, they went out. Ogbomoso became famous for its travelers and its traders, for sons and daughters who left the high ground and carried the town’s name to every market in the country and beyond, to Ghana, to the far cities, across the seas. They went, and the remarkable thing is that they came back, or sent back, for an Ogbomoso man abroad remained an Ogbomoso man, tied to the town by a thread that distance could not cut, sending money home, building houses at home, returning for the festivals, insisting that his children know where they came from. There is a saying, told with pride, that you will find an Ogbomoso person in every corner of the world, and it is very nearly true. The hunter who founded the town went out alone into a dangerous country and made something of it, and his town’s children have been doing the same ever since, scattering fearlessly and always keeping the road home."}, {"id": "undecided-ogbomoso-town#s10", "scene": "Ológun", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So when you hear the name Ogbomoso, do not think of it as just another town on the map, one dot among many. Think of the hunter alone on the high ground, unafraid of the country that frightened everyone else. Think of the head of the terror carried home, and the fierce short phrase that became a city’s name. Think of the walls that held when the cavalry came, the shield that a stubborn people made of themselves for a whole country’s sake. And think of the roads out, the children scattered to every corner of the earth and every one of them keeping the way home in his heart. The town began with one brave man who would not be afraid, and it has never stopped being that man’s town. That is why the elders tell it, and that is why it deserves its full weight in the telling, for a place that was born of courage, that survived by courage, and that still sends its courage out into the world, is not a small thing, and must never be told as one."}]}, {"id": "undecided-lalupon-river-oracle", "emoji": "⚡", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#150020,#350050)", "category": "orisa", "rating": 9.6, "duration": "10 min", "title": {"en": "Lalupon and the River Oracle", "yo": "Lalupon"}, "desc": {"en": "The founding covenant between the town of Lalupon and its river.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Orisa", "secondary": ["Orisa", "Folktales / Literary"], "source_note": "(Itan Oba, from “Lalupon and the River Oracle”)", "segments": [{"id": "undecided-lalupon-river-oracle#s1", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Not every history of the Yoruba is the history of a great king or a famous war. Some of the deepest history lives in the smaller places, in the story of how a particular town came to sit where it sits, why it keeps the customs it keeps, and what bargain its founders once made with the land and the water and the unseen powers that share the world with the living. Lalupon is such a place, a town of the old Ibadan country, and its story is bound up, as so many Yoruba town stories are, with a river and with an oracle, with the moment when wandering people found a spot to stop and asked the powers of that place whether they might stay. This is a story about how a people and a river came to an understanding, and about what happens to a town that remembers its promises, and what happens when it forgets."}, {"id": "undecided-lalupon-river-oracle#s2", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "In the old days, before a town was a town, it was a group of people looking for somewhere to be. Perhaps they had left a larger place because of a quarrel, or a war, or simply because the land where they were had grown crowded and a bold family decided to strike out and found something of their own. This was how much of Yorubaland was settled, in waves of movement, hunters going ahead into the forest, finding good ground, water nearby, game to hunt and soil to farm, and then sending word back, and slowly a camp becoming a village becoming a town. And always, at the heart of the founding, there was the question that no sensible Yoruba person would skip. Is this place willing to have us? For the Yoruba never believed the land was empty. Every river had its spirit, every grove its owner, every hill its ancient presence, and to settle without asking was to invite disaster."}, {"id": "undecided-lalupon-river-oracle#s3", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "So when the founders of Lalupon came to the place of the river, they did what wise people did. They consulted. They went to the babalawo, the priest of Ifa, the keeper of the great oracle, and they asked the questions that must be asked. Would this place receive them? What did the spirit of the river require? What must they do, and what must they never do, if they wished to prosper here? And the oracle answered, as it always answered, in the ancient verses of Ifa and in the reading of the sacred palm nuts, laying out the terms of the bargain. There would be blessing here, water that never failed, land that gave good yield, children and long life and peace, but there would be conditions, taboos to keep, sacrifices to offer, a covenant between the people and the river that must never be broken. And the founders agreed, and poured the offering, and stayed."}, {"id": "undecided-lalupon-river-oracle#s4", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "The covenant, as such covenants always did, tied the fortunes of the town to the faithfulness of its people. The river would give, so long as the people gave back. There were days when the water was sacred and no one might draw from it or fish in it or wash in it, days set aside for the river alone. There were offerings to be made at the turning of the year, when the whole town would come down to the water and the priests would pour the sacrifice and speak the old words and thank the river for another year of life. There were things that must never be done, a fish that must never be caught, a grove that must never be cut, a place along the bank where no one might go, because it belonged to the spirit and not to the people. And in return the river kept its side. In the dry season, when other towns watched their streams shrink to cracked mud and sent their daughters walking miles for water, the river of Lalupon still ran, and the town drank and farmed and lived while others thirsted."}, {"id": "undecided-lalupon-river-oracle#s5", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "For generations it worked, as these arrangements work when a people take them seriously. The town grew. The market filled. The compounds spread out from the first settlement, family after family, and children were born who had never known any home but this, who grew up with the sound of the river always in their ears, who learned before they could properly walk that the water was not merely water but a living presence that had chosen to bless them and could, if angered, choose otherwise. The old ones taught the young ones the story of the founding and the terms of the covenant, and the festival came around each year, and the offerings were made, and Lalupon prospered under the quiet guardianship of the oracle’s bargain. This is how a Yoruba town holds itself together across time, not only with walls and chiefs and markets, but with a shared memory of a sacred promise binding the living to the land and to the generations who made the deal."}, {"id": "undecided-lalupon-river-oracle#s6", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "But every such story carries within it a warning, because the danger to any covenant is not the enemy at the gate but forgetting at the hearth. A town that prospers long enough begins to take its prosperity for granted. A generation arises that did not see the hard years, that has always had water, that begins to think the river runs because rivers run and not because a promise is kept. The young grow impatient with the taboos. Why can we not fish there? Why must we not go to that part of the bank? These are old superstitions, they say, from a time before we knew better. And little by little the offerings grow smaller, the festival grows thinner, the forbidden places are crossed, the sacred days are ignored, and no one is struck down at once, which only convinces the careless that they were right all along, that there was never any power in the water, only in their grandparents’ fears."}, {"id": "undecided-lalupon-river-oracle#s7", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "What comes next in these stories is always the same in shape, though the details change from town to town. The river answers the forgetting. Perhaps a season comes when for the first time in living memory the water fails, sinking low in the dry months as it never had before, and the town that had never known thirst suddenly knows it. Perhaps sickness comes, or the fish vanish, or the rains turn strange, and the people who had laughed at the old taboos grow quiet, and the elders who remembered are asked, almost shamefully, what has to be done. And the answer is always the same. Go back. Return to the covenant. Make the offering that was neglected, restore the festival, honor the sacred places, remember the promise your fathers made, and beg the river to forgive the forgetting. And if the town does this with a whole heart, the story says, the river relents, the water returns, and the lesson is learned again, until the next generation forgets and the whole cycle begins once more."}, {"id": "undecided-lalupon-river-oracle#s8", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "Whether one believes in the literal power of the river or reads the story as the old Yoruba wisdom dressed in the clothes of myth, the meaning is the same and it is worth taking seriously. A community lives by keeping faith with what sustains it. The covenant with the river is a way of saying that a people must not take their sources of life for granted, must not foul the water that gives them drink or exhaust the land that gives them food or forget the disciplines that kept their ancestors alive. The taboos that a careless generation calls superstition are often the compressed survival wisdom of people who learned the hard way what happens when you fish a stream empty or farm a soil to death or take without ever giving back. The river spirit, in the end, is the voice of consequence, patient and slow and absolutely certain."}, {"id": "undecided-lalupon-river-oracle#s9", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So Lalupon keeps its story, as the towns of Yorubaland keep theirs, and passes it down from the old to the young at the edge of the water. It says, this is where we come from, this is the bargain that made us, this is what we owe and what we must never do, and this is what happened, more than once, when we forgot. It is history and it is warning and it is a kind of prayer, all at the same time. And the river runs, still, past the town it agreed long ago to bless, keeping its ancient side of an ancient promise, and asking only that the people, in each new generation, remember to keep theirs."}]}, {"id": "undecided-lost-tombs-of-ile-ife", "emoji": "👑", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#1a0a00,#4a2800)", "category": "itan-oba", "rating": 9.6, "duration": "10 min", "title": {"en": "The Lost Tombs of Ile-Ife", "yo": "Ile-Ife"}, "desc": {"en": "The buried royal past of Ile-Ife and its heads now scattered to foreign museums.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Itan Oba", "secondary": ["Itan Oba"], "source_note": "(Itan Oba, from “The Lost Tombs of Ile-Ife”)", "segments": [{"id": "undecided-lost-tombs-of-ile-ife#s1", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "There is a place at the center of the Yoruba world that is older than memory and holier than any other, and its name is Ile-Ife. It is the cradle, the source, the spot where, in the sacred telling, the world itself was made, where Oduduwa came down and creation began, where the first kings ruled and the first of everything happened. Every Yoruba crown traces its authority back to Ife. Every king, however great, however far away, however powerful his own city, bows in his heart toward Ife as the origin of his right to rule. And beneath the ancient ground of that sacred city lie the tombs and the treasures of the deep past, some found and marveled at, some still lost, still waiting, and the story of those lost tombs is the story of a civilization whose greatness was buried and half forgotten and then, astonishingly, dug up again to amaze the world."}, {"id": "undecided-lost-tombs-of-ile-ife#s2", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "To understand why the tombs of Ife matter so much, you have to understand what Ife once was, and here is one of the great surprises that the earth of Ife has given up. For centuries the outside world knew nothing of Ife’s ancient glory, and even assumed, in its ignorance and its prejudice, that the peoples of this part of Africa had never made great art or built great things. And then, beginning in the early twentieth century and continuing through the years that followed, the ground of Ife began to yield its secrets, and what came out of it shattered every assumption. Sculptures. Heads of bronze and brass and terracotta, cast and modeled with a naturalism, a subtlety, a sheer artistic mastery so refined that when the first of them reached Europe, scholars there could not at first believe that Africans had made them, and invented absurd theories of lost visitors from other lands, because their prejudice could not accommodate the truth."}, {"id": "undecided-lost-tombs-of-ile-ife#s3", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "But the truth was plain in the clay and the metal. These were the work of Ife, made by Yoruba hands, centuries old, dating back to a golden age when Ife flourished as a center of art and power and sacred kingship, roughly seven or eight hundred years ago and more. The famous heads, some of them almost certainly portraits of the Oonis, the sacred kings of Ife, are among the supreme achievements of world sculpture, ranking with the finest work of any civilization anywhere. The delicacy of the features, the calm dignity of the faces, the mastery of the casting, all of it announced to a doubting world that here, in this forest city, a great and sophisticated civilization had lived and made beauty on the highest level, long before the Europeans arrived who would later flatter themselves that they had found a backward land."}, {"id": "undecided-lost-tombs-of-ile-ife#s4", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Yet for all that has been found, so much remains lost, and this is the ache at the heart of the story. Ife is sacred ground, densely built over, lived on continuously for a thousand years and more, and the tombs of its ancient kings, the resting places of the Oonis and the great ones of the golden age, are not neatly marked and mapped as the tombs of Egypt’s pharaohs came to be. Many were never recorded. Many lie who knows where beneath the living city, beneath compounds and shrines and streets, their locations known only to the sacred traditions of the priests, or forgotten entirely, or remembered only as a grove that must not be disturbed, a spot that is holy for reasons the keepers may guard closely and reveal to no outsider. The Yoruba did not build pyramids to shout the location of their dead to the world. They kept their sacred places quiet, protected, sometimes deliberately hidden, and so the past of Ife is a past that hides even as it dazzles."}, {"id": "undecided-lost-tombs-of-ile-ife#s5", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "And there is a harder loss woven through it too, the same loss that shadows the story of Benin and of so much of Africa. Much of what was found in Ife did not stay in Ife. In the colonial period and after, treasures left the sacred city, some taken openly, some quietly, ending up in the museums and collections of distant lands, so that a person who wishes to look upon the greatest art of the Yoruba ancestors may have to travel across the world to a foreign museum to do it, standing before the face of an ancient Yoruba king in a glass case in a city his people never knew, while the sacred ground that held him for centuries sits far away, robbed of its own heritage. The lost tombs of Ife are lost in more ways than one, lost to time and lost to distance, and the long effort to recover, protect, and one day bring home what was scattered is part of the living story, not a closed chapter."}, {"id": "undecided-lost-tombs-of-ile-ife#s6", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "There is also, it must be said, a tension that runs through the whole matter and that the people of Ife feel keenly, between the desire to know and the duty to protect. An archaeologist wants to dig, to uncover, to bring the buried past into the light where it can be studied and admired and understood. But Ife is not a dead ruin. It is a living sacred city, and its buried kings are not merely artifacts but holy ancestors, and there are places the priests will say must never be opened, secrets that are meant to stay secret, ground that is not for the spade. And who is to say they are wrong? The reverence that kept these places sacred for a thousand years is the same reverence that preserved them at all, and there is a wisdom in the refusal to dig up everything, in leaving some of the holy dead to their rest, in accepting that not all knowledge is meant to be seized."}, {"id": "undecided-lost-tombs-of-ile-ife#s7", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "So what do the lost tombs of Ife finally mean, and why do the elders keep this story and press it on the young? They mean, first, a great and simple pride, and a corrective to a lie. For a long time the world was told, and some of the Yoruba themselves half came to believe, that their ancestors had made nothing worth remembering, that greatness and civilization and high art belonged to others. The tombs and the treasures of Ife are the answer to that lie, dug out of the sacred earth itself, undeniable, magnificent. They say to every Yoruba child, look, this is what your ancestors made, this beauty, this mastery, this civilization, seven hundred years ago and more, before anyone came to teach them anything. Carry that. Let no one ever again tell you that you come from nothing, for you come from Ife, and Ife made wonders."}, {"id": "undecided-lost-tombs-of-ile-ife#s8", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "They mean, second, the preciousness of what is buried and the duty to guard it. The story is a charge laid on the living to protect the sacred ground, to honor the ancestors who lie beneath it, to fight for the return of what was scattered across the world, and to keep the reverence that alone preserved these treasures through the long centuries. A people that loses its sacred places loses itself. A people that lets its heritage be carted off and forgotten forgets who it is. The lost tombs remind the Yoruba that the past is not gone, that it lies just beneath their feet in the holiest of their cities, and that it is theirs to cherish and defend."}, {"id": "undecided-lost-tombs-of-ile-ife#s9", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "And they mean, at the last, a certain humility before mystery, which is perhaps the deepest note of all. Not everything has been found. Not everything will be found. Some of the tombs will stay lost, some of the secrets will stay sealed, and Ife will keep its deepest holiness hidden as it always has, revealing only what it chooses to reveal. There is grace in that. The sacred city does not give up all its dead to the curiosity of the living, and the wise honor the silence as much as the discovery. The Oonis of the golden age sleep on in ground that has held them for a thousand years, their faces cast in metal now scattered across the earth, their exact resting places known only to the holy keepers or to no one at all, and Ife keeps its ancient watch above them, the cradle of a people, the source of the crowns, the place where the world began. That is the story. Some of it is dug up and shining in the light. And some of it, rightly, stays in the sacred dark."}]}, {"id": "undecided-salt-roads", "emoji": "🏛️", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#001a0a,#003a1a)", "category": "naija", "rating": 9.6, "duration": "10 min", "title": {"en": "The Salt Roads: Yoruba Commerce Before the Colonial Era", "yo": "The Salt Roads: Yoruba Commerce Before the Colonial Era"}, "desc": {"en": "The salt roads and the commerce of Yorubaland before colonial rule.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Nigeria History", "secondary": ["Nigeria History"], "source_note": "(Itan Oba, from “The Salt Roads: Yoruba Commerce Before the Colonial Era”)", "segments": [{"id": "undecided-salt-roads#s1", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "When people imagine old Africa, they too often imagine only villages and farms, simple lives lived close to the soil, cut off from the wider world until strangers came and connected it. This is one of the great falsehoods about the African past, and nowhere is it more thoroughly disproved than in the story of Yoruba trade, for the Yoruba were among the great traders of the world, weaving a web of commerce across their land and far beyond it, moving goods over hundreds of miles along established routes, running markets so sophisticated they had their own laws and calendars and specialized officials, and building much of their wealth and power not on conquest alone but on the humble, endless, brilliant business of buying and selling. To follow the salt roads of old Yorubaland is to discover a civilization of merchants, a world of commerce that hummed along for centuries before any European set foot in the interior."}, {"id": "undecided-salt-roads#s2", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Salt gives the roads their name, and salt is the right place to begin, because salt was one of the great necessities and one of the great trade goods of the old world everywhere, and Yorubaland, lying inland, had to bring its salt from elsewhere, chiefly from the coast where it was won from the sea, and from the lagoons and the salt-bearing regions. So salt moved inland from the water, carried on the heads of porters and by canoe along the lagoons and rivers, passing from market to market, growing more precious the farther it traveled from the sea, until it reached the deep interior where it was worth a great deal indeed. And moving the other way, down toward the coast, came the goods of the interior, the kola nuts so prized across all of West Africa, the cloth, the ironwork, the foodstuffs, the products of the forest and the farm. The salt roads were really the whole circulatory system of a trading civilization, and salt was only the most famous of the many things that flowed along them."}, {"id": "undecided-salt-roads#s3", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "And the goods were astonishing in their range, because the old Yoruba economy was far more specialized and productive than the lazy stereotype allows. There was cloth, above all, for the Yoruba were master weavers and dyers, producing textiles of great beauty and value, the strip-woven cloth and the deep indigo-dyed adire that were traded far and wide and worn as marks of wealth and status. There was ironwork from the blacksmiths, hoes and knives and weapons and tools, for the Yoruba smelted and worked iron with great skill. There were beads, and leather, and pottery, and palm oil, and dried fish, and the kola nut that greased every social and commercial transaction across the region, and a hundred other things besides, each with its centers of production and its routes to market. This was a diversified, productive economy, trading in manufactured goods and raw materials alike, and it was old, far older than the colonial period that later liked to imagine it had brought commerce to a land without it."}, {"id": "undecided-salt-roads#s4", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "At the heart of this whole world stood the market, the oja, and the Yoruba market was a marvel of organization that deserves to be understood in its own right, because it was far more than a place to buy and sell. The great markets ran on a cycle, often the four-day or eight-day week of the traditional calendar, so that a person knew that on such a day the market at this town would be held, and on another day the market at that town, and the traders moved in a rhythm among them, a whole region’s commerce pulsing in time. The markets were vast, drawing sellers and buyers from a wide area, laid out in sections by the goods sold, and they were governed. There were market officials, and there was, presiding over the women who dominated so much of the trade, the great office of the Iyaloja, the mother of the market, a powerful woman who kept order, settled disputes, set standards, and represented the traders to the chiefs and the king. The market had its own law, its own authority, its own peace, and to break the peace of the market was a serious offense."}, {"id": "undecided-salt-roads#s5", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "And the trade was, to a remarkable degree, in the hands of women, which is one of the most striking features of the whole system and one that astonished outsiders accustomed to keeping their women out of business. Yoruba women were traders to the bone, and the great markets were their domain, run by them, dominated by them, a sphere in which women accumulated wealth and wielded real economic power. A successful trading woman could become rich in her own right, independent, influential, a person of standing whose voice carried weight. The wealth that women built through trade was their own, and it gave Yoruba women a degree of economic independence and public power that many other societies denied their women entirely. The salt roads were, in large part, roads walked and ruled by women, and the prosperity they carried flowed in no small measure into women’s hands."}, {"id": "undecided-salt-roads#s6", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "The traders themselves were sophisticated operators, and the long-distance trade in particular required real skill and courage and organization. To move valuable goods over hundreds of miles through the territories of many different towns and peoples, some friendly and some not, was no simple thing. It required knowledge of the routes and the markets and the prices, relationships and trust built up across distances, systems of credit and partnership, ways of storing value and settling accounts, and often the physical courage to travel through dangerous country. The Yoruba trader dealt in cowrie shells, the currency of the region, counted and strung and moved in great quantities, and understood exchange rates and profit and the whole calculus of commerce as well as any merchant anywhere. This was not barter by simple folk. This was a monetized, credit-using, long-distance trading economy of real complexity, run by clever and determined people who knew exactly what they were doing."}, {"id": "undecided-salt-roads#s7", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "This trading world was tied to power, and here we see why the story belongs among the histories of kings, for the wealth of the salt roads was the wealth of kingdoms. A town that sat astride a major trade route, that controlled a great market, that could tax the goods flowing through it, grew rich and grew strong, and much of the rise of the great Yoruba powers is bound up with their control of trade. Oyo’s power rested partly on its position in the northern trade, its access to the routes that brought horses from the north, horses that made its cavalry and its empire possible. The coastal towns rose on the trade with the sea. Kings and chiefs enriched themselves from commerce, granted and protected the markets, and understood well that trade and power were two faces of the same coin. The peace that let the salt roads run was a peace the kings had an interest in keeping, and the wealth that flowed along them was wealth that built palaces and armies and the whole apparatus of the states."}, {"id": "undecided-salt-roads#s8", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "Which is exactly why the nineteenth century was such a catastrophe, for the long wars that tore Yorubaland apart did not only kill and enslave, they also strangled the trade. When the roads became too dangerous to travel, when town made war on town and the markets emptied and the routes were cut, the whole circulatory system of commerce that had sustained the civilization for centuries was choked. The salt roads that had carried prosperity now ran with refugees and armies, and the trade that flowed most freely in that dark age was the trade in captive human beings, feeding the ships. And then came the colonial conquest, which reorganized the whole economy around its own purposes, building roads and railways to carry produce down to the coast for export to Europe, imposing its own currency and its own trading firms, subordinating the old indigenous commerce to the needs of the empire, and often pushing to the margins the very traders, the women especially, who had built the trading world in the first place."}, {"id": "undecided-salt-roads#s9", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "But the story the elders tell is not, in the end, a story of loss, because the trading spirit of the Yoruba was never killed, only battered, and it rose again as such spirits do. The Yoruba remained, through the colonial period and after, a people of legendary commercial energy, their traders and market women and entrepreneurs a force to be reckoned with everywhere they went. And so the salt roads live on, not in the old routes and the cowrie shells, but in the character they helped to forge, the deep Yoruba genius for trade, for the market, for the deal, for turning goods into wealth through skill and daring and relentless work. The elders tell the story to correct the lie that their ancestors were simple and isolated, and to plant a truth in the young. You come from traders, they say, from a people who ran markets and walked the long roads and built kingdoms on commerce centuries before anyone came to teach them anything about business. That inheritance is in your blood. The salt has moved inland from the sea for a thousand years, and the road it traveled is your road too."}]}, {"id": "undecided-agbado", "emoji": "📖", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#0a1a00,#1a3a00)", "category": "iwe", "rating": 9.6, "duration": "10 min", "title": {"en": "Agbado: The Story of Corn in Yoruba Life", "yo": "Agbado"}, "desc": {"en": "The story of corn in Yoruba life.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Folktales / Literary", "secondary": ["Folktales / Literary"], "source_note": "(Itan Oba, from “Agbado: The Story of Corn in Yoruba Life”)", "segments": [{"id": "undecided-agbado#s1", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Not all history is written in the deeds of kings and the fall of cities. Some of the deepest history of a people is written in what they eat, in the plants that fill their fields and their bellies, in the crops that feed the generations and shape the rhythm of the year. And among the Yoruba, few things carry more history in a single golden ear than agbado, which is to say corn, or maize, the tall grain with the silken tassels that stands in the fields at the season’s turning and that has fed the people for so long that they can scarcely imagine a time before it. Yet there was such a time, and the story of how corn came to the Yoruba, and what it became to them, is a hidden history of the whole world, a story that reaches across oceans and ties a Yoruba farmer’s field to lands he never knew existed."}, {"id": "undecided-agbado#s2", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Here is the surprise that opens the story, and it surprises many Yoruba themselves. Corn is not originally African at all. Maize was born in the Americas, domesticated thousands of years ago by the peoples of what is now Mexico and Central America, cultivated and perfected by them over countless generations into the great grain it became. For all the long ages of the ancient world, while the civilizations of Africa and Asia and Europe rose and fell, the Americas were a world apart, and corn grew only there, unknown to the rest of humankind. The Yoruba of the ancient kingdoms, the builders of Ife and the early days of Oyo, knew nothing of it. Their grains were the old African crops, the millet and the sorghum and the fonio and the rice of the region, the yam above all, the true ancient staple of the forest Yoruba, the crop around which so much of their oldest culture turned."}, {"id": "undecided-agbado#s3", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "And then the two worlds collided. When the European ships crossed the Atlantic and linked the Americas to the rest of the earth in that fateful age, they set off a vast exchange of plants and animals and, terribly, of people, moving crops from one hemisphere to the other. Corn crossed the ocean from the Americas to Africa in this exchange, brought by the Portuguese and others to the West African coast, and from the coast it moved inland along the very trade routes that carried salt and cloth and kola, spreading from farm to farm and town to town, a strange new grain that farmers tried and found good. And corn is a generous crop, quick-growing and productive, and it took to the soils and climate of West Africa, and it spread, and it stayed, and over the generations it wove itself so deeply into the life of the Yoruba that its foreign birth was almost entirely forgotten."}, {"id": "undecided-agbado#s4", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "And how thoroughly it wove itself in. Walk through Yoruba food and you will find corn everywhere, transformed by generations of cooks into a hundred beloved forms. There is eko, or agidi, the smooth firm corn pudding wrapped in leaves, eaten with stew or with bean cakes, a food of everyday life and of celebration. There is ogi, or akamu, the fermented corn pap, smooth and slightly sour, the food of the very young and the very old and the sick, the first solid food a baby often tastes, the gentle meal for a convalescent, a food tied to the tenderest moments of care in a family. There is corn roasted whole over coals at the roadside in the season, sold by the women with their small fires, eaten hot with a piece of coconut or a pear, one of the great simple pleasures of the Yoruba year. There is corn boiled, corn ground into flour, corn in a dozen dishes, until the grain that came from across the sea sits at the very center of the table."}, {"id": "undecided-agbado#s5", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "More than that, corn shaped the rhythm of the year and the imagination of the people. Its planting and its harvest marked the seasons. The new corn, the first green ears of the season, became a thing of anticipation and even of ritual, the taste of the year’s first corn a small annual joy. Corn entered the proverbs and the songs and the sayings, for a people speaks in the images of what surrounds them, and corn surrounded the Yoruba, in the fields and the markets and the pot. The stages of the corn, the tender young ear and the hard dry mature grain, became metaphors for the stages of human life and for the passing of time. A food does this when it becomes truly central, it stops being merely something eaten and becomes part of how a people thinks and speaks and marks the turning of their days, and corn did exactly that among the Yoruba, so completely that to be Yoruba became, in part, to be a person who knew the taste of ogi and the smell of corn roasting by the road."}, {"id": "undecided-agbado#s6", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "There is a deeper lesson buried in the golden ear, and the thoughtful among the elders draw it out. Corn is a stranger that became family. It came from far away, from a people the Yoruba had never heard of, carried by the same ships and the same terrible commerce that brought so much sorrow, and yet the Yoruba took this foreign thing and made it wholly their own, adapting it, transforming it, folding it so deep into their life that it became indistinguishable from what had always been. This is a truth about culture itself, and about the Yoruba genius in particular, for the Yoruba have always been a people who take what comes to them from the wider world and remake it in their own image, whether it is a grain, a religion, a technology, or a style of music. They do not merely receive. They absorb, transform, and claim, so that the borrowed thing becomes theirs."}, {"id": "undecided-agbado#s7", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "The story of corn also carries a quiet warning, the same warning that runs through so much of the honest history of this land, because the same Atlantic exchange that brought the generous grain brought also the guns and the greed and the ships that carried the people away. History is rarely simple, and the thing that fed the Yoruba came by the same road as the thing that wounded them. The elders who tell the story of agbado do not pretend otherwise. They hold the two truths together, the gift and the grief riding the same tide, and in doing so they teach that the past is complicated, that good and ill often arrive tangled in the same bundle, and that a wise people learns to take the nourishment while never forgetting the cost of the age that delivered it."}, {"id": "undecided-agbado#s8", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "And yet, when all is said, the story of corn is finally a hopeful one, a story of resilience and of making. Consider what it means. A people had their ancient world turned upside down by forces from across the sea, forces that brought suffering beyond telling. And out of that same overturning, out of the same collision of worlds, they seized something good, a new grain, and they did not merely survive with it, they flourished, they created, they made from a foreign seed a whole cuisine, a whole set of customs, a whole thread of their culture, so rich and so beloved that their descendants would grow up thinking corn had always been theirs. That is what a living, confident culture does. It is not destroyed by contact with the world. It feeds on it. It takes the new and makes it its own and grows stronger."}, {"id": "undecided-agbado#s9", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So when a Yoruba person bites into a hot roasted ear of corn at the roadside in the season, or feeds a spoon of smooth ogi to a baby, or unwraps a leaf of eko to eat with the evening stew, they are eating history, whether they know it or not. They are tasting a journey across an ocean, a meeting of worlds, a transformation worked by their own ancestors over long generations. The golden grain in their hand was born in a distant land among a distant people, carried across the water in a hard age, and taken in and remade and loved until it became as Yoruba as the yam itself. Agbado is the humble monument to the Yoruba gift for turning what the world sends into something wholly and beautifully their own. It stands in the field each season, tall and golden, a stranger long ago made family, feeding the children of a people who took the whole turning world and, out of it, made a meal."}]}, {"id": "undecided-cocoa-king", "emoji": "👑", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#1a0a00,#4a2800)", "category": "itan-oba", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "7 min", "title": {"en": "The Cocoa King: The Story of Ile-Oluji and the Golden Pod", "yo": "Ile-Oluji"}, "desc": {"en": "Cocoa and the eastern towns, Ile-Oluji chief among them, that grew rich on the golden pod.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Itan Oba", "secondary": ["Itan Oba", "Nigeria History"], "source_note": "(Itan Oba, from “Ile-Oluji and the Cocoa King”)", "segments": [{"id": "undecided-cocoa-king#s1", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "There is a kind of wealth that comes out of the ground slowly, one tree at a time, and it does not gleam like gold or ring like coin, but it can build a town, send children across the sea to be educated, and raise a plain farmer to sit among kings. This is the story of that wealth, of the little brown pod that the Yoruba call koko, cocoa, and of the eastern towns, Ile-Oluji chief among them, that grew rich and famous upon it. But like all the best stories it is really about people, about a generation of farmers who looked at a strange new tree that no ancestor of theirs had ever planted, and decided to gamble their lives on it, and won."}, {"id": "undecided-cocoa-king#s2", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "To begin, you must picture the country before the cocoa came. The forest belt of the Yoruba, the deep green country of the Ondo and the Ijesa and the Ife and the Egba, was rich land, but its people farmed as their fathers had, yam and cocoyam and maize and palm, feeding themselves and trading a little. Then, in the closing years of the old century, the cocoa tree arrived, carried in from across the sea, from the islands and the coast where the Europeans had first planted it, and a few curious men took the seeds and set them in the deep shade of the forest. And the forest loved the tree. The very shade and damp that made the eastern country hard to farm in the old ways turned out to be exactly what the cocoa craved, and the trees flourished, and after the patient years of waiting that every tree crop demands, they began to bear.· · ·"}, {"id": "undecided-cocoa-king#s3", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "What happened next changed the whole eastern country. A man with land and patience and a little courage could plant cocoa, wait, and then sell his dried beans to the buyers who came up from the coast, and the money that came back was unlike anything the old farming had ever produced. It was cash, real cash, and a great deal of it, and it came every harvest. Farmers who had been poor became comfortable, the comfortable became rich, and a few, the shrewdest and the boldest, became rich beyond anything their grandfathers could have imagined. These were the cocoa farmers who earned the proud, half-joking, half-serious title of Cocoa King, men whose plantations spread across hundreds of acres, who employed whole villages of laborers, who owned the first motor lorries in the district and built the first storey houses with corrugated roofs that flashed in the sun so that you could see a rich man’s wealth from far off. And Ile-Oluji, sitting in the heart of that good cocoa country, became one of the great names of the boom, a town whose sons planted the golden pod and prospered mightily upon it."}, {"id": "undecided-cocoa-king#s4", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "But here is the part of the story that matters most, the part that gives it its weight, for these were not idle rich men who hoarded their money and did nothing. The cocoa wealth was poured back into the people. This was the generation that built the schools, that sent their sons and their daughters too across the sea to England to study law and medicine, that produced, out of the sweat of the cocoa farms, a whole class of educated Yoruba professionals who would go on to lead the country. A man who could barely write his own name would sell his cocoa and use the money to make his son a barrister, and that son would come home in a fine suit with letters after his name, and the old farmer, standing in his cocoa grove in his work clothes, would be prouder than any king. This is the true harvest of the cocoa, not the mansions and the lorries, which faded, but the educated generation, the doctors and lawyers and teachers and leaders that the golden pod paid for. The cocoa farmers were building the future with every pod they dried, though many of them never lived to see how tall it would grow."}, {"id": "undecided-cocoa-king#s5", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "Of course, no wealth from the ground is ever wholly safe, and the cocoa story has its bitter seasons too. The price of cocoa was set far away, in markets across the sea that no Yoruba farmer could see or control, and it rose and it fell, and when it fell the fine houses stood unfinished and the school fees went unpaid and the proud Cocoa Kings learned that a king who depends on a single crop is at the mercy of a world he cannot command. There were years of glut and years of disease in the groves, and later there came government boards that bought all the cocoa at fixed prices and took much of the profit for themselves, so that the farmer’s share of the golden pod grew thin. The great boom did not last forever, as no boom does. But the eastern cocoa towns had already done their work. Ile-Oluji and its sisters had turned a strange foreign tree into schools and scholars and a whole educated generation, and that harvest could not be taken back by any fall in price."}, {"id": "undecided-cocoa-king#s6", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So when you eat something sweetened with chocolate, far away in some cold country, spare a thought for where it began, in the deep green shade of the Yoruba forest, in the patient hands of farmers who bet on a tree. And when you pass through Ile-Oluji or any of the cocoa towns and see an old man sitting quietly outside a fading storey house, understand that you may be looking at a king, or a king’s son, one of the generation that built a corner of the modern world out of little brown pods and long patience and the deep faith that tomorrow could be made richer than today if only a man was willing to plant and to wait. The elders tell this story not for the money in it, for money comes and goes, but for the vision in it, the farmers who could see, in a seed no bigger than a thumb, a doctor, a lawyer, a nation’s future, and who planted that future row by row, and lived to watch it bear."}]}, {"id": "undecided-oyo-mesi", "emoji": "👑", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#1a0a00,#4a2800)", "category": "itan-oba", "rating": 9.4, "duration": "4 min", "title": {"en": "The Oyo Mesi: Democracy Before Democracy", "yo": "Oyo Mesi"}, "desc": {"en": "The Oyo Mesi, the council of seven who could dethrone a king: democracy before democracy.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Itan Oba", "secondary": ["Itan Oba"], "source_note": "", "segments": [{"id": "undecided-oyo-mesi#s1", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Narrator opens: When European political philosophers of the 17th century were debating the theoretical possibility of limiting the power of kings — debating Locke and Hobbes and Montesquieu — the Oyo Empire in West Africa had already built and operated a constitutional monarchy for centuries. The Oyo Mesi was the council of seven chiefs who served as the constitutional check on the power of the Alaafin. Their role was not advisory in the weak sense — not merely to make suggestions that the king could ignore. The Oyo Mesi had genuine power. Real, enforceable, sometimes deadly power."}, {"id": "undecided-oyo-mesi#s2", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "The most dramatic expression of this power was the right to present the Alaafin with an empty calabash — or, in some versions, a parrot's egg. This symbolic gesture — called \"opening the calabash\" — was a formal declaration that the Alaafin's reign had become unacceptable to the council, and that he was expected to take his own life. It was not a request. It was a constitutional requirement. Several Alaafins in Oyo's history received the calabash. Some complied. Some tried to refuse — and found that the mechanisms of the system were stronger than their individual will."}, {"id": "undecided-oyo-mesi#s3", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "The head of the Oyo Mesi was the Bashorun — a title that combined military command with civic authority. In the 18th century, a Bashorun named Gaha held the position for forty years and used it to tyrannize the palace rather than check it, killing four Alaafins and essentially running the empire as a regent. His eventual overthrow and execution by the Alaafin Abiodun was one of the most dramatic political events in Oyo's history."}, {"id": "undecided-oyo-mesi#s4", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "The Oyo Mesi system also included other checks on power. The Alaafin could not be seen eating in public. The Alaafin could not be seen drinking in public. The Alaafin, in many contexts, was required to speak through intermediaries — his words carried to others by the Ajele, his representative voices. These restrictions were not humiliations. They were technologies of governance. They prevented the kind of casual, personalized tyranny that comes when a ruler is seen as simply a more powerful person rather than as the embodiment of an office. The military system of Oyo also had its own constitutional element: the Aare Ona Kakanfo, the supreme general, could never live in the same city as the Alaafin. The war chief and the peace chief were separated by geography — a practical safeguard against the military's ability to directly intimidate the civilian government."}, {"id": "undecided-oyo-mesi#s5", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "These structures did not make Oyo perfect. No system of government does. The slave trade, the wars of expansion, the exploitation of subordinate communities — these were real and serious failures. But the existence of the Oyo Mesi, the calabash of accountability, the separation of military and civilian power — these were genuine political achievements that deserve to be recognized as such. When someone tells you that African societies before colonialism had no concept of governance, of accountability, of constitutional limits on power — remember Oyo. Remember the calabash. Remember that the Alaafin himself could be told: enough."}]}, {"id": "undecided-rise-of-oyo-empire", "emoji": "👑", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#1a0a00,#4a2800)", "category": "itan-oba", "rating": 9.4, "duration": "4 min", "title": {"en": "The Rise of the Oyo Empire: How a Town Became a Kingdom and a Kingdom Became a World", "yo": "The Rise of the Oyo Empire: How a Town Became a Kingdom and a Kingdom Became a World"}, "desc": {"en": "How Oyo grew from a village into an empire, and how its decline began.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Itan Oba", "secondary": ["Itan Oba", "Ologun / Warlords"], "source_note": "", "segments": [{"id": "undecided-rise-of-oyo-empire#s1", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Narrator opens: The first Oyo was a village. The second Oyo was a town. The third Oyo was a kingdom. And the fourth Oyo — Oyo at its peak — was an empire that made ambassadors of distant kingdoms tremble when they heard its name. How does a village become an empire? It happens the way all great things happen: slowly, then suddenly. Oranmiyan founded Oyo-Ile in the northern savanna, far from the forest belt of Ile-Ife. The location was strategic — near the Niger River, near the trade routes that stretched across the Sudan, near the horse country where cavalry could be raised and deployed. The early rulers of Oyo understood something fundamental: in the open savanna, horses win wars. So the Alaafins of Oyo built an army of horsemen unlike anything the region had ever seen."}, {"id": "undecided-rise-of-oyo-empire#s2", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "The Oyo Mesi — the council of seven chiefs who governed alongside the Alaafin — was a system of democratic monarchy that balanced royal power with civic accountability. The Bashorun, head of the Oyo Mesi, had the power to demand that an Alaafin \"open the calabash\" — a euphemism for suicide — if the king became tyrannical or ineffective. This was not a tradition of weakness. It was a tradition of order. It meant that no Alaafin, no matter how powerful, could become a despot without consequence. By the 17th century, the Oyo Empire stretched over 150,000 square kilometers. Its cavalry raided and extracted tribute from kingdoms as far as Dahomey to the west. The Alafin at the height of power did not ask for tribute. He demanded it. And it was paid."}, {"id": "undecided-rise-of-oyo-empire#s3", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "The market at Oyo-Ile was one of the largest in West Africa. Goods flowed in from the forest belt — kola nuts, palm oil, cloth — and goods flowed in from the north — salt, leather, horses, copper. Enslaved captives from wars of conquest were traded to European merchants on the coast, a bitter transaction that made the empire wealthy and history complicated. But empires do not last forever. The beginning of the end for Oyo came with internal strife — a Bashorun named Gaha who held the position for forty years, killing four Alaafins and terrorizing the palace. When Abiodun finally came to power and executed Gaha, the empire was already fractured. Then came the wars with the Fulani Jihad in the north, led by Alimi and his successors. Then came the revolt of Afonja, the most powerful war general — the Aare Ona Kakanfo — who invited the Fulani into Yorubaland and could not control what he unleashed."}, {"id": "undecided-rise-of-oyo-empire#s4", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "The fall of Oyo-Ile happened in waves. First the old capital was abandoned in the 1830s. Then the refugees moved south and founded New Oyo. And the great Yoruba wars of the 19th century — Owu, Egba, Ijaiye, Ibadan — were all, in one way or another, the children of the Oyo collapse. But even in its fall, Oyo was magnificent. Because the diaspora of Oyo citizens and culture spread Yoruba civilization across West Africa and across the Atlantic. Oyo Yoruba became the Lukumi of Cuba. Oyo Yoruba became the Nago of Brazil. The thunder drums of Sango sound in Salvador da Bahia today because of what Oyo built and what it scattered."}, {"id": "undecided-rise-of-oyo-empire#s5", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "The Oyo Empire. Larger than Portugal. Richer than many European kingdoms of its era. Governed by a system of checks and balances that European philosophers had not yet imagined. It rose over centuries and it fell in decades, and in its rising and falling, it made the modern Yoruba world. Remember it. Honor it. Tell its story."}]}, {"id": "undecided-benin-oyo-relationship", "emoji": "👑", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#1a0a00,#4a2800)", "category": "itan-oba", "rating": 9.4, "duration": "4 min", "title": {"en": "The Benin-Oyo Relationship: Brothers Who Became Rivals", "yo": "The Benin-Oyo Relationship: Brothers Who Became Rivals"}, "desc": {"en": "The Yoruba and Benin: kingdoms of one ancestor that grew into rivals and partners.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Itan Oba", "secondary": ["Itan Oba"], "source_note": "", "segments": [{"id": "undecided-benin-oyo-relationship#s1", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Narrator opens: History loves clean borders. It likes to say: here ends one people, here begins another. But the actual past is messier and more interesting. The Yoruba and the Benin Kingdom are a perfect example of this complexity — peoples who share an ancestor but grew in different directions, who fought wars but traded goods, who claim the same origin but built different worlds. Oduduwa, the first ancestor of the Yoruba, had a son called Oranmiyan. And Oranmiyan, as we have told, went to Benin. He married a Benin princess. He fathered Eweka. And Eweka became the first Oba of the current Benin royal dynasty — a dynasty that has continued unbroken to this day."}, {"id": "undecided-benin-oyo-relationship#s2", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "This means the Oba of Benin and the Alafin of Oyo are cousins. This means the Yoruba and the Edo people of Benin share a common grandfather. This is why, in the protocols of traditional diplomacy, the Oba of Benin and the Ooni of Ife have a special relationship — a relationship of mutual recognition, of shared origin, even as their kingdoms developed along separate lines. The Benin Kingdom became one of the most sophisticated political entities in pre-colonial Africa. The court of Benin produced art of extraordinary quality — the Benin Bronzes, which like the Ife Bronzes were seized by colonial forces (in this case, during the punitive expedition of 1897) and distributed to museums across Europe. The Benin Bronzes today sit primarily in the British Museum, the Weltmuseum in Vienna, and the Ethnologisches Museum in Berlin — and they have been the subject of one of the most important repatriation debates of the 21st century."}, {"id": "undecided-benin-oyo-relationship#s3", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "But before the bronzes, before the punitive expedition, before the palm oil trade and the British treaties — there was the relationship between Yoruba and Benin. There were trade routes. There were marriages. There were wars. There were periods of tribute and periods of independence. There was a complicated dance between two great kingdoms who recognized each other's legitimacy even as they competed for influence. In the 19th century, the collapse of Oyo sent waves of refugees in all directions — some went to Ibadan, some to Abeokuta, some to Ilorin. The Benin Kingdom, relatively stable in the south-east, watched the Yoruba wars with a combination of concern and calculation. The buffer states between Benin and Yoruba proper — the Itsekiri, the Urhobo, the western Ijaw — were in constant negotiation with both sides."}, {"id": "undecided-benin-oyo-relationship#s4", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "The story of Benin and Oyo is the story of how shared ancestry does not guarantee peace, but how it does guarantee recognition. Even in their wars, the Yoruba and Benin kingdoms acknowledged each other as belonging to the same family of civilizations. Even in their disputes, there was a protocol — because you do not destroy what comes from the same root you come from. Remember the Benin Bronzes. Demand their return. They are not trophies of conquest. They are the proof of what West Africa was before conquest came."}]}, {"id": "undecided-ife-bronze-heads", "emoji": "👑", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#1a0a00,#4a2800)", "category": "itan-oba", "rating": 9.4, "duration": "4 min", "title": {"en": "The Ife Bronze Heads: When Art Became Evidence of Civilization", "yo": "The Ife Bronze Heads: When Art Became Evidence of Civilization"}, "desc": {"en": "The Ife bronze heads that stunned Frobenius and proved an African civilization.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Itan Oba", "secondary": ["Itan Oba", "Orisa"], "source_note": "", "segments": [{"id": "undecided-ife-bronze-heads#s1", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Narrator opens: In 1938, German archaeologist Leo Frobenius was traveling through Nigeria when he encountered something that stopped him cold. In Ile-Ife, in the grounds of the palace of the Ooni, workers had dug up a set of bronze and terracotta heads so refined, so naturalistic, so technically perfect that Frobenius refused to believe they had been made by Africans. He claimed, in writing, that they must have come from ancient Greece. He invented a mythology — that Ife must be the lost continent of Atlantis. He was wrong. He was embarrassingly, offensively wrong. But he was not wrong about the magnificence of the objects themselves."}, {"id": "undecided-ife-bronze-heads#s2", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "The Ife bronze heads — some say there are over two hundred of them, created between the 12th and 15th centuries CE — represent the pinnacle of lost-wax bronze casting in the entire world at that time. Using a technique called cire perdue, Yoruba artists in Ile-Ife created portraits of such stunning anatomical accuracy that when modern artists and metallurgists study them, they shake their heads in wonder. The thin-walled bronze castings of Ife are thinner than almost anything European smiths were producing at the same period. The faces are serene. The eyes are knowing. The mouths are closed in the quiet dignity of permanence."}, {"id": "undecided-ife-bronze-heads#s3", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "These are not decorative objects. These are coronation objects. They represent the Ooni of Ife — the divine king — and are used in funerary and enthronement ceremonies. Some of the heads show the elaborate bead work that Yoruba kings wear. Some show the tribal marks of Ife royalty. Each one is a portrait. Each one is a name. Each one is a memory made into metal. The question that haunted Europeans when they first encountered these works was: how did they get here? How did people in medieval West Africa develop a bronze-casting technique of this sophistication?"}, {"id": "undecided-ife-bronze-heads#s4", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "The answer is: they developed it themselves. The Yoruba are a people who have always worked metal — iron, copper, bronze. The guild of smiths in Ife had centuries to perfect their craft. They had patron kings who demanded excellence. They had a spiritual framework — Ogun, the Orisa of iron, demands that metalwork be treated as sacred. And so they built, slowly, generation by generation, a tradition of metalworking that produced objects now displayed in the British Museum, the Metropolitan Museum of Art, and the National Commission for Museums in Nigeria. When Frobenius's notes were published in Europe in the early 20th century, they caused a sensation — but also a reckoning. European scholars could not explain Ife by their theories of African incapacity. So they did what they often do: they invented an external origin. Atlantis. Ancient Greece. Even the Phoenicians."}, {"id": "undecided-ife-bronze-heads#s5", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "The Yoruba did not need these explanations. They knew the truth. Their grandfathers' grandfathers made these things. The guild records — the oral histories, the palace genealogies — confirm it. The Ife heads were made in Ife. By Ife people. For Ife kings. In the name of Oduduwa and under the protection of Ogun. Today, one of the Ife bronze heads — the head of an Ooni — sits at the center of international controversy. Fourteen of the original bronzes were taken out of Nigeria during the colonial period and are held in British and American institutions. The Nigerian government has been asking for their return for decades. The conversation continues. But the conversation itself is proof: these objects matter. They matter to scholars, to collectors, to governments — and most of all, to the Yoruba people, for whom these faces are not artifacts but ancestors."}, {"id": "undecided-ife-bronze-heads#s6", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "Look at the Ife bronze head. Look at the serene eyes, the full lips, the elegant proportions. That is a Yoruba king. That is a Yoruba face. That is 700 years of looking back at you from across time. Behold what we made. Behold who we are."}]}, {"id": "undecided-great-walls-of-benin", "emoji": "👑", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#1a0a00,#4a2800)", "category": "itan-oba", "rating": 9.4, "duration": "4 min", "title": {"en": "The Great Walls of Benin: Engineering the Ancient World", "yo": "The Great Walls of Benin: Engineering the Ancient World"}, "desc": {"en": "The great earthworks of Benin, engineering of the ancient world.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Itan Oba", "secondary": ["Itan Oba"], "source_note": "", "segments": [{"id": "undecided-great-walls-of-benin#s1", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Narrator opens: The Great Wall of China is famous. The Great Wall of Benin is largely unknown outside of specialists — but by some measures, it was larger. A series of earthworks — ditches and ramparts — that surrounded the Benin Kingdom and its surrounding territories, the walls of Benin stretched for an estimated 16,000 kilometers in total length. The New Scientist magazine in 1974 described them as \"the world's largest earthworks carried out prior to the mechanical era.\" These walls were not built in a day or a generation. They were constructed over centuries, beginning around the 13th century CE and continuing to expand until the 19th century. Different sections were built by different Obas for different purposes — some as boundaries, some as military fortifications, some as flood control, some as markers of territory."}, {"id": "undecided-great-walls-of-benin#s2", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "The moat that surrounded the inner city of Benin — the royal capital — was some 11 meters wide and 17 meters deep in some sections. It was maintained by a system of drainage and reinforcement that reflected sophisticated hydraulic engineering. The inner walls of the palace compound were themselves works of extraordinary skill. The people who built these walls were not using advanced machinery. They were using human hands, baskets, and the knowledge of engineering that had been accumulated over generations. The Benin Kingdom organized labor through a system of guilds and royal obligation — the king could call upon communities to contribute labor to public works projects, and those projects included not just walls but roads, markets, and the maintenance of the palace bronzes."}, {"id": "undecided-great-walls-of-benin#s3", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "When British troops entered Benin City in 1897 — burning the city, seizing the bronzes, executing senior chiefs, and ending the kingdom's independence — they encountered a city of significant size and sophistication. They also encountered an earthwork system that confused them: they had not expected such a scale of construction from a West African kingdom. The Benin walls were largely destroyed during and after the British conquest — leveled for roads, dismantled for building materials, overgrown during the colonial period. What remains today is fragmentary, though archaeological surveys continue to map and document what was there. But the walls existed. The moats were real. The engineering was genuine. And the people who built them were the same people who cast the bronze plaques now sitting in British museums — people of vision, of organization, of technical mastery."}, {"id": "undecided-great-walls-of-benin#s4", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "Remember the walls of Benin when someone tells you that Africa had no great architectural traditions. The walls existed before most European medieval cities were founded. They stretched for thousands of kilometers. They enclosed a civilization."}]}, {"id": "undecided-languages-of-yoruba", "emoji": "📖", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#0a1a00,#1a3a00)", "category": "iwe", "rating": 9.4, "duration": "4 min", "title": {"en": "The Languages of Yoruba: How a Tongue Became a Civilization", "yo": "The Languages of Yoruba: How a Tongue Became a Civilization"}, "desc": {"en": "How the Yoruba tongue became a civilization.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Folktales / Literary", "secondary": ["Folktales / Literary", "Diaspora"], "source_note": "", "segments": [{"id": "undecided-languages-of-yoruba#s1", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Narrator opens: Every language is a way of being alive. Every language encodes a philosophy, a set of values, a particular way of parsing the world. And the Yoruba language — spoken by perhaps 50 million people across Nigeria, Benin, Togo, and the diaspora — is one of the great instruments of human civilization. Yoruba is a tonal language. Three tones — high, low, and mid — each carried on vowels, can entirely change the meaning of a word. The word \"owo\" with a high tone means money. With a mid-high pattern it means hand. With a low tone it means respect. Getting the tone wrong doesn't just create an error — it creates a different meaning entirely. Yoruba speakers develop an ear for these distinctions from infancy, and speakers who grow up without Yoruba often find the tonal system one of the most challenging aspects of the language to master."}, {"id": "undecided-languages-of-yoruba#s2", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "But Yoruba is not just a spoken language. It is also a literary language with an extraordinary poetic tradition. The orikì — praise poetry — is one of the most sophisticated verbal art forms on earth. Every family, every town, every Orisa, every notable individual has an orikì — a poem of identification and invocation that compresses history, genealogy, praise, and aspiration into a dense, rhythmically complex verbal form that can be chanted or sung. When you hear an orikì chanted by a skilled practitioner, it is like hearing a living encyclopedia — names and deeds and places compressed into lines that expand when you know the context and remain beautiful even when you don't."}, {"id": "undecided-languages-of-yoruba#s3", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "Yoruba also has one of the most complex number systems in the world. Unlike most languages that count additively (twenty-one = twenty plus one), Yoruba uses a subtractive system at key points. Forty-five is not \"forty plus five\" — it is \"five from fifty.\" Seventy is not \"sixty plus ten\" — it is \"ten from four-twenties.\" This vigesimal (base-20) system with its subtractive principle is found in very few languages globally, and linguists have studied it as a window into the cognitive history of the Yoruba people."}, {"id": "undecided-languages-of-yoruba#s4", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "The Yoruba proverb tradition is equally rich. The Yoruba say: \"Owe l'esin oro; bi oro ba sonu, owe la fi n wa a\" — Proverb is the horse of speech; when speech is lost, it is proverb we use to find it. Yoruba proverbs are not decorative ornaments of speech. They are compressed wisdom — centuries of communal thinking distilled into sentences that can be deployed with devastating precision in argument, in comfort, in teaching, in mourning. Samuel Ajayi Crowther wrote the first Yoruba grammar in 1843 and the first Yoruba dictionary in 1843 as well. The Yoruba Bible followed. The Yoruba novel would eventually emerge — with D.O. Fagunwa's Ogboju Ode Ninu Igbo Irunmale (Forest of a Thousand Daemons) published in 1938, the first novel written in an indigenous African language to be widely recognized."}, {"id": "undecided-languages-of-yoruba#s5", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "Today, Yoruba is taught in universities around the world. There are Yoruba language apps. There are Yoruba podcasts. There are Yoruba-language films. And in Cuba, in Brazil, in the United States — in every place where the Yoruba diaspora preserved its spiritual life — the Yoruba language survived in fragments, in sacred songs, in prayer formulas that kept a thread back to the source even when the source was an ocean away. Speak Yoruba. Or learn it. Or at minimum, honor it — because in that tonal complexity, in those subtractive numbers, in those praise poems and proverbs, is a record of a people's way of thinking, feeling, and being alive. The language is the civilization. And the civilization is not lost as long as the language is spoken."}]}, {"id": "undecided-seven-wonders-of-ife", "emoji": "⚡", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#150020,#350050)", "category": "orisa", "rating": 9.4, "duration": "4 min", "title": {"en": "The Seven Wonders of Ile-Ife: A Pilgrim's Guide", "yo": "Ile-Ife"}, "desc": {"en": "A pilgrim's guide to seven sacred places of Ile-Ife.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Orisa", "secondary": ["Orisa", "Itan Oba"], "source_note": "", "segments": [{"id": "undecided-seven-wonders-of-ife#s1", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Narrator opens: If you were to make a pilgrimage — a spiritual, historical journey to the very roots of Yoruba civilization — you would go to Ile-Ife. And in Ile-Ife, you would find seven places that are not just historical sites but living coordinates of identity. The first is the Palace of the Ooni — Ile-Ife's spiritual heart. The Ooni's palace is not simply a residence; it is a sacred geography, each room and courtyard associated with different Orisas, different royal rituals, different moments in the city's history. The palace holds some of the original Ife bronzes, kept in sacred preservation. To enter the inner courts is to enter time itself."}, {"id": "undecided-seven-wonders-of-ife#s2", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "The second is the Staff of Oranmiyan — the great granite obelisk that marks the place where Oranmiyan stood and declared himself. The staff is covered in iron nails driven in over centuries — each nail a prayer, a wish, a dedication from a worshiper. It stands in a grove, attended by its own priests, silent and immovable, a finger pointing at heaven. The third is the Osun Grove at Oshun — a smaller but equally ancient sacred space dedicated to the river goddess, different from the Osogbo grove but connected to it through the same spiritual lineage. The fourth is the Museum of Natural History in Ile-Ife — which, while modern in its institution, holds some of the most important archaeological finds from the city, including examples of the bronze and terracotta heads that have made Ife famous worldwide."}, {"id": "undecided-seven-wonders-of-ife#s3", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "The fifth is the Opa Moremi — the statue of Moremi Ajasoro, 42 feet of bronze raised in memory of the woman who saved the city. At dawn and dusk, the light catches the metal and the figure appears to glow. The sixth is the Igbo Oluorogbo — the grove where Moremi's son Oluorogbo is said to have been sacrificed. It is a place of profound grief and profound peace, where you feel the weight of what love is capable of costing. The seventh is the river Esimirin — the river where Moremi made her vow. Small and quiet now, bordered by farmland and houses, it still runs. And if you stand at its edge and listen, they say you can still hear the sound of a woman's promise, still flowing with the current."}, {"id": "undecided-seven-wonders-of-ife#s4", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "These are not tourist attractions. They are altars. They are memory made into landscape. They are the Yoruba people's way of saying: we were here, and here, and here — and we are still here. Visit them with respect. Visit them with silence. Visit them knowing that you are standing inside a story that is still being told."}]}, {"id": "undecided-ile-ife-centre-of-world", "emoji": "⚡", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#150020,#350050)", "category": "orisa", "rating": 9.4, "duration": "4 min", "title": {"en": "Ile-Ife: The City at the Center of the World", "yo": "Ile-Ife"}, "desc": {"en": "Ile-Ife, the city believed to be where the world began.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Orisa", "secondary": ["Orisa", "Itan Oba"], "source_note": "", "segments": [{"id": "undecided-ile-ife-centre-of-world#s1", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Narrator opens: There are cities built on rivers, built on trade routes, built on military strategy, built on the accident of geography. And then there is Ile-Ife — a city built on something none of those other cities can claim: the conviction, held unbroken for at least a millennium, that this is the place where the world began. Ile-Ife — the full name means \"House of Expansion\" or \"the place where the land widened\" — is located in present-day Osun State, in the forests of southwestern Nigeria. By any external measure, it is a mid-sized traditional city: important, respected, historical, but not the largest or wealthiest or most powerful in the region. By the internal measure of the Yoruba world — and of the diaspora civilizations that trace their origin to the Yoruba — it is the center of everything."}, {"id": "undecided-ile-ife-centre-of-world#s2", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "The tradition is specific: Oduduwa descended from heaven here. The first land was created here. The first Oba was crowned here. The first bronze was cast here. The beaded crown — the Ade Ileke that marks legitimate Yoruba kingship across the entire Yoruba world — originated here and is blessed here. When any of the sixteen principal Yoruba kings — the Obas of Ife, Oyo, Ekiti, Ondo, Ijesa, and others — are crowned, they receive their sacred regalia with a connection to Ile-Ife. The city is the source of the legitimizing claim."}, {"id": "undecided-ile-ife-centre-of-world#s3", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "The archaeological evidence supports, if not the theology, then at least the antiquity and the significance. The Ile-Ife bronzes — discovered by German archaeologist Leo Frobenius in 1910-1911 and causing a sensation in the European archaeological world that refused to believe such technical sophistication could have originated in sub-Saharan Africa — date from approximately the 12th-15th centuries CE. They depict human heads, mostly, with a naturalism and technical mastery that stands among the greatest achievements in the history of world sculpture. The lost-wax casting technique used to create them was not taught to the Ife artists by anyone from outside — it developed here, independently, reaching a level of refinement that the European world did not match until centuries later."}, {"id": "undecided-ile-ife-centre-of-world#s4", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "These bronzes were portraits — portraits of Obas and senior figures, objects of veneration and memory. They were not made for export or for the entertainment of foreign visitors. They were made for Ile-Ife's own spiritual life, its own way of holding the memory of its rulers, its own aesthetics of sacred representation. Ile-Ife today is a place of pilgrimage — for Yoruba people from across Nigeria, for diaspora communities from Brazil, Cuba, and the United States who trace their spiritual heritage to this city and who come to touch the earth their ancestors came from. The Ooni's palace, the sacred groves, the Opa Oranmiyan, the Olokun Grove, the Ore Grove — these sites carry the weight of centuries of continuous veneration."}, {"id": "undecided-ile-ife-centre-of-world#s5", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "In the Yoruba world, legitimacy flows from Ife. Power can be built anywhere — in Ibadan, in Oyo, in Lagos. But the deepest authority, the one that the cosmos recognizes — that flows only from where the first land was created. Ile-Ife. The city at the center of the world."}]}, {"id": "undecided-iseyin", "emoji": "👑", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#1a0a00,#4a2800)", "category": "itan-oba", "rating": 9.4, "duration": "4 min", "title": {"en": "The Sacred City of Iseyin: Cloth, Prayer and Kings", "yo": "Iseyin"}, "desc": {"en": "Iseyin: cloth, prayer, and kings.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Itan Oba", "secondary": ["Itan Oba"], "source_note": "", "segments": [{"id": "undecided-iseyin#s1", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Narrator opens: Iseyin is a city of looms and kings. It sits in the savanna country of what is now Oyo State, and it has been producing Aso-Oke — the handwoven cloth of Yoruba ceremony — for as long as anyone can remember. The men of Iseyin sit at their floor looms in long sheds, their feet working the pedals, their hands throwing the shuttle, producing strips of cloth that will be sewn together into garments worn at the most important moments of Yoruba life: marriage, chieftaincy, birth, burial."}, {"id": "undecided-iseyin#s2", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "Aso-Oke is not merely clothing. It is identity. The different patterns — Sanyan (the off-white or beige silk cloth), Etu (the dark navy blue), Ofi (the shiny white) — each carry specific social meanings. Sanyan is associated with antiquity, with the original Yoruba, with conservative dignity. Etu was historically associated with warriors and is now worn at funerals as well as celebrations. Ofi is the bright, festive cloth of celebration, worn by the new bride, by the jubilant, by those who have reason to shine. Iseyin's Oba — the Aseyin — is one of the senior kings of Oyo State, and his court preserves the textile traditions alongside the political ones. The palace of Iseyin is a place where you can still hear the sound of weaving — the rhythmic clatter of the loom — as background music to the affairs of state."}, {"id": "undecided-iseyin#s3", "scene": "Ìtàn Ọba", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "The significance of Iseyin extends beyond its economic role. In a Yoruba world where what you wear announces who you are — where cloth is the medium through which social transitions are marked — the city that produces the cloth holds a particular kind of cultural power. Iseyin's weavers are not artisans in the secondary sense. They are makers of the visual language through which Yoruba society communicates itself to itself. And the sacred dimension is never absent. Weaving in Iseyin has its own Orisa dimension — there are deities of craft, of thread, of the loom's precision. There are prayers said before beginning certain commissions. There are rituals that mark the completion of a particularly significant piece. Cloth and prayer. Kings and thread. In Iseyin, these things are not separate."}]}, {"id": "undecided-yoruba-and-portuguese", "emoji": "⛓️", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#1a1308,#3a2a10)", "category": "itan-eru", "rating": 9.4, "duration": "4 min", "title": {"en": "Yoruba and the Portuguese: The First European Encounter", "yo": "Yoruba and the Portuguese: The First European Encounter"}, "desc": {"en": "The first encounter between the Yoruba and the Portuguese.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Itan Eru", "secondary": ["Itan Eru", "Nigeria History"], "source_note": "", "segments": [{"id": "undecided-yoruba-and-portuguese#s1", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Narrator opens: Long before the British built their colonial apparatus, before the French drew lines on maps, before the word \"Nigeria\" existed — the Portuguese arrived on the West African coast and began the contact that would reshape the world. The Yoruba world's first sustained encounter with European civilization was with Portugal, and it left marks on Yoruba culture that are still visible five centuries later."}, {"id": "undecided-yoruba-and-portuguese#s2", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "The Portuguese reached the West African coast in the 1470s, establishing trading relationships with the coastal Yoruba and Edo communities along the Bight of Benin. The Benin Kingdom — already the dominant power in the region — received Portuguese ambassadors, traded with Portuguese merchants, and commissioned Portuguese craftsmen to teach their metalworkers specific casting techniques. The famous brass memorial heads of Benin, which reach their artistic peak in the period following Portuguese contact, show the influence of this exchange: the cross-hatching technique that appears on some of the most celebrated bronzes was a Portuguese introduction."}, {"id": "undecided-yoruba-and-portuguese#s3", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "Portuguese became the first European language of commerce along the West African coast. The pidgin Portuguese that developed in the trading ports of the Bight of Benin was the first version of what would eventually become a series of West African lingua franca — including the Krio of Sierra Leone and the various English and French-based pidgins of the region today. The Yoruba communities of the coast absorbed Portuguese words, some of which persist in Yoruba vocabulary: \"aguda\" (from \"Ajuda\"), the term for the Brazilian returnee community, is one example; specific Portuguese loan words in the names of foods and items of trade are others."}, {"id": "undecided-yoruba-and-portuguese#s4", "scene": "Ìtàn Ẹrú", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "The Portuguese also began the Atlantic slave trade from the West African coast — not to the Americas initially, but to Portugal itself and to the Portuguese Atlantic islands. The West African slave trade that would eventually consume millions of people began with small-scale Portuguese trading in the fifteenth century and expanded to catastrophic scale as the Americas were colonized and the demand for plantation labor became insatiable. The relationship between Yoruba people and Portugal was the first chapter of the European encounter — a chapter that contained within it, in miniature, everything that would follow: trade and cultural exchange alongside exploitation and violence; gifts of knowledge alongside the seed of the slave trade; the first European curiosity about Yoruba civilization alongside the beginning of the process that would nearly destroy it."}]}, {"id": "undecided-yoruba-mathematics", "emoji": "📖", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#0a1a00,#1a3a00)", "category": "iwe", "rating": 9.4, "duration": "4 min", "title": {"en": "Yoruba Mathematics: The World's Most Complex Number System", "yo": "Yoruba Mathematics: The World's Most Complex Number System"}, "desc": {"en": "Yoruba counting in base twenty, one of the world's most complex number systems.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Folktales / Literary", "secondary": ["Folktales / Literary"], "source_note": "", "segments": [{"id": "undecided-yoruba-mathematics#s1", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Narrator opens: When scholars of mathematics first systematically studied the Yoruba number system in the 20th century, many were astonished. Not because the system was primitive or simple — quite the opposite. They were astonished because the Yoruba number system is one of the most cognitively sophisticated and mathematically elegant number systems ever developed by any human community. The key feature that distinguishes Yoruba numbers from most other systems in the world is its use of subtraction as the primary computational operation for numbers in the tens and above. In most numeral systems — English, Arabic, Chinese, Igbo — numbers are built additively: twenty-one is twenty plus one, forty-five is forty plus five. Simple addition. Building up."}, {"id": "undecided-yoruba-mathematics#s2", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "Yoruba goes the other way. In Yoruba, 45 is not \"forty plus five.\" It is \"five from fifty\" (aadoota in Yoruba). 35 is not \"thirty plus five\" — it is \"five from forty\" (aadota). 70 is not \"sixty plus ten\" — it is \"ten from four-twenties\" (aadorin). 195 is not \"one hundred ninety-five\" — it is \"five from two hundred\" minus several nested subtractions. This vigesimal (base-20) system with its subtractive principle is found in very few languages worldwide. The other notable example is traditional Welsh, which uses some vigesimal elements. But the Yoruba system is more fully developed, more consistently applied, and more mathematically sophisticated than any other naturally evolved number system."}, {"id": "undecided-yoruba-mathematics#s3", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "What does this mean? It means the Yoruba number system requires the speaker to think forward from the next round number and then subtract — to approach each number by first identifying the nearest large round number above it and then calculating the difference. This is not a simple task. It requires a kind of mathematical flexibility — a willingness to work backward as well as forward — that mathematical psychologists have found genuinely interesting. Studies have been conducted comparing the arithmetic performance of Yoruba-speaking children with children speaking other languages. The results suggest that the Yoruba number system, despite its complexity, does not disadvantage Yoruba-speaking children in arithmetic — and in some cognitive domains related to number sense, it may provide advantages."}, {"id": "undecided-yoruba-mathematics#s4", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "The Yoruba number system also has specific words for mathematical operations — the vocabulary of the system is not simply adapted from general language but has terms specifically evolved for numerical computation. This suggests that Yoruba numerical practice has a long history of elaboration and refinement, passed down through generations as a specialized skill. Cowrie shells — the currency of pre-colonial Yoruba commerce — were counted in the Yoruba system. The great traders of Yorubaland, the market women and merchants who moved goods across thousands of kilometers, calculated in Yoruba numbers. The accounts were kept in this system. The mathematical literacy required for large-scale commerce in pre-colonial Yorubaland was substantial."}, {"id": "undecided-yoruba-mathematics#s5", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "When British colonial administrators arrived and introduced the British system of weights, measures, and currency, they were replacing not a numerically unsophisticated tradition but an extremely sophisticated one. The transition required Yoruba traders to essentially become bilingual in numerical systems — maintaining the Yoruba system for community commerce while learning the British system for colonial trade. Today, the Yoruba number system is under pressure from the dominance of the Hindu-Arabic numeral system that has become global. Young Yoruba people increasingly count in the additive system of the global standard and may not be fluent in the traditional subtractive system. Linguists and educators are working to document and preserve the traditional system."}, {"id": "undecided-yoruba-mathematics#s6", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "It would be a genuine cultural and mathematical loss if it disappeared. The Yoruba number system is not merely a curiosity — it is evidence of the intellectual depth of a civilization that worked out one of the most cognitively challenging number systems ever devised, and then used it for centuries of commerce, administration, and everyday life. Count on it. The Yoruba counted."}]}, {"id": "undecided-adire", "emoji": "👸", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#2a0018,#500030)", "category": "obinrin", "rating": 9.4, "duration": "4 min", "title": {"en": "Adire: The Cloth That Carries History", "yo": "Adire"}, "desc": {"en": "Adire, the indigo cloth that carries Yoruba history.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Great Yoruba Women", "secondary": ["Great Yoruba Women"], "source_note": "", "segments": [{"id": "undecided-adire#s1", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Narrator opens: There is a cloth that is made only in one way, only by Yoruba hands, only through a tradition that goes back centuries. It is called Adire — and it is not simply fabric. It is a visual language, a medium through which Yoruba women have expressed their aesthetic sense, their cultural knowledge, and their individual creativity for as long as anyone can remember. Adire means \"tied and dyed\" in Yoruba. It is a resist-dyeing technique: patterns are created on cloth by tying, sewing, painting, or stitching specific areas of the fabric before it is submerged in dye — typically indigo dye, which gives Adire its characteristic deep blue-black color. The tied or painted areas resist the dye and emerge as lighter patterns against the dark ground."}, {"id": "undecided-adire#s2", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "The technique is ancient — resist dyeing is found across West Africa and Asia, and the fundamental method is not unique to Yoruba culture. But what is uniquely Yoruba is the specific repertoire of patterns, the specific techniques, and the specific cultural meanings associated with specific designs. There are two main types of Adire. Adire eleko uses cassava paste (lafun) as a resist — the paste is painted onto the cloth through a zinc template or with a feather, creating geometric patterns and traditional motifs. Adire oniko uses tying and pleating to create circular and abstract resist patterns in the cloth before dyeing."}, {"id": "undecided-adire#s3", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "The patterns of Adire are not random. Many of them have names and meanings. Ibadandun (Ibadan is sweet) is a pattern associated with the city. Egba-osi (left-handed Egba) refers to a specific traditional design from Abeokuta. Olokun refers to patterns associated with the sea deity. Kuta (stone or pebble) describes a certain dotted pattern. Each name encodes a history — of place, of personality, of cultural reference. Adire was traditionally made by women. The dyeing process was a female occupation, connected to the economic independence of women traders, to the female guild systems of Yoruba society, and to the aesthetic traditions passed from mother to daughter. A skilled Adire maker had knowledge of chemistry (the mordants and fixatives that made the dye fast), of design (the patterns and their variations), and of commerce (how to market her cloth to best advantage)."}, {"id": "undecided-adire#s4", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "The major center of Adire production is Abeokuta — the Egba capital. In Abeokuta, the Adire tradition has been maintained through generations, and Egba Adire is considered among the finest. The Ogun State Arts and Crafts Cooperative and various NGOs working on craft preservation have been supporting Adire makers, and the cloth has found new markets internationally as interest in African textiles has grown. Contemporary fashion designers — particularly Nigerian and diaspora designers — have incorporated Adire into modern fashion in ways that honor its heritage while making it contemporary. The cloth appears on international runways. It appears in the collections of Lagos's most innovative designers. It appears in the cultural identity clothing of Yoruba events worldwide."}, {"id": "undecided-adire#s5", "scene": "Obìnrin", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "But the tradition faces challenges: the indigo dye that gives traditional Adire its characteristic color has been largely replaced by synthetic dyes, which are easier to obtain and use but produce a somewhat different result. The traditional knowledge of natural dye preparation is becoming rare. Adire. The cloth that carries history in its patterns. The visual record of Yoruba aesthetic knowledge, pressed into fabric, worn against skin, passed down through hands. Wear it. Honor the hands that made it. Know what it means."}]}, {"id": "undecided-yoruba-film-industry", "emoji": "🌟", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#00201a,#003a30)", "category": "legends", "rating": 9.4, "duration": "4 min", "title": {"en": "The Yoruba Film Industry: Nollywood's Yoruba Roots", "yo": "The Yoruba Film Industry: Nollywood's Yoruba Roots"}, "desc": {"en": "Nollywood's Yoruba roots: the Yoruba film industry.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Living Legends", "secondary": ["Living Legends"], "source_note": "", "segments": [{"id": "undecided-yoruba-film-industry#s1", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Narrator opens: Before \"Nollywood\" became a global brand — before the Nigerian film industry became the third largest in the world by volume, before Netflix was buying Nigerian content, before the world discovered that Africans had been telling themselves stories on screen for decades — there was the Yoruba traveling theatre tradition. And from that tradition came the first generation of Nigerian filmmakers, who carried the Yoruba story tradition into the new medium of film with the urgency of people who understood that stories were not entertainment but survival."}, {"id": "undecided-yoruba-film-industry#s2", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "The Yoruba traveling theatre companies of the 1950s and 1960s — particularly the companies of Hubert Ogunde, Kola Ogunmola, and Duro Ladipo — were the immediate ancestors of Yoruba cinema. These companies toured Yorubaland and beyond with theatrical productions that drew on Yoruba oral tradition, Yoruba music, and Yoruba social commentary. They performed in markets, in community centers, in any space that could hold an audience. They were professional storytellers in the oldest sense — dependent on the audience's attendance and attention for their survival."}, {"id": "undecided-yoruba-film-industry#s3", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "Hubert Ogunde — \"the father of Nigerian theatre\" — was the first to make the transition to film. His productions in the 1970s and 1980s were among the first Nigerian feature films to reach mass audiences. They were in Yoruba. They told Yoruba stories. They featured the performance styles of the Yoruba theatrical tradition. They were wildly popular and commercially successful in ways that the foreign-produced films they competed with could not match — because they were speaking directly to their audience in the audience's own language and the audience's own stories."}, {"id": "undecided-yoruba-film-industry#s4", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "The home video revolution of the late 1980s and early 1990s transformed everything. When the cost of production dropped — when consumer-grade video cameras became accessible to filmmakers who could not afford 35mm film — the dam broke. Yoruba filmmakers flooded the market. Hundreds of Yoruba-language films were produced each year, distributed on VHS cassettes through the same market networks that had always distributed goods in Yorubaland. They were cheap, fast, sometimes rough in production values, and genuinely beloved by audiences who saw their own lives on screen for the first time. The stories they told: palace intrigue, Oba succession disputes, witchcraft accusations, romantic comedies, religious conversion dramas, crime and punishment, the conflict between tradition and modernity. The Orisa appeared on screen. The Egungun danced through scenes. The Iyalode wielded her political influence. The market woman schemed and triumphed and fell and rose again."}, {"id": "undecided-yoruba-film-industry#s5", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "This was Yoruba storytelling in its most contemporary form — not a heritage performance for tourists or a scholarly text for academics, but mass entertainment for millions of people who wanted to see themselves in stories that made sense of their world. Nollywood today is a vast industry, increasingly international, increasingly English-language, increasingly sophisticated in production values. But its Yoruba roots are still visible in the stories it tells most naturally, in the characters who appear most fully realized, in the cultural logic that shapes even its most commercially calculated products. The griot told stories. The theatre company performed them. The filmmaker put them on screen. The platform streams them to the world. The story continues."}]}, {"id": "undecided-unnamed-influences", "emoji": "🌟", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#00201a,#003a30)", "category": "legends", "rating": 9.4, "duration": "4 min", "title": {"en": "Chimamanda’s Aunt: The Unnamed Influences", "yo": "Chimamanda’s Aunt: The Unnamed Influences"}, "desc": {"en": "The unnamed aunts, teachers, and elders behind every celebrated life.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Living Legends", "secondary": ["Living Legends"], "source_note": "", "segments": [{"id": "undecided-unnamed-influences#s1", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Every famous person is the tip of a network of unnamed influences. Behind every celebrated Yoruba intellectual, artist, or leader, there are mothers, aunts, teachers, grandmothers, neighbours, and community elders who said something at the right moment or modelled something at the right stage or simply made the young person feel that their particular kind of intelligence was worth developing. This story is not about"}, {"id": "undecided-unnamed-influences#s2", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "a specific person. It is about the category — the unnamed Yoruba women and men who are the upstream of the famous, who will not appear in any biography but without whom the biography would be a different, lesser story. The aunt who gave the library card. The teacher who stayed after school. The grandfather who told the stories that became the texture of the imagination."}, {"id": "undecided-unnamed-influences#s3", "scene": "Alààyè", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "Naming the famous is necessary. Remembering that fame is always collective is equally necessary. The Yoruba tradition of the oriki — the praise poetry that situates a person within their lineage, naming ancestors backward through time — understands this. You are not just yourself. You are the sum of everyone who made you possible. That sum includes people whose names the world will never know."}]}, {"id": "undecided-repatriation-question", "emoji": "🌍", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#001520,#003550)", "category": "diaspora", "rating": 9.4, "duration": "4 min", "title": {"en": "The Repatriation Question: Who Owns Yoruba Art?", "yo": "The Repatriation Question: Who Owns Yoruba Art?"}, "desc": {"en": "Who owns Yoruba art held in foreign museums?", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Diaspora", "secondary": ["Diaspora", "Itan Oba"], "source_note": "", "segments": [{"id": "undecided-repatriation-question#s1", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "In museums across Europe and North America, there are thousands of objects of Yoruba origin: bronze heads from Ife, brass works from Benin, carved wooden masquerade headdresses, ceremonial objects of every kind. They arrived through different routes — some through colonial looting (the 1897 British Punitive Expedition against Benin is the most infamous), some through"}, {"id": "undecided-repatriation-question#s2", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "colonial-era “collection,” some through purchase from impoverished communities during crisis periods. The repatriation conversation has been moving slowly for decades and somewhat faster in recent years. Germany has returned some Benin bronzes. The Smithsonian is in negotiation. The British Museum’s position remains complicated. Nigeria has built a new museum complex partly in anticipation of objects returning."}, {"id": "undecided-repatriation-question#s3", "scene": "Diaspora", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "The Yoruba community’s position is not monolithic. There are those who want everything back immediately. There are those who argue that the most important thing is access — that objects in well-funded European museums reach more eyes than they would in underfunded Nigerian institutions. There are those who say the framing of the debate is wrong: the question is not where the objects are, it is who has the right to decide where they go. That last position is probably correct."}]}, {"id": "undecided-yoruba-mosque", "emoji": "🏛️", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#001a0a,#003a1a)", "category": "naija", "rating": 9.3, "duration": "4 min", "title": {"en": "The Yoruba Mosque: Islam in Yorubaland and Beyond", "yo": "The Yoruba Mosque: Islam in Yorubaland and Beyond"}, "desc": {"en": "Islam in Yorubaland and beyond.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Nigeria History", "secondary": ["Nigeria History", "Diaspora"], "source_note": "", "segments": [{"id": "undecided-yoruba-mosque#s1", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "The popular image of Yorubaland as a place of orisa worship and Christianity misses something important: a significant proportion of Yoruba people are Muslim, and have been for centuries. Islam arrived in Yorubaland through the northern trade routes in the eighteenth century, and in some Yoruba communities — particularly in Ilorin and northern Oyo — it became the dominant religion well before the civil wars"}, {"id": "undecided-yoruba-mosque#s2", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "of the nineteenth century. The Yoruba Muslim community developed its own relationship with the tradition: maintaining some practices — Islamic naming ceremonies, Quranic education, the observance of Ramadan — while integrating them into Yoruba social structures. A Yoruba Muslim wedding, for instance, is recognisably Islamic and also recognisably Yoruba: the food, the music (carefully managed), the role of the families, the way elders are honoured."}, {"id": "undecided-yoruba-mosque#s3", "scene": "Nàìjíríà", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "In the diaspora, Yoruba Muslims occupy an interesting position — Muslim in a community that is associated in the outside world primarily with orisa religion and Christianity; Yoruba in a Muslim community that often treats African Islam with some condescension. They navigate both identity pressures with the pragmatic sophistication that Yoruba culture has always applied to impossible situations."}]}, {"id": "undecided-adunni-olorisa", "emoji": "⚡", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#150020,#350050)", "category": "orisa", "rating": 9.4, "duration": "4 min", "title": {"en": "Adunni Olorisa: When an Austrian Woman Became Yoruba", "yo": "Adunni Olorisa"}, "desc": {"en": "Susanne Wenger, the Austrian artist who became an Osun priestess and restored the Osun-Osogbo grove.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Orisa", "secondary": ["Orisa", "Diaspora"], "source_note": "", "segments": [{"id": "undecided-adunni-olorisa#s1", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Susanne Wenger arrived in Nigeria in 1950 with her then-husband, the linguist Ulli Beier. She came as an artist, a European modernist interested in African aesthetics. She stayed for the rest of her life, which lasted until 2009. She died at the age of ninety-three, having been initiated into the Oshun priesthood in the 1950s and having spent five decades restoring and creating sacred artworks in the"}, {"id": "undecided-adunni-olorisa#s2", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "Osun-Osogbo Grove. She was a strange figure in a strange position: a white European woman who had genuinely converted to a Yoruba religious tradition, who had genuinely been accepted by the priests of that tradition, who had made work in that tradition that the tradition itself recognised as valid. The Yoruba community of Osogbo gave her the name Adunni Olorisa — one who is beloved by the orisa."}, {"id": "undecided-adunni-olorisa#s3", "scene": "Òrìṣà", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "The debate about her legacy is unresolved and probably irresolvable. Was she an appropriator? A devoted servant of the tradition? An artist using African sacred space for her own purposes? A genuine practitioner? The honest answer is: all of these things, in different proportions at different times. The grove remains. The sculptures remain. The river keeps flowing. Life is not always tidy."}]}, {"id": "folk-eegun-alare", "emoji": "📖", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#0a1a00,#1a3a00)", "category": "iwe", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "7 min", "title": {"en": "Eégún Aláré: The Masquerade Who Danced Himself Into an Antelope", "yo": "Eégún Aláré"}, "desc": {"en": "Two masquerade performers in Òkèhò, bound by friendship and then by betrayal, learn that the incantations that make an àláré great can also unmake the man who forgets his limits.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Folktales / Literary", "secondary": [], "source_note": "Retelling of the novel 'Eégún Aláré' by Láwuyì Ògúnníran, a classic of Yoruba performance literature.", "segments": [{"id": "folk-eegun-alare#s1", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Gather close, my children, for tonight we enter the world of the àláré, the travelling masquerade whose feet never tire and whose cloth hides more than a face. In the town of Òkèhò there lived two men who had learned this art side by side, Ọ̀jẹ́ Lárìnnàká and Dáṣọfúnjó. They had begun as boys at the same drum, clapping out the rhythms before they could carry the heavy costume, and as men they still shared the same road, the same lodging, and the same hunger, for the coins a masquerade earns at one festival are rarely enough to carry two households to the next."}, {"id": "folk-eegun-alare#s2", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "You must understand that to be an àláré is not simply to dance. A great masquerade is a scholar of hidden things. He knows the dance, yes, and he knows the disguise, the charm sewn into the hem, the herb crushed into the dye of the cloth. But above all he knows the ọfọ̀, the incantation, the word of power that can bend what the eye sees. A lesser performer entertains. A master performer makes the crowd doubt its own eyes, makes them swear they saw a man step behind a curtain and a leopard step out. And where there is such power, my children, there is always the temptation to turn it against a rival instead of lifting it up for the people."}, {"id": "folk-eegun-alare#s3", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Hunger set the story moving, as hunger so often does. Lárìnnàká, unable to feed his household from the thin takings of his own town, resolved to travel far in search of richer audiences, to carry his art to distant festivals where the purses were heavier. But he was a married man, and his wife Ìyádùnńní was heavy with child, too near her time to walk the long roads at his side. So he did what a man does when he trusts a friend above every other: he placed Ìyádùnńní in the keeping of Dáṣọfúnjó, charged him to guard her as a brother would, and went on his way believing his home was safe behind him."}, {"id": "folk-eegun-alare#s4", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "A home left in another man's hands, my children, is a door left on the latch. In Lárìnnàká's long absence the ordinary closeness between a guardian and the woman he guarded grew warm, and then grew into something it had no right to become. Proximity did its patient work. The meals shared, the errands run, the child that came and needed a man's hand nearby, all of it wove a closeness that honour should have forbidden. When Lárìnnàká at last returned, his purse fuller but his years older, he found that the friendship he had trusted with the dearest thing he owned had quietly turned against him in his own house."}, {"id": "folk-eegun-alare#s5", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "Now a lesser story would make this a quarrel of fists, and it would end in the market square. But these were masqueraders, men of the cloth and the incantation, and such men do not settle their quarrels the way farmers do. The betrayal poisoned the friendship, and the poisoned friendship became a rivalry, and the rivalry sought the only arena these men truly respected: the contest of the masquerade, where each would call upon the incantations he had gathered across a lifetime, and the crowd and the drums would judge whose power ran deeper."}, {"id": "folk-eegun-alare#s6", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "So the duel was set, and the whole country came to watch, for the fame of these performers had spread, and there is nothing a people love so much as to see two masters test one another before the elders. The drums climbed. The cloth spun. Each man reached deeper into his store of hidden words than he had ever reached in mere performance, for this was no performance; this was a reckoning dressed as a festival. And it was here, at the summit of the contest, that the famed Ọ̀jẹ́làdé, whose gift for changing shape had carried his name from town to town, reached for the deepest word he owned and turned himself, before the eyes of the whole gathering, into an antelope, swift and trembling and beautiful, to prove that no rival alive could match the reach of his art."}, {"id": "folk-eegun-alare#s7", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "But here is the terror that sits at the very centre of our tale, my children, and you must hold it close, for it is the whole lesson. The incantation that turns a man into a beast must also carry the word that turns him back, and a word of power is a jealous and a slippery servant. In the heat and the pride of the contest, something broke. The returning word was lost, or mispoken, or snatched away, and the antelope that had been a proud and famous man could not find the road back into its own body. The crowd that had gathered to be amazed stood instead in a terrible silence, watching a human life vanish into the shape it had so cleverly borrowed."}, {"id": "folk-eegun-alare#s8", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "So ends the dance, and so begins the wisdom that the elders of Òkèhò have never allowed their children to forget. Power over appearances is the most seductive power of all, for it lets a man seem to be whatever he wishes to seem, and the one who can seem anything soon begins to believe he owes the truth nothing. But the man who lives by changing his shape may wake one morning and no longer remember which shape was truly his own. Ambition that feeds upon rivalry, friendship that curdles into betrayal, and sacred knowledge spent to defeat a neighbour rather than to serve the town, these are the three roads, and all three of them met at the body of that antelope."}, {"id": "folk-eegun-alare#s9", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "Remember it, then, when you watch the egúngún spin at the festival and the drums drive the dancers toward their frenzy. The cloth is beautiful and the feet are quick and the incantations are real, realer than the town children know. But the greatest àláré is not the one who can become an antelope to shame a rival. The greatest àláré is the one who knows, even at the dizzy height of his power, with the whole country roaring his name, exactly when to remain a man."}]}, {"id": "folk-o-le-ku", "emoji": "📖", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#0a1a00,#1a3a00)", "category": "iwe", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "6 min", "title": {"en": "Ò Le Kú: The Love That Could Not Be Killed", "yo": "Ò Le Kú"}, "desc": {"en": "A story of young love among students in which affection, jealousy, family expectation and the pressure to choose too soon braid together into joy and sorrow that neither lover can simply walk away from.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Folktales / Literary", "secondary": [], "source_note": "Retelling of the novel 'Ò Le Kú' by Akínwùnmí Ìṣọ̀lá, one of the most beloved works of modern Yoruba fiction.", "segments": [{"id": "folk-o-le-ku#s1", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Come close, my children, because not every story is about distant kings or forests full of spirits. Some of the hardest journeys a person ever walks are the journeys of the heart, taken not through strange countries but through the ordinary rooms of ordinary houses. Tonight I tell you of young people in our own towns, in the age of schools and examinations and new freedoms, whose love was so strong that the very title of their story was left as a question hanging in the air: ò le kú? Can a thing like this ever truly die?"}, {"id": "folk-o-le-ku#s2", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "At the centre of it stands a young man named Àjàní, quick of mind and warm of feeling, the sort of young man whom others are drawn to without quite knowing why. And there stands a young woman named Àsàkẹ́, whose beauty and whose spirit turned his whole world around a single point. Their affection began the way such things always begin, with glances held a heartbeat too long, with small kindnesses, with the laughter that two people share and no one else quite understands. And like a stream after the first rains, it grew larger than either of them had planned."}, {"id": "folk-o-le-ku#s3", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "But Àjàní was young, my children, and the young heart is generous to a dangerous fault. His feelings did not rest on Àsàkẹ́ alone. For there was also Lọlá, and there was Sàdé, and around these four young people the story weaves its tightening net. What might have been the simple sweetness of one love became a tangle of affections pulling in several directions at once. Each relationship carried its own tenderness, its own small history, its own quiet claim upon him. And Àjàní, rather than choosing with a clear and honest heart, allowed himself to be loved by more than one, and told himself that he loved more than one in return."}, {"id": "folk-o-le-ku#s4", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "You already know, you who have lived even a little, that such a path cannot stay sweet for long. Jealousy entered the garden where there had been only laughter. A word spoken warmly in one house was carried, cooled and sharpened, into another. A glance meant for one was seen by another. The very affection that had made these young people glow began, slowly, to burn them, because a heart that is divided among many can never give to any single one of them the whole of what its warmth seemed to promise."}, {"id": "folk-o-le-ku#s5", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "And love among the young, my children, is never in our land a private matter between two people. Behind every courting couple stand two whole families, each with its hopes and its fears, its sense of what is proper, its firm idea of whom a daughter or a son ought to marry and whom they ought to leave well alone. Marriage, in our tradition, is the joining of houses, not merely the joining of two racing hearts. And so the elders had their opinions, spoken and unspoken, and those opinions pressed upon the young lovers from every side, heavier than the young ever expect such things to be."}, {"id": "folk-o-le-ku#s6", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "So the lovers found themselves caught between two fires, and this is the true shape of their suffering. On the one side burned the feeling in their own chests, urgent, impatient, certain of itself, demanding to be answered now and all at once. On the other side stood the great weight of family and community and custom, saying wait, saying consider, saying this one is suitable and that one is not, saying there is time, when to the young it never feels as though there is time. And between those two fires, decisions were taken in haste, in the heat of a single evening, that the slow years would never afterward allow to be unmade."}, {"id": "folk-o-le-ku#s7", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "The sorrow of the tale is not that anyone in it was wicked. No, my children, that is precisely what makes it ache so, and why our people have loved it so long. There is no villain here to hate and be done with. These were good young people, full of life and genuine feeling, no crueller than you or I. Their undoing was smaller and more familiar than wickedness: they let emotion race ahead of wisdom, and they let the pressure of a single moment settle matters that should have been weighed slowly, with patience, with counsel, in the cool of many mornings rather than the heat of one night."}, {"id": "folk-o-le-ku#s8", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "By the time clarity finally came to them, as clarity always comes, a little late and a little cruel, some of the doors had already swung shut. A love that was real, that neither of them had invented or imagined, found itself standing on the wrong side of those closed doors, able to see through the crack but no longer able to pass. And that, my children, is why the storyteller refused to give the tale a tidy ending and named it instead with a question, Ò Le Kú, the love that will not die."}, {"id": "folk-o-le-ku#s9", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "For even when circumstance defeats it, even when the lovers are driven apart by their own hasty choices and by the firm hands of their houses, the feeling itself refuses to lie down and be buried. It lingers. It aches in the quiet. It asks its question into the dark long after the lamps are out. Learn from them, then, you who are young and whose hearts are strong. Let your love be deep, but do not let it run so fast that your wisdom cannot keep its feet beneath it, for the choices you make in the heat of youth are the very house in which you will have to live when you are old and the heat has cooled."}]}, {"id": "folk-atoto-arere", "emoji": "📖", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#0a1a00,#1a3a00)", "category": "iwe", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "7 min", "title": {"en": "Atótó Arére: The Long Road to the Gallows", "yo": "Atótó Arére"}, "desc": {"en": "Told backward from the shadow of the gallows, the story traces how a broken home, poverty and bad company carry a boy named Àlàbá from small thefts into the kind of crime from which there is no return.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Folktales / Literary", "secondary": [], "source_note": "Retelling in the tradition of Oládẹ̀jọ Òkédìjí's Yoruba crime fiction; a tale of how society and the individual together shape a criminal life.", "segments": [{"id": "folk-atoto-arere#s1", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Come close, my children, because this story begins where most stories are content to end, at the very foot of the gallows. A man stands there condemned, the rope already measured for his neck, and the only honest way to understand how he came to stand in that terrible place is to turn around and walk the road backward with him, step by patient step, from the noose all the way back to the cradle. And at every turning of that backward road we will stop and ask the same hard question: was this the moment, right here, when it could still have gone otherwise?"}, {"id": "folk-atoto-arere#s2", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "His troubles began, as so very many do, inside a broken home. A child needs shelter not only from the rain but from the whole hard world, and when the home that ought to be his first shelter is itself in pieces, the child is pushed out into that world long before he is ready, unguarded and unschooled. So it was with Àlàbá. He grew up without the steady hand that teaches a boy, patiently and a hundred times over, the difference between what is his and what belongs to another. And he was hungry, often and sharply hungry, and hunger, my children, is a loud and a persuasive teacher, and a very poor one."}, {"id": "folk-atoto-arere#s3", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "He ran toward the city the way the young everywhere run toward a far light, believing with his whole chest that the city would hand him opportunity simply for arriving. But the city gave him something quite different from what he had imagined. It gave him poverty with a dozen new faces. It gave him petty crime with experienced teachers. And it gave him companions who had already learned, long before he met them, how to survive in a place that had never once troubled to protect them. Chief among these companions was Saminu, and the friendship of Àlàbá and Saminu became the very engine that drove the tale toward its gallows."}, {"id": "folk-atoto-arere#s4", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "At the first, the crimes were small, the small and almost pardonable crimes of the hungry and the overlooked, a thing lifted here, a lie told there, a door left unwatched. But crime, my children, is a staircase, and it is a staircase that runs only downward, and every single step a man takes upon it makes the next step easier and more ordinary to take. What begins as the desperate shift of a hungry boy hardens, over enough seasons, into a trade, a craft, a way of being in the world. The boy who first stole because his belly was empty becomes at last the man who steals because stealing is simply who he has become."}, {"id": "folk-atoto-arere#s5", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "Now the storyteller is too honest a man to let us comfort ourselves with easy judgement, and this is the hard gift of the tale. He will not permit us to sit back and say, simply, that Àlàbá was a wicked boy and deserved his rope, and so wash our hands. But neither will he permit us the other easy comfort, the one the soft-hearted reach for, of saying that society alone is guilty and the man himself bears nothing. He holds the two truths up together, one in each hand, and he makes us feel the full weight of both at once until our arms ache with them."}, {"id": "folk-atoto-arere#s6", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "For it is true, and we must not look away from it, that the broken home and the indifferent city did their patient, grinding work upon the boy. And it is just as true, and we must not look away from this either, that at every single crossroad along that downward road, Àlàbá also chose, and that his choices, however narrowed by hunger and by company, were still in the end his own. Responsibility, in this tale, is not one clean thing held by one guilty hand. It is shared out among many, and it is heavy, and no one who touches the story walks away from it with clean hands, neither the man, nor the city, nor, if we are honest, we who watched and did nothing."}, {"id": "folk-atoto-arere#s7", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "That is the hard wisdom of the backward road. Walking it with open eyes, we pass a dozen places where a different hand laid on the boy's shoulder, a kinder word spoken at the right hour, a steadier home, a single teacher who noticed, might have turned him gently aside before the staircase steepened. And we pass, just as surely, a dozen other places where the boy himself stood at a fork and could have stopped and chosen the harder, better road, and did not. The tale will not let us keep only the half of the truth that flatters whatever we already believed when we sat down to listen."}, {"id": "folk-atoto-arere#s8", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "So we arrive once more at the gallows where we began, but now, having walked the whole road, we understand it, and understanding is a heavier thing than judgement. The rope is not truly the start of the story and not truly its end. It is the final knot in a long cord that was being braided, strand by strand, from the boy's first unprotected and unfed morning. And the storyteller leaves us standing there with a question sharper than any blade the executioner carries: how many Àlàbás are walking the streets of our own towns tonight, at this very hour, still only upon the lower steps, still close enough to the cradle to be turned back, if only someone would trouble to reach out a hand?"}, {"id": "folk-atoto-arere#s9", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "Hear it, my children, and carry it home. A child left unguarded is a debt, and it is the whole town that is one day handed the bill to pay. We cannot by ourselves mend every broken home, and the storyteller does not pretend that we can. But each of us can refuse, at least, to be the indifferent city. A kind hand offered to a boy early, when it still costs us only a little, is cheaper by far than a gallows built for a man late, when it costs us all so much."}]}, {"id": "folk-okele-akobu", "emoji": "📖", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#0a1a00,#1a3a00)", "category": "iwe", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "7 min", "title": {"en": "Òkèlè Àkóbù: The First Morsel and the Blood Oath", "yo": "Òkèlè Àkóbù"}, "desc": {"en": "Two young lovers seal their promise to marry with a blood covenant, but examinations, ambition, a new relationship and migration pull their lives in different directions until the oath of their youth returns to be reckoned with.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Folktales / Literary", "secondary": [], "source_note": "Retelling of 'Òkèlè Àkóbù' by Kẹhìndé Adépégbà, a modern Yoruba novel of young love and the weight of promises.", "segments": [{"id": "folk-okele-akobu#s1", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "My children, when two young people stand together and promise to marry, they believe, with the whole innocent strength of the young, that tomorrow will wear exactly the same face as today. They cannot yet imagine that they themselves will change, that the years will reach into their very chests and rearrange what they want. Olúọyè and Ọmọ́ládé believed this so completely, so fiercely, that an ordinary spoken promise was not enough to hold the weight of their certainty. They sealed it instead with blood, cutting a covenant between them, swearing that neither would ever take another, that their marriage was already written in the book of the future and was only waiting for the slow years to arrive and read it aloud."}, {"id": "folk-okele-akobu#s2", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "They were students when they swore it, and that, my children, is the very first thing you must take note of, for the whole tale turns upon it. A covenant is a grave and a binding thing, a chain forged to be worn for a lifetime. And they forged this one before they had lived even a tenth of that lifetime, before either of them had the faintest true idea of what the years would ask of them, of what temptations and what hungers and what wider worlds would one day come walking toward them, or of who they themselves would slowly, inevitably become."}, {"id": "folk-okele-akobu#s3", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Then the roads diverged beneath their feet, as roads will do, quietly and without announcement. Olúọyè passed his examinations and entered the great university, and the university swung open before him a far wider world than the one in which the covenant had been sworn, a world of wider people, wider talk, wider ambitions that had not existed for him before. Ọmọ́ládé, meanwhile, struggled with her books, and the examinations that lifted him left her behind, and she turned instead to the honourable and patient trade of the needle, learning to be a tailor. The two had sworn one and the same oath upon one and the same afternoon, but life had now set them in two different rivers, and the rivers were flowing apart."}, {"id": "folk-okele-akobu#s4", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "And there was a child, my children, for these things do not stay in the realm of promises. Ọmọ́ládé became a mother and bore Bídèmí, Olúọyè's own son, a small living knot that tied the two of them together even as their circumstances pulled steadily at the rope from opposite ends. A child, you must understand, is a covenant that breathes and cries and grows. Whatever the two parents might one day decide about each other, Bídèmí would stand forever as the breathing proof that the oath had once been wholly real, that the blood they had mingled had been serious blood."}, {"id": "folk-okele-akobu#s5", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "At the university Olúọyè's world grew crowded with new faces, new possibilities, new measures of what a successful man's life ought to look like. And a young man surrounded on every side by shining possibility does not always keep faith with a promise cut into his arm in a dusty schoolyard years before. A new relationship entered his life, bright and uncomplicated by the past. And ambition began its quiet whispering, as ambition does, telling him that the woman of his student days, the tailor who sat bent over her needle with his child at her side, belonged to a smaller and poorer version of himself that he was now rising up and leaving behind. Migration and advancement called him forward and away, away from the schoolyard, away from the covenant, away from the home where his son was learning to walk."}, {"id": "folk-okele-akobu#s6", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "But a blood oath, my children, is not so cheaply outrun as a young and rising man would like to believe. What is sworn in blood does not simply dissolve and wash away because one of the two who swore it has grown ambitious and acquired finer friends. The promise waits. It is patient in a way the young cannot fathom. The child grows taller and begins to ask his questions, the questions that have no comfortable answers. The woman remembers the knife and the words and the afternoon. And the man who told himself he had left his first morsel far behind him discovers, as the years turn, that the first morsel is precisely the one the body never forgets, the one that sits in the stomach of the memory long after every later meal is digested and gone."}, {"id": "folk-okele-akobu#s7", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "The storyteller sets before us, in the shape of these two lovers, the oldest tension of a changing world, the tension that each of you will feel in your own turn: the deep pull between the place you came from and the place you are straining to go. Education is a good thing, and ambition is a good thing, and no honest elder will stand before you and tell you to refuse them or to stay small out of fear. But a person who climbs the ladder by setting his foot upon his own sworn word arrives at the top of it carrying a debt that no title and no salary and no fine new house can ever quite cancel, a debt that waits for him in the quiet, wearing the face of a child."}, {"id": "folk-okele-akobu#s8", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "So consider well the covenants you make, my children, and make them slowly, and make fewer of them than your racing hearts would like, for a promise is a seed, and you will most surely eat its fruit one day, whether that fruit has ripened sweet or bitter. And consider, too, with equal care, the promises that others have made to you in the trusting, open-handed days of their youth, for to break faith with someone lightly, as though their trust weighed nothing, is to teach the whole watching world exactly what your own word is worth. Òkèlè àkóbù, the first morsel, sits in the belly long after the mouth has moved on, cheerfully, to richer and to later food."}]}, {"id": "folk-aja-lo-leru", "emoji": "📖", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#0a1a00,#1a3a00)", "category": "iwe", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "7 min", "title": {"en": "Àjà Ló Lẹ́rù: The Loft Bears the Burden", "yo": "Àjà Ló Lẹ́rù"}, "desc": {"en": "A retired policeman named Lápàdé cannot keep clear of a case of stolen money that widens into kidnapping and organized crime, and his hard-won knowledge of how criminals think becomes both his weapon and the thing that makes others suspect him.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Folktales / Literary", "secondary": [], "source_note": "Retelling of the detective novel 'Àjà Ló Lẹ́rù' by Oládẹ̀jọ Òkédìjí, a landmark of Yoruba crime fiction.", "segments": [{"id": "folk-aja-lo-leru#s1", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Come close, my children, for tonight the tale is not of spirits loose in the forest but of men loose in the city, and of one man in particular who had earned, and dearly wanted, nothing more than a little rest. His name was Lápàdé, and he had given the best and hardest years of his life to the police before at last laying down the badge in the hope of a quieter road. But listen well, for here is the first thing the tale knows that Lápàdé did not: a man who has spent a lifetime learning precisely how criminals think does not easily unlearn it, and trouble, when it comes strolling past the door of such a man, has a particular way of pausing to knock."}, {"id": "folk-aja-lo-leru#s2", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "The proverb that gives this story its name carries its secret folded up inside it, the way our proverbs always do. Àjà ló lẹ́rù, the elders say: it is the loft that bears the burden. For in the old houses, whatever is precious or whatever is shameful is carried up and hidden in the rafters, in the dim loft above the heads of all who live and talk below. And so it is, the storyteller means, with wrongdoing. What looks, down on the open floor of the house, like a small and isolated matter, has its true weight stored quietly out of sight, up above, waiting in the dark for someone who knows to climb up and look."}, {"id": "folk-aja-lo-leru#s3", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "It began, as these things so often do, with stolen money, the kind of theft at which a busy town might merely shrug and call it one man's misfortune. But Lápàdé's trained eye, even in its retirement, saw the fine threads running outward from that small theft, and when his old curiosity got the better of his longing for rest and he pulled gently upon one of those threads, the whole small matter began to unravel into something larger and far darker. The stolen money connected to a kidnapping. The kidnapping connected to the trade in forbidden goods. And all of it connected to a network of people who had organized their wickedness as carefully and as patiently as honest men organize a business, with its own ranks and its own silences."}, {"id": "folk-aja-lo-leru#s4", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "He did not walk into that darkness alone, for even a sharp man needs a companion who knows the back streets. He took up with a man named Tàfà Ìgìrìpà, and the two of them moved together through the underside of the city, reading the small signs that ordinary, comfortable eyes slide right past without seeing, a face that did not belong, a sum of money that did not add up, a story told a little too smoothly. Lápàdé's great gift was that he could sit quite still in his own chair and reconstruct in his mind how a thing had been done, and by whom, and for what reason, because he had spent decades living close inside the minds of exactly such men, learning their fears and their habits as a hunter learns the trails of game."}, {"id": "folk-aja-lo-leru#s5", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "But here, my children, is the double edge of that remarkable gift, and the storyteller sharpens it deliberately against us. The very same deep knowledge that made Lápàdé so useful also made him a suspect. For how, the comfortable began to murmur, does an honest man come to understand criminals quite so perfectly, quite so intimately, unless some shadowed part of himself lives close to them, unless he has walked further down their roads than he admits? The police who ought to have welcomed his help began instead to glance sideways at him and to wonder whether he knew rather more than an innocent man had any business knowing. And so the hunter found himself quietly watched by the very people he had come out of retirement to help."}, {"id": "folk-aja-lo-leru#s6", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "This, my children, is the particular loneliness of the one who sees clearly in a crooked world, and you would do well to remember it, for you will meet it. He is too honest by far for the criminals, who cannot buy him and therefore fear him. And he is too knowing, too comfortable in the dark, for the respectable, who cannot quite trust a man who understands the thing they are afraid of. He walks along a narrow ridge with a steep fall waiting on either hand, and he must do his difficult work while that work is doubted, must solve the crime while also proving, with every single step he takes, that he is not himself a secret thread in it."}, {"id": "folk-aja-lo-leru#s7", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "In the end, the patient reading of the threads did its quiet work, as patience generally will when it is joined to a clear eye. The hidden network was drawn out at last into the daylight. The people who had sat so comfortably behind the crimes were named and were made to stand and answer. And a kidnapped girl, who is never for one moment forgotten in the telling, was carried home at last to the arms of her own people. The burden that had been hidden all along up in the loft was brought down, and set on the floor, and weighed in the sight of everyone, and those who had carried it up there in the night were made to shoulder it now in the light."}, {"id": "folk-aja-lo-leru#s8", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "Remember the proverb, then, my children, long after you have forgotten the turns of the plot. What is hidden up in the loft does not thereby vanish; it only waits, and it grows no lighter for the waiting. And remember Lápàdé, who teaches us a harder thing: that a deep understanding of evil is a tool, and not by itself a verdict, and that the one who grasps the shape of wickedness most fully may be, for that very reason, the one most fiercely determined to drag it down and end it. To know the darkness through and through is not at all the same as to belong to it. Sometimes, my children, it is the only road by which the darkness can finally be driven out of the house."}]}, {"id": "folk-agbalagba-akan", "emoji": "📖", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#0a1a00,#1a3a00)", "category": "iwe", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "7 min", "title": {"en": "Agbalagba Akàn: The Old Crab Who Knew the Current", "yo": "Agbalagba Akàn"}, "desc": {"en": "Through the image of the old crab that has survived every tide, this crime story examines people who learn to move through society by cunning and concealment, and warns that cleverness without character turns survival into destruction.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Folktales / Literary", "secondary": [], "source_note": "Retelling of the crime novel 'Agbalagba Akàn' by Oládẹ̀jọ Òkédìjí, part of his acclaimed body of Yoruba detective fiction.", "segments": [{"id": "folk-agbalagba-akan#s1", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Come close, my children, and consider for a moment the crab. Watch him there at the shifting edge of the water, where the tide comes and goes and nothing soft survives for long. He has endured where gentler creatures were plucked up and swept away, and he has endured for a reason: he has learned the current, learned the exact angle of the hiding place, learned to move sideways and quick when the straight and honest road lies open and watched. The agbalagba akàn, the old crab, the one who has outlasted a hundred tides, is a true master of survival. And tonight's story asks a hard and searching question about the men and women who have learned to live their whole lives exactly as that old crab lives his."}, {"id": "folk-agbalagba-akan#s2", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "For there are such people in every city under the sun, my children, and you will meet them, and some of them will smile at you very warmly. They have learned to move through the whole of society precisely as the crab moves through the shallows, by cunning, by concealment, by cold and constant calculation. They read which way the tide of favour and of danger is running, and they slide crabwise across it. They hold out one friendly claw to the watching world and keep the other, the sharp one, tucked carefully out of sight behind them. And for a long season, just like the crab, they prosper and survive, exactly where more honest and less watchful people come quietly to grief."}, {"id": "folk-agbalagba-akan#s3", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Now the storyteller, working in the hard and clear-eyed tradition of the detective tale, refuses to show us wrongdoing as a single wicked deed done by a single wicked heart that we may safely hate from a distance. He shows it to us instead as something that grows up out of an environment, the way the crab itself grows up out of the mud and the salt and the restless tide. The people in this story are shaped, hour by hour and hunger by hunger, by the hard shallows in which they were set down to live, and their cunning is, at the very first, nothing worse than the shell a soft and vulnerable creature grows across its back in order not to be cracked open and eaten by the next thing that passes."}, {"id": "folk-agbalagba-akan#s4", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "But watch closely now, my children, watch what happens as the tale slowly turns, for the turning is the whole of the matter. The cunning that began its life honestly, as a shield against a hard world, hardens by use into a sword, and the sword begins to look for something to cut. The calculation that began as simple caution, as the prudence of the weak, ripens into a cold way of using other people as footholds. The concealment that began as mere self-protection curdles quietly into deceit, into the pleasure of the lie well told. Step by small step, none of them felt as a great fall at the time, the skill of merely surviving hardens into the settled practice of doing harm, and the old crab, so very clever at the business of living, grows dangerous to everyone who must share his pool of water."}, {"id": "folk-agbalagba-akan#s5", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "Against such a creature, my children, the law itself must learn to grow patient and watchful and crabwise. It cannot catch the old crab by charging straight at him down the open road, for that is precisely the road he is watching. It must learn the current as he has learned it, must wait, still and unblinking, at the hiding place, must be willing to move sideways. And so the pursuit at the heart of this tale becomes a long contest of intelligence, the cunning of the wrongdoer set against the cunning of the one who would stop him, each of them reading the same water, each of them edging sideways, until at the last it is the old crab's own cleverness, the very gift he trusted above all else, that carries him one step too far and folds the net shut around him."}, {"id": "folk-agbalagba-akan#s6", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "And there, my children, lies the hard heart of the whole warning, so hear it plainly. Cleverness is a gift of the gods, a real and a shining gift, and I will not stand here and tell you to despise it or to pretend you do not possess it. But it is a gift that arrives without a conscience fastened to it. It will serve a good purpose and a wicked one with precisely equal willingness and equal skill, for cleverness itself does not know, and does not care, which of the two it is being asked to serve. The knowing of that difference lives somewhere else entirely, in the thing our elders named ìwà, character. Cleverness chooses only the how of a thing. It is character alone, and never cleverness, that chooses the whether."}, {"id": "folk-agbalagba-akan#s7", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "So the old crab who knew every current in the water and every hiding place along the bank is undone in the end, and mark well that he is not undone by a cleverer crab, for there was always going to be a cleverer crab somewhere. He is undone by the one flaw that no amount of cleverness can ever reach down and repair: he had skill without character, cunning without ìwà, and so his lifelong gift for survival turned at last, quietly and inevitably, into the engine of his own destruction. He had saved himself, nimbly and proudly, from every small danger the tide ever sent against him, and he could not, in the end, save himself from the one danger that had been inside his own shell all along."}, {"id": "folk-agbalagba-akan#s8", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "Learn it well, and learn it early. You will meet a great many clever people as you go through your life, and you will be tempted, as the young always are, to envy them, for cleverness glitters and is quick and wins the first rounds. But train yourself to ask the second question, the one the glitter is designed to make you forget. Clever toward what end? Cunning placed at the service of what? A sharp and nimble mind set upon a crooked spine will carry a person very far and very fast, and then, one ordinary afternoon, it will carry him clean off a cliff. Be clever, my children, if the gods have shaped you so. But let it be ìwà, and never cunning, that keeps its hand upon the tiller of your life."}]}, {"id": "folk-jagua-jagua", "emoji": "📖", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#0a1a00,#1a3a00)", "category": "iwe", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "7 min", "title": {"en": "Jàgùà Jàgùà: The Hunt for Quick Glory", "yo": "Jàgùà Jàgùà"}, "desc": {"en": "A satirical tale of a forceful, predatory ambition set loose in a world that increasingly measures people by money and appearance, and of how the fastest road upward is so often the one that costs a person their character.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Folktales / Literary", "secondary": [], "source_note": "Retelling in the satirical vein of Akínwùnmí Ìṣọ̀lá's social fiction; a study of ambition, deception and the clash of tradition and modern aspiration.", "segments": [{"id": "folk-jagua-jagua#s1", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Come close, my children, and let me tell you tonight of a hunger that goes about wearing a very fine name. Jàgùà Jàgùà, the people called it, after the sleek and forceful and predatory thing that prowls the bush and simply takes whatever it has decided it wants. It is the name they gave to a certain kind of ambition, one that has learned to treat the whole of human society as a bush to be hunted through, where wealth and status and advantage are the fat game trembling in the grass, and where the cleverest and the most ruthless hunter is the one who eats best and sleeps soundest."}, {"id": "folk-jagua-jagua#s2", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "The world through which this story moves is one that you, my children, will recognize all too easily, for in very many ways it is simply our own, only drawn a little sharper so that we can see it. It is a world that has begun, quietly and then not so quietly, to measure a person not by character or by wisdom or by a lifetime of patient service, but by money, by influence, by the cut of the cloth on his back and the shine of the shoe on his foot. And in such a world, a person who burns to rise, and to rise quickly, soon stumbles upon a bitter and a simple truth: that the fastest road leading upward is very, very rarely the honest one."}, {"id": "folk-jagua-jagua#s3", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Our hunter, then, is caught, as so many in our changing time are caught, standing between two different worlds with a foot uneasily in each. Behind him stand the old expectations, the slow and respectable paths that our fathers and their fathers walked, where a man built his good name the way a man builds a wall, one honest brick upon another across the whole length of a lifetime. And before him, gleaming and impatient, glitters the new world of the city and the school and the sudden fortune, forever whispering its promise that a clever person need not wait his turn in the long line at all, but may reach out now, today, this very afternoon, and seize his full portion."}, {"id": "folk-jagua-jagua#s4", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "Now education, and money, and the bright quick life of the modern town are not in themselves evil things, and the storyteller is far too wise and too fair to stand up and pretend to you that they are. But they arrive, every one of them, carrying new temptations cunningly dressed up as opportunities. The person who might have grown patient is told, on every side and by every voice he admires, that patience is a disease of fools. The person who might have stayed honest is shown a dozen dishonest men who have already arrived ahead of him, laughing, well fed, finely dressed, seated comfortably at the very table he is still standing outside of, and no lightning has struck a single one of them."}, {"id": "folk-jagua-jagua#s5", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "And so, as you already feel it must, deception becomes the chosen tool, picked up almost without a decision being made. Our ambitious one learns to wear one face in the crowded marketplace and to keep a quite different one folded away for private use. He learns to say the thing that pleases while planning the thing that profits, to climb lightly upon the trust of others the way a hunter climbs a leaning tree to reach the bird's nest, caring nothing for the tree. And for a season, my children, it works, exactly as such things always work for a season, and the quick glory he hunted comes trotting out of the grass toward him, and he feels himself vindicated."}, {"id": "folk-jagua-jagua#s6", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "But the storyteller, remember, is a satirist, and the particular gift of the satirist is to let us laugh at the hunter even as we watch him succeed, so that our laughter does half the teaching. For there is something deeply and comically foolish lodged at the very centre of all this glittering cleverness, something the predatory man, for all his sharpness, cannot see about his own self. He has grown so busy, so expert, so wholly absorbed in the hunting of other people that he has entirely forgotten one small fact: that he too lives down in the bush, that the bush keeps its own slow accounts and its own rough justice, and that a man who treats every neighbour he passes as prey will look up one ordinary morning and find that he has no neighbours left in all the world, only other hunters, circling, hungry, and eyeing him."}, {"id": "folk-jagua-jagua#s7", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "The humour of the tale, then, is the kind that leaves a small bruise behind after the laugh has faded, which is the only kind of humour our elders ever thought worth the breath. We laugh at the bold schemes and the borrowed finery; we laugh at the fine clothes bought on the strength of a trust that will shortly be betrayed; we laugh at the bold brass face worn over the empty purpose. And then, quite suddenly, the laughter catches and sticks in the throat, because in the predatory hunger we have been laughing at we recognize, with a start, a hunger of our own, the one we have felt on our own hard evenings, when the honest road looked impossibly long and the crooked one looked so very bright and so very near."}, {"id": "folk-jagua-jagua#s8", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "Hold the lesson, then, gently in one hand but firmly, for it is a double thing and both halves are true. Ambition is not your enemy, my children; hear that clearly, for the frightened and the lazy will tell you otherwise. A person wholly without ambition builds nothing, leaves nothing, and is no use to his people. But an ambition that has slipped its reins and learned to treat the whole world as its private hunting ground will, in the fullness of time, turn and devour the very one who first set it loose. So rise, my children. Rise as high as the gifts the gods gave you can possibly carry you. But rise as a builder standing among neighbours, and not as a jaguar crouched among prey, for the glory that is seized the quickest is, without a single exception I have ever seen, the glory that is lost the soonest."}]}, {"id": "folk-saworoide", "emoji": "📖", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#0a1a00,#1a3a00)", "category": "iwe", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "8 min", "title": {"en": "Ṣaworoidẹ: The Brass Bell and the King's Covenant", "yo": "Ṣaworoidẹ"}, "desc": {"en": "In the kingdom of Jogbo a king may not simply wear the crown; he must enter an ancient covenant sealed by the sacred brass bell Ṣaworoidẹ. When Lápité cheats the ritual and rules for himself, the old covenant returns as the instrument of his reckoning.", "yo": "Ní ìjọba Jọ̀gbọ̀, ọba kò lè kàn dé adé lásán; ó gbọ́dọ̀ wọ májẹ̀mú ìgbàanì tí a fi agogo idẹ mímọ́ Ṣaworoidẹ dì. Nígbà tí Lápité rẹ́ ààtò náà jẹ tí ó sì ń ṣàkóso fún ara rẹ̀, májẹ̀mú àtijọ́ padà wá gẹ́gẹ́ bí ohun èlò ìjíhìn rẹ̀."}, "yo_full": true, "primary_category": "Folktales / Literary", "secondary": [], "source_note": "Retelling of the story of 'Ṣaworoidẹ', written by Akínwùnmí Ìṣọ̀lá (famously filmed by Túndé Kẹ́lání); a parable of power bound by covenant.", "segments": [{"id": "folk-saworoide#s1", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Come close, my children, for in the kingdom of Jogbo a crown was never a simple thing to lift and to wear. In many lands a strong man seizes a throne, sets the beaded crown upon his own head, and commands everyone within reach of his voice to bow down and obey, and that is the whole of the matter. But it was not so, never so, in Jogbo. Before any man alive could rule in that place, he had first to go down and enter into an ancient covenant with the people themselves, and standing at the very centre of that covenant, old beyond the memory of the oldest, was a sacred brass bell that the people called the Ṣaworoidẹ.", "yo": "Ẹ súnmọ́ tòsí, ẹ̀yin ọmọ mi, nítorí ní ìjọba Jọ̀gbọ̀, adé kì í ṣe ohun tí à ń gbé dé lásán. Ní ọ̀pọ̀lọpọ̀ ilẹ̀, alágbára kan a gba ìtẹ́, kí ó dé adé ìlẹ̀kẹ̀ sí orí ara rẹ̀, kí ó sì pàṣẹ fún gbogbo ẹni tí ohùn rẹ̀ bá dé pé kí wọ́n foríbalẹ̀, bẹ́ẹ̀ sì ni gbogbo rẹ̀. Ṣùgbọ́n kì í ṣe bẹ́ẹ̀, kì í ṣe bẹ́ẹ̀ láé, ní Jọ̀gbọ̀. Kí ẹnikẹ́ni tó lè ṣàkóso níbẹ̀, ó gbọ́dọ̀ kọ́kọ́ sọ̀kalẹ̀ bá àwọn ènìyàn fúnra wọn dá májẹ̀mú ìgbàanì, àti ní àárín gbùngbùn májẹ̀mú náà, tí ó ti gbó ju ìrántí àgbàlagbà jùlọ lọ, ni agogo idẹ mímọ́ kan dúró, tí àwọn ènìyàn ń pè ní Ṣaworoidẹ."}, {"id": "folk-saworoide#s2", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "The meaning of the bell was as plain as it was heavy, and the people of Jogbo drank it in with their mothers' milk. Authority, said the bell, comes always bound and chained to responsibility, and the two may never be prised apart. The great crown of Jogbo carried within it powerful incantations, worked into its very beads and leather by the ancestors in the deep past, and in the binding ritual the body of the king himself was marked and joined to it, so that the throne, and the man who sat upon it, and the covenant beneath them both, became in that hour one single and inseparable thing. A true king of Jogbo could feel the wellbeing of his people in his own flesh, like warmth or like fever, and he could not profit from their suffering without the covenant itself turning, slowly and surely, against his own body.", "yo": "Ìtumọ̀ agogo náà ṣe kedere bí ó ṣe wúwo tó, àwọn ará Jọ̀gbọ̀ sì mu ú pẹ̀lú wàrà ìyá wọn. Àṣẹ, ni agogo náà wí, máa ń wá ní ìdè pẹ̀lú ojúṣe nígbà gbogbo, a kò sì lè ya àwọn méjèèjì sọ́tọ̀ láé. Adé ńlá Jọ̀gbọ̀ gbé àwọn ọfọ̀ alágbára sínú rẹ̀, tí àwọn baba ńlá fi ọgbọ́n kọ sínú ìlẹ̀kẹ̀ àti awọ rẹ̀ láti ìgbà pípẹ́, àti nínú ààtò ìdè náà, a fi àmì sí ara ọba fúnra rẹ̀ a sì so ó mọ́ adé, kí ìtẹ́, àti ọkùnrin tí ó jókòó sórí rẹ̀, àti májẹ̀mú tí ó wà lábẹ́ wọn, lè di ohun kan ṣoṣo tí a kò lè pín ní wákàtí náà. Ọba tòótọ́ ní Jọ̀gbọ̀ a máa rí ìlera àwọn ènìyàn rẹ̀ nínú ẹran-ara rẹ̀, bí ìgbóná tàbí bí ibà, kò sì lè jèrè nínú ìjìyà wọn láìjẹ́ pé májẹ̀mú náà á yíjú padà, pẹ̀lẹ́pẹ̀lẹ́ ṣùgbọ́n dájúdájú, sí ara rẹ̀."}, {"id": "folk-saworoide#s3", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Then, my children, came Lápité, who wanted the throne with his whole greedy heart but wanted nothing at all to do with the chains that held the throne fast to its duty. He desired, keenly, the power that the crown conferred, and he despised, utterly, the responsibility that the crown demanded in exchange. And so he did a terrible and a fateful thing: he cheated the sacred process itself. With the eager help of those who stood to grow fat at his side, he evaded the binding ritual, he slipped past the marking, he took up the office of king without ever truly entering the covenant beneath it, and he sat himself down upon the ancient throne of Jogbo as a king in name only, a man who had never once agreed, in his marked flesh, to be a king in duty.", "yo": "Nígbà náà, ẹ̀yin ọmọ mi, ni Lápité dé, ẹni tí ó fẹ́ ìtẹ́ pẹ̀lú gbogbo ọkàn oníwọra rẹ̀ ṣùgbọ́n tí kò fẹ́ nǹkan kan ṣe pẹ̀lú ẹ̀wọ̀n tí ó dì ìtẹ́ mọ́ ojúṣe rẹ̀. Ó ṣe àfẹ́rí, gidigidi, agbára tí adé ń fúnni, ó sì kórìíra, pátápátá, ojúṣe tí adé ń béèrè ní ìpadàbọ̀. Nítorí náà ó ṣe ohun burúkú tí ó sì kún fún ìparí: ó rẹ́ ààtò mímọ́ fúnra rẹ̀ jẹ. Pẹ̀lú ìrànlọ́wọ́ onítara àwọn tí yóò sanra lẹ́gbẹ̀ẹ́ rẹ̀, ó yẹra fún ààtò ìdè náà, ó yọ́ kọjá àmì-sísàmì náà, ó gba ipò ọba láìsí wíwọ májẹ̀mú tí ó wà lábẹ́ rẹ̀ ní tòótọ́, ó sì jókòó sórí ìtẹ́ ìgbàanì Jọ̀gbọ̀ gẹ́gẹ́ bí ọba lórúkọ nìkan, ọkùnrin tí kò tí ì gbà rí, nínú ẹran-ara rẹ̀ tí a sàmì sí, láti jẹ ọba nínú ojúṣe."}, {"id": "folk-saworoide#s4", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "And he surrounded himself at once, as such rulers always and everywhere do, with people who fed greedily upon his corruption and who learned to call that feeding loyalty. Around Lápité there gathered the hungry chiefs, the crooked traders, the hired strongmen, all of them men who understood very well the one thing their master had made possible: that a king without a covenant is simply a great door left standing open in the night, and that behind such an open door the accumulated wealth of a whole people can be carried quietly out, basket by basket, and shared around a private fire among friends while the people sleep.", "yo": "Ó sì yí ara rẹ̀ ká lẹ́sẹ̀kẹsẹ̀, gẹ́gẹ́ bí irú àwọn alákòóso bẹ́ẹ̀ ṣe máa ń ṣe nígbà gbogbo àti níbi gbogbo, pẹ̀lú àwọn ènìyàn tí wọ́n ń fi ìwọra jẹun lórí ìbàjẹ́ rẹ̀ tí wọ́n sì kọ́ láti pe jíjẹun náà ní ìdúró ṣinṣin. Yí Lápité ká ni àwọn ìjòyè oníwọra, àwọn oníṣòwò arúkèrúdò, àti àwọn alágbára-ọ̀wọ́, gbogbo wọn jẹ́ ènìyàn tí wọ́n lóye ohun kan ṣoṣo tí ọ̀gá wọn ti mú ṣeé ṣe: pé ọba láìsí májẹ̀mú dàbí ìlẹ̀kùn ńlá tí a fi sílẹ̀ ní ṣíṣí ní òru, àti pé lẹ́yìn irú ìlẹ̀kùn tí ó ṣí bẹ́ẹ̀, a lè gbé ọrọ̀ tí a kójọ ti gbogbo ènìyàn jáde ní ìdákẹ́jẹ́ẹ́, apẹ̀rẹ̀ kan lẹ́yìn apẹ̀rẹ̀, kí a sì pín in yí iná ìkọ̀kọ̀ ká láàrín àwọn ọ̀rẹ́ nígbà tí àwọn ènìyàn bá ń sùn."}, {"id": "folk-saworoide#s5", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "So Jogbo, that had been a kingdom, was plundered by its own rulers, which is the saddest plundering of all, for against it there is no wall to build and no gate to shut. The public treasure, the common store that had been gathered and kept for the roads and the markets and the lean and hungry season, melted away into private fortune. The forests were sold off over the heads of the people who lived by them. The common wealth was eaten in the dark. And the ordinary men and women of Jogbo carried the whole weight of the cost upon their own backs, in hunger that gnawed and in a fear that taught them to be silent. The corruption that sat at the top pressed downward, as corruption always presses, floor by floor, until it reached and crushed the poorest hut at the very bottom, for it is the whole body, my children, that is made to bear the weight of a rotten and a swollen head.", "yo": "Bẹ́ẹ̀ ni a kó Jọ̀gbọ̀, tí ó ti jẹ́ ìjọba rí, lọ́rùn látọwọ́ àwọn alákòóso rẹ̀ fúnra wọn, èyí tí ó jẹ́ ìkógun tí ó bàjẹ́ jùlọ, nítorí kò sí ògiri láti kọ́ lòdì sí i, kò sì sí ẹnu-ọ̀nà láti tì. Ìṣúra gbogbo-gbòò, àkójọ-ọrọ̀ tí a kójọ tí a sì pa mọ́ fún àwọn ọ̀nà àti àwọn ọjà àti àkókò ọ̀dá àti ebi, yọ́ lọ di ọrọ̀ ìkọ̀kọ̀. A ta àwọn igbó lórí orí àwọn ènìyàn tí wọ́n ń gbé nípasẹ̀ wọn. A jẹ ọrọ̀ wọ́pọ̀ nínú òkùnkùn. Àwọn ọkùnrin àti obìnrin lásán ní Jọ̀gbọ̀ sì ru gbogbo ẹrù owó náà lórí ẹ̀yìn ara wọn, nínú ebi tí ń bù jẹ àti nínú ìbẹ̀rù tí ó kọ́ wọn láti dákẹ́. Ìbàjẹ́ tí ó jókòó ní orí tẹ̀ mọ́lẹ̀, bí ìbàjẹ́ ṣe máa ń tẹ̀ nígbà gbogbo, ilẹ̀-kílẹ̀, títí tí ó fi dé tí ó sì run ahéré tálákà jùlọ ní ìsàlẹ̀ gan-an, nítorí gbogbo ara ni, ẹ̀yin ọmọ mi, à ń mú ru ẹrù orí tí ó ti bàjẹ́ tí ó sì wú."}, {"id": "folk-saworoide#s6", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "But the covenant, my children, hear this and hold it, the covenant had not died merely because one grasping man had refused it. It had done no more than withdraw a little way off, and wait. The sacred bell still hung in its appointed place, patient as brass is patient. The drummer Àyàngalu still carried, deep in his hands and deeper in his memory, the true and secret rhythm of the covenant, the rhythm that calls old powers to attention. The ancestors do not forget an oath merely because the living have found it convenient to pretend it was never sworn, and the brass, above all things, does not forget. What Lápité had so cleverly evaded was never a mere formality to be skipped by a busy man. It was a living power. And a living power that has been cheated of its due, my children, does not simply shrug and wander away.", "yo": "Ṣùgbọ́n májẹ̀mú náà, ẹ̀yin ọmọ mi, ẹ gbọ́ èyí kí ẹ sì dì í mú, májẹ̀mú náà kò kú nítorí pé ọkùnrin oníwọra kan ṣoṣo ni ó kọ̀ ọ́. Ohun tí ó ṣe ni pé ó fà sẹ́yìn díẹ̀, ó sì dúró de. Agogo mímọ́ náà ṣì so sí ibi tí a yàn fún un, pẹ̀lú sùúrù bí idẹ ṣe ní sùúrù. Àyàngalu onílù ṣì gbé, jíjìn nínú ọwọ́ rẹ̀ àti jíjìn jù nínú ìrántí rẹ̀, ìlù tòótọ́ àti ìkọ̀kọ̀ ti májẹ̀mú náà, ìlù tí ó ń pe àwọn agbára àtijọ́ sí ìfarabalẹ̀. Àwọn baba ńlá kì í gbàgbé ìbúra nítorí pé ó rọrùn fún alààyè láti ṣe bí ẹni pé a kò búra rẹ̀ rí, idẹ, ju gbogbo nǹkan lọ, kì í gbàgbé. Ohun tí Lápité fi ọgbọ́n yẹra fún kì í ṣe ààtò lásán tí ọkùnrin tí ó ní ìkánjú lè fò. Agbára alààyè ni. Agbára alààyè tí a sì ti rẹ́ jẹ, ẹ̀yin ọmọ mi, kì í wulẹ̀ gbọn èjìká kí ó sì rìn kúrò."}, {"id": "folk-saworoide#s7", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "In the fullness of the story, then, the old covenant came back, and it came back wearing the face of accountability, which is the face that patient things wear when at last they move. The crown that Lápité had snatched up without the binding could not, in the final reckoning, be worn upon an unbound head with impunity, for the very incantations woven into it answered not to the man but to the covenant he had scorned and refused. The bell that he had dismissed in his heart as a bit of old ceremony, a quaint noise for the credulous, proved itself to be the single truest law in all of Jogbo, a law that no bribe however heavy could soften and no soldier however fierce could frighten, and for one plain reason: because it was not written on paper that men could burn, but into the throne itself, into the very seat of his stolen power.", "yo": "Nínú ìkún-òye ìtàn náà, nígbà náà, májẹ̀mú àtijọ́ padà wá, ó sì padà wá pẹ̀lú ojú ìjíhìn, èyí tí ó jẹ́ ojú tí àwọn ohun onísùúrù ń wọ̀ nígbà tí wọ́n bá gbéra níkẹyìn. Adé tí Lápité ti gbà láìsí ìdè náà kò lè, nínú ìṣirò ìkẹyìn, wọ̀ lórí orí tí a kò dè ní àìlẹ́bi, nítorí àwọn ọfọ̀ tí a hun sínú rẹ̀ dáhùn kì í ṣe sí ọkùnrin náà bí kò ṣe sí májẹ̀mú tí ó gàn tí ó sì kọ̀. Agogo tí ó ti kọ̀ sílẹ̀ nínú ọkàn rẹ̀ gẹ́gẹ́ bí àjẹkù ààtò àtijọ́, ariwo tí kò níláárí fún àwọn onígbàgbọ́ asán, fi ara rẹ̀ hàn gẹ́gẹ́ bí òfin tòótọ́ kan ṣoṣo ní gbogbo Jọ̀gbọ̀, òfin tí àbẹ̀tẹ́lẹ̀ wúwo tó bá wù kí ó rí kò lè rọ̀ àti tí ọmọ-ogun líle tó bá wù kí ó rí kò lè dẹ́rù bà, àti fún ìdí kan tí ó ṣe kedere: nítorí a kò kọ ọ́ sórí bébà tí àwọn ènìyàn lè sun, bí kò ṣe sínú ìtẹ́ fúnra rẹ̀, sínú ìjókòó agbára tí ó jí gan-an."}, {"id": "folk-saworoide#s8", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "So the man who had thought himself free of all restraint discovered, too late, that he had been sitting all along upon the instrument of his own judgement, and that the louder he had laughed at the covenant, the more surely he had been calling it toward himself. The ceremony he mocked was the fuse he had lit. The duty he fled was the hand already closing upon his collar. And the bell rang.", "yo": "Bẹ́ẹ̀ ni ọkùnrin tí ó ti rò pé òun ti bọ́ lọ́wọ́ gbogbo ìdíwọ́ wá rí, lẹ́yìn àkókò, pé òun ti jókòó ní gbogbo ìgbà lórí ohun èlò ìdájọ́ ara rẹ̀, àti pé bí òun ṣe ń rẹ́rìn-ín sí májẹ̀mú náà sí i, bẹ́ẹ̀ ni òun ṣe ń pè é wá sọ́dọ̀ ara rẹ̀ dájúdájú sí i. Ààtò tí ó fi ṣe yẹ̀yẹ́ ni fìùsì tí ó dáná sí. Ojúṣe tí ó sá fún ni ọwọ́ tí ó ti ń dì kọlu ọrùn rẹ̀. Agogo sì dún."}, {"id": "folk-saworoide#s9", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "Hear, then, the wisdom of Jogbo, my children, for it was never truly about one dead king in one old town, but speaks to every age and to every office that men have ever devised. Power is never, ever meant to stand alone upon its own two feet. The crown and the covenant are two halves of one single thing, and the ruler who grasps greedily at the first while shrugging off the second is building his whole house upon a hidden and a widening fault. Let those of you who would one day lead, in a palace or a parish or a company or a single household, remember the Ṣaworoidẹ: an authority that will not bind itself, in its own flesh, to responsibility is not strength at all, whatever it looks like in the noonday of its pride. It is only theft, my children, that has put on a crown and sat down to dine, waiting, though it does not know it is waiting, for the bell to ring.", "yo": "Ẹ gbọ́, nígbà náà, ọgbọ́n Jọ̀gbọ̀, ẹ̀yin ọmọ mi, nítorí kò jẹ́ nípa ọba kan tí ó ti kú ní ìlú àtijọ́ kan rárá, bí kò ṣe pé ó bá gbogbo sànmánì àti gbogbo ipò tí àwọn ènìyàn ti dá sílẹ̀ rí sọ̀rọ̀. A kì í pète, láéláé, kí agbára dúró ní òun nìkan lórí ẹsẹ̀ ara rẹ̀ méjèèjì. Adé àti májẹ̀mú jẹ́ ìdajì méjì ohun kan ṣoṣo, alákòóso tí ó bá fi ìwọra gbá àkọ́kọ́ mú nígbà tí ó ń gbọn èkejì sẹ́yìn ń kọ́ gbogbo ilé rẹ̀ sórí àṣìṣe tí ó fara sin tí ó sì ń fẹ̀. Ẹ jẹ́ kí àwọn nínú yín tí yóò ṣe olórí ní ọjọ́ kan, nínú ààfin tàbí ìjọ tàbí ilé-iṣẹ́ tàbí agboolé kan ṣoṣo, rántí Ṣaworoidẹ: àṣẹ tí kò ní dì ara rẹ̀, nínú ẹran-ara rẹ̀, mọ́ ojúṣe kì í ṣe agbára rárá, bí ó ti wù kí ó rí ní ọ̀sán gangan ìgbéraga rẹ̀. Olè ni, ẹ̀yin ọmọ mi, tí ó ti wọ adé tí ó sì jókòó láti jẹun, tí ó ń dúró, bí ó tilẹ̀ jẹ́ pé kò mọ̀ pé òun ń dúró, de ìgbà tí agogo yóò dún."}]}, {"id": "folk-agogo-eewo", "emoji": "📖", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#0a1a00,#1a3a00)", "category": "iwe", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "8 min", "title": {"en": "Agogo Èèwọ̀: The Forbidden Gong and the New King", "yo": "Agogo Èèwọ̀"}, "desc": {"en": "In the sequel to the Jogbo covenant, a new ruler refuses to preserve the comfortable corruption the chiefs expect of him, and the sacred forbidden gong becomes the instrument by which hidden guilt is finally dragged into the light.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Folktales / Literary", "secondary": [], "source_note": "Retelling of 'Agogo Èèwọ̀' by Akínwùnmí Ìṣọ̀lá (filmed by Túndé Kẹ́lání), the sequel to Ṣaworoidẹ; a parable of reform against entrenched power.", "segments": [{"id": "folk-agogo-eewo#s1", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "My children, do not ever let anyone persuade you that the removing of one corrupt ruler is the same thing as the removing of corruption. That is the sweet and lazy lie that a weary people tells itself in the first glad days after a tyrant falls, and it has cost many a hopeful town its second disappointment. When the rotten king is gone at last, the people who grew fat and comfortable in his long shadow are, very often, still sitting exactly where they always sat, in the cool of the palace, still smiling their patient smiles, still waiting to teach the next ruler how things are quietly done around here. So it was in the kingdom of Jogbo, after the great reckoning of the brass bell, when a new king came up at last to the troubled and the emptied throne."}, {"id": "folk-agogo-eewo#s2", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "The chiefs of Jogbo had their expectations laid out and ready, as neat as cloth folded for market. They had lent their hands to the settling of the crown upon the new ruler's head, and they believed, with the comfortable certainty of men who have never yet been refused, that they understood the bargain they had struck. The king, they reasoned among themselves, would keep the throne, and the honour, and the praise of the drummers, and the fine beaded crown. But the chiefs, they were quite sure, would keep the other things, the quiet arrangements, the overlooked accounts, the generous share of the common wealth that long habit had taught them to think of as simply, rightfully, their own. The king would reign in the sunlight; the chiefs would feed in the shade."}, {"id": "folk-agogo-eewo#s3", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "But the new ruler, my children, had not climbed to that throne in order to be the faithful keeper of the old sickness. Instead of blessing the comfortable arrangement with a wink and a full cup, he set about doing the single most dangerous thing a reformer can ever do in a corrupted place: he began, quietly and then plainly, to demand fairness. He asked, out loud, where the wealth had gone. He asked that the offices of the kingdom be made to serve the people of the kingdom, rather than the people who happened to hold the offices. He treated the throne, in short, exactly as the old covenant had always meant for it to be treated, as a trust laid upon him, and not as a trough set out for his convenience."}, {"id": "folk-agogo-eewo#s4", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "And at once, my children, the chiefs understood the size of their mistake, and the understanding turned their smiles cold. They had lifted onto the throne a man they could not lead by the nose, and a king who cannot be led is, to men such as these, not a blessing to be enjoyed but a threat to be managed. So they turned, as the entrenched and the guilty always turn, to the well-worn weapons that had never yet failed them: to manipulation, to the whispered scheme in the dark corridor, to the warm false friendship offered with one hand while the other hand works at the knife, to the slow and patient labour of undermining a leader who will not be bought at any price. For their true aim, mark this well, was never merely to defeat one stubborn man. Their aim was to preserve, whole and breathing, an entire system that had made them rich, of which the stubborn man was only the latest and most inconvenient obstacle."}, {"id": "folk-agogo-eewo#s5", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "Now you must understand the gong that gives this second story its name, for it is the hinge on which the whole tale swings. The Agogo Èèwọ̀ is the forbidden gong, a sacred instrument of oath and of ordeal, kept apart and brought out only in the gravest extremity. When it is sounded, and the people are made to come forward and swear upon it, the gong takes no account whatever of a man's rank, or his wealth, or the cleverness of his tongue. It answers to one thing only, and that thing is the truth. To the honest man who swears upon it truly, it is silent, and safe, and no more than cold metal. But to the guilty man who dares to swear falsely upon it, the forbidden gong is death. It is, my children, the one voice in all the kingdom that simply cannot be bribed, cannot be flattered, and cannot be frightened."}, {"id": "folk-agogo-eewo#s6", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "And so, against all the patient schemes of the chiefs, against the whispers and the false friendships and the undermining, the forbidden gong becomes at last the great leveller, the one court that their cunning cannot reach around. Where the ordinary courts of men can be twisted, where witnesses can be purchased and judges can be frightened into blindness, the èèwọ̀ stands wholly outside the reach of all of it. It drags the hidden guilt of the powerful up into the pitiless light, not by clever argument, which the guilty are always better at than the innocent, but by ordeal, which no argument can survive. And the men who had thought themselves forever untouchable, armoured in rank and ritual, are brought at the last to a reckoning that no amount of cunning can talk its way out of."}, {"id": "folk-agogo-eewo#s7", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "So this second story completes the hard lesson that the first one only began, and the two must be held together, my children, like the two halves of a kola nut. In Ṣaworoidẹ we learned that power itself must be bound, in the ruler's own flesh, to the covenant, or it rots. But here in Agogo Èèwọ̀ we learn the harder and the less welcome second half of the truth: that even after the covenant is restored and the right king is seated, the great work is not finished, is nowhere near finished, because the many who feasted upon the old corruption do not vanish with the one tyrant, and they do not surrender quietly, and they do not play fair. Reform, hear this clearly, is never a single bright victory to be won and then forgotten. It is a long siege, and the reformer must steel himself to be undermined, patiently and from within, by the very people who smiled and helped to place him on his throne."}, {"id": "folk-agogo-eewo#s8", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "Remember, then, both the gongs of Jogbo, my children, and keep them both alive in whatever house or office the gods one day set you over. Remember the brass bell above, the Ṣaworoidẹ, that binds the ruler to his sworn duty and will not let him forget it. And remember the forbidden gong below, the Agogo Èèwọ̀, that strips the painted mask from those who hide their guilt behind their rank and their ritual and their fine words. A people who keep both of these alive, the binding covenant above and the fearless truth below, can cleanse even a palace that has been rotten for a generation. But a people who allow both of them to fall silent, out of weariness or out of fear, will be ruled, forever and without appeal, by the comfortable and the corrupt."}]}, {"id": "folk-koseegbe", "emoji": "📖", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#0a1a00,#1a3a00)", "category": "iwe", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "8 min", "title": {"en": "Kòṣeégbé: The Honest Officer They Could Not Buy", "yo": "Kòṣeégbé"}, "desc": {"en": "An honest customs officer named Mako is raised to a senior post and sets out to cleanse an institution rotten with fraud. Unable to corrupt him, his enemies set traps to make an honest man appear guilty.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Folktales / Literary", "secondary": [], "source_note": "Retelling of 'Kòṣeégbé' by Akínwùnmí Ìṣọ̀lá, a Yoruba novel on integrity and the price of standing alone against corruption.", "segments": [{"id": "folk-koseegbe#s1", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Come close, my children, and imagine with me a place of work, an office, a service, where every single person inside it knows, in their bones, that something is deeply wrong, and where every single person has nonetheless quietly agreed, year upon year, that the wrong thing is simply the way business is done here. No one speaks of it aloud, because nearly everyone profits from it, or fears the ones who do, or has long since grown too tired to go on fighting it. Now imagine, into that settled and comfortable rot, there walks one new officer, who looks steadily at the wrongness that everyone has agreed not to see, and says, plainly and without raising his voice, the one word that no one there has said in years: No. This will not continue."}, {"id": "folk-koseegbe#s2", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "His name was Mako, and he was that rare and difficult and uncomfortable kind of man upon whom corruption can find no handle to grip. He was intelligent, and he was diligent, and, hardest of all to forgive, he was principled the whole way through, not merely upon the smooth surface where principle can be admired and praised, but down underneath, in the dark, where principle is actually tested and where most men quietly make their peace. When the service raised him to a senior customs appointment, he saw at once, with his clear and unwelcome eyes, an institution eaten hollow from within by fraud and by long practice of illegality, and he resolved, simply and terribly, to clean it."}, {"id": "folk-koseegbe#s3", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Now understand well the size of the danger in such a resolve, my children, for the storyteller wants you to feel it in your own stomach. A corrupt institution is not a basket of bad individual apples that a man may pick out and throw away one by one. It is a single living web, a thing of many strands, in which a great many people are bound together, strand to strand, by shared guilt and by shared profit, so that each depends upon the silence of the rest. To threaten the fraud at any one point is to pluck at the whole web at once, and the whole web feels the pull in the same instant, and it draws together, all of it, and it turns, all of it, upon the single hand that dared to pluck."}, {"id": "folk-koseegbe#s4", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "The people who fed upon the fraud moved first, as such people always move first, to do the thing that had never once failed them in all their comfortable years: they tried to buy him. For this is the first and the deepest assumption of the corrupt, the very ground they stand on, that every man alive has his price, that integrity is never more than a bargaining posture adopted to drive that price up, that the honest officer is merely a clever trader holding out, with a straight face, for a larger cut. So they made their offer. And Mako refused it. And they raised the offer, puzzled now, and he refused again. And they raised it higher still, and higher, and slowly, over those refusals, a cold and unfamiliar understanding settled upon them like a chill in the blood: this one, against all their experience of the world, truly could not be bought."}, {"id": "folk-koseegbe#s5", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "And so, having failed with the first weapon, they turned to the second, which is far crueller than the first, and far harder to stand against. If they could not make the honest man dishonest, then they would make the whole world believe that he already was. They set about laying traps, careful ones, patient ones, each designed with real cunning so that an innocent man would be made to appear guilty. A document slipped quietly into a place where it should never have been found. A transaction arranged, from the shadows, to bear his name. A story, plausible and poisonous, whispered into exactly the right ears at exactly the right time. Their aim now was to drown his stubborn integrity in the mere appearance of corruption, to muddy the water so thoroughly that no onlooker could any longer tell the one clean man apart from the many dirty ones."}, {"id": "folk-koseegbe#s6", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "This, my children, is the loneliest hour in the whole life of the upright, and you must know about it before it comes to you, as in some form it surely will. It is one thing to refuse a bribe, hard as that can be; the conscience afterward is clear and the sleep is sound and a man knows what he is. But it is another thing altogether, a far heavier thing, to be made to look to the whole world like the very thing you have spent yourself fighting, to stand and watch your own good name, the work of a lifetime, taken up and used as a weapon against you by the very people whose crimes you refused to share. Many a good officer, who stood like iron against the first weapon and never wavered, has broken clean in half at exactly this second point, and reasoned bitterly that if he is to be called corrupt no matter what he does, he may as well, after all, take the money and the quiet life."}, {"id": "folk-koseegbe#s7", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "But the very title of the tale is its unbending spine, and the storyteller gave it that title on purpose. Kòṣeégbé: it cannot be done carelessly, it is not a thing to be casually taken up on a good day and as casually set down on a hard one. Integrity, the title insists, is not a pleasant mood that visits a man when the weather of his life is fair. It is a load-bearing wall. And Mako had built his whole house squarely upon it, down to the foundation, so that when the storms of slander came howling against him, as they were always going to come, the house shook to its rafters, yes, but it stood. The traps, laid with such patience and such care, proved in the end to reveal far more about the men who had laid them than about the man they had been laid for, for a trap, my children, is a confession in the shape of a snare."}, {"id": "folk-koseegbe#s8", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "Hear the lesson, then, and keep it close, folded away against the day you will need it, for you will need it. The world does not always reward the honest quickly, and this is the thing the children's tales leave out and the grown tales put back in. Sometimes the world punishes the honest first, and loudest, and most unfairly of all. The corrupt will try, first, to buy you, and if they find your price is set too high for their purse, they will turn and try to paint you in their own filthy colours so that no one can tell you apart. Stand anyway, my children. Stand anyway. For a clean name that is dragged through the mud for a single hard season shines all the brighter when the slow rain of truth at last comes down and washes it. Kòṣeégbé. Some things must not be done carelessly, and the keeping of your own character is the very first of them."}]}, {"id": "folk-ireke-onibudo", "emoji": "📖", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#0a1a00,#1a3a00)", "category": "iwe", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "8 min", "title": {"en": "Ìrèké Oníbùdó: The Orphan Who Walked Through the Country of the Dead", "yo": "Ìrèké Oníbùdó"}, "desc": {"en": "An orphaned young man, driven from home by a false accusation, journeys through the City of the Dead, the town of the Backward People and the grip of a terrible mermaid, aided by his dead mother, until loss and danger carry him at last to kingship and wisdom.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Folktales / Literary", "secondary": [], "source_note": "Retelling of the novel 'Ìrèké Oníbùdó' by D.O. Fágúnwà, a masterwork of Yoruba supernatural fiction.", "segments": [{"id": "folk-ireke-onibudo#s1", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Come close, my children, and settle yourselves, for tonight I tell you the story of Ìrèké Oníbùdó, a man whose single lifetime held packed within it enough strange and terrible journeys to fill the lives of a dozen ordinary men. His beginnings were as bare as a yard swept clean before dawn. His parents died and left him early, with no shelter and no protection, in a world that has never once been gentle to the unprotected. And so his story begins, as so very many of the great Yoruba journeys begin, with a lone orphan standing small and uncertain at the edge of a wide, dark, and dangerous country, with no road marked out before him and no hand to hold."}, {"id": "folk-ireke-onibudo#s2", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "He found for himself, in time, a modest and an honest living, raising chickens, asking nothing of the wide world but to be let alone in peace with his small and clucking flock. But peace, my children, is not always granted to the humble merely because they are humble and ask for little. A false accusation rose up against him, a shameful matter concerning a farmer's wife, and though Ìrèké was wholly innocent of it, innocence is a thin and a poor shield when the powerful have already made up their minds and want a man gone. To save his very life he fled into the night, and in that fleeing he stepped, without knowing it, clean off the ordinary roads that men walk and onto the hidden roads that lead down and out of the world we know and into the other one."}, {"id": "folk-ireke-onibudo#s3", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "His long journey carried him first through Irewesi, the City of the Dead, where no living foot belongs and where every settled law of the ordinary world is stood upon its head. And from there it carried him onward to Ero-ehin, the town of the Backward People, where the customs of men all run in reverse, where what we honour they despise and what we bury they praise, and where a traveller is forced, whether he will or no, to learn just how much of what he had always taken for solid truth was in fact only the habit of his own village. For this is the deep art of Fágúnwà, who tells this tale: he builds these upside-down and inside-out countries not merely to astonish us, though they do astonish, but to hold up a dark mirror, so that in staring at these strange lands we are made, at last, to look honestly again at our own."}, {"id": "folk-ireke-onibudo#s4", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "And then the sea itself rose up against him, as if the land had not held perils enough. His ship was swallowed whole by the grey water, and out of the depths he fell into the cold grip of the terrible Gargantuan-Mermaid, a vast and ancient being of the deep places who held him fast as her captive and set before him a series of tasks that no strength of mortal arm or cleverness of mortal mind could ever hope to accomplish. And it was here, my children, sunk to the very bottom of his fortunes and his hope, that Ìrèké Oníbùdó learned the first great secret of all the great journeys: that the traveller, when the water closes over his head, cannot survive upon the strength that he carried in by himself alone."}, {"id": "folk-ireke-onibudo#s5", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "For in his deepest extremity, when the impossible tasks of the mermaid would surely have crushed and ended him, his dead mother came to him. Death had taken her out of the world of the living, yes, and set her beyond his sight, but death had not taken, and could not take, her love for the son she had left behind, and out of that far country beyond the grave she came to him, to counsel him and to guide his hands through the very trials that would otherwise have been his ending. This, my children, hold it gently, is one of the deepest truths our fathers wove into these old and frightening tales: that the bond between a mother and her child is not severed even by death itself, and that the ancestors who have gone on before us keep a watch, unseen, over the hard roads that we their children must still walk."}, {"id": "folk-ireke-onibudo#s6", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "So with her unseen help he survived even the mermaid of the deep, and the long journey carried him onward still, through further wonders and further terrors, and here is the thing you must see about it, the thing that is easy to miss amid all the monsters. Each peril that did not kill him stripped something away from him, some fear, some weakness, some childish softness, and each one laid something new in its place, some hardness that was wisdom, some patience that was strength. He who had set out a frightened orphan clutching at his few chickens was being forged, trial by fiery trial, into something larger and heavier and more fit to bear weight. For that, my children, is the hidden purpose of the whole Fágúnwà journey: the forest and the deep and the country of the dead are not only terrors to be fled, they are a furnace, and the frightened boy who is thrown into that furnace comes out of it, if the gods spare him, a man of true weight and settled substance."}, {"id": "folk-ireke-onibudo#s7", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "And so the road that had begun in bleak orphanhood and bitter false accusation ended, at the very last, in kingship and in deep understanding. The one who had owned nothing in all the world but a handful of chickens came at length into a true authority over others, and mark this carefully, my children, for it is the pith of the whole tale: he came into it not because blind fortune happened one idle day to smile upon him, but because he had passed, in his own flesh and spirit, through the City of the Dead and the grip of the deep and the furnace of a hundred trials, and had come out the far side purified, carrying within him the one kind of wisdom that cannot be taught by any teacher and cannot be bought at any price, the wisdom that only suffering, honestly borne and not run from, is able to give."}, {"id": "folk-ireke-onibudo#s8", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "Hold the shape of this story, then, my children, deep in your memory, for it is not the shape of one strange man's life only. It is the shape of a great many lives, and it may yet prove to be the shape of yours. We begin, all of us, unprotected and small. We are driven, some of us, by an injustice we did not deserve, out onto hard roads we would never have chosen. We meet, every one of us, terrors along those roads that are far too great for our own unaided strength. And we are saved, when we are saved, by help that reaches us from beyond our own selves, by a love that outlasts even death. And if we endure, if we do not lie down, the very journey that so nearly destroyed us becomes, in the end, the making of us. For the sugarcane, the elders say, grows sweetest of all where it has had to fight hardest through the hard and stony ground to reach the light."}]}, {"id": "folk-adiitu-olodumare", "emoji": "📖", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#0a1a00,#1a3a00)", "category": "iwe", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "8 min", "title": {"en": "Àdììtú Olódùmarè: The Mystery of God", "yo": "Àdììtú Olódùmarè"}, "desc": {"en": "Born into poverty, the young Àdììtú journeys through hardship and strange encounters that become a meditation on destiny, human choice, suffering and the unsearchable mystery of God.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Folktales / Literary", "secondary": [], "source_note": "Retelling of the novel 'Àdììtú Olódùmarè' by D.O. Fágúnwà, his philosophical masterwork on providence and the human lot.", "segments": [{"id": "folk-adiitu-olodumare#s1", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "My children, some stories open with gold heaped bright in the hand, and some stories open with nothing at all but hunger gnawing in the belly. The story of Àdììtú opens with the hunger, and it opens, too, with a question, a hard and a patient question that will follow the boy to the very end of his long road and that will follow us, if we are honest, to the end of ours: must a hard and a bitter beginning decide the whole shape of a person's life, or is there perhaps a hand at work above our beginnings, writing a longer and a stranger story than our small eyes, down here at the start of it, are able to read?"}, {"id": "folk-adiitu-olodumare#s2", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "He was born in the town of Ìlàkòse, to Òbìrí-Ayé his father and to Ìpọnjú-dìran his mother, and even the names his parents carried bring the weather of his childhood blowing into the room, for they speak, those names, of the hardness of the world and of a suffering handed down like a cloak from one generation to the next. His father had once been a prosperous farmer, a man of standing, but poor management and a taste for excess had, between them, pulled down everything the man had built, so that Àdììtú entered not merely a poor house but a house that had known wealth and had lost it, which is, my children, a particular and a bitter kind of poverty, the kind that lies awake remembering better days."}, {"id": "folk-adiitu-olodumare#s3", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "Poverty soon reached in and interrupted even the boy's schooling, closing a door that had barely begun to open, and here Àdììtú learned early, far too early, a hard lesson that the comfortable are mercifully spared until much later, if they must learn it at all: that talent and ambition do not always, or even often, defeat the plain brute fact of material circumstance. A bright child in a poor house may stand and watch the shining doors of his own future swing shut, one after another, not through any fault or failing of his own, not for lack of wit or will, but simply and only because there is no money in the house to hold them open. It is one of the bitterest lessons the world teaches, and many a gifted life has been quietly broken upon it in the dark."}, {"id": "folk-adiitu-olodumare#s4", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "So he left the house of his hungry childhood and went out onto the open road, and the road, in Fágúnwà's telling, is never merely a road; it becomes at once a long procession of teachers, each one wearing a strange disguise. Àdììtú encountered, as he walked, people who stood as living pictures of the different ways that a human being may choose to live. Some among them possessed great wealth joined to no wisdom at all, rich and busy hands fastened to empty and rattling heads. Others possessed deep knowledge joined to no kindness, learned men, clever men, and cold as river stones. And others still possessed almost nothing in all the world that could be weighed or counted, and yet carried within them a banked and glowing hope, like a coal kept alive under ash, and these last, my children, the storyteller quietly shows us, were very often the richest of them all."}, {"id": "folk-adiitu-olodumare#s5", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "And through every one of these strange encounters the great question that opened the tale grew only deeper and more insistent, refusing every easy answer the boy tried to give it. Why does suffering fall where it falls, and pass by where it passes? Why is this child set down into plenty and that one, no worse and no better, into bare want? How much of a single human life is truly chosen by the one who must live it, and how much of it is written out beforehand by a hand that we can neither see nor stay? Fágúnwà, and this is the measure of his greatness and his reverence, does not pretend to crack the great question open like a kola nut and hold up the clean white halves for our inspection. He is far too honest a man, and far too reverent, for that cheap comfort. He names the whole matter, instead, by its one true name: Àdììtú Olódùmarè, the mystery of God."}, {"id": "folk-adiitu-olodumare#s6", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "For that, my children, is both the title of the tale and the beating heart of it. There is a providence that works, patiently and unseen, through our free choices and through our unchosen sufferings alike, bending even our worst hardships, in ways we cannot trace while we are inside them, toward some end that we were never able to foresee from where we stood. And it is a mystery, àdììtú, mark the word, not a riddle to be cleverly solved and then set aside, but a great depth to be stood before in silence. The truly wise do not stand up and claim to have measured it with their little measuring strings. They learn, instead, something humbler and far harder: to keep walking forward within the mystery, doing the right that is in front of them by whatever light they have been given, and trusting that the longer story, the one they cannot read, is held in surer and in kinder hands than their own."}, {"id": "folk-adiitu-olodumare#s7", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "And so Àdììtú, who began his days in hunger and in an inherited sorrow not of his own making, is carried by that very mystery, through all his trials and his strange teachers, toward meaning at the last, and understand clearly how, for it is the whole point. He arrives there not because he finally unravelled the riddle of God and stood triumphant over it, for no man does that or ever will. He arrives there because he kept faith, and kept walking, and kept doing the right in front of him, even while the great riddle was busy unravelling him, humbling him, enlarging him. His poverty, heavy as it was, did not have the final word over his life. His bitter beginning did not, in the end, decide his ending. The hand that worked above his beginning had been writing, all along, a far longer line than the frightened boy, down at the bottom of the page, had ever been able to read."}, {"id": "folk-adiitu-olodumare#s8", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "Carry this one home with you tonight, my children, and carry it with special care, those of you who were yourselves born into hard and hungry houses, for this story was told for you above all. Your beginning is a single sentence; it is not the whole of the book, and you are not the author of its first page. So do the right that is before you by the light that you have been given. Choose well, and bravely, in every place where the choosing is truly yours to do, and bear with a steady courage all that is not. And when you stand, as every thinking person must one day stand, before the great and unanswerable question of why the world is shaped as it is, do not let it turn you bitter, and do not let it puff you up with false and easy answers. Stand before it instead with a humble and a hoping heart, for it is the mystery of God, àdììtú Olódùmarè, and it is deeper by far than all our complaint, and kinder, in the end, than all our fear."}]}, {"id": "folk-rere-run", "emoji": "📖", "grad": "linear-gradient(135deg,#0a1a00,#1a3a00)", "category": "iwe", "rating": 9.5, "duration": "8 min", "title": {"en": "Rẹ̀rẹ̀ Rún: When the Wrong Road Runs Out", "yo": "Rẹ̀rẹ̀ Rún"}, "desc": {"en": "In a hard social world where cleverness is both a survival skill and a danger, reputation, crime and the pressures of the city draw people into conflict, and small choices grow, unseen, into consequences that cannot be escaped.", "yo": ""}, "yo_full": false, "primary_category": "Folktales / Literary", "secondary": [], "source_note": "Retelling of 'Rẹ̀rẹ̀ Rún' by Oládẹ̀jọ Òkédìjí, a Yoruba work on wrongdoing, consequence and the social pressures of the modern town.", "segments": [{"id": "folk-rere-run#s1", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌBẸ̀RẸ̀ · OPENING", "en": "Come close, my children, for tonight's tale wears no fine cloth and journeys to no distant and glittering kingdom. It is set down squarely in the hard, pressing, crowded world of the modern town, the world most of you will spend your lives inside, where people are packed close together, where a person's reputation is the only coin that spends everywhere, and where a sharp and ready wit can be, at one and the same time, the very thing that keeps a person alive and the very thing that, in the end, drags them down."}, {"id": "folk-rere-run#s2", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌGÒkè · RISING", "en": "In such a world, my children, cleverness is no luxury to be admired at leisure; it is a daily tool of plain survival. The person who cannot read the faces of others quickly and surely, who cannot find the hidden path when the open one is blocked, who cannot talk their way past the closed door and the suspicious eye, goes under, and the crowd closes over them without a ripple. And so the people of this story have all, in their own different ways and out of their own different needs, learned to be clever, have learned to navigate a difficult and an unforgiving environment where the honest, straight, patient road leads, far too often, only to the very back of a very long line."}, {"id": "folk-rere-run#s3", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌYÍPADÀ · TURNING", "en": "But the storyteller, working in the hard-eyed and unflinching tradition of his craft, sets himself to watch, closely and without sentiment, what happens when that survival-cleverness collides with the particular pressures of the crowded town. Reputation, in such a place, must be defended at almost any cost, for in a close community where everyone knows everyone a damaged name can quietly starve a whole family. Opportunity, when it passes, must be seized at once and without too many questions, for it does not come strolling by a second time. And the sheer pressure of the city, its ceaseless noise, its gnawing need, its endless cruel comparison of neighbour against neighbour, presses in upon people day and night until they begin to make the small compromises that seem, each one at the moment it is made, to be almost nothing at all."}, {"id": "folk-rere-run#s4", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "That, my children, is the quiet engine of the whole tragedy, and you must watch it very closely, for it does not announce itself with trumpets, and that is precisely its danger. No one in this story wakes up on a bright morning and decides, cleanly and deliberately, to become a wicked person. That is not how it goes, not here and not in your own lives. Instead, each person makes one small choice under pressure, a minor dishonesty here, a slight betrayal there, a convenient silence kept when a word should have been spoken. And each small choice, like a single grain of sand dropped onto a scale, joins quietly to all the others that came before it, until what began as a careless handful has become a heavy and a growing heap, and the heap becomes, at the last, a weight that no strength remaining to the person can any longer lift off their own chest."}, {"id": "folk-rere-run#s5", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "AGBÁRA · CLIMAX", "en": "And so the conflicts grow, as grow they must, for a weight like that cannot be carried in peace. Crime, which first drew certain people together in a kind of grim fellowship, soon sets those same people hard against one another, each suspecting each. Reputation, which was to be protected, hardens into a brittle idol that must now be defended by worse and worse means, until the defending is a greater crime than the first thing defended. And the very cleverness that was meant to solve the person's problems begins, instead, to manufacture new ones without end, for a lie once told must be guarded by a second lie, and a wrong once done must be covered over by a further wrong, and so the clever are kept forever busy. The people of the tale are caught now in a current of their own slow making, and the storyteller shows us, without mercy and without raising his voice, how that current quietly quickens toward the falls."}, {"id": "folk-rere-run#s6", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPinnu · RECKONING", "en": "The very title of the tale speaks the ending aloud before the ending arrives, the way our truest proverbs always do. Rẹ̀rẹ̀ rún: the thing is spoiled, it is scattered, it is brought down to ruin past all mending. For there comes a point upon the wrong road, my children, hear this and fear it a little, a point past which the traveller can no longer turn back the way he came, because the long accumulation of his own small choices has quietly closed and barred every exit behind him, one by one, while he was looking the other way. And the only direction left open to him then is the one direction he never meant to take, the one that runs straight down to the fall. The storyteller is merciless, and deliberately so, in showing us that this fatal point is reached not by one great and dramatic crime that a man might have seen coming and refused, but by the patient, invisible addition of a hundred little ones that no one thought worth refusing."}, {"id": "folk-rere-run#s7", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌPARÍ · RESOLUTION", "en": "And so, as in all of Òkédìjí's hard and honest tales, we are left at the end not with a comfortable moral painted brightly on the surface for children, but with a difficult truth dug out slowly from underneath, the kind a person must sit with. Cunning, the tale says, may well help a person to slip out of one pressing problem today; that much is true, and the clever will always tell you so. But if that cunning is not bound and guided by character, by ìwà, it will turn its clever hands at once to the building of the next problem, and the next, each one larger than the last. The wrong road does not punish you at the first step, when you could still so easily have turned; that is the whole cruelty and the whole cunning of it. It lets you walk, and walk, and feel clever and safe for a long while, and then it runs out from under your feet, all at once, with no warning and no bottom."}, {"id": "folk-rere-run#s8", "scene": "Ìwé", "mood": "ÌRÁNTÍ · LEGACY", "en": "Hear it, then, and be warned in good time, my children, while the warning can still do its work. Watch the small choices, above all the small ones, for they are the true architects of the person you will become, far more than the great and rare temptations that announce themselves and can be grandly refused. It is the little compromises, the ones that seem at the time far too small to matter, that quietly lay, stone by patient stone, the whole smooth road to ruin. Keep your cleverness, by all means; the gods gave it to you and it is no sin to use it. But set ìwà, character, to walk ahead of it and to hold the lamp, for cleverness without character arrives, every single time, soon or late, by a short road or a long one, at the one place our elders named rẹ̀rẹ̀ rún, the place where the thing is spoiled beyond all mending."}]}];
